window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-08-11T15:40:50.010289Z","count":211,"pages":[{"title":"Forteana","url":"/forteana/","description":"Forteana is the loose field of stories, reports and arguments that cluster around strange events which seem to fall between accepted categories: fish or...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Forteana Forteana Forteana Forteana Forteana is the loose field of stories, reports and arguments that cluster around strange events which seem to fall between accepted categories: fish or... Forteana is the loose field of stories, reports and arguments that cluster around strange events which seem to fall between accepted categories: fish or frogs said to fall from the sky, ghostly figures, cryptids, unusual aerial sightings, uncanny coincidences, miraculous claims, and other anomalies that are reported often enough to be culturally... forteana Introduction The useful way to read Forteana is not as a catalogue of things that must be true. It is better understood as a culture of anomaly: part folklore , part sceptical journalism, part history of science, and part challenge to how societies decide what counts as evidence. The strongest Fortean tradition keeps belief suspended. It asks why a report survives, what witnesses thought they saw, how institutions responded, and whether ordinary explanations are sufficient without pretending that every strange story proves a paranormal world. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia What makes something Fortean? A Fortean item is not just “weird”. It usually has three features: it is reported as a real world event, it resists easy classification, and it sits awkwardly between established explanation and popular wonder. Forteana therefore overlaps with paranormal studies, folklore, cryptozoology, unidentified flying object reports, psychical research and unusual natural history, but it is not identical with any one of them. The Society for Psychical Research’s Psi Encyclopedia notes that the boundaries vary, with commonly listed areas including parapsychology , cryptozoology and UFOlogy, while broader surveys add animal falls, odd hums, spontaneous human combustion claims, crop circles, miracles and hoaxes. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia That breadth is the point. Forteana is a category for cases that do not sit neatly inside one discipline. A report of a light in the sky might involve astronomy, aircraft recognition, military secrecy, psychology, rumour, local journalism and myth making. A report of raining fish might involve meteorology, animal behaviour, newspaper exaggeration and witness memory. A haunting story might involve architecture, bereavement, folklore, fraud, sleep paralysis or genuine uncertainty. Forteana keeps these possibilities in play long enough to examine them. The word also carries a stance. A Fortean approach is not the same as simply believing in monsters, ghosts or alien visitors. Fort’s own writing was playful, abrasive and often satirical. Later commentators have stressed that he offered extravagant hypotheses, such as a “Super Sargasso Sea” in the sky, partly to mock the confidence with which conventional explanations were sometimes offered. [cantab.net] cantab.net Chapter 1 < Moore and Forrest, More Things Chapter 1 < Moore and Forrest, More Things Charles Fort and the “damned” facts Charles Fort was born in Albany, New York, in 1874 and became a journalist and writer before turning his attention to anomalous reports gathered from newspapers, magazines and scientific journals. Scholar Charlotte Sleigh describes a decisive shift in the mid 1910s, when Fort began collecting unusual phenomena from published sources and reworked that material, with encouragement from novelist Theodore Dreiser, into four books: The Book of the Damned in 1919, New Lands in 1923, Lo! in 1931 and Wild Talents in 1932. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Fort’s method was simple but disruptive. He compiled reports that seemed to have been ignored, minimised or explained away: odd lights, strange falls, missing people, improbable coincidences and other “outcast” data. Project Gutenberg’s entry for The Book of the Damned summarises the book as a catalogue of anomalous phenomena that mainstream science had excluded or “damned”, including unusual sky sightings, mysterious falls of fish and frogs, poltergeist claims and unexplained disappearances. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project Gutenberg The Book of the Damned by Charles Fort Project Gutenberg Project Gutenberg The Book of the Damned by Charles Fort Project Gutenberg His target was not only individual explanations. It was the authority by which explanations become respectable. Joshua Blu Buhs, writing for The Public Domain Review , argues that Fort’s quarrel with science centred on its habit of cutting reality into accepted and rejected pieces; Fort’s own philosophy blurred “true” and “false” into an unstable middle realm. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... This is why Fort remains more interesting than a simple crank or credulous collector. He was often unfair to science, and many of his cases were weakly sourced by modern standards. Yet he recognised a real problem: institutions can be too quick to discard awkward observations, while enthusiasts can be too quick to turn uncertainty into belief. Forteana lives in that tension. The classic Fortean case: things falling from the sky If one image defines Forteana, it is probably the rain of frogs or fish. Fort collected many reports of anomalous falls: not just animals, but ice, sulphur, blood like substances, hay, insects, shells, birds and other odd material. The Psi Encyclopedia calls anomalous falls “archetypical” Fortean phenomena because they are transient, recurrent, hard to investigate after the event, and easily surrounded by supernatural or mythical interpretation. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Modern explanations often reduce the mystery without erasing the fascination. Waterspouts, whirlwinds, storms and tornadic activity can lift small animals or debris and deposit them elsewhere; in some frog cases, animals may simply emerge from hiding during rain and create the impression of a fall. A 2021 fish fall in Texas has even been discussed as possibly involving seabird regurgitation rather than a sky borne shower. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia The Fortean lesson is not that fish rain proves a hidden cosmic mechanism. It is that the same report can be several things at once: a meteorological puzzle, a newspaper story, a witness memory problem, a piece of local folklore and a reminder that rare natural events can sound impossible when stripped of context. Forteana is strongest when it holds those layers together rather than forcing a premature verdict. Forteana, science and scepticism Forteana is often confused with anti science, but the better distinction is between scepticism and dismissal. Scepticism asks for evidence, considers alternative explanations and accepts that some cases may remain unresolved. Dismissal decides in advance that a report is not worth attention. Fort attacked the latter, though he sometimes veered into rhetorical overreach against science as a whole. The related field of anomalistics gives this tension a more formal shape. The Psi Encyclopedia describes anomalistics as the study of unexplained claims without committing to belief or disbelief, treating anomalies as opportunities for disciplined inquiry. It notes that anomalistics overlaps with Forteana but places stronger emphasis on scientific evaluation, with Marcello Truzzi framing it as a neutral “friend of the court” in disputes over extraordinary claims. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia Sceptical writers have pushed back against Fort’s legacy from the other side. Joe Nickell, writing in Skeptical Inquirer , characterises Fort as someone who enjoyed taunting orthodox scientists with reports such as rains of frogs and other poorly examined anomalies. That criticism matters: a large part of Fortean material is anecdotal, duplicated, misreported, exaggerated or later explained. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Open source on skepticalinquirer.org. A balanced Fortean method therefore asks four practical questions: What exactly was reported? Strip the case back to the earliest available account, not the later legend. Who reported it, and through what channel? A police statement, local newspaper item, memoir and online retelling do not carry equal weight. What ordinary explanations fit the details? Weather, animals, hoaxes, misidentification , memory and media amplification often"},{"title":"Animal Falls","url":"/animal-falls/","description":"Reports of raining fish and frogs show how rare natural events can become lasting mysteries.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Animal Falls Can Fish and Frogs Really Fall From the Sky? Animal Falls Animal Falls Can Fish and Frogs Really Fall From the Sky? Reports of raining fish and frogs show how rare natural events can become lasting mysteries. Reports of raining fish and frogs show how rare natural events can become lasting mysteries. mechanism What witnesses usually report Waterspouts, storms, and animals Why explained cases still fascinate forteana animal falls Introduction Yes: fish, frogs and other small animals can plausibly fall from the sky, but the useful answer is less magical than the classic Fortean image suggests. The strongest ordinary explanations are selective lifting by storm updraughts or tornadic waterspouts , local flooding and wind scattering, animals emerging after rain, and, in some fish cases, birds dropping or regurgitating prey. The mystery usually begins because witnesses see the last stage — animals suddenly on roads, roofs, gardens or pavements — rather than the earlier stage that moved them there. The Library of Congress notes that scientists commonly point to tornadic waterspouts as a plausible mechanism for some fish and frog falls, while NOAA distinguishes these dangerous thunderstorm linked waterspouts from weaker fair weather waterspouts. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Most scientists agree that salt, stones, fish, or frogs can be The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Animal falls remain one of the most recognisable forms of Forteana because they sit in a perfect middle zone: strange enough to feel impossible, physical enough to leave bodies on the ground, and ordinary enough that weather, animals and human reporting can often explain them without erasing their oddness. Charles Fort ’s The Book of the Damned helped make rains of frogs, fish and other “falls” part of the Fortean canon, and later summaries of Forteana still list anomalous falls from the sky as a central category. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... What witnesses usually report The typical animal fall report is not a scientist watching fish rise from a pond into a funnel cloud. It is a resident, driver, shopkeeper or farm worker noticing small creatures during or just after rain: fish on a street, frogs on a path, worms on a pavement, or birds dropping suddenly across a neighbourhood. That matters because the observation is often real while the implied story — that the animals formed in the clouds or travelled a long distance through the sky — may be an inference. Several details recur in credible and semi credible reports. The animals are usually small, light and numerous. They are often all of one kind, such as tiny fish or frogs, rather than a random scoop of pond life. The event is often associated with storms, heavy rain, gust fronts or recent flooding. In older accounts, the precise time, weather radar, species identification and collection records are often missing, which makes later assessment difficult. The Library of Congress’s overview, for example, treats the phenomenon as rare but physically possible, while also presenting it through reported cases and explanatory hypotheses rather than as a routinely observed meteorological process. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Most scientists agree that salt, stones, fish, or frogs can be The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... That gap between the seen and the unseen is where the Fortean charge comes from. “Fish on the ground after a storm” is a fact that can be photographed. “Fish were sucked from a lake and carried over town” is a proposed mechanism. The best investigations keep those two claims separate. A report becomes stronger when there are many independent witnesses, animals are found on roofs or in truck beds rather than only beside drains, the species is identified, and local weather and animal behaviour are checked. The opposite is also true. A report becomes weaker when the animals could have washed out of a blocked drain, emerged from saturated soil, moved overland during wet weather, been dumped by people, or been dropped by feeding birds. Many supposed falls may not be falls at all. They may be sudden appearances that witnesses reasonably interpret as falls because rain, surprise and folklore provide a ready made story. Waterspouts, storms and animals The most familiar ordinary explanation is the waterspout. In simple terms, a waterspout is a rotating column of air over water. NOAA explains that waterspouts come in two broad types: tornadic waterspouts, which develop downward from thunderstorms, and fair weather waterspouts, which begin near the water surface and work upward under developing cumulus clouds. [National Ocean Service] oceanservice.noaa.gov National Ocean ServiceWhat is a waterspout?16 Jun 2024 — While tornadic waterspouts develop downward in a thunderstorm, a fair weather wa... For animal falls, the more relevant candidate is usually the tornadic or storm related version, because it has stronger updraughts and is connected with severe weather. The basic idea is straightforward: a vortex or intense updraught passes over shallow water, a pond edge, a flooded field or a concentrated school of small fish, lifts some of the contents, carries them for a short distance, and drops them when the circulation weakens. The Library of Congress cites the view that tornadic waterspouts may lift frogs, fish and other small objects, and notes a Purdue meteorologist’s comment that small ponds can be emptied by passing tornadoes. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Most scientists agree that salt, stones, fish, or frogs can be The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... This explanation is plausible, but it is not a magic key for every case. It has limits: Distance matters. Waterspouts and tornadoes are better at moving small objects locally than at neatly transporting one species across great distances. Selection matters. A real pond contains mud, plants, insects, snails, larvae and mixed species. A fall consisting almost entirely of one kind of fish or frog needs extra explanation. Observation matters. It is rare for people to document the full sequence: animals in water, vortex lifting them, transport, and fall. Weather matters. If there is no evidence of a waterspout, tornado or strong convective storm, the waterspout explanation becomes less convincing. That last point is important because popular retellings often use “waterspout” as a default ending. It sounds scientific, so it can become a tidier story than the evidence allows. A careful explanation should ask what kind of water body was nearby, what the weather was doing, whether a vortex was reported, whether radar or local meteorological records support strong uplift, and whether the animals’ condition fits storm transport. Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube When the fish did fall: Texarkana as a modern test case The December 2021 fish fall in Texarkana, Texas, is valuable because it shows how an animal fall case can move from instant folklore to evidence based sorting. Residents reported fish on streets and properties during a storm, and early public explanations leaned towards the standard waterspout idea. Local reporting and city statements treated it as an uncommon but real example of “animal rain”. [https://www.wdbj7.com] wdbj7.com Open source on wdbj7.com. Later investigation complicated that neat explanation. Independent researchers Paul Cropper and Sharon A. Hill collected witness accounts, photographs and video evidence, and obtained help identifying the fish. Hill reports that the fish were identified as gizzard shad and showed signs consistent with partial digestion. The researchers argued that the best explanation was not a waterspout but birds, probably cormorants, regurgitating fish while caught in storm conditions. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com Sharon A. Hill Texarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill Sharon A. Hill Texarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill The case is especially useful because it did not simply debunk the fall. Some evidence supported actual descent from above: fish were reportedly found on roofs, in truck beds and on exposed surfaces where simple flooding was hard to reconcile. The question shifted from “Did fish appear?” to “What lifted or dropped them?” In that sense, Texarkana is a model Fortean case: the event was strange, the first explanation was plausible but too broad, and a more specific ordinary mechanism emerged when species, condition, weather and local wildlife were examined. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com Sharon A. Hill Texarkana Fish Rain"},{"title":"Checklist","url":"/checklist/","description":"A strong animal-fall investigation separates what witnesses saw from what moved the animals there, then tests weather, species, location, and alternatives.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"Checklist How to Check an Animal Fall Story Checklist Checklist How to Check an Animal Fall Story A strong animal fall investigation separates what witnesses saw from what moved the animals there, then tests weather, species, location, and alternatives. A strong animal fall investigation separates what witnesses saw from what moved the animals there, then tests weather, species, location, and alternatives. policy intervention Record the exact scene and surfaces Identify species and animal condition Compare weather, wildlife, flooding, and human causes forteana animal falls checklist Introduction Animal fall reports are easiest to assess when investigators treat them as time sensitive field observations rather than as ready made mysteries. The central question is not whether witnesses genuinely found fish, frogs or other animals where they seemed unexpected, but how those animals arrived there. The first hours after discovery are crucial because rain, scavengers, traffic, clean up and repeated retelling can rapidly erase or distort the original evidence. A careful investigation separates observed facts from proposed explanations and tests each explanation against the physical evidence, local weather , species involved and surrounding landscape. This approach fits both scientific practice and the Fortean tradition of documenting unusual events without assuming either a paranormal or a mundane answer in advance. Guidance from wildlife health investigators also shows the importance of recording location, condition and context before specimens deteriorate or are moved. [USGS+2U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Field Manual of Wildlife Diseases U.S Geological SurveyJan 1, 2021 — The purpose of this manual is to provide information that will enhance the ecological understanding and co... Record the exact scene before anything changes The first task is to preserve information, not to explain it. Even a convincing looking animal fall can become impossible to evaluate if the original scene is poorly documented. Record immediately: The exact date and local time. GPS coordinates or a precise address. The weather at the time of discovery and during the preceding 24 hours. Whether rain was still falling or had already stopped. The approximate number of animals present. Whether they were concentrated in one patch, spread over a line, or scattered widely. The surfaces on which they were found, such as roofs, pavements, grass, parked vehicles or open fields. Photographs should be taken before animals are touched or collected. Wide angle photographs establish the overall distribution, while close up images document individual animals and surrounding features such as drains, gutters, ponds, streams, roofs or nearby trees. Witnesses should be asked to distinguish clearly between what they directly observed and what they inferred. \"I found fish after heavy rain\" is an observation . \"The fish fell from the clouds\" is a hypothesis that requires independent evidence. Identify the animals and their condition Species identification often narrows the range of plausible explanations more effectively than dramatic witness testimony. Useful questions include: Are all animals the same species or are several species mixed together? Are they similar in size and age? Are they freshwater, marine or terrestrial species? Are they common in nearby ponds, rivers or wetlands? Are they alive, freshly dead or decomposing? Uniformity can be informative. Reports involving many similarly sized juvenile fish, for example, are more consistent with a local source than a random collection from different habitats. Conversely, a mixture of unrelated species may suggest flooding, dumping or another transport mechanism. Condition also matters. Investigators should note visible injuries, missing scales or limbs, signs of bird predation, drying, mud deposits or crushing by vehicles. Wildlife disease investigations routinely stress documenting carcass condition because decomposition rapidly removes diagnostic clues. [USGS+2USGS] usgs.gov Necropsy & Pathology U.S. Geological SurveyThese scientists examine carcasses to verify species and condition, collect and process... Raining Fish in Yoro Honduras: Nature's Magic Unveiled! Channel: PlanTrip Open on YouTube Compare the report with recent weather Weather evidence should be gathered independently rather than relying solely on witness memory . Check: Thunderstorms or severe convective weather. Tornado or waterspout reports. Wind direction and estimated strength. Radar imagery where available. Rainfall totals. Local flooding or overflowing drainage systems. The timing matters as much as the weather itself. If the reported discovery occurred hours after a storm had passed, alternative transport mechanisms may have operated between the storm and the discovery. Conversely, an event occurring during an intense storm with documented rotating weather systems deserves closer examination because storm related lifting mechanisms have long been proposed for some animal fall reports. [NOAA Fisheries] fisheries.noaa.gov NOAA FisheriesNOAA Fisheries: Welcome to NOAANOAA Fisheries—the trusted government authority on the science and management of fish, other... Look for nearby natural sources Many apparently anomalous falls occur surprisingly close to ordinary sources. Survey the surrounding area for: Ponds. Streams. Flooded fields. Temporary pools. Drainage channels. Fish farms. Reservoirs. Coastal waters, if relevant. Measure approximate distances rather than estimating casually. A small pond only a few hundred metres away may prove more significant than a large lake several kilometres distant. The ground itself should also be examined. Mud splashes, flood debris, fresh runoff channels or water marks on vegetation may indicate recent transport across the landscape rather than through the air. Test ordinary alternatives one by one Rather than asking whether an event is mysterious, investigators should ask which explanation best fits all available evidence. Possible alternatives include: Storm transport. Strong storm updraughts or waterspouts lifting small aquatic animals. Flooding. Overflowing water carrying animals onto roads, gardens or fields. Overland movement. Frogs and other amphibians naturally dispersing during wet weather. Bird activity. Predators dropping or regurgitating prey while flying. Human causes. Discarded bait, fish stocking, deliberate hoaxes or accidental spills. Drainage systems. Animals emerging from culverts, storm drains or overflowing sewers. Each explanation should be tested against the observed distribution. For example, animals found only beside a flooded ditch support a different interpretation from animals reportedly discovered across rooftops in several streets. Why is it raining with animals? Channel: Profu' de geogra' Open on YouTube Evaluate the pattern of distribution The spatial pattern often provides stronger evidence than the number of animals. Questions worth documenting include: Does the distribution follow the direction of recent wind? Are animals concentrated in depressions where water collected? Are roofs affected as well as the ground? Are enclosed gardens involved? Are sheltered areas untouched while exposed surfaces contain animals? Does the distribution continue across property boundaries? A genuine transport event should usually produce a pattern that reflects physical processes rather than arbitrary placement. Interview witnesses carefully Witness interviews should be conducted as soon as possible, before stories become harmonised through conversation or media coverage. Useful questions include: When was the first animal noticed? Did anyone actually see animals descending? Was there hail, strong wind or unusual cloud structure? Who discovered the event first? Were photographs taken immediately? Were any animals removed before others arrived? Independent accounts should be preserved separately. Agreement on unexpected details can strengthen a report, while contradictions may reveal how assumptions entered the narrative over time. Animal Rain From Sky in Telangana Why Fish ‘rain’ from the sky in India Tornado upsc ias cse Channel: StudY LoveR VeeR Open on YouTube Preserve specimens appropriately Where collection is lawful and appropriate, representative specimens should be preserved carefully for later identification rather than gathering every animal indiscriminately. Good practice includes: Wearing appropriate protective equipment. Recording exactly where each specimen was collected. Keeping specimens cool if laboratory examination may follow. Avoiding contamination between specimens. Labelling containers with location, time and collector. Wildlife health guidance consistently emphasises that specimen value declines rapidly once decomposition, scavenging or poor handling begins. [USGS+2USGS] usgs.gov diagnostic case submission guidelines Diagnostic Case Submission GuidelinesJul 22, 2024 — Carcasses must"},{"title":"Marksville","url":"/marksville/","description":"The Marksville case remains memorable because it has named reporting and a clear date, yet still shows why older animal falls rarely settle mechanisms.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"Marksville What Marksville Can and Cannot Prove Marksville Marksville What Marksville Can and Cannot Prove The Marksville case remains memorable because it has named reporting and a clear date, yet still shows why older animal falls rarely settle mechanisms. The Marksville case remains memorable because it has named reporting and a clear date, yet still shows why older animal falls rarely settle mechanisms. event time window The 1947 Louisiana report Why named testimony matters What older records leave unresolved forteana animal falls marksville Introduction The reported fish fall at Marksville, Louisiana, on 23 October 1947 remains one of the best known American examples of an alleged \"rain of animals\" because it was investigated by a trained fisheries biologist soon after it occurred rather than surviving only as local folklore . That combination of a specific date, named witnesses and a published scientific account gives the incident unusual historical value. At the same time, it illustrates the limits of what older reports can establish. Even with contemporary investigation, no one directly observed the mechanism that brought the fish into the air, no meteorological record conclusively identified a waterspout, and many of the details that modern investigators would seek were never collected. Rather than proving that fish routinely fall from storms, the Marksville case demonstrates both how surprisingly well documented some historical reports can be and why they still leave important questions unresolved. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... The 1947 Louisiana report On the morning of 23 October 1947, residents of Marksville reported fish falling over a relatively confined part of the town between roughly seven and eight o'clock. Accounts described fish scattered across streets, pavements and rooftops over an area approximately 1,000 feet (about 300 metres) long and 75–80 feet (23–24 metres) wide. Vehicles reportedly crushed fish already lying on the road while some witnesses claimed they were struck by falling specimens. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... What distinguishes this case from many nineteenth century anecdotes is the presence of Alexander D. Bajkov, a fisheries biologist who happened to be in Marksville conducting field work. After hearing reports from a waitress while eating breakfast, he examined the scene, collected specimens and later published a paper in the journal Science titled \"Do Fish Fall from the Sky?\" in 1949. His account identified the animals as local freshwater species rather than exotic or marine fish. They included largemouth bass, goggle eye (warmouth), sunfish, minnows and hickory shad, generally measuring between about two and nine inches long. NMFS Scientific Publications Office+2Skeptics Stack Exchange [spo.nmfs.noaa.gov] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... Bajkov also recorded named witnesses, including local businessmen and a bank employee, rather than relying solely on anonymous testimony. That level of documentation makes the Marksville report far stronger than many historical animal fall stories whose origins cannot be traced beyond newspaper retellings . [Skeptics Stack Exchange] skeptics.stackexchange.com has it ever rained fish Skeptics Stack ExchangeHas it ever rained fish?17 May 2015 — There have been several reports of \"raining fish\" where fish fall from the s... Published: May 2015 Why named testimony matters Historical reports often become stronger when they include identifiable observers, preserved specimens and prompt investigation. The Marksville event contains all three. Several features increase its credibility as a genuine observational event: The investigator was a professional fisheries biologist rather than a later compiler of unusual stories. Fish were reportedly collected and identified shortly after the event. Multiple named local witnesses independently described fish falling rather than merely finding them later. The reported distribution covered a defined corridor instead of an entire town, consistent with a localised atmospheric event rather than widespread flooding. NMFS Scientific Publications Office+2Skeptics Stack Exchange [spo.nmfs.noaa.gov] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... Importantly, these strengths support one conclusion more confidently than another. They make it more likely that fish genuinely appeared across part of Marksville under unusual circumstances. They do not by themselves establish exactly how the fish reached the ground. This distinction is central in historical Fortean research. Reliable witness testimony can establish that something unexpected happened while remaining insufficient to determine its physical cause. Raining fish in 1947?! Channel: WAFB Open on YouTube What older records leave unresolved The enduring fascination of Marksville comes from the gap between well documented observations and missing evidence. No direct observation of transport No witness described seeing fish lifted into the atmosphere. Observers only encountered the final stage: fish descending or already lying on streets. Consequently, the most dramatic part of the story—the journey through the air—was inferred rather than observed. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... Limited meteorological evidence Modern investigators would compare eyewitness reports with Doppler radar, satellite imagery, detailed wind fields and high resolution storm records. None of these existed in 1947. Later discussions often suggest a waterspout or other strong atmospheric vortex as the most plausible mechanism because such phenomena can transport lightweight aquatic animals. However, no definitive meteorological observation confirms that one occurred at the relevant place and time. [The Weather Network] theweathernetwork.com The Weather NetworkOctober 23, 1947 Fish fall from the sky23 Oct 2020 — On October 23, 1947, the forecast was incorrect for Marksville... Published: October 23, 1947 Incomplete environmental reconstruction Although the fish were identified, many details that could test competing explanations were never systematically recorded. For example: the precise condition of every specimen; exact weather conditions throughout the preceding hours; water levels in nearby bayous; whether similar fish appeared outside the reported fall zone; [thefortean.com] thefortean.com Fish Fall Archives The ForteanThe Honduran media is reporting that the annual fish rain in Yoro, Honduras took place on Saturday 16 June.Read more... comprehensive mapping of where every specimen landed. These gaps are unsurprising for a 1947 field investigation but limit what later researchers can infer. October 23: It Be Rainin' Fish! Channel: The Weather Network Open on YouTube Why the waterspout explanation remains plausible but unproven Bajkov himself regarded atmospheric transport as the most likely explanation, and later discussions have generally favoured some form of vortex associated with nearby water. Marksville lies close to waterways such as Spring Bayou, providing an obvious potential source for small freshwater fish. A rotating column of air could, in principle, lift fish from shallow water and deposit them over a narrow area after losing strength. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov 1947 in Marksville, Louisiana (Bajkov, 1949). Between 7 and 8 o 'clock of that morrdng rish, raneing from...Read more... Yet plausibility is not proof. No photograph captured such a vortex, no observer reported seeing fish lifted from the bayou, and alternative mechanisms—while often less persuasive—cannot be eliminated with certainty decades later. The case therefore occupies an intermediate position. It is considerably stronger than folklore unsupported by identifiable witnesses, but it falls short of providing a complete observational record of atmospheric transport. Fish Raining from the Sky Channel: inFact with Brian Dunning Open on YouTube What Marksville can and cannot prove The Marksville fish fall remains historically important because it shows that reports of animal falls are not invariably products of rumour or embellishment. A professional investigator documented the event, identified the fish and recorded named eyewitnesses. Those features make it one of the most substantial twentieth century American cases. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov"},{"title":"Not Falling","url":"/not-falling/","description":"Some animal falls may begin on the ground, where flooded drains, saturated soil, and wet-weather migration create sudden appearances.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"Not Falling When Animal Rain Did Not Fall Not Falling Not Falling When Animal Rain Did Not Fall Some animal falls may begin on the ground, where flooded drains, saturated soil, and wet weather migration create sudden appearances. Some animal falls may begin on the ground, where flooded drains, saturated soil, and wet weather migration create sudden appearances. population context Floodwater, drains, and roadside deposits Frogs and worms moving after rain Clues that separate falls from appearances forteana animal falls not falling Introduction Not every apparent \"rain of animals\" involves anything falling from the sky. In many cases, heavy rain changes the landscape rather than transporting animals through the air. Floodwater fills drains, overflows ponds, saturates soil and creates temporary routes across normally dry ground. Frogs, toads, salamanders, worms and small fish can then appear suddenly on roads, pavements and gardens, giving witnesses the understandable impression that they have just fallen with the rain. Investigators of Fortean reports therefore treat \"animals found after rain\" and \"animals observed falling from the sky\" as two separate questions. The distinction matters because many convincing looking incidents can be explained entirely by flooding, drainage systems and wet weather animal movement rather than aerial transport. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?What is unusual in reports of animal rainfalls is the uniformity of the... Floodwater, drains and roadside deposits Heavy rainfall can rapidly redistribute small animals without lifting them into the atmosphere. Urban drainage networks, roadside ditches, streams and flooded fields act as temporary transport systems. As water levels rise, fish may escape from ponds into overflow channels, tadpoles can be washed downstream, and aquatic invertebrates may accumulate wherever floodwater slows or recedes. When floodwater drains away, it often leaves animals concentrated in depressions, gutters, car parks or road edges. Someone arriving after the storm sees dozens of fish or frogs apparently appearing \"out of nowhere\", even though they were carried horizontally by flowing water rather than vertically through the air. This mechanism is particularly plausible where reports are confined to low lying areas, drainage channels or places immediately connected to nearby water. Flooding can also expose animals already living underground. Burrows fill with water, leaf litter is displaced and saturated soil forces some species to seek drier ground. The sudden visibility of animals is therefore often an ecological response to changing ground conditions rather than evidence of an atmospheric event. The Library of Congress specifically notes that some supposed animal rains are false reports arising after storms when observers encounter unusually large numbers of animals on the ground that did not actually fall from the sky. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?What is unusual in reports of animal rainfalls is the uniformity of the... Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Frogs and worms moving after rain Rain dramatically alters how many animals are active on the surface. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rain of animals Rain of animalsRain of flightless animals and things has been reported throughout history. In the Bible, a rain of frogs is described... Amphibians are especially sensitive to moisture because their permeable skin dries quickly in dry conditions. Warm, wet nights trigger mass movements from woodland or underground refuges towards breeding ponds and seasonal pools. Conservation organisations monitor these migrations because thousands of frogs, toads and salamanders may cross roads during a single rainy evening, a phenomenon often called \"Big Night\" in North America. Volunteer surveys have documented tens of thousands of migrating amphibians using these routes, demonstrating that large numbers can appear almost simultaneously without any aerial transport. [Department of Environmental Conservation] dec.ny.gov Department of Environmental Conservation Amphibian Migrations And Road Crossings Department of Environmental ConservationAmphibian Migrations And Road Crossings NYSDECThe Amphibian Migrations and Road Crossings (AM&R... Earthworms provide another classic example. After heavy rain, pavements and roads may suddenly become covered with worms. Researchers continue to debate the precise balance of causes, but several mechanisms are supported: saturated soil changes oxygen availability for some species; wet surfaces allow safer overland travel without desiccation; rain related vibrations or other environmental cues may stimulate emergence in certain circumstances. Importantly, not every worm in the soil emerges, and the behaviour varies among species. The result is nevertheless dramatic enough that people unfamiliar with the biology may assume the worms have arrived with the rainfall itself. [Cool Green Science+2The Times] blog.nature.org Cool Green ScienceThe Real Reason You See Earthworms After RainApril 15, 2019 — 15 Apr 2019 — Another common explanation for worm emergen... Published: April 15, 2019 These wet weather migrations can be remarkably local. A neighbourhood that appeared empty an hour earlier may suddenly contain hundreds of frogs or worms simply because environmental conditions crossed the threshold that triggered movement. Raining spiders, frogs, fish and ... meat. Yes, meat. Channel: The Why Files Open on YouTube Why these events resemble animal rain Several psychological and observational factors make overland movement easy to mistake for falling animals . First, people usually witness the aftermath rather than the movement itself. Rain reduces visibility, encourages people to remain indoors and masks gradual animal dispersal. When the weather clears, the animals are already scattered across streets or gardens. Second, rainfall provides an obvious narrative link. If animals are first noticed during or immediately after a storm, it is natural to connect the two events by assuming they descended with the rain. Third, sudden abundance is inherently surprising. Many amphibians spend most of the year hidden underground or beneath vegetation. Their synchronised emergence after favourable weather can make a familiar species appear unexpectedly exotic or mysterious. Historical reports often preserve only the observation that animals were \"found after heavy rain\", while omitting whether anyone actually witnessed them falling. Later retellings may gradually transform a report of sudden appearance into a report of literal animal rain. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?What is unusual in reports of animal rainfalls is the uniformity of the... Clues that separate falls from appearances Investigators evaluating alleged animal rains look for practical clues that distinguish genuine aerial deposition from ground level redistribution. Evidence favouring ordinary flooding or overland movement includes: animals concentrated beside drains, culverts, streams or flood channels; obvious recent flooding connecting ponds or wetlands to the discovery site; live amphibians actively hopping or crawling away rather than lying where they supposedly landed; worms or amphibians appearing over several hours as rain continues; observations confined to roads, pavements and gardens rather than roofs, elevated structures or enclosed spaces. Evidence that would strengthen the case for an actual atmospheric fall includes: multiple independent witnesses seeing animals descend through the air; animals found on rooftops, elevated decks or isolated surfaces difficult to reach by crawling or flowing water; deposition patterns inconsistent with local drainage or flooding; weather conditions capable of producing powerful storm updraughts. Because most reports lack these stronger indicators, many remain better explained as sudden appearances caused by rain driven ecological processes than as confirmed examples of animals transported through the atmosphere. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?What is unusual in reports of animal rainfalls is the uniformity of the... Were U Wondering: Why do worms come out on the sidewalks after it rains? Channel: UW Madison Open on YouTube Why this explanation matters in Forteana Within Fortean studies, recognising flood driven appearances prevents different phenomena from being merged into a single mystery. A witness who genuinely finds dozens of frogs after a storm has observed something unusual and worth documenting. The crucial question is not whether the frogs appeared unexpectedly—they clearly did—but how they reached that location. Separating horizontal movement from aerial"},{"title":"One Species","url":"/one-species/","description":"A fall made mostly of one species raises a harder question than scattered pond debris because real water bodies contain messy mixtures of life.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"One Species Why Do Animal Falls Look So Selective? One Species One Species Why Do Animal Falls Look So Selective? A fall made mostly of one species raises a harder question than scattered pond debris because real water bodies contain messy mixtures of life. A fall made mostly of one species raises a harder question than scattered pond debris because real water bodies contain messy mixtures of life. mechanism The selection problem in fish and frog falls Schools, shorelines, and concentrated animals When selectivity points away from storm uplift forteana animal falls one species Introduction One of the most persistent puzzles in reports of animal falls is not simply that fish, frogs or worms appear after a storm, but that they often appear to consist almost entirely of a single species. This matters because the leading natural explanation—storm uplift by waterspouts or powerful updraughts—would seem, at first glance, likely to collect a mixed sample of whatever happened to be present in the source water. The apparent selectivity therefore raises a legitimate question: why do so many reports describe only minnows, only frogs or only one kind of worm? The answer is less straightforward than either sceptics or believers sometimes suggest. A single species fall is not impossible under ordinary processes, but it demands more specific conditions than the simple image of a storm scooping up an entire pond. Whether a report strengthens or weakens the storm hypothesis depends on the ecology of the animals involved, the likely source habitat, and how carefully the species was actually identified. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Although waterspouts are the most commonly offered explanation The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... The selection problem in fish and frog falls The intuitive objection is simple. Natural ponds, lakes and marshes are biologically messy. Even small freshwater habitats usually contain multiple fish species alongside amphibians, aquatic insects, crustaceans, snails, plants and floating debris. A random sample lifted wholesale from such an environment ought to look similarly mixed. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Natural History MuseumPond life: facts about pond habitats, plants and animalsFind out about the animals and plants that rely on ponds, t... That expectation makes reports of \"nothing but small perch\", \"only tiny frogs\", or \"all the fish were the same kind\" seem harder to reconcile with an indiscriminate lifting mechanism. However, the apparent contradiction is not as decisive as it first appears. Several factors can produce genuine concentration without requiring a mysterious process: Many fish naturally form schools composed almost entirely of one species. Young fish of the same age often occupy shallow water together. Amphibians gather seasonally in breeding ponds and may emerge in large numbers at similar life stages. Certain habitats are already dominated by a single species because predators, vegetation or water chemistry favour it. The issue therefore becomes one of probabilities rather than impossibilities. A storm intersecting one dense aggregation could produce a much more selective sample than one passing over a biologically diverse pond. What Wildlife lives in UK Ponds? Channel: A Shot Of Wildlife Open on YouTube Schools, shorelines and concentrated animals Animal populations are rarely distributed evenly across a body of water. Many small freshwater fish spend much of their lives schooling with their own species, particularly juveniles. Shallow margins can become crowded with thousands of similarly sized individuals while deeper water contains different species altogether. A rotating updraught that intersects only part of this habitat would not necessarily collect a representative sample of the entire ecosystem. Likewise, amphibians often become highly concentrated during breeding seasons. Temporary pools may contain enormous numbers of tadpoles or newly metamorphosed froglets with relatively few other vertebrates present. Heavy rain and storms also coincide with periods when frogs move across land, increasing the chance that witnesses encounter large numbers of one species after bad weather without requiring long distance aerial transport. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Natural History MuseumPond life: facts about pond habitats, plants and animalsFind out about the animals and plants that rely on ponds, t... This ecological clustering means that \"single species\" does not automatically imply selective atmospheric sorting. It may instead reflect selective availability at the source. Why reports can appear more selective than they really are Witness descriptions often simplify what they observe. People frequently report \"it rained fish\" after noticing dozens of similar looking animals scattered across a street. Unless specimens are collected and identified by specialists, subtle differences between related species may go unnoticed. A mixture of several minnow species can easily become \"all little fish\" in news reports. The opposite also occurs. Investigators sometimes discover that an apparently uniform fall actually included several species once careful identification was carried out. Historical accounts illustrate this point. For example, the well known Marksville , Louisiana fish fall was remembered as a fish rain, yet later examination reported several freshwater species rather than a perfectly uniform collection. [NMFS Scientific Publications Office] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov NMFS Scientific Publications OfficeRAINS OF FISHESApril 6, 2011 — by BOFC FISHERIES · 1961 — The fish were fresh water species native to... Published: April 6, 2011 Reporting biases reinforce the impression of selectivity: Journalists tend to emphasise the dominant species. Witnesses naturally focus on the most obvious animals. Small invertebrates or plant fragments may go unnoticed. Few historical cases preserved specimens for later analysis. As a result, \"only one species\" often reflects the available observations rather than a complete biological inventory. Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube When selectivity points away from storm uplift Even allowing for schooling behaviour and reporting bias, some descriptions remain awkward for the simplest waterspout explanation. Investigators become more cautious when reports describe: perfect uniformity with no accompanying debris; animals all of identical size and developmental stage; species that are unlikely to have shared the same source habitat; no plausible water source within transport range; weather conditions inconsistent with strong convective storms. [facebook.com] facebook.com into strong updrafts created by intense storms. When these upward... These circumstances do not automatically rule out ordinary explanations , but they require additional assumptions. A waterspout capable of lifting aquatic animals might also be expected to entrain vegetation, mud, aquatic insects or multiple fish species unless it intercepted an unusually concentrated group. The more selective the reported sample, the narrower the range of ordinary scenarios becomes. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Although waterspouts are the most commonly offered explanation The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... This is one reason researchers prefer well documented modern cases with preserved specimens, radar data and meteorological records over colourful historical anecdotes. Selectivity is a challenge, not a refutation Within Fortean discussions, single species falls are sometimes presented as evidence against conventional meteorological explanations altogether. Most scientists take a more cautious position. The waterspout hypothesis was never intended to explain every reported animal fall. It is offered as a plausible mechanism for some cases, particularly those involving small aquatic animals associated with severe weather. Even the Library of Congress presents waterspouts as the most commonly proposed explanation rather than an established solution to every report. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Although waterspouts are the most commonly offered explanation The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Consequently, species selectivity functions best as a critical test rather than a decisive argument. It asks investigators to examine whether the ecology of the reported animals matches what would be expected from a real source population. If the reported uniformity can be explained by schooling, breeding aggregations or habitat structure, the ordinary explanation remains viable. If it cannot, the report deserves closer scrutiny—but not necessarily a supernatural"},{"title":"Texarkana Fish","url":"/texarkana-fish/","description":"The Texarkana fish fall shows how a strange storm report can shift from waterspout folklore to a more specific bird-prey explanation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"Texarkana Fish Did Fish Really Rain on Texarkana? Texarkana Fish Texarkana Fish Did Fish Really Rain on Texarkana? The Texarkana fish fall shows how a strange storm report can shift from waterspout folklore to a more specific bird prey explanation. The Texarkana fish fall shows how a strange storm report can shift from waterspout folklore to a more specific bird prey explanation. event time window What witnesses found after the storm Why gizzard shad changed the explanation How birds can mimic animal rain forteana animal falls texarkana fish Introduction On 29 December 2021, residents of Texarkana reported one of the most widely documented modern cases of \"fish rain\". After a powerful thunderstorm passed through the city, dozens to hundreds of small fish were found scattered across roads, car parks, rooftops, truck beds and even an airport runway. The event quickly became international news and was initially explained in the familiar way: a waterspout had supposedly lifted fish from a nearby lake before dropping them over the city. However, a subsequent investigation proposed a far more specific—and arguably more evidence based—explanation. Rather than being carried by a rotating column of air, the fish were probably regurgitated by flocks of fish eating birds caught in the storm. The Texarkana case has therefore become an important example within Fortean studies because it illustrates how careful investigation can replace a traditional explanation with one that better fits the physical evidence. [https://www.wdbj7.com] wdbj7.com Source details in endnotes. Published: December 30, 2021 What witnesses found after the storm The thunderstorm struck Texarkana during the afternoon of 29 December 2021. Almost immediately afterwards, residents across several parts of the city began posting photographs of small silvery fish lying on pavements, roads, lawns and commercial premises. Workers at Texarkana Regional Airport reported collecting substantial quantities of fish from the runway, while other witnesses found fish in the beds of pickup trucks and on rooftops. These details proved especially significant. Fish on roads could potentially have been washed there by floodwater, but fish resting on elevated surfaces such as roofs and inside open vehicle beds strongly suggested that at least some had fallen from above rather than simply being carried across the ground. Security camera footage obtained from a local tyre business also appeared to capture fish striking the ground during the storm, providing unusually direct evidence that the animals were genuinely falling through the air rather than merely being discovered after rainfall. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... The affected area extended for several miles rather than being confined to the edge of a single pond or drainage channel. That distribution made a simple flooding explanation increasingly difficult to sustain, while still leaving open the question of what mechanism had actually delivered the fish. Fish rain from sky in Texarkana Channel: KTAL News Open on YouTube Why gizzard shad changed the explanation A crucial development came when biologists examined preserved specimens and identified them as juvenile gizzard shad ( Dorosoma cepedianum ), a common freshwater fish found throughout reservoirs and rivers in the region. Identification of the species did more than satisfy curiosity: it narrowed the list of realistic transport mechanisms. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... Investigators Sharon A. Hill and Paul Cropper noted several observations that pointed away from the traditional waterspout explanation. First, the fish reportedly showed signs consistent with having passed through a predator's digestive system rather than appearing as freshly netted fish. Second, eyewitnesses and airport personnel recalled large numbers of cormorants in the area around the time of the storm. Cormorants commonly feed on schools of gizzard shad and are known to regurgitate recently swallowed prey under some circumstances. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... The investigation also examined competing possibilities: Flooding: inconsistent with fish found on roofs and elevated surfaces. Aircraft accident or deliberate dumping: flight records and the distribution pattern did not support this. Waterspout: meteorological review reportedly found no evidence that a waterspout had formed during the storm. Birds: matched both the species involved and the known feeding behaviour of local cormorants. [Popular Science+2Sharon A. Hill] popsci.com fish falling from sky mystery Popular ScienceWe may know why fish rained from the sky over Texas6 Jul 2022 — Experts hypothesized that the storm whipped up a tornadic... Rather than treating \"fish rain\" as a single meteorological phenomenon, the investigation argued that this particular event had a biological explanation. How birds can mimic animal rain The proposed mechanism is surprisingly straightforward. Cormorants often feed communally, swallowing multiple small fish before flying to resting sites. Birds under sudden stress—including severe weather , rapid take off or disturbance—may disgorge stomach contents. Although the precise biological trigger is not fully understood, regurgitation is well documented in cormorants and several other fish eating birds. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... If a flock carrying recently swallowed shad encountered the advancing thunderstorm, numerous birds could potentially expel fish while airborne over the same general area. Because the fish would fall during heavy rain and hail, observers on the ground would naturally experience what appeared to be fish raining from the sky. This mechanism also explains several features that waterspouts struggle to account for: The fish belonged largely to a single prey species favoured by local fish eating birds. Some specimens reportedly showed evidence consistent with partial digestion. The fish were scattered across a broad urban area rather than deposited in a narrow trail expected from some storm transport scenarios. Independent reports confirmed cormorants were present locally during the event. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... The explanation does not require fish to survive violent atmospheric transport or remain suspended inside a vortex before being released many kilometres away. Fish falling from the sky during storm in Texas Channel: News 4 (WOAI) San Antonio Open on YouTube Texarkana Fish Rain or Fishnado Dec 2021 dropped waterfowl too Channel: Liv In Progress Open on YouTube Why the case matters for Fortean investigations The Texarkana event remains unusual because the observation itself is not seriously disputed: fish really did fall onto parts of the city during the storm. What changed was the proposed cause. Early news coverage repeated the familiar folklore that waterspouts regularly suck fish from lakes before depositing them inland, an explanation frequently attached to reports of animal rain. Later investigation argued that this default assumption did not fit either the local meteorology or the biological evidence from the recovered fish. Instead, the researchers concluded that bird regurgitation provided the best match for all available observations. https://www.wdbj7.com+2Sharon A. Hill [wdbj7.com] wdbj7.com Source details in endnotes. Published: December 30, 2021 Importantly, the bird hypothesis remains an inference rather than direct proof. No one observed cormorants expelling the fish over Texarkana, and no mechanism can be confirmed with absolute certainty after the fact. Nevertheless, many investigators regard it as a stronger explanation because it attempts to account simultaneously for the species involved, the weather conditions, witness reports, the physical condition of the fish and the absence of evidence for a waterspout. [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com HillTexarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill18 Jun 2022 — The fish that fell in a December thunderstorm likely came from the n... For Forteana more broadly, the Texarkana case demonstrates that extraordinary looking events do not always require extraordinary atmospheric mechanisms. Sometimes the mystery lies not in whether something fell from the sky—it clearly did—but in correctly identifying what was already flying overhead."},{"title":"Waterspouts","url":"/waterspouts/","description":"Waterspouts can plausibly lift small animals, but the mechanism works best under narrow weather, distance, and evidence conditions.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Animal Falls","level":3,"text":"Waterspouts Can Waterspouts Really Lift Fish? Waterspouts Waterspouts Can Waterspouts Really Lift Fish? Waterspouts can plausibly lift small animals, but the mechanism works best under narrow weather, distance, and evidence conditions. Waterspouts can plausibly lift small animals, but the mechanism works best under narrow weather, distance, and evidence conditions. mechanism Tornadic versus fair weather waterspouts What storm uplift can and cannot explain Evidence checks before accepting the claim forteana animal falls waterspouts Introduction Waterspouts are the leading ordinary explanation for many reports of fish or frog falls, but they are not a universal solution to every claim of animals apparently \"raining\" from the sky. The basic mechanism is physically plausible: a rotating column of air over water can lift small aquatic animals into a storm, transport them over a limited distance, and release them when the updraught weakens. However, this explanation only fits reports that match the right weather conditions, geography and physical evidence. Scientists therefore treat waterspouts as a genuine but limited mechanism rather than a catch all answer for every unusual animal deposit after rain. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Within Fortean studies, this distinction matters. Accepting that some animal falls have a natural meteorological cause does not mean every reported event shares that origin. The strength of the waterspout explanation depends on whether the evidence matches the known behaviour of severe storms. Waterspouts Channel: usoceangov Open on YouTube Tornadic versus fair weather waterspouts Not all waterspouts are equally capable of transporting animals. Meteorologists distinguish between two main types: Tornadic waterspouts develop from severe thunderstorms or tornadoes that move over water. They contain much stronger rotating updraughts and are the most plausible mechanism for lifting fish, frogs and other lightweight aquatic animals. Fair weather waterspouts form beneath developing cumulus clouds, usually in relatively calm conditions. Although visually dramatic, they are generally weaker, shorter lived and less likely to transport significant numbers of animals over long distances. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... This distinction is often lost in popular retellings . Simply observing a waterspout over a lake or sea does not automatically explain an animal fall. The event must involve a storm capable of sustaining enough upward airflow to entrain both spray and small organisms. The lifting process is also commonly misunderstood. Contrary to the old image of a funnel \"drinking\" water from below, the circulation develops within the atmosphere and extends downward. Most of the visible funnel consists of condensed water droplets rather than a solid column of seawater. Small objects become caught in the surrounding airflow rather than travelling inside a tube of liquid water. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Fish Raining from the Sky Channel: inFact with Brian Dunning Open on YouTube What storm uplift can and cannot explain The waterspout hypothesis works best under fairly specific conditions. It is most convincing when: the animals are small and lightweight; they normally live close to the water surface; the deposit occurs shortly after severe weather; there is a plausible nearby source such as a pond, river, lake or coastline; the animals belong largely to one species or similar size class. This selective pattern makes physical sense. A waterspout passing over shallow water is unlikely to collect every organism equally. Surface dwelling fish or recently metamorphosed frogs are much more easily entrained than heavy bottom dwelling species. The mechanism also explains why many reported falls involve surprisingly uniform collections of animals. Witnesses often report dozens or hundreds of similar sized fish rather than a random mixture of aquatic life. That consistency is compatible with lifting a local shoal or breeding aggregation rather than scooping up an entire ecosystem. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Equally important are the limits. Waterspouts are poor explanations for reports involving: large mammals or other heavy animals; species with no plausible nearby aquatic habitat; deposits occurring without any evidence of recent convective storms; isolated animals appearing over very large regions simultaneously. The stronger the claimed transport distance becomes, the more demanding the meteorological explanation also becomes. While storms can move animals some distance, extraordinary claims of extremely long range transport generally lack direct observational support. Most modern discussions therefore favour relatively local movement from nearby water sources rather than journeys of hundreds of kilometres. [HowStuffWorks] science.howstuffworks.com How Stuff Works Can it really rain frogs? Waterspouts can move across hundreds or thousands of miles, although that's extremely rare.Read more... Evidence checks before accepting the claim A convincing waterspout explanation depends on more than the existence of unusual weather. Investigators typically ask several practical questions. Was there suitable weather? Severe thunderstorms, radar signatures, tornado reports or documented waterspouts strengthen the case. Ordinary rainfall alone does not. Where is the nearest source population? The closer an appropriate pond, estuary, reservoir or coastline lies to the reported fall, the more plausible storm transport becomes. What species were involved? Accurate identification matters. Marine fish found inland suggest different possibilities from freshwater minnows found near rivers. Misidentification can easily distort later retellings. How were the animals distributed? A narrow, localised deposition path aligns better with storm release than a broad regional scatter. Were the animals alive? Some reported fish and frogs survive the event, which is consistent with relatively brief aerial transport. Others are injured or dead, making it harder to distinguish between storm transport, flooding or other mechanisms without additional evidence. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... These checks separate the observable facts from the proposed mechanism. Finding fish on the ground is evidence of a deposit. Concluding they arrived via a waterspout requires additional meteorological and biological support. Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Why waterspouts remain the leading explanation No scientist has routinely observed a waterspout lifting fish and later watched the same animals fall elsewhere in a fully documented chain of evidence. Nevertheless, the mechanism fits established atmospheric physics well enough that meteorologists regard it as the most plausible explanation for at least some verified animal fall reports. The combination of rotating updraughts, known transport of debris, and repeated historical associations with severe storms makes it considerably stronger than supernatural or unknown force explanations. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... At the same time, the explanation should not be overextended. Some alleged animal falls may instead reflect local flooding, animals emerging after heavy rain, birds dropping prey, or inaccurate reporting. Invoking a waterspout without matching weather evidence merely replaces one unsupported story with another. The strongest position is therefore a balanced one: waterspouts are a real atmospheric mechanism capable of producing genuine fish and frog falls under suitable conditions, but each reported event must be tested against the available meteorological, biological and geographical evidence rather than assumed to share the same origin. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Waterspouts Really Lift Fish?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains waterspouts and severe weather fundamentals. See on Amazon Book The AMS Weather Book By Jack Williams Covers weather phenomena including severe storms. See on"},{"title":"Anomalistics","url":"/anomalistics/","description":"Anomalistics offers a disciplined way to study extraordinary claims without deciding in advance.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Anomalistics What Is Anomalistics, and Why Does It Matter? Anomalistics Anomalistics What Is Anomalistics, and Why Does It Matter? Anomalistics offers a disciplined way to study extraordinary claims without deciding in advance. Anomalistics offers a disciplined way to study extraordinary claims without deciding in advance. policy intervention How it differs from Forteana Neutral inquiry into claims Where scientific evaluation helps forteana anomalistics Introduction Anomalistics is the disciplined study of claims that appear to fall outside ordinary explanation , without deciding at the start whether they are genuine discoveries, mistakes, hoaxes, folklore, psychological effects or misclassified natural events. Within Forteana , it matters because it turns “strange stories” into examinable claims: What exactly was reported? What evidence exists? Which explanations have been ruled out? What would count as a better test? The field’s value is not that it proves ghosts, cryptids, UFOs or other extraordinary phenomena. Its value is procedural: it offers a neutral way to handle anomalous reports before belief, ridicule or institutional embarrassment harden into a conclusion. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics takes unexplained claims seriously without committing itself to... That makes anomalistics a governance practice as much as an intellectual curiosity. It asks how researchers, public agencies, journalists and archives should treat claims that are uncertain but socially persistent. A neutral claim study does not give every claim equal credibility; it gives every claim a fair pathway through definition, documentation, alternative explanations and evidence standards . This is especially important in Forteana, where reports often cross boundaries between science, folklore, psychology , security, media and personal experience. [newdualism.org] newdualism.org Truzzi on Scientific UnorthodoxyMy general concern is to try to foster an interdisciplinary program, best called anomalistics, on the stu... How anomalistics differs from Forteana Forteana is the wider cultural field: a tradition of collecting and discussing odd reports that seem to resist accepted categories. Anomalistics is narrower and more method conscious. It formalises the question, “How should we study this anomaly?” rather than simply preserving the anomaly as a curiosity. The Society for Psychical Research’s Psi Encyclopedia describes anomalistics as taking unexplained claims seriously without committing to one outcome, and as treating anomalies as opportunities for disciplined inquiry rather than immediate belief or dismissal . [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics takes unexplained claims seriously without committing itself to... The distinction is easiest to see in the move from collection to evaluation. Charles Fort gathered “damned” facts to challenge the complacency of official explanation. Later anomalistics kept the Fortean interest in rejected or awkward data, but added a stronger demand for method: classification, source criticism, cross disciplinary expertise, replication where possible, and careful separation between the report itself and the interpretation placed upon it. Roger W. Wescott is widely credited with coining “anomalistics” in 1973, defining it as the serious and systematic study of phenomena that do not fit common sense or established scientific pictures of reality. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. This difference prevents two common errors. The first is credulity: treating an unexplained report as if it automatically supports the most dramatic hypothesis. The second is premature debunking: treating an odd claim as worthless because it sounds implausible. Anomalistics instead keeps the claim open long enough to ask what kind of claim it is. A light in the sky may be an aviation issue, an astronomical misidentification, a sensor artefact, a military secrecy problem, a witness memory problem, a hoax, or a genuinely unresolved observation. Neutral inquiry begins by refusing to collapse all those possibilities into one story. [newdualism.org] newdualism.org Truzzi on Scientific UnorthodoxyMy general concern is to try to foster an interdisciplinary program, best called anomalistics, on the stu... Neutral inquiry into claims Neutral claim study is not neutrality about truth. It is neutrality about procedure. The investigator does not promise that the extraordinary claim is likely, but also does not decide in advance that the witness, claimant or archive must be wrong. In practical terms, this means distinguishing four things that are often blurred together: the experience someone reports, the record of that report, the proposed explanation, and the wider belief system that grows around it. [Academia] academia.edu Open source on academia.edu. Marcello Truzzi, a sociologist closely associated with organised scepticism and anomalistics, argued for an interdisciplinary study of facts that seem unexplained by current models. His point was that an anomaly’s final home is not known at the start. A UFO case, for example, may end up belonging to astronomy, sociology, psychology, meteorology or military technology. That uncertainty is why anomalistics requires procedural humility: the first task is not to pick a side, but to build a case file that different kinds of expertise can examine. [newdualism.org] newdualism.org Truzzi on Scientific UnorthodoxyMy general concern is to try to foster an interdisciplinary program, best called anomalistics, on the stu... A neutral study normally asks several linked questions: What is the minimum claim? “A witness saw a moving light at 21:10” is different from “an alien craft flew overhead”. What is the evidence chain? First hand testimony, photographs, instrument logs, medical records, newspaper retellings and later folklore do not carry the same weight. What ordinary explanations fit? Misperception, coincidence, fraud, equipment error, environmental conditions and cultural expectation must be tested before exotic explanations. What remains unexplained after testing? A residue of uncertainty is not the same as proof of the paranormal. What new observation would change the assessment? A good anomalistic study defines what better evidence would look like. This approach is useful because many anomalous claims are socially real even when their proposed explanations are not. People do report exceptional experiences; communities do preserve legends; governments do receive UAP reports; scientists do encounter outliers in data. Anomalistics asks how to handle such material without either inflating it into revelation or discarding it as embarrassment. [American Psychological Association] apa.org Open source on apa.org. UAP Independent Study Event Post Meeting Media Teleconference (May 31, 2023) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 30.3K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 1 hour 2 minutes Open on YouTube Where scientific evaluation helps most Scientific evaluation helps anomalistics most when it improves the quality of the question. The strongest contribution is often not a spectacular answer, but better discrimination between cases that can be explained, cases that are too poorly documented to assess, and cases that deserve fresh data collection. NASA’s 2023 independent study on unidentified anomalous phenomena made this point directly: without extensive, well characterised data, many observations are nearly impossible to verify or explain, so the immediate need is better data acquisition rather than dramatic interpretation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Open source on nasa.gov. UAP research shows why this matters. Many historical cases are built from witness testimony, incomplete sensor records, military secrecy and retrospective interpretation. The US All domain Anomaly Resolution Office reported in 2024 that it had found no verifiable evidence that any UAP sighting represented extraterrestrial activity, and no evidence that the US government or private industry had access to extraterrestrial technology. That finding does not mean every sighting was neatly explained; it means the strongest official review did not support the most extraordinary popular claim. [U.S. Department of War] war.gov dod report discounts sightings of extraterrestrial technology dod report discounts sightings of extraterrestrial technology At the same time, scientific evaluation can make anomaly study more serious by moving it away from anecdote. The Galileo Project, for instance, has argued for instrumented, transparent and systematic observation of aerial phenomena, including wide field cameras, narrow field characterisation, passive radar methods, radio monitoring, microphones and environmental sensors. The important shift is"},{"title":"Evidence Chains","url":"/evidence-chains/","description":"Testimony, photos, sensor logs, medical records, and folklore retellings each change how much weight a case can carry.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"Evidence Chains How Strong Is the Case File? Evidence Chains Evidence Chains How Strong Is the Case File? Testimony, photos, sensor logs, medical records, and folklore retellings each change how much weight a case can carry. Testimony, photos, sensor logs, medical records, and folklore retellings each change how much weight a case can carry. dataset evidence Ranking first hand and second hand evidence What photos, logs, and records can and cannot prove How retellings turn reports into folklore forteana anomalistics evidence chains Introduction The strength of an anomalous case file rarely depends on a single dramatic photograph, witness, or document. Instead, it depends on an evidence chain : a sequence of records showing how information was created, preserved, compared, and independently confirmed. Within anomalistics, the central question is not simply whether a report is unusual, but whether the supporting evidence survives careful source criticism. A compelling story with poor documentation may carry less weight than a mundane report backed by multiple independent records. Conversely, an impressive looking photograph or medical record may establish that something happened without identifying what caused it. Evidence chains therefore help separate the report itself from the interpretations later attached to it. [dokumen.pub] dokumen.pub anomalous scientific evidence in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions. The existing paradigm is modified to subsume the new evidence... Many famous Fortean cases persist because they contain mixed forms of evidence rather than a single decisive proof. Eyewitness accounts, photographs, radar returns, medical examinations, newspaper archives and later retellings often reinforce one another in some respects while contradicting one another in others. Understanding how these different sources interact is more useful than asking whether any one item \"proves\" an extraordinary claim. How should evidence be ranked? Anomalistics generally evaluates evidence by considering independence, reliability, documentation and preservation , not by how extraordinary the claim appears. Different forms of evidence answer different questions. A practical ranking is: 1. Contemporaneous primary records. Notes, recordings, emergency calls, instrument logs or official documents created during or immediately after an event are usually the strongest starting point because they minimise later memory changes. 2. Independent eyewitness testimony. Multiple witnesses who cannot easily influence one another may strengthen confidence that an event occurred, although they may still disagree about details. 3. Physical traces. Samples, photographs with verified provenance, environmental measurements or preserved artefacts can sometimes be re examined by later investigators. 4. Institutional records. Medical files, weather observations, aviation logs, police reports and similar documents can establish timing, location or consequences even when they do not explain the underlying cause. 5. Later recollections. Interviews conducted months or years afterwards remain valuable but are vulnerable to memory reconstruction, media influence and repeated retelling. 6. Second hand and third hand accounts. Stories passed through intermediaries generally lose evidential weight unless the original source can be recovered. This hierarchy does not imply that lower ranked evidence is false. Rather, each additional layer between the event and the surviving record introduces more opportunities for misunderstanding, omission or embellishment. What can testimony establish? Eyewitness testimony forms the backbone of many Fortean case files because unusual events are often unexpected and leave little physical evidence. Testimony can reliably establish that someone experienced something unusual , but it is less reliable for identifying precisely what caused the experience . Several factors increase the value of witness evidence: statements recorded immediately after the event; multiple independent witnesses; agreement on core observations despite differences in peripheral details; witnesses with different backgrounds describing similar features; preservation of original interviews rather than edited summaries. The opposite conditions weaken a testimony chain. Long delays before interviewing witnesses, leading questions, exposure to media coverage and repeated retelling can all reshape memories. Psychological research has consistently shown that confidence and accuracy do not necessarily increase together, making confidence alone an unreliable indicator of truth. [dokumen.pub] dokumen.pub anomalous scientific evidence in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions. The existing paradigm is modified to subsume the new evidence... Importantly, inconsistent details do not automatically invalidate a case. Independent witnesses often remember different aspects of genuine events. Investigators instead examine whether the central observations remain stable across separate accounts. UFO Videos Explained: Mick West's Expert Analysis Channel: TWiT Tech Podcast Network · Views: 2.5K · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube What photos, logs and records can—and cannot—prove Physical documentation is often perceived as objective, yet each type of record has important limitations. Photographs and video Images can establish that something appeared in front of a camera at a particular time if their provenance is secure. They are generally less effective at establishing: distance; size; speed; identity of the object; whether unusual appearance resulted from perspective, exposure, reflections or optical artefacts. Without the original file, metadata or documented chain of custody, even authentic looking images become difficult to evaluate. Sensor logs Radar returns, infrared recordings, seismic instruments, radiation detectors or environmental sensors provide measurements rather than interpretations. Their value depends on: calibration; maintenance history; operating conditions; independent confirmation by other instruments. An unexplained sensor anomaly demonstrates that an instrument detected something outside expected parameters, not necessarily that an extraordinary phenomenon occurred. Instrument faults, environmental interference and processing errors must also be considered. Medical records Medical documentation can establish injuries, physiological changes or psychological symptoms following a reported event. However, medical records generally do not determine the origin of those findings. A documented burn, elevated stress response or unusual laboratory result confirms a patient's condition at examination but does not by itself identify whether the cause was accidental, environmental, intentional or anomalous. Official documents Police reports, military logs, aviation records and emergency communications often provide strong chronological anchors because they are created for operational purposes rather than later storytelling. Even so, they usually document observations and responses rather than final explanations. Why evidence chains matter more than isolated items The strongest anomalous case files usually contain converging evidence rather than a single spectacular exhibit. For example, an investigator might compare: witness statements; emergency dispatch records; weather observations; aircraft movements; astronomical conditions; photographs; hospital records; later interviews. Each source covers a different part of the event. Agreement across independently created records generally increases confidence that the documented observations reflect a real occurrence, even if the ultimate explanation remains uncertain. Equally important is identifying where evidence chains break. Missing originals, undocumented copies, uncertain dates or unclear ownership reduce confidence because later researchers cannot independently verify the material. Pentagon UFO files show no alien evidence, analyst says Channel: Reuters · Views: 21.3K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 1 minute 10 seconds Open on YouTube How retellings become folklore Many celebrated Fortean cases gradually shift from documented reports into folklore . This process often follows a recognisable pattern: an initial witness account; newspaper coverage emphasising unusual details; later books combining multiple versions; repeated retellings that remove uncertainty; internet summaries presenting disputed details as established facts. Each stage can subtly alter the narrative. Details omitted in the earliest reports may later become accepted as central features simply because they are repeated frequently. Over decades, separate incidents may even merge into a single legendary account. Witness dialogue becomes reconstructed, timelines simplify and ambiguous observations become increasingly definite. The resulting story may retain a historical core while differing substantially from the surviving"},{"title":"Better Sensors","url":"/better-sensors/","description":"Calibrated cameras, radar, microphones, and environmental sensors can replace rumor chains with records other people can check.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"Better Sensors Can Better Instruments Improve Weird Cases? Better Sensors Better Sensors Can Better Instruments Improve Weird Cases? Calibrated cameras, radar, microphones, and environmental sensors can replace rumor chains with records other people can check. Calibrated cameras, radar, microphones, and environmental sensors can replace rumor chains with records other people can check. policy intervention Why accidental sightings are hard to verify What multi sensor records add to a case Transparency, calibration, and independent review forteana anomalistics better sensors Introduction Can better instruments improve the study of strange or unexplained events? In many respects, yes—but not because they automatically prove extraordinary claims. Within anomalistics, the strongest argument for improved instrumentation is that calibrated, well documented measurements replace memory, rumour and speculation with evidence that other investigators can independently examine. Rather than asking witnesses to remember exactly what they saw or heard, researchers can compare synchronised records from cameras, microphones, radar, weather sensors and other instruments. When unusual events remain unexplained after such scrutiny, they become more valuable scientifically because the underlying observations are better documented rather than merely more dramatic. Modern sensor research also shows that combining multiple independent data streams generally produces more reliable anomaly detection than relying on any single instrument. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Smart Anomaly Detection in Sensor Systems: A Multi Perspective Review Smart Anomaly Detection in Sensor Systems: A Multi Perspective ReviewOctober 27, 2020... Published: October 27, 2020 This emphasis reflects the procedural goals of anomalistics rather than a commitment to any particular explanation. Better instrumentation cannot eliminate hoaxes , equipment faults or misinterpretations, but it can make those possibilities easier to test. Why accidental sightings are hard to verify Most famous Fortean reports share the same limitation: they were never designed to be observations. A witness unexpectedly encounters something unusual, reacts emotionally, and only afterwards attempts to reconstruct what happened. By then, crucial information is often unavailable. Typical missing information includes: Exact time and location with verified timestamps. Viewing direction and elevation. Weather conditions and atmospheric visibility. Camera settings, lens characteristics and image compression. Independent observations from nearby locations. Environmental measurements such as sound, electromagnetic conditions or aircraft traffic. Human perception introduces further uncertainty. Distance, size, speed and brightness are often estimated poorly during unexpected events, particularly at night or under stress. Even sincere witnesses can unintentionally reshape memories as they discuss an incident or consume media coverage afterwards. Because of these limitations, investigators frequently discover that potentially important questions simply cannot be answered after the fact. Instrumented observation attempts to reduce that problem by creating objective records before anyone knows whether an event will become significant. What multi sensor records add to a case The central idea behind instrumented observation is redundancy. Instead of trusting a single source of information, researchers compare several independent measurements that describe the same event from different perspectives. A useful observation system might include: High resolution visible light cameras. Low light or infrared cameras. Calibrated microphones recording ambient sound. Weather stations measuring wind, humidity and temperature. GPS based timing to synchronise all recordings. Where available, independent radar or air traffic information. Each sensor answers different questions. A video may show motion but reveal little about distance. Radar may estimate range and velocity but not appearance. Audio recordings can establish whether an object produced identifiable sounds. Weather data can determine whether atmospheric conditions were favourable for optical effects such as temperature inversions, reflections or unusual cloud formations. The greatest evidential value comes when independent sensors agree. If multiple calibrated systems record the same event while remaining physically independent, investigators can compare their measurements instead of relying on interpretation alone. Multi sensor anomaly detection has become a major research area in engineering precisely because combining heterogeneous sensor streams generally improves reliability and helps distinguish genuine events from isolated sensor errors. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Smart Anomaly Detection in Sensor Systems: A Multi Perspective Review Smart Anomaly Detection in Sensor Systems: A Multi Perspective ReviewOctober 27, 2020... Published: October 27, 2020 Equally important is disagreement. If one camera records an apparent anomaly while nearby instruments do not, investigators gain evidence that the effect may be caused by sensor limitations, reflections or equipment malfunction rather than an external phenomenon. Inside the AI Alien Hunting Project at Harvard Channel: Bloomberg Originals · Views: 542.8K · Uploaded: June 2025 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Why calibration matters as much as the sensors An expensive camera is not automatically a scientific instrument. Calibration means documenting how an instrument performs under known conditions so that its measurements can be interpreted correctly. Without calibration, unusual recordings may simply reflect unknown characteristics of the equipment. Important calibration practices include: Recording instrument specifications and firmware versions. Testing focus, distortion and field of view. Verifying accurate timestamps. Measuring sensor noise under normal conditions. Documenting maintenance and configuration changes. Retaining original, uncompressed data files whenever possible. These practices allow later investigators to distinguish between environmental events and artefacts introduced by the recording system itself. The same principle is well established in environmental monitoring. Acoustic monitoring, for example, increasingly combines calibrated microphones with automated event classification while carefully accounting for irrelevant sounds, sensor limitations and measurement uncertainty rather than assuming every detected event reflects the target phenomenon. [norsonic.com] norsonic.com Open source on norsonic.com. Transparency makes evidence stronger Instrumented observation is valuable only if others can examine the evidence. Good practice therefore extends beyond collecting data to preserving its provenance. Researchers increasingly recommend retaining: Original files rather than edited versions. Metadata showing capture time and device settings. Processing logs describing every alteration. Calibration records. Observer notes written immediately after the event. Public descriptions of analytical methods where practical. Transparency reduces both intentional fraud and unintentional confirmation bias. Other investigators can repeat image processing, verify timestamps or identify overlooked conventional explanations. This approach reflects a broader trend across many scientific fields, where reproducibility depends not merely on publishing conclusions but on preserving enough information for independent verification. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Independent review is more important than impressive equipment High quality instruments cannot eliminate observer bias if only one group analyses the results. Independent review allows specialists with different expertise to evaluate the same evidence. Depending on the case, this may involve: Optical imaging experts examining camera artefacts. Meteorologists assessing atmospheric conditions. Aviation specialists reviewing aircraft movements. Astronomers checking celestial objects. Audio engineers evaluating recorded sounds. Data analysts examining synchronisation and sensor performance. Different disciplines often identify ordinary explanations that fall outside the experience of anomaly investigators. Conversely, if multiple specialists independently conclude that a well recorded event remains unexplained, the case gains methodological significance even if no extraordinary explanation follows. The distinction is important. \"Unexplained\" means that current evidence has not produced a satisfactory conventional account—not that a particular extraordinary interpretation has been confirmed. Practical limits of better sensors Improved instrumentation does not guarantee decisive answers. Several limitations remain: Rare events may still occur outside instrument"},{"title":"Minimum Claims","url":"/minimum-claims/","description":"A strange report becomes easier to test when investigators separate the smallest reported event from the dramatic story attached to it.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"Minimum Claims What Was Actually Reported? Minimum Claims Minimum Claims What Was Actually Reported? A strange report becomes easier to test when investigators separate the smallest reported event from the dramatic story attached to it. A strange report becomes easier to test when investigators separate the smallest reported event from the dramatic story attached to it. mechanism Separating experience, record, and interpretation Turning dramatic claims into testable minimum claims Common mistakes when the explanation outruns the report forteana anomalistics minimum claims Introduction A central technique in anomalistics is reducing a strange story to its minimum claim : the smallest statement that can be independently checked before any explanation is considered. This approach prevents investigators from arguing over dramatic interpretations before establishing what, if anything, actually happened. Instead of asking whether a witness encountered a ghost, an extraterrestrial craft or an unknown creature, the first question becomes: what was actually reported? This distinction matters because eyewitness experience , documentary records and later interpretations often become blended over time. Marcello Truzzi's conception of anomalistics as a neutral \"friend of the court\" emphasises evaluating claims without assuming either belief or disbelief, making careful claim definition the starting point of investigation rather than its conclusion. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Marcello Truzzi cast anomalistics as a neutral 'friend of the court' in cont... Separating experience, record and interpretation The most useful way to analyse an unusual report is to divide it into three separate layers. The reported experience is what the witness believes they perceived. For example: \"I saw a bright object hovering silently for about thirty seconds.\" This is not yet evidence of any particular phenomenon. It is simply a description of an experience. The record is the evidence that the experience was reported. That might include: an interview a written statement a police report photographs audio or video radar data multiple independent witnesses Records vary greatly in quality. A contemporary written report generally carries different evidential weight from a story repeated decades later, even if both describe the same event. The interpretation is the explanation attached to the report: \"It was an alien spacecraft.\" \"It was a ghost.\" \"It was interdimensional.\" \"It was an unknown natural phenomenon.\" These interpretations may eventually prove correct or incorrect, but they are logically separate from the observation itself. Anomalistics deliberately postpones evaluating explanations until the observational claim has been defined as precisely as possible. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Marcello Truzzi cast anomalistics as a neutral 'friend of the court' in cont... Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Turning dramatic reports into minimum claims The minimum claim method works by repeatedly asking what can be established without assuming the explanation. Consider the statement: \"A ghost appeared in my bedroom.\" Broken into minimum claims, it becomes: 1. A person reported seeing something. 2. The event allegedly occurred in a bedroom. 3. The witness described a human shaped figure. 4. The witness experienced fear. 5. No physical evidence was recovered (or physical evidence was recovered, if applicable). Notice that none of these statements requires accepting that a ghost existed. They identify observations and documented facts rather than conclusions. The same technique applies across Fortean subjects. Dramatic claimMinimum testable claim\"A UFO landed.\"A witness reported an object descending to the ground at a particular location and time.\" Bigfoot crossed the road.\"A witness observed a large unidentified biped from a stated distance under stated lighting conditions.\"The house is haunted.\"Occupants independently reported hearing unexplained footsteps during specified periods.\"Time stopped.\"A witness believed an interval lasted much longer or shorter than indicated by clocks. Each version removes unnecessary assumptions while preserving the report itself. Why minimum claims improve verification Reducing reports to their smallest verifiable elements provides several practical advantages. First, different parts of a story often require different kinds of evidence. The time and location can be checked against weather records, astronomical data or aircraft movements, while descriptions of emotional reactions may rely entirely on witness testimony. Second, minimum claims allow partial confirmation. An investigation might verify that unusual lights were present in the sky while finding no evidence that they displayed impossible manoeuvres. Confirmation therefore becomes incremental rather than all or nothing. Third, investigators avoid creating false choices between complete belief and complete dismissal . A report can be accurate in some respects and mistaken in others. This approach also aligns with broader scientific reasoning. Rather than treating an extraordinary narrative as a single proposition, investigators examine each factual component individually before considering broader hypotheses. The evidential burden therefore attaches to each specific claim rather than to the story as a whole. [PMC+2Springer] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCExtraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence by PE Tressoldi · 2011 · Cited by 121 — “Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence” was a phrase made popular by Carl Sagan... Common mistakes when the explanation outruns the report Many famous anomalous cases become harder to evaluate because later retellings quietly expand the original report. Several recurring errors appear across Fortean literature. Treating labels as observations Statements such as \"the witness saw a ghost\" or \"the object was a UFO\" appear descriptive but actually introduce interpretation. A better record distinguishes between: \"the witness reported a translucent human figure\" \"the witness interpreted it as a ghost\" This preserves the observation without prejudging its cause. Chris French Weird Science: An Introduction to Anomalistic Psychology Channel: Goldsmiths Art Open on YouTube Expanding memories over time Retellings often become richer than original accounts. Later interviews may include additional dialogue, colours, movements or meanings that are absent from the earliest records. For this reason, investigators generally prioritise the earliest available documentation while noting how narratives evolve across subsequent retellings. Case study methods in anomalistics place considerable emphasis on reconstructing the chronology of reports rather than relying solely on mature versions of the story. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) N Equals 1: Single Case Studies in AnomalisticsJanuary 1, 2019 — 15 Aug 2022 — This volume is intended to give an overv... Published: January 1, 2019 Confusing absence of explanation with evidence for one explanation An unresolved observation does not automatically support the most extraordinary hypothesis. For example: \"No aircraft were identified\" does not demonstrate extraterrestrial origin. \"No conventional explanation has been found\" does not establish paranormal causation. \"Experts remain uncertain\" is not equivalent to confirmation. The minimum claim remains exactly what has been demonstrated and nothing more. Combining multiple uncertain claims Narratives sometimes merge several individually uncertain elements: an unusual light missing time electronic malfunction strange dreams later interpretations Each component should be evaluated independently before deciding whether they genuinely form a single event. A practical implementation workflow A neutral investigator can apply the minimum claim approach through a simple sequence. Christopher French: Introduction to Anomalistic Psychology Channel: Ratio BG Open on YouTube 1. Record the witness's account without interruption. 2. Separate observations from interpretations. 3. Identify each independent factual claim. 4. Determine which claims are objectively testable. 5. Seek independent records for each claim separately. 6. Evaluate explanations only after the underlying claims have been assessed. Importantly, the workflow does not assume that every claim will be resolved. Some observations remain unexplained because evidence is incomplete, not because they necessarily indicate an unknown phenomenon. Why this approach matters in anomalistics Minimum claims provide a common language for investigators with very different"},{"title":"Ordinary Causes","url":"/ordinary-causes/","description":"Misperception, coincidence, equipment error, fraud, and weather can explain many odd reports without accusing witnesses of bad faith.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"Ordinary Causes When Weird Reports Have Ordinary Causes Ordinary Causes Ordinary Causes When Weird Reports Have Ordinary Causes Misperception, coincidence, equipment error, fraud, and weather can explain many odd reports without accusing witnesses of bad faith. Misperception, coincidence, equipment error, fraud, and weather can explain many odd reports without accusing witnesses of bad faith. mechanism Why sincere witnesses can still be wrong Environmental and equipment explanations Fraud, coincidence, and cultural expectation forteana anomalistics ordinary causes Introduction Many reports of strange lights, mysterious figures or apparently impossible events turn out to have ordinary explanations without implying that the witnesses were dishonest, unintelligent or inventing stories. This is a central principle of anomalistics: investigate conventional possibilities thoroughly before treating an event as genuinely unexplained. The goal is not to dismiss unusual experiences, but to distinguish between the experience itself and the mechanism that produced it. In practice, many seemingly extraordinary sightings are resolved through a combination of human perception, environmental conditions, equipment limitations, coincidence or deliberate deception. This approach reduces error while preserving genuine uncertainty where evidence remains incomplete. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Why sincere witnesses can still be wrong One of the most persistent misconceptions is that if a witness is honest, the reported explanation must also be accurate. Anomalistics separates sincerity from accuracy. A person may accurately describe what they remember seeing while misidentifying its cause. Human perception is not a passive recording device. Vision depends heavily on context, expectation, lighting, distance and prior knowledge. Under poor viewing conditions the brain fills in missing information, often creating a coherent but incorrect interpretation. Memory then reconstructs the event each time it is recalled, meaning confidence can increase even when details become less reliable. Psychological research on anomalistic experiences has repeatedly shown that cognitive biases, subjective validation, expectation effects and the reconstructive nature of memory can produce convincing but mistaken accounts without any intent to deceive. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anomalistic psychology Anomalistic psychology Several common conditions increase the chance of misinterpretation: Viewing unfamiliar objects at night, where distance and size are difficult to judge. Brief observations during surprise or stress. Strong emotional involvement. Discussions with other witnesses after the event, which can unintentionally harmonise memories. Exposure to media stories that provide ready made interpretations. None of these factors imply that witnesses are unreliable in general. They simply reflect normal properties of human perception. Environmental and equipment explanations Many famous \"mystery\" sightings have later been traced to ordinary physical processes that initially looked extraordinary because observers lacked the necessary context. One of Geology’s Biggest Mysteries Channel: Astrum Earth · Views: 228.0K · Uploaded: December 2025 · Length: 33 minutes Open on YouTube Atmospheric effects The atmosphere can dramatically distort what people see. Temperature inversions, haze, ice crystals and unusual cloud layers may produce mirages, halos or distorted images of distant lights. Stars and planets close to the horizon often appear to flicker, change colour or seem to move because of atmospheric turbulence rather than any movement of the object itself. Rare natural phenomena can also resemble paranormal events. Ball lightning remains an unusual and incompletely understood atmospheric phenomenon, but its existence demonstrates that unfamiliar natural events need not imply supernatural causes. Similarly, reports of luminous phenomena during earthquakes include both genuine geophysical hypotheses and more mundane explanations such as electrical arcing from damaged power lines. The existence of one natural explanation does not mean every report shares the same cause, illustrating why investigators examine each case individually. [Wikipedia+2USGS] Wikipedia Ball lightning Ball lightning Astronomy mistaken for anomalies Bright planets, meteors, satellite flares and rocket launches have repeatedly generated reports of unidentified aerial phenomena. Venus, in particular, has historically been mistaken for hovering aircraft or unusual craft because it can remain bright near the horizon for long periods and appears to shimmer dramatically through turbulent air. Astronomical explanations become especially convincing when sightings match predictable celestial positions, allowing investigators to reconstruct what observers were likely seeing. Instrument and sensor limitations Modern sightings increasingly involve cameras, drones and electronic sensors rather than unaided observation . These technologies introduce their own sources of error. Common examples include: Lens flare from bright light sources. Motion blur caused by camera movement. Compression artefacts in digital video. Autofocus errors. Infrared reflections invisible to the naked eye. Radar returns produced by atmospheric conditions or signal processing. A sensor records physical signals rather than objective reality. Interpreting those signals requires understanding the instrument's limitations as well as its strengths. Unexplained Earthquake Light Phenomenon Finally Captured on Camera Channel: Anton Petrov Open on YouTube Fraud, coincidence and cultural expectation Although most investigators do not assume deception, deliberate fraud has always been part of the history of extraordinary claims. Hoaxes range from practical jokes and fabricated photographs to commercial schemes and attempts to attract publicity. Importantly, anomalistics treats fraud as only one possible explanation among many. Assuming every unusual report is fake is no more justified than assuming every report proves an extraordinary phenomenon. Researchers instead ask whether independent evidence supports the claim and whether ordinary mechanisms have been adequately excluded. Historical studies of disputes over paranormal claims show that scientific investigations have long distinguished between honest error, methodological mistakes and deliberate deception rather than collapsing them into a single category. [Scribd] scribd.com Peter Lamont Extraordinary Beliefs A Historical AP PDFeither as evidence of extraordinary phenomena, or else as examples of error... Coincidence also plays a larger role than intuition suggests. Humans naturally search for meaningful patterns, particularly during emotionally significant events. A strange light appearing on the same evening as an unusual dream or local rumour may seem connected despite having no causal relationship. This tendency towards pattern recognition is a normal cognitive process and often helps explain why unrelated events become linked into compelling narratives. Cultural expectations further shape interpretation. During different historical periods, similar experiences have been described as fairy encounters, religious visions, ghost sightings, airships, flying saucers or secret military aircraft. The underlying observation may remain broadly similar while the preferred explanation reflects contemporary beliefs and available cultural stories. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Why ordinary explanations are tested first Testing conventional explanations before extraordinary ones follows a practical logic rather than a philosophical commitment against unusual phenomena. Ordinary explanations generally: [scribd.com] scribd.com Peter Lamont Extraordinary Beliefs A Historical AP PDFeither as evidence of extraordinary phenomena, or else as examples of error... Have independently established mechanisms. Can often be tested against weather records, astronomical data or technical evidence. Require fewer unsupported assumptions. Frequently account for many reports simultaneously. This does not mean every case receives a conventional solution. Some reports remain unresolved because evidence is incomplete, contradictory or too limited to support any confident conclusion. In anomalistics, \"unexplained\" is not treated as evidence for the paranormal; it usually means there is insufficient information to distinguish between competing explanations. Marcello Truzzi argued that anomalistics should evaluate anomaly claims using conventional reasoning while remaining open to evidence rather than prematurely accepting or rejecting unusual claims. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The value of ordinary causes in neutral investigation Ordinary explanations perform"},{"title":"Psychology","url":"/psychology/","description":"Psychological explanations can take unusual experiences seriously while showing how memory, expectation, and meaning shape reports.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"Psychology Why Sincere Witnesses Can Be Mistaken Psychology Psychology Why Sincere Witnesses Can Be Mistaken Psychological explanations can take unusual experiences seriously while showing how memory, expectation, and meaning shape reports. Psychological explanations can take unusual experiences seriously while showing how memory, expectation, and meaning shape reports. population context Memory and expectation in unusual experiences Why explanation is not the same as ridicule How beliefs turn experiences into durable claims forteana anomalistics psychology Introduction Many people who report ghosts , apparitions , precognitive dreams, out of body experiences or other seemingly paranormal events are completely sincere. Anomalistic psychology begins from that premise. Rather than assuming that witnesses are dishonest or that their experiences prove paranormal forces, it asks how ordinary psychological processes can produce extraordinary experiences and why those experiences often feel utterly convincing. [Sydney Symposium] sydneysymposium.unsw.edu.au One of the central topics of anomalistic psychology is the unreliability of memory. After all, when assessing someone's claim...Read more... Within anomalistics, this approach is valuable because it separates the experience from its interpretation. A person may genuinely hear footsteps in an empty house, feel a presence while unable to move, or vividly remember a striking coincidence. The psychological question is not whether the witness is lying, but how perception, memory, expectation, emotion and cultural beliefs combine to shape what is experienced and later reported. This allows investigators to take witnesses seriously while remaining neutral about supernatural explanations. [Sydney Symposium] sydneysymposium.unsw.edu.au One of the central topics of anomalistic psychology is the unreliability of memory. After all, when assessing someone's claim...Read more... Memory and expectation in unusual experiences Human perception is not a passive recording device. The brain constantly predicts what is likely to happen and fills gaps using previous experience, beliefs and context. Most of the time this is highly adaptive, but in unusual or ambiguous situations it can create experiences that feel far more definite than the underlying sensory information warrants. This is especially important in reports of paranormal events because such experiences often occur under conditions where perception is already difficult: Darkness or poor visibility. Emotional stress or bereavement. Fatigue or disrupted sleep. Unexpected sounds or ambiguous visual stimuli. Situations where a person already expects something unusual. Laboratory studies illustrate how expectation changes interpretation. In one well known investigation of supposedly haunted locations, participants who were told that a building was haunted reported significantly more unusual sensations than those given a neutral explanation, despite exploring the same environment. Expectation did not invent every sensation, but it influenced how ordinary perceptions were interpreted. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anomalistic psychology Anomalistic psychology Memory adds another layer. Recalling an event is a reconstructive process rather than replaying a perfect recording. Each retelling may incorporate new assumptions, conversations or interpretations. Over time, a puzzling event can become a coherent supernatural narrative without deliberate deception. Anomalistic psychologists therefore distinguish between the original experience and the later memory of that experience. [Sydney Symposium] sydneysymposium.unsw.edu.au One of the central topics of anomalistic psychology is the unreliability of memory. After all, when assessing someone's claim...Read more... Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Why explanation is not the same as ridicule One of the central misunderstandings surrounding psychological explanations is the belief that they dismiss witnesses. In practice, anomalistic psychology argues almost the opposite. An experience can be psychologically understandable while remaining emotionally profound. Someone who senses the presence of a recently deceased relative during grief may have undergone a genuine subjective experience, regardless of whether an external entity was present. Explaining possible cognitive or neurological mechanisms does not imply that the comfort, fear or significance of the event was unreal. This distinction is particularly important in clinical settings. Mental health professionals generally assess how an experience affects a person's wellbeing rather than assuming that all unusual experiences indicate illness. Many anomalous experiences occur in psychologically healthy individuals and never develop into psychiatric disorders. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous Experiences, Trauma, and Symbolization by T Rabeyron · 2015 · Cited by 70 — Anomalous or exceptional experiences are uncommon experiences which are usually interpreted as be... For anomalistics, respectful investigation requires holding two ideas simultaneously: The witness may be completely honest. The witness's interpretation may nevertheless be mistaken. Keeping these possibilities separate reduces both credulity and unfair scepticism. Sleep, consciousness and vivid paranormal experiences Some of the most striking paranormal reports occur during transitions between sleeping and waking, when the brain can briefly combine elements of dreaming with conscious awareness. Sleep paralysis is a well studied example. During an episode, people may awaken while temporarily unable to move. Many report a powerful feeling that another presence is in the room, accompanied by vivid visual, auditory or tactile sensations. Across different cultures these experiences have often been interpreted as ghosts, demons, alien visitors or supernatural attacks. Modern sleep research explains many such episodes as temporary overlap between REM (rapid eye movement) sleep processes and wakefulness rather than evidence for external entities. [American Psychological Association+2Wikipedia] apa.org American Psychological AssociationSpeaking of Psychology: Ghosts, Ouija boards, and ESPFrench discusses how psychological explanations su... Systematic reviews have also found consistent associations between paranormal experiences or beliefs and several sleep related phenomena, including: Sleep paralysis. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Sleep paralysisSleep paralysis is a state, during waking up or falling asleep, in which a person is conscious but in a complete state... Hypnagogic hallucinations while falling asleep. Hypnopompic hallucinations while waking. Lucid dreaming. [pure.hud.ac.uk] pure.hud.ac.uk lucid dreaming nightmares and sleep paralysis associations with r Dreaming, Nightmares, and Sleep Paralysisby KG Drinkwater · 2020 · Cited by 33 — Focusing on lucid dreaming, this paper examined relation... Frequent nightmares. These associations do not prove that all paranormal reports originate during sleep. Instead, they identify one important pathway through which exceptionally vivid experiences can arise naturally. [PubMed+2PMC] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Associations between sleep variables and ostensibly...18 Apr 2023 — Overall, there were positive associations between many sleep v... How beliefs turn experiences into durable claims An unusual event rarely remains an isolated observation . People naturally search for explanations that fit their existing worldview. The same unexplained sound might therefore become: evidence of a ghost for one person; a sign of psychic ability for another; an unknown natural phenomenon for a third; simple coincidence for someone else. Psychologists refer to this as top down interpretation: prior beliefs influence how ambiguous information is understood. Once an explanation has been adopted, several well known cognitive tendencies can strengthen it. Confirmation bias encourages attention to experiences that support an existing belief while contradictory events receive less attention. Pattern perception allows meaningful connections to emerge from chance coincidences . Source monitoring errors can blur the distinction between something personally witnessed, imagined or later heard from others. These mechanisms are common features of everyday cognition rather than unique flaws found only among paranormal believers. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Paranormal beliefs and cognitive function: A systematic review...by CE Dean · 2022 · Cited by 60 — Cognitive biases explain religious... Importantly, anomalistic psychology does not claim that everyone who holds paranormal beliefs exhibits the same thinking style or reasoning ability. Reviews of the literature find numerous statistical associations between belief, cognitive biases and certain reasoning tendencies,"},{"title":"UAP Data Gaps","url":"/uap-data-gaps/","description":"Recent UAP reviews show that unresolved sightings are often evidence problems first, not proof of extraterrestrial technology.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Anomalistics","level":3,"text":"UAP Data Gaps Why Unidentified Does Not Mean Alien UAP Data Gaps UAP Data Gaps Why Unidentified Does Not Mean Alien Recent UAP reviews show that unresolved sightings are often evidence problems first, not proof of extraterrestrial technology. Recent UAP reviews show that unresolved sightings are often evidence problems first, not proof of extraterrestrial technology. dataset evidence Why poor data leaves sightings unresolved What official reviews can and cannot conclude How better records narrow uncertainty forteana anomalistics uap data gaps Introduction Unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) occupy an unusual position in modern anomalistics because they highlight a recurring problem in evidence rather than a proven mystery. Many reported sightings remain unidentified not because investigators have confirmed extraordinary technology, but because the available information is incomplete, ambiguous or technically inadequate. Recent reviews by government agencies and independent scientific panels have repeatedly reached the same broad conclusion: \"unidentified\" is a description of the current state of the evidence, not a conclusion about the object's nature. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... This distinction matters for public policy as much as for scientific inquiry. Military organisations must determine whether a report represents a flight safety issue or foreign technology, while scientists ask whether the available observations are sufficient for reliable analysis. Both approaches encounter the same obstacle: poor quality data often prevents firm conclusions in either direction. Why poor data leaves sightings unresolved The most common reason a UAP case remains unresolved is not that it displays impossible behaviour, but that investigators lack enough reliable information to identify it confidently. Several recurring problems appear across official reviews: Single source observations. A lone witness, camera or sensor provides few opportunities for independent verification. Incomplete sensor information. Without knowing how a radar, infrared camera or optical system was configured, analysts cannot fully evaluate what it recorded. Missing contextual data. Weather conditions, aircraft traffic, astronomical objects and environmental information may be absent or incomplete. Limited recording duration. Many sightings last only seconds, preventing analysts from reconstructing a complete flight path. Poor image quality. Compression artefacts, digital zoom, motion blur and low light conditions can produce misleading appearances. NASA's independent UAP study emphasised that current analysis is hindered by poor sensor calibration, limited metadata and the absence of multiple independent measurements. Those are technical shortcomings rather than evidence for extraordinary explanations. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... An important consequence follows: an event can remain unidentified simply because there is insufficient information to eliminate ordinary explanations with confidence. What official reviews can and cannot conclude Official investigations often receive public attention because they involve military reporting systems or intelligence agencies. However, their conclusions are narrower than many people assume. What reviews are designed to answer Government investigations primarily ask questions relevant to national security and aviation: Does the object threaten aircraft? Could it represent foreign surveillance or advanced technology? Is there evidence of sensor malfunction? Can the observation be matched to known aircraft, balloons, drones or natural phenomena? These questions differ from asking whether extraterrestrial visitors exist. The investigative standard is practical attribution rather than philosophical certainty. For example, the US Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has repeatedly stated that its mission is to resolve reports through evidence based analysis and determine whether they involve identifiable objects, foreign capabilities or genuine unknowns requiring further investigation. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO HomeRead UAP Records from NARA; UAP Reporting Trends; UAP Imagery; NEW INFORMATION PAPER & ANIMATION: Correlations of Satelli... REPLAY! NASA Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Update Channel: The Launch Pad · Views: 6.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube What official reviews cannot establish Official reviews also have important limits. They generally cannot conclude: that every reported sighting has been explained; that an unresolved case demonstrates non human technology; that the absence of evidence proves unusual phenomena never occur; that classified information automatically confirms extraordinary claims. The inability to identify an object is fundamentally different from demonstrating what it actually was. NASA made this distinction explicit in its 2023 independent report, noting that it found no evidence that reviewed UAP had an extraterrestrial origin while also recognising that many observations remain insufficiently documented for definitive scientific conclusions. [NASA] nasa.gov update nasa shares uap independent study report names director UPDATE: NASA Shares UAP Independent Study ReportSep 14, 2023 — The report contains the external study team's findings and recommendat... Why \"unidentified\" is a category, not an explanation One of the most persistent misunderstandings in public discussion is treating \"unidentified\" as though it describes a particular kind of object. In reality, it describes the current status of an investigation. A report may remain unidentified because: crucial evidence has been lost; witnesses disagree; instruments produced conflicting measurements; the event cannot be reconstructed; several ordinary explanations remain plausible. This is common across many investigative fields. Accident investigators, astronomers and forensic scientists frequently distinguish between cases that are unexplained because evidence is missing and cases that positively support an extraordinary hypothesis. From an anomalistics perspective, unresolved cases remain valuable precisely because they reveal the limits of available evidence rather than automatically expanding the range of accepted explanations. NASA UFO report finds no evidence UAP have extraterrestrial origins FULL Channel: Global News · Views: 37.7K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Lessons from recent AARO reporting Recent annual reports illustrate how data quality directly affects case resolution. The Fiscal Year 2024 consolidated report states that AARO received 757 reports during the reporting period. Many were successfully attributed to familiar causes such as balloons, birds, unmanned aerial systems and other ordinary objects after additional analysis became available. Others remained unresolved because investigators lacked timely, actionable sensor data rather than because they demonstrated confirmed anomalous technology. The report also notes a small subset of reports with unusual characteristics that warrant continued investigation, while stopping short of assigning extraordinary explanations. [U.S. Department of War] media.defense.gov FY24 CONSOLIDATED ANNUAL REPORT ON UAP 508 Department of WarFiscal Year 2024 Consolidated Annual Report on...14 Nov 2024 — AARO is also expanding engagement with foreign partners... This pattern demonstrates an important statistical point. Case resolution is not fixed at the moment of observation. Reports frequently move from \"unidentified\" to \"identified\" when new radar records, flight logs, imagery or witness information become available. The same reports therefore illustrate both institutional caution and the practical reality that uncertainty often reflects incomplete information rather than enduring mystery. How better records narrow uncertainty Both NASA and AARO argue that improving future evidence is more valuable than endlessly debating old, poorly documented cases. Key improvements include: calibrated optical and infrared sensors; accurate timestamps synchronised across systems; preserved raw sensor data rather than compressed imagery; multiple independent observing platforms; detailed environmental records; standardised reporting procedures; better sharing of data across agencies. NASA also highlighted opportunities to use artificial intelligence to identify known aircraft, atmospheric phenomena and sensor artefacts more consistently, allowing investigators to focus attention on genuinely difficult cases. Better civilian reporting standards and reduced stigma around reporting unusual observations"},{"title":"Doubt","url":"/doubt/","description":"Fortean thinking works best when belief and disbelief both stay provisional long enough to examine the case.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Doubt Why Forteana Works Best in Suspended Judgment Doubt Doubt Why Forteana Works Best in Suspended Judgment Fortean thinking works best when belief and disbelief both stay provisional long enough to examine the case. Fortean thinking works best when belief and disbelief both stay provisional long enough to examine the case. mechanism The danger of instant belief The danger of instant dismissal How to hold uncertainty honestly forteana doubt Introduction Forteana works best when belief and disbelief are both kept provisional. A Fortean case usually begins with a report that is strange enough to resist easy filing: a light in the sky, a creature seen at the edge of visibility, a fall of animals, a haunting, a coincidence that feels engineered. The weakest responses are instant belief, which turns mystery into doctrine, and instant dismissal , which turns scepticism into a reflex. Suspended judgment is the more useful middle practice: take the report seriously enough to investigate, but not so seriously that the preferred explanation outruns the evidence. That stance fits the tradition Charles Fort helped create. In The Book of the Damned , Fort described his subject as “excluded” data: observations and reports pushed outside accepted categories. His provocation was not simply that science was wrong and marvels were true; it was that systems of knowledge can decide too quickly what belongs and what does not. The modern Fortean task is to keep that irritation alive without mistaking every rejected claim for a hidden truth. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedTHE BOOK OF THE DAMNED. 1. A procession of the damned. By the damned, I mean the excluded. We shal... Why suspended judgment is the Fortean default Suspended judgment is not indecision for its own sake. It is a practical response to reports whose evidence is incomplete, mixed, degraded, second hand, emotionally charged or difficult to reproduce. In ordinary life, people often ask whether a strange claim is “true or false”. Forteana usually requires a prior question: what kind of claim is this, and what would count as a responsible level of confidence? Anomalistics, the organised study of anomalies, is useful here because it treats unusual reports as investigable without assuming that they must be paranormal. The Psi Encyclopedia describes anomalistics as multidisciplinary: an alleged UFO might require psychology, astronomy, physics, aircraft recognition, social history or several of these at once. It also notes that anomalistics is governed by scientific norms including scepticism, disinterestedness and openness to original data. That combination is close to the Fortean ideal: curiosity with brakes. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... The point is not to split the difference between believers and debunkers. Some claims are false. Some are probably true but misdescribed. Some are unresolved only because crucial data were never collected. Some may mark rare natural events, hoaxes, memory errors, folklore patterns or genuine gaps in present knowledge. Suspended judgment is the discipline of not letting any one of those possibilities become the conclusion before the case has earned it. A helpful Fortean question is therefore not “Do I believe this?” but “What position does the evidence justify today?” That position may be strong acceptance, strong rejection, tentative explanation, open uncertainty, or simple archival interest. The value lies in matching confidence to evidence. The danger of instant belief Instant belief is tempting because Fortean material often arrives with a strong human charge. A witness seems sincere. A photograph looks uncanny. A local tradition has lasted for decades. A scientific institution appears evasive. The story feels more vivid than the explanation. In those conditions, belief can feel like respect: respect for the witness, for the mystery, or for possibilities that official culture has neglected. The problem is that sincerity is not the same as accuracy. Anomalistic psychology, the branch of psychology that examines unusual experiences and paranormal beliefs through known psychological and physical factors, gives Fortean readers a useful caution. Chris French, associated with Goldsmiths and its Anomalistic Psychology Research Unit, is described by Goldsmiths as specialising in non paranormal explanations for ostensibly paranormal experiences. That framing matters because it does not require witnesses to be stupid, dishonest or attention seeking. It treats error as a normal feature of perception, memory and interpretation. [Goldsmiths, University of London] gold.ac.uk smiths, University of LondonChristopher FrenchChris is an expert in the area of anomalistic psychology, focusing upon non paranormal... Research on memory and paranormal belief supports that caution. A study using conjuring to examine memory distortion notes that paranormal belief and reports of anomalous experiences have been linked with susceptibility to false memories, suggesting that some strange reports may arise from ordinary memory processes rather than deliberate invention. This is especially important in Forteana, where many famous cases survive through retelling, interviews, newspaper summaries and retrospective accounts rather than through fresh, instrumented observation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Magic and memory: using conjuring to explore the effects of...by K Wilson · 2014 · Cited by 44 — A number of individual difference me... Instant belief also narrows the investigation. Once a haunting is treated as a ghost, the draught, floor vibration, sleep state, grief context, hoax possibility and local legend may receive less attention. Once a light in the sky is treated as an alien craft, Venus, aircraft, balloons, rockets, atmospheric effects and military activity become “debunking” rather than ordinary lines of inquiry. The believer may still gather evidence, but the case has already been sorted into a meaning. In Fortean terms, this is a loss. The richest cases are often interesting precisely because they sit at the junction of witness experience, cultural expectation, environmental detail and evidential ambiguity. Overbelief flattens that junction into a single answer. Putting Paranormal Claims to the Test Chris French Channel: Center for Inquiry Open on YouTube The danger of instant dismissal Instant dismissal is the mirror image failure. It protects the reader from gullibility, but it can also protect them from information. A strange report may be poorly evidenced, but that does not mean it has no value. It may preserve a rare weather event, a misidentified astronomical object, a social panic, a local rumour system, an institutional blind spot or a witness’s honest attempt to describe something unfamiliar. Fort’s anger was directed at exclusion that happens too confidently. His “damned” facts were not necessarily facts in the modern evidential sense; many were newspaper clippings, odd scientific notes and reports of uncertain quality. But his broader challenge remains useful: when an institution or expert community rejects a report, it is worth asking whether the rejection follows from careful analysis, lack of evidence, category prejudice, reputational anxiety or mere impatience. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedTHE BOOK OF THE DAMNED. 1. A procession of the damned. By the damned, I mean the excluded. We shal... Marcello Truzzi, a sociologist associated with scientific scepticism and anomalistics, is helpful here. He is widely linked to the phrase that extraordinary claims require extraordinary proof, but his view of scepticism was not simply “reject the weird”. In his essay on pseudo scepticism, Truzzi argued that the burden of proof falls on the claimant and that more extraordinary claims carry a heavier burden, while also warning against sceptics who make confident negative claims without meeting their own burden. [anomalist.com] anomalist.com Open source on anomalist.com. This distinction is central to good Fortean method. “Not proved” is not the same as “disproved”. “Probably misidentified” is not the same as “the witness lied”. “No reliable evidence for a paranormal explanation” is not the same as “nothing happened”. The best scepticism is not contempt; it is disciplined proportion. Project Blue Book , the United States Air Force’s UFO investigation programme, shows why this matters. The National Archives notes that the project’s records were declassified, that the project closed in 1969, and that its files are available for examination. A Project Blue Book document distinguishes identified reports from cases with insufficient data, defining insufficient data cases as those missing essential elements such as"},{"title":"Anomalistics","url":"/anomalistics-c705ed/","description":"Anomalistics gives strange claims a disciplined home by combining open investigation with ordinary scientific caution.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Anomalistics Can anomalies be studied without belief? Anomalistics Anomalistics Can anomalies be studied without belief? Anomalistics gives strange claims a disciplined home by combining open investigation with ordinary scientific caution. Anomalistics gives strange claims a disciplined home by combining open investigation with ordinary scientific caution. policy intervention What anomalistics studies Why multiple disciplines matter Where Fortean curiosity meets sceptical method forteana doubt anomalistics Introduction Anomalistics asks a simple but demanding question: can strange claims be investigated seriously without assuming they are either true or false? Within the Fortean tradition, the answer is yes. Rather than treating unusual reports as proof of hidden forces or dismissing them as nonsense, anomalistics creates a disciplined framework for examining anomalies with ordinary scientific standards. Curiosity is encouraged, but it is restrained by evidence, critical testing and a willingness to change conclusions when better explanations emerge. This approach makes anomalistics a practical expression of suspended judgment: it keeps unusual reports open to investigation while preventing fascination from becoming certainty. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... What anomalistics studies Anomalistics is the systematic investigation of reports, observations and events that appear to fall outside current expectations or accepted explanations. The defining feature is not that the subject is paranormal, but that it is anomalous—something that resists immediate classification. That distinction matters. A report of mysterious lights, an apparently haunted building, an unexpected atmospheric phenomenon or an unusual coincidence may all enter anomalistic study for the same reason: they require explanation, not endorsement. The field does not begin by assuming that conventional science is wrong, nor that witnesses are necessarily mistaken. Instead, it asks what combination of evidence, observation and analysis can account for what was reported. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... Marcello Truzzi, one of the figures most closely associated with anomalistics, argued that the discipline occupies a middle ground between unquestioning belief and reflexive debunking. He described scepticism as an investigative attitude rather than a predetermined conclusion, emphasising that claims deserve proportionate scrutiny rather than automatic rejection. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Marcello Truzzi Psi EncyclopediaMarcello Truzzi Psi Encyclopedia27 May 2026 — Known for distinguishing open minded scepticism from debunking, he edited... Published: May 2026 Why multiple disciplines matter One reason anomalistics differs from many discussions of paranormal claims is that it assumes no single discipline can explain every anomaly. A single case may require expertise from several fields: Astronomy to identify planets, meteors or rare atmospheric events mistaken for unidentified objects. Psychology to understand perception, expectation, memory and eyewitness testimony. Physics and engineering to evaluate measurements, photographs, recordings or instrument behaviour. Meteorology to assess unusual weather or optical effects. Folklore and history to understand how local traditions shape reports and interpretations. The Psi Encyclopedia notes that an alleged UFO, for example, may ultimately require psychology, astronomy, aviation knowledge and physics rather than belonging exclusively to any one subject. The anomaly itself determines which expertise is needed, not the other way around. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... This multidisciplinary approach also reduces the temptation to force every case into a favourite explanation. Instead of asking whether an event is \"really paranormal\", investigators ask which evidence each discipline can contribute and where genuine uncertainty remains. Chris French Weird Science: An Introduction to Anomalistic Psychology Channel: Goldsmiths Art Open on YouTube Where Fortean curiosity meets sceptical method The attraction of Forteana has always been its willingness to notice reports that conventional categories struggle to accommodate. Anomalistics preserves that curiosity while adding what might be called scientific brakes. Those brakes consist of familiar research practices rather than special rules for extraordinary subjects: collecting original witness accounts where possible; preserving physical evidence before interpretation; comparing competing explanations rather than defending one preferred theory; recognising uncertainty when evidence is incomplete; remaining willing to abandon an attractive explanation if better evidence appears. The Psi Encyclopedia characterises anomalistics as being guided by the ordinary norms of scientific inquiry, including organised scepticism , disinterestedness and openness to original data. In other words, the methods are conventional even when the subject matter is unusual. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... This balance explains why anomalistics often frustrates both committed believers and determined debunkers. It rarely provides immediate certainty. Instead, it attempts to match confidence to the available evidence. Why psychology is part of the investigation, not a dismissal One common misunderstanding is that introducing psychology automatically means dismissing witnesses. In practice, anomalistic psychology treats unusual experiences as genuine experiences while asking how they arose. Researchers such as Chris French have focused on explaining apparently paranormal experiences through established psychological and physical mechanisms where possible. This includes work on perception, memory, suggestion, sleep phenomena, expectation and cognitive biases. None of these explanations require assuming that witnesses are dishonest or irrational. Ordinary mental processes operating under unusual circumstances can produce vivid and sincere reports. [Goldsmiths, University of London] gold.ac.uk smiths, University of LondonChristopher FrenchChris is an expert in the area of anomalistic psychology, focusing upon non paranormal... This perspective changes the investigative question. Rather than asking whether someone \"made it up\", anomalistic psychology asks what combination of environmental conditions and human cognition produced the reported experience . That approach is especially valuable in Fortean investigations because many reports occur under poor observational conditions: darkness, surprise, emotional stress, ambiguous stimuli or long delays before recollection. Psychological analysis therefore complements, rather than replaces, physical investigation. Paranormal Activity: An Introduction to Anomalistic Psychology – Prof. Chris French Channel: AQNB Productions Open on YouTube What happens when no explanation is found? Anomalistics does not require every case to reach a definite conclusion. Some reports are eventually explained through ordinary causes that were initially overlooked. Others are exposed as frauds or misunderstandings. Some remain unresolved because key evidence was never collected, observations conflict or the event cannot be reproduced. A small remainder continue to resist straightforward explanation without necessarily demonstrating anything supernatural. This willingness to leave cases unresolved is one of the discipline's defining strengths. Instead of filling evidential gaps with speculation, anomalistics accepts that uncertainty is sometimes the most accurate conclusion. That position preserves the possibility of future investigation without inflating mystery into proof. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... An Introduction to Paranormal Psychology with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution Open on YouTube Why anomalistics matters within Forteana For Fortean readers, anomalistics offers a practical method rather than a belief system. Charles Fort encouraged attention to observations that established systems ignored or excluded, but anomalistics adds"},{"title":"Blue Book","url":"/blue-book-be7e27/","description":"Project Blue Book shows how some UFO reports remain unresolved because basic facts were never collected.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Blue Book When UFO cases are under described Blue Book Blue Book When UFO cases are under described Project Blue Book shows how some UFO reports remain unresolved because basic facts were never collected. Project Blue Book shows how some UFO reports remain unresolved because basic facts were never collected. dataset evidence What Project Blue Book tried to sort The difference between unidentified and insufficient How missing basics limit conclusions forteana doubt blue book Introduction Project Blue Book is often remembered for the number of UFO reports it labelled \"unidentified\", but one of its most important lessons is easier to overlook: many cases could never be meaningfully judged because they lacked basic information. That distinction matters for anyone interested in Forteana and suspended judgment. An unexplained case is not automatically a mysterious one, and an unresolved file does not necessarily point towards extraordinary phenomena. Sometimes investigators simply did not have enough reliable evidence to reach any conclusion at all. Project Blue Book's records therefore demonstrate a fundamental problem in anomaly research: the quality of any conclusion is limited by the quality of the information collected at the time. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... What Project Blue Book tried to sort Between 1952 and 1969, the United States Air Force used Project Blue Book to investigate reports of unidentified flying objects. Its official objectives were practical rather than speculative: determine whether reports represented a threat to national security and analyse them using available scientific methods. By the project's closure, investigators had processed 12,618 reports, the great majority of which were eventually identified as aircraft, balloons, astronomical objects, weather phenomena or other conventional causes. The Air Force stated that 701 cases remained officially unidentified. [Air Force] af.mil Air Force Unidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The decision to discontinue UFO investigations was based on an evaluation of... Less well known is the project's classification system. Blue Book did not simply divide reports into \"explained\" and \"unexplained\". Official guidance recognised three broad outcomes: Identified : enough evidence existed to assign a conventional explanation. Insufficient data : too little reliable information existed to evaluate the report properly. Unidentified : sufficient information had been collected, yet investigators could not confidently match the report to a known cause. [esd.whs.mil] esd.whs.mil Project Blue BookThe Air Force groups its evaluations of UFO reports under three general headings: (1) identified. (2) insufficient data... This distinction is central to evidence based thinking. A case only entered the \"unidentified\" category after investigators judged that the available information was adequate for analysis but still failed to support an ordinary explanation . UFO Interview, 1966 Channel: US National Archives · Views: 688.8K · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 4 minutes 31 seconds Open on YouTube The difference between unidentified and insufficient A common misunderstanding is that every unresolved Blue Book case represents a genuine mystery. The project itself treated matters differently. An \"insufficient data\" report might include only a brief statement such as someone seeing \"a bright object\" without giving the exact location, time, weather conditions, duration, direction of travel, angular size, or the identities of other witnesses. Without those details, investigators could not compare the sighting against astronomical events, aircraft movements, balloon launches or meteorological conditions. The report could not be confirmed or rejected because there was simply too little information. By contrast, an \"unidentified\" case was one in which investigators believed there was enough observational detail to attempt identification, yet no conventional explanation satisfactorily accounted for the evidence under the project's standards. Whether those standards were always applied consistently is debated, but the distinction itself was explicit in Blue Book documentation. [esd.whs.mil+2CIA] esd.whs.mil Project Blue BookThe Air Force groups its evaluations of UFO reports under three general headings: (1) identified. (2) insufficient data... This difference is important for Fortean reasoning because uncertainty has more than one cause. A report may remain unresolved because: the observation was genuinely unusual; the available evidence was incomplete; critical physical evidence was never collected; later verification became impossible because too much time had passed. These situations all produce uncertainty, but they do not carry the same evidential weight. How missing basics limit conclusions Blue Book repeatedly encountered cases where ordinary investigative questions had gone unanswered before reports reached analysts. Missing information commonly included: precise date and time; exact observing location; weather and visibility; direction, altitude and apparent movement; duration of the observation; independent witnesses; photographs or radar confirmation; prompt interviews while memories remained fresh. Without these basics, even experienced investigators could rarely distinguish between aircraft, planets, meteors, atmospheric effects or genuinely anomalous observations. Former Blue Book director Captain Edward J. Ruppelt described many such reports as ending up stamped \"Insufficient Data for Evaluation\" before effectively disappearing into administrative files. His account illustrates a practical rather than philosophical limitation: investigators cannot solve cases when the raw observations are incomplete. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Open source on gutenberg.org. The problem is not unique to UFO reports. It appears throughout anomalistic research whenever witnesses report an event after memories have faded, locations cannot be reconstructed, or physical traces were never documented. Project Blue Book Channel: Daily Dose Documentary · Views: 397 · Uploaded: October 2021 · Length: 3 minutes 36 seconds Open on YouTube A statistical lesson from Blue Book The distinction between inadequate and adequate evidence became especially clear during the preparation of Project Blue Book Special Report No. 14, produced with assistance from the Battelle Memorial Institute. Rather than treating every report equally, analysts first separated cases into those with sufficient information and those lacking enough evidence for evaluation. Only after this filtering were reports classified as known or unknown. According to the report, around 9% of the analysed cases fell into the insufficient information category, while approximately 22% were categorised as unknown after investigators judged that enough information existed for meaningful analysis. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book The report also introduced an important nuance often lost in popular discussion: case quality mattered. Better documented reports were not automatically easier to explain. Some statistically higher quality reports remained unidentified even after extensive review. That finding has generated decades of debate about interpretation, but it does not eliminate the separate category of reports that simply lacked enough evidence from the outset. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Project Blue Book: America's Obsession with UFOs Channel: Origins OSU · Views: 1.6K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why this matters for suspended judgment Within Forteana , Project Blue Book is valuable not because it proves extraordinary claims, nor because it dismisses them, but because it demonstrates how evidence should be separated from speculation. Official files can preserve genuine uncertainty without implying extraordinary conclusions. A report classified as \"insufficient data\" is not hidden proof of anything; it is an acknowledgement that investigators lacked the information needed to reach a reliable judgement. Likewise, an \"unidentified\" classification records the limits of that particular investigation, not confirmation of extraterrestrial craft or any other specific explanation. [Air Force] af.mil Air Force Unidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The decision to discontinue UFO investigations was based on an evaluation of... This is precisely where suspended judgment becomes useful. Rather than asking whether every unexplained report is true or false, the more productive question is whether the available evidence supports any conclusion at all. Blue Book's files repeatedly show that, in many cases, the most"},{"title":"Experience","url":"/experience/","description":"A person may genuinely see or hear something strange while the paranormal explanation remains unproven.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Experience Did something happen, or was it paranormal? Experience Experience Did something happen, or was it paranormal? A person may genuinely see or hear something strange while the paranormal explanation remains unproven. A person may genuinely see or hear something strange while the paranormal explanation remains unproven. mechanism Reported experience versus proposed cause Ordinary explanations that preserve the event How residual uncertainty should be worded forteana doubt experience Introduction One of the most useful habits in Fortean thinking is to separate what someone experienced from what they concluded caused it . A person may genuinely see an unexplained light, hear footsteps in an empty house, or experience an intensely real presence. None of those reports automatically establish ghosts, extraterrestrial craft or other paranormal causes. Equally, questioning the explanation does not require dismissing the experience as dishonest or imaginary. The distinction allows unusual events to be investigated without forcing a premature choice between unquestioning belief and blanket scepticism. It also reflects a long tradition in anomalistics and psychology , where researchers distinguish between the observation itself and the interpretation attached to it. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous Experiences, Trauma, and Symbolization by T Rabeyron · 2015 · Cited by 71 — Anomalous or exceptional experiences are uncommon experiences which are usually interpreted as be... Within Forteana , this distinction is especially important because haunting reports and UFO sightings often begin with sincere eyewitness testimony but end with competing explanations. Treating those two stages separately preserves respect for witnesses while keeping conclusions proportionate to the available evidence. Reported experience versus proposed cause A witness normally reports a chain of events rather than a finished explanation. Consider two common examples: \"I saw a bright object moving silently across the sky.\" \"I heard footsteps upstairs and saw a dark figure in the hallway.\" Those are descriptions of experience. Statements such as \"it was an alien spacecraft\" or \"the house is haunted\" belong to a different category. They are explanatory hypotheses rather than direct observations. Researchers studying subjective paranormal experiences have repeatedly noted that people often combine these two levels into a single account. This can make later investigation difficult because memory of the original sensory experience becomes intertwined with later interpretation, discussion and cultural expectations. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Paranormal Experience, Belief in the...October 31, 2017 — this study investigated the prevalence of subjective paranormal ex... Published: October 31, 2017 This distinction matters because several explanations can often fit the same experience. A silent light in the sky might ultimately prove to have been an astronomical object, military aircraft, atmospheric phenomenon or something genuinely unidentified. Likewise, an apparent apparition might involve perceptual factors, environmental influences, an unusual psychological state, deliberate deception, or—if ordinary explanations fail—an unresolved anomaly. The experience is one question; its cause is another. Why witnesses can be accurate about the experience but mistaken about the cause Separating experience from interpretation does not imply that witnesses are unreliable. Human perception is generally effective, but it evolved to construct meaningful interpretations from incomplete information rather than to function as a perfect recording device. In both haunting and UFO reports , witnesses usually describe events that occur under conditions known to complicate perception: darkness or twilight unexpected or emotionally charged situations long viewing distances unfamiliar sounds brief observation times fragmented memories reconstructed afterwards Psychological research therefore distinguishes between the authenticity of an experience and the accuracy of the explanation assigned to it. An intensely vivid experience may still be misunderstood without implying deception or mental illness. [Sydney Symposium+2American Psychological Association] sydneysymposium.unsw.edu.au Sydney SymposiumThe Psychology of Paranormal Beliefsby CC French · Cited by 4 — anomalistic psychology attempts to explain paranormal and... This approach also avoids a false dilemma. A witness need not be either \"completely correct\" or \"completely mistaken\". Someone may accurately report seeing an unusual aerial object while being wrong about its origin. Equally, a person may genuinely experience an overwhelming sense of presence without establishing that a supernatural entity was objectively present. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Ordinary explanations that preserve the event A common misunderstanding is that proposing an ordinary explanation means claiming that \"nothing happened\". In careful anomalistic work, the opposite is usually true. Something did happen: the task is to identify what. Hauntings Many reported hauntings contain experiences that are psychologically or environmentally well documented: hypnagogic or hypnopompic experiences during transitions between sleeping and waking sleep paralysis accompanied by a sensed presence ambiguous sounds interpreted within an emotionally charged setting expectation effects created by being told that a location is haunted environmental conditions such as lighting, acoustics or other physical features that encourage unusual perceptions One well known experiment found that people informed a building was haunted reported significantly more unusual experiences than those told it was simply undergoing renovation, illustrating how expectation can shape interpretation without requiring deliberate imagination. Other investigations of allegedly haunted locations found that reports clustered in areas with distinctive environmental characteristics, suggesting that physical surroundings may influence perception while still leaving the experience subjectively genuine. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Anomalistic psychology Anomalistic psychology Importantly, these explanations do not require inventing experiences that never occurred. They attempt to explain why real perceptions were interpreted as paranormal. UFO reports The same principle applies to UFO cases. Many famous sightings initially involved genuinely unusual observations: unfamiliar aircraft astronomical objects viewed under unusual atmospheric conditions satellites balloons optical effects rare meteorological phenomena In these cases the witness may accurately describe what they saw while misidentifying its nature. The existence of many solved UFO reports does not imply that every report is solved. It simply demonstrates why separating observation from explanation is methodologically valuable. Modern discussions often use the term \"unidentified\" precisely because it avoids assuming either conventional or extraordinary causes before sufficient evidence exists. An Introduction to Paranormal Psychology with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution Open on YouTube How culture shapes interpretation Experiences do not occur in a cultural vacuum. People interpret ambiguous events using concepts already available within their society. Across history, similar experiences have sometimes been explained as: spirits fairies religious apparitions witches extraterrestrials interdimensional beings unknown natural phenomena The underlying experience may share certain features while the explanatory framework changes over time. This observation has interested both psychologists and historians of anomalous experience because it suggests that interpretation is influenced by available cultural narratives as well as by sensory information itself. The changing language of explanation does not automatically invalidate any particular case, but it cautions against assuming that the first interpretation is necessarily the correct one. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous Experiences, Trauma, and Symbolization by T Rabeyron · 2015 · Cited by 71 — Anomalous or exceptional experiences are uncommon experiences which are usually interpreted as be... How residual uncertainty should be worded Careful Fortean writing benefits from distinguishing different levels of confidence. Instead of writing: \"The witness encountered a ghost.\" A more accurate description is: \"The witness reported seeing a figure that they interpreted as a ghost.\" Likewise, rather than stating: \"An alien craft landed.\" It is more precise to write: \"Witnesses reported observing an unidentified object, later interpreted by some as extraterrestrial.\" This style preserves three important distinctions: The observation : what was perceived. The"},{"title":"Memory Drift","url":"/memory-drift-4a0032/","description":"Many famous anomalies survive through interviews and retellings, where memory can shift without anyone intending to deceive.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Memory Drift How retellings reshape strange cases Memory Drift Memory Drift How retellings reshape strange cases Many famous anomalies survive through interviews and retellings, where memory can shift without anyone intending to deceive. Many famous anomalies survive through interviews and retellings, where memory can shift without anyone intending to deceive. mechanism Why memory changes after unusual events How interviews and newspapers alter cases What fresh records can preserve forteana doubt memory drift Introduction Many famous Fortean cases survive not because they were documented immediately, but because they were retold months, years or even decades later. That makes human memory one of the most important—and least appreciated—parts of the evidence. Modern psychological research shows that memory is not a recording stored unchanged in the mind. Instead, each act of remembering reconstructs an event, making it vulnerable to later conversations, interviews, newspaper accounts and personal reflection. This does not mean witnesses are dishonest. On the contrary, sincere people can confidently recall details that were added, altered or strengthened long after the original experience . [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 268 — Research that specifically examines eyewi... For readers interested in Forteana , this matters because many celebrated anomalies depend almost entirely on recollections collected well after the event. Understanding how memory drifts helps explain why strange cases often grow more detailed over time without requiring fraud or deliberate embellishment. It also provides a practical framework for suspended judgment: treating witness testimony as valuable evidence while recognising that memory itself is part of the phenomenon that must be examined. Why memory changes after unusual events An unusual experience is often emotionally powerful, surprising and difficult to interpret. Those qualities make it memorable, but they do not guarantee accurate recall. Research in cognitive psychology has consistently found that remembering is a reconstructive process. Each recollection draws on fragments of the original experience together with later knowledge, expectations and interpretation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive and neural mechanisms underlying false memoriesby AG Lentoor · 2023 · Cited by 13 — Memory distortions due to misinformation... For Fortean reports, several mechanisms are especially relevant: Reconstruction rather than replay. Memories are rebuilt whenever they are recalled, not replayed like a recording. Source monitoring errors. People may accurately remember a detail but misremember where it came from, confusing something they witnessed with something later read in a newspaper or heard from another witness. The misinformation effect. Later information can become incorporated into genuine memories, especially when presented by trusted or authoritative sources. Schema driven interpretation. People naturally reshape memories to fit familiar cultural narratives or expectations, making odd events appear more coherent over time. [PMC+2BPS PsychHub] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monitoring errors (Lindsay, 1990; Lindsay...R... These processes are ordinary features of memory rather than signs of pathology. They affect eyewitnesses in everyday settings and become even more significant when recollections concern confusing, unexpected or emotionally charged experiences. One implication is particularly important for anomalous reports: confidence is not a reliable measure of accuracy. Studies of eyewitness memory repeatedly show only weak relationships between how certain people feel and whether their recollections are objectively correct. Confidence itself can increase after repeated questioning or positive feedback without improving accuracy. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 268 — Research that specifically examines eyewi... How interviews and newspapers alter cases Many classic paranormal narratives pass through several stages before reaching the public: an initial experience, conversations with family or friends, interviews by investigators, newspaper coverage, books and documentaries. Each stage introduces opportunities for memory to shift. An Introduction to Paranormal Psychology with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution Open on YouTube Repeated interviews Interviewing is necessary for documenting unusual experiences, but the style of questioning matters greatly. Suggestive questions can introduce details that witnesses later remember as part of the original event. Experimental work by Elizabeth Loftus and colleagues demonstrated that even subtle wording changes can influence later recollection. Questions implying the existence of an object or emphasising dramatic action increase the likelihood that participants later report details they never actually observed. These findings have been replicated across decades of research into eyewitness testimony. [BPS PsychHub+2PMC] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub The history of an idea: The misinformation effect BPS PsychHubThe history of an idea: The misinformation effect Loftus24 Dec 2025 — There it is defined as the notion that a person's rec... For paranormal investigations, this means that questions such as \"What colour was the glowing figure?\" differ fundamentally from \"Did you notice anything unusual?\" The first assumes a figure existed; the second leaves the witness free to describe the experience without embedded suggestions. Newspaper influence Media coverage can become part of a witness's later memory. Once an account is published, witnesses often read interpretations that include additional descriptions, speculative explanations or details supplied by others. Psychologists describe this as post event information. Rather than replacing the original memory entirely, later information may blend with authentic recollection until the witness no longer distinguishes between direct experience and subsequent reporting. [BPS PsychHub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub The history of an idea: The misinformation effect BPS PsychHubThe history of an idea: The misinformation effect Loftus24 Dec 2025 — There it is defined as the notion that a person's rec... This process helps explain why older retellings of famous Fortean cases sometimes become richer than the earliest surviving reports. New descriptive elements may enter gradually through repeated exposure rather than conscious invention. Conversations between witnesses Witnesses rarely remain isolated after extraordinary events. They compare memories, discuss confusing details and attempt to construct a shared narrative. Research on memory conformity shows that such discussions can cause witnesses' recollections to converge, even when some of the shared details were initially remembered by only one participant. The resulting account may feel more convincing because multiple people agree, while actually reflecting mutual influence rather than independent confirmation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Misinformation effect Misinformation effect Why strange experiences are especially vulnerable Paranormal reports possess characteristics that make memory distortion particularly plausible without implying that the original experience was imaginary. First, anomalous events often unfold rapidly under poor viewing conditions or during moments of surprise. Witnesses may immediately begin interpreting what they saw because they lack an obvious explanation. Second, uncertainty encourages later reconstruction. When people remember incomplete experiences, they naturally fill gaps using prior knowledge and expectations. Someone familiar with UFO stories may interpret an ambiguous light differently from someone whose background is aviation or astronomy. Third, extraordinary experiences are often retold repeatedly. Every retelling provides another opportunity for reconstruction. Details may become more coherent, emotionally satisfying or culturally recognisable while drifting away from the earliest recollection. This gradual evolution differs from deliberate hoaxing. A witness can sincerely believe an increasingly elaborate narrative because each reconstruction feels subjectively authentic. [PMC+2American Psychological Association] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cognitive and neural mechanisms underlying false memoriesby AG Lentoor · 2023 · Cited by 13 — Memory distortions due to misinformation... The misinformation effect Elizabeth Loftus Nobel Prize Summit 2023 Channel: Nobel Prize Open on YouTube What fresh records can preserve Because memory changes over time, investigators generally place greater evidential weight"},{"title":"Proof Burden","url":"/proof-burden/","description":"Truzzi's approach separates fair scepticism from confident dismissal by asking who has proved what.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Proof Burden What extraordinary proof really means Proof Burden Proof Burden What extraordinary proof really means Truzzi's approach separates fair scepticism from confident dismissal by asking who has proved what. Truzzi's approach separates fair scepticism from confident dismissal by asking who has proved what. policy intervention Why claimants carry the first burden When sceptics also need evidence How to state doubt without overclaiming forteana doubt proof burden Introduction Marcello Truzzi's approach to extraordinary claims is often reduced to the familiar phrase \"extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence\". That summary misses his more important point. Truzzi argued that the key issue is not simply how much evidence an unusual claim needs, but who is responsible for providing it and when that responsibility shifts. Within Fortean inquiry, this provides a practical rule for handling anomalous reports: claimants bear the initial burden of showing why a remarkable claim should be accepted, while sceptics who go beyond withholding belief and begin making positive counter claims also incur evidential responsibilities of their own. This framework aims to preserve genuine scepticism without allowing either belief or dismissal to outrun the available evidence. [anomalist.com] anomalist.com Commentaries: On Pseudo SkepticismIn science, the burden of proof falls upon the claimant; and the more extraordinary a claim, the heavie... Why claimants carry the first burden Truzzi accepted a basic scientific principle: anyone proposing a new factual claim must provide evidence sufficient to justify it. The more the claim conflicts with well established knowledge or accumulated experience , the stronger that evidence must be. This is what he meant by \"extraordinary proof\". It did not imply a mysterious category of evidence available only for paranormal subjects. Rather, claims that would require revising accepted knowledge naturally demand more convincing support than claims already consistent with existing evidence. A report that a familiar bird was seen in a local park requires relatively little evidence; a report that a long extinct animal is alive, or that psychic perception has been demonstrated under controlled conditions, requires substantially more because it challenges a much larger body of accepted observations. [anomalist.com+2Wikipedia] anomalist.com Commentaries: On Pseudo SkepticismIn science, the burden of proof falls upon the claimant; and the more extraordinary a claim, the heavie... For Fortean subjects, this means unusual testimony should neither be dismissed automatically nor accepted merely because it is sincere. The initial task is to ask whether the evidence presented is proportionate to the claim being made. What to do when “Research Shows” shuts you down: A guide for parents and teachers (Ep 70) Channel: Chalk & Talk with Anna Stokke Open on YouTube When sceptics also need evidence Truzzi's distinctive contribution was insisting that the burden of proof is not permanently attached to believers alone. He argued that a genuine sceptic occupies an agnostic position: the evidence has not yet established the claim. Simply declining to accept a proposition requires no proof beyond showing that the available evidence is insufficient. However, the situation changes once a sceptic moves from saying \"this has not been demonstrated\" to asserting \"this definitely did not happen\" or \"the witness was mistaken\", \"it was a hoax\", or \"it must have been psychological\". Those become positive explanatory claims that also require evidence. Truzzi criticised what he called \"pseudoscepticism\" because, in his view, some investigators demanded rigorous proof from claimants while allowing speculative conventional explanations to pass with much weaker support. If someone confidently attributes a UFO sighting to Venus, a cryptid report to deliberate fraud, or a haunting to suggestion, those explanations should themselves be supported by evidence rather than assumed simply because they are familiar. [anomalist.com+2Wikipedia] anomalist.com Commentaries: On Pseudo SkepticismIn science, the burden of proof falls upon the claimant; and the more extraordinary a claim, the heavie... This does not place paranormal and conventional explanations on identical footing. Conventional explanations often begin with a higher prior plausibility because they are consistent with existing knowledge. Nevertheless, plausibility alone is not proof. What extraordinary proof really means One of the most common misunderstandings is that \"extraordinary evidence\" refers to a special kind of evidence. Truzzi's formulation is better understood as a proportional standard. The strength of evidence should match the consequences of accepting the claim. A proposition requiring extensive revision of established understanding should be supported by correspondingly strong, independent and reproducible evidence. In practice, stronger proof usually involves several features working together: Independent witnesses or observations. Reliable documentation collected close to the event. Alternative explanations being carefully investigated. Replication where replication is possible. Transparency about methods and uncertainties. The standard therefore concerns cumulative evidential weight rather than dramatic or spectacular proof. Philosophers discussing the principle have similarly argued that \"extraordinary\" refers to the amount of evidential support needed to overcome a large body of contrary observations, not to a fundamentally different category of evidence. [Springer] link.springer.com The crux of the matter...Read more... The Amazing Randi and American Skepticism with Mitch Horowitz (4K Reboot) Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube How to state doubt without overclaiming Truzzi's framework encourages careful language because certainty can exceed what the evidence justifies on either side. Useful distinctions include: \"The evidence is insufficient.\" This is a sceptical judgement about the present state of knowledge. \"The claim is false.\" This is a stronger factual assertion requiring supporting evidence. \"A conventional explanation is possible.\" This identifies a plausible hypothesis. \"A conventional explanation has been demonstrated.\" This requires evidence that the proposed explanation actually fits the case. For Fortean investigations, these distinctions matter because many historical reports remain incomplete. Missing photographs, degraded records, contradictory witness accounts or absent physical evidence may prevent either confirmation or refutation. Suspended judgement is therefore not indecision but recognition that confidence should reflect evidential quality rather than preference. Why Truzzi's policy remains influential Truzzi's formulation continues to shape debates over UFO reports , cryptids , paranormal experiences and other anomalous claims because it offers a governance principle for disagreement rather than a verdict about any specific phenomenon. Believers are reminded that sincerity and possibility do not establish truth. Sceptics are reminded that disbelief is strongest when it distinguishes between \"not demonstrated\" and \"demonstrably false\". Both sides are encouraged to match confidence to evidence rather than identity or expectation. Within Forteana , this approach supports investigation without credulity and criticism without dogmatic dismissal. It treats the burden of proof as a dynamic responsibility that follows whichever participant is making the substantive factual claim at that stage of the discussion. [anomalist.com+2Wikipedia] anomalist.com Commentaries: On Pseudo SkepticismIn science, the burden of proof falls upon the claimant; and the more extraordinary a claim, the heavie... David Drew: Debunking Theatre Thunderbolts Channel: The Thunderbolts Project Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What extraordinary proof really means. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Why People Believe Weird Things By Michael Shermer Addresses evidence, skepticism and extraordinary assertions. See on Amazon Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Covers evidence standards for extraordinary claims. See on Amazon Book Calling Bullshit By Carl T. Bergstrom, Jevin D. West Reinforces careful assessment of evidence. See on Amazon Book Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds By Charles Mackay Rating: 4.0/5 from 5 Google Books ratings Historic perspective on extraordinary collective beliefs. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Why People Believe Weird Things The Demon Haunted World Calling Bullshit As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland"},{"title":"Witnesses","url":"/witnesses-4a6958/","description":"A sincere witness can describe a real experience while still misreading its cause, details, or meaning.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Doubt","level":3,"text":"Witnesses Can honest witnesses still be wrong? Witnesses Witnesses Can honest witnesses still be wrong? A sincere witness can describe a real experience while still misreading its cause, details, or meaning. A sincere witness can describe a real experience while still misreading its cause, details, or meaning. population context Why sincerity is not proof Perception, memory, and interpretation Respectful ways to test testimony forteana doubt witnesses Introduction Can honest witnesses still be wrong? Yes. Within Forteana , this is one of the most important distinctions to understand. A person may sincerely report an unusual experience , accurately describe many of its features, and still be mistaken about what caused it or how it should be interpreted. Recognising that difference allows investigators to respect witnesses without treating testimony as conclusive proof of extraordinary events. This distinction sits at the heart of suspended judgment. Strange reports often begin with people who appear credible, distressed or genuinely puzzled. Their sincerity deserves careful attention because it tells us something important: they believe they experienced something unusual. What it does not automatically establish is the objective nature of the event. Modern psychology , legal research on eyewitness evidence and contemporary guidance for investigating unidentified anomalous phenomena all point towards the same conclusion: witness testimony is valuable evidence, but it is only one part of a responsible investigation. [PMC+2NASA Science] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov New Insights on Expert Opinion About Eyewitness Memory...by TM Seale Carlisle · 2024 · Cited by 31 — An eyewitness's perception and m... Why sincerity is not proof One of the most common mistakes in discussions of Fortean cases is confusing honesty with accuracy. These are different questions: Is the witness deliberately lying? Did the witness genuinely perceive something unusual? Is the witness's explanation the correct one? Someone can answer \"yes\" to the first two questions while the third remains uncertain. This distinction matters because perception is not a camera recording reality. Human observers actively interpret what they see using previous knowledge, expectations, lighting conditions, distance, movement and emotional state. Memory then reconstructs the experience rather than replaying it exactly. Even highly intelligent, experienced or professional observers remain subject to these normal cognitive processes. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov New Insights on Expert Opinion About Eyewitness Memory...by TM Seale Carlisle · 2024 · Cited by 31 — An eyewitness's perception and m... For Fortean investigators, this means a witness should neither be dismissed because the claim sounds strange nor believed simply because they appear convincing. Credibility concerns the witness; accuracy concerns the observation . Psychology Eyewitness Testimony Channel: Cognito · Views: 2.6K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube How perception shapes strange experiences Many reports begin with an authentic sensory experience that later acquires an uncertain interpretation. Someone might accurately report: an unusually bright object in the sky an unexpected sound an oddly shaped silhouette missing time as personally experienced an emotional feeling of fear or awe Those observations may all be genuine. The uncertainty usually enters when identifying their cause. At night, the human visual system has relatively poor information about distance and size. Without familiar reference points, bright lights can appear to change speed, hover or accelerate dramatically when they are actually ordinary aircraft, astronomical objects or atmospheric effects. Likewise, unusual weather , reflections or optical illusions may produce experiences that feel extraordinary even when no exotic phenomenon is present. None of this requires dishonesty; it reflects normal limits of perception. [The NESS] theness.com The NESSSightings: UFOs and Visual Perception The NESSby MB Fineman — Hynek's (1972) repeated emphasis on the reliability of UFO witnes... Forteana benefits from recognising this because it preserves potentially valuable observations without demanding premature conclusions. The Bad Science of Eyewitnesses Channel: Vsauce2 Open on YouTube Memory is reconstructed, not replayed Psychological research has repeatedly shown that memory changes over time. Rather than storing a perfect recording, the brain reconstructs past experiences each time they are recalled. During that reconstruction, later conversations, media reports, photographs and repeated questioning can gradually become mixed with the original experience. Researchers refer to this as post event information or memory contamination. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov New Insights on Expert Opinion About Eyewitness Memory...by TM Seale Carlisle · 2024 · Cited by 31 — An eyewitness's perception and m... This has several important consequences for strange reports. Immediate accounts are usually more valuable. Early descriptions often preserve details that later become simplified or unintentionally altered. Repeated retelling can increase confidence without increasing accuracy. A witness may become increasingly certain of details that were originally uncertain. Later information may merge into memory. Reading newspaper coverage, discussing the event with other witnesses or viewing online reconstructions can subtly reshape recollection. Importantly, these changes are usually unconscious. Witnesses are not inventing stories; they are remembering events through the normal workings of human memory. Confidence is not the same as accuracy People often judge testimony by confidence. Someone who speaks calmly, remembers vivid details and appears emotionally sincere naturally seems persuasive. Yet decades of eyewitness research have found that confidence alone is not a reliable indicator of factual accuracy, particularly after memories have been influenced by repeated discussion or suggestion. Experts broadly agree that witness confidence can be shaped by later experiences rather than simply reflecting the quality of the original observation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov New Insights on Expert Opinion About Eyewitness Memory...by TM Seale Carlisle · 2024 · Cited by 31 — An eyewitness's perception and m... This finding helps explain why disagreements over famous Fortean cases often persist. Believers may argue that witnesses remained convinced for decades. Sceptics may reply that conviction does not establish correctness. Both statements can be true simultaneously. A witness can remain completely sincere throughout life while still being mistaken about what happened. NASA holds news brief on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) Independent Study Report Channel: Yahoo Finance · Views: 8.3K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Respectful ways to test testimony A good investigation does not treat witnesses as either heroes or fools. Instead, it separates the person from the claim. Useful practice includes: recording accounts as soon as possible after the event encouraging witnesses to describe observations before offering explanations documenting weather, lighting, location and timing comparing independent accounts before witnesses influence one another checking photographs, radar records, astronomical conditions and other external evidence clearly distinguishing observed facts from later interpretation This approach mirrors recommendations made in modern UAP research, which emphasise collecting calibrated measurements and preserving high quality observational data rather than relying solely on personal recollection. NASA's independent study concluded that the greatest obstacle to understanding many unexplained reports is not necessarily witness reliability but the lack of complete, standardised supporting data. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... Why this distinction matters in Forteana Charles Fort encouraged readers to pay attention to reports that mainstream thinking might overlook. That does not require treating every witness as infallible. In fact, the strongest Fortean approach benefits from maintaining two ideas simultaneously: witnesses deserve respect because unusual experiences are often sincerely reported; extraordinary interpretations require evidence beyond sincere testimony alone. Holding those ideas together avoids two common errors. One is dismissing people because their experiences sound implausible. The other is assuming that honesty automatically confirms an extraordinary explanation. Suspended judgment therefore becomes an investigative discipline rather than a compromise. It allows testimony to remain valuable evidence without asking it to carry more weight than it can reasonably"},{"title":"Damned Book","url":"/damned-book/","description":"Fort's 1919 book made rejected reports its subject and shaped the style of later anomaly writing.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Damned Book What Made The Book of the Damned Different? Damned Book Damned Book What Made The Book of the Damned Different? Fort's 1919 book made rejected reports its subject and shaped the style of later anomaly writing. Fort's 1919 book made rejected reports its subject and shaped the style of later anomaly writing. dataset evidence The meaning of damned facts The range of strange reports Its influence on later Forteana forteana damned book Introduction The Book of the Damned , published in 1919, is the blueprint for Forteana because it turns rejected reports into the subject rather than the embarrassment. Charles Fort did not simply gather curiosities; he built a method and a style around what he called “damned” data: observations, newspaper items and scientific notices that appeared to have been excluded because they did not fit accepted categories. The result was not a normal book of paranormal claims, nor a scientific treatise, nor a hoax catalogue. It was an argument about evidence, authority and the awkward residue left behind when institutions decide what counts as real. [Wikisource] en.wikisource.org Chapter 1 The Book of the Damned/Chapter 126 Jul 2024 — A PROCESSION of the damned. By the damned, I mean the excluded. We shall have a p... That is why the book still matters within Forteana. Later anomaly writing inherited Fort’s central move: collect the marginal case, keep it in view, compare it with other marginal cases, and resist the urge to settle too quickly into either belief or dismissal . Its lasting influence lies less in proving fish falls, strange lights or disappearing people than in creating a recognisable Fortean posture: sceptical of orthodoxy, suspicious of tidy explanation, fond of pattern, and willing to use absurdity as a tool of criticism. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... Why “Damned Facts” Changed the Subject Fort’s opening move was simple and disruptive. “By the damned, I mean the excluded,” he wrote at the start of The Book of the Damned . The phrase matters because it shifts attention from the strangeness of individual reports to the process by which some reports become respectable evidence and others become intellectual refuse. His “procession” is not only a parade of oddities; it is a challenge to the gatekeeping habits of modern knowledge. [Wikisource] en.wikisource.org Chapter 1 The Book of the Damned/Chapter 126 Jul 2024 — A PROCESSION of the damned. By the damned, I mean the excluded. We shall have a p... In ordinary scientific writing, anomalies often appear as temporary problems to be solved, corrected or discarded. Fort made them the centre of the page. He was interested in the reports that seemed to have been pushed aside: not necessarily because they were true, but because their rejection could reveal the assumptions of the people doing the rejecting. A fall of frogs, a luminous object in the sky or an impossible looking archaeological find becomes, in Fort’s hands, a test case in intellectual housekeeping. Who filed it away? On what grounds? Was the explanation stronger than the report, or merely more acceptable? This is the first Fortean blueprint: anomaly as evidence of classification pressure. Fort did not need every case to be true in a literal sense for the book to work. The accumulation itself was the argument. If enough reports kept appearing at the margins of newspapers , journals and local testimony, then the border between “fact” and “nonsense” looked less clean than confident experts liked to imagine. Joshua Blu Buhs, writing on Fort’s 1919 book, describes this as part of Fort’s larger quarrel with science’s tendency to cut reality into pieces, accepting some as truth and casting others out as false. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... Fort’s own philosophical label for this uncertainty was “intermediatism”. He resisted final categories: real and unreal, fact and error, science and superstition. In the Fortean tradition that followed, this became a characteristic stance. A report could be taken seriously without being believed absolutely. An explanation could be plausible without being treated as complete. The “damned fact” was valuable because it forced a pause before closure. What Made the Book’s Evidence Feel Different? The evidence in The Book of the Damned feels unlike ordinary case collection because Fort arranged reports as pressure rather than proof. He was not building one clean demonstration. He was creating mass: files, snippets, fragments, repetitions, parallels and inconvenient survivals. The reader is not led through a tidy courtroom brief; the reader is made to feel the weight of neglected material passing by. Fort drew heavily on published sources, including newspapers, magazines and scientific journals. That choice gave the book its peculiar authority and its enduring problem. On the one hand, he was not merely inventing folklore from nothing. He was exhuming items that had appeared in public print, sometimes in technical or semi technical contexts. On the other hand, he often treated those sources as raw material for provocation rather than as evidence to be checked with modern standards of attribution, context and error correction. The book’s strength and weakness come from the same habit: it preserves the excluded report before it establishes what really happened. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Open source on skepticalinquirer.org. That is why later Fortean readers often treat The Book of the Damned less as a verified database than as an archive of leads. Some cases may point to misreported weather, folklore, astronomical events, hoaxes, translation errors or misunderstood natural phenomena. Others may remain genuinely puzzling because the original documentation is thin or scattered. Fort’s innovation was not modern verification; it was the insistence that discarded material could be gathered and made intellectually active. The book also differs from many earlier wonder books because it attacks explanation itself. Fort repeatedly mocks explanations that seem to be offered too quickly, especially when they explain a strange report by forcing it into an accepted framework. His target is not simply “science” as knowledge, but dogmatism in scientific culture: the habit of acting as if prevailing theories have already decided what evidence is allowed to mean. Later critics have fairly noted that this can make Fort destructive as well as illuminating, because he often punctures explanations more confidently than he supplies reliable alternatives. [cantab.net] cantab.net Chapter 1. Charles Fort and the ForteansFort called his precious items of data “The Damned” or “The Excluded”, because they were damned b... The result is a style that became central to Forteana: scepticism aimed in two directions. Fort asks readers to be sceptical of extraordinary claims, but also sceptical of ordinary dismissals. The Fortean question is not “Which mystery proves the impossible?” It is “What happens to evidence when the available categories are too narrow, too fashionable or too self protective?” The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube The Range of Strange Reports The book’s most memorable feature is its range. Fort moves across falls from the sky, unusual aerial lights, strange substances , apparent disappearances, odd archaeological suggestions, poltergeist like events and reports of creatures or beings that conventional knowledge did not comfortably admit. Project Gutenberg’s summary of the book lists anomalous sky sightings, falls of fish and frogs, poltergeist activity, giants, fairies and unexplained disappearances among its topics. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... That breadth became a Fortean signature. Most specialised traditions narrow their field: ghosts belong to psychical research, strange animals to cryptozoology, sky lights to UFO history, anomalous falls to meteorology or folklore. Fort’s book instead makes them neighbours. The connection is not that all these events share one cause. The connection is that they all expose a similar problem: reported experience exceeds the categories available to describe it. Several clusters show how the blueprint works: Falls from the sky: Fort was especially drawn to reports of fish, frogs, stones, blood like rain and other unlikely substances falling from above. These cases let him attack the assumption that all such reports must be either fraud, error or"},{"title":"Bad Answers","url":"/bad-answers/","description":"Fort made premature explanation look suspicious, but his own attacks could be sharper than his alternatives.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Bad Answers When Explanations Became Fort's Real Target Bad Answers Bad Answers When Explanations Became Fort's Real Target Fort made premature explanation look suspicious, but his own attacks could be sharper than his alternatives. Fort made premature explanation look suspicious, but his own attacks could be sharper than his alternatives. mechanism Mockery of tidy explanations The risk of destructive scepticism Why Forteana kept the double challenge forteana damned book bad answers Introduction One of the most influential ideas in The Book of the Damned is not a particular mystery but a habit of mind. Charles Fort repeatedly argued that bad explanations can be more damaging than unanswered questions. Instead of rushing to explain unusual reports, he invited readers to examine whether the explanation itself had been accepted because it was genuinely convincing or merely because it restored intellectual order. That scepticism towards premature certainty became a defining feature of Forteana . At the same time, it exposed Fort's own method to an important criticism: attacking weak explanations is not the same as establishing that an anomaly is genuine. His legacy therefore rests on a productive tension between exposing overconfidence and avoiding the trap of replacing it with endless doubt. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedThe Project Gutenberg EBook of The Book of the Damned, by Charles Fort. This eBook is for the use... When Explanations Became Fort's Real Target Fort often appeared to be writing about mysterious falls, strange lights or impossible artefacts, but many of his sharpest attacks were aimed elsewhere. His real target was the habit of explaining awkward evidence away before investigating it properly. In The Book of the Damned , he repeatedly assembled examples in which scientific authorities appeared to settle on familiar causes with striking speed. Rather than asking readers to accept extraordinary alternatives, he asked whether the conventional answer had actually earned its confidence. His famous opening declaration—\"By the damned, I mean the excluded\"—frames the book as an examination of how evidence is filtered, not simply what evidence exists. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedThe Project Gutenberg EBook of The Book of the Damned, by Charles Fort. This eBook is for the use... This style worked through accumulation rather than demonstration. Fort juxtaposed dozens of reports from newspapers and scientific journals, encouraging readers to notice recurring patterns in official behaviour: anomalies were dismissed as errors before detailed investigation; inconsistent reports were ignored once a convenient explanation appeared; institutional consensus could become stronger than the evidence supporting it. The result is less a catalogue of paranormal claims than a sustained critique of intellectual closure. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Mockery of tidy explanations Fort rarely answered an explanation with another carefully argued theory. Instead, he often responded with irony, exaggeration and parody. His invented ideas—such as the \" Super Sargasso Sea \", an imaginary region where lost objects accumulated before falling back to Earth—were probably not intended as literal scientific proposals. Rather, they demonstrated how easy it is to invent an apparently coherent explanation that fits selected facts. Fort himself later remarked that he believed nothing he had written, reinforcing the impression that many of his speculative theories were deliberately provocative rather than doctrinal. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort This rhetorical strategy created an uncomfortable symmetry. If an official explanation could be accepted with insufficient evidence, then an outrageous alternative could sometimes seem no less justified. Fort used absurdity to expose what he saw as unwarranted confidence. The Risk of Destructive Scepticism Fort's method was powerful because it questioned authority without demanding immediate belief in extraordinary claims. Yet critics have long argued that it also carried significant risks. The central problem is that exposing flaws in one explanation does not automatically strengthen another. A newspaper account may be unreliable. A witness may be mistaken. Records may be incomplete. Showing that an accepted explanation is weak leaves several possibilities open, including the possibility that the original report is also defective. This criticism has often been directed at Fort's argumentative style: he concentrated on identifying weaknesses rather than weighing competing explanations systematically; he frequently treated absence of certainty as evidence that orthodox accounts were inadequate; he sometimes allowed rhetorical force to substitute for careful evaluation of individual cases. Because of this, later commentators have described him as a brilliant critic of intellectual complacency but an unreliable guide to what actually happened in many of his favourite mysteries. Martin Gardner famously characterised Fort as a largely destructive critic who dismantled existing explanations without constructing persuasive alternatives, while other historians have argued that satire, rather than literal theorising, was his real objective. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned The danger is familiar well beyond Forteana. If every explanation is treated with equal suspicion, evidence can lose its ability to discriminate between better and worse accounts. Healthy scepticism can slide into permanent suspension of judgement, where all interpretations appear equally uncertain regardless of their evidential support. Why Forteana Kept the Double Challenge Fort's lasting influence comes from preserving two questions at once. The first asks whether accepted explanations genuinely fit the evidence. The second asks whether extraordinary claims deserve equally rigorous examination. Later Fortean writers often embraced both principles together, arguing that anomalies should neither be dismissed reflexively nor accepted enthusiastically. This \"double challenge\" distinguishes Forteana from straightforward belief in the paranormal. The ideal Fortean position is not simply to defend mysterious reports but to maintain pressure on every explanation, conventional or unconventional, until it has earned confidence through evidence. That approach has shaped later discussions of unexplained phenomena in several ways: unusual reports are treated as prompts for investigation rather than immediate proof; institutional consensus is questioned without assuming conspiracy or suppression; speculative explanations are recognised as hypotheses, not conclusions; uncertainty itself is accepted as a legitimate temporary outcome. Historians of Fort have noted that his deepest contribution lay less in particular mysteries than in his refusal to allow certainty to arrive too quickly. His method encouraged readers to examine how knowledge is organised, how anomalies are classified and how confidence is justified. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — It was an incongruous start to what would be... THE BOOK OF THE DAMNED Part 2 of 2 FULL AudioBook Greatest AudioBooks Channel: Greatest AudioBooks Open on YouTube A Lasting Lesson Fort's anti explanation style remains influential because it identified a genuine weakness in human reasoning: the temptation to prefer tidy answers over messy evidence. His satire exposed how institutions can become attached to explanatory comfort, even when important questions remain unresolved. Its limitation is equally clear. Criticising an explanation is only the beginning of inquiry, not its end. The most durable legacy of The Book of the Damned is therefore not blanket distrust of science or automatic sympathy for extraordinary claims. It is the insistence that both dismissal and belief should meet the same standard: explanations should be judged by how well they fit the evidence, not by how quickly they restore certainty. THE BOOK OF THE DAMNED Part 1 of 2 FULL AudioBook Greatest AudioBooks Channel: Greatest AudioBooks Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When Explanations Became Fort's Real Target. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Book of the Damned By Charles Fort Demonstrates Fort's critique of premature explanations. See on Amazon Book The Complete Books of Charles Fort By Charles Fort Expands Fort's skeptical method across multiple works. See on Amazon Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Provides a contrasting perspective on evidence and skepticism. See on Amazon Book Charles Fort By Jim Steinmeyer"},{"title":"Vanishings","url":"/vanishings/","description":"Fort's disappearance reports turned anomaly collecting toward witnesses, fear, rumour, and missing documentation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Vanishings Why Fortean Vanishings Feel Different Vanishings Vanishings Why Fortean Vanishings Feel Different Fort's disappearance reports turned anomaly collecting toward witnesses, fear, rumour, and missing documentation. Fort's disappearance reports turned anomaly collecting toward witnesses, fear, rumour, and missing documentation. dataset evidence Disappearance stories in Fort's range Witnesses, rumour, and missing records The human stakes of anomaly collecting forteana damned book vanishings Introduction Within Charles Fort 's The Book of the Damned , unexplained disappearances occupy a distinctive place. Unlike accounts of strange objects falling from the sky or mysterious lights, disappearances unsettle because the anomaly is human. A missing person leaves behind interrupted routines, uncertain witnesses, incomplete paperwork and emotional consequences that cannot easily be filed away. Fort used such reports not primarily to prove extraordinary forces were at work, but to demonstrate how unresolved events expose the limits of tidy explanations and institutional certainty. In the Fortean tradition, vanishings become \"damned facts\": cases that remain awkward because neither conventional explanations nor paranormal speculation can fully account for them. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... Disappearance stories in Fort's range Fort did not develop a systematic theory of missing persons. Instead, he assembled reports from newspapers , official publications and historical accounts, treating them as part of a larger collection of anomalies that resisted comfortable classification. His catalogue included people allegedly disappearing under unusual circumstances, maritime mysteries such as the Mary Celeste (which he examined more extensively in later work), and historical episodes where records themselves appeared confused or contradictory. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned This method mattered more than any individual case. Rather than arguing that every disappearance represented supernatural intervention, Fort repeatedly juxtaposed: isolated newspaper reports; conflicting witness accounts; incomplete official investigations; historical incidents preserved only through secondary retellings . The accumulation created a different kind of evidence. Readers encountered not a single compelling mystery but a pattern of unresolved documentation. The anomaly often lay as much in the surviving record as in the disappearance itself. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... Fort's treatment therefore differs from later paranormal literature that attempts to identify one hidden cause—whether extraterrestrial, cryptid or occult. His concern was that reality continually produced cases that neither certainty nor dismissal handled well. 3 BIZARRE & UNEXPLAINED Disappearances! Channel: Missing Persons Mysteries with Steve Stockton Open on YouTube Why witnesses become part of the anomaly A striking feature of Fortean disappearances is that witnesses frequently become as important as the missing individual. When someone vanishes unexpectedly, the surviving evidence is almost entirely human: memories that change over time; contradictory statements; rumours spreading beyond verified facts; journalists compressing complex events into dramatic narratives; investigators forced to work with incomplete information. Fort recognised that these secondary effects deserve attention because they reveal how anomalies develop socially. The disappearance itself may remain unexplained, but the changing story becomes observable. This emphasis distinguishes Fortean thinking from straightforward mystery solving. Instead of asking only, \"What happened?\", Fort implicitly asks: Which details survived? Which details disappeared? Who decided which version became accepted? What evidence was quietly discarded? That focus reflects his broader criticism that institutions routinely classify inconvenient information as error or irrelevance before fully understanding it. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... Missing records can be as important as missing people One recurring Fortean theme is the disappearance of documentation itself. Historical vanishings often rely upon: fragmented archives; newspaper summaries rather than original testimony; missing official files; contradictory dates; repeated retellings that gradually acquire new details. From a conventional historical perspective, these weaknesses reduce reliability. Fort acknowledged such weaknesses but also regarded them as worthy of study because they illustrate how evidence is preserved unevenly. A disappearance may therefore remain mysterious for two different reasons: 1. The original event was genuinely unusual. 2. The surviving record became damaged, selective or incomplete. Fort resisted collapsing these possibilities into one answer. The uncertainty itself becomes part of the phenomenon. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... Best of Missing Persons Mysteries! Channel: Missing Persons Mysteries with Steve Stockton Open on YouTube The human stakes of anomaly collecting Disappearances occupy a special place within Forteana because they involve irreversible human absence rather than unusual natural events. A rain of frogs or an unexplained light can be discussed with curiosity. A missing person immediately introduces grief, fear and unresolved responsibility. This changes the emotional character of anomaly collecting. The questions become practical as well as philosophical: Was the person ever found? Did authorities investigate adequately? Were witnesses believed? Did rumours overwhelm evidence? Can later researchers reconstruct what actually happened? Fort generally avoided exploiting tragedy for emotional effect. Instead, disappearances reinforced his argument that certainty is often claimed before the available evidence justifies it. When official explanations appeared incomplete, or when no explanation emerged at all, the unresolved case exposed the limits of confident classification rather than proving extraordinary causes. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... Why Fortean vanishings feel different Many traditions contain stories of people who disappear mysteriously. Fortean disappearances differ because they are presented as problems of evidence rather than folklore alone. Several characteristics recur: Ordinary settings. The missing person often disappears during everyday activity rather than within a mythic landscape. Documentary traces. Newspaper reports, official correspondence and witness testimony remain central. Persistent uncertainty. Neither sceptical nor paranormal explanations completely resolve every detail. Attention to process. Researchers examine how reports circulate, change and are forgotten as much as the disappearance itself. This makes Fortean vanishings intellectually different from ghost stories or legends. Their power lies in occupying an uncomfortable middle ground where documentation exists but remains incomplete. Continuing influence on Fortean investigation Later Fortean writers inherited Fort's attitude more than his individual cases. Modern collections of unexplained disappearances frequently compare reports across decades while remaining alert to mundane explanations such as accidents, reporting errors, deliberate disappearances or flawed journalism alongside genuinely unresolved elements. That balanced posture reflects Fort's idea of \"intermediatism\": resisting premature certainty in either direction. A disappearance is neither automatically paranormal nor automatically solved simply because a plausible explanation exists. The surviving evidence must be weighed on its own merits, recognising both the limits of historical records and the human tendency to construct satisfying narratives around unresolved events. [The Public Domain Review+2Wikipedia] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned His quarrel with science centers on its failure to recognize the universe's wholeness. Scientists... The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why"},{"title":"Odd Beings","url":"/odd-beings/","description":"Fort's treatment of impossible beings blurred folklore, journalism, and scientific notice into one uneasy archive.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Odd Beings Why Did Fort File Fairies Beside Facts? Odd Beings Odd Beings Why Did Fort File Fairies Beside Facts? Fort's treatment of impossible beings blurred folklore, journalism, and scientific notice into one uneasy archive. Fort's treatment of impossible beings blurred folklore, journalism, and scientific notice into one uneasy archive. population context Folklore categories in printed sources Giants, fairies, and credibility problems How Forteana absorbed creature traditions forteana damned book odd beings Introduction Charles Fort did not treat giants and fairies as relics of medieval imagination. In The Book of the Damned , he placed them alongside newspaper reports, archaeological curiosities, scientific notices and eyewitness accounts, deliberately refusing to separate folklore from apparently factual documentation. His purpose was not to prove that fairies or giants existed. Instead, he challenged the confidence with which modern institutions decided that some reports belonged to science while others belonged to legend. In doing so, Fort created one of the defining habits of Forteana : examining stories about extraordinary beings not as settled truths or obvious fantasies, but as evidence that classification itself deserves scrutiny. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... This approach remains distinctive because it occupies an uncomfortable middle ground. Folklore explains why such beings appear in cultural tradition, while archaeology, anthropology and history test physical claims against material evidence. Fort deliberately blurred those boundaries, asking why reports of impossible beings continued to appear in modern printed sources long after educated society declared them extinct. Why did Fort file fairies beside facts? Unlike a collector of myths, Fort searched newspapers , scientific journals and antiquarian publications for reports that looked as though they had entered the documentary record before quietly disappearing from respectable discussion. Fairies and giants interested him because they illustrated how difficult it could be to draw a firm line between inherited folklore and modern testimony. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... His treatment differed from both believers and sceptics. A believer might accept reports as literal encounters. A sceptic might dismiss them immediately as superstition or fraud. Fort instead asked why reports reached publication at all, why editors or scholars considered them worth recording, and why later generations felt obliged to explain them away. For Fort, the existence of printed testimony was itself an anomaly. Even if every individual case proved mistaken, the persistence of such reports raised questions about how evidence entered—and later exited—the accepted record. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Folklore categories in printed sources By the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, folklore had become an organised field of study. Scholars collected oral traditions not because they believed every story, but because they regarded them as important cultural evidence. Newspapers likewise reported local discoveries, strange traditions and alleged archaeological finds with varying degrees of scepticism. Fort exploited this mixed archive. Instead of distinguishing sharply between folklore collections and scientific periodicals, he placed both into the same catalogue of anomalies. A newspaper account describing mysterious footprints, an antiquarian note about oversized burial remains, or a report of curious carved objects associated with \"fairies\" could all become part of his growing collection of excluded observations. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... This was controversial because modern readers usually expect different standards of evidence from different publications. Fort intentionally resisted that hierarchy. His files suggested that credibility often depended less on the reported event than on where the report happened to be filed. The result was not rigorous proof of extraordinary beings. Rather, it exposed how journalism, local history, folklore and scientific reporting often overlapped before later editors imposed cleaner disciplinary boundaries. 'Dark Folklore' with Mark Norman (The Haunted Landscape) Channel: Conway Hall Open on YouTube Giants, fairies and credibility problems Fort's discussions of giants and fairies illustrate recurring problems that continue to shape Fortean debates. Giant discoveries Victorian and Edwardian newspapers frequently printed stories describing unusually large skeletons, oversized tools or mysterious prehistoric remains. Fort drew attention to such accounts because they appeared in seemingly respectable publications rather than purely fictional literature. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... Modern archaeology provides little support for these reports. Many famous \"giant skeleton\" discoveries were never properly documented, involved misidentified animal remains, exaggerated measurements or outright newspaper hoaxes . Despite repeated investigation, no verified archaeological evidence demonstrates the existence of a race of enormous humans beyond normal biological variation. For Forteans, however, the important question is often historical rather than biological: why were these stories repeatedly published, circulated and then forgotten? Fairies in modern records Fairies posed a different challenge. Unlike giant skeletons, fairy reports rarely centred on physical remains. Instead they involved encounters, strange lights, miniature coffins, unusual stone objects or local traditions attached to specific landscapes. Fort noted these because they occupied an ambiguous space between folklore and observation. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... Modern folklore research generally interprets these accounts as expressions of local belief, symbolic storytelling, social memory or psychological experience rather than evidence for hidden biological species. Yet Fort regarded their persistence as evidence that modern rationalism had not eliminated older ways of interpreting unusual events. How Forteana absorbed creature traditions Fort helped transform creatures such as fairies and giants from purely folkloric figures into what later became \"Fortean\" subjects. This shift involved several important changes. The setting became modern. Reports increasingly came from newspapers, museum collections, archaeological claims and eyewitness testimony rather than medieval chronicles alone. The emphasis moved from belief to documentation. Instead of asking whether fairies existed, Fortean writers asked why official records contained recurring references to impossible beings. Contradictions became valuable. Cases that were incomplete, poorly explained or internally inconsistent interested Fort precisely because they resisted tidy classification. This method influenced later anomalistic writing, where legends, cryptids , unexplained archaeology and eyewitness testimony are often discussed together—not because they share a common explanation, but because they challenge conventional boundaries between accepted knowledge and rejected reports. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... What modern evidence actually shows From the perspective of contemporary scholarship, the evidence for literal giants or fairies remains extremely weak. Archaeology has developed far stricter excavation standards than those available in Fort's day. Claims involving giant skeletons now require documented excavation records, secure provenance, anatomical analysis and independent verification. Extraordinary claims almost never survive that process. Similarly, historians of folklore have shown that fairy traditions differ dramatically across regions and periods, making it difficult to treat them as reports of a single objective phenomenon. Instead, they reflect changing religious ideas, local landscapes, social anxieties and storytelling traditions. That does not eliminate the value of Fort's observations. Rather, it changes their significance. His archive demonstrates how difficult it can be to separate observation from interpretation once reports pass through newspapers, local memory, scientific institutions and popular culture. The strongest lesson is therefore methodological rather"},{"title":"Poltergeists","url":"/poltergeists-edffb6/","description":"Fort's uncanny household cases helped make disturbances, witnesses, and institutional response part of the phenomenon.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Poltergeists What Poltergeist Reports Added to Fort's Archive Poltergeists Poltergeists What Poltergeist Reports Added to Fort's Archive Fort's uncanny household cases helped make disturbances, witnesses, and institutional response part of the phenomenon. Fort's uncanny household cases helped make disturbances, witnesses, and institutional response part of the phenomenon. dataset evidence Household disturbance reports Documentation gaps and witness pressure Why uncanny events broadened Forteana forteana damned book poltergeists Introduction Poltergeist like reports occupy a smaller place in Charles Fort 's archive than his famous collections of strange falls, mysterious lights and unexplained objects, but they are important because they shifted Forteana into the domestic sphere. Instead of looking upward at unusual events in the sky, Fort also examined reports of houses where stones appeared to fly from invisible sources, windows shattered without an identified culprit, or household objects behaved in ways that witnesses and police could not readily explain. He did not present these cases as proof of spirits. Rather, he treated them as examples of reports that remained awkwardly unresolved after official investigation, making them another class of his \"damned\" facts. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com London Times, Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13July 1, 1919 — Instances of stones that have been thrown, or that have fallen, upon... Published: July 1, 1919 Within The Book of the Damned , these accounts broadened the Fortean project by showing that anomalies were not confined to remote landscapes or spectacular natural phenomena. They could unfold in ordinary homes, involve multiple witnesses and attract direct attention from neighbours, police and newspapers . That shift helped establish a lasting Fortean interest in everyday experiences that resist straightforward classification. [Newberry Library] newberry.org poltergeists the paranormal and flying discs in the sky Newberry LibraryPoltergeists, the Paranormal, and Flying Discs in the Sky8 Oct 2024 — The Book of the Damned is organized into twenty eig... Book of the Damned by Charles Hoy FORT read by Various Part 2/2 Full Audio Book Channel: LibriVox Audiobooks Open on YouTube Household disturbance reports Fort introduced his discussion by acknowledging that his own collection merged into what were commonly labelled \"poltergeist\" cases. His interest centred less on ghost traditions than on recurring reports of physical disturbances that appeared to lack an identifiable cause. He drew heavily from newspaper accounts, especially British reporting, assembling incidents that shared a recognisable pattern. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com London Times, Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13July 1, 1919 — Instances of stones that have been thrown, or that have fallen, upon... Published: July 1, 1919 Typical reports in his archive include: Houses repeatedly struck by stones or other small objects from directions that observers could not determine. Windows broken despite guards, neighbours or police watching the property. Localised attacks affecting one household rather than an entire neighbourhood. Episodes that continued despite organised attempts to identify pranksters. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com London Times, Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13July 1, 1919 — Instances of stones that have been thrown, or that have fallen, upon... Published: July 1, 1919 One of Fort's better known examples came from The Times of London, describing houses in Bermondsey in 1872 that were bombarded with stones over several hours. According to the contemporary report, windows were smashed, children injured and numerous policemen stationed nearby, yet no source for the missiles could be identified. Fort presented the account largely through quotation, allowing the apparent failure of conventional investigation to become part of the anomaly itself. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com London Times, Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13July 1, 1919 — Instances of stones that have been thrown, or that have fallen, upon... Published: July 1, 1919 Another case, also drawn from The Times , concerned Mrs Charton's house at Sutton Court House in Chiswick in 1841. Windows reportedly continued to break while police officers and members of the household maintained watch around a detached building enclosed by walls. Fort highlighted the repeated inability of investigators to catch anyone responsible, not because he claimed supernatural forces had been demonstrated, but because the official record itself acknowledged an unresolved problem. [Newberry Library] newberry.org poltergeists the paranormal and flying discs in the sky Newberry LibraryPoltergeists, the Paranormal, and Flying Discs in the Sky8 Oct 2024 — The Book of the Damned is organized into twenty eig... These examples illustrate an important feature of Fort's method. The focus was not spectacular violence but persistent, apparently physical events recorded in ordinary settings and discussed in respectable newspapers. Book of the Damned by Charles Hoy FORT read by Various Part 1/2 Full Audio Book Channel: LibriVox Audiobooks Open on YouTube Documentation gaps and witness pressure Fort's treatment of these incidents differed from later paranormal authors who attempted to identify a single hidden mechanism. Instead, he concentrated on the weaknesses and contradictions within the documentation itself. Several features repeatedly attracted his attention: Multiple witnesses without consensus. Many reports involved families, neighbours and police, yet agreement about what had happened remained incomplete. Institutional acknowledgement without resolution. Authorities often investigated but reached no satisfying conclusion. Fragmentary reporting. Newspaper accounts frequently described dramatic beginnings but offered little follow up explaining whether the mystery had eventually been solved. Absence of decisive evidence. Neither supernatural explanations nor ordinary ones could be demonstrated from the surviving reports. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com London Times, Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13July 1, 1919 — Instances of stones that have been thrown, or that have fallen, upon... Published: July 1, 1919 These gaps were central to Fort's argument. He was less interested in proving invisible agents than in showing how unresolved events accumulated within the historical record. Scientific and legal institutions generally required decisive explanations, while newspapers often lost interest once a story ceased to be newsworthy. The resulting archive contained many incomplete narratives that could not easily be classified as either established fact or exposed fraud. This approach explains why Fort often reproduced lengthy extracts from newspapers instead of rewriting them into polished stories. The original wording, with its uncertainties, official statements and missing conclusions, demonstrated the untidy character of anomalous evidence more effectively than a simplified retelling could. [Internet Archive] archive.org Internet ArchiveThe book of the damned, by Charles Fort25 Jan 2007 — The book of the damned, by Charles Fort. by: Fort, Charles, 1874 193... Why uncanny events broadened Forteana The inclusion of poltergeist like reports expanded the scope of Forteana in several important ways. First, they introduced a strongly witness centred perspective. A rain of frogs or an unusual astronomical observation might be recorded by specialists, but household disturbances were experienced by families, servants, neighbours and local police. The credibility of ordinary people therefore became part of the evidence itself. Second, these reports blurred disciplinary boundaries. They touched criminal investigation, journalism, folklore and emerging psychical research simultaneously. Fort did not attempt to resolve those competing interpretations. Instead, he placed them alongside meteorological anomalies, unexplained falls and archaeological oddities as different expressions of the same larger problem: the persistence of observations that resisted stable classification. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... Finally, domestic disturbances reinforced Fort's broader challenge to intellectual certainty. A broken window was not extraordinary because broken windows never happen; it became anomalous only when repeated investigation failed to establish how or why the damage occurred. In this sense, the anomaly lay as much in the inadequacy of explanation as in the event itself. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort."},{"title":"Sky Reports","url":"/sky-reports/","description":"Fort's older sky reports show how UFO-style questions existed before flying saucers had a name.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Sky Reports Were UFO Questions Already in Fort's Files? Sky Reports Sky Reports Were UFO Questions Already in Fort's Files? Fort's older sky reports show how UFO style questions existed before flying saucers had a name. Fort's older sky reports show how UFO style questions existed before flying saucers had a name. dataset evidence Strange lights before 1947 Why later UFO writers mined Fort What source checking can and cannot prove forteana damned book sky reports Introduction Were UFO questions already present in Charles Fort 's files before the term \"flying saucer\" existed? In an important sense, yes—but not because Fort documented modern UFOs. Rather, The Book of the Damned assembled a long trail of reports describing unexplained lights, luminous objects, strange aerial forms and unusual atmospheric events from well before 1947. These accounts became a template for later Fortean and UFO researchers, who argued that unexplained aerial phenomena had a much deeper history than the post war saucer era. At the same time, many of the reports remain difficult to verify, and historians caution that newspaper sensationalism, folklore and incomplete records complicate attempts to interpret them as evidence of a single continuing phenomenon. [Encyclopedia.com] encyclopedia.com In fact, the first UFO book, Charles Fort 's The Book of the Damned, was published in 1919.... books by an equally influential theorist... Strange lights before 1947 Fort's contribution was not to discover unknown events but to collect scattered printed reports that had largely been forgotten. Working through newspapers , scientific journals and magazines, he assembled examples that seemed to resist straightforward explanation. Within his broader catalogue of \"damned\" data were numerous descriptions of unusual appearances in the sky. Several recurring patterns appear in the material that later readers associated with UFO history: Persistent luminous objects reported by multiple witnesses. Objects described as discs, cigars, globes or elongated lights , although the terminology reflected the technology and language of their own era rather than later \"flying saucer\" imagery. Unexpected aerial manoeuvres that observers considered inconsistent with known aircraft or astronomical bodies. Repeated newspaper reports from different locations describing comparable aerial curiosities. Fort rarely presented these as proof of extraterrestrial visitation. Instead, he argued that the existence of numerous awkward reports deserved attention because they had been dismissed too quickly or absorbed into conventional explanations without adequate examination. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned One of Fort's recurring themes involved mysterious atmospheric lights that appeared before accepted scientific explanations existed. He pointed, for example, to unusual glowing skies reported before the 1883 eruption of Krakatoa, questioning whether later explanations had been applied too broadly to earlier observations. His purpose was less to prove a mystery than to illustrate how scientific consensus sometimes develops after the fact. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned Another important category consisted of nineteenth century reports of unexplained aerial craft. These accounts usually described elongated, dirigible like objects rather than metallic discs. Because powered flight itself was a new technological dream, witnesses interpreted unknown aerial objects through the vocabulary available to them. The best known examples are the American \"mystery airship\" waves of 1896–97. Newspapers carried hundreds of reports of illuminated airships, strange lights and occasional claims of encounters with their occupants. Modern historians generally regard these reports as a mixture of misidentifications, rumours, hoaxes and genuine unexplained observations, but they remain important because they demonstrate that widespread aerial mystery narratives existed half a century before the Kenneth Arnold sighting of 1947. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mystery airship Mystery airship HISTORY OF UFOs 🛸 AND ALIENS 👽 COMPILATION From ancient times to our present day Channel: The Rest is History Open on YouTube Why later UFO writers mined Fort After 1947, researchers searching for historical precedents naturally returned to Fort's compilations. His books had already assembled thousands of reports that seemed to resemble aspects of the emerging UFO phenomenon. The influence worked in several ways. First, Fort provided an archive rather than a theory . Later investigators did not need to begin historical research from scratch because he had already identified newspapers and journals containing unusual aerial reports. Second, he encouraged comparative reading across centuries . Instead of treating each case as isolated, Fort juxtaposed reports from different countries and decades, inviting readers to look for recurring patterns without insisting they all had a common cause. Third, he established the Fortean habit of distinguishing the report from the explanation . A witness might sincerely report an extraordinary light without that automatically confirming any particular interpretation. This distinction later became influential among researchers who preferred to discuss unidentified aerial phenomena without immediately concluding they represented extraterrestrial spacecraft. Researchers such as Jacques Vallée expanded this historical approach, arguing that reports of unexplained aerial phenomena long predated modern UFO culture. Vallée's historical catalogues drew upon many of the same nineteenth century and earlier sources that Fort had helped popularise, although Vallée generally attempted more systematic source criticism than Fort himself employed. [Internet Archive+2Encyclopedia.com] archive.org Internet Archive Full text of \"Wonders In The Sky Internet ArchiveFull text of \"Wonders In The Sky Unexplained Aerial...Wonders in the sky: unexplained aerial objects from antiquity to... What source checking can and cannot prove The greatest strength of Fort's aerial catalogue is also its greatest weakness. Most of his material originated in published newspapers and journals rather than private anecdotes. That means many reports can, at least in principle, be traced back to identifiable publications. Fort's cases therefore remain valuable starting points for historical investigation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort However, several important limitations remain. Many original reports survive only in abbreviated newspaper form. Journalists often rewrote local stories, omitted witness details or repeated material from other papers, making it difficult to determine how many genuinely independent observations existed. Sensational journalism influenced reporting. The late nineteenth century was an era of intense newspaper competition, and dramatic aerial stories could spread rapidly between publications, accumulating embellishments as they travelled. The mystery airship wave is a classic example of this process. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mystery airship Mystery airship Modern terminology cannot simply be projected backwards. Witnesses described what they believed they saw using the concepts available in their own time. An observer in 1897 might compare an unknown object to an airship; someone in 1947 might call a similar sighting a flying saucer; a modern observer may describe a drone or unidentified aerial phenomenon. Similar descriptions do not necessarily indicate the same underlying cause. Some reports have acquired legendary status through repetition. Cases copied from one anomaly collection to another can gradually appear stronger than their documentary foundations justify. Modern historians therefore attempt to return to the earliest available newspaper or journal rather than relying on later retellings . For these reasons, Fort's files function better as historical evidence trails than as established evidence for any specific explanation. They point researchers towards recurring reports that deserve examination but rarely settle the question themselves. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube London Fortean Society: UFO Culture and Why We See Saucers with Sarah Scoles Channel: Conway Hall Open on YouTube Why these early sky reports still matter in Forteana Within the Fortean tradition, the significance of pre 1947 sky anomalies lies less in demonstrating that extraterrestrial craft visited earlier centuries than in challenging the assumption that aerial mysteries began with the flying saucer era. Fort showed that unexplained lights, strange aerial objects and disputed atmospheric phenomena had already entered printed public discourse decades before modern UFO terminology existed. Whether later readers interpret those reports as misidentifications, cultural myths,"},{"title":"Strange Rain","url":"/strange-rain/","description":"Fort used reports of uncanny rain and falling matter to test the limits of ordinary weather explanation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Damned Book","level":3,"text":"Strange Rain When Rain Looked Too Strange to Explain Strange Rain Strange Rain When Rain Looked Too Strange to Explain Fort used reports of uncanny rain and falling matter to test the limits of ordinary weather explanation. Fort used reports of uncanny rain and falling matter to test the limits of ordinary weather explanation. population context Blood like rain in Fortean files Weather, error, hoax, or residue Why substance falls suited Fort's method forteana damned book strange rain Introduction Reports of blood like rain, gelatinous matter, ash, dust, fibres and other apparently impossible substances falling from the sky occupy a distinctive place in The Book of the Damned . For Charles Fort , these accounts were not merely colourful oddities. They were ideal test cases for his central claim that accepted knowledge often deals with anomalies by explaining them away too quickly or ignoring them altogether. Rather than arguing that every report was literally true, Fort collected dozens of examples to ask a different question: were the conventional explanations always better supported than the original observations? [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Scientists mutilate reality, cut it into piec... Within the wider Fortean tradition, strange rain became one of the clearest demonstrations of Fort's method. A report of crimson rain, black dust or mysterious slime could rarely be repeated under controlled conditions, yet such events appeared often enough in newspapers , weather records and scientific correspondence to resist simple dismissal . The resulting tension—between eyewitness testimony, imperfect evidence and competing explanations—remains one of the defining features of Fortean thinking. Blood like rain in Fortean files Among the most striking reports collected by Fort were accounts of rain described as resembling blood. Such stories have appeared in European chronicles for well over two thousand years. Ancient and medieval writers frequently interpreted them as omens of war, plague or divine judgement, while nineteenth century observers increasingly attempted to investigate them as physical phenomena rather than supernatural signs. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Blood rain Blood rain Fort deliberately juxtaposed reports from different periods and locations. Instead of treating each event as an isolated curiosity, he suggested that their repetition deserved attention. His purpose was not to revive medieval beliefs but to question whether modern investigators had become too confident in dismissing reports that sounded impossible. The cases varied considerably: Rainwater reported as bright red or dark crimson. Localised showers staining buildings, vegetation or clothing. Deposits of reddish dust accompanying rainfall. Reports where witnesses initially believed actual blood had fallen. Fort rarely insisted that the substance genuinely was blood. Instead, he highlighted how observers and investigators often reached radically different conclusions despite examining the same event. Whimsical. Wooly. Weird. Charles Fort's \"Book of the Damned!\" Channel: Rudolph Kohn Open on YouTube Weather, error, hoax, or residue? Modern meteorology provides several well established mechanisms that explain many historical reports without requiring unknown atmospheric phenomena. Red or blood like rain is now commonly understood to result from microscopic particles suspended in rainwater. These may include: Windblown desert dust carried hundreds or even thousands of kilometres through the atmosphere. Spores or algae containing red pigments. Fine mineral particles mixed into rain clouds before precipitation. Scientific investigation has demonstrated that numerous modern cases fall into these categories. The widely reported red rain in Kerala, India, for example, prompted years of investigation before researchers concluded that biological particles, rather than actual blood, accounted for the unusual colour. Similar explanations have been proposed for many earlier European reports. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Blood rain Blood rain This does not mean every historical account can be reconstructed with certainty. Many reports survive only as brief newspaper items or references in local chronicles, making independent verification impossible. In some instances exaggeration, poor observation or deliberate hoaxes remain plausible explanations. Fort considered that uncertainty itself significant. He argued that uncertainty should encourage investigation rather than immediate exclusion. More than red rain: the catalogue of strange falls Although blood like rain attracted attention because of its dramatic appearance, Fort placed it within a broader category of anomalous falls. Throughout The Book of the Damned , he assembled reports describing substances that seemed equally difficult to classify. Examples included alleged falls of: Black dust or soot covering large areas. Yellow powders resembling pollen. Fibrous material described as hair like or woolly. Jelly like masses later compared with what folklore calls \"star jelly\". Ash, cinders or slag apparently arriving without an obvious local source. Fort also cited museum curators who reported receiving numerous objects that witnesses insisted had fallen from the sky, including pieces of coal, slag and stone. Scientific authorities generally rejected these claims on the grounds that the objects were ordinary terrestrial materials already lying on the ground before storms or lightning strikes. Fort regarded this recurring pattern of rejection as evidence of institutional filtering rather than proof that every witness was mistaken. [Roccioletti] roccioletti.com THE BOOK OF THE DAMNEDby C FORT · Cited by 162 — THE BOOK OF THE DAMNED Chapter IX. 86. Page 89. I'd like to know what strange, damned, excommunicated things have been sent to museums by persons who have felt... Importantly, Fort often admitted that conventional explanations might be correct in individual cases. His criticism focused less on specific conclusions than on what he saw as a reluctance to examine inconvenient reports with equal seriousness. THE BOOK OF THE DAMNED Part 2 of 2 FULL AudioBook Greatest AudioBooks Channel: Greatest AudioBooks Open on YouTube Why substance falls suited Fort's method Reports of strange substances falling from the sky perfectly matched Fort's broader argument about \"damned\" facts. Unlike spectacular claims that required belief in entirely new forces of nature, coloured rain and mysterious deposits occupied an uncomfortable middle ground. They were: Usually reported by ordinary witnesses rather than professional paranormal investigators. Sometimes accompanied by physical material that could be examined. Occasionally mentioned in scientific journals before being dismissed. Difficult to repeat experimentally. This combination prevented easy resolution. Complete belief was unwarranted, yet complete dismissal could also appear premature. Fort therefore used accumulation as his principal strategy. One report of red rain might be explained as dust. A second might involve pollen. A third could prove fraudulent. But he argued that repeatedly encountering unusual atmospheric deposits across decades and countries justified asking broader questions about how evidence was evaluated. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Scientists mutilate reality, cut it into piec... What later science changed—and what it did not Many examples that impressed Fort now have straightforward scientific explanations. Atmospheric transport of Saharan dust regularly colours rain across Europe. Algal spores and airborne microorganisms can produce striking pigmentation. Industrial pollution, volcanic ash and wildfire smoke are also capable of generating unusual precipitation and deposits. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Blood rain Blood rain From a scientific perspective, these explanations substantially reduce the mystery surrounding many historical reports of blood like rain. However, they do not entirely eliminate the historical interest of Fort's collection. His enduring contribution lies less in demonstrating that extraordinary substances truly fell from the sky than in documenting how observations moved between eyewitness testimony, journalism, scientific investigation and official rejection. Some reports proved explicable; others remain too poorly documented to assess. Together they illustrate the uncertain boundary between anomaly and explanation that fascinated Fort. For readers of The Book of the Damned , strange rain therefore functions as more than an atmospheric curiosity. It exemplifies the book's larger challenge: not that conventional science is necessarily wrong, but that the path from observation to accepted explanation is often more complicated—and more"},{"title":"Charles Fort","url":"/charles-fort/","description":"Charles Fort turned rejected reports into a lasting challenge about who decides what counts as evidence.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Charles Fort Why Charles Fort Still Haunts Strange Evidence Charles Fort Charles Fort Why Charles Fort Still Haunts Strange Evidence Charles Fort turned rejected reports into a lasting challenge about who decides what counts as evidence. Charles Fort turned rejected reports into a lasting challenge about who decides what counts as evidence. entity From journalist to anomaly collector The idea of damned facts What Fort got right and wrong forteana charles fort Introduction Charles Fort still matters to Forteana because he gave strange evidence a problem name: “ damned facts ”. In The Book of the Damned (1919), he argued that some reports are not rejected because they are false, but because they are inconvenient, badly classified, unfashionable, or embarrassing to established theory. His famous opening definition was stark: “By the damned, I mean the excluded.” [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org The Project Gutenberg eBook of The Book Of The Damned, by Charles Fort... That idea did not make Fort a reliable scientist, and it did not make every falling frog, strange light, poltergeist report or impossible looking disappearance true. What it did was create a durable way of asking who gets to decide what counts as evidence. Fort’s legacy is therefore less a set of answers than a pressure test: when an observation seems absurd, do we investigate it, explain it away, file it as folklore , or refuse to see it at all? From journalist to anomaly collector Charles Hoy Fort was born in 1874 and worked first as a journalist and fiction writer before becoming the figure now attached to Forteana. The Encyclopedia of Science Fiction describes him as a United States journalist and author, active before 1900, who published realistic and humorous short stories before moving into the work that made him famous. His novel The Outcast Manufacturers appeared in 1909, but it was only loosely connected to his later reputation as a collector of anomalies. [SF Encyclopedia] sf encyclopedia.com SF Encyclopedia SFE: Fort, Charles SF Encyclopedia SFE: Fort, Charles The decisive shift came when Fort began treating libraries as mines of rejected evidence. Charlotte Sleigh’s study of Fort notes that he first gathered anomalous material from scientific journals and newspapers into lost manuscripts called X and Y . With encouragement from the novelist and editor Theodore Dreiser, he reworked that material into the four books on which his reputation rests: The Book of the Damned in 1919, New Lands in 1923, Lo! in 1931 and Wild Talents in 1932. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Fort’s working life also helps explain the texture of his books. He was not primarily a field investigator interviewing witnesses at fresh scenes. He was a reader, clipper, arranger and provocateur. Sleigh records that Fort divided his final fifteen years between New York and London, using the British Library reading room in London and the New York Public Library in the United States to search scientific journals, magazines and newspapers. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science That method gave him both reach and weakness. He could range across decades, continents and disciplines, noticing recurring patterns that specialists might never place side by side. But he also depended heavily on printed reports, sometimes old, fragmentary, second hand or badly checked. Fort’s archive was powerful as a challenge to certainty; it was much weaker as a way of proving any single marvel. The idea of “damned facts” Fort’s “damned facts” were not simply paranormal claims. They were reported observations that had, in his view, been excluded from respectable knowledge because they did not fit the dominant explanations of the day. In the opening of The Book of the Damned , he frames the work as a procession of excluded data: “Science has excluded” them, and he will make them march before the reader. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org The Project Gutenberg eBook of The Book Of The Damned, by Charles Fort... The book’s title is therefore more precise than it first appears. “Damned” does not just mean strange, cursed or spooky. It means condemned by a system of selection. Fort was interested in the border where a report changes status: from observation to error, from testimony to superstition, from anomaly to embarrassment, from fact to non fact. The Public Domain Review’s 2024 essay on The Book of the Damned argues that the book is not merely a heap of weird stories. Its reports of strange falls and other anomalies are interwoven with attacks on the intellectual systems Fort thought were too confident, including geometry, evolution, Newtonian mechanics and geology. The essay reads Fort’s quarrel with science as a quarrel with dividing reality into authorised truth and excluded falsehood. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... That is why Fort became foundational for Forteana. He did not merely say, “Here are strange things.” He asked why some strange things are allowed to become scientific puzzles while others are treated as rubbish. The Fortean habit begins there: not with belief in every report, but with suspicion of premature dismissal. Whimsical. Wooly. Weird. Charles Fort's \"Book of the Damned!\" Channel: Rudolph Kohn Open on YouTube Why The Book of the Damned felt different Fort was not the first person to collect wonders, prodigies or curiosities. What made The Book of the Damned feel different was its mix of data, satire, philosophy and literary attack. Sleigh describes the book as having two interwoven strands: repeated accounts of strange phenomena presented as “data”, and repeated “pot shots at science”. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science Kent Academic Repository The Silences of Science The examples were deliberately unruly. Fort wrote about things said to have fallen from the sky, including fish, frogs, stones, dusts and organic matter; he also ranged into odd lights, astronomical puzzles, mysterious appearances and disappearances , poltergeist claims and other reports that seemed to resist ordinary classification. Project Gutenberg’s listing summarises the book as a 1919 nonfiction work cataloguing phenomena that mainstream science had excluded or “damned”. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... The strongest image in Fort’s work is the fall from the sky. A rain of frogs or fish is not just a curiosity; it is a category problem. If animals fall in a town, the report may invite meteorology, zoology, newspaper exaggeration, witness error, hoax, folklore or some unknown mechanism. Fort’s move was to hold the awkwardness in place rather than immediately smoothing it into one explanation. His style also kept the reader off balance. The Public Domain Review notes that Fort could propose bizarre possibilities — a “Super Sargasso Sea” in the upper atmosphere, cosmic debris, living universe imagery — while also undercutting the authority of explanation itself. The result is not a standard scientific theory. It is a deliberate disturbance of the reader’s confidence in all neat theories, including Fort’s own. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... Fort’s challenge to scientific authority Fort’s central accusation was not that scientists were always wrong. It was that science, like religion before it, could become a “dominant” system: a way of organising reality that decides in advance which kinds of fact are respectable. The Public Domain Review summarises this as Fort’s attack on the habit of cutting reality into pieces, with some pieces called truth and others damned as false. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... That accusation still has force because real knowledge systems do filter evidence. They have to. No discipline can treat every rumour, anecdote and misprint as equally important. Good science depends on standards of replication, measurement, mechanism and peer criticism. Without those filters, the record fills with errors. Fort’s lasting challenge is that filtering can become circular. A report may be ignored because it is anomalous; then its lack of investigation is used as evidence that it was never worth investigating. The damned fact disappears twice: first from the official explanation, then from the memory of why it was excluded. This is where Fort remains useful even to sceptical readers. He forces a distinction between “unexplained” and “true”, but also between “explained” and “properly examined”. A weak report should"},{"title":"The Book","url":"/the-book/","description":"Fort's first major book mixed anomaly catalogues with satire, attacking overconfident explanations as much as ignorance.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"The Book Why The Book of the Damned Felt Different The Book The Book Why The Book of the Damned Felt Different Fort's first major book mixed anomaly catalogues with satire, attacking overconfident explanations as much as ignorance. Fort's first major book mixed anomaly catalogues with satire, attacking overconfident explanations as much as ignorance. entity Data lists and literary attack Science as Fort's target Why Fort undermined his own theories forteana charles fort the book Introduction The Book of the Damned (1919) made Charles Fort famous not simply because it collected reports of strange phenomena, but because it presented those reports as part of a sustained literary attack on intellectual certainty. Rather than arguing that every mysterious event was genuine, Fort used anomalies to expose what he saw as the overconfidence of institutions that claimed to possess complete explanations of reality. The book is therefore best understood as satire directed at certainty itself. Its catalogue of impossible rains, unexplained disappearances and curious astronomical observations was inseparable from its mockery of scientific dogmatism, sweeping theories and the human desire to believe that every puzzle has already been solved. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... This combination of anomaly collection and ironic criticism is what distinguished The Book of the Damned from earlier compilations of marvels. Fort did not simply ask readers to accept extraordinary claims. Instead, he encouraged them to become suspicious of anyone—scientist, philosopher or believer—who claimed final authority over knowledge. That scepticism extended even to his own ideas. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... Why the book felt different Most earlier books about unexplained events attempted either to defend supernatural explanations or to dismiss unusual reports as mistakes . Fort pursued a different strategy. He assembled hundreds of reports drawn from newspapers , scientific journals and official publications, then arranged them into a running argument that the greatest mystery was not the events themselves but the confidence with which authorities declared them impossible. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... The famous opening declaration captures this approach. Fort announces \"a procession of the damned\", defining the damned as \"the excluded\"—observations left outside accepted systems of knowledge. The phrase reframes the issue from whether an individual report is true to how institutions decide what deserves investigation in the first place. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedTHE BOOK OF THE DAMNED. 1. A procession of the damned. By the damned, I mean the excluded. We shal... The result is a book that often resembles a courtroom cross examination rather than a scientific treatise. Each curious report becomes another witness called to challenge the confidence of established explanations rather than a standalone proof of paranormal phenomena. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Data lists and literary attack Fort's method depended on accumulation. A single report of fish falling from the sky or an unexplained luminous object could be dismissed as error. Dozens of similar reports from different places and times forced readers to confront a more uncomfortable question: why were such accounts repeatedly published yet rarely incorporated into accepted knowledge? [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... The catalogue itself, however, was only half of the performance. Between examples Fort inserted sarcastic commentary, invented dialogues, exaggerated comparisons and deliberately provocative claims. He mocked the confidence of experts by imitating their language while stretching it towards absurdity. This rhetorical style made the book feel less like an encyclopaedia of mysteries than a satirical essay disguised as an archive. Several recurring techniques define the book's humour: Mock certainty. Fort exaggerated scientific confidence until it sounded ridiculous, suggesting that official explanations often rested on convention as much as evidence. Role reversal. Scientists became the credulous believers, while collectors of anomalies became the sceptics asking awkward questions. Accumulation as comedy. Endless lists of strange reports create both intellectual pressure and comic excess, making readers aware of the difficulty of maintaining neat classifications. Ironical exaggeration. Fort frequently pushed arguments beyond plausibility to expose the weaknesses of rigid systems rather than to establish new doctrines. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... Because of this style, modern readers sometimes mistake Fort's rhetoric for literal advocacy of every speculation he mentions. His humour often depends on blurring that boundary. Science as Fort's target Fort's principal target was not scientific investigation itself but what he regarded as scientific absolutism. He repeatedly compared scientific orthodoxy with earlier religious certainty, arguing that dominant intellectual systems naturally exclude inconvenient observations in order to preserve coherence. According to Fort, every age constructs its own \"dominant\" worldview, and each dominant eventually dismisses facts that fail to fit its framework. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... Importantly, Fort criticised attitudes rather than specific disciplines alone. Geology, astronomy, evolution and physics appear throughout the book, but usually as examples of broader habits of thought: confidence, classification and the temptation to confuse useful models with complete truth. The satire therefore reaches beyond early twentieth century science and addresses a recurring human tendency to mistake successful explanations for exhaustive ones. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... This distinction explains why the book continues to attract both admirers and critics. Readers sympathetic to scientific inquiry can still recognise Fort's warning against institutional complacency without accepting his treatment of anomalous evidence. Whimsical. Wooly. Weird. Charles Fort's \"Book of the Damned!\" Channel: Rudolph Kohn Open on YouTube Why Fort undermined his own theories One of the most distinctive features of The Book of the Damned is that Fort repeatedly weakens his own speculative explanations. He famously introduces imaginative possibilities—including the notion of a \" Super Sargasso Sea \", a mysterious region where lost objects accumulate before falling back to Earth—but rarely insists they are literally true. Instead, such ideas function as demonstrations that if accepted theories can rely on assumptions, alternative assumptions can be invented just as easily. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned This habit serves several purposes. First, it prevents readers from replacing one rigid orthodoxy with another. If Fort offered a complete theory explaining every anomaly, he would become guilty of the same certainty he criticised. Second, it shifts attention away from the speculative explanation and back towards the neglected observation . The existence of unexplained reports matters more, in Fort's argument, than any immediate attempt to explain them. Third, it turns the entire book into a satire of theory building itself. Every grand explanation, including Fort's own, is presented as provisional, playful and vulnerable to future contradiction. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — ” In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, For... For this reason, many historians describe Fort less as a prophet of the paranormal than"},{"title":"Damned Facts","url":"/damned-facts/","description":"Fort's phrase 'damned facts' turns weird reports into a question about who gets to decide what counts as evidence.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"Damned Facts What Did Fort Mean by Damned Facts? Damned Facts Damned Facts What Did Fort Mean by Damned Facts? Fort's phrase 'damned facts' turns weird reports into a question about who gets to decide what counts as evidence. Fort's phrase 'damned facts' turns weird reports into a question about who gets to decide what counts as evidence. mechanism The meaning of 'the excluded' How facts become non facts Why the idea still matters forteana charles fort damned facts Introduction Charles Fort 's idea of “damned facts” was not simply a label for bizarre stories. It was a challenge to the process by which observations become accepted evidence. In The Book of the Damned (1919), Fort argued that countless reported events were not necessarily rejected because they had been disproved, but because they did not fit prevailing scientific assumptions, disciplinary boundaries, or accepted theories. His famous declaration, “By the damned, I mean the excluded,” framed the issue as one of selection rather than mere error. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... The lasting significance of this idea lies less in whether Fort's individual examples were correct than in the question he forced readers to ask: what happens between an observation being recorded and becoming an accepted fact? That question remains relevant to discussions about evidence, scientific judgement, and the treatment of anomalies, even though many of Fort's own claims have since been explained, corrected, or rejected. The Meaning of \"the Excluded\" Fort used the word \"excluded\" in a very specific sense. He believed that science constantly filtered observations, accepting those that reinforced existing theories while sidelining those that appeared contradictory. His criticism was aimed less at individual scientists than at what he saw as the institutional tendency to preserve coherent systems of knowledge. In the opening pages of The Book of the Damned , Fort promises a \"procession of data that Science has excluded.\" The point is important: he rarely claimed that every excluded report was true. Instead, he argued that exclusion itself deserved examination because it revealed how knowledge is organised. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Fort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or...Read more... Fort regarded accepted knowledge as provisional rather than absolute. Every scientific theory, in his view, inevitably simplified reality. That meant some observations would always be treated as mistakes , coincidences , or curiosities until a broader framework could accommodate them. This made his work philosophical as much as paranormal. His central concern was not flying objects or mysterious rains in isolation but the rules governing admission into accepted knowledge. How Facts Become Non Facts Fort proposed that observations pass through several informal filters before becoming recognised facts. His description was literary rather than systematic, but the mechanism can be understood through several recurring themes. Conflict with existing theory. If an observation directly contradicted accepted explanations, researchers often looked first for recording errors, instrument failures, or ordinary causes before considering a revision of theory. Fort accepted that such caution was reasonable but argued that it could become habitual dismissal . [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science counts of strange phenomena are insistently rehearsed as 'data', interspersed with pot shots...Read more... Problems of classification. Many reports combined features belonging to different disciplines. A strange object in the sky might interest astronomers, meteorologists, or geologists without fitting comfortably into any of those fields. Fort believed these boundary cases were especially vulnerable to neglect. Source credibility. Fort collected reports from newspapers, scientific journals, magazines, and historical records. Modern readers often notice that many originated as second hand accounts. Fort's point, however, was that reports were frequently discarded because of who reported them rather than because every factual detail had been investigated. Accumulated embarrassment. Once an anomaly gained a reputation as absurd, future investigators became less willing to study it. Fort argued that ridicule itself functioned as a filter separating respectable questions from unacceptable ones. Together these mechanisms transformed observations into what he called \"damned facts\"—reports that remained recorded but effectively ceased to participate in scientific discussion. Whimsical. Wooly. Weird. Charles Fort's \"Book of the Damned!\" Channel: Rudolph Kohn Open on YouTube Why Fort Collected So Many Anomalies Modern readers sometimes assume Fort wanted to prove paranormal phenomena. His books suggest a different strategy. Rather than building one carefully tested case, he assembled hundreds of disconnected reports: unusual objects falling from the sky; mysterious atmospheric phenomena; unexplained disappearances ; reports of unidentified aerial objects before the modern UFO era; archaeological finds that appeared difficult to classify. Individually, many examples were weak. Fort acknowledged uncertainty repeatedly and often presented multiple speculative explanations, including ones he clearly intended humorously. His argument relied on accumulation: while any single report might be mistaken, he believed the sheer number of excluded observations suggested a larger problem with scientific certainty. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science counts of strange phenomena are insistently rehearsed as 'data', interspersed with pot shots...Read more... This explains why The Book of the Damned often feels less like a scientific monograph than a sustained catalogue of exceptions. The Difference Between Exclusion and Proof One of the most common misunderstandings of Fort is the assumption that exposing weaknesses in accepted explanations automatically validates extraordinary alternatives. Fort himself rarely claimed such certainty. Instead, he frequently suspended judgement. His books contain speculative ideas—including the famous suggestion that \"I think we're property\"—yet these were often presented as provocative hypotheses rather than conclusions established by evidence. Charlotte Sleigh notes that The Book of the Damned interweaves anomalous \"data\" with sustained attacks on scientific confidence, making it as much a critique of intellectual systems as a catalogue of mysteries. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science counts of strange phenomena are insistently rehearsed as 'data', interspersed with pot shots...Read more... Modern philosophy of science largely agrees with one limited aspect of Fort's position: rejecting one explanation does not automatically establish another. Evidence requires positive support, not merely criticism of existing theories. This distinction separates Fort's enduring philosophical influence from the evidential strength of his individual claims. Episode 230 Charles Fort Our Supernatural Father Part 1 Channel: Astonishing Legends Open on YouTube Why Many of Fort's Examples Failed to Convince Scientists Scientists generally did not ignore Fort because they believed anomalies could never exist. Instead, many regarded his evidence as methodologically inadequate. Several recurring problems appear throughout his collections: many reports lacked independent verification; eyewitness accounts were often separated from the events by years or decades; newspapers sometimes repeated local rumours without investigation; multiple versions of the same incident circulated with conflicting details; Fort rarely distinguished strongly between well documented observations and weak anecdotes. Critics such as Joe Nickell have argued that this inconsistent treatment of evidence prevents Fort's collections from functioning as reliable scientific documentation, even while acknowledging his importance as a critic of intellectual complacency. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical InquirerCharles Fort: Purveyor of the Unprobedby J Nickell — He began by writing The Book of the Damned, explaining in the int... The weakness of individual cases therefore did not eliminate Fort's broader philosophical question, but it did limit the persuasive force of his empirical argument. Why the Idea Still Matters Fort's phrase \"damned facts\" survives because it identifies a genuine tension in knowledge making. Scientific progress depends on filtering unreliable observations. Without scepticism, error quickly overwhelms evidence. Yet history also shows that occasionally an initially"},{"title":"Falling Animals","url":"/falling-animals/","description":"Rains of frogs and fish show Fort's core move: hold an absurd report open before forcing it into one tidy explanation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"Falling Animals Why Falling Frogs Became Fortean Icons Falling Animals Falling Animals Why Falling Frogs Became Fortean Icons Rains of frogs and fish show Fort's core move: hold an absurd report open before forcing it into one tidy explanation. Rains of frogs and fish show Fort's core move: hold an absurd report open before forcing it into one tidy explanation. mechanism Why sky falls mattered to Fort Possible explanations and misreadings The category problem behind the marvel forteana charles fort falling animals Introduction Reports of frogs, fish and other small animals falling from the sky became the most recognisable examples in Charles Fort 's collection of \" damned facts \". Their importance was never simply that they were strange. Rather, they exposed what Fort regarded as a deeper problem: when an event does not fit established categories, people often rush to force it into a familiar explanation or dismiss it altogether. In The Book of the Damned (1919), repeated accounts of animal falls became a testing ground for this argument. Fort was less interested in proving that animals literally descended from mysterious realms than in showing how awkward observations challenge the boundaries between accepted fact, folklore, meteorology and error. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedFor instance, chemical analysis can reveal that almost any dead man was poisoned with arsenic, we'... Why sky falls mattered to Fort Fort's books contain dozens of reports of fish, frogs, worms, jelly like substances and other biological material allegedly falling during rainstorms. Many came from newspapers , scientific journals and local correspondents rather than occult literature, a choice that was central to his method. By collecting reports that had already appeared in respectable publications, he could ask why they rarely became part of mainstream scientific discussion. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedFor instance, chemical analysis can reveal that almost any dead man was poisoned with arsenic, we'... A frequently repeated example was an 1873 report from Kansas City describing a shower of frogs that supposedly \"darkened the air\". Fort juxtaposed this with numerous similar accounts from Europe and North America, not because any single case was conclusive, but because the accumulation itself became his argument. If independent observers repeatedly described similar events across decades, he believed they deserved more attention than they usually received. [Live Science] livescience.com me, \"The Book of the Damned\": Any number of small animals have been Live ScienceRaining Frogs & Fish: A Whirlwind of TheoriesApril 11, 2014 — 10 Apr 2014 — Charles Fort was an early collector of reports ab... Published: April 11, 2014 This illustrates a characteristic feature of Fortean thinking. Instead of treating each report as an isolated curiosity, Fort arranged them into patterns that challenged conventional classification. The emphasis shifted from asking, \"Did this exact frog shower happen exactly as described?\" to asking, \"Why do reports like this keep recurring, and why are they so difficult to fit into existing categories?\" [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Possible explanations and misreadings Modern science does not regard most reports of falling animals as evidence for unknown forces. Instead, several natural mechanisms can account for at least some well documented cases. The best supported explanation involves waterspouts or tornado like vortices. Powerful rotating columns of air can lift lightweight aquatic animals from ponds, rivers or shallow coastal waters and transport them some distance before depositing them elsewhere. This explains why many reported falls involve only one species of fish or frog and often occur during severe weather. [The Library of Congress+2centerforinquiry.org] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Other cases may arise through different mechanisms: Birds sometimes drop or regurgitate fish while flying. Heavy rain can suddenly reveal animals already present on the ground, creating the illusion that they have fallen from the sky. Eyewitnesses may exaggerate unusual events, while newspapers historically favoured dramatic reporting. Separate incidents may become merged through retelling until they appear more mysterious than the original observations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Rain of animals Rain of animals Importantly, these explanations do not eliminate every historical report. Many older accounts lack enough detail to determine exactly what happened. For Fort, that uncertainty itself was revealing. He argued that science too often moved directly from \"insufficient evidence\" to \"nothing happened\", whereas he preferred to leave some cases unresolved until better evidence existed. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedFor instance, chemical analysis can reveal that almost any dead man was poisoned with arsenic, we'... The category problem behind the marvel The lasting significance of falling animal stories lies less in meteorology than in classification. Imagine encountering hundreds of frogs scattered across a road immediately after a violent storm. Depending on the assumptions brought to the scene, observers might classify the event as: an unusual but natural weather event; a mistaken interpretation of frogs emerging after rain; unreliable local folklore; an unexplained anomaly requiring further investigation. Fort argued that the choice among these categories often occurred before the evidence had been fully examined. Once an event entered the label of \"mistake\" or \"folklore\", it effectively disappeared from scientific discussion. It became one of the \"damned\" facts excluded from respectable knowledge. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the DamnedFor instance, chemical analysis can reveal that almost any dead man was poisoned with arsenic, we'... This is why rains of frogs became such powerful Fortean icons. They sit precisely at the boundary between the ordinary and the extraordinary. Modern meteorology can plausibly explain many examples, yet individual reports vary widely in quality, leaving some impossible to verify or confidently reconstruct. The phenomenon therefore illustrates the tension between incomplete evidence and the human desire for tidy explanations. Fort himself occasionally speculated about highly unconventional possibilities, including fantastical ideas such as a \"Super Sargasso Sea\" where lost objects accumulated before returning to Earth. Most historians of his work interpret these proposals less as settled beliefs than as provocative thought experiments intended to expose the confidence with which conventional explanations were accepted. His larger point remained methodological rather than literal: if every anomaly is explained away too quickly, opportunities to improve understanding may be lost. [Wikipedia+2The Public Domain Review] Wikipedia The Book of the Damned The Book of the Damned Book Of The Damned, by Charles Hoy Fort, Part 1, Chapters 1 14, Full Length Audiobook Channel: The Classic Archives Old Time Radio Channel Open on YouTube Whimsical. Wooly. Weird. Charles Fort's \"Book of the Damned!\" Channel: Rudolph Kohn Open on YouTube Why falling frogs became Fortean icons Among all of Fort's examples, falling animals proved especially memorable because they combine vivid imagery with an accessible philosophical puzzle. Almost anyone can imagine the surprise of finding fish on a street after a storm. The event demands classification: is it weather, biology, coincidence, reporting error or something genuinely unexplained? That immediate uncertainty captures the essence of Forteana . The stories endure not because they demonstrate the paranormal, but because they reveal how knowledge is organised. Falling frogs became symbols of Fort's challenge to rigid systems of thought precisely because they resist easy placement. Even when later scientific explanations solve many individual cases, the broader lesson remains: anomalous reports deserve careful investigation before they are either embraced as marvels or discarded as impossibilities. [The Public Domain Review+2centerforinquiry.org] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... Amazon book picks"},{"title":"Library Method","url":"/library-method/","description":"Fort built his strange archive from printed reports, giving him huge reach but weak control over witness quality and verification.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"Library Method How Reliable Was Fort's Anomaly Archive? Library Method Library Method How Reliable Was Fort's Anomaly Archive? Fort built his strange archive from printed reports, giving him huge reach but weak control over witness quality and verification. Fort built his strange archive from printed reports, giving him huge reach but weak control over witness quality and verification. dataset evidence Libraries as anomaly mines Reach across time and disciplines The problem of second hand reports forteana charles fort library method Introduction Charles Fort 's anomaly archive was remarkable not because he travelled the world investigating mysteries, but because he turned libraries into laboratories. Rather than interviewing witnesses or collecting physical evidence, Fort spent years reading scientific journals, newspapers and magazines in search of reports that appeared to contradict accepted knowledge. This method gave him extraordinary breadth. He could compare obscure reports separated by decades, countries and scientific disciplines, identifying recurring patterns that specialists rarely considered together. At the same time, it imposed severe limits. Most of his evidence was second hand, filtered through editors, journalists and correspondents whose accuracy he could seldom verify. Understanding this tension is essential to understanding both the enduring appeal and the principal weakness of Fort's work. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic RepositoryCharles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of scienceSeptember 27, 2016 — by C Sleigh · 2016 · Cited by 4 — Fort was... Published: September 27, 2016 Libraries as anomaly mines Fort's research was built around reading rather than fieldwork . After abandoning earlier unpublished manuscripts known as X and Y , he devoted years to searching printed sources in the reading rooms of the British Museum (now the British Library collections) and the New York Public Library. These institutions gave him access to scientific periodicals, learned society publications, local newspapers and popular magazines spanning long periods and multiple countries. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic RepositoryCharles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of scienceSeptember 27, 2016 — by C Sleigh · 2016 · Cited by 4 — Fort was... Published: September 27, 2016 His approach differed sharply from that of an investigative journalist or naturalist. Instead of asking whether an individual report could be confirmed, Fort searched for accumulation. One unexplained rain of frogs might be dismissed as an error. Fifty similar reports scattered through different publications became, in his view, evidence that conventional explanations deserved re examination. This archival strategy offered several advantages. It exposed forgotten observations buried in specialist literature. It allowed comparisons across astronomy, meteorology, geology, zoology and folklore . It highlighted anomalies that had received little follow up despite appearing in otherwise respectable publications. It reduced dependence on any single institution or scientific discipline. Fort therefore treated libraries not simply as repositories of accepted knowledge but as vast collections of overlooked observations waiting to be rearranged into new patterns. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic RepositoryCharles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of scienceSeptember 27, 2016 — by C Sleigh · 2016 · Cited by 4 — Fort was... Published: September 27, 2016 Reach across time and disciplines One of the strengths of Fort's library method was its ability to connect reports that had originally appeared in completely different contexts. A provincial newspaper might print an account of unusual material falling from the sky. A scientific society could publish a short note describing an unexplained atmospheric event. A natural history journal might record an animal behaving in an unexpected way. Individually, such reports often disappeared into archives. Fort deliberately assembled them into thematic collections. This method anticipated, in a loose sense, later forms of comparative database research. Rather than treating each anomaly as an isolated curiosity, he looked for repetition across geography and history. Repeated reports of mysterious aerial objects, unusual falls of organic material or unexplained disappearances interested him because they suggested recurring phenomena rather than isolated mistakes . Importantly, Fort rarely claimed that any single report proved an extraordinary conclusion. Instead, he argued that the cumulative existence of many difficult reports challenged excessive confidence in complete scientific explanations. His books therefore function more as catalogues of unresolved questions than as collections of demonstrated facts. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... Lo! (2/2) 💖 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube The problem of second hand reports The same features that made Fort's archive unusually broad also made it difficult to evaluate. Almost every stage of the evidence chain involved another person's judgement. A typical anomaly might pass through several layers before reaching Fort: 1. An observer witnessed an unusual event. 2. A local correspondent described it. 3. A newspaper editor shortened or rewrote the account. 4. Another publication reprinted it. 5. Fort extracted the report years later. At each stage, details could be lost, exaggerated or misunderstood. By the time Fort encountered the account, opportunities for interviewing witnesses, examining locations or collecting physical evidence had usually disappeared. This dependence on printed testimony meant that Fort often could not distinguish between accurate observation , reporting error, misunderstanding, folklore, satire or deliberate fabrication. Modern historians of science therefore view his archive as an important record of anomalous reporting rather than a verified catalogue of anomalous events. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic RepositoryCharles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of scienceSeptember 27, 2016 — by C Sleigh · 2016 · Cited by 4 — Fort was... Published: September 27, 2016 Why newspaper clippings are not the same as evidence Fort frequently cited reports originating in newspapers because newspapers documented unusual local events that scientific journals ignored. However, newspapers have long mixed careful reporting with hearsay, sensationalism and editorial embellishment. Several problems recur throughout Fort's sources: Variable witness quality. Many reports identify no eyewitnesses or provide only brief summaries. Missing follow up. Initial reports often lack later investigation that might confirm or explain the event. Editorial compression. Newspapers routinely shortened complex incidents, removing context that later readers would need. Duplicate circulation. One original story could be reprinted by numerous newspapers, creating the appearance of independent corroboration when there was only a single source. Changing standards. Nineteenth century reporting practices varied enormously between publications and regions. These limitations do not automatically make the reports false. They simply mean that clipping an account from print is very different from independently verifying what occurred. This distinction is one of the central criticisms made by both sceptical commentators and sympathetic historians. Fort successfully demonstrated that unusual reports existed in abundance; he did not establish that each report accurately described reality. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical InquirerCharles Fort: Purveyor of the Unprobedby J Nickell — He spent the remainder of his life scouring old newspapers and oth... Episode 230 Charles Fort Our Supernatural Father Part 1 Channel: Astonishing Legends Open on YouTube Arrangement as argument Fort did more than collect reports. He carefully arranged them. His books move rapidly from one anomaly to another, often presenting dozens of examples before offering commentary. This editorial technique creates an impression of overwhelming accumulation. Readers encounter repeated examples before having time to investigate any single case in depth. Critics have argued that this style can magnify weak evidence by sheer volume. If dozens of poorly documented reports are presented consecutively, the collection may appear stronger than the individual cases warrant. Supporters respond that this was precisely Fort's point. He wanted readers to question the assumption that every inconvenient observation had already been adequately explained. His books were intended as provocations against intellectual certainty"},{"title":"Suspended Belief","url":"/suspended-belief/","description":"Fort's most useful legacy is not belief in every marvel, but the habit of delaying dismissal without abandoning standards.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"Suspended Belief Can You Be Skeptical and Fortean? Suspended Belief Suspended Belief Can You Be Skeptical and Fortean? Fort's most useful legacy is not belief in every marvel, but the habit of delaying dismissal without abandoning standards. Fort's most useful legacy is not belief in every marvel, but the habit of delaying dismissal without abandoning standards. population context Unexplained is not proven Explained is not always closed How Forteana uses disciplined doubt forteana charles fort suspended belief Introduction A common misunderstanding is that being a Fortean means believing every strange story. Charles Fort 's own legacy points in a more useful direction. His enduring contribution is not a catalogue of mysteries to accept, but a habit of resisting premature certainty. A Fortean reader neither dismisses unusual reports because they sound impossible nor accepts them simply because they are unexplained. Instead, they keep judgement open while demanding better evidence. This attitude can be described as suspended commitment : recognising that an unexplained report is not the same thing as a proven phenomenon, while also recognising that an official explanation is not automatically the last word. Within the tradition of Forteana , this balance between curiosity and criticism is one of Fort's most practical and intellectually durable ideas. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, Fort... Can you be skeptical and Fortean? The answer is yes—provided scepticism is understood as careful inquiry rather than automatic disbelief. Fort repeatedly criticised what he saw as the tendency of institutions to discard awkward observations because they disrupted accepted theories. His complaint was directed less at science as a method than at the human habit of protecting established systems from inconvenient evidence. He argued that exclusion itself deserved examination. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project GutenbergThe Book of the Damned by Charles FortFort catalogs anomalous phenomena that mainstream science has excluded or \"damned\"... Modern Forteana has largely inherited this approach rather than a blanket belief in paranormal claims. Many writers describe themselves as interested in anomalies while remaining agnostic about what caused them. The question becomes, \"What happened?\" rather than \"Which extraordinary explanation must be true?\" [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort This distinction matters because it separates Fortean thinking from both unquestioning belief and reflexive debunking. A sceptical Fortean accepts uncertainty as a temporary position instead of treating uncertainty as a problem that must immediately be resolved. Unexplained is not proven One of the easiest mistakes in discussing anomalies is to confuse the absence of an explanation with evidence for a favourite explanation. A strange light in the sky may remain unidentified for decades. That does not establish that it was an extraterrestrial spacecraft. Likewise, an unusual coincidence does not automatically imply hidden forces, psychic abilities or conspiracy. Fort himself often offered deliberately extravagant speculative ideas, but scholars generally regard these less as settled beliefs than as rhetorical devices intended to expose overconfidence in supposedly complete explanations. His books frequently provoke readers into questioning certainty rather than accepting a replacement doctrine. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, Fort... A disciplined Fortean reader therefore distinguishes between several very different claims: A report exists. The report appears sincere or well documented. No satisfactory explanation has yet been established. A particular extraordinary explanation is correct. Only the first three may be justified by the available evidence. The fourth usually requires much stronger support. Keeping these levels separate prevents mystery from becoming mythology. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Explained is not always closed The opposite mistake is assuming that every official explanation permanently settles a case. History contains examples where accepted scientific or historical views changed after overlooked evidence received renewed attention. Fort's broader point was that institutions, like individuals, can become attached to prevailing assumptions. Reports may be ignored because they appear trivial, embarrassing or incompatible with current models rather than because they have been carefully investigated. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — In rescuing the excluded, damned facts, Fort... That does not mean every rejected claim will eventually be vindicated. Most will not. Instead, the Fortean lesson is procedural: Ask whether the evidence was actually examined. Distinguish dismissal from investigation. Revisit old assumptions when genuinely new evidence appears. Accept correction in either direction. Sometimes further research strengthens an unusual claim. More often it reveals mundane causes such as observational error, folklore , hoaxes or misunderstood natural phenomena. Either outcome represents progress because both depend upon investigation rather than assumption. How Forteana uses disciplined doubt Suspended commitment is best understood as a practical method rather than a philosophical slogan. A disciplined Fortean reader typically follows several habits. Separate the report from the interpretation. Witnesses can accurately report what they experienced while being mistaken about what produced it. Judge evidence, not expectations. A bizarre claim should neither receive automatic acceptance nor automatic rejection solely because it conflicts with familiar beliefs. Look for independent confirmation. Multiple reliable sources, physical evidence and contemporaneous documentation deserve more weight than repeated retellings of the same story. Expect ordinary explanations first—but not exclusively. Common causes explain most anomalies. Remaining open to less familiar possibilities does not require treating them as equally probable. Remain willing to change your mind. Suspended commitment is temporary. Better evidence should eventually move the conclusion towards acceptance, rejection or continued uncertainty. These habits closely resemble the broader ideals of careful critical inquiry while preserving Fort's insistence that anomalies deserve fair consideration rather than ridicule. [ResearchGate+2PhilPapers] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Why the future is ForteanIn particular, this essay takes up the Fortean approach. This approach examines areas of human... Episode 230 Charles Fort Our Supernatural Father Part 1 Channel: Astonishing Legends Open on YouTube The value of uncertainty Many readers find uncertainty uncomfortable because it feels incomplete. Forteana instead treats uncertainty as an honest description of the current state of knowledge. This attitude has two important consequences. First, it reduces the temptation to invent explanations merely to eliminate ambiguity. A mystery is allowed to remain a mystery when the evidence does not justify stronger conclusions. Second, it encourages continued observation . An unresolved report is not a failed investigation; it is a reminder that knowledge develops over time, and that some questions remain open because the available evidence is genuinely insufficient. In this sense, suspended commitment is less about paranormal belief than intellectual discipline. 67. Fortean Times Talk (with Ian Simmons) Channel: Chinwag Podcast Open on YouTube A Fortean approach to reading strange claims Applied to newspapers, witness testimony or online discussions, suspended commitment encourages a simple sequence of questions: 1. What is actually being claimed? 2. What evidence supports the claim? 3. Which parts are observations and which are interpretations? 4. Have plausible conventional explanations been properly considered? 5. Does the remaining uncertainty justify belief, disbelief or simply withholding judgement? For Fort, the greatest danger was not being wrong about a particular mystery. It was becoming so certain that inconvenient evidence ceased to be visible. The strongest Fortean reader therefore remains curious without becoming credulous, and sceptical without becoming dismissive—a position that treats disciplined doubt as a strength rather than a weakness. [The Public Domain Review]"},{"title":"Super Sargasso","url":"/super-sargasso/","description":"Fort's bizarre proposals were less settled theories than tools for unsettling the reader's trust in neat explanations.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Charles Fort","level":3,"text":"Super Sargasso Did Fort Believe His Weirdest Theory? Super Sargasso Super Sargasso Did Fort Believe His Weirdest Theory? Fort's bizarre proposals were less settled theories than tools for unsettling the reader's trust in neat explanations. Fort's bizarre proposals were less settled theories than tools for unsettling the reader's trust in neat explanations. mechanism What the Super Sargasso Sea was Why Fort floated impossible models How playful speculation shaped Forteana forteana charles fort super sargasso Introduction Did Charles Fort really believe in the Super Sargasso Sea? The shortest answer is: probably not in the straightforward sense that many later readers assume. The Super Sargasso Sea was Fort's most famous imaginary mechanism, a vast region above the Earth where lost objects, strange materials and unexplained debris supposedly accumulated before occasionally falling back to the ground. Yet Fort repeatedly treated it as a provisional idea rather than a settled doctrine. He introduced bizarre cosmic models, only to undermine them with irony, qualifications and contradictory alternatives. This instability was deliberate. Rather than replacing conventional science with a new orthodoxy, Fort used impossible explanations to expose how readily people accept confident theories simply because they are presented with certainty. That habit of playful but unsettling speculation became one of the defining features of Forteana . [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Joshua Blu Buhs recounts Fort's early life, u... What the Super Sargasso Sea was Fort borrowed the name from the real Sargasso Sea in the Atlantic Ocean, long associated with drifting seaweed and stories of ships trapped in calm waters. He imagined an analogous region above the Earth's atmosphere: a cosmic reservoir where objects accumulated after disappearing from ordinary experience . Within this speculative realm, Fort placed almost anything that conventional explanations struggled to account for. Reports of frogs, fish, seeds, stones, mysterious ice, coloured rain or other odd materials falling from the sky could, in this fictional geography, be understood as debris periodically released from this celestial storehouse. He even joked that future aviators might one day travel through it, finding lost objects and messages from other worlds. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com Internet Sacred Text ArchiveThe Book of the Damned: 13The Book of the Damned, by Charles Fort, [1919], at sacred texts.com. 13... Someti... The theory was intentionally elastic. Sometimes the Super Sargasso Sea functioned as a holding area where earthly objects temporarily accumulated before returning to Earth. At other times it appeared almost as an interplanetary dumping ground filled with material from unknown civilisations or other worlds. Fort never stabilised these competing versions into a coherent cosmology. [Roccioletti] roccioletti.com THE BOOK OF THE DAMNEDby C FORT · Cited by 162 — Super−Sargasso Sea—or from what for convenience we call the Super−Sargasso Sea—which by no means has been taken into full acceptance yet... Episode 230 Charles Fort Our Supernatural Father Part 1 Channel: Astonishing Legends Open on YouTube Why Fort floated impossible models The Super Sargasso Sea illustrates an important feature of Fort's writing: explanation was often less important than exposing the limits of explanation itself. Rather than claiming he had solved the mystery of anomalous reports, Fort frequently argued that his bizarre proposal fitted the available evidence no worse than many accepted scientific theories of his day. His point was rhetorical. If readers laughed at the Super Sargasso Sea, they should also ask why they accepted other speculative models with equal confidence when the evidence remained incomplete. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Joshua Blu Buhs recounts Fort's early life, u... This approach reflected Fort's wider philosophical habit of resisting certainty. Throughout The Book of the Damned , he presents hypotheses only to weaken them later, replacing one conjecture with another or openly admitting that none is satisfactory. The shifting explanations force readers to experience uncertainty instead of escaping it through a single comforting answer. [pearl hifi.com] pearl hifi.com The Complete Books of Charles Fort October 31, 2020 — He did not believe with Berkeley, Fort said, that all things exist only in the beholder's consciousness; he believed... Published: October 31, 2020 His method can be understood as operating on three levels: As a narrative device: the Super Sargasso Sea links otherwise disconnected anomalies into an entertaining imaginative framework. As a critique of certainty: it demonstrates how easily comprehensive theories can be invented from scattered observations. As intellectual provocation: it encourages readers to distinguish between dismissing an explanation because it is strange and rejecting it because the evidence is genuinely inadequate. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Joshua Blu Buhs recounts Fort's early life, u... Did Fort actually believe it? The enduring debate over the Super Sargasso Sea exists because Fort deliberately blurred the line between seriousness and satire. Some passages present the idea with apparent confidence, describing regions where storms might dislodge accumulated material and send it raining onto Earth. Elsewhere, however, he inserts qualifying phrases that subtly undercut his own authority. Referring to the Super Sargasso Sea, he remarks that it is accepted only \"for convenience\" and \"by no means has been taken into full acceptance yet\", signalling that the model itself is provisional rather than dogmatic. [Roccioletti] roccioletti.com THE BOOK OF THE DAMNEDby C FORT · Cited by 162 — Super−Sargasso Sea—or from what for convenience we call the Super−Sargasso Sea—which by no means has been taken into full acceptance yet... His correspondence and broader philosophy reinforce this ambiguity. Fort repeatedly resisted presenting final explanations, preferring what scholars have described as a worldview in constant revision rather than a completed system. Modern historians of his work therefore tend to interpret the Super Sargasso Sea as an imaginative instrument for thinking about anomalies, not as a literal scientific proposal he expected readers to adopt. [pearl hifi.com] pearl hifi.com The Complete Books of Charles Fort October 31, 2020 — He did not believe with Berkeley, Fort said, that all things exist only in the beholder's consciousness; he believed... Published: October 31, 2020 The ambiguity was not a flaw in his writing but part of its purpose. Readers searching for a definitive answer are instead confronted with a demonstration of how quickly certainty dissolves when evidence is fragmentary. Lo! (1/2) 💖 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Lo! (2/2) 💖 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube How playful speculation shaped Forteana Later Fortean culture inherited this attitude more than any particular theory. Few modern Forteans accept the Super Sargasso Sea as a physical location. What endured was Fort's willingness to entertain unusual possibilities without insisting they become unquestionable truths. Forteana became less a catalogue of beliefs than a style of inquiry that keeps anomalous reports open to examination while remaining sceptical of every grand explanation, including its own. This distinguishes Fort from many later paranormal writers. Where some promoted comprehensive hidden world theories, Fort habitually destabilised his own ideas before anyone else could. His speculative models functioned as thought experiments that revealed how scientific, religious and paranormal explanations alike can harden into dogma if they stop questioning themselves. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Joshua Blu Buhs recounts Fort's early life, u... The Super Sargasso Sea therefore survives not because it convincingly explains raining frogs or mysterious disappearances , but because it captures Fort's central intellectual strategy. He used an extravagant fictional mechanism to remind readers that the greatest danger was not having too few explanations, but becoming too certain that any one explanation had to be correct. Amazon book picks"},{"title":"Crop Circles","url":"/crop-circles/","description":"Crop circles show how landscape art, deception, media attention, and mystery can reinforce each other.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Crop Circles Why Crop Circles Became a Modern Mystery Crop Circles Crop Circles Why Crop Circles Became a Modern Mystery Crop circles show how landscape art, deception, media attention, and mystery can reinforce each other. Crop circles show how landscape art, deception, media attention, and mystery can reinforce each other. event time window The field pattern as evidence Hoaxes and human makers Why belief survived exposure forteana crop circles Introduction Crop circles became a modern Fortean mystery because they joined three things that usually pull in different directions: visible physical traces, obvious human mischief, and a persistent feeling that the landscape had been briefly enchanted. The strongest evidence points to human making: the best known modern wave was publicly exposed in 1991 when Doug Bower and Dave Chorley said they had made hundreds of formations in southern England, and later demonstrations showed that sophisticated patterns could be made with simple tools, planning and darkness. Yet exposure did not end the fascination. It changed the mystery from “Who or what made these?” into “Why did so many people still want them to mean more?” Los Angeles Times+2Rupert Sheldrake – Author and Biologist [latimes.com] latimes.com Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax September 10, 1991 — 10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed that for 13 years, they have been responsib... Published: September 10, 1991 That makes crop circles a useful Fortean case. They are not best understood as a simple contest between believers and debunkers. They show how evidence can be theatrical, how hoaxes can become art, how media attention can multiply a phenomenon, and how belief can survive even when many examples are openly artificial. The field pattern itself is the evidence, but it is also a stage. The field pattern as evidence A crop circle is a pattern made by flattening a standing crop, usually a cereal crop such as wheat or barley. Early popular reports focused on simple circular patches; later formations became elaborate pictograms, grids, spirals, symbolic figures and mathematically suggestive designs. This shift matters because the phenomenon’s evidential force depended on a very direct impression: something large, neat and apparently sudden had appeared in a field overnight. For Fortean readers, the key feature is not merely that the patterns looked strange. It is that they occupied ordinary farmland while seeming to speak the visual language of geometry, ritual sites and aerial signs. Many famous English formations appeared in Wiltshire , Hampshire and nearby counties, an area already thick with prehistoric monuments and alternative spiritual tourism . A geographical study of 2002 English crop circles found that reported formations were highly clustered, especially around cultural heritage sites such as Avebury, near more densely populated areas, and along major road corridors. That distribution is much easier to reconcile with human access, visibility and tourism than with an unknown natural or extraterrestrial process. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circles Spatial distribution of England's crop circles The location pattern also explains why crop circles became so potent. A design near Avebury or Silbury Hill does not arrive as a neutral agricultural anomaly. It appears inside a landscape already read by visitors through archaeology, folklore , ley line speculation and New Age pilgrimage. Even a human made formation can feel more charged when it lies beside older monuments whose builders are remote and partly mysterious. The aerial viewpoint made the mystery stronger. From the ground, a circle could be walked, touched and inspected; from the air, it became an image. This dual nature gave crop circles their peculiar authority. They were physical enough to be measured, photographed and argued over, but pictorial enough to circulate in newspapers , books, television documentaries and later online galleries. The phenomenon lived as much in reproduction as in the field. Hoaxes and human makers The modern crop circle story is inseparable from Doug Bower and Dave Chorley. In September 1991, the two British men said they had been making crop circles for 13 years in southern England. Contemporary reporting described them as painters who had used simple means and presented their actions as a long running prank; the Los Angeles Times reported that they claimed responsibility for circles first appearing in southern England and that crop circle writer Pat Delgado initially judged a demonstrated formation as beyond human making before the hoax was revealed. [Los Angeles Times] latimes.com Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax September 10, 1991 — 10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed that for 13 years, they have been responsib... Published: September 10, 1991 Their method was important because it was almost disappointingly simple. Accounts of Bower and Chorley describe the use of planks, rope and a sighting device attached to a cap to keep lines straight. That simplicity undercut one of the core arguments for mystery: that precision implied an advanced non human cause. Later makers refined the same basic principle. A rope fixes a radius; a board or “stomper” flattens stalks; pre planned geometry turns a field into a temporary drawing surface. The exposure did not prove that Bower and Chorley made every formation. It did something more damaging to paranormal certainty: it showed that at least some circles judged impressive by enthusiasts could be made by people, under ordinary constraints, and then misread by investigators. Once that possibility was established, complexity alone could no longer carry the evidential weight it had carried in the 1980s. Public tests reinforced the point. Rupert Sheldrake’s account of a crop circle making competition co sponsored by The Guardian and The Cerealogist describes a 1992 event in which teams were given a difficult design and a night time time limit; the winning team used items such as rope, PVC pipe, a plank, string, a telescopic device and stepladders. Sheldrake concluded that humans could reproduce the main features of state of the art formations at that time. [Rupert Sheldrake – Author and Biologist] sheldrake.org Rupert Sheldrake – Author and BiologistThe Crop Circle Making Competition Rupert SheldrakeThe competition was co sponsored by The Guard... A decade later, a Discovery Channel project asked five MIT students to design, build and analyse their own crop circle. MIT’s own report says the students were recruited not just to make a formation, but to examine features sometimes claimed to distinguish “real” circles from known fakes. This kind of demonstration shifted the burden of proof. The question was no longer whether human beings could make impressive crop circles, but whether any alleged anomaly remained after controls, documentation and known human techniques were considered. [MIT News] news.mit.edu crop circles mits most ambitious hack crop circles mits most ambitious hack Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube From prank to landscape art Calling crop circles “hoaxes” is accurate in many cases, but incomplete. A hoax normally aims to make a false claim believable. Crop circles did that, but they also became a form of land art : anonymous, temporary, illegal or semi legal, dependent on place, weather , timing and audience interpretation. The Circlemakers collective made this ambiguity explicit. Its members, including John Lundberg and Rob Irving, treated circle making as an art practice rooted in deception, folklore and public reaction. A Circlemakers interview says the works are made anonymously by artists, but “to be effective they must not be viewed as art”. That sentence captures the central paradox: once a crop circle is signed, commissioned or explained, it loses some of the charge that made it powerful. [circlemakers.org] circlemakers.org Open source on circlemakers.org. This is why crop circles sit awkwardly between vandalism, performance, folk prank and public artwork. The farmer may see damaged crops. The maker may see a temporary drawing. The believer may see a message. The journalist may see a summer mystery story. The tourist may see a sacred landscape briefly activated. No single interpretation exhausts the event. By the 2000s, the art world and media dimensions were obvious. Circlemakers documented commissioned and demonstration works, including a 2004 National Geographic TV formation near Silbury Hill that incorporated a square spiral. Wired’s 2006 reporting on an elaborate three dimensional style formation near Ashbury, Oxfordshire, quoted Lundberg arguing that even highly complex circles could be made by human artists and describing the"},{"title":"1991 Confession","url":"/1991-confession/","description":"The 1991 confession showed that impressive crop circles could be made by people and forced the mystery to change shape.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"1991 Confession Did the 1991 Confession End the Mystery? 1991 Confession 1991 Confession Did the 1991 Confession End the Mystery? The 1991 confession showed that impressive crop circles could be made by people and forced the mystery to change shape. The 1991 confession showed that impressive crop circles could be made by people and forced the mystery to change shape. event time window What Bower and Chorley said they made Why the demonstrated methods mattered How believers and investigators adapted afterward forteana crop circles 1991 confession Introduction The public confession by Doug Bower and Dave Chorley in September 1991 did not end the crop circle mystery, but it fundamentally changed it. Until then, many discussions centred on whether increasingly elaborate formations required an unknown natural force, extraterrestrial intelligence or another unexplained cause. After the confession, the central question shifted. The existence of convincing human made formations was no longer hypothetical: two men had described their methods, demonstrated them publicly and claimed responsibility for hundreds of circles created over more than a decade. Contemporary reporting, later historical accounts and demonstrations all showed that impressive designs could be produced with simple tools, forcing believers and investigators alike to reconsider what counted as persuasive evidence. [Los Angeles Times+2Wikipedia] latimes.com la xpm 1991 09 10 mn 2463 story Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed... What Bower and Chorley said they made Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, two retired men from southern England, told the newspaper Today that they had begun making crop circles in the late 1970s after Bower had read about the 1966 Tully \"saucer nest\" incident in Australia. According to their account, they initially intended to create simple circular patterns that would encourage speculation about UFO landings. As public fascination grew, they continued the project for years, gradually refining their techniques while enjoying the growing mythology surrounding the formations. [Today I Found Out+2thecroppie.com] todayifoundout.com two men started phenomenon crop circles planks wire Today I Found OutThe Story Behind the Crop Circle Phenomenon29 Mar 2016 — With his wife's blessing, Bower and Chorley continued making cr... The pair did not claim responsibility for every crop circle ever reported. Rather, they said they had created more than 200 formations between 1978 and 1991 and had produced virtually all of the well known English circles before the late 1980s, after which they believed copycat makers had begun creating their own designs. This distinction is often overlooked in later retellings , which sometimes exaggerate their confession into a claim that they had made every crop circle worldwide. [Wikipedia+2thecroppie.com] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Their confession also included practical details that strengthened its credibility. They described using little more than wooden planks attached to ropes to flatten crops, together with a simple wire sight fixed to a cap to maintain straight lines and symmetry. The methods were ordinary rather than technological, which was precisely why they proved so disruptive to paranormal explanations. [Today I Found Out] todayifoundout.com two men started phenomenon crop circles planks wire Today I Found OutThe Story Behind the Crop Circle Phenomenon29 Mar 2016 — With his wife's blessing, Bower and Chorley continued making cr... BBC1 'Painting Circles in the Corn' ( crop circles ) Jim Chorley... Channel: Jim Chorley Open on YouTube Why the demonstrated methods mattered The confession alone might have remained controversial had it not been accompanied by demonstration. Under journalistic observation , Bower and Chorley recreated a crop circle using the techniques they described. The resulting formation was then shown to Pat Delgado, one of the best known crop circle investigators and co author of Circular Evidence . Believing he was examining a newly discovered formation, Delgado reportedly judged it to possess characteristics that indicated a genuine unexplained event. Only afterwards was he told that the circle had been created by the two confessed hoaxers. [Los Angeles Times+2www2.nau.edu] latimes.com la xpm 1991 09 10 mn 2463 story Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed... This episode became one of the defining moments in the history of crop circle research because it demonstrated an important methodological problem. If experienced investigators could not reliably distinguish a freshly made human construction from one they regarded as authentic, then appearance alone could no longer serve as convincing evidence for extraordinary origins. The burden increasingly shifted from asking whether a formation looked mysterious to asking whether there was independent evidence that it could not have been produced by people. [Los Angeles Times] latimes.com la xpm 1991 09 10 mn 2463 story Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed... Equally significant was the revelation that complexity itself was not proof of non human agency. Until then, many arguments had relied on the assumption that large, geometrically precise patterns exceeded what two people could create overnight. Bower and Chorley's demonstration showed that careful planning, simple surveying methods and familiarity with the landscape could produce results that many observers had assumed required advanced technology or unknown forces. [Today I Found Out] todayifoundout.com two men started phenomenon crop circles planks wire Today I Found OutThe Story Behind the Crop Circle Phenomenon29 Mar 2016 — With his wife's blessing, Bower and Chorley continued making cr... Why the mystery survived The confession dramatically weakened claims that crop circles as a whole represented unexplained physical phenomena, but it did not persuade everyone. Some researchers argued that Bower and Chorley had explained only a portion of the known formations. Others pointed to increasingly elaborate pictograms that appeared after the confession, suggesting that these exceeded the abilities demonstrated by the two men. Critics of the confession also noted that crop circles had appeared outside Britain, beyond locations where Bower and Chorley had worked. [Today I Found Out] todayifoundout.com two men started phenomenon crop circles planks wire Today I Found OutThe Story Behind the Crop Circle Phenomenon29 Mar 2016 — With his wife's blessing, Bower and Chorley continued making cr... Supporters of the human origin explanation responded that these objections actually illustrated the confession's wider influence. Once the methods became publicly known, other artists and pranksters adopted and expanded them. Rather than disproving the confession, the growing diversity of crop circles could be understood as evidence of imitation. What had begun as a local prank had become an international form of environmental art, performance and media spectacle. [Historic Mysteries+2Smithsonian Magazine] historicmysteries.com Historic MysteriesDoug, Dave, and Crop Circles: The Men Who Conned...22 Apr 2023 — Bower and Chorley claimed to have made all crop circl... This shift also explains why crop circle debates became less about whether humans could make sophisticated formations and more about whether any individual case displayed evidence that genuinely ruled out human authorship. The discussion became narrower and more case specific instead of relying on the overall existence of crop circles as evidence for extraordinary causes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle How are Crop Circles Made? Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube How believers and investigators adapted afterward After 1991, crop circle research increasingly divided into two approaches. Skeptical investigators regarded the confession as establishing a default explanation: any new formation should be presumed human made unless compelling evidence suggested otherwise. They argued that demonstrated techniques, copycat activity and later commercial circle making made this the simplest explanation for most cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Believers, by contrast, refined their standards. Rather than treating every crop circle as potentially paranormal, they began distinguishing between ordinary human made formations and a smaller category of allegedly \"genuine\" circles. Proposed indicators included unusual plant changes, reported luminous phenomena or exceptional geometric precision. These claims remained controversial, but the important historical point is that they represented an adaptation to the challenge posed by"},{"title":"Aerial Image","url":"/aerial-image/","description":"Crop circles became media objects because they looked more complete from above than they did on the ground.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"Aerial Image Why Crop Circles Needed the Camera Aerial Image Aerial Image Why Crop Circles Needed the Camera Crop circles became media objects because they looked more complete from above than they did on the ground. Crop circles became media objects because they looked more complete from above than they did on the ground. dataset evidence Ground inspection versus aerial spectacle Newspapers, television and online image circulation Why photographs made each formation easier to believe and share forteana crop circles aerial image Introduction Crop circles became a mass media phenomenon not because most people walked through them, but because millions saw them from above. The decisive image was not a single iconic photograph but a new way of looking at farmland: aerial photographs revealed complete geometric designs that were impossible to appreciate from ground level. Newspapers , television programmes, books and, later, websites transformed flattened crops into striking graphic symbols that appeared almost abstract when viewed vertically. That shift in perspective was central to the mystery. A person standing inside a formation often saw little more than bent stalks, whereas an aircraft or helicopter camera revealed circles, spirals and elaborate pictograms that looked intentional, mysterious and visually compelling. The crop circle story therefore spread through images as much as through eyewitness testimony, with photography shaping both belief and scepticism. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Ground inspection versus aerial spectacle One of the defining features of crop circles is that they were designed—or at least experienced most effectively—from above. Early investigators walking through fields measured stem damage, searched for footprints and examined plant growth, but these close inspections could not easily convey the overall pattern. The dramatic effect emerged only when photographers climbed hills, used ladders, flew in light aircraft or hired helicopters. This difference changed how the phenomenon was interpreted. From the ground: Individual paths and flattened stems appeared fragmented. Curves were difficult to recognise. The scale of a formation was almost impossible to judge. From the air: Simple circles became perfect geometric figures. Complex formations appeared as coherent designs. Their apparent precision encouraged speculation that they required unusual methods or intelligence. The aerial viewpoint therefore supplied the visual evidence that made crop circles memorable. Without it, many formations would have looked like ordinary patches of damaged crop rather than carefully composed images. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube Newspapers, television and the circulation of images The rapid growth of crop circle reports during the 1980s closely matched expanding media coverage. Local newspapers initially treated the circles as rural curiosities, but aerial photographs gave editors something visually irresistible: clean, symmetrical patterns filling an entire page. Television amplified this effect. Helicopter footage allowed viewers to glide above newly discovered formations, revealing shapes that unfolded gradually as the camera climbed. Documentary programmes repeatedly used this cinematic reveal, reinforcing the impression that each new discovery represented something extraordinary rather than flattened grain. Books devoted to crop circles relied heavily on aerial photography. Collections of full page overhead images encouraged readers to compare designs across different years, creating the impression of an evolving visual language. Researchers such as Colin Andrews and Pat Delgado assembled catalogues in which photographs became the primary evidence, often more influential than written descriptions or field measurements. By the early 1990s, the visual archive itself had become one of the phenomenon's main attractions. [Wikipedia+2Amazon] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Why photographs made the mystery easier to believe Aerial photographs did more than document formations—they changed how people judged them. Several psychological factors made the images persuasive: Completeness. A photograph showed the entire design instantly, avoiding the fragmented experience of walking through a field. Symmetry. Humans naturally interpret highly regular geometric forms as intentional, making circles and intricate pictograms appear purposeful. Scale. Seeing patterns hundreds of metres across encouraged assumptions that they would have been difficult to construct secretly. Distance. The overhead viewpoint removed practical clues such as tractor tracks, entry routes or disturbed crop around the edges, simplifying the scene into an elegant graphic image. These characteristics did not prove unusual origins, but they made unusual explanations feel more plausible. The camera compressed a messy agricultural landscape into a striking visual symbol that could be understood in seconds. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle The image outlived the field Crop circles are unusually temporary. Farmers often harvested the crop within days or weeks, and weather gradually erased the flattened patterns. Photography solved this problem by preserving formations long after they disappeared. As a result, the public often encountered crop circles only as photographs. A formation that existed for a few days could circulate internationally for years in magazines, calendars, documentaries and later on internet galleries. The image became more durable than the physical object itself. This also encouraged comparison. Readers could place photographs from different years side by side, creating narratives about increasing complexity or apparent symbolism that would have been difficult to appreciate through isolated visits to individual fields. The archive of aerial imagery therefore became an important part of the mythology surrounding crop circles. [Amazon] amazon.co.uk dp AmazonRound in Circles: Physicists, Poltergeists, Pranksters And ...One day, in the summer of 1991, the author ventured to Wiltshire and... From helicopters to drones Modern drone photography has made overhead views inexpensive and immediate, but it follows the same visual logic established during the 1980s. Today, newly discovered formations are typically photographed from the air within hours and shared globally through news websites and social media. Ironically, this technological change has also made human creation easier to understand. Drone footage frequently reveals planning marks, construction methods and the relationship between formations and surrounding farm tracks, offering perspectives that earlier still photographs often concealed. At the same time, the dramatic overhead view remains the preferred way of presenting crop circles, demonstrating that their cultural power still depends on aerial imagery rather than the experience of standing in the field itself. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Crop Circle Mystery National Geographic Channel: National Geographic Open on YouTube Why the camera mattered more than any single photograph There was no single aerial photograph that suddenly made crop circles famous. Instead, the phenomenon became famous because aerial photography consistently transformed ordinary farmland into compelling visual puzzles. Each new overhead image reinforced the last, allowing newspapers, television and later the internet to present crop circles as instantly recognisable symbols of mystery. In that sense, the camera was not merely recording the phenomenon—it was one of the mechanisms that created its cultural impact. Crop circles became enduring objects of Fortean fascination because they were both landscapes and pictures, and the picture was often the version that people remembered. [Wikipedia+2National Geographic] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Crop Circles I Didn't Know That National Geographic UK Channel: National Geographic UK Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Crop Circles Needed the Camera. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Provides critical thinking tools for evaluating extraordinary photographic claims. See on Amazon Book Crop circles By Colin Andrews Emphasises visual evidence and interpretation of formations. See on Amazon Book Circular evidence By Pat Delgado, Colin Andrews Explores the rise of crop circles through photographs and field documentation. See on Amazon Book Mysteries By Colin Wilson First published 1978. Subjects: Occultism, Parapsychology, Supernatural, Curiosities and wonders. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The Demon Haunted World Crop circles Circular evidence As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases."},{"title":"Belief Survives","url":"/belief-survives/","description":"Belief survived because each new formation could be treated as a fresh case rather than disproved by earlier fakes.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"Belief Survives Why Exposure Did Not Kill Belief Belief Survives Belief Survives Why Exposure Did Not Kill Belief Belief survived because each new formation could be treated as a fresh case rather than disproved by earlier fakes. Belief survived because each new formation could be treated as a fresh case rather than disproved by earlier fakes. mechanism The genuine remainder argument Experience, atmosphere and claimed anomalies Why hoaxes can contaminate a mystery without ending it forteana crop circles belief survives Introduction The exposure of famous crop circle hoaxes did not end belief in the phenomenon because the debate quickly shifted from whether some circles were faked to whether all circles were faked . Within Fortean culture, the confession by Doug Bower and Dave Chorley in 1991 was widely accepted as evidence that many formations were human made, but it was not universally accepted as evidence against every reported case. Instead, believers increasingly argued that successful hoaxes merely complicated the mystery by hiding genuinely unexplained formations among deliberate imitations. This \"genuine remainder\" position, combined with the emotional impact of visiting crop circles in person and the continuing appearance of new formations, allowed belief to persist despite repeated exposures. [Los Angeles Times+2Wikipedia] latimes.com la xpm 1991 09 10 mn 2463 story Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed... The genuine remainder argument The most important mechanism behind the survival of belief was the distinction between known hoaxes and unresolved cases . Rather than denying that fraud existed, many enthusiasts incorporated hoaxes into their worldview. They argued that exposing one fake no more disproved every crop circle than exposing one forged painting disproved the existence of genuine art. Each newly discovered formation was therefore treated as a separate case requiring its own investigation. This reasoning had several consequences: Confessions explained only the formations that particular makers admitted producing. More elaborate or technically impressive designs could be presented as different from the circles attributed to known hoaxers. Reports of unusual lights, equipment failures or allegedly abnormal plant characteristics were treated as independent evidence rather than being dismissed alongside admitted fakes. From within this framework, hoax exposure actually narrowed the field of investigation instead of closing it. Believers accepted that some circles were human made while maintaining that a smaller number remained unexplained. [ResearchGate+2Smithsonian Magazine] researchgate.net Finding God in Grain: Crop Circles, Rationality, and...September 1, 2018 — Crop circles, which are intricate geometric patte... Published: September 1, 2018 Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube Experience often outweighed the confession For many visitors, the attraction of crop circles was not based solely on newspaper stories about mysterious patterns. It came from direct personal experience . The atmosphere of the sites Standing inside a large formation could produce a powerful impression regardless of its origin. The geometry, scale and setting in the English countryside encouraged interpretations involving sacred landscapes, prehistoric monuments and altered states of awareness. For visitors already interested in Fortean mysteries, these experiences often carried more psychological weight than later reports about hoaxers. Researchers who have interviewed believers have found that crop circles frequently function as places of spiritual experience rather than simply puzzles requiring scientific explanation. Personal meaning therefore became resistant to external refutation because it depended on subjective experience rather than solely on claims about physical causation. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Finding God in Grain: Crop Circles, Rationality, and...September 1, 2018 — Crop circles, which are intricate geometric patte... Published: September 1, 2018 Claimed anomalies Believers also continued to point to reports of unusual plant damage, magnetic effects, animal behaviour or strange lights near some formations. Although these claims remain disputed and have not convinced the scientific community, they allowed supporters to argue that the mystery involved more than flattened crops alone. Whether or not such reports withstand critical examination, their existence helped separate particular formations from admitted hoaxes in the minds of believers. A confession explaining one field did not automatically explain another where witnesses believed additional phenomena had occurred. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Why new formations kept resetting the debate Crop circles possess an unusual feature compared with many paranormal claims: they continually produce fresh physical examples. Every growing season brought new formations, new photographs and new eyewitness accounts. As a result, public attention repeatedly shifted away from earlier exposures toward the latest appearance. This cycle encouraged several recurring questions: Was this formation different from earlier ones? Could people really have produced such complexity overnight? Were all reported witnesses mistaken? If many circles were hoaxes, who made this particular one? Because every new formation could be evaluated independently, previous debunkings rarely settled discussion permanently. Instead, they became part of the background against which each fresh case was argued. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian MagazineCrop Circles: The Art of the Hoax15 Dec 2009 — Crop circles are a lens through which we can explore the nature and ap... How the Crop Circle Phenomenon Got Started Channel: Today I Found Out Open on YouTube Why hoaxes can strengthen rather than end a mystery Paradoxically, widespread hoaxing can sometimes reinforce a Fortean mystery instead of destroying it. If numerous people imitate an unexplained phenomenon, observers may conclude that the original phenomenon was significant enough to inspire imitation. In crop circle culture this produced an enduring distinction between copycats and authentic formations . The same process appears in other anomalous traditions, where believers routinely separate deliberate fraud from what they regard as genuinely puzzling reports. Once this distinction becomes accepted within a community, exposing additional hoaxes has diminishing impact because fraud is already expected as part of the phenomenon. From a critical perspective, however, this creates a methodological problem. Every disconfirmed case can be removed from consideration while the remaining cases continue to be described as unexplained. Critics argue that this makes the hypothesis increasingly difficult to test because the category of \"genuine\" examples changes whenever new evidence appears. Anomalistic psychology identifies similar patterns across many forms of paranormal belief, where contradictory evidence often leads to refinement rather than abandonment of the underlying belief. [Wikipedia+2PMC] Wikipedia Anomalistic psychology Anomalistic psychology A lesson beyond crop circles The crop circle story illustrates a broader feature of Fortean belief systems: exposing fraud does not necessarily eliminate a mystery if participants already distinguish between deception and authentic anomalies. The 1991 confessions by Doug Bower and Dave Chorley fundamentally changed public understanding of crop circles by demonstrating that impressive formations could be produced with simple tools and careful planning. Yet they did not eliminate belief because many enthusiasts interpreted the confession as explaining some circles rather than all of them. [Los Angeles Times] latimes.com la xpm 1991 09 10 mn 2463 story Los Angeles Times'Crop Circles' Their Prank, 2 Britons Say: Hoax10 Sept 1991 — British painters Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, 62, claimed... In that sense, the persistence of crop circle belief was driven less by denial of hoaxes than by a continuing conviction that the remaining unexplained cases deserved separate consideration. Whether that distinction is persuasive depends largely on how one evaluates the available evidence, but it explains why exposure alone proved insufficient to end one of the best known modern Fortean mysteries. BBC1 'Painting Circles in the Corn' ( crop circles ) Jim Chorley... Channel: Jim Chorley Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Exposure Did Not Kill Belief. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Why People Believe Weird Things By Michael Shermer Examines mechanisms behind persistent paranormal belief. See on Amazon Book The Demon"},{"title":"Land Art","url":"/land-art/","description":"Some makers treated crop circles as art that only worked when viewers were not sure whether it was art at all.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"Land Art When a Hoax Becomes Land Art Land Art Land Art When a Hoax Becomes Land Art Some makers treated crop circles as art that only worked when viewers were not sure whether it was art at all. Some makers treated crop circles as art that only worked when viewers were not sure whether it was art at all. mechanism Why anonymity gives the work its charge The roles of farmer, maker, believer and tourist How commissioned circles changed the illusion forteana crop circles land art Introduction Crop circles occupy an unusual place in modern culture because they can be understood simultaneously as hoaxes , performances and works of land art. Unlike most public artworks, they gained much of their power from uncertainty. A crop circle that was openly announced as an artwork became an impressive geometric design in a field; a crop circle that appeared anonymously overnight invited speculation, investigation and belief. For many makers, that ambiguity was not an accidental side effect but part of the artwork itself. The audience was not simply looking at a pattern but participating in a social drama about evidence, authorship and imagination. This makes crop circles a distinctive form of anonymous land art, where the disappearance of the artist was as important as the visible design. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Why anonymity gives the work its charge Traditional land art often identifies its creator, allowing viewers to appreciate technique, intention and artistic reputation. Crop circles invert that relationship. The maker deliberately withdraws, leaving only the finished image and the mystery surrounding it. That absence changes how the work is experienced. Instead of asking whether a design is beautiful or technically accomplished, observers first ask who made it and how. The artwork becomes inseparable from competing explanations. Believers may interpret it as evidence of unknown forces, sceptics as evidence of human ingenuity, while others simply admire the visual achievement regardless of origin. The uncertainty itself becomes part of the aesthetic experience . [The Guardian] theguardian.com The Guardianwere crop circles actually great art? Art and design5 May 2022 — Crop circles could never be commodified but should rightfully be recognised as works of equal value and impo... Published: May 2022 This mechanism distinguishes crop circles from conventional public sculpture or environmental art: The artist is invisible. Recognition is postponed or may never occur. The artwork is temporary. Crops continue growing or are harvested within weeks. The primary audience often encounters photographs first. The aerial image becomes the lasting artwork, while the physical installation disappears. Interpretation is open ended. Viewers cannot rely on gallery labels, catalogues or artist statements. Because authorship remains hidden, viewers often project their own beliefs onto the work. In this sense, the crop circle functions less like a static object than a performance unfolding through media reports, investigation and debate. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube The roles of farmer, maker, believer and tourist Crop circles create an unusual network of participants, each giving the work a different meaning. The maker designs and executes the formation, often under cover of darkness using careful planning, simple tools or, in later years, surveying techniques and GPS assisted layout for complex geometry. Success depends partly on remaining anonymous long enough for discovery. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle The farmer owns the canvas but rarely volunteers it. Crop circles therefore occupy an uncomfortable position between artistic intervention and property damage. Some landowners regarded them as costly vandalism because flattened crops reduced yields and attracted trespassers. Others accepted visitor access or even benefited from donations and increased tourism , creating an uneasy economic relationship between artwork and agriculture. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle The believer completes part of the artwork by treating the formation as evidence rather than design. Theories involving Earth energies, extraterrestrials or unknown natural forces transformed anonymous geometry into a site of pilgrimage. Whether or not these interpretations were intended by the makers, they became inseparable from the phenomenon's cultural life. [Fair Observer] fairobserver.com crop circles elusive land art Fair ObserverCrop Circles: Elusive Land Art20 May 2012 — Crop circles: the infamous and strange formations created on crop fields, where... Published: May 2012 The tourist experiences the formation as both spectacle and destination. During the peak years of the phenomenon, visitors travelled to southern England not only to inspect the circles but to participate in a landscape already associated with Avebury, Stonehenge and other prehistoric monuments. Helicopter flights, guidebooks, walking tours and local businesses became part of the ecosystem surrounding the artwork. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle These overlapping roles mean that a crop circle is never just a visual object. It is also an event whose meaning depends on how different participants respond to it. When the prank became an artistic practice The 1991 confession by Doug Bower and Dave Chorley did not end crop circles. Instead, it shifted attention from mysterious origins towards creative practice. Bower and Chorley demonstrated that sophisticated formations could be produced with modest equipment and careful planning. Yet their revelation also encouraged others to treat crop circle making as a legitimate artistic discipline rather than merely an elaborate joke. Instead of collapsing after the confession, the phenomenon evolved into a community of increasingly ambitious makers producing larger and more intricate geometric compositions. Physicist Richard Taylor later observed that the exposure of early hoaxes helped inspire a second generation of crop artists rather than extinguishing the practice. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Groups such as Circlemakers openly embraced crop circles as environmental art while still appreciating the theatrical value of anonymity. Members including John Lundberg and Rod Dickinson combined artistic experimentation with commentary on media, belief and spectacle. Their work blurred distinctions between conceptual art, performance, practical geometry and cultural prank. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia John Lundberg John Lundberg This evolution complicates the common assumption that \"hoax\" and \"art\" are mutually exclusive. A work can deliberately deceive about its authorship while remaining artistically ambitious in its design, execution and cultural impact. Crop Circles Can’t Sleep? Learn About One of History’s Strangest Mysteries Channel: I Can't Sleep Podcast Open on YouTube How commissioned circles changed the illusion As the reputation of crop circle makers grew, businesses and media organisations began hiring them to produce formations deliberately. Commercial commissions marked an important turning point. Instead of mysterious overnight discoveries, some circles became advertisements, album artwork or promotional events. Circlemakers produced formations for musicians, television programmes and commercial brands, while documentaries commissioned makers to demonstrate how complex circles could be created. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia John Lundberg John Lundberg This altered the artistic mechanism in several ways. First, the audience no longer wondered whether humans had made the formation; that question had already been answered. Attention shifted to technical skill and visual impact. Second, commissioned work weakened the delicate relationship between mystery and interpretation. Once a sponsor, client or camera crew was involved, the circle became closer to conventional environmental design than anonymous land art. Finally, commercialisation exposed a tension within the movement itself. Some observers argued that commissioned circles demonstrated the makers' craftsmanship, while others felt that removing uncertainty stripped away the very quality that had made crop circles culturally distinctive. Anonymous circles invited stories, investigations and competing explanations; branded circles primarily promoted products or events. [The Guardian+2Wikipedia] theguardian.com The Guardianwere crop circles actually great art? Art and design5 May 2022 — Crop circles could never be commodified but should rightfully be recognised as works of equal value and impo... Published: May 2022 A critique of anonymous land art Seeing crop circles as land art does not resolve their ethical problems. Anonymous creation leaves unresolved questions about consent, ownership and responsibility. Unlike"},{"title":"Circle Tools","url":"/circle-tools/","description":"Ropes, planks and careful planning can turn a dark field into a precise temporary drawing surface.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"Circle Tools How Simple Tools Made Impressive Circles Circle Tools Circle Tools How Simple Tools Made Impressive Circles Ropes, planks and careful planning can turn a dark field into a precise temporary drawing surface. Ropes, planks and careful planning can turn a dark field into a precise temporary drawing surface. mechanism The plank, rope and sighting device method Why geometry can look more mysterious than it is What tool based making explains and does not explain forteana crop circles circle tools Introduction One of the most persistent questions in the crop circle debate is whether ordinary people could really produce vast, geometrically precise formations in a single night. Decades of demonstrations, investigations and admissions by crop circle makers indicate that they could. The key was not sophisticated machinery but careful planning combined with remarkably simple equipment: a wooden plank for flattening crops, ropes for measuring curves, and basic sighting devices for keeping long lines straight. These methods did not rely on mysterious technology. Instead, they applied familiar principles of surveying, geometry and teamwork to an open landscape. [cdn.centerforinquiry.org+2Skeptical Inquirer] cdn.centerforinquiry.org Circular Reasoning:by JOE NICKELL — Joe Nickell has written about crop circles in his book Real life X Files (2001)... Understanding these tools does not explain every story told about crop circles, nor does it settle every claim surrounding individual formations. What it does explain is how impressive patterns that appear almost impossible at first glance can be produced by skilled people working methodically in darkness. The plank, rope and sighting device method The classic method associated with early British crop circle makers, particularly Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, relied on equipment that would not look out of place in a garden shed. Contemporary accounts consistently describe four essential elements: A wooden plank , usually around four feet (1.2 metres) long, used to press crops down evenly without uprooting them. A rope or length of string , attached to a central point to define accurate circles. A simple sighting aid , famously a baseball cap fitted with a wire loop, allowing the maker to align distant landmarks and walk straight. A pre drawn plan , often sketched on paper before entering the field. [www2.nau.edu+2Wikipedia] nau.edu Crop Circles NAUAt the center of the intended site, Bower held one end of the string. The other end was attached to the plank, held horizontally at kn... The plank was not dragged behind the makers. Instead, it was laid across the standing crop, and body weight was applied by stepping onto it. This bent the stems in a consistent direction while reducing breakage, especially when the plants were naturally flexible because of moisture or recent rain. The operator then lifted the plank forward and repeated the process in overlapping steps, much like laying temporary footprints. [www2.nau.edu] nau.edu Crop Circles NAUAt the center of the intended site, Bower held one end of the string. The other end was attached to the plank, held horizontally at kn... For simple circles, one person could remain at the centre holding the rope while another walked around at the required radius. More elaborate designs simply repeated this principle with multiple centres, different rope lengths and carefully measured transitions between arcs. Straight lines, often assumed to require sophisticated surveying equipment, could instead be maintained by sighting along a distant tree, telegraph pole or hill using the wire fixed to the cap. Once the initial reference line existed, additional geometric elements could be measured from it much like a surveyor laying out a construction site. [www2.nau.edu] nau.edu Crop Circles NAUAt the center of the intended site, Bower held one end of the string. The other end was attached to the plank, held horizontally at kn... The QI Crop Circle QI Series 8 Episode 3 Hoaxes Preview BBC Channel: BBC Open on YouTube Why geometry can look more mysterious than it is The apparent complexity of many crop circles comes less from difficult construction than from visual perception. Viewed from ground level, participants see only a narrow section of the design. From the air, however, separate arcs suddenly combine into symmetrical figures. Human observers naturally underestimate how much structure can emerge from repeating a few simple geometric operations. Several factors amplify this effect: Repeated circles of different sizes create intricate looking patterns from only a handful of measurements. Rotational symmetry makes small positioning errors difficult to detect from above. Empty standing crop acts as negative space, allowing simple flattened paths to define elaborate shapes. Large scale encourages viewers to assume equally large technical difficulty. Investigators and sceptics have repeatedly demonstrated that formations appearing highly sophisticated in aerial photographs can be recreated through patient measurement rather than advanced technology. Teams organised by investigators including Joe Nickell and Benjamin Radford have produced convincing crop circle designs using ordinary materials in only a few hours. [Skeptical Inquirer+2cdn.centerforinquiry.org] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical InquirerCrop Circles: A Not So Convincing CaseIn fact I have personally created crop circle designs, including with Joe Nickell... The important point is that geometry scales efficiently. Doubling the size of a design does not double its conceptual complexity. Once reference points are established, very large patterns can be extended through straightforward repetition. How the Crop Circle Phenomenon Got Started Channel: Today I Found Out Open on YouTube Planning mattered more than specialised equipment Accounts from crop circle makers consistently emphasise preparation over improvisation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Rather than inventing a design while standing in a field, successful teams typically: Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube 1. Produced a scale drawing. 2. Identified key measurement points. 3. Selected entry routes, often existing tractor tramlines to minimise visible footprints. 4. Assigned individual responsibilities before work began. 5. Executed the design systematically rather than artistically improvising. www2.nau.edu+2Skeptical Inquirer This approach resembles laying out a temporary engineering project more than spontaneous artistic performance. Time spent planning often reduced the amount of time required inside the field itself. The location also mattered. Fields close to roads, with predictable tramlines and accessible boundaries, made movement easier while increasing the likelihood that the finished work would be discovered quickly. This pattern matches broader geographical analyses showing that crop circles cluster near accessible landscapes and well known heritage areas rather than appearing randomly. Wikipedia Why people underestimated what these tools could achieve Many early observers judged crop circles primarily from the finished image rather than the construction process. Several assumptions contributed to this misjudgement: Night work seemed impossible. In reality, experienced makers relied on moonlight, familiarity with the field and careful preparation rather than bright illumination. Christianity Today Precision implied advanced equipment. Basic surveying techniques have produced accurate layouts for centuries using little more than ropes and fixed reference points. Large size suggested heavy machinery. Flattening crops requires surprisingly little force when stems are bent rather than crushed. No witnesses meant no makers. Crop circles were often completed in relatively quiet rural locations during short summer nights, making observation unlikely despite nearby roads. The famous 1991 public demonstration following Bower and Chorley's confession illustrated this problem. Even after seeing circles reproduced with simple equipment, some enthusiasts continued to reject the explanation because the finished appearance seemed incompatible with the modest tools involved. Wikipedia What tool based making explains and does not explain The plank and rope method explains many characteristics commonly cited as mysterious. It accounts for: clean circular outlines; repeated geometric motifs; consistent flattening of crops; overnight appearance; increasing design complexity over time as makers refined their techniques; similarities among formations produced in different countries after methods became widely known. Skeptical Inquirer+2Wikipedia However, it does not automatically resolve every claim associated with crop circles. Individual reports sometimes include disputed observations concerning plant damage, unusual lights or eyewitness testimony."},{"title":"Wiltshire","url":"/wiltshire/","description":"Crop circles clustered around heritage landscapes where access, visibility and atmosphere made them more powerful.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crop Circles","level":3,"text":"Wiltshire Why Wiltshire Became Crop Circle Country Wiltshire Wiltshire Why Wiltshire Became Crop Circle Country Crop circles clustered around heritage landscapes where access, visibility and atmosphere made them more powerful. Crop circles clustered around heritage landscapes where access, visibility and atmosphere made them more powerful. population context Clustering near Avebury and Silbury Hill Roads, population and visibility as practical clues How ancient monuments changed the meaning of new patterns forteana crop circles wiltshire Introduction Wiltshire became the symbolic heart of the modern crop circle phenomenon not because it possesses mysterious physical properties, but because it combines an unusually dense concentration of prehistoric monuments with open farmland, good road access, and a landscape already rich in archaeological and spiritual associations. As crop circle reports multiplied from the late 1970s onwards, many of the most famous formations appeared around Avebury, Silbury Hill and the Vale of Pewsey. That geographical pattern shaped public interpretation as much as the designs themselves. A geometric formation beside one of Europe's greatest prehistoric ceremonial landscapes naturally invited speculation about ancient knowledge, earth energies and forgotten traditions, even when the strongest evidence pointed towards deliberate human creation. Research into where crop circles appeared suggests that practical factors such as accessibility, visibility and tourism explain much of this clustering, while the monuments themselves amplified the cultural meaning of each new formation. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circlesThere is, then, a long history of landscape artistry in Wiltshire that preceded the crop ci... Clustering around Avebury and Silbury Hill The landscape surrounding Avebury is unlike almost anywhere else in Britain. Within a relatively compact area stand the great Avebury henge and stone circles, Silbury Hill, the West Kennet Avenue, the West Kennet Long Barrow, Windmill Hill and numerous burial mounds. Together they form the northern part of the Stonehenge and Avebury UNESCO World Heritage Site, an archaeological landscape that has attracted researchers, visitors and alternative spiritual movements for decades. [Bournemouth University] bournemouth.ac.uk Bournemouth UniversityAvebury LandscapesExtensive geophysical surveys of Avebury World Heritage Site address big questions, such as the s... When crop circles became widely reported, this same area emerged as one of their principal locations. Rather than being scattered randomly across southern England, formations repeatedly appeared in fields surrounding these monuments, particularly across the chalk downs of north Wiltshire and the Vale of Pewsey. Famous sites such as Alton Barnes, Milk Hill, East Field and fields around Silbury Hill became familiar names in crop circle literature because new formations appeared there year after year. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circlesThere is, then, a long history of landscape artistry in Wiltshire that preceded the crop ci... This concentration helped create a powerful visual association. Visitors could stand among Neolithic monuments before walking a short distance to inspect a newly discovered crop formation. The proximity encouraged many observers to see the modern designs as part of a continuous sacred landscape, regardless of whether there was any historical connection between prehistoric builders and contemporary circle makers. Avebury The Largest Stone Circle in the World 4K Aerial Film Megalithomania Channel: MegalithomaniaUK Open on YouTube Roads, population and visibility offer a simpler explanation One of the strongest geographical studies of English crop circles was conducted by geographer Jeremy Northcote using Geographic Information Systems (GIS). Rather than asking whether crop circles clustered around ancient monuments alone, the research examined whether their locations also matched ordinary human geography. The study found that reported formations were significantly concentrated: near major roads and transport routes; within reach of medium density population centres; close to well known heritage monuments such as Avebury and Stonehenge; in landscapes that were easy to access and highly visible. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circlesThere is, then, a long history of landscape artistry in Wiltshire that preceded the crop ci... These findings matter because the same characteristics would benefit human creators. A field that is easy to reach after dark, visible from nearby roads or hills, and likely to attract photographers the following morning provides the greatest audience for an elaborate design. Wiltshire satisfies all of these conditions. Much of the county consists of large cereal fields with gentle rolling topography that allows aerial photography and distant viewing. Tourism linked to prehistoric monuments also ensures rapid discovery and extensive media coverage. In other words, the geography that made the region archaeologically famous also made it an ideal stage for large scale landscape art. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circlesThere is, then, a long history of landscape artistry in Wiltshire that preceded the crop ci... Why ancient monuments changed the meaning of new patterns The monuments themselves did not simply provide nearby landmarks. They influenced how people interpreted crop circles. Avebury and Silbury Hill have long occupied an unusual place in British culture. Archaeologists investigate them as remarkable Neolithic engineering projects, while folklore , ley line theories and alternative spiritual traditions have invested them with additional symbolic meaning. By the late twentieth century, the area already attracted visitors interested in earth mysteries, prehistoric astronomy and sacred landscapes. Against that background, a freshly discovered crop formation rarely appeared as an isolated agricultural curiosity. Instead, many visitors interpreted it through existing beliefs about the landscape. A geometric pattern beside Silbury Hill seemed more suggestive than an identical design in an anonymous field elsewhere because it appeared within a setting already regarded as ancient and mysterious. [Bournemouth University] bournemouth.ac.uk Bournemouth UniversityAvebury LandscapesExtensive geophysical surveys of Avebury World Heritage Site address big questions, such as the s... This illustrates an important point within Fortean studies. The location itself contributed to the phenomenon's power. The same flattened crop pattern acquired different meanings depending on where it appeared. Archaeological context, local legend and visual spectacle combined to create an experience that photographs alone could not fully explain. What's Making England's Crop Circles? Channel: The Department of Information Open on YouTube The landscape encouraged both art and belief Wiltshire's prehistoric landscape also fostered a community of enthusiasts, researchers, photographers and artists. During the main crop circle seasons of the 1990s and 2000s, visitors travelled specifically to the county in anticipation of new formations, and local exhibitions, guided visits and study groups developed around the annual appearance of circles. The phenomenon became intertwined with seasonal tourism in the Avebury and Pewsey areas, reinforcing Wiltshire's identity as \"crop circle country.\" [Visit Wiltshire] visitwiltshire.co.uk Visit WiltshireCrop Circle Visitor Centre & ExhibitionCrop Circle Visitor Centre & Exhibition in Honeystreet is the meeting point for all... This concentration created a feedback loop: spectacular locations attracted more visitors; greater visitor numbers increased reporting and photography; media attention enhanced Wiltshire's reputation; artists knew their work would receive maximum exposure if placed near famous monuments. The resulting cycle helps explain why the county remained disproportionately prominent in crop circle culture even after public demonstrations showed that many elaborate formations could be produced by organised teams. What the clustering tells us The geographical concentration of crop circles around Wiltshire's prehistoric monuments is real, but its interpretation depends on the question being asked. For those seeking paranormal explanations, the proximity to ancient ceremonial sites has often been presented as evidence that unusual forces operate within the landscape. By contrast, geographical research shows that the same clustering is consistent with ordinary human factors: accessibility, visibility, tourism and cultural significance. The monuments did not necessarily generate the circles; they made them more meaningful, more photogenic and more likely to be noticed. [siue.edu] siue.edu Spatial distribution of England's crop circlesThere"},{"title":"Cryptids","url":"/cryptids/","description":"Cryptid reports turn unknown animals, folklore, misidentification, and wishful thinking into one evidence problem.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Cryptids When Does a Creature Report Become a Cryptid? Cryptids Cryptids When Does a Creature Report Become a Cryptid? Cryptid reports turn unknown animals, folklore, misidentification, and wishful thinking into one evidence problem. Cryptid reports turn unknown animals, folklore, misidentification, and wishful thinking into one evidence problem. population context Known animals and mistakes Repeated reports and local lore What evidence would change the case forteana cryptids Introduction A creature report becomes a cryptid when it is treated as more than a single strange sighting but less than an established animal: a possible population, repeatedly reported in a place or tradition, yet still lacking the specimen, clear image, genetic sequence, or ecological trace that would move it into ordinary natural history. That is why cryptids sit so neatly inside Forteana . They are not simply “monsters”; they are evidence problems at the edge of zoology, folklore, misidentification , local knowledge, wishful thinking, and the real fact that some animals are genuinely elusive. The boundary matters because natural history has, on rare occasions, absorbed animals once known to outsiders mainly through reports, skins, carcasses, tracks, or local accounts. The okapi was described for Western science in 1901 after skins and skulls reached zoologists, while the coelacanth became famous after a living lineage thought absent from the modern world was recognised from a 1938 catch off South Africa. But those examples do not make every cryptid plausible. They show the opposite lesson as well: natural history changes its mind when ambiguous reports become durable, examinable evidence. [ZSL+2Royal Museum for Central Africa] zsl.org history of zsl The History of ZSLOctober 31, 2022 — 1901. The shy okapi is first discovered by the western world by ZSL Fellow Sir Harry Johnston, ex... Published: October 31, 2022 Why cryptids belong on the boundary Cryptozoology is usually described as the search for hidden, disputed, or unverified animals. Its Fortean interest lies in the way it borrows the language of natural history while often depending on the materials that science finds weakest: eyewitness memory, repeated anecdotes, local reputation, blurred images, casts, hairs without secure provenance, and stories that have changed through retelling. Scholars and critics have therefore treated cryptozoology as a fringe or pseudoscientific field, not as a normal branch of zoology, because the existence of its target animals is usually not supported by reproducible physical evidence. [University of Guelph Journals+2ResearchGate] journal.lib.uoguelph.ca Open source on uoguelph.ca. The category is nevertheless more interesting than a simple “true or false” test. A cryptid claim can point to at least four different things: An unknown or poorly known animal , especially in remote, deep, forested, nocturnal, or politically difficult environments. A known animal in the wrong interpretive frame , such as a bear, seal, eel, shark, owl, deer, escaped exotic pet, or unusually large individual. A cultural animal , where a named creature organises fear, memory, place identity, warnings, humour, tourism , or local belonging. A bad evidence chain , where a weak sighting becomes a stronger story through repetition, media framing, selective memory, hoaxing, or the loss of original data. That mixture is why cryptids are a boundary problem rather than merely a zoological one. Zoology asks whether a breeding population exists. Folklore asks how a story functions and travels. Forteana asks why the report remains unsettled: why people keep seeing something, why the evidence never quite closes the case, and why the same case can be read as discovery, mistake, legend, or desire. Known animals, honest mistakes, and the monster shaped gap Many cryptid reports start with a real animal. The Fortean question is what happens during the conversion from animal encounter to extraordinary claim. Distance, darkness, fear, expectation, poor scale cues, water distortion, decay, and local legend can all turn an ordinary creature into a stranger one. This does not require witnesses to be foolish or dishonest. It only requires a difficult observation and a ready made category into which the mind can place it. The Loch Ness Monster is the cleanest modern example of how a famous cryptid can become a test case for improved evidence. In 2018, a University of Otago led team took hundreds of environmental DNA samples from Loch Ness, looking for biological traces shed into the water by animals. The public framing leaned into the monster legend, but the scientific result was more modest and more useful: the study found no evidence for large unknown reptiles, plesiosaurs, sharks, catfish, or sturgeon, while finding a large amount of eel DNA. The researchers did not prove a giant eel was responsible for every sighting; they showed that a biological explanation involving eels fitted the evidence better than a surviving prehistoric monster. [University of Otago+2University of Otago] otago.ac.nz otago scientist reveals loch ness monster could be a giant eel otago scientist reveals loch ness monster could be a giant eel Sea serpent cases show a similar pattern. A decomposing basking shark can lose its lower jaw and gill region, leaving a small looking head, long spine, and trailing tissue that resemble a marine reptile carcass. This “pseudo plesiosaur” problem has repeatedly complicated sea monster claims, because a dead animal changes shape as it decays. A 2026 report on the Canadian “Cadborosaurus” tradition again highlighted how old photographs, missing specimens, and local interpretation can keep a carcass case alive long after the original material has vanished. [The Guardian] theguardian.com The Guardian Shark or sea monster? The Canadian marine mystery that still intrigues experts 90 years on Experts now believe the carcass was likely a decomposing basking shark, known to transform significantly during decay, often appearing as... Hair evidence has also become less forgiving. In 2014, Bryan Sykes and colleagues published a genetic analysis of hair samples attributed to yeti, Bigfoot, sasquatch, almasty, and other “anomalous primates”. Most samples that produced DNA matched known animals, including bears, horses, cows, canids, deer, humans, and other familiar species; some failed to produce usable sequences. The paper did not end belief in wild hominoids, but it changed the evidential stakes. A hair is no longer persuasive because it looks odd in a photograph or comes with a dramatic story. It must survive chain of custody questions, contamination checks, and comparison with reference databases. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. The lesson is not that all witnesses are wrong. It is that a natural history claim needs evidence that remains informative after the story is stripped away. A footprint cast without a clear trackway, a blurry animal crossing a road, a night sound in a forest, or a carcass photographed but not preserved can be culturally powerful and scientifically weak at the same time. Coelacanths, Living Fossils of the Sea Channel: Natural World Facts Open on YouTube Repeated reports and local lore Repeated reports are the oxygen of cryptid cases. A one off sighting may be odd, but a named creature becomes a cryptid when reports cluster across time, place, and community: lake monsters in specific bodies of water, hairy wild people in forest regions, black cats in rural Britain, thunderbirds in large bird sightings, or sea serpents along particular coasts. Repetition gives the case weight, but it can also create a feedback loop. Once a place has a creature name, ambiguous observations are easier to file under that name. Local knowledge complicates the sceptical story. The okapi is often used, sometimes too casually, as the “cryptids can be real” example. What actually happened is more precise. The animal was not unknown to people living in and around its Central African forest habitat. It became “new” when specimens reached European zoological institutions and could be described in the taxonomic system. Sir Harry Johnston’s role in 1901 depended on local information and on physical material: strips of hide, then a more complete skin and skulls. The case shows that scientific recognition can lag behind local familiarity, but it also shows that recognition did not rest on hearsay alone. [ZSL+2Royal Museum for Central Africa] zsl.org history of zsl The History of ZSLOctober 31, 2022 — 1901. The shy okapi is first discovered by the western world by ZSL Fellow Sir Harry Johnston, ex... Published: October 31, 2022 The okapi remains a useful boundary animal because it is elusive even after classification. It is a real, endangered forest giraffid endemic to the Democratic Republic of Congo, and conservation accounts still describe it as shy, under studied, and difficult to survey."},{"title":"Among The Most Persistent Explanations For Alleged Sea...","url":"/among-the-most-persistent-explanations/","description":"Rotting basking sharks can look strange enough to keep sea-serpent claims alive after the specimen is gone.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Among The Most Persistent Explanations For Alleged Sea... When a Shark Becomes a Sea Monster Among The Most Persistent Explanations For Alleged Sea... Among The Most Persistent Explanations For Alleged Sea... When a Shark Becomes a Sea Monster Rotting basking sharks can look strange enough to keep sea serpent claims alive after the specimen is gone. Rotting basking sharks can look strange enough to keep sea serpent claims alive after the specimen is gone. mechanism How decay changes a basking shark silhouette Why missing specimens keep old cases unsettled What carcass photos can and cannot prove forteana cryptids sea serpents Introduction Among the most persistent explanations for alleged sea serpent carcasses is not an unknown marine reptile but the ordinary basking shark in an extraordinary state of decay. This idea has become a classic example in Forteana because it shows how a genuine biological process can create convincing monster like evidence even after the living animal has disappeared. Instead of relying on mistaken eyewitness observations alone, many famous sea serpent cases have centred on decomposing bodies, fragmentary photographs and memories of specimens that were later discarded. The mechanism matters because it demonstrates how natural decay can systematically alter an animal's appearance. A large basking shark can lose exactly those features that identify it as a shark while leaving behind a silhouette that resembles the long necked marine reptiles familiar from popular culture. Although not every mysterious carcass has been conclusively identified this way, the \"pseudo plesiosaur\" produced by basking shark decomposition has become one of the strongest natural explanations for many celebrated sea monster claims. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass How decay changes a basking shark silhouette A freshly dead basking shark looks unmistakably like a shark. The transformation begins because its anatomy does not decay evenly. The first structures to break down are usually the lower jaw, the soft tissues supporting the enormous filter feeding mouth, the gill arches, and much of the tissue around the head. The dorsal fin and the upper and lower lobes of the tail are also vulnerable to decomposition and scavengers. What remains is supported by tougher connective tissues and the unusually robust vertebral column of this cartilaginous fish. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass The result is a body with several misleading features: The collapsed head appears much smaller than it originally was. The exposed vertebral column gives the impression of a long, flexible neck. The surviving paired pectoral and pelvic fins resemble four paddle like limbs. Loss of the shark's distinctive tail fin leaves a long tapering tail. Missing gill structures remove one of the clearest clues that the animal was a shark. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass To someone unfamiliar with shark decomposition, the remaining body can appear surprisingly similar to popular illustrations of a plesiosaur or traditional sea serpent. This resemblance is not because sharks evolve into monster like forms, but because decomposition selectively removes the anatomical features that would otherwise reveal their identity. 3 More Sea Monster Carcasses and Their Explanations Channel: TREY the Explainer Open on YouTube Why basking sharks are especially prone to misidentification Not every shark produces this effect. Basking sharks are unusually well suited to creating deceptive carcasses because of their size and anatomy. Adults commonly reach seven to eight metres in length and possess enormous mouths adapted for filter feeding. Unlike most sharks, the tissues around these jaws are relatively delicate once decomposition begins. Their skeleton is made largely of cartilage, but parts of the skull and vertebral column remain sufficiently durable to preserve an elongated framework after softer tissues disappear. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Basking shark Basking shark The species also inhabits temperate coastal waters where strandings and floating carcasses are more likely to be encountered by fishermen and beachgoers. Before legal protections were introduced in many regions, basking sharks were far more numerous in some northern waters, increasing the chances that decomposing specimens would occasionally be found. Famous cases that shaped the explanation The best known example is the Zuiyō Maru carcass recovered by a Japanese fishing vessel off New Zealand in 1977. Crew members photographed an approximately ten metre decomposing animal with what appeared to be a long neck, four fins and a tail. The images spread internationally and prompted speculation that the remains belonged to a surviving plesiosaur or unknown sea monster. The physical specimen itself was discarded because of its condition, but tissue samples and photographs survived. Amino acid analyses and anatomical comparisons led most marine biologists to conclude that the carcass was almost certainly a decomposing basking shark. Later reviews found that the recorded body proportions matched a large basking shark far better than any known plesiosaur, and photographs even revealed remnants of a dorsal fin that had initially been overlooked. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass An earlier example is the Stronsay Beast , which washed ashore in Scotland in 1808. Contemporary observers described a remarkably long neck and unusual body proportions, fuelling nineteenth century sea serpent debates. Modern anatomical studies, including examination of surviving vertebrae, strongly support identification as a decomposed basking shark rather than an unknown marine reptile. [Reddit] reddit.com comparisons with a basking shark and the Zuiyo Maru comparisons with a basking shark and the Zuiyo Maru...January 22, 2025 — Basking shark carcasses seem almost tailor made to inspir... Published: January 22, 2025 The same explanation has also been proposed for several Canadian \"Cadborosaurus\" carcasses. Although individual cases differ and some remain disputed because the original material has been lost, decomposed basking sharks repeatedly provide a closer anatomical match than hypothetical marine reptiles. [Tetrapod Zoology+2Wikipedia] tetzoo.com cadborosaurus carcass review Tetrapod ZoologyThe Case of the Cadborosaurus Carcass: a Review17 Nov 2020 — At right: Basking shark decomposition and the creation of a... Why missing specimens keep old cases unsettled Many famous sea serpent carcasses remain controversial for a simple reason: the evidence disappeared. Rotting marine animals are unpleasant to handle, difficult to preserve and often discarded quickly. Before modern DNA methods became routine, investigators frequently retained only a few photographs or small tissue samples. Sometimes even those samples were lost. As a result, later researchers must reconstruct events from measurements, witness testimony and ageing photographs rather than examining the entire specimen. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass This creates an evidential gap that is familiar throughout Forteana. Supporters of extraordinary explanations can argue that scientists never examined the complete animal, while sceptics point out that the surviving evidence fits known biological processes remarkably well. The absence of the original specimen prevents either side from performing new analyses that might settle the matter conclusively. What carcass photographs can and cannot prove Photographs are valuable but limited. They can preserve proportions, visible fins, vertebral outlines and surface textures that allow later anatomical comparison. In the Zuiyō Maru case, reanalysis of photographs helped identify features consistent with a shark rather than a marine reptile. Combined with biochemical evidence from preserved tissue, the images became much stronger evidence than eyewitness descriptions alone. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Zuiyo maru carcass Zuiyo maru carcass However, photographs also have significant limitations. Camera angle can exaggerate apparent neck length. Missing scale makes overall proportions uncertain. Decomposition and scavenging may hide diagnostic features. Two dimensional images cannot reveal internal anatomy. Important tissues may already have detached before photography. For this reason, photographs by themselves rarely establish the identity of an unknown carcass. They become most useful when interpreted alongside measurements, preserved tissue, anatomical comparisons and knowledge of decomposition. 4 Sea Monster Carcasses and their Explanations Channel: TREY the Explainer Open on YouTube What this mechanism tells us about sea serpent reports The basking shark explanation illustrates a recurring pattern in Forteana: extraordinary conclusions can arise from ordinary organisms passing through unusual natural processes. Rather than"},{"title":"Hair DNA","url":"/hair-dna/","description":"Genetic testing has made odd hairs less persuasive unless their origin, handling, and sequences hold up.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Hair DNA What Bigfoot Hair Tests Really Show Hair DNA Hair DNA What Bigfoot Hair Tests Really Show Genetic testing has made odd hairs less persuasive unless their origin, handling, and sequences hold up. Genetic testing has made odd hairs less persuasive unless their origin, handling, and sequences hold up. dataset evidence Why strange looking hair is weak evidence What the Sykes study found in alleged cryptid samples How contamination and chain of custody change the claim forteana cryptids hair dna Introduction Hair has long been one of the most frequently claimed forms of physical evidence for Bigfoot . Unlike footprints or eyewitness accounts, hair can in principle be examined under a microscope and tested genetically. Modern DNA sequencing has therefore changed the standards for evaluating such claims. A hair that merely looks unusual is no longer persuasive unless it can be shown to come from a securely documented source, remain uncontaminated, and produce genetic data that cannot be matched to any known species. That shift has exposed a central weakness in many Bigfoot claims. While unusual looking hairs are regularly reported from forests, fences, tree bark and alleged bedding sites, almost all published genetic investigations have identified them as belonging to ordinary mammals or humans. The problem is not simply whether an unknown primate exists, but whether the evidence chain behind each sample is strong enough to support such an extraordinary conclusion. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... Why strange looking hair is weak evidence Hair can appear highly distinctive without belonging to an unknown animal. Colour, thickness, weathering, fungal damage, age and environmental exposure all alter its appearance. Even experienced wildlife biologists generally avoid identifying species from visual inspection alone because many mammal hairs overlap in structure. DNA has largely replaced morphology as the preferred method for identifying unknown hair. However, hair presents its own technical limitations: Hair shafts usually contain little or no nuclear DNA because the cells that formed them have died. Most successful analyses rely on mitochondrial DNA, which is more abundant but carries less information than a complete genome. Environmental exposure rapidly degrades DNA through sunlight, moisture, microbes and repeated handling. Human contamination is common because alleged cryptid samples are often collected informally rather than under forensic conditions. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... Consequently, an unusual looking hair is better regarded as a candidate for laboratory identification than as evidence of an unknown species. The Bigfoot DNA Paper and Dr. Melba Ketchum Channel: Journey to the Fringe podcast Open on YouTube What the Sykes study found in alleged cryptid samples The most influential published investigation remains the 2014 study led by geneticist Bryan Sykes in the Proceedings of the Royal Society B . The project invited museums, researchers and private collectors worldwide to submit hairs attributed to Bigfoot, Sasquatch, Yeti, Almasty and other alleged wild hominoids. After screening submissions for suitability, the researchers sequenced mitochondrial DNA from 30 samples using established laboratory procedures. The outcome was striking. Rather than revealing an unknown primate, nearly every sample matched familiar mammals, including bears, horses, cattle, dogs, wolves, raccoons, deer, porcupines, sheep and humans. None provided genetic evidence for an undiscovered ape or hominin. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... Two Himalayan samples initially attracted considerable attention because their short mitochondrial sequences resembled DNA from an ancient polar bear. This led to speculation about an unknown bear lineage rather than a Yeti. Later researchers argued that the apparent match resulted from the limited DNA fragment analysed and incomplete reference databases, proposing instead that the hairs probably came from known Himalayan brown bears. The episode illustrated how preliminary genetic matches can change as comparative databases improve. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org Skeptical InquirerNo Reason to Believe That Sykes's Yeti Bear Cryptid Existsby EE Gutiérrez — Sykes and company claimed that these sampl... Although the Sykes study cannot prove that Bigfoot does not exist, it established an important empirical benchmark: when purported cryptid hair has been subjected to systematic genetic testing under published methods, the identified sources have consistently been known animals. How contamination and chain of custody change the claim The greatest weakness in most alleged Bigfoot hair evidence lies not in laboratory technology but in sample provenance. A convincing biological specimen requires an unbroken chain of custody showing: exactly where and when the hair was found; who collected it; how it was packaged and stored; whether other people handled it; whether contamination from humans, pets or wildlife was possible. Many Bigfoot hairs fail these basic forensic requirements. Samples may have been collected weeks after the alleged encounter, stored in envelopes or plastic bags, repeatedly handled by investigators, or passed among private collectors before reaching a laboratory. By then, establishing whether recovered DNA originated from the hair itself or from later contamination can be extremely difficult. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... This explains why even a surprising laboratory result would receive careful scrutiny. An unidentified DNA sequence is not automatically evidence for a new species. Scientists must first exclude contamination, sequencing artefacts, degraded DNA and incomplete reference databases before considering more extraordinary explanations. Expedition Bigfoot S4 E2 DNA Test Results From White Hairs Found At Bigfoot Location [HD] [2023] Channel: WotNot Open on YouTube Why claimed Bigfoot DNA studies remain unconvincing The best known contrary claim came from veterinarian Melba Ketchum, who announced that Bigfoot represented a hybrid hominin species based on genetic testing of alleged samples. The work attracted widespread publicity but was not accepted by the mainstream genetics community. Criticism focused on several issues: uncertain sample provenance, extraordinary biological conclusions unsupported by independently verified specimens, and publication in a journal created after repeated difficulties securing acceptance in established peer reviewed journals. Independent experts argued that the reported genetic evidence did not justify the paper's conclusions, and the study has not become part of accepted zoological evidence for an unknown North American primate. [Live Science+2Ars Technica] livescience.com 27140 bigfoot dna study questioned Live ScienceBigfoot DNA Discovered? Not So Fast14 Feb 2013 — The study, by Melba S. Ketchum, suggested such cryptids had sex with modern... The contrast with the Sykes project is instructive. Both involved alleged cryptid material, but one relied on systematic identification of submitted samples while the other made far more ambitious claims that have not been independently replicated. What the DNA evidence problem really shows Hair remains one of the most common forms of alleged Bigfoot evidence because it is easy to collect and often appears unusual. Modern genetics, however, has fundamentally altered how such finds are evaluated. Today, the evidential burden extends well beyond obtaining a strange hair. Researchers would need multiple independently collected samples with secure provenance, reproducible DNA sequences, and genetic results inconsistent with every known mammal. Ideally, those findings would also agree with other forms of evidence, such as tissue, environmental DNA, or a verifiable specimen. To date, published analyses have consistently pointed in the opposite direction. Rather than revealing an unknown North American primate, they have shown that purported Bigfoot hairs almost always originate from familiar species, while the few initially puzzling results have become less mysterious as comparative genetic databases and analytical methods have improved. [PMC+2Retraction Watch] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... Finding Bigfoot: Sasquatch DNA Found? Channel: Animal Planet Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Bigfoot Hair Tests Really Show. Use"},{"title":"Giant Squid","url":"/giant-squid/","description":"The giant squid shows how a monster-colored tradition can become ordinary biology through specimens and live images.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Giant Squid When the Giant Squid Left Legend Behind Giant Squid Giant Squid When the Giant Squid Left Legend Behind The giant squid shows how a monster colored tradition can become ordinary biology through specimens and live images. The giant squid shows how a monster colored tradition can become ordinary biology through specimens and live images. event time window Why old sailor stories were not enough How carcasses and whale evidence built the case What live deep sea photography changed forteana cryptids giant squid Introduction The giant squid occupies a unique place in Forteana because it demonstrates that a creature can move from the edge of folklore into established zoology without ever fitting the simple story of a \"myth proved true\". For centuries, reports from sailors described enormous tentacled animals capable of terrifying encounters at sea, yet these accounts lacked the physical evidence needed to convince most naturalists. Instead of one dramatic discovery settling the question, confidence grew gradually through stranded specimens, remains recovered from predators, and eventually photographs and video taken in the deep ocean. The giant squid therefore marks an important boundary case in natural history: not a cryptid that remained mysterious, but one whose reality emerged through the slow accumulation of evidence rather than a single decisive moment. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov First ever observations of a live giant squid in the wildby T Kubodera · 2005 · Cited by 103 — Here, we show the first wild images... Why old sailor stories were not enough Legends of immense sea creatures appear across many maritime cultures, most famously in the Scandinavian tradition of the kraken. Early modern sailors also described giant tentacles, enormous eyes and battles between whales and unknown cephalopods. Such stories were often dismissed because they were inconsistent, difficult to verify and frequently embellished through repeated retelling. From the perspective of nineteenth century science, eyewitness testimony alone could not establish a new species. Reports were hampered by conditions that are now recognised as ideal for observational error: poor weather , rough seas, fleeting encounters and uncertain estimates of size. Even honest witnesses could not provide specimens, measurements or anatomical details that other researchers could examine independently. This scepticism was not entirely unreasonable. The deep sea was largely unexplored, and many supposed sea monsters later proved to be decomposing whales, floating masses of tissue or misidentified known animals. The challenge was distinguishing extraordinary but genuine observations from stories that had grown in the telling. The turning point began when zoologists received physical remains. In 1857, the Danish naturalist Japetus Steenstrup formally described Architeuthis using fragmentary material from the Bahamas, providing the first scientific framework for what had previously been a creature of uncertain reputation. Even then, complete specimens remained exceptionally rare, and debate continued over the animal's appearance, behaviour and maximum size. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia List of giant squid specimens and sightings List of giant squid specimens and sightings The Kraken — Lord of the Ocean Depths 🐙🌊 Myth… or TERRIFYING REALITY? Channel: Explorer Official Open on YouTube How carcasses and whale evidence built the case Scientific confidence in the giant squid accumulated because several independent lines of evidence converged over more than a century rather than relying on spectacular sightings. Stranded and accidentally caught specimens became increasingly important. Although most were damaged or decomposing, they allowed researchers to describe the squid's anatomy, estimate its size and compare specimens from different oceans. Commercial fishing occasionally brought giant squid to the surface, adding further museum material for study. These specimens showed that the animal was real even if its natural behaviour remained almost completely unknown. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia List of giant squid specimens and sightings List of giant squid specimens and sightings Just as significant was evidence from sperm whales. Long before scientists observed living giant squid underwater, sperm whales repeatedly provided indirect confirmation of their existence. Whale stomachs frequently contained giant squid beaks, which resist digestion and can be identified to species. Marine biologists also documented the characteristic circular scars left by the squid's powerful suckers on the heads and bodies of sperm whales. Together, these findings demonstrated not only that giant squid existed but that they formed a regular part of the ecology of the deep ocean. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov First ever observations of a live giant squid in the wildby T Kubodera · 2005 · Cited by 103 — Here, we show the first wild images... This indirect evidence changed the debate in an important way. The question was no longer whether giant squid existed, but why humans almost never encountered healthy individuals in their natural habitat. Their apparent rarity turned out to be largely a consequence of their environment. Living hundreds of metres below the surface, they inhabit a region of the ocean that remained largely inaccessible until modern deep sea technology became available. For Fortean studies, this distinction matters. The giant squid was never accepted because eyewitness stories became more convincing. It was accepted because independent physical evidence repeatedly confirmed the animal despite the absence of direct observation . What live deep sea photography changed By the beginning of the twenty first century, giant squid had long been recognised scientifically, yet they remained among the least observed large animals on Earth. Marine biologist Richard Ellis famously described obtaining an image of a live giant squid as one of natural history's greatest remaining challenges. A breakthrough came in 2004, when Tsunemi Kubodera and Kyoichi Mori photographed a live giant squid attacking bait nearly 900 metres below the surface near Japan's Ogasawara Islands. Rather than searching blindly, the researchers used decades of knowledge about sperm whale feeding behaviour to identify where giant squid were most likely to occur. The camera captured hundreds of images, and a severed tentacle recovered from the encounter confirmed the animal's identity through anatomical and DNA analysis. Their results, published in 2005, provided the first images of a healthy giant squid behaving naturally in the wild. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov First ever observations of a live giant squid in the wildby T Kubodera · 2005 · Cited by 103 — Here, we show the first wild images... The images also overturned several assumptions. Instead of appearing as a sluggish drifting scavenger, the squid actively attacked bait with remarkable speed and strength, suggesting it was an energetic predator capable of rapid strikes using its specialised feeding tentacles. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov First ever observations of a live giant squid in the wildby T Kubodera · 2005 · Cited by 103 — Here, we show the first wild images... An even more dramatic milestone followed in 2012, when Kubodera collaborated with Edith Widder and Steve O'Shea to obtain the first sustained video footage of a giant squid swimming freely in its deep sea habitat. Viewers could finally observe the animal's movement, posture and behaviour directly rather than reconstructing them from damaged carcasses. Although the footage lasted only minutes, it fundamentally altered public perception. The giant squid ceased to be known primarily through washed up remains and became recognisable as a living deep sea predator. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Tsunemi Kubodera Tsunemi Kubodera Kraken vs Colossal Squid vs Giant Squid: Myth & Reality of Sea Monsters Channel: Uncharted Origin Open on YouTube The Giant Squid's Biggest Mystery Channel: SciFri Open on YouTube What the giant squid teaches about the boundary of natural history The history of the giant squid illustrates that folklore and science are not always opposites. Sailors were reporting encounters with unusually large squid long before zoologists could verify them. Many individual stories were undoubtedly exaggerated or mistaken, yet they were attached to a real biological phenomenon that science lacked the means to investigate directly. Equally important, the case shows why cryptid narratives cannot simply point to the giant squid as proof that every disputed creature will eventually be confirmed. The decisive evidence did not come from accumulating anecdotes alone. It came from preserved specimens, anatomical study, ecological evidence from sperm whales, genetic confirmation and, finally, direct observation in the animal's natural environment. Each stage reduced uncertainty by providing evidence that other"},{"title":"Local Lore","url":"/local-lore/","description":"Local creature traditions can preserve useful observations, but they can also organize fear, identity, humor, and mistake.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Local Lore When Local Lore Points to an Animal Local Lore Local Lore When Local Lore Points to an Animal Local creature traditions can preserve useful observations, but they can also organize fear, identity, humor, and mistake. Local creature traditions can preserve useful observations, but they can also organize fear, identity, humor, and mistake. population context How repeated reports create a named creature When tradition preserves ecological clues When the creature is doing cultural work forteana cryptids local lore Introduction Local cryptid traditions occupy an unusual position within Forteana because they can preserve two very different kinds of information at once. On one hand, repeated reports from the same landscape sometimes draw attention to genuine ecological features: an overlooked species, an unusual animal population, recurring environmental conditions, or predictable patterns of behaviour. On the other, the same stories often become cultural animals—named creatures that express local identity, caution, humour, memory, or shared experience regardless of whether the reported animal exists as described. The important question is therefore not simply whether a cryptid is \"real\". Instead, the evidence asks what repeated local reports are actually pointing towards. Sometimes they lead investigators to known wildlife viewed under difficult conditions. Sometimes they preserve fragments of ecological knowledge. Sometimes they become enduring symbols that organise how a community thinks about a particular place. Understanding that distinction helps explain why local cryptid traditions persist even when zoological evidence remains inconclusive. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. How repeated reports create a named creature A single unexplained sighting rarely becomes folklore . Local cryptids emerge when many observations accumulate within the same geographical area and acquire a shared name. Once a creature has a recognised identity, later witnesses often interpret ambiguous experiences through that existing narrative. This process has several consequences. Reports become geographically concentrated. Particular lakes, forests, marshes or roads become associated with one creature rather than many unrelated animals. Descriptions gradually stabilise. Witnesses begin using similar features because previous accounts provide a reference point. The landscape itself gains meaning. Certain places become known as locations where \"the creature lives\", encouraging both attention and storytelling. The Beast of Bray Road in Wisconsin illustrates this process. Initial reports of a wolf like animal developed over decades into a named local cryptid connected with one rural road. Journalistic coverage, books and community discussion reinforced a recognisable identity even though sceptical explanations continued to favour misidentified wolves or large dogs. The locality became as important as the creature itself. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Beast of Bray Road Beast of Bray Road A similar pattern appears in numerous regional legends where a cryptid's identity depends less on unique biological evidence than on repeated local recognition. The name allows scattered experiences, rumours and stories to be treated as evidence for a single enduring population. Every Real Animal Behind A Mythical Creature Explained Channel: The Dark Mythologist Open on YouTube When tradition preserves ecological clues Although cryptid traditions are not reliable biological evidence on their own, dismissing all local reports would overlook an important source of environmental knowledge. People who spend generations fishing, farming, hunting or travelling through the same landscape often notice recurring natural phenomena long before scientists formally investigate them. Local traditions may preserve ecological clues by recording: seasonal appearances of uncommon animals; unusual migrations; dangerous habitats; recurring sounds or tracks; changes in wildlife abundance; encounters with rare individuals. Ethnozoology—the study of human knowledge about animals—has repeatedly shown that traditional ecological knowledge can complement scientific surveys, particularly in remote regions where formal biological sampling is limited. Local names and stories sometimes preserve observations of genuine species even when the explanation attached to them becomes embellished over time. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. This does not mean every cryptid conceals an undiscovered animal. Instead, it suggests that recurring reports deserve careful separation into different components: the observable event; the interpretation given by witnesses; the later folklore built around both. That distinction allows investigators to ask whether multiple reports reflect a real environmental pattern without accepting every supernatural or extraordinary conclusion. When repeated sightings become evidence of culture rather than zoology Many local cryptids eventually perform functions that have little to do with identifying unknown animals. Instead they become cultural animals: creatures that help communities explain their surroundings, express regional identity or reinforce shared memories. The creature survives because it is socially useful rather than biologically verified. Several recurring cultural roles appear across local traditions. Marking dangerous places. Rivers, caves, bogs and forests that present genuine hazards are often associated with distinctive creatures. The story encourages caution while making the landscape memorable. Creating local identity. A community may embrace a cryptid as part of its heritage, producing festivals, museum exhibits, artwork or tourism centred on the legend rather than on claims of scientific proof. Organising unusual experiences. Strange noises, fleeting glimpses or unexplained animal behaviour become easier to discuss once a recognised local creature exists to explain them. Expressing humour and shared storytelling. Some traditions persist because people enjoy participating in a communal legend, regardless of literal belief. The Lake Worth Monster in Texas demonstrates this cultural transformation. Following reports in 1969, the creature became woven into local identity through annual events, public storytelling and municipal promotion. Whether or not the original sightings reflected an unknown animal became less significant than the legend's role within community culture. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Lake Worth Monster Lake Worth Monster The Beast of Bray Road and \"Embracing the Strange\" Flyover Culture Channel: Flyover Culture Open on YouTube Why known animals often remain hidden inside the legend Many enduring cryptid traditions appear to originate from real animals observed under unusual circumstances rather than entirely imaginary creatures. Several mechanisms repeatedly occur. Rare individuals may appear much larger or differently coloured than expected. Escaped captive animals can briefly establish unfamiliar local populations. Behaviour seen at night, across water or through vegetation is difficult to interpret accurately. Existing folklore encourages witnesses to notice certain features while overlooking others. The Beast of Dean illustrates this ambiguity. Historical accounts describe a mysterious beast in the Forest of Dean, yet contemporary evidence suggests the stories developed around unusually large wild boar, animals genuinely capable of causing substantial damage to woodland and farmland. The legend therefore reflects both a real ecological presence and a folkloric enlargement of it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Beast of Dean Beast of Dean This pattern appears repeatedly in Fortean investigations. The biological core may be ordinary, while the cultural interpretation gradually becomes extraordinary. Reading local lore as evidence Neither complete belief nor complete dismissal provides the most useful approach to local cryptid traditions. Instead, different kinds of evidence should be considered separately. QuestionUseful evidenceIs there a recurring environmental observation ? Independent reports, seasonality, habitat consistency, ecological plausibility.Is there evidence for an unknown species?Physical specimens, DNA, photographs with provenance, repeatable biological traces.Is the tradition changing through storytelling?Variations across decades, media influence, increasing descriptive detail.Does the creature serve a social purpose?Festivals, tourism, local identity, cautionary tales, humour and community memory. Viewed this way, a cryptid tradition can be informative even when its zoological claim fails. A legend may accurately preserve information about wildlife behaviour, hazardous terrain or local environmental history while simultaneously exaggerating the creature that inspired it. Why this matters for Forteana Local cryptid lore demonstrates that unexplained reports need not resolve into a simple choice between fact and"},{"title":"Ness DNA","url":"/ness-dna-897a8b/","description":"Loch Ness shows how modern DNA testing can narrow a monster story without solving every sighting.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Ness DNA Did DNA Change the Loch Ness Case? Ness DNA Ness DNA Did DNA Change the Loch Ness Case? Loch Ness shows how modern DNA testing can narrow a monster story without solving every sighting. Loch Ness shows how modern DNA testing can narrow a monster story without solving every sighting. dataset evidence What environmental DNA can and cannot detect Why eel DNA mattered more than plesiosaurs What sightings still leave unresolved forteana cryptids ness dna Introduction The environmental DNA (eDNA) survey of Loch Ness is one of the most important scientific investigations ever applied to a famous cryptid. Rather than searching for a creature directly, researchers sampled traces of genetic material left behind in the water by organisms living in or passing through the loch. The results did not solve every Loch Ness sighting, but they significantly narrowed the range of biologically plausible explanations. They found no evidence for large reptiles such as a surviving plesiosaur, nor for several other commonly proposed candidates, while discovering that eel DNA was abundant throughout the loch. That finding did not demonstrate that \"Nessie\" is a giant eel, but it shifted discussion from prehistoric monsters towards an explanation involving an ordinary species whose appearance could sometimes be mistaken under unusual conditions. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... What environmental DNA can and cannot detect Environmental DNA is genetic material that organisms continually shed into their surroundings through skin cells, mucus, waste, eggs and other biological material. In aquatic environments, these traces can be collected by filtering water and sequencing the DNA fragments they contain. Because each species has distinctive genetic markers, researchers can reconstruct much of a lake's biodiversity without needing to capture every animal directly. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... In 2018, Professor Neil Gemmell and colleagues collected around 250 water samples from multiple locations and depths across Loch Ness. Their analysis identified roughly 3,000 species or taxonomic groups represented in the DNA recovered from the loch. The survey was designed as a biodiversity study rather than a dedicated monster hunt, although the famous legend provided an opportunity to demonstrate the capabilities of eDNA research to a global audience. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... The study's strongest conclusions were negative ones. Researchers found no detectable DNA consistent with large reptiles, making the long popular surviving plesiosaur hypothesis highly implausible. They likewise found no evidence supporting populations of sharks, catfish or sturgeon—animals that have occasionally been proposed as explanations for unusual sightings. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... However, eDNA has important limitations: It identifies species that have left detectable genetic traces, not individual animals. Failure to detect DNA is strong evidence against a substantial resident population but is not absolute proof that no individual organism has ever entered the loch. The method cannot estimate body size from DNA alone. It cannot determine which animal, if any, produced a reported sighting decades earlier. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... These limitations matter because the Loch Ness question concerns not merely whether animals are present, but whether eyewitness reports describe something biologically extraordinary. Neil Gemmell Environmental DNA for Biodiversity, Biosecurity, Global Health and Monster Hunting Channel: Genomics Aotearoa Open on YouTube Why eel DNA mattered more than plesiosaurs The most widely reported finding from the study was the abundance of European eel DNA. Eels were detected throughout the loch, confirming that they are a common component of its ecosystem. Since eDNA cannot distinguish between a normal sized eel and an unusually large one, the data left open the possibility—though not the probability—that exceptionally large individuals could exist. Professor Gemmell therefore described large eels as a hypothesis that his data could not eliminate, while stressing that the evidence did not demonstrate the existence of giant specimens. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... This distinction is often lost in popular retellings . Headlines suggesting that DNA \"proved Nessie was an eel\" overstated the results. The study showed only that eels are unquestionably present and that no competing evidence supported more exotic biological candidates. An explanation involving an ordinary animal therefore became more parsimonious than one requiring an unknown giant reptile. [The Guardian] theguardian.com loch ness monster could be a giant eel say scientists The GuardianLoch Ness monster could be a giant eel, say scientistsSep 5, 2019 — Loch Ness monster could be a giant eel, say scientists ·... From an evidential perspective, the eel hypothesis has several advantages over the plesiosaur idea: European eels are known residents of Scottish freshwaters. Their elongated bodies can create unusual silhouettes, particularly in poor visibility. Swimming eels generate sinuous surface movements that may resemble some witness descriptions. Their confirmed presence requires no undiscovered species or surviving prehistoric lineage. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something FishySep 5, 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Ge... None of these points establishes that every reported sighting involved an eel. Rather, they demonstrate that a known species fits the available biological evidence better than previously popular monster models. Does the giant eel hypothesis itself stand up? Although the eDNA survey made the eel explanation more attractive than a plesiosaur, it did not establish that truly gigantic eels inhabit Loch Ness. Subsequent statistical work using historical measurements of European eels has examined how likely unusually large individuals would be. The analysis suggested that eels approaching one metre in length are uncommon but biologically possible within a large population. By contrast, eels several metres long—the sizes required for many classic Nessie reports—are effectively unsupported by the available growth data. The probability of naturally occurring six metre European eels was estimated to be essentially zero. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The Loch Ness Monster: If It's Real, Could It Be an Eel?The Monsters of Loch Ness. London, UK. Macdonald and Jane's; 19... This distinction is important because the phrase \"giant eel\" covers a wide range of possibilities. A one metre eel surfacing unexpectedly could plausibly contribute to some brief, ambiguous sightings. It cannot readily account for reports describing massive necks, multiple humps, or animals estimated at many metres in length. Consequently, the giant eel hypothesis is best understood as a partial explanatory model rather than a universal solution. Loch Ness monster might just be a giant eel Channel: KiniTV Open on YouTube What sightings still leave unresolved The eDNA study changed the debate without ending it. Many Loch Ness reports remain unexplained in the narrow sense that there is insufficient information to identify what witnesses actually observed. Several categories of reports remain difficult to test: Brief observations made at long distances. Sightings affected by poor weather , waves or unusual lighting. Historical accounts made before modern recording equipment. Reports lacking photographs, physical evidence or independent corroboration. Importantly, these unresolved cases are not positive evidence for an unknown animal. They are simply observations that cannot"},{"title":"Okapi","url":"/okapi/","description":"The okapi became a scientific animal only when local knowledge was joined to skins, skulls, and taxonomy.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Cryptids","level":3,"text":"Okapi Why the Okapi Was Not Just a Rumor Okapi Okapi Why the Okapi Was Not Just a Rumor The okapi became a scientific animal only when local knowledge was joined to skins, skulls, and taxonomy. The okapi became a scientific animal only when local knowledge was joined to skins, skulls, and taxonomy. entity What outsiders missed and local people knew Why specimens changed the scientific status How a known animal can remain hard to know forteana cryptids okapi Introduction The okapi is often presented as a rare example of a “cryptid that turned out to be real”. That description is only partly correct. The animal was unknown to Western zoology until 1901, yet it was never an unknown creature to the Indigenous communities who lived alongside it in the forests of what is now the Democratic Republic of the Congo. What delayed its acceptance by science was not a lack of stories, but the absence of physical specimens that could be examined, compared and formally described. The history of the okapi therefore illustrates an important boundary in natural history and Forteana : hearsay can point investigators in the right direction, but it does not by itself establish a new species. The decisive shift came only when local knowledge was joined to skins, skulls and anatomical evidence that could be studied independently. Royal Museum for Central Africa+2IELC LibGuides [africamuseum.be] africamuseum.be Royal Museum for Central Africa Okapis Royal Museum for Central Africa Royal Museum for Central AfricaOkapis Royal Museum for Central Africa Tervuren10 Mar 2022 — The AfricaMuseum today houses one of the... What outsiders missed and local people knew For generations, the okapi was part of the lived environment of forest peoples in the Ituri region. Hunters understood its habits, recognised its tracks and had names for the animal long before European naturalists became interested in it. The problem was not that the animal was mythical, but that colonial era scientific networks had little direct access to either the forests or the knowledge of the people who lived there. [Royal Museum for Central Africa] africamuseum.be Royal Museum for Central Africa Okapis Royal Museum for Central Africa Royal Museum for Central AfricaOkapis Royal Museum for Central Africa Tervuren10 Mar 2022 — The AfricaMuseum today houses one of the... European explorers heard repeated accounts of a mysterious forest animal during the late nineteenth century. These reports were often filtered through several layers of translation and assumption. Some interpreted the descriptions as referring to an unusual zebra because of references to striped legs, while others imagined a forest horse or an \"African unicorn\". Such interpretations reflected the expectations of outsiders rather than the understanding of local observers, whose descriptions referred to a real animal already familiar within the region. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Sir Harry Johnston's role was therefore less one of discovering an unknown beast than of bringing together Indigenous testimony with material evidence. Local guides directed his attention to tracks and helped him obtain pieces of skin and eventually a skull. Without that assistance, the animal would almost certainly have remained another rumour within European scientific circles for longer. [Wikipedia+2ResearchGate] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Who \"Discovered\" the Okapi? Channel: Bizarre Beasts Open on YouTube Why specimens changed the scientific status The decisive moment in the okapi story was not the first report but the first examinable remains. When Johnston sent striped skin fragments and a skull to Britain, zoologists could compare them with known mammals. The striped hide initially encouraged comparisons with zebras, leading the species first to be described as Equus johnstoni . However, the skull revealed unmistakable giraffid features, including the characteristic horn like ossicones. Further anatomical study showed that the animal represented neither a horse nor a zebra but a previously unrecognised relative of the giraffe, now classified as Okapia johnstoni . [Wikipedia+2IELC LibGuides] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. This sequence demonstrates how taxonomy works in practice. Scientific recognition depends on evidence that other researchers can inspect, preserve and compare. A specimen allows measurements, anatomical descriptions, museum collections and later genetic work. Stories alone cannot perform those functions, no matter how numerous or sincere the witnesses. The history is also more complex than older discovery narratives suggested. Research into museum collections has shown that fragments of okapi skin had reached Europe before Johnston's famous specimens, although they were not immediately recognised for what they were. This underlines that collecting an object is not enough by itself; scientific interpretation requires that the material be identified, compared and incorporated into a broader taxonomic framework. [Tetrapod Zoology] tetzoo.com the discovery of the okapi part 2 Tetrapod ZoologyThe Discovery of the Okapi, Part 2April 28, 2023 — 28 Apr 2023 — Harry Johnston is credited with being the first European... Published: April 28, 2023 How a known animal can remain hard to know The okapi also illustrates that an animal can be completely real while remaining exceptionally difficult to observe. Several factors contributed to its late recognition by Western science: It inhabits dense equatorial rainforest where visibility is poor. It is naturally shy and largely solitary. Its range is geographically restricted. Nineteenth century European expeditions visited only a small fraction of its habitat. Reliable information depended heavily on local hunters rather than casual travellers. [IELC LibGuides+2IUCN Portals] ielc.libguides.com Okapi (Okapia johnstoni) Fact Sheet: Taxonomy & HistoryOkapi were unknown to the western world (occupy dense African rain f... These circumstances made second hand reports plausible but difficult to verify. In modern terms, the okapi occupied an information gap rather than a biological impossibility. The forests concealed the animal from outsiders, but not from the communities who interacted with it routinely. This distinction is important because it explains why the okapi should not be treated as evidence that any persistent cryptid report is likely to prove true. The okapi already existed within a functioning network of local ecological knowledge. What Western zoology lacked was direct access to verifiable specimens, not a shortage of eyewitness accounts. [ResearchGate+2Tetrapod Zoology] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Discovering the Okapi: Western Science, Indigenous...1 Nov 2025 — Pooley tells the story of the okapi's \"discovery\" in... OKAPI: THE RETURN OF THE UNICORN Documentary Film Channel: Kickstarter History Open on YouTube Discovering the Okapi Channel: Linnean Society Open on YouTube What the okapi teaches about hearsay in Forteana Within discussions of cryptids , the okapi is frequently invoked as proof that science sometimes dismisses genuine animals. The historical record supports a narrower and more useful lesson. Local testimony mattered because it directed investigators towards a real species. Yet the testimony did not by itself change scientific consensus. Consensus shifted only when physical remains could be examined by multiple experts, described in published taxonomy and preserved in museum collections. That transition transformed the okapi from an intriguing report into an accepted species. [IELC LibGuides+2IUCN Portals] ielc.libguides.com Okapi (Okapia johnstoni) Fact Sheet: Taxonomy & HistoryOkapi were unknown to the western world (occupy dense African rain f... The case therefore marks the limit of hearsay rather than its triumph. Reliable reports, even when repeated over many years, remain provisional unless they can be connected to durable evidence. In the language of Forteana, the okapi crossed the boundary between mystery and natural history not because enough people believed in it, but because belief was eventually accompanied by specimens that anyone qualified could examine. That is why the okapi remains an important comparison in discussions of cryptids, while also reminding readers that the standards for recognising a new animal have always depended on evidence that extends beyond stories alone. Tetrapod Zoology+2Royal Museum for Central Africa [tetzoo.com] tetzoo.com the discovery of the okapi part 1 Tetrapod ZoologyThe Discovery of the Okapi, Part 112 Apr 2023 — This specimen was key in the naming of Ray Lankester's naming of the genu... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why the Okapi Was Not Just a Rumor. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Hunting Monsters By Darren Naish Uses historical discoveries to frame cryptid claims. See on Amazon Book The"},{"title":"Crypto vs Forteana","url":"/crypto-vs-forteana/","description":"Cryptozoology focuses on hidden animals, while Forteana treats creature reports as part of a wider anomaly culture.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Crypto vs Forteana Is Cryptozoology the Same as Forteana? Crypto vs Forteana Crypto vs Forteana Is Cryptozoology the Same as Forteana? Cryptozoology focuses on hidden animals, while Forteana treats creature reports as part of a wider anomaly culture. Cryptozoology focuses on hidden animals, while Forteana treats creature reports as part of a wider anomaly culture. population context Where the fields overlap Why animal evidence differs When folklore becomes natural history forteana crypto vs forteana Introduction Cryptozoology is not the same as Forteana , although the two often meet at the water’s edge, in the forest, or in a witness report about an animal that should not be there. Cryptozoology narrows the question to hidden, disputed, extinct, or out of place animals: could this creature exist as a real population, specimen, or misidentified known species? Forteana treats the same report as part of a wider anomaly culture, where the point is not only whether an animal exists, but how strange reports travel through newspapers , folklore, science, scepticism, tourism, hoaxing, and local memory. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana Psi EncyclopediaForteana Psi Encyclopedia Society for Psychical Research23 Nov 2014 — The category, inspired by writer and researcher... That difference matters because animal claims behave differently from many other Fortean claims. A ghost story may remain unresolved as testimony, atmosphere, or belief; a hidden animal claim eventually collides with ecology, habitat, breeding populations, bodies, DNA, tracks, camera traps, museum specimens, and taxonomic description. Forteana can preserve the ambiguity. Cryptozoology has to ask whether the ambiguity can become natural history. Where the Fields Overlap The overlap is easiest to see in famous creature cases: Bigfoot , the Loch Ness Monster, sea serpents, mystery cats, the yeti, surviving thylacines, and reports of animals outside their known range. These subjects appear naturally in Fortean collections because they are strange, persistent, witness heavy, and culturally productive. They also belong to cryptozoology because they present an animal shaped claim: not merely “something odd happened”, but “a creature may be there”. The Society for Psychical Research’s Psi Encyclopedia describes Forteana as a broad category inspired by Charles Fort , ranging from strange falls and cryptids to UFOs, miracles and balls of light. That breadth is precisely why cryptozoology can sit inside Forteana without being identical to it. A cryptid report is one kind of anomaly among many; Forteana is the room, not the animal. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana Psi EncyclopediaForteana Psi Encyclopedia Society for Psychical Research23 Nov 2014 — The category, inspired by writer and researcher... Cryptozoology’s narrower identity developed around “hidden animals”. UCL’s Museums and Collections blog gives the plain working definition: cryptozoology is the study of hidden animals or “cryptids”, including animals unknown to science, supposedly extinct animals such as the thylacine, and animals beyond their normal range, such as big cats reported in parts of Britain. [blogs.ucl.ac.uk] blogs.ucl.ac.uk Why I like cryptozoologists – an UnConventional view17 Nov 2011 — Cryptozoology, for those who don't know, is the study of hidden animals... The Centre for Fortean Zoology, a British organisation whose name deliberately blends the traditions, describes itself as dedicated to cryptozoology: the study of unknown animals and allied disciplines. [The Centre for Fortean Zoology] cfz.org.uk The Centre for Fortean ZoologyThe Centre for Fortean ZoologyThe Centre for Fortean Zoology (CFZ) is a professional and scientific organis... The shared territory is therefore not a fixed list of monsters, but a style of attention. A Fortean reader asks why a creature report resists neat dismissal. A cryptozoological reader asks whether the report could point to a real animal: a new species, a remnant population, an escaped exotic, a known species in an unexpected place, or a misread encounter with ordinary wildlife. Why Animal Evidence Differs Creature reports have a built in biological test that many Fortean subjects do not. If a large animal exists, it must eat, move, leave traces, reproduce if it is a population, and occupy a habitat that can sustain it. That does not mean the animal will be easy to find. Deep oceans, dense forests, caves, mountain ranges, war zones, and politically difficult field sites can hide animals from formal science for a long time. But the claim remains tied to physical evidence. This is where cryptozoology is both stronger and weaker than general Forteana. It is stronger because some animal mysteries can be resolved by ordinary natural history. The coelacanth is the classic example: coelacanths were thought to have vanished tens of millions of years ago until a living specimen was found in 1938, a discovery now treated as a landmark in zoology rather than as an eternal anomaly. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Discover the story of this strange fish Natural History MuseumCoelacanths: The fish that 'outdid' the Loch Ness MonsterCoelacanths were thought to have been extinct for 70 milli... The giant squid offers another example of an elusive animal moving from sea monster reputation into documented biology; the Smithsonian notes that the first photograph of a live giant squid in its natural habitat was taken in 2004 by Japanese researchers using a baited camera system. [Smithsonian Ocean] ocean.si.edu Open source on si.edu. It is weaker because the evidence bar rises sharply once a claim becomes biological. A fleeting silhouette, a local name, or a sincere witness can keep a Fortean story alive, but it cannot by itself establish a breeding animal population. A hidden animal needs stronger forms of support: a body, diagnostic tissue, clear photographs or video with scale and location, repeatable environmental DNA, reliable tracks, droppings, hairs, feeding signs, or a pattern of independent observations that fits ecology rather than legend. The Loch Ness Monster shows the distinction clearly. As Forteana, Nessie is a durable cultural anomaly: a mixture of eyewitness testimony, local economy, photographic controversy, landscape, humour, hope, and myth. As cryptozoology, it becomes a testable claim about what large animals could live in Loch Ness. A 2019 environmental DNA project led by Professor Neil Gemmell collected water samples from the loch and cast doubt on the existence of a large unknown reptile or monster, while leaving eel based explanations more plausible than plesiosaur style claims. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz otago scientist reveals loch ness monster could be a giant eel otago scientist reveals loch ness monster could be a giant eel The Fortean version can survive the DNA result as a study of belief, misidentification , local tradition and media life. The cryptozoological version has to absorb the biological constraint: if no convincing DNA, body, breeding evidence or ecological pathway appears, the animal claim becomes harder to sustain. The Cryptid That Turned Out To Be Real Channel: Storied Open on YouTube When Folklore Becomes Natural History Cryptozoology is most interesting when it sits between local knowledge and formal taxonomy. Many animal discoveries begin with people who already know the landscape: fishers, hunters, farmers, Indigenous communities, traders, guides, or people who encounter animals as part of daily life. Formal zoology may arrive later with specimens, descriptions, comparisons and names. That pathway is why cryptozoology cannot be dismissed simply as “monster hunting”. It points to a real historical pattern: unfamiliar animals can be known locally before they are recognised by outside science. The problem is that this true pattern is often stretched too far. The fact that the coelacanth or giant squid became scientifically documented does not make every lake monster, ape man or surviving prehistoric reptile likely. It proves only that the world is incompletely known and that good evidence can change a claim’s status. A useful way to separate folklore from emerging natural history is to ask what changes as evidence accumulates: Does the description stabilise? Folkloric creatures often shift shape across retellings. Natural history candidates tend to become more consistent as observations improve. Does the claim fit habitat and population needs? A single escaped animal is different from a hidden breeding population. The second requires food, mates, territory and long term survival. Can evidence be independently checked? Tissue, bones, high quality photographs, DNA and museum specimens matter because other people can examine them. Does the explanation become less extraordinary over time? A mystery cat may become an escaped lynx, puma or large domestic hybrid. A"},{"title":"Bigfoot","url":"/bigfoot/","description":"Bigfoot reports become harder to assess when the question shifts from strange encounters to food, habitat, mates, traces, and repeatable evidence.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Bigfoot Could Bigfoot Work as a Real Animal? Bigfoot Bigfoot Could Bigfoot Work as a Real Animal? Bigfoot reports become harder to assess when the question shifts from strange encounters to food, habitat, mates, traces, and repeatable evidence. Bigfoot reports become harder to assess when the question shifts from strange encounters to food, habitat, mates, traces, and repeatable evidence. population context Witness reports versus biological claims Habitat, food, and population pressure What evidence would change the case forteana crypto vs forteana bigfoot Introduction Bigfoot sits at the point where cryptozoology meets biology. Witnesses may sincerely report seeing a large, ape like figure, but if Bigfoot is proposed as a real animal rather than a purely folkloric or Fortean phenomenon, it must satisfy the same ecological rules as every other large mammal. A viable species cannot exist as a handful of isolated individuals. It requires a breeding population, sufficient food, suitable habitat, genetic diversity, and physical traces that accumulate over time. This shift—from evaluating individual encounters to evaluating whether an entire species could persist—is where the debate changes most dramatically. While reports continue to be collected across North America, the central scientific question is not whether unusual sightings occur, but whether those sightings are accompanied by evidence consistent with a hidden population of large primates. [Newsweek] newsweek.com Scientist Has Very Simple Explanation for BigfootJan 30, 2023 — \"Based on statistical considerations, it is likely that many supp... Witness reports versus biological claims Thousands of Bigfoot reports have been recorded over many decades, particularly from heavily forested parts of the Pacific Northwest, western Canada and parts of the eastern United States. Many witnesses describe a tall, bipedal, hair covered figure, unusual vocalisations, wood knocks, or large footprints. These recurring descriptions make Bigfoot an enduring subject within Forteana because they appear remarkably consistent across different regions and generations. From a biological perspective, however, eyewitness testimony is only the beginning of an investigation. Zoology relies on evidence that can be independently examined and reproduced. A population of undiscovered great primates would be expected to leave multiple categories of physical evidence, including: authenticated skeletal remains or bodies; repeated DNA samples from independent locations; diagnostic hair or tissue; verifiable environmental DNA; nests, feeding sites or faeces attributable to an unknown species; clear photographic or video evidence supported by location and scale. Individual reports may remain unexplained without demonstrating that an unknown species exists. This distinction explains why many zoologists regard the Bigfoot question differently from the cultural importance of Bigfoot stories themselves. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... One example is the extensive genetic examination of purported Bigfoot hair samples published in Proceedings of the Royal Society B . Researchers analysed dozens of samples submitted from around the world. Nearly all proved to originate from known mammals such as bears, horses, cattle, deer, dogs or humans rather than an unidentified primate. Although this did not eliminate every possible future claim, it demonstrated that some of the strongest physical evidence presented over many years failed scientific testing. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... The Unsettling Biology of Sasquatch: A Deep Dive into the Science of Bigfoot Channel: Thought Potato Open on YouTube Habitat, food, and population pressure The greatest challenge for the biological Bigfoot hypothesis is not whether one elusive individual could avoid people, but whether an entire breeding population could remain undiscovered. How many individuals would be needed? Conservation biology shows that long term survival generally requires substantially more than a few scattered animals. Small populations face inbreeding, random mortality and loss of genetic diversity. Although the exact number depends on lifespan, reproduction and movement between groups, a large mammal persisting over centuries would ordinarily require at least several hundred breeding individuals, and probably considerably more over a continental range. [The Guardian] theguardian.com Witnesses described large, ape like creatures, a strong earthy smell, and unexplained knocking sounds, all aligning with traditional Sasq... If such a population existed, it would create cumulative biological evidence rather than isolated encounters. Food and territory An ape standing over two metres tall would require significant daily energy intake. Even an omnivorous species would need reliable access to fruit, vegetation, insects, fish, small animals or large prey depending on its ecology. Large mammals also require space. Bears, cougars and wolves occupy measurable home ranges and leave detectable signs through tracks, scat, hair, feeding remains and repeated camera trap images. A breeding population of large primates distributed across North America would likewise be expected to leave a persistent ecological footprint extending well beyond occasional eyewitness reports. Supporters often argue that the forests of the Pacific Northwest and western Canada are vast enough to conceal unknown animals. Scientists generally agree that these landscapes remain incompletely explored, but they also note that they are extensively used by foresters, hunters, biologists, Indigenous communities, hikers, aerial surveys and increasingly dense networks of remote cameras. Maintaining an undiscovered population becomes progressively more difficult as monitoring technology improves. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Bones are rarer than many people assume—but not absent forever A common argument is that few bear skeletons are found naturally, so the absence of Bigfoot bones proves little. It is true that carcasses decompose rapidly and scavengers disperse remains. Nevertheless, established large mammals leave recoverable skeletal material, road casualties, museum specimens, fossils or genetically identifiable remains over long periods. For Bigfoot, despite decades of searches and thousands of reported encounters, no authenticated bones or bodies have entered the scientific record as evidence for a previously unknown North American ape. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Why traces matter more than sightings Modern wildlife biology increasingly relies on methods that detect species without directly observing them. Environmental DNA (eDNA) allows researchers to identify animals through tiny fragments of genetic material left behind in water, soil or sediments. Camera traps continuously monitor remote habitats for elusive species. Together with genetic analysis of hair, faeces and tissue, these techniques have transformed surveys for rare mammals. These methods have successfully documented species once considered exceptionally difficult to observe, including snow leopards, rare forest carnivores and previously overlooked populations of known mammals. If a widespread population of large primates existed across North America, many researchers argue that repeated positive results from these techniques would be expected eventually. The absence of consistent genetic evidence does not logically prove non existence, but it raises the evidential threshold. As technology becomes more sensitive, continued failure to detect an unknown species weighs increasingly against the biological hypothesis. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Keywords: yeti, almasty, bigfoot, sasquatch, mitochondrial DNA. 1. Introduction. Despite several...Read more... Expedition Bigfoot S4 E2 DNA Test Results From White Hairs Found At Bigfoot Location [HD] [2023] Channel: WotNot Open on YouTube What evidence would change the case? Most scientists are not opposed to discovering new species; zoology continues to describe previously unknown mammals, reptiles and amphibians every year. The issue is the quality of evidence required. Evidence capable of fundamentally changing the Bigfoot debate would include: an independently verified body or partial skeleton; DNA from multiple laboratories identifying an unknown primate lineage; repeated environmental DNA detections from different regions; high quality photographs or video supported by corroborating physical evidence; a combination of footprints, biological samples and repeat observations all pointing to the same unidentified species. Importantly, no single blurry photograph or isolated footprint would normally satisfy this standard. The strongest"},{"title":"Big Cats","url":"/big-cats/","description":"British big cat stories sit between phantom folklore, escaped exotic animals, museum evidence, and the unanswered question of breeding populations.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Big Cats Are Britain's Big Cats Real? Big Cats Big Cats Are Britain's Big Cats Real? British big cat stories sit between phantom folklore, escaped exotic animals, museum evidence, and the unanswered question of breeding populations. British big cat stories sit between phantom folklore, escaped exotic animals, museum evidence, and the unanswered question of breeding populations. population context Phantom panthers as modern folklore Museum evidence for escaped cats Why a breeding population is harder to prove forteana crypto vs forteana big cats Introduction Reports of large cats roaming the British countryside occupy an unusual position between cryptozoology and Forteana . Unlike many legendary creatures, Britain's \"phantom panthers\" exist in a landscape where some extraordinary claims have turned out to be true: individual non native wild cats have genuinely escaped or been released, and several have been captured or recovered. At the same time, decades of sightings have not produced convincing evidence that self sustaining breeding populations of large exotic cats exist across Britain. This combination of confirmed escapees, persistent eyewitness testimony, media attention and limited physical evidence explains why British big cats remain one of the most debated subjects in modern Fortean culture rather than a settled question of zoology. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Phantom panthers as modern folklore British big cat stories differ from traditional folklore because most originated within living memory. Instead of medieval legends, many famous cases emerged during the 1970s and 1980s, when reports of large black cats began appearing in local newspapers across rural Britain. Places such as Bodmin Moor in Cornwall, Exmoor in Devon and Somerset, the Cotswolds and parts of Scotland each developed their own named \"beast\", transforming scattered sightings into enduring regional legends. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Several factors helped these stories spread: Remote landscapes where brief sightings are difficult to verify. Livestock deaths that invited speculation about unusual predators. Local newspapers that repeatedly reported eyewitness accounts. Television documentaries and popular books that linked otherwise separate incidents into a national mystery. Folklorists have argued that this pattern resembles the creation of a modern legend more than the discovery of an unknown species. Rather than a single identifiable animal, the \"British big cat\" became a flexible narrative into which new sightings could easily fit. Once a district acquired a reputation for a mysterious cat, later reports were more readily interpreted through that existing story. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats This does not necessarily mean every witness was mistaken. Instead, it illustrates an important Fortean principle: sincere observations can become woven into a cultural narrative that grows independently of any individual event. The Beast of Bodmin Moor Big Cat Sightings and Evidence Cryptozoology TNWKS Ep.55 Channel: Zak Wylde Productions Open on YouTube Museum evidence shows escaped cats are genuinely part of the story The strongest evidence supporting part of the mystery comes not from photographs but from actual animals. Over the past century, Britain has produced several well documented cases of non native felids being recovered. Examples include: A Canadian lynx shot in Devon in 1903, later shown through examination to have spent time in captivity before its death. A live puma captured in the Scottish Highlands in 1980, nicknamed \"Felicity\", whose tame behaviour suggested recent escape from captivity. A Eurasian lynx captured alive in north London in 2001 before being transferred to a zoo. Occasional discoveries of jungle cats, caracals and other exotic species believed to have escaped from private ownership. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats These cases demonstrate that Britain has, at times, contained genuinely free roaming exotic cats. They also make eyewitness testimony harder to dismiss outright. A witness describing an unfamiliar feline is not necessarily imagining an impossible animal. The history of exotic pet ownership also contributes to this explanation. Following the introduction of the Dangerous Wild Animals Act 1976 , many enthusiasts have suggested that owners unwilling or unable to licence large cats released them into the countryside. While the scale of any such releases remains uncertain, government records confirm that escapes of non native cats have occurred over subsequent decades. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Why famous \"beasts\" remain unproven The best known British big cat legends illustrate both the strengths and weaknesses of the available evidence. The Beast of Bodmin Moor became nationally famous after repeated sightings and reports of livestock losses. In the mid 1990s an official investigation examined witness reports, photographs, tracks and alleged physical evidence but found no verifiable proof that a large exotic cat was living on the moor. Shortly afterwards, a leopard skull discovered nearby briefly appeared to support the legend until examination showed it had been cut from a taxidermy specimen, probably originating from a leopard skin rug rather than a wild animal. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats The Beast of Exmoor followed a similar trajectory. Reports of sheep apparently killed by an unknown predator generated widespread publicity during the early 1980s and prompted an unusually high profile search effort. Despite extensive investigation, no confirmed large cat was identified, and many livestock deaths were later attributed to more ordinary predators or scavengers. Nevertheless, sightings continue decades after the original events, illustrating how an unresolved mystery can become part of local identity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats These examples highlight an important distinction. A compelling local legend may preserve genuine unexplained observations while simultaneously attracting misidentifications, rumours and retrospective embellishment. Why a breeding population is harder to prove Individual escapees require relatively little evidence. A single animal can survive for months or even years before dying or being recaptured. A permanent breeding population is a far more demanding ecological claim. To establish such a population, researchers would expect evidence such as: repeated verified observations of multiple individuals; evidence of breeding, including females with dependent young; consistent prey remains showing characteristic feeding patterns; recoverable DNA from multiple locations; road casualties, skeletal remains or carcasses appearing over time; a geographical distribution matching known feline ecology. Despite thousands of reported sightings, this broader pattern has not emerged convincingly. Government agencies have repeatedly stated that no credible evidence demonstrates established populations of large non native cats in Britain. Sceptical zoologists also note that breeding predators would be expected to leave a much stronger ecological footprint than isolated reports alone suggest. [The Guardian] theguardian.com She first learned about the alleged sightings from local farmers in November 2023. Her purported sighting occurred when she found a fresh... Published: November 2023 This does not exclude the occasional escaped animal. Rather, it separates two different questions: Can exotic cats occasionally be found loose in Britain? Yes, documented cases show they can. Do stable wild populations of pumas, leopards or black panthers exist across Britain? Current evidence has not demonstrated this. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats British Big Cats: Ice Age Survivors or Escaped Illicit Pets? Channel: Icy Sedgwick Open on YouTube Why the mystery persists British big cats remain compelling because they occupy a genuine grey area between zoology and folklore. Most cryptids begin with no confirmed specimens whatsoever. British big cats are unusual because confirmed escaped exotic felids provide a real biological foundation beneath the stories. This makes some eyewitness reports entirely plausible without validating every claim. At the same time, repeated failures to obtain decisive evidence have encouraged sceptical explanations. Large domestic cats, dogs, deer viewed under poor conditions, perspective errors and expectations shaped by local legend all contribute to mistaken identifications. Modern photography has not eliminated this problem because fleeting encounters in uneven terrain rarely produce images with enough scale or clarity for reliable identification. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Within Forteana, this unresolved status is precisely what gives the subject its"},{"title":"Coelacanth","url":"/coelacanth/","description":"The coelacanth proves that surprising animals can be found, but it does not make every monster report equally plausible.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Coelacanth What the Coelacanth Really Proves Coelacanth Coelacanth What the Coelacanth Really Proves The coelacanth proves that surprising animals can be found, but it does not make every monster report equally plausible. The coelacanth proves that surprising animals can be found, but it does not make every monster report equally plausible. entity A real animal rediscovered by science Why the case inspires cryptozoology Where the analogy breaks down forteana crypto vs forteana coelacanth Introduction The rediscovery of the coelacanth is one of the most remarkable events in twentieth century zoology. A fish lineage believed to have vanished with the dinosaurs was found alive in 1938, proving that scientific knowledge is always open to revision when new evidence appears. That story has made the coelacanth a favourite example in discussions of cryptozoology, where it is often invoked to argue that legendary creatures might also await discovery. The lesson, however, is more limited than it first appears. The coelacanth demonstrates that overlooked species can survive in poorly explored habitats and that assumptions based on an incomplete fossil record can be overturned. It does not show that every famous cryptid has an equal chance of existing. Understanding why the coelacanth was rediscovered—and why its case differs from claims about creatures such as Bigfoot or the Loch Ness Monster—provides a useful guide to separating genuine zoological surprises from unsupported speculation. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Discover the story of this strange fish Natural History MuseumCoelacanths: The fish that 'outdid' the Loch Ness MonsterCoelacanths were thought to have been extinct for 70 milli... A Real Animal Rediscovered by Science The modern story began on 22 December 1938, when museum curator Marjorie Courtenay Latimer examined an unusual fish caught by a trawler off South Africa. Ichthyologist J. L. B. Smith recognised it as a living member of the coelacanth lineage, a group known previously only from fossils thought to have disappeared roughly 66–70 million years earlier. The species was later named Latimeria chalumnae in honour of its discoverer and the nearby Chalumna River. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Discover the story of this strange fish Natural History MuseumCoelacanths: The fish that 'outdid' the Loch Ness MonsterCoelacanths were thought to have been extinct for 70 milli... The discovery was extraordinary because it contradicted a widely accepted scientific conclusion based on the fossil record. Importantly, however, it did not overturn evolutionary theory. Instead, it showed that the fossil record is incomplete and that extinction inferred from missing fossils is sometimes revised when living populations are found. Subsequent decades transformed the coelacanth from an isolated curiosity into a well studied species. Populations were documented around the Comoro Islands and elsewhere in the western Indian Ocean, while a second living species, Latimeria menadoensis , was identified in Indonesia during the 1990s. Researchers gradually learned that these fish inhabit steep volcanic slopes and underwater caves at depths of roughly 150–700 metres—habitats that had received little scientific attention before modern diving technology. [Smithsonian Ocean+2Conservation International] ocean.si.edu Smithsonian OceanCoelacanth Smithsonian OceanThe first living coelacanth was discovered in 1938 and bears the scientific name Latimeria... Modern research has continued to refine this picture. Studies have revealed exceptionally slow growth, lifespans approaching a century and unusually long reproductive cycles, characteristics consistent with a stable deep sea existence rather than a rapidly reproducing coastal fish. [The Guardian] theguardian.com mysterious coelacanth fish can live for 100 years study The GuardianMysterious coelacanth fish can live for 100 years – study18 Jun 2021 — The coelacanth – a giant, mysterious fish that has sur... Coelacanths, Living Fossils of the Sea Channel: Natural World Facts Open on YouTube Why the Case Inspires Cryptozoology For cryptozoologists, the coelacanth represents a genuine success story. It demonstrates several principles that are difficult to dismiss. First, science does occasionally discover large vertebrates that were previously unknown or believed extinct. Although such discoveries are uncommon, they are not impossible. Second, local knowledge mattered. Fishers in the western Indian Ocean were familiar with the animal long before it entered scientific literature. The rediscovery reminds researchers that indigenous and local observations can point towards genuine biological phenomena rather than being dismissed automatically. [Natural History Museum] nhm.ac.uk Discover the story of this strange fish Natural History MuseumCoelacanths: The fish that 'outdid' the Loch Ness MonsterCoelacanths were thought to have been extinct for 70 milli... Third, the discovery illustrates the importance of unexplored environments. Deep marine habitats remain far less accessible than most terrestrial landscapes, making them plausible places for elusive species to persist unnoticed. For these reasons, the coelacanth occupies a symbolic place within cryptozoology. Bernard Heuvelmans and later writers frequently cited it alongside animals such as the okapi and giant squid as examples of creatures that moved from disputed reports into accepted zoology. The broader message—that science sometimes expands through unexpected discoveries—is entirely legitimate. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia On the Track of Unknown Animals On the Track of Unknown Animals Where the Analogy Breaks Down The coelacanth is often treated as proof that any legendary creature could exist. This is where the comparison becomes misleading. The fish was never known solely from folklore . Long before 1938, coelacanths were well established in the fossil record. Scientists were therefore not debating whether such animals had ever existed, but whether the lineage had survived into modern times. Rediscovery changed the timeline of extinction, not the existence of the group itself. [Smithsonian Ocean] ocean.si.edu Smithsonian OceanCoelacanth Smithsonian OceanThe first living coelacanth was discovered in 1938 and bears the scientific name Latimeria... Equally important is habitat. Coelacanths occupy deep offshore environments that are difficult and expensive to explore. Large terrestrial mammals, by contrast, require extensive food resources, leave tracks, shed hair, produce carcasses and interact with landscapes that are now heavily surveyed through roads, satellites, camera traps and ecological fieldwork . The scale of evidence also differs sharply. After the initial rediscovery, additional specimens followed, allowing anatomical study, genetic analysis, ecological observation and eventually underwater photography. The case rapidly accumulated independent lines of evidence rather than relying indefinitely on eyewitness testimony. [Smithsonian Ocean] ocean.si.edu Smithsonian OceanCoelacanth Smithsonian OceanThe first living coelacanth was discovered in 1938 and bears the scientific name Latimeria... This pattern contrasts with famous cryptids whose evidence remains dominated by anecdotal reports despite decades of investigation. In those cases, expectations rise because prolonged existence of a breeding population should generate increasingly abundant physical evidence. Animated Life: The Living Fossil Fish HHMI BioInteractive Video Channel: biointeractive Open on YouTube What the Coelacanth Actually Teaches The strongest lesson is neither that \"monsters are real\" nor that hidden animals never exist. Instead, the coelacanth encourages a more disciplined form of scientific openness. Several principles emerge: Surprising discoveries are possible, especially in poorly explored habitats. Fossil records, while powerful, are incomplete and can occasionally underestimate a lineage's survival. Extraordinary claims become convincing only when multiple forms of physical evidence accumulate. Local observations deserve investigation, but they are the beginning of scientific enquiry rather than its conclusion. The coelacanth therefore supports cautious optimism rather than unrestricted belief. It argues against dismissing every unusual animal report out of hand, while equally arguing against treating every unexplained sighting as evidence of an undiscovered species. Why the Coelacanth Remains a Useful Cautionary Example Within the broader relationship between cryptozoology and Forteana , the coelacanth occupies a unique position because it is both a genuine scientific surprise and a reminder of the limits of analogy. Its rediscovery demonstrates that nature can still confound expectations. At the same time, its success as a scientific case rested on recoverable specimens, repeatable observations, ecological consistency and decades of subsequent research. Those features"},{"title":"Proof Test","url":"/proof-test/","description":"Creature stories become cryptozoological claims only when tracks, bodies, DNA, images, and ecology can be checked against natural history.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Proof Test What Counts as Proof of a Cryptid? Proof Test Proof Test What Counts as Proof of a Cryptid? Creature stories become cryptozoological claims only when tracks, bodies, DNA, images, and ecology can be checked against natural history. Creature stories become cryptozoological claims only when tracks, bodies, DNA, images, and ecology can be checked against natural history. mechanism Why testimony is not enough Physical traces that can be checked Population evidence versus one off encounters forteana crypto vs forteana proof test Introduction The central question in cryptozoology is not whether witnesses are sincere, but what kind of evidence could establish that an unknown animal really exists. Hidden animal claims differ from many other Fortean subjects because they are ultimately constrained by biology. A large vertebrate must leave physical traces, occupy habitat, reproduce, and interact with its environment in ways that can be independently tested. The evidence threshold therefore rises far above testimony alone. A report may justify investigation, but it does not by itself demonstrate the existence of a previously unknown species or a surviving population. This distinction explains why cryptozoological debates increasingly focus on verifiable evidence rather than stories. Modern wildlife biology provides multiple ways to test claims, from environmental DNA (eDNA) and camera traps to forensic examination of tracks and genetic analysis of hair or tissue. These methods do not guarantee that every hidden animal will be found, but they establish clear standards for what would count as convincing proof. Why Testimony Is Not Enough Eyewitness reports often provide the starting point for investigations, but they are among the weakest forms of biological evidence. Human perception is vulnerable to distance, poor lighting, stress, expectation, and memory reconstruction. Independent witnesses may genuinely describe unusual experiences while still misidentifying familiar animals or environmental features. Scientists therefore treat testimony as a hypothesis generator rather than proof. A cluster of reports can identify locations worth surveying, reveal seasonal patterns, or suggest which habitats deserve closer examination. However, no amount of anecdotal consistency can substitute for evidence that can be independently examined. This approach also explains why cryptozoological claims are judged differently from folklore . A local tradition may preserve valuable ecological observations, but once the claim becomes \"an undiscovered breeding animal exists here\", it enters the realm of zoology, where repeatable physical evidence becomes essential. How we can detect pretty much anything Hélène Morlon and Anna Papadopoulou Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Which Physical Traces Can Actually Be Checked? The strongest evidence is material that other researchers can inspect, preserve, and analyse. Different forms of evidence carry different levels of confidence. Whole specimens. A complete body, skeleton, or living captured animal remains the highest standard because anatomy, genetics, and taxonomy can all be independently evaluated. Diagnostic tissue. Hair with intact follicles, blood, skin, bone, or teeth can often yield DNA suitable for comparison with known species. Environmental DNA (eDNA). Animals continuously shed genetic material into water, soil, snow, and air. Modern sequencing can detect species without directly observing them, making eDNA a powerful screening tool for elusive wildlife. However, positive results require careful controls against contamination, while negative results cannot absolutely prove absence because detection depends on sampling conditions and DNA persistence. [PMC+2National Geographic] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Properties and Limitations of eDNA Substrates for Terrestrial...by B Zhao · 2026 · Cited by 8 — Environmental DNA (eDNA) constitutes... Tracks and trace evidence. Footprints, scat, feeding marks, nests, or burrows become persuasive only when documented with scale, location, repeatability, and expert comparison against known animals. Images and video. Clear photographs or footage gain credibility when accompanied by metadata, known camera position, multiple viewpoints, and contextual information that allows size and distance to be estimated. Evidence becomes progressively stronger when several independent categories agree. For example, repeated camera trap images, matching tracks, and recoverable DNA from the same location would be substantially more persuasive than any single observation alone. Bigfoot and Loch Ness Monster DNA? Channel: TREY the Explainer Open on YouTube Why One Encounter Is Different from a Population A common misunderstanding is that one unexplained sighting automatically implies an undiscovered species. Biology requires a further step. With very rare exceptions, large vertebrates persist because they exist as breeding populations. Such populations inevitably produce ecological signatures over time: multiple individuals rather than isolated encounters; repeated detections across seasons; genetic diversity; carcasses from natural deaths; feeding signs and prey interactions; predictable habitat use. An isolated photograph or eyewitness account may suggest that something unusual occurred, but it does not explain how an entire population could survive while leaving almost no trace. As the proposed animal increases in body size, longevity, or population requirements, the expected amount of recoverable evidence also increases. This is why claims involving large mammals generally face a much higher evidential threshold than claims involving small amphibians, insects, or deep sea organisms, where undiscovered species continue to be described regularly. Modern Wildlife Methods Raise the Bar Technological advances have changed what investigators can reasonably expect to detect. Camera traps can monitor remote habitats continuously for months without disturbing wildlife. Large scale deployments routinely document elusive carnivores and other rarely seen mammals that would otherwise escape direct observation. Likewise, systematic eDNA surveys can detect animals from genetic material left behind in water or soil, often identifying species that traditional surveys miss. Used together, these techniques provide complementary evidence rather than relying solely on chance encounters. [Research Collection+2ResearchGate] research collection.ethz.ch Research Collection Comparing Watershed‐Based e DNA Sampling and Camera Research CollectionComparing Watershed‐Based eDNA Sampling and Camera...February 18, 2026 — by K Sherub · 2026 — Combined, the two metho... Published: February 18, 2026 Because these tools have become increasingly effective, prolonged absence of detectable evidence becomes more informative than it was several decades ago. It does not prove a species is impossible, but it narrows the range of plausible explanations. The Loch Ness Example Loch Ness illustrates how evidence thresholds operate in practice. [illumina.com] illumina.com loch ness edna If there is a monster in Loch Ness, this geneticist will find it10 Jan 2025 — Professor Neil Gemmell has a new plan to expand environment... For decades, eyewitness accounts, sonar anomalies, and disputed photographs sustained the idea that an unknown large creature inhabited the loch. From a Fortean perspective, those reports remain culturally significant regardless of their biological interpretation. From a cryptozoological perspective, however, the key question is whether the loch contains evidence consistent with an undiscovered large vertebrate. A comprehensive environmental DNA survey sampled hundreds of locations throughout the loch and compared recovered genetic material with known species. Researchers found abundant evidence for familiar organisms, including substantial eel DNA, but no genetic evidence supporting the presence of large reptiles or other proposed \"monster\" candidates. The findings did not absolutely exclude every possibility, yet they substantially reduced the plausibility of claims involving a persistent population of large unknown aquatic animals. [University of Otago+2illumina.com] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sept 2019 — DNA from each sample was captured, extracted and... The case demonstrates an important principle: scientific investigations rarely prove a negative with complete certainty, but accumulating high quality negative evidence can make particular hypotheses increasingly difficult to sustain. Science Stories: Loch Ness eDNA results, Poop Knives, and Skeleton Lovers Channel: TREY the Explainer Open on YouTube What Counts as Proof? No single checklist guarantees acceptance, but hidden animal claims become genuinely compelling when several independent lines of evidence converge. A convincing case would"},{"title":"Ness DNA","url":"/ness-dna/","description":"Environmental DNA turned Nessie from a famous mystery into a sharper question about what large animals Loch Ness could realistically support.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Ness DNA What Did DNA Do to Nessie? Ness DNA Ness DNA What Did DNA Do to Nessie? Environmental DNA turned Nessie from a famous mystery into a sharper question about what large animals Loch Ness could realistically support. Environmental DNA turned Nessie from a famous mystery into a sharper question about what large animals Loch Ness could realistically support. dataset evidence The monster claim as biology What the Loch Ness DNA search tested Why eel explanations changed the debate forteana crypto vs forteana ness dna Introduction The environmental DNA (eDNA) survey of Loch Ness changed the debate about the Loch Ness Monster by reframing it as a biological question rather than simply a legendary mystery. Instead of searching directly for a creature, researchers asked a more fundamental question: what animals actually leave detectable genetic traces in the loch? The results did not identify an unknown large vertebrate, but they did provide the most comprehensive genetic snapshot yet of the loch's ecosystem. That did not \"disprove\" every possible monster claim, but it substantially narrowed which explanations remain biologically plausible. Within the broader world of Forteana , the study illustrates how folklore can persist even as scientific evidence reshapes the range of realistic interpretations. The monster claim as biology For decades, many proposed explanations for Nessie assumed the presence of a large, permanent animal population. Suggestions have ranged from surviving prehistoric reptiles and giant fish to oversized amphibians and unknown mammals. All of these ideas share one requirement: if such animals inhabit Loch Ness, they should leave biological evidence behind. Environmental DNA offers a way to test that expectation. Every aquatic organism sheds tiny fragments of genetic material into its surroundings through skin cells, mucus, waste and decaying tissue. By collecting water samples and sequencing the DNA they contain, researchers can identify many of the species living in a body of water without ever capturing or directly observing them. This approach does not search for a specific monster. Instead, it asks whether the ecosystem contains evidence consistent with the kinds of animals proposed in monster theories. In that sense, the Loch Ness project became less a monster hunt than a large scale biodiversity survey using genetics. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sep 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Gem... Loch Ness monster might just be a giant eel Channel: KiniTV Open on YouTube What the Loch Ness DNA search tested The project, led by Professor Neil Gemmell of the University of Otago, sampled approximately 250 water samples from locations around the loch, including surface water, deep water and shoreline sites. Researchers extracted DNA from each sample and compared millions of genetic sequences against international reference databases to identify the organisms present. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sep 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Gem... The survey was designed to evaluate several broad classes of explanations for Nessie rather than to prove or disprove folklore itself. Among the principal questions were whether the loch contained evidence for: Large reptiles, such as the frequently proposed plesiosaur hypothesis. Large exotic fish, including catfish or sturgeon. Unexpected vertebrates that should be detectable through their DNA. Known native species that might account for eyewitness reports. The findings were striking in what they did—and did not—show. Researchers found abundant DNA from the organisms expected in a Highland freshwater ecosystem, including numerous fish, birds, mammals, amphibians and microorganisms. However, they found no evidence of reptilian DNA that would support the idea of a surviving prehistoric reptile. Likewise, they detected no genetic evidence for large resident animals such as catfish or sturgeon, both of which have occasionally been proposed as explanations for sightings. [University of Otago+2BioTechniques] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sep 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Gem... Importantly, the study did not claim that every individual animal in the loch would inevitably be detected. Environmental DNA depends on organisms shedding sufficient genetic material into sampled water, and extremely rare or transient visitors might escape detection. Nevertheless, the absence of DNA from any population of large unknown vertebrates substantially weakens hypotheses that require such animals to inhabit Loch Ness over long periods. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sep 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Gem... Professor Neil Gemmell prepares for his trip to Loch Ness in the hunt for Nessie Channel: University of Otago Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka Open on YouTube Why eel explanations changed the debate The most discussed outcome was not what was absent, but what was abundant. European eel DNA appeared in large quantities throughout the samples. This did not demonstrate the existence of giant eels, because environmental DNA measures genetic presence rather than body size. A large amount of eel DNA could simply reflect many ordinary eels rather than a few exceptionally large ones. Professor Gemmell emphasised precisely this limitation. [University of Otago] otago.ac.nz first edna study of loch ness points to something fishy University of OtagoFirst eDNA Study Of Loch Ness Points To Something Fishy5 Sep 2019 — University of Otago geneticist, Professor Neil Gem... Even so, the findings shifted the conversation because the eel hypothesis requires far fewer extraordinary assumptions than prehistoric survivors or unknown reptiles. European eels are already known to inhabit Loch Ness. If some individuals reached unusual sizes—or if ordinary eels were occasionally seen under poor viewing conditions—they could contribute to at least some reported sightings. The study therefore altered the hierarchy of explanations: Plesiosaur style creatures became considerably less plausible because no reptilian DNA was detected. Large introduced fish , such as catfish or sturgeon, also lost support through the absence of their genetic signatures. Large eels remained biologically conceivable, although unproven, because eels are unquestionably present in the loch and the DNA evidence is entirely consistent with their existence. [The Guardian+2lochnessproject.org] theguardian.com The GuardianLoch Ness monster could be a giant eel, say scientists5 Sep 2019 — But Prof Neil Gemmell, who led the study, said he couldn't... The key distinction is that the DNA evidence does not positively identify giant eels. Rather, it removes many alternative biological explanations while leaving one comparatively ordinary possibility still open. What environmental DNA can and cannot prove The Loch Ness survey demonstrated the power of environmental DNA as an ecological tool, but it also highlighted its limits. Environmental DNA excels at answering questions about the overall composition of an ecosystem. It can reveal which species are present, detect invasive organisms and monitor biodiversity without extensive trapping or visual surveys. These strengths have made it increasingly important in conservation biology and wildlife management well beyond Loch Ness. [hudsonalpha.org] hudsonalpha.org How the Loch Ness monster could help save marine life23 Sept 2019 — The researchers sequenced DNA from 250 samples of Loch Ness water, co... However, eDNA has important constraints: It cannot reliably estimate the size of individual animals. It cannot determine whether DNA came from one large individual or many smaller ones. Rare, short lived or recently arrived organisms may not be detected. Detection depends on available genetic reference databases and successful sampling. Consequently, the absence of unknown monster DNA is powerful evidence against a long term resident population of large undiscovered vertebrates, but it is not a logical proof that no unusual animal could ever pass through the loch. DNA Evidence Reveals The Loch Ness Monster Could Be A Giant Eel Loch Ness The New Evidence Channel: Discovery UK Open on YouTube Why Nessie remains a Fortean icon The environmental DNA survey transformed Nessie from a broad mystery into a more sharply defined scientific question. It demonstrated that cryptozoological claims can be evaluated using modern ecological methods"},{"title":"Thylacine","url":"/thylacine/","description":"Thylacine reports keep a lost animal alive in public memory, but cryptozoology asks why conclusive traces have not appeared since 1936.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Crypto vs Forteana","level":3,"text":"Thylacine Why Do Thylacine Sightings Persist? Thylacine Thylacine Why Do Thylacine Sightings Persist? Thylacine reports keep a lost animal alive in public memory, but cryptozoology asks why conclusive traces have not appeared since 1936. Thylacine reports keep a lost animal alive in public memory, but cryptozoology asks why conclusive traces have not appeared since 1936. event time window The official extinction problem Night sightings and suitable habitat Bodies, DNA, roadkill, and missing proof forteana crypto vs forteana thylacine Introduction Reports of living thylacines have continued for decades after the species was officially declared extinct, making the animal one of cryptozoology's most persistent test cases. Unlike legendary creatures with no accepted historical existence, the thylacine unquestionably existed, survived into the twentieth century, and disappeared under intense human pressure. That history gives modern sighting claims unusual emotional force. The central question is not whether the species once lived, but whether a remnant population escaped detection after the death of the last confirmed captive animal in 1936. Within the broader overlap between cryptozoology and Forteana , the thylacine occupies a distinctive position because the claim is biologically testable. A surviving population of a dog sized apex predator should eventually produce bodies, genetic material, reliable photographs, breeding evidence or repeated physical traces. Thousands of reported encounters therefore create a tension: sincere witness testimony continues, yet conclusive biological evidence remains absent. The official extinction problem The last confirmed wild thylacine was captured in 1933 and died in Hobart Zoo in September 1936. Although the species was not formally declared extinct until decades later, conservation authorities and the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) regard the available evidence as showing that no verified population survived into modern times. The IUCN notes that numerous reports have continued since 1936 but that none has produced conclusive proof. [IUCN Red List+2cites.org] iucnredlist.org IUCN Red ListThylacinus cynocephalus, ThylacineListed as Extinct. The last confirmed record of a wild individual is from 1933; it was cap... This distinction is important. Official extinction is not based on the absence of rumours but on the failure of repeated investigations to obtain verifiable evidence despite many opportunities. Governments, wildlife agencies and independent researchers have repeatedly examined reports, installed camera traps and reviewed witness statements without recovering an authenticated specimen. At the same time, extinction is rarely a precise moment. Statistical models that combine confirmed records with unconfirmed sightings have suggested the species may have survived in remote parts of Tasmania for several years after the last captive animal died. One widely discussed analysis estimated the most probable extinction date around 1940, while acknowledging considerable uncertainty. Other modelling exercises, using different assumptions about witness reliability, have proposed survival into later decades, but these conclusions depend heavily on how much weight is assigned to unverified reports rather than new physical discoveries. [conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com+2ResearchGate] conbio.onlinelibrary.wiley.com Estimating the extinction date of the thylacine with mixed...by CJ Carlson · 2018 · Cited by 20 — Although the last captive individual d... Why I Believe The Tasmanian Tiger Is Still Alive... Channel: Forrest Galante Open on YouTube Why do night time sightings continue? Most post 1936 reports share striking similarities. Witnesses typically describe a medium sized animal crossing a road at dusk or night, often emphasising: a stiff, thick tail held almost straight behind the body; a sloping back; dark stripes across the hindquarters; an unusual, bounding or awkward gait; behaviour unlike a domestic dog. These recurring descriptions explain why many witnesses remain convinced they saw something unusual rather than simply misidentifying common wildlife. Large areas of western and south western Tasmania are mountainous, densely forested and sparsely inhabited. From a cryptozoological perspective, such landscapes appear capable of concealing elusive animals for extended periods. Searchers frequently argue that modern maps underestimate how little of this terrain is regularly visited on foot. Journalistic accounts of long running searches note that even experienced wildlife biologists acknowledge the landscape's remoteness while still judging long term survival to be extremely unlikely. [The New Yorker] newyorker.com The New Yorker The Obsessive Search for the Tasmanian Tiger Thought to be extinct since the 1930s following widespread hunting and habitat destruction, numerous sightings have been reported and uno... However, suitable habitat alone is not enough. Habitat can explain why occasional encounters might be difficult, but it cannot explain the continued absence of a breeding population's cumulative ecological footprint. Why eyewitness testimony has not resolved the question The thylacine illustrates a recurring problem in cryptozoology: large numbers of witnesses do not automatically strengthen a biological claim if the reports cannot be independently verified. Researchers who have catalogued sightings note that witness quality varies enormously. Some observers are experienced bush workers or park staff. Others report only a brief glimpse under poor lighting conditions. Reports also tend to cluster after periods of intense media attention, suggesting that publicity increases both observation and interpretation. Investigators have documented several recurring sources of error: unusual views of dogs with erect tails or mange; foxes or feral dogs seen briefly at night; striped lighting effects across an animal's hindquarters; expectations created by publicity surrounding recent searches; retrospective embellishment as memories are retold over many years. This does not imply that witnesses are dishonest. Rather, cryptozoology distinguishes between sincerity and reliability. An honest observer can still misidentify an unfamiliar animal during a fleeting encounter. Extinct No More Tasmanian Tiger Filmed on Mainland Australia (Oct 2025) YOWZA Channel: ambiguous world Open on YouTube Bodies, DNA, roadkill and the missing proof The strongest argument against survival is not the absence of sightings but the absence of physical evidence expected from any surviving carnivore population. A viable breeding population would be expected eventually to leave multiple independent forms of evidence, including: road killed animals; fresh skeletal remains; verified hair or tissue samples; diagnostic scat containing recoverable DNA; repeatable environmental DNA from waterways or soil; high quality camera trap photographs; evidence of breeding individuals across multiple generations. Despite repeated searches and continuing public interest, none of these evidence types has produced an authenticated modern thylacine. Claims occasionally surface involving photographs, alleged DNA samples or carcasses, but these have not survived independent scientific scrutiny. Several widely publicised images have proved too indistinct for identification, while purported genetic evidence has either lacked proper provenance, failed replication or has not been published in a form allowing independent verification. Government investigations and academic researchers therefore continue to regard the evidential record as insufficient. [CBS News+2cites.org] cbsnews.com tasmanian tiger sightings investigated CBS NewsScientists investigate Tasmanian tiger sightingsMar 31, 2017 — Since then, no conclusive evidence has emerged to suggest that Tas... This evidential gap grows more significant with time. A hidden predator can evade observation, but sustaining an undiscovered breeding population for many decades without producing authenticated remains or genetic material becomes progressively less plausible. Why the debate remains alive The persistence of thylacine sightings reflects more than unresolved zoology. The animal has become a symbol of extinction, regret and the hope that humanity may not have completely erased a remarkable species. This symbolic role helps explain why new reports attract attention even when previous investigations have proved inconclusive. Every credible witness appears to reopen the possibility that the official story is incomplete. Advances in trail cameras, environmental DNA sampling and automated wildlife monitoring also encourage the belief that improved technology could eventually settle the question. Yet these same technologies strengthen the sceptical position as well. Camera trap networks now document elusive mammals across the world with increasing efficiency, and environmental DNA"},{"title":"Folklore","url":"/folklore/","description":"Folklore explains why some strange reports survive even when proof remains weak or disputed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Folklore Why Strange Stories Survive Without Proof Folklore Folklore Why Strange Stories Survive Without Proof Folklore explains why some strange reports survive even when proof remains weak or disputed. Folklore explains why some strange reports survive even when proof remains weak or disputed. population context Stories as cultural evidence Local identity and repetition Meaning versus confirmation forteana folklore Introduction Folklore is not proof that a strange event happened exactly as told. In Forteana , its value is different: folklore shows what a community found memorable, frightening, useful, funny, sacred, shameful or identity forming enough to repeat. A ghost, cryptid, UFO rumour or local curse may survive for decades with weak physical evidence because it does cultural work. It gives a place a story, turns uncertainty into shared language, warns outsiders, explains danger, marks grief, or makes a landscape feel charged with meaning. Folklore is therefore evidence of meaning rather than confirmation of fact. That distinction is crucial. A legend can be historically inaccurate and still reveal real patterns of belief, anxiety, belonging and memory. Folklorists treat such stories as part of living expressive culture: traditions, narratives and practices shared within groups and passed through speech, imitation, performance and now digital circulation. The Fortean reader’s task is not to ask only “Did this creature, ghost or omen exist?” but also “Why did this story become worth keeping?” [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of Congress Collections Policy Statement: Folklore and Folklife The Library of CongressCollections Policy Statement: Folklore and FolklifeMay 5, 2022 — This statement refers to all materials that perta... Published: May 5, 2022 Stories as cultural evidence A strange story becomes folklore when it travels beyond a single incident and enters shared use. It may be retold at sleepovers, in pubs, on local tours, in family warnings, in newspaper anniversaries, on podcasts, in memes, or through festival culture. Each retelling can alter details while preserving a recognisable core. That mutability is not a defect from a folklore perspective; it is one sign that the story is being adapted to new audiences and needs. This is why folklore sits naturally inside Forteana. Fortean material often begins as a reported anomaly: a figure on a road, lights over a town, a monster in a lake, a curse attached to a bridge, footsteps in an empty building. Folklore explains why some of those reports keep circulating even when photographs are disputed, witnesses disagree, official evidence is absent, or sceptical explanations exist. Survival does not prove the anomaly, but it does prove that the story has become socially meaningful. The Library of Congress defines folklore and folklife as dynamic expressive culture and everyday practice shared within groups, including beliefs, narratives, customs and symbolic forms. The legal definition of American folklife similarly stresses expressive culture shared within familial, ethnic, occupational, religious and regional groups, usually maintained without formal instruction. Those definitions matter for Forteana because many strange stories are not merely claims about things “out there”; they are forms of group expression. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of Congress Collections Policy Statement: Folklore and Folklife The Library of CongressCollections Policy Statement: Folklore and FolklifeMay 5, 2022 — This statement refers to all materials that perta... Published: May 5, 2022 Folklorist Alan Dundes argued that folklore offers a people’s “unselfconscious picture of themselves”. That is one of the most useful principles for reading Fortean traditions. A monster story may reveal less about zoology than about a community’s sense of wilderness, danger, marginality or pride. A ghost story may reveal how people talk about unfinished business, inheritance, trauma, guilt or the persistence of the dead in ordinary life. A UFO legend may reveal hopes and fears about technology, secrecy, invasion, salvation or official distrust. [JSTOR] jstor.org The Analytical Essays of Alan DundesJuly 1, 2007 — Dundes argued that in folklore, more than in other forms of human evidence, “one... Published: July 1, 2007 This does not mean that every strange story is “only symbolic”. The stronger point is that folklore can preserve several kinds of evidence at once: Evidence of belief: what people considered possible, plausible or worth debating. Evidence of place: which roads, bridges, lakes, woods, ruins or houses became charged with story. Evidence of social pressure: what a community feared, mocked, protected or warned against. Evidence of repetition: which details proved durable enough to survive retelling. Evidence of uncertainty: where proof remained weak but meaning remained strong. That last category is especially Fortean. Folklore keeps open a zone between fact and invention. It allows a story to matter without forcing it to pass as laboratory evidence. Why legends survive when proof is weak The most durable strange stories usually do more than entertain. They solve a social or emotional problem. They give shape to fear, turn a confusing place into a memorable one, or let people discuss forbidden subjects indirectly. A ghost story can speak about bereavement without sounding like a sermon. A cryptid legend can make a neglected town distinctive. A UFO rumour can condense suspicion of military secrecy into a single memorable image in the sky. Contemporary legend scholarship is useful here because it treats “ urban legends ” not as trivial lies but as living narratives that communicate and negotiate anomalous experience . The International Society for Contemporary Legend Research notes that such legends are not purely modern inventions; similar processes of using narrative to handle anomalous experience can be traced across long periods, and supposedly local stories often appear in multiple forms around the world. [contemporarylegend.org] contemporarylegend.org International Society for Contemporary Legend Research...Contemporary legends are contemporary to the teller and audience, not solely to... That pattern is visible across Forteana. A haunted road is rarely just a road. It may become a place where teenagers test courage, where parents warn children away from danger, where a local death is remembered, or where outsiders are marked as outsiders because they do not know the story. A monster lake is rarely just a lake. It becomes a stage on which tourism , regional pride, scepticism, media attention and wonder all meet. Folklorists also use the concept of ostension , meaning cases where people act out, perform, or interpret real events through the frame of an existing legend. This is important for Fortean material because stories do not merely describe behaviour; they can shape it. People visit haunted sites because they have heard the legend. They interpret an ambiguous sound as evidence because the story has primed them. They may even stage events that resemble the legend. In such cases, folklore becomes part of the event world it claims to describe. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore This helps explain why weak proof does not necessarily weaken a legend. In ordinary evidential terms, a disputed photograph or vague sighting may reduce confidence. In folklore terms, dispute can keep the story alive by giving people something to argue over. The lack of closure becomes part of the attraction. Local identity and repetition Fortean folklore often survives because it belongs somewhere. A story gains power when it attaches itself to a recognisable landscape: the Pine Barrens, Loch Ness , Point Pleasant, an old theatre, a lonely bridge, a moor, a stretch of coast. The location gives the story texture, and the story gives the location personality. Scholar S. Elizabeth Bird’s work on local legends of place argues that such narratives help people construct a sense of place and cultural identity, including ideas about who belongs and who does not. Local legends may not reliably preserve “history” in a narrow factual sense, but they reveal how people make moral, emotional and social sense of landscape. [Academia] academia.edu Open source on academia.edu. The Jersey Devil is a clear example. The creature is tied to the New Jersey Pine Barrens and has circulated in varied forms for more than two centuries. Atlantic County’s own historical account describes the Jersey Devil as an authentic folk legend whose details vary with the people who tell it, blending folk belief with South Jersey history. The Pinelands Preservation Alliance similarly presents the creature as part of the cultural history of the region, not simply as an unresolved zoological claim. [Atlantic County NJ] atlanticcountynj.gov Atlantic County NJJersey Devil Atlantic County NJJersey Devil That"},{"title":"Haunted Roads","url":"/haunted-roads/","description":"Haunted-road legends often preserve local warnings about danger, grief and belonging long after the original facts become uncertain.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Haunted Roads Why Do Haunted Roads Keep Warning People? Haunted Roads Haunted Roads Why Do Haunted Roads Keep Warning People? Haunted road legends often preserve local warnings about danger, grief and belonging long after the original facts become uncertain. Haunted road legends often preserve local warnings about danger, grief and belonging long after the original facts become uncertain. population context Roads as places of danger and memory How retellings turn warnings into folklore When weak evidence still shapes behavior forteana folklore haunted roads Introduction Haunted road legends endure because they do more than claim that a ghost appears on a particular stretch of tarmac. They preserve a community's sense that a place demands caution, respect or remembrance even after the original event has faded from living memory. Within Fortean traditions, the road itself becomes a repository for grief, danger and local identity. Whether the reported figure is a phantom hitchhiker, a child crossing the road, or a mysterious woman in white, the recurring warning often outlives any verifiable historical trigger. The legend's persistence is therefore less about proving supernatural events than about explaining why certain places continue to feel significant and why people keep treating them differently. Roads as places of danger and memory Roads are unusual settings for folklore because they are transitional spaces. People rarely linger on them, yet countless lives pass through them. They connect communities while also exposing travellers to unfamiliar landscapes, darkness, weather and high speed risk. A fatal accident, violent crime or unexplained disappearance can therefore become attached to the location in a particularly durable way. Unlike a ruined castle or abandoned house, a road continues to be used every day. Every passing driver becomes a potential audience for the story. This constant reuse allows legends to remain active rather than becoming historical curiosities. Many haunted road traditions also overlap with real practices of remembrance. Roadside memorials, flowers, crosses and plaques publicly mark places where sudden deaths occurred. These memorials remind motorists that an ordinary stretch of road has become emotionally charged, reinforcing the idea that tragedy remains present in the landscape long after the event itself. Research into roadside memorials has found that they serve not only private grief but also public warning, encouraging safer driving and preserving the memory of unexpected deaths. [Archaeology and Material Culture] paulmullins.wordpress.com Archaeology and Material Culture The Archaeology of Roadside Memorials Archaeology and Material CultureThe Archaeology of Roadside MemorialsJuly 24, 2012 — 24 Jul 2012 — Roadside shrines spring up when people... Published: July 24, 2012 From a Fortean perspective, this matters because folklore and memorial practice can reinforce one another. A place already associated with loss becomes more receptive to ghost stories, while ghost stories encourage later generations to notice and remember the location. 12 Haunted Roads No One Dares to Drive at Night Stories Brought to Life Channel: History & Legends United States Open on YouTube How retellings turn warnings into folklore Most haunted road legends evolve through repetition rather than eyewitness certainty. Each retelling may alter names, dates or details, yet the central lesson remains stable. Common recurring patterns include: a ghostly pedestrian appearing where fatal accidents have occurred; a phantom hitchhiker who disappears before reaching a destination; [youtube.com] youtube.com 12 Haunted Roads No One Dares to Drive at Night Stories Brought to Life Lydia, The Phantom Hitchhiker... mysterious lights encouraging drivers to slow down; invisible hazards linked to sharp bends, bridges or isolated junctions; warnings never to stop, turn around or mock the location. These narratives work because they are memorable behavioural scripts. Even listeners who doubt the supernatural often remember the practical advice attached to the tale. Folklorists have long noted that legends survive by adapting to new audiences while preserving their social function. The precise ghost may change, but the warning remains recognisable. In this way, haunted roads become examples of folklore acting as a cultural memory system rather than a fixed historical record. The famous stories surrounding Blue Bell Hill in Kent illustrate this process. Different versions describe different apparitions , including a young woman or child appearing in the road, yet the repeated theme is always the same: drivers unexpectedly encounter someone where previous tragedy is believed to have occurred. Over decades, reports have varied considerably, but the location itself has retained its reputation as a place demanding heightened attention. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia A229 road A229 road When weak evidence still shapes behaviour One of the most interesting features of haunted road traditions is that they often influence behaviour regardless of whether people believe the supernatural explanation. Drivers may: reduce their speed near a supposedly haunted bend; avoid stopping after dark; refuse to use a particular shortcut at night; point out the location to passengers; preserve local customs connected with the site. From a Fortean perspective, this demonstrates an important distinction between factual proof and cultural influence. The available evidence for ghosts on particular roads is generally weak, inconsistent or anecdotal. Investigations of recurring motifs such as phantom hitchhikers have repeatedly concluded that these stories fit well established folklore patterns and lack reliable corroborating evidence for paranormal claims. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Vanishing hitchhiker Vanishing hitchhiker Yet the behavioural consequences can be entirely real. If a dangerous bend becomes famous through legend, more motorists may approach it cautiously. Even when the original accident has been forgotten or historical details become confused, the folklore continues performing a practical function. Pennsylvania Legends & Lore: Ghosts of the Commonwealth Channel: wpsu Open on YouTube Why particular roads become legendary Not every dangerous road becomes haunted. Several conditions appear to make a location especially likely to acquire enduring supernatural traditions. First, there is usually an emotionally powerful event, such as a fatal collision, unexplained disappearance or violent death. Second, the landscape itself often supports storytelling. Long wooded roads, isolated bridges, tunnels and stretches with limited visibility naturally encourage uncertainty and imaginative interpretation. Third, repetition matters. Local newspapers , family stories, paranormal books, television programmes and online discussions continually renew awareness of the location. Each generation inherits the legend while adapting it to contemporary expectations. Finally, ambiguity helps rather than harms the legend. Because witnesses rarely agree completely, the story remains flexible enough to absorb new experiences. A driver who glimpses an unexpected figure in fog or poor lighting may reinterpret an ordinary perception through an already familiar narrative. Haunted roads as evidence of meaning Within the broader study of Forteana , haunted roads demonstrate why folklore should not be evaluated solely as failed history or unverified paranormal testimony. Their greatest value lies in revealing how communities preserve meaning. A haunted road can simultaneously function as: a memorial for sudden loss; a warning about genuine physical danger; a shared local identity marker; a rite of passage for younger generations who dare one another to visit; a continuing reminder that some places are believed to deserve caution and respect. The ghost itself may never be verified, but the warning survives because it continues to satisfy emotional, social and practical needs. The road becomes a place where grief is remembered, danger is made memorable and uncertainty is transformed into a story that people are still willing to pass on. 15 Most Haunted Roads on Earth Channel: Top Fives Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Do Haunted Roads Keep Warning People?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Why People Believe Weird Things By Michael Shermer Helps readers evaluate enduring supernatural claims critically. See on Amazon Book The Vanishing Hitchhiker: American Urban Legends and Their Me... By Jan Harold Brunvand Covers phantom hitchhikers and warning legends directly. See on Amazon Book Ghostland: In Search of a Haunted Country By Edward Parnell Examines haunted landscapes and cultural memory. See on Amazon Book The Penguin Book of Ghosts By Jacqueline Simpson Provides folklore context for haunted places and roads."},{"title":"Jersey Devil","url":"/jersey-devil/","description":"The Jersey Devil shows how a disputed creature can become a durable symbol of wilderness, regional pride and local storytelling.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Jersey Devil How the Jersey Devil Became Pine Barrens Identity Jersey Devil Jersey Devil How the Jersey Devil Became Pine Barrens Identity The Jersey Devil shows how a disputed creature can become a durable symbol of wilderness, regional pride and local storytelling. The Jersey Devil shows how a disputed creature can become a durable symbol of wilderness, regional pride and local storytelling. entity The Pine Barrens as legend landscape Family curse, wilderness and local reputation Why sceptical retellings still strengthen the legend forteana folklore jersey devil Introduction The Jersey Devil is one of the clearest examples in American folklore of a legendary creature becoming inseparable from a particular landscape. Whether or not anyone accepts the creature as real, the legend has helped define the identity of New Jersey's Pine Barrens for more than a century. It transforms an extensive forest into a place of mystery, marks the region as culturally distinct from surrounding urban areas, and gives residents a story that is recognised far beyond the state itself. Within Forteana , this makes the Jersey Devil valuable not simply as a disputed cryptid but as evidence of how folklore can shape regional identity even when the underlying events remain unverified. [National Geographic] nationalgeographic.com the devil went down to new jersey The author took a dim view of the...Read more... Unlike many monster traditions that drift between locations, the Jersey Devil is firmly anchored to one landscape. The creature's identity, the Pine Barrens and local history have become so closely linked that discussing one almost inevitably leads to the others. The Pine Barrens as legend landscape The New Jersey Pine Barrens cover more than one million acres of sandy forests, cedar swamps and sparsely populated settlements. For centuries the region's isolation made it appear unfamiliar and even threatening to outsiders. Poor roads, scattered communities and extensive woodland encouraged stories that blurred the boundary between ordinary wildlife and supernatural encounters. [Atlantic County NJ] atlanticcountynj.gov Atlantic County NJJersey Devil Fact or Fiction?The birthplace of the Jersey Devil is called the Pine Barrens. The Devil's Origins. One of the most famous stories tells... Folklore thrives in places where geography leaves room for uncertainty. The Pine Barrens provided several conditions that encouraged this process: Dense woodland where unusual sounds were difficult to identify. Sparse settlement, allowing stories to spread without frequent contradiction. A long history of travellers describing the region as remote and dangerous. Existing traditions involving ghosts , hidden treasure and mysterious figures, into which the Jersey Devil naturally fitted. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jersey Devil Jersey Devil The landscape therefore became more than the alleged habitat of a creature. It became a stage on which local identity was performed. Visitors entered expecting mystery, while residents inherited stories that reinforced the uniqueness of their home. This relationship runs both ways. The Pine Barrens make the Jersey Devil believable within local imagination, while the Jersey Devil gives the Pine Barrens a distinctive symbolic identity unlike any other American forest. How New Jersey's Pine Barrens became The Creepiest Place in America (The Jersey Devil ) Channel: IT'S HISTORY Open on YouTube Family curse, wilderness and local reputation The best known version of the legend centres on Mother Leeds, who supposedly cursed her unborn thirteenth child, declaring that it should become the devil. After birth the infant transformed into a winged creature and escaped into the surrounding forest. Numerous versions exist, differing in dates, locations and details, but nearly all connect the creature directly with the Pine Barrens. [Pinelands Alliance+2Atlantic County NJ] pinelandsalliance.org Pinelands AllianceThe Jersey Devil and FolkloreThe most widely held belief about the origin of the Jersey Devil is that Mrs. Leeds, a res... Modern historical research has complicated this familiar story. Historian Brian Regal argues that the legend grew partly from political and religious disputes surrounding the Leeds family in colonial New Jersey rather than from a single supernatural tale. Daniel Leeds and later his son Titan Leeds became controversial figures because of conflicts with Quaker authorities and political rivals. Over time, references to the \"Leeds Devil\" gradually evolved into the more familiar \"Jersey Devil\". Benjamin Franklin's rivalry with Titan Leeds over competing almanacs contributed to the family's increasingly demonic public image. [njs.libraries.rutgers.edu+2njhumanities.org] njs.libraries.rutgers.edu View of \"THE JERSEY DEVIL: A POLITICAL ANIMAL\"by B Regal · 2015 · Cited by 5 — The only element of the legend with historical connection... From a folklore perspective, this historical complexity strengthens rather than weakens the legend. It demonstrates how monster traditions often emerge through the blending of several elements: Family reputation and local gossip. Religious and political conflict. Isolated landscapes associated with danger. Oral storytelling that reshapes historical memory over generations. Instead of originating from one dramatic event, the Jersey Devil appears to have accumulated symbolic meaning through repeated retelling, with each generation adapting the story to its own understanding of the Pine Barrens. The wilderness itself also became part of the creature's character. The Devil is rarely imagined wandering cities or farmland. It belongs to forests, swamps and forgotten roads, making the landscape an active participant in the legend rather than merely its setting. The True Story of the Jersey Devil Channel: New Jersey Office of the Governor Open on YouTube Why sceptical retellings still strengthen the legend The Jersey Devil provides an instructive example of how scepticism can reinforce folklore instead of eliminating it. Natural explanations have been proposed for generations. Suggested causes include misidentified birds or mammals, fabricated stories, exaggerated newspaper reports, children's cautionary tales and misunderstandings shaped by the reputation of the Pine Barrens. Researchers have also pointed to the famous wave of reported sightings in January 1909 as an example of mass social excitement amplified by newspapers rather than evidence of an unknown creature. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jersey Devil Jersey Devil Yet these sceptical interpretations rarely displace the legend. Instead, they become part of it. Each anniversary article, historical investigation or television documentary introduces the Jersey Devil to new audiences. Even when the conclusion is that there is no convincing biological evidence for the creature, the story itself receives another retelling. This keeps the Pine Barrens culturally associated with mystery while allowing readers to enjoy the tradition without committing to belief. The legend has also become a source of regional pride. It appears in tourism , museums, local festivals, sports branding, books and educational programmes exploring New Jersey history. In these settings, the Devil functions less as a literal monster than as an emblem of a landscape with its own distinctive character. [Atlantic County NJ] atlanticcountynj.gov Atlantic County NJJersey Devil Fact or Fiction?The birthplace of the Jersey Devil is called the Pine Barrens. The Devil's Origins. One of the most famous stories tells... For Fortean studies, this is especially revealing. The enduring importance of the Jersey Devil lies not in demonstrating that an unknown animal inhabits the Pine Barrens, but in showing how a disputed creature can become part of a community's cultural identity. The legend survives because it gives people a memorable way to talk about wilderness, local history, isolation and belonging. The repeated cycle of belief, doubt , investigation and retelling ensures that the Jersey Devil remains both a cryptid and a symbol of the Pine Barrens themselves. The Jersey Devil The History Behind New Jersey's Most Famous Cryptid Channel: Mythology & Fiction Explained Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How the Jersey Devil Became Pine Barrens Identity. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The vanishing hitchhiker By Jan Harold Brunvand Explains how legends become embedded in culture and place. See on Amazon Book American monsters By Linda S. Godfrey Provides broader context for legendary creatures tied to local identity. See on Amazon Book Monsters of New Jersey By Loren Coleman, Bruce G. Hallenbeck Focuses on New Jersey monster traditions including the Jersey Devil. See on Amazon Book Weird New Jersey By Mark Moran and Mark Sceurman Features the Jersey Devil and the folklore of the Pine Barrens. See on Amazon Browse"},{"title":"Loch Ness","url":"/loch-ness/","description":"Nessie shows how repeated sightings, disputed images and visitor culture can turn uncertainty into a place brand.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Loch Ness How Nessie Turned Mystery Into Place Branding Loch Ness Loch Ness How Nessie Turned Mystery Into Place Branding Nessie shows how repeated sightings, disputed images and visitor culture can turn uncertainty into a place brand. Nessie shows how repeated sightings, disputed images and visitor culture can turn uncertainty into a place brand. entity From sightings to global recognition Tourism, landscape and Highland mystery Why disputed proof can still attract visitors forteana folklore loch ness Introduction Loch Ness is one of the clearest examples in Forteana of how an unresolved mystery can reshape the identity of a place. Whether the Loch Ness Monster exists is a separate question from what the legend has achieved. Repeated sightings, famous but disputed photographs, scientific expeditions and decades of media attention have transformed a Highland freshwater loch into a globally recognised destination. The enduring appeal of Nessie demonstrates that uncertainty itself can become culturally and economically valuable. Rather than diminishing interest, the lack of conclusive proof has encouraged visitors to experience the landscape for themselves, making Loch Ness a case study in how folklore becomes evidence of meaning rather than evidence of a monster. [VisitScotland] visitscotland.com Visit Scotland The Loch Ness Monster The Loch Ness Monster Is Nessie Real?What does Nessie look like? Loch Ness Monster is said to be long, green, and mysterio... From sightings to global recognition Although stories connected with Loch Ness are often traced to the account of Saint Columba in the sixth century, the modern Nessie phenomenon dates from 1933. Reports of an unusual creature, widely circulated through newspapers , coincided with improved road access around the loch, making it easier for visitors to travel there and witness the landscape first hand. Public fascination expanded rapidly as newspapers published eyewitness accounts and, in 1934, the famous \"Surgeon's Photograph\", long accepted by many as convincing evidence before eventually being exposed as an elaborate hoax. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster The exposure of that photograph did not end the legend. Instead, it became part of the story. Every claimed photograph, sonar contact or eyewitness report now joins a historical sequence in which evidence is debated rather than settled. This continuous cycle of claim, investigation and reinterpretation keeps the mystery alive without requiring definitive confirmation. For students of folklore, this persistence is significant. Nessie has survived because the legend continually adapts to new forms of evidence and new media. Newspaper reports became television documentaries, which became internet forums, livestreamed searches and digital sighting registers. The narrative remains active because each generation can participate in testing, questioning or retelling it. The Loch Ness Monster Explained: Saints, Hoaxes, DNA Tests, and Why Nessie Endures Channel: Terra Glitch Open on YouTube Tourism, landscape and Highland mystery Loch Ness is marketed not simply as a location where a monster might live, but as a landscape whose atmosphere encourages imagination. Tourism campaigns consistently combine the immense depth of the loch, dramatic Highland scenery, ruined castles and stories of unexplained sightings into a single visitor experience. The natural environment becomes inseparable from the legend itself. [Visit Inverness Loch Ness] visitinvernesslochness.com Visit Inverness Loch NessThe Loch Ness MonsterThe Loch Ness Monster, or Nessie, as we like to call her, is one of the world's greatest un... Visitor attractions reinforce this approach by presenting science and folklore together rather than treating them as opposites. Museums, exhibitions and boat cruises explain geological history, research expeditions and famous sightings while inviting visitors to decide for themselves what they think. Modern attractions increasingly frame the experience as participating in an ongoing investigation rather than merely consuming a fictional story. [The Loch Ness Experience] lochness.com Find out more. LN Deepscan... This strategy creates a distinctive form of place branding: The landscape gains an instantly recognisable identity through a single legendary figure. Visitors arrive for different reasons, including curiosity, scepticism, photography, family entertainment or interest in folklore. Scientific investigations become attractions in their own right because they extend rather than resolve the mystery. Local businesses benefit from a shared symbol that appears across tours, accommodation, souvenirs and events. Unlike many heritage sites, Loch Ness does not depend upon visitors accepting one official interpretation. Believers, sceptics and undecided observers all find reasons to visit because each perspective contributes to the continuing story. Why disputed proof can still attract visitors From an evidential perspective, Loch Ness is notable because disputed evidence often strengthens public interest instead of weakening it. The exposure of hoaxes such as the \"Surgeon's Photograph\" did not end tourism. Instead, the distinction between genuine observations, mistaken identifications and deliberate fabrications became another layer of the legend. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster This reflects an important feature of Fortean culture. Complete proof would largely end debate, while complete dismissal would remove the mystery. Loch Ness occupies the productive middle ground where uncertainty continues to invite participation. Visitors can examine historical photographs, hear competing explanations, watch the water themselves and become part of a story that remains unresolved. Recent developments illustrate that maintaining credibility has become part of preserving the brand. As artificial intelligence has made fabricated images easier to create, the Loch Ness Centre has stated that submitted sightings are reviewed by experienced investigators before being promoted publicly. Rather than accepting every dramatic image, tourism organisations increasingly argue that protecting the mystery requires distinguishing genuine anomalies from manufactured fakes. [The Scottish Sun] thescottishsun.co.uk The rise of AI generated images, portraying misleading anomalies such as humps on the water, has prompted the center to enhance its verif... This approach shows that the attraction is not based simply on encouraging belief. Instead, it depends on maintaining a careful balance between imagination, investigation and responsible scepticism. Loch Ness Monster vs Science Real or Fantasy? Full Documentary HD Channel: Wisdom Land Open on YouTube The REAL story of the Loch Ness Monster Channel: Scotland Unplugged Open on YouTube What Nessie reveals about folklore as evidence of meaning Within the broader study of Forteana , Nessie demonstrates that the cultural impact of a legend can be measured independently of its biological reality. The monster has shaped how millions of people imagine the Scottish Highlands, influenced local identity, inspired decades of scientific inquiry and generated a tourism economy centred on mystery rather than certainty. [Continuum Attractions] continuumattractions.com Open source on continuumattractions.com. Loch Ness therefore illustrates a central principle of folklore as evidence of meaning. The enduring importance of Nessie lies less in proving that an unknown animal inhabits the loch than in showing how repeated stories, contested evidence and shared curiosity can transform an ordinary landscape into one of the world's most recognisable places of mystery. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Nessie Turned Mystery Into Place Branding. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The vanishing hitchhiker By Jan Harold Brunvand Helps explain why extraordinary stories persist despite disputed evidence. See on Amazon Book The Loch Ness monster By Steuart Campbell Examines the evidence and history behind the Loch Ness phenomenon. See on Amazon Book Abominable Science By Donald R. Prothero, Daniel Loxton Includes Loch Ness as a case study in enduring monster traditions. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The vanishing hitchhiker The Loch Ness monster Abominable Science As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Example eBay listing Mystery forest Framed Art Print Framed Wall Art Poster Canvas Print Picture Search eBay.co.uk : mystery wall art Browse similar on eBay.co.uk Example eBay listing Mystery gang silhouette Framed Art Framed Wall Art Poster Canvas Print Picture Search eBay.co.uk : mystery wall art"},{"title":"Mothman","url":"/mothman-1a0a9a/","description":"Mothman endures because Point Pleasant turned a frightening 1960s legend into shared memory, public identity and ritual.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Mothman Why Mothman Belongs to Point Pleasant Mothman Mothman Why Mothman Belongs to Point Pleasant Mothman endures because Point Pleasant turned a frightening 1960s legend into shared memory, public identity and ritual. Mothman endures because Point Pleasant turned a frightening 1960s legend into shared memory, public identity and ritual. entity Sightings, disaster and later retellings Statues, museums and festival identity How public memory changes monster evidence forteana folklore mothman Introduction The Mothman legend survives not because convincing physical evidence has accumulated, but because the story became woven into the public memory of one place: Point Pleasant, West Virginia. Within Forteana , this makes Mothman an important example of folklore as evidence of meaning rather than proof. The reported sightings of 1966–67, the collapse of the Silver Bridge in December 1967, and decades of local commemoration have transformed an unsettling local legend into a lasting symbol of community identity. Rather than asking only whether the creature existed, it is equally important to ask why Point Pleasant continues to remember it, celebrate it and reinterpret it. The answer lies in the interaction between historical tragedy, repeated storytelling and public ritual. Mothman has become part of the town's shared language for discussing place, memory and resilience, even though opinions differ sharply over whether the sightings represented anything paranormal. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2scholarworks.iu.edu] folklife.si.edu Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature had laid low since the d... Sightings, disaster and later retellings Most accounts begin with a cluster of reported sightings between November 1966 and December 1967 around the former TNT industrial area outside Point Pleasant. Witnesses described a large winged figure with glowing red eyes, and local newspapers rapidly spread the reports. Over roughly thirteen months, dozens of people claimed unusual experiences, although descriptions varied considerably. [scholarworks.iu.edu] scholarworks.iu.edu First sighted in November of 1966, These reports stopped after the collapse of the Silver... The turning point came with the collapse of the Silver Bridge on 15 December 1967, which killed 46 people. The disaster had a well established engineering cause involving the failure of a suspension eyebar, but later writers, most famously John Keel in The Mothman Prophecies , connected the earlier sightings with the tragedy by portraying Mothman as a supernatural warning or omen. That interpretation became one of the defining versions of the legend, despite there being no historical evidence that the creature caused or predicted the collapse. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular MechanicsMothman and the Collapse of Point Pleasant's Silver Bridge31 Aug 2023 — During the initial Mothman sightings of 1966, pa... Folklore researchers note that this association was strengthened after the event rather than during it. As memories were retold, the bridge disaster increasingly became the emotional ending of the Mothman story, creating a coherent narrative that linked strange sightings with real historical loss. The legend therefore grew not simply from eyewitness reports but from the way later generations organised those reports into a meaningful sequence. [scholarworks.iu.edu+2ResearchGate] scholarworks.iu.edu First sighted in November of 1966, These reports stopped after the collapse of the Silver... Inside the Mothman Museum Point Pleasant, WV Channel: Travel with Trey Open on YouTube How Point Pleasant made Mothman part of its identity One reason the legend has endured is that Point Pleasant gradually chose to embrace it rather than distance itself from it. What began as an unsettling episode became part of the town's public identity through museums, public art and recurring community events. Visible expressions of that identity now include: The Mothman Museum, which preserves newspaper reports, memorabilia and material connected with the legend. The stainless steel Mothman statue in the town centre, which has become one of Point Pleasant's best known landmarks. The annual Mothman Festival, bringing visitors, researchers, artists, vendors and enthusiasts together each year. Walking tours, themed businesses and local souvenirs that reinforce the creature's association with the town rather than with anonymous folklore. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2Southern Living] folklife.si.edu Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature had laid low since the d... These commemorations perform an important cultural function. Instead of allowing the story to remain a fading newspaper curiosity from the 1960s, they continually introduce it to new audiences. Children encounter it through local traditions, tourists through festivals and museums, and online audiences through photographs and social media. In folklore terms, these are acts of transmission that keep the legend alive. The result is that Mothman now represents Point Pleasant almost as much as Point Pleasant represents Mothman. The creature functions as a civic emblem regardless of whether visitors regard it as paranormal, symbolic or fictional. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature had laid low since the d... Mothman: The Legend Of Point Pleasant And The Evidence Boogeymen Documentary Central Channel: Documentary Central Open on YouTube Why public memory differs from historical evidence The Mothman story illustrates an important distinction between historical documentation and collective memory. Historical investigation asks questions such as: What exactly did witnesses report? Which events can be independently verified? How reliable are contemporary records? What ordinary explanations remain possible? Public memory asks different questions: Which version of the story does the community keep repeating? Which events become symbolic? [digitalcommons.usu.edu] digitalcommons.usu.edu Narrative Hijacking: Mothman and the Silver Bridge Collapseby J Daly · 2020 — This paper examines the commemoration and folklorization of... What values or emotions does the legend express? Why does the story remain meaningful decades later? These approaches are not competitors. They answer different kinds of questions. The bridge collapse is a documented historical disaster with established engineering explanations. The connection between Mothman and the disaster belongs to folklore because it reflects how communities organise memory, assign significance and create narrative meaning after traumatic events. Folklorists therefore study the association itself as cultural evidence rather than as proof that a supernatural warning occurred. [ResearchGate+2digitalcommons.usu.edu] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Mothman, the Silver Bridge Collapse, and...By examining the songs, media, and material culture associated with the leg... Remembering tragedy without replacing it Researchers have also pointed out a tension within modern Mothman culture. Tourism has undoubtedly helped Point Pleasant by attracting visitors and supporting local businesses, but some scholars argue that commercial celebration can overshadow the human reality of the Silver Bridge disaster. Local perspectives are not always identical. For some residents, Mothman is an enjoyable civic mascot. For others, remembrance of the 46 victims remains the more important story. Academic work on commemoration suggests that folklore and historical tragedy have gradually become intertwined, sometimes in ways that blur their separate meanings. [digitalcommons.usu.edu+2ResearchGate] digitalcommons.usu.edu Narrative Hijacking: Mothman and the Silver Bridge Collapseby J Daly · 2020 — This paper examines the commemoration and folklorization of... Rather than seeing this as a contradiction, folklorists often interpret it as evidence that public memory evolves. Communities rarely preserve the past unchanged. Instead, they continually renegotiate which events deserve attention and how those events should be represented. The True Story Behind The Mothman of West Virginia Channel: grimmlifecollective Open on YouTube How Mothman changes what counts as evidence Within the broader Fortean perspective, Mothman demonstrates why folklore should not be judged solely by the standards used to evaluate eyewitness testimony or physical traces. The enduring evidence is not a recovered specimen or conclusive photograph. Instead, it consists of: The persistence of a shared narrative over multiple generations. Material culture such as statues, museums and commemorative objects. Annual rituals that reinforce local identity. Continued public association between Point Pleasant and"},{"title":"Ostension","url":"/ostension/","description":"Ostension explains why people may visit, perform or interpret real events through a legend they already know.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Ostension When Legends Start Shaping Real Events Ostension Ostension When Legends Start Shaping Real Events Ostension explains why people may visit, perform or interpret real events through a legend they already know. Ostension explains why people may visit, perform or interpret real events through a legend they already know. mechanism What ostension means in folklore Haunted visits, staged scares and primed interpretation Why performance complicates Fortean evidence forteana folklore ostension Introduction Ostension is one of the most important ideas in modern folklore studies because it explains how legends can begin to shape real behaviour rather than merely describe it. Instead of asking whether a ghost story, monster tale or cursed location is factually true, ostension asks how the story influences what people do, what they expect to experience and how they interpret ambiguous events. Within Forteana, this matters because reports of hauntings, cryptids and other anomalies may be influenced by pre existing legends, making folklore part of the event itself rather than simply a later explanation. The result is a complex feedback loop in which narrative, expectation and lived experience continually reinforce one another. [Wikipedia+2doiserbia.nb.rs] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore What ostension means in folklore The term was introduced into folklore research by folklorists Linda Dégh and Andrew Vázsonyi in their influential 1983 paper Does the Word \"Dog\" Bite? . They argued that legends should not be treated only as stories. Sometimes people actively perform them, imitate them or interpret ordinary events through their framework. Their famous observation was that \"fact can become narrative and narrative can become fact\", highlighting that legends may influence behaviour in ways that create apparently legendary events. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore Folklorists generally distinguish several closely related forms: Direct ostension occurs when someone deliberately acts out a legendary scenario. Quasi ostension occurs when an ambiguous event is interpreted through the lens of an existing legend, even if no connection actually exists. Pseudo ostension involves someone intentionally creating a legend like incident, often as a hoax or performance, knowing that observers will recognise the familiar story pattern. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore For Fortean investigation, quasi ostension is often the most significant. Witnesses may honestly report extraordinary experiences while unknowingly interpreting incomplete sensory information according to stories they already know. THE GHOST CHILDREN OF MUNGER ROAD Channel: W!ZARD Radio Media Open on YouTube Haunted visits, staged scares and primed interpretation One of the clearest examples of ostension is the phenomenon known as legend tripping . Individuals or groups deliberately visit locations associated with local ghost stories, monsters or supernatural traditions—often at night—to see whether the legend is true. These journeys are not simply tourism . The legend provides expectations that shape attention, emotions and interpretation throughout the visit. [Wikipedia+2dokumen.pub] Wikipedia Legend tripping Legend tripping Typical legend trip locations include: abandoned hospitals and houses isolated bridges and tunnels cemeteries remote woodland roads caves and ruins associated with local folklore These settings naturally produce unfamiliar sounds, darkness, uncertainty and heightened emotional arousal. Participants who already expect paranormal activity become more likely to notice ambiguous stimuli and interpret them as confirmation of the legend. This does not imply dishonesty. Psychological research consistently shows that expectation strongly influences perception, especially in uncertain environments. Folklore provides the interpretive script. [dokumen.pub+2doiserbia.nb.rs] dokumen.pub ruth are known to folklorists as legend trips—ritual performances in which...Read more... Adolescent legend trips have attracted particular attention because they combine fear, social bonding and rites of passage. Visiting a haunted location becomes a performance of courage as much as an investigation. The experience often matters regardless of whether anything objectively unusual occurs. The shared narrative itself becomes part of the event. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Playing with Fear: Interpreting the Adolescent Legend Trip Tuisk 2018). Some folklorists have referred to the youth's dare challenges related to supernatural lore in certain types of places as \"le... Not all ostension is spontaneous. Some individuals intentionally recreate legendary scenes to frighten others, attract attention or generate publicity. Such performances can later become incorporated into the legend's continuing history, making it increasingly difficult to distinguish original reports from deliberate imitation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore Why performance complicates Fortean evidence For investigators of unusual claims, ostension presents a methodological challenge. A report influenced by folklore is not necessarily fabricated, but neither is it independent evidence for the legend. Several mechanisms may operate simultaneously: witnesses expect particular experiences because they know the story participants unconsciously search for confirming details ordinary events become fitted into familiar narrative patterns later retellings become increasingly aligned with established legend motifs deliberate performances and hoaxes become mixed with sincere testimony This means that the legend is not merely explaining the report afterwards—it may have helped generate the circumstances under which the report emerged. [Wikipedia+2dokumen.pub] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore The relationship is especially important in haunted location investigations. If visitors already know they should hear footsteps, encounter a ghostly woman or witness mysterious lights, those expectations influence where they look, what they remember and which details they later emphasise. Multiple independent looking accounts may therefore reflect a shared cultural template rather than entirely separate observations. [doiserbia.nb.rs] doiserbia.nb.rs According to these legend scholars, ostensive actions crib behaviour based on, or influenced by, folklore and...Read more... SCARY OVERNIGHT HUNT FOR THE GOAT MAN! (Pope Lick Monster) Channel: AmericanXplorers Open on YouTube Ostension in contemporary digital folklore The internet has expanded ostension rather than replacing it. Online communities circulate legends rapidly, provide maps to supposedly haunted places and encourage users to recreate supernatural rituals themselves. Videos, photographs and discussion forums become part of the legend's performance rather than simply documenting it. Researchers have described this as online legend tripping, where digital participation and physical visits reinforce one another. [dokumen.pub] dokumen.pub ruth are known to folklorists as legend trips—ritual performances in which...Read more... The evolution of the Slender Man myth illustrates a related process. Instead of an old oral legend inspiring real world behaviour, a collaboratively created internet narrative developed folklore like traditions that eventually influenced real actions. Folklorist Jeffrey Tolbert has described this as a form of \"reverse ostension\", in which folklore like narratives are first constructed and then become frameworks through which later experiences and behaviours are organised. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore Digital media also accelerates pseudo ostension. Staged paranormal encounters, edited videos and deliberately mysterious performances can spread widely before their origins are understood. Even when exposed, they may continue to reinforce existing legends because audiences remember the imagery more readily than later corrections. S1E1: The Internet is The New Woods (Monsters, Ostension, & Moral Panics) Channel: Digital Folklore Podcast Open on YouTube Why ostension matters when assessing Fortean reports Ostension does not demonstrate that ghosts , monsters or other anomalous phenomena are unreal. Instead, it identifies a mechanism by which folklore becomes an active participant in experience. This makes it an essential concept for evaluating unusual claims. Rather than treating folklore as contamination of evidence, folklorists regard it as evidence in its own right—evidence about expectation, belief, social performance and the ways communities construct meaningful encounters with the unknown. For Fortean research, recognising ostension encourages a more careful reading of witness testimony. The question shifts from simply asking whether an event happened to examining how existing legends may have shaped what"},{"title":"Urban Legends","url":"/urban-legends/","description":"Contemporary legend research helps explain why strange stories travel, mutate and remain meaningful across different communities.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Folklore","level":3,"text":"Urban Legends Why Strange Stories Travel Between Communities Urban Legends Urban Legends Why Strange Stories Travel Between Communities Contemporary legend research helps explain why strange stories travel, mutate and remain meaningful across different communities. Contemporary legend research helps explain why strange stories travel, mutate and remain meaningful across different communities. dataset evidence How local stories become repeatable legends Mutation, repetition and recognizable cores What travelling legends reveal about shared anxieties forteana folklore urban legends Introduction Urban legends occupy a distinctive place within Forteana because they blur the boundary between reported experience and shared storytelling. Rather than asking whether every account of a ghost, cryptid or mysterious stranger is literally true, contemporary legend research asks a different question: why do certain extraordinary stories spread rapidly, survive repeated scepticism and remain meaningful across different communities? From this perspective, urban legends are evidence of how people interpret anomalous experiences, communicate uncertainty and negotiate danger in everyday life. They are not simply fictional tales but living folklore , continually reshaped as they move between neighbourhoods, workplaces, schools, online communities and countries. [contemporarylegend.org] contemporarylegend.org The same International Society for Contemporary Legend Research...Contemporary (“urban”) legends are one of the most pervasive forms of folklore... For the study of Forteana , this makes urban legends valuable regardless of whether any individual event can be verified. Their persistence reveals recurring patterns of fear, curiosity, distrust and wonder. A ghost story attached to a bridge, a rumour of strange lights above a housing estate or a tale of an uncanny hitchhiker becomes culturally important because people keep adapting it to new circumstances while preserving a recognisable narrative core. How local stories become repeatable legends Many contemporary legends begin with a seemingly specific incident. A witness claims to have encountered something inexplicable, a newspaper reports an unusual occurrence, or a local rumour spreads after an accident. At first the story may resemble what folklorists call a memorate : a first person account of an extraordinary experience. Over time, however, repeated retellings gradually detach the narrative from its original teller and transform it into a communal legend. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Several features encourage this transformation: The event is presented as plausible rather than impossible. The location is recognisable enough to feel authentic. The experience carries an emotional lesson or warning. Details remain flexible while the central incident stays recognisable. This flexibility explains why identical narrative structures appear in different regions with only minor changes. A haunted road becomes another haunted road in a neighbouring county. A mysterious creature reported in one woodland acquires a new local habitat elsewhere. The landscape changes, but the underlying narrative survives. Folklorists therefore prefer the term contemporary legend over urban legend in many cases. The stories are not confined to cities, nor are they necessarily modern inventions. Instead, they remain contemporary because each generation updates them for its own environment while preserving familiar themes. [contemporarylegend.org] contemporarylegend.org The same International Society for Contemporary Legend Research...Contemporary (“urban”) legends are one of the most pervasive forms of folklore... UFOs, UAPs, and the Quest for Understanding Channel: Question Explore Discover Open on YouTube Mutation, repetition and recognisable cores Urban legends rarely remain unchanged. Their survival depends precisely on their ability to mutate without losing their identity. A useful example is the widespread \"friend of a friend\" structure, often abbreviated by researchers as FOAF. Instead of claiming \"this happened to me\", the narrator says it happened to someone personally connected to them. This creates both distance and credibility: the source feels close enough to trust but far enough to resist verification. The narrative acquires authority without producing direct evidence. [EBSCO+2btboces.org] ebsco.com Urban folklore (urban legends) Social Sciences and...Many urban legends gain credibility through the familiar structure of \"a fri... Across many paranormal legends, the same narrative mechanisms appear repeatedly: Transferable settings: abandoned hospitals, isolated roads, woods, railway bridges and old houses . Stable emotional themes: fear, curiosity, guilt, forbidden knowledge or punishment. Variable surface details: names, dates, locations and descriptions shift to match local circumstances. Persistent narrative outcome: the warning, mystery or uncanny encounter remains intact. From a Fortean perspective, this matters because repetition demonstrates cultural transmission rather than independent confirmation. Dozens of similar stories do not necessarily indicate dozens of identical anomalous events. Instead, they show that certain narrative patterns successfully organise uncertain experiences into memorable forms. Conspiracy Theories, Folklore and Belief Channel: Library of Congress Open on YouTube Why paranormal legends travel so easily Stories involving ghosts, cryptids and unexplained encounters possess several qualities that make them unusually portable. First, they explain ambiguous experiences without demanding technical expertise. An unexplained noise, an unusual animal sighting or an unexpected light in the sky already possesses uncertainty. A legend provides an existing framework into which that uncertainty can be placed. Secondly, these stories are emotionally memorable. Research on contemporary legends consistently finds that narratives involving danger, disgust, surprise or moral tension spread more successfully than emotionally neutral accounts. Fear and fascination increase the likelihood that a story will be repeated. [Folklore] folklore.ee Perspectives on Contemporary Legend Perspectives on Contemporary LegendAugust 29, 2016 — A paradigm of today's research of contemporary legends is the continued focu... Published: August 29, 2016 Thirdly, legends often avoid definitive endings. Unlike detective stories, they leave important questions unresolved: Was the witness mistaken? Was there a deliberate hoax? Did something genuinely unusual occur? Could ordinary explanations account for only part of the experience? That ambiguity encourages continued discussion rather than closure, allowing the story to circulate long after any original incident has faded from public memory. What travelling legends reveal about shared anxieties Urban legends are often strongest during periods of rapid social change because they express concerns that are difficult to discuss directly. Within Fortean traditions, recurring anxieties include: mistrust of official institutions; uncertainty surrounding new technologies; fears about strangers and hidden threats; concern that familiar places may conceal unknown dangers; unease about blurred boundaries between the ordinary and the extraordinary. Because these concerns are widely shared, similar legends frequently emerge independently or adapt across national boundaries. The supernatural elements vary, but the underlying social pressures remain recognisable. For example, UFO rumours frequently flourish alongside concerns about secrecy and government power, while haunted location stories often become attached to places already associated with tragedy or contested local history. Cryptid traditions commonly develop around landscapes perceived as remote, dangerous or culturally marginal. In each case, the legend expresses collective uncertainty more effectively than a straightforward factual report could. Folk Horror and Urban Legends Josh Zeman & Dr. Diane Rodgers Channel: Sinister with Josh Zeman Open on YouTube When stories influence behaviour One of the most important insights from contemporary legend research is that legends do not merely describe behaviour; they can also produce it. Researchers use the concept of ostension to describe situations in which people consciously or unconsciously act out legendary narratives. This includes visiting supposedly haunted locations, performing rituals associated with local legends or interpreting ambiguous events through an already familiar story. The related practice of legend tripping —travelling to sites connected with supernatural rumours—shows how narrative can shape real world movement, tourism, social rituals and even perceived experience. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ostension (folklore Ostension (folklore For Forteana, this creates an important feedback loop: 1. A strange story circulates. 2. People visit the"},{"title":"Fieldwork","url":"/fieldwork/","description":"A careful Fortean investigation starts with dates, sources, locations, witnesses, and ordinary explanations.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Fieldwork How to Investigate a Strange Report Carefully Fieldwork Fieldwork How to Investigate a Strange Report Carefully A careful Fortean investigation starts with dates, sources, locations, witnesses, and ordinary explanations. A careful Fortean investigation starts with dates, sources, locations, witnesses, and ordinary explanations. policy intervention Secure the earliest account Record witnesses and conditions Test ordinary explanations first forteana fieldwork Introduction Fortean fieldwork is the careful investigation of a strange report before it hardens into a legend. The beginner’s job is not to prove ghosts, monsters, UFOs or impossible weather, but to preserve the earliest account, locate the event in time and place, identify witnesses, and test ordinary explanations before reaching for extraordinary ones. That means asking plain questions, separating first hand testimony from retellings, checking environmental and astronomical conditions, and keeping a written record that another person could audit. The Society for Psychical Research describes many hauntings, apparitions and similar cases as “spontaneous” because they occur without warning; that makes early documentation especially valuable. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk Guidance Notes for Investigators of Spontaneous Cases1 Jan 2018 — These guidance notes are intended to be helpful for those who seek to i... A Fortean investigator therefore sits between archivist, local reporter and sceptical naturalist. The work begins with humility: witnesses may be sincere, memories may be fragile, and mundane explanations may be subtle rather than obvious. Secure the Earliest Account The first version of a strange report is usually the most precious. Later versions may be more dramatic, but they are also more likely to have been shaped by retelling, local rumour, press interest, social media comments, or the witness’s own attempts to make sense of what happened. In practical terms, the fieldworker should try to find the earliest written message, diary entry, emergency call, newspaper notice, social post, photograph, video file or direct statement connected to the event. This matters because witness memory is not a fixed recording. The UK Parliamentary Office of Science and Technology notes that memories can be influenced by the conditions under which an event was witnessed and by exposure to inaccurate information afterwards; it also highlights open questions and confidence checks as ways to reduce undue influence. [Research Briefings] researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk Research Briefings For Fortean cases, the same principle applies outside criminal justice: ask what the person noticed first, what they said before hearing other explanations, and what details appeared only after discussion. A useful first account record should capture: 1. Date and time: exact if possible, approximate if not, with time zone and whether the clock was checked. 2. Location: address, grid reference, landmark, room, road direction, viewpoint, or map pin. 3. Duration: seconds, minutes, repeated episodes, or uncertain. 4. Direction and movement: where the witness was facing, where the phenomenon appeared, and where it went. 5. Weather, lighting and sound: cloud, fog, rain, wind, moonlight, street lighting, machinery, traffic, animals. 6. Witness state: tiredness, fear, intoxication, illness, recent bereavement, expectation, or prior knowledge of the site’s reputation. 7. Original media: unedited photos, videos, audio files, metadata, screenshots of first messages, and the device used. The key is to record uncertainty rather than smooth it away. “About 9 pm, maybe later” is better than a falsely precise time. “I first thought it was a person” is not a weakness; it is a clue. Record Witnesses and Conditions A beginner’s best tool is not an electromagnetic meter, night vision camera or dramatic vigil. It is a calm interview conducted before witnesses have compared stories. ASSAP, a long running UK organisation concerned with anomalous experiences, argues that the best haunting evidence comes from first hand, casual, independent witnesses: people who were not trying to experience anything paranormal and who have not been influenced by other witnesses or a site’s reputation. [assap.ac.uk] assap.ac.uk scientific ghost research scientific ghost research The interview should begin with an open invitation: “Tell me what happened from the beginning.” Avoid feeding the witness a theory. “Did you see a tall shadow figure?” is much worse than “What did you see?” If the witness uses a loaded word such as “ghost”, “craft” or “creature”, gently separate the label from the observation: “When you say ghost, what did you actually see, hear or feel?” Good witness handling also means protecting people. Some reports involve fear, grief, sleep disturbance, family conflict, or possible health hazards. Treat the witness as a person, not as a source of exciting content. Do not publish names, addresses, recordings, children’s testimony, medical details or private grief without informed consent. Ethical guidelines for haunting investigations have been developed precisely because these cases can involve vulnerable people and sensitive domestic spaces. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. Keep Witnesses Separate Where Possible If several people report the same event, interview them separately before holding any group discussion. Ask each person to draw the scene, mark their position, estimate times and describe sensory details. Later comparison may reveal agreement, but it may also reveal that one person saw the object while another only heard the reaction, or that one dramatic detail entered the story after the group had already talked. The aim is not to catch people out. It is to distinguish shared observation from shared interpretation. A family saying “we all saw it” may mean everyone saw the same figure, or it may mean one person saw something, shouted, and everyone else saw the aftermath. Elite Witness Testimonies! TOP 25 UFO ENCOUNTERS BY ASTRONAUTS, PILOTS AND MILITARY OFFICIALS Channel: FilmIsNow Movies · Views: 5.8K · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 55 minutes Open on YouTube Document the Site Like a Surveyor A site visit should capture the ordinary setting in detail. Photograph the witness viewpoint in daylight and, where safe, under similar lighting to the original report. Note reflective windows, passing headlights, nearby roads, railways, flight paths, animals, loose pipes, heating systems, draughts, water tanks, trees brushing walls, and sources of low frequency vibration or intermittent sound. In haunting cases, experimental work led by psychologist Richard Wiseman found that reported unusual experiences in allegedly haunted locations may relate to environmental variables such as lighting, magnetic fields and expectation rather than “ghostly” activity. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. That does not automatically explain every report, but it gives the fieldworker a practical checklist: before asking whether a house is haunted, ask what the house is doing. Test Ordinary Explanations First The most Fortean mistake is not belief; it is premature certainty. A good fieldworker tests ordinary explanations first because they are common, varied and often surprising. Many famous “anomalies” do not collapse into one simple explanation. They may involve weather, memory, optical effects, folklore , fraud, equipment artefacts and honest misperception all at once. For sky reports, start with the sky. The US Air Force’s Project Blue Book materials list common UFO report sources such as bright stars and planets, comets, fireballs, meteors, auroral effects and celestial bodies seen through haze, fog, moving cloud or unusual conditions. [esd.whs.mil] esd.whs.mil Project Blue Book Project Blue Book The National Archives notes that Project Blue Book’s records are declassified and that the project closed in 1969, which makes them a useful historical model for how reports were filed, not a current reporting service. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National Archives Project BLUE BOOK For strange falls from the sky, look at weather before wonder. A 1961 US Fish and Wildlife Service leaflet on “rains of fishes” begins from the observation that during violent thunderstorms and heavy rain, objects normally found on land or water sometimes appear to fall from the sky, especially small live animals. [spo.nmfs.noaa.gov] spo.nmfs.noaa.gov Open source on noaa.gov. The Library of Congress similarly notes that many scientists consider tornadic waterspouts a plausible explanation for some fish and frog falls, while the National Weather Service distinguishes tornadic waterspouts from fair weather waterspouts. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Open source on loc.gov. For indoor apparitions, odours, voices, presences or repeated “haunting” sensations, check health and"},{"title":"First Accounts","url":"/first-accounts/","description":"The earliest version of a strange report often shows what was observed before rumor, retelling, or interpretation changed the story.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"First Accounts Why the First Strange Report Matters Most First Accounts First Accounts Why the First Strange Report Matters Most The earliest version of a strange report often shows what was observed before rumor, retelling, or interpretation changed the story. The earliest version of a strange report often shows what was observed before rumor, retelling, or interpretation changed the story. dataset evidence What counts as the earliest account How retelling changes details What to preserve before interpretation forteana fieldwork first accounts Introduction The earliest version of a strange report is often the most valuable piece of evidence a Fortean investigator will ever obtain. Before rumours spread, witnesses compare memories, or newspapers and social media supply ready made explanations, there is usually a brief period when observations are closest to what the witness actually experienced. That first account is rarely perfect, but it is typically less contaminated by later interpretation than any version recorded days or weeks afterwards. Research on eyewitness memory consistently shows that recall can be altered by later conversations, leading questions and media coverage, making prompt documentation one of the strongest safeguards available to investigators. [BPS PsychHub+2PMC] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub The history of an idea: The misinformation effect BPS PsychHubThe history of an idea: The misinformation effect Loftus24 Dec 2025 — Misinformation effects in eyewitness memory: The pres... For beginners in Fortean fieldwork , this principle matters more than sophisticated equipment. A notebook, careful interview technique and attention to chronology will usually preserve more useful evidence than speculative theories about ghosts, cryptids or unidentified aerial phenomena. What Counts as the Earliest Account? An \"earliest account\" is the first record created before substantial outside influence has had time to shape the witness's memory. It is not necessarily the first published version, nor the most detailed. Useful early records include: A text message sent immediately after the event. A diary entry written the same day. An emergency telephone call. An unedited audio recording. A contemporaneous social media post. The first interview conducted before discussion with other witnesses. Original photographs or videos together with their metadata. These sources matter because they often preserve uncertainty. A witness might initially report, \"I saw a light moving behind the trees,\" while later retellings become \"I saw a structured craft hovering over the woods.\" The observation may be identical, but interpretation has changed. For Fortean investigations, preserving the distinction between observation and explanation is critical. \"A dark figure crossed the road\" is an observation. \"It was a ghost\" is an interpretation that may have developed later. How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube How Retelling Changes Details Human memory is reconstructive rather than photographic. Each act of remembering involves rebuilding an event rather than replaying a perfect recording. Decades of psychological research, particularly work associated with Elizabeth Loftus and subsequent researchers, has demonstrated the \"misinformation effect\": exposure to inaccurate post event information can become incorporated into sincere recollections. [BPS PsychHub+2PMC] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub The history of an idea: The misinformation effect BPS PsychHubThe history of an idea: The misinformation effect Loftus24 Dec 2025 — Misinformation effects in eyewitness memory: The pres... Several common processes are especially relevant to Fortean reports. Witness discussion. Two people who experienced the same event often exchange details afterwards. Over time they may unknowingly adopt each other's memories until both accounts become unusually similar. Agreement reached after discussion should not automatically be treated as independent corroboration. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineNews as a form of post event information: the effect of...by Z Crittenden · 2026 — Decades of research into the m... Media influence. Newspaper articles, television reports and online discussions frequently introduce descriptions that witnesses later repeat. Experimental research shows that even news coverage containing misleading information can alter later recall. [Taylor & Francis Online] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineNews as a form of post event information: the effect of...by Z Crittenden · 2026 — Decades of research into the m... Repeated storytelling. Every retelling encourages a witness to organise the event into a coherent narrative. Missing details become filled in, uncertain timings become precise, and tentative impressions gradually harden into confident statements. Community expectations. Once a location gains a reputation for hauntings, mysterious animals or UFO sightings, new witnesses may unconsciously interpret ambiguous experiences through that existing folklore rather than describing only what they observed. None of these mechanisms requires deception. Honest witnesses can experience genuine memory change while remaining completely sincere. Why Legends Grow Faster Than Evidence Many well known Fortean traditions began with brief, uncertain reports that accumulated dramatic features over time. A typical pattern is: 1. An unusual event is witnessed. 2. An initial account contains uncertainty and missing information. 3. Friends, neighbours or journalists retell the story. 4. Additional descriptive details appear. 5. Later versions cite earlier retellings rather than the original witness. 6. The expanded narrative becomes accepted as the \"real\" story. This progression explains why investigators often seek archived newspapers, police logs, private letters or original interview notes instead of relying on later books or websites. Early documents frequently contain fewer extraordinary claims but more reliable observational detail. Importantly, discovering differences between early and later accounts does not automatically disprove an unusual event. Instead, it helps identify which elements belong to direct testimony and which emerged through later interpretation. What to Preserve Before Interpretation A good first account record captures observable facts before asking what they might mean. Useful priorities include: Exact wording. Record the witness's own language rather than paraphrasing. Chronology. Establish what happened first, next and last. Immediate impressions. Ask what they thought at the moment, not after reflection. Uncertainty. Preserve estimates, hesitations and alternative possibilities. Environmental conditions. Lighting, weather , background noise and visibility often become harder to reconstruct later. Independent accounts. Interview witnesses separately whenever practical before they compare memories. Open ended questions generally produce more reliable information than leading ones. \"Tell me everything you remember\" encourages observation. Questions such as \"Did the creature have glowing eyes?\" introduce details that may later become incorporated into memory. Research underpinning the cognitive interview method similarly favours non leading recall over suggestive questioning. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive interview Cognitive interview Scott Fraser: The problem with eyewitness testimony Channel: TED · Views: 246.2K · Uploaded: September 2012 · Length: 20 minutes Open on YouTube Separating Observation from Meaning One of the most useful habits in Fortean fieldwork is to divide every statement into two categories. Observation describes something directly experienced: \"I heard three loud knocks.\" \"The light moved from west to east.\" \"The animal disappeared behind the hedge.\" Interpretation attempts to explain those observations: \"A ghost knocked.\" \"It was an alien spacecraft.\" \"It was an unknown creature.\" The distinction allows later investigators to revisit the evidence if new information becomes available. A description of movement, sound or appearance remains useful regardless of whether the eventual explanation changes. This approach also reduces confirmation bias. Rather than collecting reports that fit a preferred theory, investigators preserve the raw observations from which multiple explanations can be evaluated. Why First Accounts Remain the Strongest Evidence Early witness reports are not infallible. People can misperceive events, estimate distances poorly and forget details even within minutes. Nevertheless, the earliest available account usually provides the closest approximation to what the witness originally experienced before memory becomes mixed with conversation, publicity or folklore. [PMC+2BPS PsychHub] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Behavioral Account of the"},{"title":"Animal Falls","url":"/animal-falls-a133c1/","description":"Reports of animals falling from the sky become more useful when investigators first test storms, waterspouts, flooding, and local animal sources.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"Animal Falls Can It Really Rain Fish or Frogs? Animal Falls Animal Falls Can It Really Rain Fish or Frogs? Reports of animals falling from the sky become more useful when investigators first test storms, waterspouts, flooding, and local animal sources. Reports of animals falling from the sky become more useful when investigators first test storms, waterspouts, flooding, and local animal sources. mechanism What witnesses usually report Waterspouts and storm explanations Evidence to collect at the scene forteana fieldwork animal falls Introduction Reports of fish or frogs apparently falling from the sky are among the best known examples in Fortean literature, but they are also some of the most approachable for practical investigation. While such events can seem extraordinary, meteorologists have long proposed mechanisms that can account for at least some cases, especially where severe weather , waterspouts or flooding are involved. The key lesson for a beginner is that an unusual outcome does not automatically require an unusual cause. Before treating an animal fall as a mystery, investigate the weather, the landscape and the animals themselves. For fieldworkers, these reports are valuable because they combine eyewitness testimony with physical evidence that can often be examined. The distribution of the animals, their condition, recent weather records and nearby water sources may reveal whether a storm transported them, floodwater deposited them or another process created the illusion of a \"rain\" of animals. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... What witnesses usually report Accounts of animal falls share several recurring features despite occurring in different countries and different centuries. Witnesses commonly describe: Fish, frogs or tadpoles appearing immediately after heavy rain or thunderstorms. [earthsky.org] earthsky.org raining fish and frogs They may pick up fish or frogs or other small creatures, carry them some distance and then drop...Read more... Animals concentrated within a surprisingly small area rather than spread across an entire town. Only one species being present, often individuals of similar size. Some animals still alive and active after landing. Little or no obvious source of the animals nearby. These details matter because they help distinguish between several different mechanisms. A concentrated patch of small fish after a violent storm suggests a different process from frogs appearing across flooded gardens over several hours. Historical reports include repeated fish falls in Honduras, often known as lluvia de peces , and numerous documented fish and frog falls in Europe, North America and Asia. Although these events have become part of Fortean folklore , many individual cases remain poorly documented beyond newspaper accounts, making contemporary field investigation especially valuable. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Waterspouts and storm explanations The leading meteorological explanation involves waterspouts and, less commonly, tornadoes crossing lakes, rivers or coastal waters. A waterspout is a rotating column of air over water. Strong tornadic waterspouts are capable of lifting spray, lightweight debris and, potentially, small aquatic animals into the circulation. Once carried aloft, fish or amphibians may be transported some distance before falling when the storm weakens or changes structure. Meteorologists consider this physically plausible, although direct observation of animals being lifted and later dropped is extremely rare. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... Several observations support this explanation: Most reported animals are small and lightweight. Many reports follow thunderstorms or severe weather. Fish and frogs are often associated with shallow water where they are easier to lift. [earthsky.org] earthsky.org raining fish and frogs They may pick up fish or frogs or other small creatures, carry them some distance and then drop...Read more... Falls usually involve aquatic rather than terrestrial animals. However, investigators should avoid assuming every report resulted from a waterspout. Some reported falls occur without evidence of nearby severe storms, while others happen far from any documented vortex. In those cases, additional explanations deserve consideration rather than forcing every event into the same model. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... When \"animal rain\" is not actually rain One of the most useful habits in Fortean fieldwork is to ask whether anything truly fell from the sky at all. Heavy rainfall and flash flooding can wash fish from overflowing ponds, streams or drainage channels into roads, gardens and fields. When the water rapidly drains away, observers may encounter scattered fish with no obvious explanation, especially if they did not witness the flooding itself. Frogs provide another example. During wet weather, many amphibians emerge simultaneously to migrate or breed. Large numbers appearing suddenly after rain can create the impression that they have fallen from above when they have actually emerged from nearby vegetation, ditches or temporary pools. Similar processes have been suggested for some historical reports involving worms, snails and other small animals. [Modern Farmer] modernfarmer.com things fall sky Modern FarmerWhen Animals Fall from the Sky18 Mar 2014 — But what about the fish and frogs? “It is certainly within the realm of possibil... Birds can also produce misleading evidence. Predatory birds and seabirds occasionally drop fish in flight, either accidentally or when disturbed. Localised falls of damaged fish during otherwise calm weather have been linked to this behaviour rather than storms. [EarthSky] earthsky.org raining fish and frogs They may pick up fish or frogs or other small creatures, carry them some distance and then drop...Read more... Evidence to collect at the scene A careful investigation begins before the animals are disturbed or removed. Record the following as quickly as possible: The precise location using a map reference or GPS. The date and exact time of discovery. Recent weather, including thunderstorms, hail, tornado warnings or waterspout reports. The size of the affected area. Whether the animals are alive, injured or dead. Whether all belong to one species and similar age. Nearby ponds, rivers, drainage ditches, reservoirs or flooded ground. Photographs showing the original distribution before collection. Collect environmental information alongside biological evidence. Weather radar archives, local meteorological observations and storm reports may indicate whether a rotating storm passed nearby. Likewise, checking local flood reports may reveal an overlooked source for displaced animals. If practical, identify the species. Knowing whether fish normally inhabit a nearby pond or whether the frogs are juvenile individuals emerging from a breeding site can greatly narrow the possibilities. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov can it rain frogs fish and other objects The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Many scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for fro... WX Fun 1on1: Does it really rain frogs and fish? Channel: WVTM 13 News Open on YouTube How to assess competing explanations Rather than asking, \"Could this be paranormal?\", a Fortean investigator should compare competing explanations against the available evidence. Questions worth asking include: Was severe weather documented in the relevant place and time? Is there a plausible water source within the transport distance? Does the species naturally occur locally? Are the animals clean, muddy or physically damaged? Is there independent confirmation from multiple witnesses? Does the reported distribution match aerial deposition or ground transport? The strongest cases are those with independent witnesses, preserved specimens, precise weather records and clear timelines. Conversely, stories repeated years later without documentation should be treated cautiously, regardless of how dramatic they sound. What these cases teach beginners Fish and frog falls illustrate an important principle of Fortean investigation: unusual observations deserve"},{"title":"House Hazards","url":"/house-hazards/","description":"Carbon monoxide, vibration, mould, draughts, sleep disruption, and expectation can turn a frightening house into a misleading witness environment.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"House Hazards When the Haunted House Is the Clue House Hazards House Hazards When the Haunted House Is the Clue Carbon monoxide, vibration, mould, draughts, sleep disruption, and expectation can turn a frightening house into a misleading witness environment. Carbon monoxide, vibration, mould, draughts, sleep disruption, and expectation can turn a frightening house into a misleading witness environment. mechanism Safety checks before paranormal claims Environmental causes of presences and sounds When to pause an investigation forteana fieldwork house hazards Introduction A beginner investigating a reputedly haunted house should treat the building itself as potential evidence before treating it as a stage for paranormal events. Many classic haunting reports—shadowy figures, unexplained voices, oppressive atmospheres, mysterious knocks, feelings of being watched, or waking to a presence in the room—can overlap with genuine environmental hazards or well understood effects of buildings on human perception. That does not prove every haunting has an ordinary explanation , but it does mean safety checks come before extraordinary claims. Modern environmental science, occupational health research, and even guidance from experienced psychical investigators all point to the same practical lesson: if a location is making people ill, disorientated or sleep deprived, the first priority is identifying and removing those risks rather than collecting ghost stories. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Environmental Factors Misidentified as Paranormal Activity CO leaks, faulty appliances, infrasound sources) demonstrate natural causes behind classic haunt reports. We...Read more... Safety checks before paranormal claims The first question should never be \"What ghost is here?\" but \"Is this building safe to enter?\" Older buildings may contain hazards that produce unusual sensations while also creating convincing ghost narratives. Before beginning any investigation, check for obvious structural dangers, damaged wiring, poor ventilation, active heating appliances, mould, unstable floors and signs of water damage. If occupants report headaches, nausea, dizziness or confusion alongside paranormal experiences, treat those symptoms as potential medical warning signs rather than as supporting evidence for a haunting. [American Lung Association] lung.org American Lung AssociationEnter if You Dare: 6 Spooky Things Lurking Inside Your...Some signs that you might have CO in your home include... Particular attention should be given to: Carbon monoxide risks from faulty boilers, fires or flues. Poor ventilation , leading to elevated carbon dioxide and stale air. Extensive damp and mould growth. Electrical faults that present fire or shock hazards. Structural instability , especially in abandoned properties. Animals , including rodents or birds in roof spaces, which often generate unexplained noises. For Fortean fieldwork , documenting these conditions is valuable even if they do not fully explain every report. Eliminating hazards narrows the range of possible explanations and protects both investigators and witnesses. Environmental causes of presences and sounds Carbon monoxide: the invisible hazard Carbon monoxide (CO) deserves priority because it is both dangerous and capable of producing symptoms that witnesses may interpret as paranormal. It is colourless, odourless and produced by incomplete combustion from faulty gas appliances, blocked chimneys, wood burners and similar equipment. Low level or chronic exposure may produce headaches, dizziness, confusion, disturbed sleep, vivid dreams and, in some cases, hallucinations or intense feelings of dread. These experiences can become woven into stories of haunted rooms or oppressive presences if the underlying poisoning goes unrecognised. Public health organisations therefore recommend working CO alarms and prompt inspection of fuel burning appliances whenever exposure is suspected. [American Lung Association] lung.org American Lung AssociationEnter if You Dare: 6 Spooky Things Lurking Inside Your...Some signs that you might have CO in your home include... The often cited 1913 investigation of a supposedly haunted Boston house found that a defective furnace was leaking combustion gases into occupied rooms. Residents reported nocturnal figures, strange sensations and frightening experiences that ceased once the ventilation problem was identified. While this single historical case does not explain all haunting traditions, it remains an important reminder that apparently supernatural reports can have life threatening environmental causes. [Wigmore on Alcohol] wigmoreonalcohol.com Discover how this invisible gas shaped history Wigmore on AlcoholCarbon Monoxide: 3 Chilling Truths Behind GhostsMarch 10, 2026 — 10 Mar 2026 — Carbon Monoxide may explain ghost sighti... Published: March 10, 2026 Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Mould, damp and poor indoor air Persistent damp encourages mould growth, affecting indoor air quality. Exposure to mould is strongly associated with respiratory irritation, headaches, fatigue and difficulty concentrating, while living in damp housing is linked with poorer sleep and reduced wellbeing. Although mould does not reliably produce ghostly hallucinations by itself, feeling chronically unwell in a gloomy building can make ordinary creaks, shadows or ambiguous sensations seem far more significant. A neglected building that \"feels wrong\" may therefore be creating both genuine illness and a setting in which paranormal interpretations become more likely. [Big Think] bigthink.com Big Think Is the “paranormal” actually due to gas leaks and toxic mold? Big ThinkIs the “paranormal” actually due to gas leaks and toxic mold?November 11, 2023 — 11 Nov 2023 — Various environmental phenomena... Published: November 11, 2023 Vibration, low frequency sound and strange sensations Not every unsettling sensation comes from something visible. Mechanical systems such as boilers, ventilation equipment, pumps and industrial machinery can generate low frequency vibration or infrasound. Research into infrasound suggests that while it does not reliably create visions of ghosts , it can contribute to unease, anxiety, discomfort or an indefinable sense that something is wrong, particularly in suggestive environments. Combined with darkness, unfamiliar surroundings and prior expectations, these subtle sensations may encourage witnesses to interpret ambiguous experiences as supernatural. [The Guardian] theguardian.com The Guardian Spooky feelings in old houses may be caused by boiler The GuardianSpooky feelings in old houses may be caused by boiler...April 27, 2026 — 27 Apr 2026 — Inaudible infrasound from old pipes a... Published: April 27, 2026 Similarly, ordinary building movement can generate: Expanding or contracting timber that produces knocks. Water pipes that click or bang as temperatures change. Wind passing through gaps that creates whistles or voices. Loose fixtures that vibrate intermittently. Roof spaces occupied by birds, squirrels or rodents. These mechanisms are often intermittent, making them appear mysterious until carefully traced. Draughts and \"cold spots\" Cold patches are among the oldest features of haunting reports, yet many buildings naturally develop localised temperature differences. Poor insulation, hidden air leaks, chimney effects, open floor voids and poorly sealed doors can create distinct streams of cold air. These draughts often occur in narrow corridors, staircases and older stone buildings—the very locations frequently described as haunted. Rather than assuming a supernatural cause, investigators should note weather conditions, ventilation patterns and heating operation before assigning significance to isolated cold spots. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Environmental Factors Misidentified as Paranormal Activity CO leaks, faulty appliances, infrasound sources) demonstrate natural causes behind classic haunt reports. We...Read more... Can the Power of Specific Frequencies Unlock a Haunting Effect Mythbusters Discovery Channel: Discovery Open on YouTube Why witnesses can sincerely misinterpret a building Environmental conditions do not operate in isolation. They interact with normal human psychology . A person exploring an unfamiliar building at night is already dealing with uncertainty. If they are tired, anxious or aware of the property's haunted reputation , ambiguous sights and sounds become easier to interpret as meaningful. Several factors commonly reinforce one another: Sleep disruption , especially repeated awakenings, increases dream like experiences and confusion between sleep and wakefulness. Expectation encourages attention towards unusual noises while ignoring ordinary explanations. Pareidolia leads people to perceive"},{"title":"Ethics","url":"/ethics/","description":"A haunting investigation can expose grief, fear, children, addresses, and health details, so consent and privacy are part of the evidence work.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"Ethics The Human Side of Ghost Reports Ethics Ethics The Human Side of Ghost Reports A haunting investigation can expose grief, fear, children, addresses, and health details, so consent and privacy are part of the evidence work. A haunting investigation can expose grief, fear, children, addresses, and health details, so consent and privacy are part of the evidence work. policy intervention Consent before publishing names or recordings Handling grief, fear, and children carefully When evidence should stay private forteana fieldwork ethics Introduction Haunting investigations often take place in ordinary homes, workplaces and communities rather than controlled research settings. That means the most important ethical questions are frequently about people rather than paranormal claims. A careful Fortean investigator protects witnesses, respects private property, avoids increasing fear, and recognises that reports may involve grief, illness, family conflict or vulnerable individuals. Good ethics are therefore part of good evidence: people who feel respected are more likely to provide accurate accounts, while careless publicity can distort testimony, damage reputations and make future investigation impossible. Ethical guidance produced for psychical research has long stressed informed consent, confidentiality, appropriate referrals and the investigator's responsibility towards those seeking help. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Ethical guidelines for the investigation of haunting 17, 2007 — The paper is divided into three sections: (a) ge... Published: November 17, 2007 Consent before publishing names or recordings Consent should be viewed as an ongoing process rather than a single signature. Before recording interviews, photographing a location or publishing audio, investigators should explain what will be collected, how it may be used, who may see it and whether it could later appear online or in books, talks or documentaries. Participants should understand that they can decline particular forms of recording or request anonymity where practical. Ethical guidance developed specifically for haunting investigations identifies informed consent and confidentiality as central obligations, while broader research ethics guidance recommends making consent clear, specific and proportionate to the intended use of recordings. [ResearchGate+2the sra.org.uk] researchgate.net Research Gate Ethical guidelines for the investigation of haunting 17, 2007 — The paper is divided into three sections: (a) ge... Published: November 17, 2007 In practice, this means avoiding assumptions such as: A homeowner inviting investigators inside is not automatically consenting to public release of photographs. Permission to record an interview is not necessarily permission to upload it to social media. One family member cannot normally grant consent on behalf of every resident or visitor. A witness may agree to anonymous quotation while refusing publication of their name, address or voice. Changing circumstances also matter. Someone who initially welcomes publicity may later experience unwanted attention from neighbours, journalists or online commentators. Reviewing publication plans before release is often better practice than relying on an old verbal agreement. PARANORMAL INVESTIGATION: ETHICS Guest, Randall Keller FAMP Channel: Tracey Lockwood Open on YouTube Handling grief, fear and children carefully Many haunting reports arise during emotionally difficult periods. Bereavement, sleep disruption, anxiety, family stress and significant life changes frequently shape how experiences are interpreted, regardless of whether any unusual event has an ordinary explanation . Ethical investigators should therefore avoid presenting themselves as counsellors, clergy or medical professionals unless they genuinely hold those qualifications. When someone appears distressed, the priority becomes their wellbeing rather than collecting dramatic testimony. Guidance on haunting investigations specifically discusses the investigator's pastoral role and the importance of recognising when referral to appropriate services is more responsible than continuing an investigation. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Ethical guidelines for the investigation of haunting 17, 2007 — The paper is divided into three sections: (a) ge... Published: November 17, 2007 Children deserve additional protection. Interviews should be appropriate to their age and understanding, should avoid leading questions, and should not encourage frightening interpretations of ordinary events. Modern research ethics for children consistently emphasises informed participation, privacy and safeguarding over the collection of interesting data. If there are concerns that a child may be at risk of harm, safeguarding responsibilities can override ordinary expectations of confidentiality. user research.education.gov.uk+2eprints.ncrm.ac.uk [user research.education.gov.uk] user research.education.gov.uk Research with children and young peopleGuidance and ideas to help you plan and conduct research with children and young people successful... Practical safeguards include: Interview adults before speaking with children whenever appropriate. Avoid asking children to repeat frightening stories in front of an audience or camera. Never pressure children to revisit locations that frighten them. Distinguish clearly between what a child directly observed and what they later heard from adults or siblings. Stop an interview if it becomes upsetting rather than pursuing dramatic details. What Are The Ethical Rules For Recording Paranormal Activity In Private Places? Channel: Inside the Supernatural Open on YouTube When evidence should stay private Not every genuine piece of evidence belongs in the public domain. Exact addresses, interior photographs, security camera layouts, medical information, family disputes and identifiable recordings can expose witnesses to harassment or unwanted tourism . Sometimes withholding information actually strengthens an investigation. Retaining confidential details allows future witnesses to provide independent accounts without being influenced by material already circulating online. It also reduces the risk that fabricated reports simply repeat published descriptions. Sensitive material may be better: Archived privately with the owner's permission. Shared only with trusted collaborators who need it for verification. Published in anonymised form. Withheld indefinitely if publication would create disproportionate harm. The value of evidence should never be judged solely by how widely it can be shared. Respect homes, property and communities A reputedly haunted building is still someone's home, workplace or heritage site. Investigators should obtain permission before entering private property, avoid disrupting neighbours and leave locations exactly as they found them. Historic buildings deserve particular care because repeated unsupervised visits can damage fragile sites or encourage trespassing. Ethics also extend to online behaviour. Publicly identifying private residences as \"haunted\" can attract curiosity seekers long after an investigation ends. Families who simply reported unexplained sounds or experiences may become unwilling tourist attractions through no fault of their own. Responsible investigators therefore separate the public interest in unusual reports from the public's curiosity about specific households. Avoid creating the story you are trying to investigate Ethical interviewing and reliable evidence reinforce each other. Leading questions, exaggerated reactions and repeated suggestions can unintentionally reshape memories. Witnesses may begin to describe what they think investigators expect instead of what they originally experienced. A more ethical approach is to: Ask open questions before mentioning paranormal interpretations. Record uncertainty rather than filling gaps with speculation. Keep independent witnesses separate until each has given an account. Distinguish observation from interpretation in notes and publications. Report contradictory evidence rather than editing it away. These practices reduce both investigator influence and confirmation bias, making later evaluation more credible. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk Notes for InvestigatorsFor instance, crisis apparitions might require confirmation of a reported death from newspaper notices or public r... Transparency, money and conflicts of interest Financial incentives can create ethical tensions. Charging for investigations, selling dramatic narratives or guaranteeing paranormal conclusions may encourage investigators to overstate weak evidence or discourage ordinary explanations. Ethical guidelines for haunting investigations recommend being transparent about fees, expertise, equipment limitations and investigative goals. Witnesses should understand whether they are receiving a research visit, an entertainment experience"},{"title":"Witnesses","url":"/witnesses-ef1bfb/","description":"Interviewing witnesses separately helps reveal who saw the phenomenon directly and which details spread through conversation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"Witnesses Did Everyone Really See the Same Thing? Witnesses Witnesses Did Everyone Really See the Same Thing? Interviewing witnesses separately helps reveal who saw the phenomenon directly and which details spread through conversation. Interviewing witnesses separately helps reveal who saw the phenomenon directly and which details spread through conversation. policy intervention Why witnesses should be separated early Questions that avoid feeding a theory Comparing drawings, times, and sensory details forteana fieldwork witnesses Introduction When several people report the same unusual event, it is tempting to treat their testimony as a single, stronger piece of evidence. In practice, the opposite is often true. Once witnesses begin discussing what they saw, heard or thought, individual memories can become blended. For anyone carrying out beginner level Fortean fieldwork , one of the most valuable early steps is to interview witnesses separately before they compare notes. Doing so does not imply that anyone is dishonest. Instead, it helps distinguish direct observation from details acquired through conversation, media coverage or later reflection. Research on eyewitness memory consistently shows that co witness discussion can alter later recollections, making independent accounts far more valuable than a single agreed story. [PMC+2Wiley Online Library] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCOnline misinformation can distort witnesses' memories by M Kękuś · 2024 · Cited by 2 — The memory conformity effect occurs when people witness a given incident and then talk to each other... Why witnesses should be separated early The purpose of separate interviews is to preserve the original memory of each person. A family, a group of friends or a crowd may all have experienced the same incident, but not from the same position or with the same attention. One person may have watched the sky while another noticed sounds, lighting or nearby reactions. Those differences are useful evidence rather than problems to eliminate. Psychological research describes this as the memory conformity or co witness effect. After discussing an event, witnesses frequently incorporate information supplied by others into their own recollections, sometimes becoming genuinely convinced they observed details that they actually learned later. This does not necessarily involve deliberate fabrication; instead, people may lose track of whether a memory came from direct perception or another person's description. [PMC+2ResearchGate] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCOnline misinformation can distort witnesses' memories by M Kękuś · 2024 · Cited by 2 — The memory conformity effect occurs when people witness a given incident and then talk to each other... For Fortean investigations, this distinction matters because many reports emerge within close knit groups. By the time an investigator arrives, the witnesses may already have spent hours or days talking through the experience together. Recording what each person remembers independently provides a clearer picture of: which observations were genuinely shared; which details came from only one witness; where memories diverge; how interpretations developed over time. Agreement is informative, but so is disagreement. Differences often reflect different viewpoints rather than errors. Ask questions that do not feed a theory Separate interviews are only effective if the questioning itself avoids introducing new ideas. Begin with an invitation such as, \"Please tell me everything you remember from the beginning.\" Allow the witness to complete their account before asking specific follow up questions. This approach reduces the chance that the interviewer accidentally supplies information that becomes incorporated into later memory. Research on eyewitness interviewing has repeatedly shown that suggestive questions can alter recall, whereas open ended retrieval generally produces more reliable descriptions. [OUP Academic+2Social Research Update] academic.oup.com Academic Eyewitness Memory OUP AcademicEyewitness Memory Oxford Academic22 Mar 2023 — Indeed, the very act of testing memory changes (i.e., contaminates) memory... Useful follow up questions include: Where exactly were you standing? What first attracted your attention? What happened next? How certain are you about that detail? Is there anything you remember seeing but are unsure about? Did you discuss the event with anyone before speaking with me? Avoid questions that assume a conclusion, such as: \"Did the object land?\" \"Did everyone see the creature?\" \"Was it glowing green?\" \"Did it disappear into another dimension?\" These questions risk replacing observation with interpretation. A better practice is to separate the description from the explanation. If someone says they saw \"a ghost\", ask what they actually observed: a shape, movement, sound, temperature change or something else. \"Eyewitness Memory and the Social Science Research\" Channel: University of Virginia School of Law · Views: 7.7K · Uploaded: April 2013 · Length: 1 hour 14 minutes Open on YouTube Comparing drawings, times and sensory details Once every witness has completed an independent interview, the accounts can be compared systematically. Sketches before discussion Ask each witness to produce a simple sketch from memory before seeing anyone else's drawing. Artistic ability is irrelevant. The aim is to capture relative position, movement, landmarks and perspective. Comparing independent sketches can reveal patterns such as: consistent direction of travel; differing estimates of altitude or distance; whether all witnesses actually viewed the same object; which landmarks each person used for orientation. If several drawings converge on the same unusual feature despite being completed independently, that agreement deserves attention. Equally, obvious differences may simply reflect different viewing positions rather than conflicting testimony. Times and sequence People are often less accurate about timing than they believe. Rather than asking for exact minutes immediately, ask witnesses to describe the sequence of events. Useful prompts include: What were you doing immediately beforehand? What happened first? What happened immediately afterwards? Did anyone leave or arrive during the event? Later, compare these accounts with photographs, mobile phone timestamps, emergency calls or messages. Independent timelines often reveal where memory is strongest and where later discussion may have filled gaps. Separate the senses Witnesses rarely experience an event in exactly the same way. One person may report: an unusual light; while another remembers: silence; a strong smell; animal reactions; an unexpected feeling of vibration. Recording each sensory channel separately prevents vivid visual descriptions from overshadowing other observations that may prove equally important. How ACCURATE is Eyewitness Testimony? Leading Questions & Post Event Discussion Channel: Bear it in MIND Open on YouTube What differences actually tell you Beginners sometimes worry that inconsistent accounts weaken a case. In reality, modest differences are normal. People observe different aspects of the same event because they differ in: viewing angle; attention; eyesight or hearing; expectations; previous experience; the point at which they first noticed the event. Research shows that complete agreement after extensive discussion may actually be less informative than partially overlapping independent accounts. Witnesses who exchange information can unintentionally converge on a common narrative, making later agreement difficult to interpret as truly independent corroboration. [ResearchGate+2Wiley Online Library] researchgate.net Memory Conformity: Can Eyewitnesses Influence Each...The current study investigated memory conformity effects between indivi... The investigator's task is therefore not to force every statement into a single version but to identify: observations repeated independently; observations unique to one witness; uncertainties honestly acknowledged; details that appear only after group discussion. That pattern often reveals more than a polished collective story. Practical fieldwork tips For beginner Fortean investigations, a few habits make separate witness interviews much more effective: Speak to each witness individually as soon as practical. Record who was present during each interview. Ask whether the witness has already discussed the event with others. Note confidence separately from certainty; a confident delivery does not guarantee accuracy. Preserve uncertainty instead of encouraging witnesses to resolve discrepancies. Compare accounts only after every interview has been completed. Keep original notes, sketches and recordings rather than replacing them with a merged summary. These practices help preserve the raw observations that later investigators can evaluate independently. Even if an unusual report ultimately has an ordinary explanation , carefully"},{"title":"Sky Checks","url":"/sky-checks/","description":"Bright planets, meteors, haze, cloud, aircraft, and unusual viewing conditions should be checked before a sky report becomes a UFO case.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Fieldwork","level":3,"text":"Sky Checks Before Calling It a UFO Sky Checks Sky Checks Before Calling It a UFO Bright planets, meteors, haze, cloud, aircraft, and unusual viewing conditions should be checked before a sky report becomes a UFO case. Bright planets, meteors, haze, cloud, aircraft, and unusual viewing conditions should be checked before a sky report becomes a UFO case. mechanism Common sky objects mistaken for anomalies Weather and visibility checks How to record direction and movement forteana fieldwork sky checks Introduction Most reports of mysterious lights in the sky do not begin with deception or fantasy. They begin with a sincere witness who has seen something unusual under unfamiliar viewing conditions. For a beginner in Fortean fieldwork , the most valuable habit is therefore simple: check ordinary astronomy before treating a report as a UFO case. The exact time, viewing direction, weather, and location often provide enough information to identify a bright planet, meteor, aircraft, satellite or atmospheric effect without dismissing the witness's experience . Careful sky checks preserve credibility, reduce false leads and ensure that genuinely unusual reports are separated from events that have well understood natural explanations. Astronomers, aviation investigators and long running UFO investigations have all found that many reports can be resolved through this method. [nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Identifying UFOs and UAPs1 Dec 2013 — Venus shining bright and low has been reported many times as a UFO. Do you think you have spotted a... Common sky objects mistaken for anomalies A surprising number of dramatic reports involve familiar astronomical objects viewed under unfamiliar circumstances. Bright planets. Venus is the classic example. As the brightest planet, it can appear brilliant shortly after sunset or before sunrise, especially when low on the horizon. Atmospheric turbulence near the horizon can make it shimmer, flash different colours and seem to move. Because it remains almost stationary while foreground clouds drift, observers sometimes describe it as a hovering craft. Jupiter can produce similar reports, while Sirius, the brightest star, is also frequently misidentified when atmospheric scintillation causes rapid colour changes. [nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Identifying UFOs and UAPs1 Dec 2013 — Venus shining bright and low has been reported many times as a UFO. Do you think you have spotted a... Meteors and fireballs. A bright meteor may last only a few seconds yet be visible across hundreds of kilometres. Larger fireballs can fragment, leave glowing trails or produce delayed sounds, creating reports of exploding craft or descending objects. Checking reports from astronomical organisations or meteor networks for the relevant date often resolves such cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Satellites. Satellites travel steadily across the sky without engine noise. Some become dramatically brighter for a short period as sunlight reflects from their surfaces. Although the famous Iridium satellite flares are now largely historical, other satellites can still brighten unexpectedly, particularly soon after sunset or before sunrise. [nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Identifying UFOs and UAPs1 Dec 2013 — Venus shining bright and low has been reported many times as a UFO. Do you think you have spotted a... Aircraft viewed from unusual angles. An aircraft approaching directly towards an observer may appear almost stationary while its landing lights grow brighter. At night, navigation lights and flashing strobes can be difficult to interpret without obvious reference points. Military refuelling formations, helicopters and aircraft holding patterns have repeatedly generated UFO reports . [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr The light from a jet aids to identification of flying objectsMay 15, 2012 — Lights used on the Boeing KC 135 Stratotanker aircraft for night refueling mission... Published: May 15, 2012 The important lesson is not that witnesses are careless. Human perception estimates distance, speed and size poorly when the sky provides few visual references. Jupiter and Venus 'could be mistaken for UFOs' Channel: The Telegraph · Views: 22.9K · Uploaded: March 2012 · Length: 1 minute 57 seconds Open on YouTube Why weather changes what people think they saw Atmospheric conditions often transform ordinary celestial objects into apparently extraordinary ones. Near the horizon, light passes through much more atmosphere than it does overhead. Refraction bends light, while turbulence causes stars and planets to twinkle strongly. Thin cloud, haze or drifting mist can briefly obscure an object, making it appear to dart, pulse or vanish behind invisible obstacles. Temperature differences in the atmosphere can also distort shapes or produce shimmering effects. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Atmospheric refraction Atmospheric refraction Cloud itself deserves attention before any extraordinary interpretation. Thin cloud can produce halos or diffuse glowing patches around bright objects. Lenticular clouds have long been described as resembling flying saucers because they form smooth, lens shaped layers that appear motionless over hills or mountains. High humidity and airborne particles increase scattering, making lights appear larger or more colourful than they really are. Low cloud can reflect city lighting, searchlights or aircraft illumination into unexpected shapes. [Farmers' Almanac] farmersalmanac.com Farmers' Almanac UFO Sighting? See This List Before Calling The Authorities!August 30, 2023 — 20 Things Often Misidentified As UFO Sightings · 1) Venus · 2) Meteor “Fir... Published: August 30, 2023 Recording local weather —including cloud cover, visibility, wind direction and recent storms—often proves as valuable as interviewing witnesses. Before calling it a UFO A practical sky check requires only a few essential questions asked in the correct order. First establish the exact time, preferably from a phone, camera metadata or another recorded source rather than memory alone. Even a difference of ten or fifteen minutes may alter the position of bright planets or the visibility of satellites. Next determine direction. Ask the witness where they were standing and which way they were facing. Compass bearings are ideal, but references such as \"above the church tower\" or \"over the western ridge\" are also useful because they can later be converted into approximate azimuths. Then estimate altitude. A convenient field method is to measure with an outstretched hand: Fist at arm's length: roughly 10 degrees. Three middle fingers: roughly 5 degrees. Little finger: roughly 1 degree. These estimates allow later comparison with astronomy software or sky charts. Finally, record movement carefully. Separate actual movement from apparent movement. A witness may describe an object \"following the car\", when the apparent motion resulted from changing perspective while driving. Likewise, a bright planet seen through moving cloud may appear to accelerate or weave even though it remains fixed relative to the stars. [nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Identifying UFOs and UAPs1 Dec 2013 — Venus shining bright and low has been reported many times as a UFO. Do you think you have spotted a... Starlink Satellites Mistaken As UFOs Over Pittsburgh Channel: CBS Pittsburgh · Views: 3.2K · Uploaded: December 2021 · Length: 30 seconds Open on YouTube Historical lessons from UFO investigations Many official and civilian investigations have reached similar conclusions despite differing assumptions about UFOs. Studies of resolved reports consistently show that astronomical objects, aircraft and balloons account for a large proportion of identified cases. Bright planets alone have repeatedly generated waves of reports when particularly prominent in the evening or morning sky. Investigators have also found that witness sincerity and mistaken identification are entirely compatible: an honest observer can accurately report what they perceived while still misidentifying its cause. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Identification studies of UFOs Identification studies of UFOs This historical pattern explains why experienced investigators resist immediate conclusions. A report should remain \"unidentified\" only after ordinary possibilities have been checked rather than before. Identified Flying Objects Oct 29th Nov 3rd Star Gazers Channel: South Florida PBS Open on YouTube A fieldworker's minimum astronomy checklist For beginners, the aim is not to become an astronomer but to collect enough information that an astronomical explanation can be tested later. Record: Exact date and local time. Observation location. Viewing direction. Estimated height above the horizon. Duration. Apparent brightness compared with the Moon, Venus or nearby stars. Colour and any changes in colour. Whether the object remained fixed, travelled steadily,"},{"title":"Strange News","url":"/strange-news/","description":"Fortean journalism turns odd reports into public stories, sometimes clarifying them and sometimes amplifying confusion.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Strange News How Strange News Becomes Fortean History Strange News Strange News How Strange News Becomes Fortean History Fortean journalism turns odd reports into public stories, sometimes clarifying them and sometimes amplifying confusion. Fortean journalism turns odd reports into public stories, sometimes clarifying them and sometimes amplifying confusion. policy intervention Local report to wider retelling Headlines and exaggeration Responsible anomaly reporting forteana strange news Introduction Fortean journalism is the newsroom side of forteana: the process by which an odd local report becomes a public story, a clipping, a rumour, a magazine item, an archive entry and, sometimes, a lasting legend. Its importance is not that newspapers prove monsters, UFOs or impossible rains. It is that news framing decides which strange claims are preserved, how cautiously they are described, who gets treated as credible, and whether uncertainty is clarified or made more exciting. Charles Fort built his work from reported anomalies, and later Fortean outlets such as Fortean Times turned that habit into a continuing record of “weird world news”, reporting and research. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... The best Fortean journalism therefore sits between credulity and dismissal . It asks: what exactly was reported, by whom, where did the wording change, what ordinary checks were attempted, and what did later retellings add? The weakest version simply packages ambiguity as mystery. The strongest version preserves strangeness without pretending that confusion is evidence. From Local Report to Wider Retelling Many Fortean stories begin modestly: a witness speaks to a local reporter, a police officer, a small town editor or a specialist newsletter. The first version often matters more than later legend because it fixes the vocabulary. A “thing”, a “creature”, a “flying saucer” or a “mystery noise” gives later writers a ready made frame. Charles Fort’s own method depended on this public trail. He collected reports from newspapers, magazines and scientific journals, filing anomalies that seemed to contradict confident explanations. Historian Charlotte Sleigh notes that Fort gathered astronomical newspaper clippings with particular energy, especially material that appeared to undercut accepted predictions or expert confidence. [kar.kent.ac.uk] kar.kent.ac.uk The Silences of Science The Silences of Science Public Domain Review’s account of Fort’s Book of the Damned similarly stresses that his archive grew from “raining frogs, mysterious disappearances , baffling objects in the sky” and other reports he found in print. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain Review... That origin matters because Fortean history is often less a record of events than a record of reports about events. A local article may capture a witness’s immediate uncertainty, while later retellings tidy the story into a cleaner myth. The Fortean reader has to compare layers: first report, follow up, national pickup, specialist retelling, sceptical explanation, anniversary feature and tourist board afterlife. The Mothman story shows this process clearly. In November 1966, the Point Pleasant case entered print through strikingly hesitant language: the local headline described a “man sized bird”, “creature” and “something”, preserving the witnesses’ inability to categorise what they believed they had seen. The Smithsonian Center for Folklife and Cultural Heritage traces how the account moved from local fear and newspaper coverage into John Keel’s later book, a Hollywood film, a museum, a statue and a regional identity marker. [folklife.si.edu] folklife.si.edu Open source on si.edu. The case became Fortean not simply because people reported a winged figure, but because journalism gave the uncertainty a durable public form. Warminster offers a British example. In 1965, Arthur Shuttlewood of the Warminster Journal reported local accounts of vibrations, menacing sounds and unexplained night time sights. A photograph passed from local hands through Shuttlewood to the Daily Mirror helped push the “Warminster Thing” from town rumour into national UFO culture. A 2025 retrospective in the Warminster Journal quotes former Ministry of Defence investigator Nick Pope describing how the story gained “critical mass”: reports accumulated, mainstream media arrived, public meetings were held, and the sightings became “something else”. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Open source on warminsterjournal.co.uk. The pattern is central to Fortean journalism. A strange event rarely travels unchanged. It is translated at each stage: Witness phrase to headline: uncertainty becomes a memorable label. Local story to national item: a community disturbance becomes entertainment, threat or mystery. News item to Fortean archive: scattered reports become a case file. Archive to legend: later writers select the most vivid details and often drop the qualifying language. A responsible Fortean account keeps those transformations visible. Headlines Can Preserve Doubt or Manufacture Mystery Strange news is especially vulnerable to headline pressure. Editors need a concise hook; Fortean material offers ready made novelty. But the choice between “residents report unexplained lights” and “UFO terror over town” is not cosmetic. It changes the reader’s evidential starting point. The Mothman headline is memorable because it does not fully settle the category: “bird”, “creature”, “something”. That uncertainty is good Fortean evidence because it tells us the original report was unstable. Later versions that call the figure simply “Mothman” make the case easier to remember but also more creature like than the first wording required. [folklife.si.edu] folklife.si.edu Open source on si.edu. Warminster shows the opposite risk: repeated reports, public meetings and national attention can turn a cluster of ambiguous observations into a branded mystery. Pope’s retrospective comments are useful precisely because they do not flatten the story into either proof or debunking. He points to possible explanations such as military exercises, flares and meteorological activity, while also noting that nobody gave local people a definitive explanation at the time. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Open source on warminsterjournal.co.uk. That gap is where Fortean history grows: not from confirmed impossibility, but from a failure of timely, trusted explanation. Modern misinformation research helps explain why strange news travels so well. MIT’s summary of a major Science study found that false news on Twitter spread farther, faster and more broadly than true news, with false stories 70 per cent more likely to be retweeted in the dataset examined. The researchers also found that people, not only bots, drove the faster spread. [MIT News] news.mit.edu Open source on mit.edu. Fortean stories are not automatically false, but they often contain the ingredients that make stories shareable: novelty, surprise, emotional charge and unresolved interpretation. This creates a special duty for editors. Strange news headlines should avoid pretending that a witness report is a verified phenomenon. A headline can be vivid without overclaiming: “Residents report booming sky noises” is more honest than “mystery weapon shakes town” unless there is evidence for a weapon. The Fortean value is not weakened by caution. It is strengthened, because later readers can see what was actually known. UFOs discovered in The National Archives Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 39.7K · Uploaded: May 2008 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Specialist Outlets Turn Clippings into a Continuing Record Fortean journalism is not only mainstream news accidentally producing oddities. It also includes specialist publications that deliberately collect, compare and revisit strange reports. Fortean Times is the most visible example in Britain. The Guardian described it in 2002 as the “journal of strange phenomena”, filled with UFOs, crop circles and apparitions, and noted that it was founded in 1973 to continue Fort’s work. [The Guardian] theguardian.com Open source on theguardian.com. The magazine’s role is important because a clipping by itself is fragile. Specialist Fortean outlets preserve small items that would otherwise vanish: odd animal falls, unusual coincidences, local apparitions, anomalous lights, strange archaeological claims and corrections to earlier reports. They also create a community of readers who send in clippings, challenge explanations and notice repeated motifs. That archival function can clarify confusion. A specialist editor may recognise that a “mystery light” resembles Chinese lantern reports from a particular period, that a “monster” description echoes known folklore, or that a dramatic"},{"title":"Retellings","url":"/retellings/","description":"Comparing first reports with later retellings reveals what was witnessed, what was added, and what uncertainty disappeared.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Retellings What Later Legends Add to First Reports Retellings Retellings What Later Legends Add to First Reports Comparing first reports with later retellings reveals what was witnessed, what was added, and what uncertainty disappeared. Comparing first reports with later retellings reveals what was witnessed, what was added, and what uncertainty disappeared. mechanism Why first wording matters most How national pickup changes local stories A comparison checklist for later versions forteana strange news retellings Introduction The strongest evidence in many classic Fortean cases is often found not in the most famous version of the story but in the earliest one. Initial newspaper reports, police statements and local interviews usually preserve uncertainty: witnesses describe \"something\", \"a bird like creature\", \"a strange light\" or \"an unusual sound\" because they do not yet know what they have seen. As a case spreads through national newspapers , books, documentaries and the internet, that uncertainty frequently shrinks. Details become more dramatic, loose connections become firm conclusions, and later writers may unintentionally combine multiple reports into a single, cleaner narrative. Understanding this process is essential to reading Fortean journalism critically. It does not determine whether an event was ordinary or extraordinary, but it helps distinguish what witnesses originally reported from what later tradition added. [SHURA] shura.shu.ac.uk The Mothman of West Virginia: A case study in Legendary...March 4, 2020 — by D Clarke · 2022 · Cited by 6 — The book is a classic o... Published: March 4, 2020 Why first wording matters most The first published account has a unique evidential value because it is usually closest in time to the event. Although first reports can contain mistakes made under pressure, they generally preserve three important features that are often weakened in later retellings: Uncertainty: witnesses frequently admit what they could not identify. Original vocabulary: descriptions are given before a memorable label becomes attached. Chronology: early reports often separate individual incidents that later become merged. As stories circulate, editors naturally favour memorable phrases over hesitant ones. A headline needs a recognisable subject, whereas eyewitnesses often do not have one. The result is a gradual shift from observation to interpretation. The famous Point Pleasant reports illustrate this clearly. The first newspaper headline did not announce that \"Mothman\" had appeared. Instead it referred to a \"Man Sized Bird...Creature...Something\", reflecting genuine uncertainty about what had been seen. The later name \"Mothman\" quickly became the dominant frame, encouraging readers to imagine a specific creature rather than an unidentified observation. Researchers of the legend have noted that this shift influenced virtually every later retelling. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2SHURA] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... The same pattern appears in many cryptid and UFO reports . Initial witnesses often reject confident interpretations. In the first Point Pleasant coverage, one witness explicitly insisted that the object was not a flying saucer. Later accounts, however, frequently discuss UFO connections because those ideas entered the story afterwards through subsequent investigations and publications rather than through the original witness statements. [UW Milwaukee] uwm.edu UW Milwaukee History student tells the story of the Mothman UW MilwaukeeHistory student tells the story of the MothmanJuly 12, 2021 — 12 Jul 2021 — In mid November, 1966, in the small town of Point... Published: July 12, 2021 The Legendary Mothman and the Evolution of Folklore Jack Daly Channel: Jon Solo Open on YouTube How national pickup changes local stories Once an unusual local report attracts wider attention, several mechanisms tend to reshape it. A memorable label replaces descriptive language Names such as \"Mothman\", \"Nessie\" or the \"Flatwoods Monster\" are useful shorthand, but they also encourage readers to assume that separate reports concern the same entity. Early reports often contain much broader or more ambiguous descriptions than the later label suggests. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster Separate incidents become a single narrative Local newspapers commonly publish sightings over days or weeks as distinct events. Later books often arrange them into a coherent storyline with a beginning, middle and end. This creates a more satisfying narrative but can blur differences between witnesses, dates and locations. Studies of the Mothman tradition note that later authors connected diverse experiences—including UFO reports, mysterious visitors, unusual lights and eventually the collapse of the Silver Bridge—into one integrated legend. Those links became culturally influential even though they emerged progressively rather than appearing fully formed in the earliest reporting. [SHURA] shura.shu.ac.uk The Mothman of West Virginia: A case study in Legendary...March 4, 2020 — by D Clarke · 2022 · Cited by 6 — The book is a classic o... Published: March 4, 2020 Uncertainty gradually disappears Journalists working close to an event often quote witnesses directly and preserve hesitation. Anniversary articles, documentaries and popular books are more likely to summarise rather than quote in full. Each summary risks replacing cautious wording with certainty. A witness who originally said \"it looked like\" may later be remembered as saying \"it was\". That change may seem minor, yet it alters how readers judge the evidence. Additional folklore attaches itself As publicity increases, unrelated experiences are sometimes retrospectively connected to the famous case. Later researchers have documented how reports from different countries and earlier decades have occasionally been reclassified as examples of Mothman like encounters, even though they were not originally interpreted that way. This illustrates how legends expand by absorbing older material once a recognised narrative exists. [IUScholarWorks] scholarworks.iu.edu ScholarWorksThe Mothman: The Migration of a Local Legendby E HASKEN — In an interview on March 13, 2016, Ashley Wamsley shared the foll... Published: March 13, 2016 THE CHUPACABRA HOAX: How a Hollywood Movie Created a Real Life Monster Paskvan Podcasts Channel: Paskvan Podcast Open on YouTube Case examples of evolving narratives The Mothman story remains one of the clearest demonstrations of how a Fortean report evolves through successive layers of retelling. The November 1966 newspaper coverage presented frightened witnesses describing an unidentified winged figure. Subsequent local reporting expanded the number of sightings. John Keel's later investigations added broader paranormal themes, including alleged Men in Black encounters and wider patterns of unexplained phenomena. The 2002 film adaptation then introduced further dramatic restructuring for storytelling purposes, bringing the legend to audiences far beyond West Virginia. Each stage preserved elements of earlier reporting while also introducing new emphases. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2SHURA] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... The modern Loch Ness Monster tradition followed a similar, although longer, process. Early newspaper reports from 1933 described unusual disturbances in the water using varied language such as \"beast\" or \"whale like fish\". As international coverage increased, the single image of a long necked monster became dominant. Later discussions often focus on iconic photographs, particularly the \"Surgeon's Photograph\", despite its eventual exposure as a hoax. The famous image came to shape public memory more strongly than many earlier written accounts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster These examples demonstrate that later versions are not necessarily false; rather, they often represent a mixture of original testimony, later interpretation and accumulated folklore. A comparison checklist for later versions Readers comparing an original report with a later retelling can ask several practical questions. QuestionWhy it mattersDoes the witness's original wording still appear?Direct quotations preserve uncertainty better than summaries.Have new details appeared that were absent from the earliest reports?Added descriptions may originate from later speculation rather than observation.Has a descriptive phrase become a fixed label?Naming can narrow interpretation before the evidence justifies it.Are multiple incidents now presented as one event?Merged narratives can exaggerate consistency.Are later"},{"title":"Clippings","url":"/clippings/","description":"Specialist outlets keep small strange reports alive, but their value depends on clear sourcing, correction, and restraint.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Clippings Why Weird Clippings Still Matter Clippings Clippings Why Weird Clippings Still Matter Specialist outlets keep small strange reports alive, but their value depends on clear sourcing, correction, and restraint. Specialist outlets keep small strange reports alive, but their value depends on clear sourcing, correction, and restraint. entity How specialist outlets preserve fragile reports Reader tips, corrections, and recurring motifs The risk of repeating mystery without checks forteana strange news clippings Introduction Fortean Times occupies a distinctive place in Fortean journalism because it treats odd newspaper clippings as the beginning of an investigation rather than the end of one. Following the example of Charles Fort, who built his books from thousands of reports gathered from newspapers, journals and scientific publications, the magazine preserves unusual stories that might otherwise disappear while encouraging readers to ask where they came from, how they changed over time and whether later evidence supports or weakens them. This approach gives seemingly trivial local reports lasting historical value without assuming that every mystery is genuine. At its best, a clipped oddity becomes a traceable document in the history of an unexplained claim rather than a piece of folklore detached from its sources. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... How specialist outlets preserve fragile reports Before searchable online archives, many unusual local stories had remarkably short lives. A report about a strange animal, unexplained lights or an odd weather event might appear in a provincial newspaper and then vanish from public view within days. Enthusiasts clipped these articles, exchanged them by post and gradually assembled collections that preserved material larger newspapers and libraries often ignored. Fortean Times , founded in 1973 from Bob Rickard's newsletter The News , inherited this tradition while giving it a more systematic form. Rather than relying solely on dramatic feature articles, it created regular sections that gathered short reports from newspapers around the world. Many of these items were supplied by readers, who sent original clippings or references to local publications. This crowdsourced archive helped preserve stories that would otherwise have been difficult to rediscover years later. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times The value of this practice extends beyond paranormal interests. Local newspapers frequently record eyewitness descriptions before later retellings simplify, exaggerate or fictionalise events. Even when an unusual report ultimately receives an ordinary explanation , the original clipping remains useful evidence of how the event was first understood and presented. Why a clipping is evidence of reporting, not proof of the event One of the most important Fortean habits is separating the existence of a report from the truth of the reported event. A newspaper clipping demonstrates that: someone made a claim; an editor considered it newsworthy; specific wording entered the public record; later writers can compare their versions against the earliest published account. It does not automatically demonstrate that the underlying event occurred exactly as described. This distinction mirrors Charles Fort 's own method. He collected anomalies because they challenged assumptions about certainty, not because every report deserved unquestioning belief. Modern Fortean Times has repeatedly described its editorial stance as one of \"benevolent scepticism\", aiming neither to dismiss unusual claims automatically nor to promote them without examination. Original sourcing therefore matters as much as the strange claim itself. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned The Public Domain ReviewStrange Gods: Charles Fort's Book of the Damned (1919)26 Nov 2024 — Rains of blood and frogs, mysterious disappea... The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube Reader tips, corrections and recurring motifs The magazine's long running interaction with readers is one reason clipped oddities retain research value instead of becoming a simple catalogue of curiosities. Readers contribute by: locating forgotten newspaper reports; identifying the original publication behind widely repeated stories; correcting dates, locations and quotations; supplying follow up coverage that may explain or complicate an earlier mystery; recognising recurring legends that have appeared repeatedly under different names. This collaborative approach has practical consequences. A mysterious story that appears unique may later prove to have circulated decades earlier with only minor changes. Conversely, several apparently unrelated reports may reveal a consistent historical pattern once their original sources are assembled. An early feature, Forteana Corrigenda , was specifically intended to identify errors that had accumulated in Fortean literature through repeated quotation and misattribution. That emphasis on correction reflects an understanding that copying without verification gradually transforms uncertain reports into apparently established facts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times The risk of repeating mystery without checks The same clipping that preserves valuable evidence can also preserve mistakes. Several problems commonly emerge when unusual reports are copied repeatedly: headlines become more dramatic than the underlying article; witness uncertainty disappears in later summaries; explanatory follow up stories are omitted; separate incidents become merged into a single legend; secondary books quote one another instead of the original newspaper. These changes can make a routine local curiosity appear stronger than the historical record supports. The problem is not unique to Fortean subjects, but it is especially important where dramatic narratives attract repeated retelling. Specialist publications reduce this risk when they cite original newspapers, distinguish confirmed facts from reported claims and publish corrections when new information appears. They increase it when unattributed anecdotes are reproduced simply because they fit an appealing mystery. 67. Fortean Times Talk (with Ian Simmons) Channel: Chinwag Podcast Open on YouTube Why clipped oddities still matter A single strange clipping rarely settles an unexplained event, but large collections reveal something more valuable: patterns in how societies report anomalies. Across decades of Fortean Times , readers can trace recurring themes including mysterious animals, unusual weather, folklore, UFO reports, bizarre accidents, mistaken identities, urban legends and stories later exposed as hoaxes. Looking across hundreds of reports allows researchers to compare language, media framing and cultural assumptions rather than treating every case in isolation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times The accumulated archive also helps distinguish genuinely unusual incidents from recurring storytelling traditions. When a modern report resembles dozens of earlier newspaper accounts, researchers gain context that would be impossible from a single clipping alone. Edinburgh Fortean Society Weird World of Charles Fort Channel: Gordon Rutter Open on YouTube Reading Fortean clippings critically The most useful way to approach a clipped oddity is neither to accept nor reject it immediately, but to ask practical questions: Can the original newspaper or publication be identified? Is the clipping complete, or only a headline or extract? Were follow up reports published? Do independent contemporary sources describe the same event? Has the story changed in later books, documentaries or online retellings? Were corrections or alternative explanations published afterwards? Approached in this way, clipped oddities become historical documents rather than isolated curiosities. Their lasting value lies less in proving extraordinary events than in preserving fragile records of how extraordinary claims entered—and sometimes evolved within—the public record. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Weird Clippings Still Matter. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Book of the Damned By Charles Fort Originates the clipping based Fortean tradition. See on Amazon Book The Complete Books of Charles Fort By Charles Fort Collects the foundational Fortean texts in one volume. See on Amazon Book Wild talents By Charles Fort Expands on Fortean documentation of extraordinary claims. See on Amazon Book Lo! By Charles Fort Continues Fort's examination of anomalous reports. See on Amazon Browse more"},{"title":"Official Gaps","url":"/official-gaps/","description":"When authorities fail to give timely, trusted explanations, ambiguous reports can become stronger in public memory rather than weaker.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Official Gaps When No Answer Becomes the Story Official Gaps Official Gaps When No Answer Becomes the Story When authorities fail to give timely, trusted explanations, ambiguous reports can become stronger in public memory rather than weaker. When authorities fail to give timely, trusted explanations, ambiguous reports can become stronger in public memory rather than weaker. policy intervention Why delayed explanations create space for rumours Warminster as a public trust problem How official files can clarify or deepen doubt forteana strange news official gaps Introduction Official silence does not create Fortean rumours on its own, but it often gives them room to grow. When unusual events attract public attention and authorities either cannot, will not or do not provide a timely explanation, the absence of trusted information becomes part of the story. In Fortean journalism , this communication gap is significant because witnesses, reporters and later writers often remember not only the original incident but also the feeling that \"nobody ever explained it\". That perception can strengthen a legend long after the underlying events have faded. The pattern appears repeatedly in strange news history. Local uncertainty becomes amplified when official statements are delayed, fragmented or narrowly focused on administrative concerns rather than public understanding. The resulting vacuum encourages speculation, competing narratives and selective memory, making the rumour itself more durable than the original evidence. Why delayed explanations create space for rumours A common assumption is that rumours spread because people reject official information. In many Fortean cases, the more important problem is that there is little official information to reject in the first place. Several mechanisms make this communication gap especially powerful: Uncertainty becomes memorable. People tend to remember unresolved events more vividly than cases with prompt, credible explanations. Independent witnesses compare notes. In the absence of authoritative clarification, individuals begin validating one another's experiences instead. Journalists continue reporting developments. Each new witness becomes news because no accepted explanation closes the story. Later retellings simplify the uncertainty. What began as \"unexplained at the time\" gradually becomes \"never explained.\" From a governance perspective, silence is rarely interpreted as neutrality. Members of the public may read it as uncertainty, indifference, secrecy or even confirmation that officials know more than they are willing to disclose. Whether or not those interpretations are justified, they influence how unusual reports are remembered. This dynamic is particularly important in Fortean journalism because the archive itself often consists of newspaper reports, witness interviews and official correspondence rather than definitive physical evidence. The absence of an official conclusion therefore becomes another document in the case history. 1973: UFO SPOTTING in WARMINSTER, WILTSHIRE Nationwide Weird and Wonderful BBC Archive Channel: BBC Archive · Views: 22.8K · Uploaded: August 2023 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Warminster as a public trust problem The Warminster sightings of the mid 1960s illustrate how communication failures can become part of a legend's survival rather than merely its background. The first reports concerned mysterious sounds, vibrations and later unusual aerial objects seen around the Wiltshire garrison town. Arthur Shuttlewood, reporting for the Warminster Journal , documented witness accounts with unusual persistence. As reports accumulated, national newspapers , television crews and UFO investigators arrived, transforming a local mystery into one of Britain's best known UFO cases. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — A former Ministry of Defence (M... Published: August 26, 2025 What kept the story alive was not simply the number of sightings but the absence of a single trusted explanation that satisfied residents. Warminster occupied an especially difficult position because it lay close to extensive military training areas on Salisbury Plain. Military activity offered plausible explanations for some observations, including aircraft, flares and unusual sounds. Yet these explanations were rarely presented in a way that addressed every reported incident or every public concern. Former Ministry of Defence UFO investigator Nick Pope later observed that nobody definitively told local people, \"this is what you saw\", leaving the case permanently open in public memory. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — A former Ministry of Defence (M... Published: August 26, 2025 The result was a classic public trust problem rather than simply a UFO mystery. Instead of producing closure, official ambiguity encouraged several parallel interpretations: some residents accepted military explanations; others believed genuine unknown phenomena remained; UFO researchers treated unresolved reports as evidence worthy of continued investigation; sceptics concluded that media attention itself had amplified ordinary events. Because no authoritative narrative displaced the others, each continued circulating alongside the rest. Public meetings intended to calm concerns had similarly mixed effects. Officials, investigators and researchers offered multiple possible explanations—including military exercises, satellites, atmospheric phenomena and misidentifications—but acknowledged that not every report could be resolved to everyone's satisfaction. Rather than ending discussion, this reinforced the perception that something remained unexplained. [Weird Wiltshire] weird wiltshire.co.uk Warminster Thing HM38 Weird WiltshireWarminster Thing HM38.pdf29 Aug 2024 — In the year 2000, the Warminster Thing, as the locals named it, was re examined by... How official files can clarify or deepen doubt One reason Fortean researchers continue revisiting older cases is that government records are periodically released decades after the events. These archives often perform two apparently contradictory functions. First, they can reduce mystery by documenting how authorities actually handled reports. Files may reveal routine investigations, correspondence, radar checks or assessments that sightings presented no defence significance. Such records challenge later assumptions that officials secretly confirmed extraordinary events. Secondly, the same files can deepen public curiosity because they reveal uncertainty inside government itself. Officials sometimes admitted that individual reports could not be positively identified, not because they believed they involved extraterrestrial craft but because available evidence was insufficient. An unresolved file therefore documents administrative uncertainty rather than paranormal confirmation. This distinction is frequently lost in popular retellings. \"Unidentified\" becomes \"unexplainable\", and \"insufficient evidence\" becomes \"official admission\". Responsible Fortean journalism separates these categories carefully. Debunking viral rumors – A story of modern folklore Jack Werner TEDxStockholm Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube Why silence often becomes part of the legend Many famous Fortean stories survive because they contain two overlapping narratives. The first concerns the unusual event itself. The second concerns how institutions responded—or failed to respond. Readers frequently remember statements such as: officials never explained it; authorities ignored witnesses; files remained classified for years; investigators closed the case without satisfying local people. Whether entirely accurate or partly shaped by later memory, these claims become narrative anchors. The institutional response acquires symbolic importance independent of the original incident. For historians of Fortean culture, this explains why communication strategy matters. Public confidence is influenced not only by whether authorities possess answers but by whether they explain what they know, acknowledge what they do not know and distinguish between uncertainty, ongoing investigation and genuine absence of evidence. The governance lesson for Fortean journalism The strongest Fortean reporting does not treat official silence as proof of extraordinary phenomena. Instead, it asks why communication failed and how that failure affected public belief. Warminster demonstrates that rumours can become stronger when official explanations are delayed, fragmented or poorly communicated, even if plausible conventional explanations exist for many individual reports. The legend persisted because unresolved experiences, inconsistent messaging and sustained media attention reinforced one another"},{"title":"Headlines","url":"/headlines/","description":"A cautious headline can preserve uncertainty, while a dramatic one can make a witness report look more solid than it is.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Headlines When Headlines Make Monsters Feel Real Headlines Headlines When Headlines Make Monsters Feel Real A cautious headline can preserve uncertainty, while a dramatic one can make a witness report look more solid than it is. A cautious headline can preserve uncertainty, while a dramatic one can make a witness report look more solid than it is. mechanism Witness wording versus headline wording How labels harden into public memory Safer headline choices for strange news forteana strange news headlines Introduction In Fortean journalism , headlines do more than attract attention: they shape how readers remember unusual events. A cautious witness may describe seeing “something”, “an animal” or “an unexplained light”, yet a headline can transform that uncertainty into “Monster”, “UFO” or “Beast”. Once a memorable label appears in print, later retellings often repeat the headline rather than the witness's actual words. Over time, the headline becomes the story, and the original ambiguity fades from public memory. This is one of the key mechanisms by which strange reports become legends. The process does not require deliberate fabrication. It can emerge through ordinary editorial decisions, the need for concise headlines and the commercial value of memorable language. Research on headline framing shows that headlines strongly influence how readers interpret stories, sometimes even when the article itself contains important qualifications. [Center for Media Engagement] mediaengagement.org Center for Media Engagement The Current State of News Headlines These short statements located at the beginning of an article frame the information that is to...Read more... Witness Wording Versus Headline Wording The first description of an unusual event is often tentative. Witnesses commonly hesitate because they are describing something they cannot confidently identify. They may say: \"I saw something large.\" \"It looked like a bird, but I'm not sure.\" \"There was a strange light.\" \"It sounded unlike anything I've heard before.\" Editors, however, need a short phrase that communicates quickly. A headline cannot easily accommodate long expressions of uncertainty. As a result, it often compresses possibilities into categories: Witness wordingPossible headline\"Something with wings\"\"Flying Monster Seen\"\"Unusual lights\"\"UFO Over Town\"\"Large unidentified animal\"\"Mystery Beast Terrorises Village\" The difference appears small, but it changes the reader's starting assumption. The witness reports uncertainty; the headline supplies identity. The early reporting surrounding the Point Pleasant sightings illustrates this distinction. Contemporary newspaper coverage used hesitant expressions such as \"man sized bird\", \"creature\" and \"something\", reflecting the witnesses' inability to classify what they had seen. Later retellings increasingly replaced those uncertain descriptions with the now famous label \"Mothman\", making the category appear far more settled than the original reporting suggested. Monsters, meaning and media: A Halloween dive into horror cinema The Vandal Theory (S10Ep5) Channel: University of Idaho Open on YouTube How Labels Harden Into Public Memory Headlines have an unusual advantage over the body of an article: they are what people remember. Many readers skim headlines without reading the full report. Others encounter only reproduced headlines in later books, documentaries, websites or social media posts. Each repetition reinforces the same label until it begins to feel like an established fact. This produces several stages of transformation: 1. Observation : someone reports an unexplained experience . 2. Headline: the report receives a memorable label. 3. Repetition: other publications reuse the headline terminology. 4. Memory: the label becomes the accepted name of the event. 5. Legend: later audiences remember the label rather than the uncertainty. In Fortean history, this is often more influential than the underlying evidence itself. The persistence of terms such as \"monster\", \"phantom\", \"beast\" or \"UFO\" reflects the survival of editorial language rather than independent confirmation. Communication research consistently shows that headlines frame readers' interpretation before they encounter supporting details, making first impressions especially influential. [Center for Media Engagement] mediaengagement.org Center for Media Engagement The Current State of News Headlines These short statements located at the beginning of an article frame the information that is to...Read more... How the legend of West Virginia's Mothman has grown over the years Channel: WCHS TV Open on YouTube Why Dramatic Headlines Feel More Certain Several features of headline writing unintentionally increase apparent certainty. Category replacement. Rather than describing an observation, the headline names an object. \"Unknown creature\" becomes \"monster\". Removal of hedging. Words such as \"apparently\", \"possibly\", \"reportedly\" and \"may have\" are often omitted because they consume valuable space. Use of definitive nouns. Headlines prefer compact labels. \"Ghost\", \"Big Cat\" or \"UFO\" reads more cleanly than \"object witnesses could not identify\". Emotional vocabulary. Terms like \"terror\", \"haunts\", \"stalks\" or \"menace\" imply consequences that the original witnesses may never have claimed. Each editorial decision seems minor in isolation. Together they can produce a story that feels much more definite than the underlying testimony. Research on sensational headline construction shows that intensified language and framing increase attention, even though they may shift readers' interpretation of the underlying report. [isoj.org] isoj.org tional forms appear in headline writing, including forward referencing, personalization, “soft” news structures...Read more... The Gap Between the Headline and the Story A revealing exercise is to compare headlines with the opening paragraphs beneath them. Many strange news articles immediately introduce qualifications after the headline: police found no physical evidence; witnesses disagreed about what they saw; weather conditions were poor; investigators suggested ordinary explanations remained possible; photographs were inconclusive. Readers who stop at the headline never encounter these caveats. Even readers who continue may retain the stronger first impression created by the title. Studies of science and health journalism have identified a similar pattern: exaggeration or stronger framing can appear before readers reach the more nuanced discussion in the article itself. Although Fortean reporting differs in subject matter, the communication mechanism is comparable—certainty can be amplified through presentation rather than through stronger evidence. [BMJ] bmj.com Improving the accuracy of academic press releases...Read more... Safer Headline Choices for Strange News Responsible reporting does not require stripping away curiosity. It requires preserving the distinction between observation and interpretation. More reliable headline practices include: describing what was reported rather than naming what it supposedly was; keeping uncertainty visible when witnesses themselves were uncertain; distinguishing eyewitness claims from verified facts; avoiding emotionally loaded labels unless they are clearly attributed; reserving dramatic terminology for direct quotations rather than adopting it as the newspaper's own voice. For example: \"Residents Report Unexplained Lights\" preserves uncertainty. \"Witnesses Describe Large Winged Figure\" reports testimony without asserting identity. \"Police Investigate Reports of Strange Animal\" accurately separates investigation from conclusion. These headlines remain engaging while avoiding the impression that an unresolved report has already become established fact. FROM THE ARCHIVES: West Virginia's legendary Mothman inspires a major motion picture Channel: WCHS TV Open on YouTube Why This Matters in Fortean Journalism Many famous Fortean stories survived because newspapers recorded them. Yet newspapers also reshaped them. The surviving archive is therefore a mixture of eyewitness testimony, editorial judgement and later repetition. For readers interested in strange phenomena, this distinction is essential. A headline is not simply a summary of evidence; it is part of the evidence's history. Understanding how a cautious description became a memorable label helps explain why some reports evolved into enduring legends while others quietly disappeared. Reading beyond the headline—and comparing it with the witness's own words—is often the simplest way to separate the original mystery from the mythology that accumulated around it. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When Headlines Make Monsters Feel Real. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Made to Stick By Chip Heath, Dan Heath Explains why memorable wording outlives nuance. See on Amazon Book"},{"title":"Virality","url":"/virality/","description":"Research on fast-spreading false news helps explain why novelty, surprise, and uncertainty give strange reports extra momentum.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Virality Why Strange Stories Spread So Fast Virality Virality Why Strange Stories Spread So Fast Research on fast spreading false news helps explain why novelty, surprise, and uncertainty give strange reports extra momentum. Research on fast spreading false news helps explain why novelty, surprise, and uncertainty give strange reports extra momentum. dataset evidence Novelty and surprise as sharing fuel People, not just bots, as spreaders What editors can learn from misinformation research forteana strange news virality Introduction Why do bizarre stories so often outrun careful reporting? Research into false news suggests that the answer lies less in the subject matter itself than in how people respond to novelty, surprise and uncertainty. This has direct relevance to Fortean journalism , where reports of unexplained creatures, mysterious lights or extraordinary coincidences naturally possess many of the qualities that encourage rapid sharing. Modern social platforms have accelerated a pattern that long pre dates the internet: unusual stories attract attention precisely because they are unusual. The key lesson from misinformation research is not that every strange report is false. Rather, it is that unusual claims face a communication environment in which the most surprising version of a story often spreads more effectively than the most carefully qualified one. For anyone studying forteana , understanding why this happens is as important as evaluating the original evidence. Novelty and surprise are powerful sharing incentives The most influential dataset on this question comes from researchers at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, who analysed around 126,000 news stories shared by roughly three million Twitter users between 2006 and 2017. The stories were independently classified as true or false using multiple professional fact checking organisations. The researchers found that false stories spread farther, faster, more deeply and more broadly than true ones across nearly every news category. False stories were also significantly more likely to reach large audiences quickly. [Science] science.org There is worldwide concern over false news and the possibility that it can influence political, economic, and...Read more... Their analysis offered an important explanation. False stories tended to contain greater novelty than truthful ones, while replies showed stronger expressions of surprise and disgust. In other words, people were not merely sharing information; they were sharing emotional experiences and unexpected discoveries. [Science] science.org There is worldwide concern over false news and the possibility that it can influence political, economic, and...Read more... This finding helps explain why Fortean stories often travel so effectively. A report of an unidentified animal, unexplained object or impossible event already possesses the characteristics that research identifies as highly shareable: it violates expectations; it offers conversational value because it is unusual; it leaves uncertainty unresolved, encouraging speculation; it often produces surprise before careful evaluation. None of these qualities determine whether a report is accurate. They simply increase the likelihood that people will pass it on. Don’t blame bots, fake news is spread by humans Sinan Aral TEDxCERN Channel: TEDx Talks Open on YouTube People, not just bots, drive the spread One of the most surprising conclusions from the MIT study was that automated accounts were not the main explanation for the faster spread of false stories. Bots amplified both true and false information at similar rates. Even after accounting for bot activity, human users remained substantially more likely to circulate false or highly questionable stories than verified ones. [MIT Initiative on the Digital Economy] ide.mit.edu MIT Initiative on the Digital EconomyTHE SPREAD OF TRUE AND FALSE NEWS ONLINEby S Vosoughi — Contrary to conventional wisdom, robots acce... This matters for strange news because it shifts attention from technological manipulation to ordinary human behaviour. People frequently share remarkable claims because doing so signals curiosity, humour, concern or excitement. An extraordinary report can become socially valuable long before anyone investigates whether it is accurate. Older Fortean newspaper stories demonstrate this dynamic well. A local report describing \"something unusual\" may be reproduced nationally because editors recognise its entertainment value. Modern social media simply removes editorial gatekeepers and allows readers themselves to become distributors. Research on misinformation therefore suggests that viral circulation is not evidence of credibility. Instead, popularity may simply reflect a story's ability to generate surprise. Strange stories evolve as they spread False news research also highlights another feature familiar to Fortean historians: stories tend to become simpler and more memorable as they circulate. Early reports often contain hesitation: witnesses describe uncertainty; journalists include conflicting accounts; investigators list multiple explanations. Later retellings frequently compress this ambiguity into a cleaner narrative. Caveats disappear, dramatic details are repeated, and speculative interpretations gradually become remembered as established facts. This process mirrors what researchers describe as information diffusion rather than deliberate fabrication. Many participants may honestly believe they are repeating the original story while unknowingly passing on an altered version. Each retelling can favour the detail that is most memorable rather than the one that is best supported. For Fortean case studies, this means the earliest available reports often deserve the greatest attention. Later popularity may reveal more about cultural transmission than about the original event. How false news can spread Noah Tavlin Channel: TED Ed · Views: 2.6M · Uploaded: August 2015 · Length: 3 minutes 42 seconds Open on YouTube What editors can learn from misinformation research Research into false news offers practical lessons that apply directly to responsible reporting of anomalous claims. First, uncertainty should remain visible. A headline that accurately reflects witness uncertainty is less likely to create a misleading narrative than one that confidently labels an unexplained report as a monster, UFO or miracle. Second, provenance matters. Readers benefit from knowing whether information comes from a first hand witness, a local newspaper, a later compilation or a social media repost. Each additional layer introduces opportunities for distortion. Third, novelty deserves extra scrutiny rather than automatic promotion. Because surprising claims naturally attract attention, journalists have reason to apply greater verification rather than lower standards. UNESCO's journalism guidance similarly argues that reporters should distinguish between verified facts, rumours and speculation, avoid amplifying unsupported claims through sensational presentation, and make verification methods transparent wherever possible. [UNESCO Digital Library] unesdoc.unesco.org Much of the discourse on 'fake news' conflates two notions: misinformation and...Read more... For editors covering Fortean subjects, these principles do not eliminate mystery. Instead, they preserve the distinction between an unusual observation and the stronger claims that later retellings may attach to it. Why the digital era magnifies an old Fortean problem The challenge facing modern Fortean journalism is not entirely new. Newspapers have long rewarded novelty because unusual stories attract readers. What has changed is the speed and scale of transmission. Contemporary audiences increasingly encounter news through social and video platforms rather than directly from news organisations, creating more opportunities for striking but weakly sourced material to circulate without its original context. Concerns about misinformation have risen alongside this shift. [reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk] reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk Overview and key findings of the 2026 Digital News Report6 days ago — Concerns about fake news are also up, by 4pp to 62% on average, with... For forteana, this means that the classic lifecycle of a strange report—from local observation to national curiosity and eventually to legend—can now unfold within hours rather than years. The underlying psychology identified by misinformation research remains remarkably consistent: people are naturally drawn to stories that are surprising, emotionally engaging and difficult to explain. Understanding that tendency helps explain why odd stories travel so far, while also reminding readers that widespread circulation should never be mistaken for strong evidence. Why does misinformation spread faster than truth? The Stream Channel: Al Jazeera English · Views: 3.6K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 25"},{"title":"Warminster","url":"/warminster/","description":"Warminster shows how local sounds, sightings, photographs, meetings, and national coverage can turn ambiguity into a lasting UFO case.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Strange News","level":3,"text":"Warminster How Warminster Became a UFO Flap Warminster Warminster How Warminster Became a UFO Flap Warminster shows how local sounds, sightings, photographs, meetings, and national coverage can turn ambiguity into a lasting UFO case. Warminster shows how local sounds, sightings, photographs, meetings, and national coverage can turn ambiguity into a lasting UFO case. event time window The local reports that started the story How media attention created critical mass Possible explanations and the missing official answer forteana strange news warminster Introduction The Warminster Thing is one of Britain's best known UFO flaps: a period in which reports of unexplained phenomena multiplied, attracted widespread publicity and became a self sustaining public mystery. What makes Warminster historically significant is not simply that people reported strange lights or unidentified objects, but that an evolving mixture of unexplained sounds, visual sightings, photographs, public meetings and intense newspaper coverage transformed a local curiosity into a lasting case within British forteana . It is an unusually clear example of how journalism can shape the life of an anomalous event without necessarily resolving what, if anything, originally happened. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — Arthur Shuttlewood, former feat... Published: August 26, 2025 Unlike many isolated UFO reports , the Warminster case unfolded over months rather than minutes. Each new report was interpreted alongside earlier ones, encouraging witnesses, journalists and visitors to view fresh experiences through the growing story of the \"Thing\". That cumulative process—the creation of a UFO flap—is as important to understanding Warminster as any individual sighting. I Looked for Flying Saucers in Britain's UFO Town Channel: Andrew Made A Film Open on YouTube The local reports that started the story Most histories of the Warminster Thing trace its beginning to the Christmas period of 1964, when residents described hearing extraordinary sounds around the town. Witnesses reported violent scraping, humming, booming and vibrations, often comparing the noises to giant objects striking rooftops or moving overhead. In many cases, people found no obvious source when they looked outside. [Vocal] vocal.media The Warminster \"Thing\" FYI The Warminster \"Thing\" FYI Vocal MediaWarminster Journal journalist Arthur Shuttlewood reported about the series of strange soni... These reports might have faded into local folklore had they not been collected by Arthur Shuttlewood, a journalist at the Warminster Journal . Rather than treating the incidents as isolated oddities, Shuttlewood assembled witness statements, compared similarities and continued reporting new accounts as they emerged. During 1965, reports expanded beyond mysterious sounds to include unusual lights, metallic looking objects, cigar shaped forms and unexplained aerial movements over the countryside surrounding Warminster. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — Arthur Shuttlewood, former feat... Published: August 26, 2025 The label \"the Warminster Thing\" was itself an important journalistic choice. It deliberately avoided identifying the phenomenon as a spacecraft, aircraft or natural event. That ambiguity allowed many different experiences—noises, lights, photographs and personal encounters—to become part of one evolving narrative rather than remaining separate unexplained incidents. [jahernandez.com] jahernandez.com the warminster thing and decades of unexplained phenomena in england However, Arthur Shuttlewood continued his work, publishing several books on the subject...Read more... Warminster UFO Analysis Channel: Nexpo · Views: 281.9K · Uploaded: December 2017 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube How media attention created critical mass Warminster demonstrates how a UFO flap develops through feedback between witnesses and publicity rather than through a single dramatic event. One turning point came when local resident Gordon Faulkner photographed an indistinct object that appeared disc shaped. Shuttlewood passed the image to the Daily Mirror , which published it in September 1965. Although the photograph itself was inconclusive, national publication transformed a local mystery into a story of wider public interest. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — Arthur Shuttlewood, former feat... Published: August 26, 2025 National newspapers , television crews and UFO enthusiasts soon arrived in Warminster. Sky watch gatherings became organised events, with visitors travelling specifically in the hope of witnessing unexplained phenomena. Public meetings attracted large audiences, and Shuttlewood himself went on to publish books devoted to the mystery, ensuring that reports continued to circulate long after the original wave of sightings. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Arthur Shuttlewood Arthur Shuttlewood Former Ministry of Defence UFO investigator Nick Pope has described Warminster as distinctive because it crossed the threshold from isolated reports to a community wide phenomenon. Rather than one witness describing one event, dozens—and eventually hundreds—of people believed they had experienced something unusual. Media attention amplified that accumulation until the story acquired what Pope calls \"critical mass\", becoming a recognised national UFO case rather than simply a collection of local anecdotes. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — Arthur Shuttlewood, former feat... Published: August 26, 2025 From the perspective of Fortean journalism , this sequence is especially revealing: Initial reports remained cautious and centred on unexplained sounds. Continued local reporting encouraged additional witnesses to come forward. National newspapers supplied a memorable visual focus through the published photograph. Television coverage and organised sky watches reinforced Warminster's reputation. Later books and conferences preserved the case as part of British UFO history long after the main wave had ended. [Warminster Journal] warminsterjournal.co.uk Warminster JournalUFO expert gives verdict on Warminster Thing 60 years onAugust 26, 2025 — 26 Aug 2025 — Arthur Shuttlewood, former feat... Published: August 26, 2025 Why Warminster became more than a collection of sightings Many UFO reports disappear because they lack continuity. Warminster followed a different trajectory because several factors reinforced one another. First, the reports were unusually varied. Witnesses described sounds, vibrations, lights, apparent craft, effects on animals and occasional claims of vehicle interference. Although these experiences were not identical, they were increasingly interpreted as parts of a single mystery. [jahernandez.com] jahernandez.com the warminster thing and decades of unexplained phenomena in england However, Arthur Shuttlewood continued his work, publishing several books on the subject...Read more... Second, the geography helped maintain interest. Locations such as Cley Hill became associated with repeated observations, encouraging both local residents and visitors to watch the skies from recognised vantage points. Over time, these places acquired their own reputation within British UFO culture. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cley Hill Cley Hill Third, Shuttlewood acted as more than a reporter. By compiling testimony, corresponding with witnesses, publishing books and encouraging investigation, he became an active participant in preserving the case. His changing views—from initial scepticism to personal belief after his own reported experience —also shaped how later readers understood the story. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Arthur Shuttlewood Arthur Shuttlewood These developments illustrate a recurring pattern in Fortean history: once a locality becomes identified with unexplained events, subsequent reports are more likely to be interpreted within that established narrative. 1973: UFO SPOTTING in WARMINSTER, WILTSHIRE Nationwide Weird and Wonderful BBC Archive Channel: BBC Archive · Views: 22.8K · Uploaded: August 2023 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Possible explanations and the missing official answer Despite its enduring reputation, no single explanation has achieved broad acceptance. Some researchers have suggested that at least part of the phenomenon reflected ordinary aerial activity. Warminster lies close to Salisbury Plain, an area with extensive military training, aircraft movements and artillery exercises. Under particular weather conditions, distant sounds can travel unexpectedly, potentially"},{"title":"Taxonomies","url":"/taxonomies/","description":"Lists of anomalies help readers navigate the field, but rigid categories can hide why the cases are interesting.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Taxonomies Can Strange Phenomena Be Neatly Sorted? Taxonomies Taxonomies Can Strange Phenomena Be Neatly Sorted? Lists of anomalies help readers navigate the field, but rigid categories can hide why the cases are interesting. Lists of anomalies help readers navigate the field, but rigid categories can hide why the cases are interesting. mechanism Why categories help Where categories break down How mixed cases should be handled forteana taxonomies Introduction Fortean taxonomies are useful because the field is too wide to navigate as a single heap of oddities. A reader needs some way to distinguish strange falls, anomalous lights, cryptid reports, hauntings, miracle claims, psychical experiences, unusual deaths and modern unidentified anomalous phenomena. Yet the moment those categories become too tidy, they can erase the very thing that makes a Fortean case worth noticing: its refusal to behave like a normal specimen in a normal drawer. The point of a good Fortean taxonomy is therefore not to “solve” every case by naming its shelf. It is to govern uncertainty without killing it. Charles Fort ’s own starting point was not a neat paranormal map but a protest against excluded data: in The Book of the Damned , he described his “damned” facts as data that science had excluded, and his examples crossed boundaries between odd weather, strange falls, objects in the sky, apparitions, disappearances and physical curiosities. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Open source on gutenberg.org. A Fortean taxonomy works best when it helps readers compare cases, track patterns and test explanations, while still preserving ambiguity, overlap and the possibility that a case matters precisely because it belongs in more than one box. An Irreverent take on the History of Fortean Times magazine with Ian Simmons Channel: The Megalithic Portal Open on YouTube Why Categories Help Classification is not the enemy of Forteana . Without categories, the field becomes a blur: a raining fish report, a phantom big cat sighting and a radar visual UAP report all become merely “weird”. That may be entertaining, but it is poor investigation. Categories let readers ask sharper questions: Was there a physical trace? Was the report first hand or retold? Was the phenomenon seen by multiple witnesses? Did it resemble a known folklore pattern, a misidentified natural event, an instrument error, a hoax, or a genuinely unresolved observation ? This is why later Fortean and anomalistics traditions keep producing organising schemes. The Society for Psychical Research’s Psi Encyclopedia describes Forteana as ranging across strange falls, cryptids , UFOs, miracles and balls of light, while anomalistics is often framed as the systematic study of anomaly claims that do not fit accepted scientific pictures of reality. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana Psi Encyclopedia Forteana The point is not that all those things are equally credible. It is that a reader or researcher needs a way to separate types of claim before judging them. A useful taxonomy also protects weak cases from being over promoted. If a reported “monster” is really an out of place animal panic, that belongs in a different analytical stream from a zoological claim about an undiscovered breeding population. Folklore scholarship on mystery animal appearances has shown how reports of large cats or “beasts” can become regional legends when they violate familiar boundaries between wild and domestic, local and exotic, ordinary and threatening. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Appearances of Beasts and Mystery Cats in France Research Gate Appearances of Beasts and Mystery Cats in France A label such as “phantom big cat” does not prove the animal exists, but it does gather comparable reports and invite better questions about ecology, media spread, witness description and local fear. In UFO research, J. Allen Hynek’s close encounter scale shows the practical benefit of classification. Hynek separated distant sightings into forms such as nocturnal lights, daylight discs and radar visual reports, then distinguished closer cases according to whether they involved simple observation, physical effects, or reported entities. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The scheme became famous partly through popular culture, but its investigative value lies in something quieter: it tells researchers which claims require which kinds of evidence. A light in the sky, a landing trace and an entity encounter should not be evaluated as though they were the same evidential problem. Categories also make archives usable. Fortean Times , founded as The News in 1973 and renamed in 1976, developed regular streams for ghosts, cryptozoology, UFOs, odd deaths, archaeological curiosities, reader experiences and sceptical myth checking. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times That editorial architecture matters because Forteana is cumulative. Old clippings, local reports and marginal observations become more useful when they can be compared across time rather than rediscovered as isolated curiosities. Where Categories Break Down The trouble begins when a category is treated as an explanation. “Ghost”, “UFO”, “cryptid” or “miracle” often names a puzzle, not a cause. A ghost report may be a bereavement experience, a local legend, a hoax, a misperception, an architectural effect, a sleep related event, a religious interpretation, or a story that has changed in retelling. Academic discussion often prefers narrower terms such as “apparitional experience” because “ghost” already implies a theory about survival after death, while the experience itself may admit several competing explanations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Apparitional experience Apparitional experience The same problem appears in aerial cases. “UFO” once sounded like a descriptive label, but it acquired such strong extraterrestrial associations that official and scientific discussions increasingly use “UAP” to avoid importing popular assumptions. NASA’s 2023 UAP independent study stressed that many cases are difficult to analyse because of poor sensor calibration, missing metadata, lack of multiple measurements and inadequate baseline data. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science Independent Study Team Report Science Independent Study Team Report In other words, the label “unidentified” is often a statement about the available information, not about the nature of the object. Rigid categories also flatten mixed cases. Fort’s own catalogues regularly moved between physical oddities, sky phenomena, animal falls, psychic seeming events and absurd coincidences. Project Gutenberg’s summary of The Book of the Damned lists UFO like sightings, fish and frog falls, poltergeist activity, unexplained disappearances and evidence for giants and fairies under the same broad project of excluded anomalies. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Open source on gutenberg.org. That unruliness is not a defect to be edited away. It is part of Fort’s method: he wanted to show that official knowledge often sorted inconvenient reports out of sight before they could trouble the system. The category can even become a social identity. Cryptozoology, for example, can be approached as a zoological claim, a folklore pattern, a media phenomenon, a local identity marker, a tourist economy, or a pseudoscientific subculture. Mainstream reference and scholarly sources distinguish cryptozoology from academic zoology, while still noting that the term “cryptid” has become central to the subculture’s way of rebranding older monster traditions in more scientific sounding language. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. A rigid “animal/not animal” sorting misses the cultural work these creatures do in communities. A final failure mode is premature debunking by taxonomy. Once a case is filed as “folklore”, some readers assume it has no empirical content. Once filed as “UFO”, others assume extraterrestrial technology. Once filed as “mass hysteria”, witness experience may be dismissed too quickly. Fortean governance needs a category system that slows down these leaps rather than accelerates them. Charles Fort and the Forteans with Joshua Buhs Channel: Hermitix Podcast Open on YouTube How Mixed Cases Should Be Handled Mixed Fortean cases should be handled with layered classification. Instead of asking “What is it?” first, a better taxonomy asks several smaller questions: what was reported, what evidence exists, how the story travelled, what explanations have been proposed, and what remains unresolved. This keeps the case investigable without pretending its strangeness has vanished. A practical Fortean record can classify a case along four axes: Phenomenon type: light, sound, apparition, animal, object, bodily effect, fall from the sky, coincidence, disappearance, trace, message, or environmental change. Evidence type: first hand testimony, multiple witnesses,"},{"title":"Archive Streams","url":"/archive-streams/","description":"Regular columns and archives turn scattered odd reports into comparable streams without making them fully ordinary.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"Archive Streams How Clippings Become Patterns of Weirdness Archive Streams Archive Streams How Clippings Become Patterns of Weirdness Regular columns and archives turn scattered odd reports into comparable streams without making them fully ordinary. Regular columns and archives turn scattered odd reports into comparable streams without making them fully ordinary. entity Why recurring categories make archives usable What editorial streams reveal and conceal How comparison can create new questions forteana taxonomies archive streams Introduction The value of the Fortean Times archive lies not simply in preserving strange stories but in making them comparable. Forteana has always depended on collecting scattered reports from newspapers , letters, books and personal testimony. By organising those reports into recurring editorial streams rather than treating every anomaly as an isolated curiosity, Fortean Times created a practical way to recognise recurring motifs while preserving uncertainty. Its archive functions less like a catalogue of solved mysteries than a machine for generating comparisons: every new report is read alongside dozens of older ones, inviting readers to ask whether they are seeing repetition, coincidence, folklore, media influence or something genuinely unexplained. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times Why recurring categories make archives usable Charles Fort himself assembled thousands of press cuttings because he believed isolated anomalies gained meaning when viewed together rather than separately. Fortean Times extended that principle into a long running editorial system. Instead of publishing unrelated oddities in random order, it developed recurring sections devoted to ghosts , UFOs, cryptozoology, archaeology, strange deaths, reader experiences, myths and historical curiosities. The result is not a rigid taxonomy but an archive where similar claims accumulate over decades. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com Internet Sacred Text Archivethe complete works of charles fortWorks of Charles Fort and texts on unexplained phenomena, anomalies, and pa... This continuity changes how readers encounter the material. A report of an unexplained light is no longer judged only against ordinary astronomy or aviation. It can also be compared with previous waves of similar reports, differences in witness testimony, geographical clusters and changing cultural interpretations. Likewise, an unusual animal sighting becomes part of a much larger stream of cryptozoological claims rather than remaining an isolated newspaper curiosity. The archive therefore encourages comparative reading rather than simple belief or disbelief. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times The magazine's long publication history reinforces this effect. Beginning as The News in 1973 before becoming Fortean Times in 1976, it has accumulated decades of recurring coverage, allowing readers to compare reports across generations instead of within a single news cycle. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times Charles Fort and the Forteans with Joshua Buhs Channel: Hermitix Podcast Open on YouTube What editorial streams reveal and conceal Regular columns perform an important editorial function. They reveal patterns by ensuring that similar material is consistently collected, but they also influence which patterns become visible. Several long running features illustrate this process: Strange Days gathers unusual news stories from newspapers around the world, creating a continuous record of contemporary anomalies rather than isolated headlines . Ghostwatch , Alien Zoo and UFO Files maintain specialised streams that allow readers to compare reports within particular traditions. Strange Deaths assembles unusual fatalities that would otherwise disappear into local reporting. Mythconceptions deliberately tests popular claims against available evidence, preventing the archive from becoming merely a repository of folklore . Fortean Bureau of Investigation revisits older cases, showing how interpretations change as new evidence or better historical research emerges. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times Because these streams continue month after month, they produce a form of editorial memory. A single report rarely appears decisive, but repeated reporting allows readers to notice recurring witness descriptions, repeated investigative mistakes, persistent rumours and recurring hoaxes. At the same time, editorial organisation inevitably shapes perception. A mystery assigned to a UFO column may be read differently from the same report discussed as folklore, psychology or atmospheric science. The archive therefore does not merely record patterns; it participates in creating the categories through which later readers recognise those patterns. This is one reason Fortean Times has consistently described its editorial stance as one of \"benevolent scepticism\" rather than advocacy for any single paranormal interpretation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times How comparison creates new questions The archive's greatest strength is that comparison often generates questions that individual cases cannot. When readers encounter multiple reports across many years, they can ask: Do witness descriptions remain stable over time, or do they change with popular culture? Are reports geographically clustered or widely dispersed? Which recurring features survive independent reporting? Which details appear only after heavy media attention? How often do apparently mysterious reports later receive conventional explanations? These are historical and comparative questions rather than questions of immediate belief. The archive encourages investigation by highlighting similarities and differences instead of demanding a single explanation. An equally important consequence is the preservation of anomalies that resist neat classification. Some reports migrate between categories over time. A supposed monster may later become an example of folklore transmission. An alleged haunting may develop into a case study in memory or suggestion. Conversely, apparently mundane reports may become historically significant because similar accounts continue to appear decades later. The archive allows these shifts because older material remains available for reinterpretation rather than being discarded once a news cycle ends. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times Lo! (1/2) 💖 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube The Book of the Damned by Charles Fort Full Audiobook of Forbidden Knowledge & Anomalies Channel: Fractured Paradigms Open on YouTube The archive as a living comparative record Unlike a conventional encyclopaedia, the Fortean Times archive is not organised around fixed conclusions. New material continually alters the context in which older reports are understood. Editorial collections, back issues and digital archives make it possible to trace how recurring themes evolve over time, revealing changes in reporting practices, investigative standards and public fascination with particular forms of anomaly. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Fortean Times Fortean Times This is why the archive functions as pattern making machinery rather than simply storage. Its recurring editorial streams transform disconnected clippings into comparable sequences without stripping away ambiguity. Readers are encouraged to recognise families of phenomena, recurring narrative structures and investigative problems, while accepting that many individual cases remain unresolved. In that balance between organisation and uncertainty lies one of the defining strengths of Fortean inquiry. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Clippings Become Patterns of Weirdness. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Book of the Damned By Charles Fort Built from collected reports that reveal recurring anomalous patterns. See on Amazon Book The mammoth encyclopedia of the unsolved By Colin Wilson First published 2000. Subjects: Curiosities and wonders. See on Amazon Book Mysteries By Colin Wilson First published 1978. Subjects: Occultism, Parapsychology, Supernatural, Curiosities and wonders. See on Amazon Book \"Fortean Times\" By Paul Sieveking First published 1992. Subjects: Death, Anecdotes, Humor, Curiosities and wonders. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The Book of the Damned The mammoth encyclopedia of the unsolved Mysteries As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Example eBay listing Introverted Meteorology Framed Wall Art Poster Canvas Print Picture Search eBay.co.uk : meteorology poster Browse similar on eBay.co.uk Example eBay listing"},{"title":"Hynek Scale","url":"/hynek-scale-d78280/","description":"Hynek's encounter scale shows why a distant light, a radar-visual report, and a landing trace demand different evidence tests.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"Hynek Scale Why Not All UFO Reports Ask the Same Question Hynek Scale Hynek Scale Why Not All UFO Reports Ask the Same Question Hynek's encounter scale shows why a distant light, a radar visual report, and a landing trace demand different evidence tests. Hynek's encounter scale shows why a distant light, a radar visual report, and a landing trace demand different evidence tests. dataset evidence Distant sightings versus close encounters What each class asks investigators to verify Where the scale helps and where it strains forteana taxonomies hynek scale Introduction J. Allen Hynek's close encounter scale became influential not because it ranked UFO reports from ordinary to extraordinary, but because it separated different kinds of claims that require different kinds of evidence. Rather than treating every sighting as a single mystery, Hynek divided reports by distance, observational conditions and the presence of physical effects or entities. That shift changed the investigative question. A distant light in the night sky, a radar confirmed observation and a reported landing with ground traces are not simply stronger or weaker versions of the same event—they are different evidential problems that call for different methods of verification. Hynek introduced this framework in The UFO Experience (1972), drawing on years of reviewing cases during and after his work with the US Air Force's UFO investigations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter Within Forteana , this approach illustrates how classification can preserve the strangeness of unusual reports while avoiding the mistake of judging every case by the same standards. Distant sightings versus close encounters Hynek's system first separates observations made at a distance from those involving close proximity. The distinction is practical rather than dramatic. His three categories for distant observations were: Nocturnal Lights (NL): unexplained lights seen at night. Daylight Discs (DD): structured objects observed during daylight, regardless of whether they were literally disc shaped. Radar Visual (RV): reports in which a visual observation was accompanied by radar detection. Each category presents different opportunities—and limitations—for investigation. A nocturnal light may leave little beyond witness testimony and astronomical conditions. A daylight object may allow better estimates of shape, motion and duration. A radar visual case introduces instrument data, although radar evidence itself requires careful scrutiny because atmospheric effects, equipment limitations and interpretation errors can all produce false returns. Hynek did not regard radar confirmation as automatic proof; instead, it represented an additional line of evidence that could be compared against eyewitness accounts. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter Only after separating these distant observations did Hynek introduce the \"close encounter\" categories, generally applying to observations within roughly 500 feet (150 metres), where ordinary misidentification should, in principle, become less likely. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The UFO Experience: Evidence Behind Close… by J. Allen Hynek · Audiobook preview Channel: Google Play Books · Views: 1.5K · Uploaded: January 2024 · Length: 1 hour 8 minutes Open on YouTube What each class asks investigators to verify The real value of the close encounter scale lies in the questions each category encourages investigators to ask. Close Encounter of the First Kind (CE1) concerns a nearby observation with no claimed physical interaction. The primary task is to establish what was actually seen. Investigators compare witness descriptions, viewing conditions, duration, estimated distance, direction of travel and possible conventional explanations. Even at close range, perception can be unreliable, so consistency between independent witnesses becomes important. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter Close Encounter of the Second Kind (CE2) introduces alleged physical effects. These may include ground impressions, scorched vegetation, electromagnetic interference, effects on vehicles or other measurable traces. Here the investigation shifts from eyewitness psychology towards physical evidence. Questions include: Can the trace be documented and dated? Is there a conventional environmental explanation? Was the evidence collected promptly enough to preserve its value? Do laboratory findings match the witness account? Because physical traces can be independently examined, CE2 reports potentially allow evidence that survives beyond human memory, although many famous cases have ultimately proved ambiguous or poorly documented. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter Close Encounter of the Third Kind (CE3) involves reports of apparent occupants or entities associated with the object. This does not simply add another observation; it introduces an entirely different evidential challenge. Investigators must now evaluate witness perception of living figures, behavioural descriptions, communication claims and possible psychological influences alongside the original sighting. Such reports often become difficult to corroborate because they depend heavily on testimony rather than durable physical evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The important point is that Hynek's categories do not imply increasing certainty. They identify increasing complexity in the evidence that must be evaluated. Why the scale improved UFO investigation Hynek's classification helped move UFO investigation away from treating all reports as interchangeable anecdotes. For example, if a researcher receives: a brief report of an unusual light, a radar track with multiple observers, and a case involving alleged landing marks, it makes little sense to evaluate all three using identical criteria. The first depends primarily on observational reliability. The second requires technical analysis of radar performance alongside eyewitness accounts. The third calls for site examination, photography, sampling and documentation before environmental changes erase possible evidence. This distinction also improves statistical work. Researchers can compare similar reports rather than mixing fundamentally different phenomena into a single database. Later investigators, including Allan Hendry at the Center for UFO Studies, used Hynek's categories while examining how many reported UFOs could ultimately be identified as aircraft, astronomical objects or other conventional phenomena. Classification made those comparisons more meaningful because similar cases were grouped together before analysis. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Identification studies of UFOs Identification studies of UFOs In this sense, Hynek's system functions less like a theory of UFOs than like a filing system for evidence. Ancient Aliens: Project Blue Book Proves UFO Phenomenon (Season 16) History Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube Where the scale helps and where it strains The classification remains widely recognised because it is intuitive and easy to apply, but researchers have long noted its limitations. One difficulty is that reports often fit more than one category. An object might first appear as a distant daylight sighting before approaching close enough to qualify as a close encounter. A radar visual event could also involve alleged physical traces. The boundaries are therefore not always clean. Another criticism is that the distant categories classify the observer's experience rather than the reported behaviour of the phenomenon. Jacques Vallée later argued that this makes computer based comparison difficult because descriptions such as \"disc\", \"light\" or \"triangle\" depend partly on witness perception. He proposed behavioural classifications that focus on what the reported object did rather than how it appeared. Even so, Vallée retained Hynek's close encounter categories because they remained useful for distinguishing reports involving physical traces or entities from simple observations. [ufo com.net] ufo com.net nd radar visual cases, Hynek's close encounter designations: AN1 events are anomalies like amorphous... The familiar \"fourth\" and \"fifth\" close encounters were also not part of Hynek's original 1972 scheme. Later writers introduced additional categories, particularly for abduction narratives and other claimed interactions, but these extensions are not universally accepted and reflect different theoretical assumptions from Hynek's original evidence oriented framework. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter Why the scale still matters in Forteana Within Fortean studies, Hynek's scale demonstrates that classification need not diminish mystery. Instead, it separates questions before attempting answers. A report of unexplained lights asks whether observers accurately described an aerial event. A radar visual case asks whether"},{"title":"Label Traps","url":"/label-traps/","description":"Labels like ghost, UFO, cryptid, and miracle can hide uncertainty when readers mistake a filing term for a cause.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"Label Traps When the Label Starts Solving the Mystery Label Traps Label Traps When the Label Starts Solving the Mystery Labels like ghost, UFO, cryptid, and miracle can hide uncertainty when readers mistake a filing term for a cause. Labels like ghost, UFO, cryptid, and miracle can hide uncertainty when readers mistake a filing term for a cause. mechanism Why category names feel explanatory Ghosts, UFOs, cryptids, and miracles as examples How to preserve competing explanations forteana taxonomies label traps Introduction In Fortean research, labels such as ghost , UFO , cryptid and miracle are meant to organise reports, not explain them. The problem begins when a filing category quietly becomes a causal claim. A witness may report an unexplained light, a strange animal or an unusual experience , but once the case is labelled, readers often start treating the label as if it has already answered what happened. The mystery shifts from \"What was observed?\" to \"What kind of ghost was it?\" or \"Which cryptid was seen?\", even though the underlying event may still be poorly understood. This is one of the most persistent traps in Fortean thinking. Good classification helps compare similar reports and identify patterns, but bad classification creates the illusion that uncertainty has disappeared. Charles Fort 's own approach was far more cautious: he collected anomalies precisely because he believed premature explanations often concealed rather than resolved the interesting questions. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort Why Category Names Feel Explanatory Human beings naturally use categories to simplify unfamiliar experiences. Psychologists have long recognised that naming something reduces uncertainty, even when the name describes only appearance rather than cause. Within Forteana , this tendency is especially powerful because many reports begin with incomplete observations made under unusual conditions. A witness may see: an unidentified light moving across the sky; a large animal glimpsed briefly in poor visibility; an apparently impossible coincidence; an unexplained sound or apparition. None of these observations inherently identifies a cause. Yet the moment someone writes \"UFO sighting\", \" Bigfoot encounter\" or \"haunting\", the report acquires a narrative framework. Readers begin filling in assumptions that were never part of the original evidence. This shift is subtle because the vocabulary itself appears neutral. Strictly speaking, \"unidentified flying object\" merely describes an object that has not yet been identified. In public culture, however, \"UFO\" rapidly accumulated associations with extraterrestrial spacecraft. Even researchers such as J. Allen Hynek repeatedly stressed that the classification of reports should not be mistaken for an explanation of them. His reporting categories were intended to distinguish kinds of observations, not to establish what produced them. [Inlibra+2Academia] inlibra.com Researching the Paranormalof categories for labeling reports and UFO incidents. As a result of his research and involvement in UFO... The same mechanism affects nearly every Fortean category. The Illusion of Explanatory Depth: You Don’t Understand It Like You Think Channel: Bull and Thread Open on YouTube When the Label Quietly Solves the Mystery The danger is not simply inaccurate language. It is that explanation starts creeping into the investigation before evidence justifies it. Instead of asking: What exactly was reported? Which details are independently confirmed? What competing explanations remain possible? discussion often jumps immediately to questions that already assume the label is correct: Which ghost was responsible? Which species of cryptid was involved? Which alien civilisation visited? Which miracle occurred? At that point, the category has stopped functioning as an archive label and started functioning as a hidden conclusion. This process narrows inquiry in two ways. First, contradictory evidence is more easily ignored because it no longer fits the accepted story. Second, alternative explanations receive less attention because the report has effectively been \"solved\" linguistically before being solved evidentially. For Fortean investigation, this is particularly risky because many cases remain genuinely ambiguous for decades. Ghosts, UFOs, Cryptids and Miracles: Four Different Versions of the Same Trap Although these labels refer to very different traditions, they often produce the same reasoning error. Ghosts A witness reports seeing an unidentified figure in a building. Calling the report a ghost sighting already implies survival after death, even if the original testimony merely described an unexplained visual experience. Other possibilities—including misperception, environmental factors, psychological effects, deliberate deception or an unidentified ordinary person—may receive far less attention once the haunting narrative becomes established. The report itself may never have claimed a ghost was present. UFOs The history of UFO research provides perhaps the clearest example. Originally, \"unidentified\" simply acknowledged that investigators lacked enough information to identify an aerial object. Over time, public culture increasingly treated \"UFO\" as shorthand for alien spacecraft. Many investigators have criticised this shift because it transformed an observational category into a preferred explanation despite many reports eventually receiving conventional identifications and others remaining unresolved rather than extraterrestrial. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana UFO question and believed that UFOs were amenable to conventional explanations. 24Dick (1996). Dick reports that, despite widespread apathy... You Don't Know Anything The Illusion of Explanatory Depth FutureIQ Channel: Future IQ Open on YouTube Cryptids Reports of unusual animals illustrate another version of the problem. Someone may report an unfamiliar creature, an unexpectedly large cat or an animal seen only briefly. Filing the case under cryptid can be useful for comparison with similar reports. It becomes misleading only if the classification is interpreted as evidence that an undiscovered species exists. The report belongs in the archive because identification remains uncertain—not because the mystery has already been resolved in favour of an unknown animal. Miracles Religious traditions often classify extraordinary recoveries, visions or unusual events as miracles . For historians and Fortean researchers alike, however, \"miracle\" identifies a theological interpretation rather than an independently demonstrated mechanism. The observed event may be genuine while its ultimate cause remains disputed. Separating the report from the interpretation allows different explanatory frameworks to remain visible instead of collapsing into one conclusion. Preserving Competing Explanations One of the strengths of careful anomalistics is that it deliberately separates observation from interpretation. Researchers associated with anomalistics have argued that reports should first be described accurately before stronger explanatory claims are introduced. The discipline's emphasis is on evaluating extraordinary claims without assuming either supernatural or conventional explanations in advance. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. A useful Fortean record therefore keeps several layers distinct: Observation: what witnesses actually reported. Evidence: photographs, traces, documents or independent testimony. Classification: which collection of similar reports it resembles. Interpretation: proposed explanations. Conclusion: what, if anything, the available evidence justifies. Keeping these layers separate allows competing hypotheses to coexist until evidence favours one over the others. For example, an unexplained aerial report might simultaneously be classified as: an unidentified aerial observation; a multiple witness event; a radar supported case; an unresolved investigation. None of those labels requires an extraterrestrial explanation. Likewise, an apparition report can remain a documented experience without committing the investigator to either survival after death or psychological misperception. Logical Fallacies Channel: ProfPash Open on YouTube A Better Habit for Fortean Thinking Charles Fort repeatedly resisted final explanations because he believed certainty often arrived faster than understanding. His collections of anomalous reports deliberately crossed conventional boundaries rather than forcing every case into a settled worldview. The enduring value of that approach is methodological rather than supernatural: preserve the anomaly until the evidence genuinely narrows the possibilities. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort Seen this way, taxonomies are tools of organisation rather than verdicts. They help researchers compare reports, identify recurring patterns and"},{"title":"Layered Cases","url":"/layered-cases/","description":"A layered record can separate what was reported, what evidence exists, how the story spread, and what remains unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"Layered Cases How to File a Case Without Flattening It Layered Cases Layered Cases How to File a Case Without Flattening It A layered record can separate what was reported, what evidence exists, how the story spread, and what remains unresolved. A layered record can separate what was reported, what evidence exists, how the story spread, and what remains unresolved. policy intervention The four useful axes of a Fortean record How mixed cases cross category lines A sample record for a difficult anomaly forteana taxonomies layered cases Introduction Many of the most memorable Fortean reports resist a single label. A single case may begin as a witness account, acquire physical evidence, become shaped by folklore , spread through newspapers and social media, and accumulate competing explanations without any one of them settling the matter. Trying to force such a case into one category—\"ghost\", \"UFO\", \"cryptid\" or \"hoax\"—often strips away the very features that make it worth studying. A layered classification offers a practical alternative. Instead of asking what a case is , it records different dimensions separately: what was reported, what evidence exists, how the story developed, and which explanations remain plausible. This approach fits the long standing Fortean tradition of preserving anomalous reports while recognising that uncertainty is itself valuable information. Charles Fort collected observations precisely because they crossed conventional boundaries, while later anomalistics has increasingly treated complex reports as multidisciplinary problems rather than isolated mysteries. [ufology news.com+2avalonlibrary.net] ufology news.com Bilyk A. Anomalous Phenomena methodology and practice of research 2020 Anomalous phenomena17 Jan 2014 — We call it Fortean phenomena after the American Charles Hoy Fort (1874…1932) who wrote several books abo... The four useful axes of a Fortean record A layered record does not replace familiar Fortean categories. Instead, it adds structure without pretending that every anomaly belongs in only one drawer. Axis 1: What was actually reported? The first layer records the primary claim exactly as it was made, before interpretation. Questions include: Who experienced the event? Was it first hand, second hand or reconstructed later? What was directly observed? What happened in chronological order? Which elements entered only through later retellings? Separating observation from interpretation prevents an early assumption from shaping every later discussion. A witness who reports \"a bright object descending silently\" has not necessarily reported a spacecraft. Likewise, hearing unexplained footsteps is not automatically a haunting. This descriptive layer remains deliberately neutral. Axis 2: What evidence survives? The second layer classifies evidence rather than conclusions. Evidence may include: eyewitness testimony; photographs or video; radar or instrumental records; environmental traces; medical documentation; archival records; independent witness agreement; absence of corroboration. Importantly, different evidence types are logged separately rather than merged into a single judgement of \"strong\" or \"weak\". A case may contain excellent witness testimony but no physical traces, or convincing photographs alongside uncertain provenance. Research on anomalous claims repeatedly shows that witness testimony, documentary records and physical traces each require different standards of evaluation rather than being treated as interchangeable forms of proof. [Scribd] scribd.com Scientific Exploration: Journal of PDF Sciencewitness testimony as valid evidence for the Survival of Human Consciousness after... Cosmos Lecture with Prof. Adam Frank June 2024 Channel: Society For UAP Studies Open on YouTube Axis 3: How did the story spread? The social history of a case deserves its own classification because many Fortean reports evolve substantially after the original event. Useful questions include: Did newspapers reshape the narrative? Did local folklore absorb the event? Were new witnesses inspired after publicity? Did later books merge separate incidents? Have online discussions introduced details absent from the earliest reports? This layer records cultural transmission rather than treating every version of the story as equally primary. In many classic Fortean cases, the media history becomes almost as significant as the reported phenomenon itself. A local mystery may gradually accumulate dramatic embellishments, artistic reconstructions, conspiracy claims or paranormal interpretations that were absent from the earliest documentation. [dn720405.ca.archive.org] dn720405.ca.archive.org The Fringes of Reason: Whole Earth Catalog An Explanation? Chroniclers of the Unexplained by Ted Schultz. Access to Forteana. Access to Freaky Folklore... media. Whether or not these peculiar not... Axis 4: Which explanations remain open? The final layer records competing explanations without requiring an immediate verdict. Possible explanatory classes might include: known natural phenomena; human activity; psychological or perceptual factors; folklore amplification; deliberate fabrication; genuinely unresolved anomaly. Crucially, several explanations can remain active simultaneously. For example, a report might have: genuine witness testimony; [archive.org] archive.org Zetetic.Scholar No 12 13 1987 djvu.txt testimony, especially as evidence in criminal cases. Of special relevance to those concerned with witness reports on anomalies. Recommend... inaccurate interpretation; later folklore embellishment; unresolved physical evidence. Those statements are compatible rather than mutually exclusive. How mixed cases cross category lines Many famous Fortean reports become difficult precisely because several traditions intersect. A rural light may begin as an unidentified aerial observation but later become attached to local ghost stories. A supposed cryptid encounter may produce footprints that attract zoological investigation while simultaneously entering regional folklore. Reports of miraculous healing may combine medical records, religious interpretation, psychological experience and media attention. Flattening these into a single category loses important information. Instead, a layered approach allows one case to belong simultaneously to several analytical streams: LayerPossible classificationPrimary reportUnidentified light observed by two witnessesPhysical evidenceGround traces reported but not independently verifiedCultural contextExisting local ghost traditionsInformation historyNational media coverage expanded witness claimsCurrent statusCompeting natural, psychological and unexplained interpretations remain Nothing in this structure forces an investigator either to endorse or dismiss the anomaly. Ancient Aliens: Project Blue Book Proves UFO Phenomenon (Season 16) History Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube Why layered records reduce false certainty Traditional filing systems encourage premature closure. If a report is filed solely as a UFO case, later folklore elements may be ignored. If it is archived solely as folklore, physical evidence may disappear from consideration. If it becomes labelled a hoax, genuine observations that preceded the fabrication may never be revisited. Layered classification instead distinguishes between different kinds of certainty. For example: The event occurred. Certain witnesses sincerely reported it. The physical evidence is disputed. Later retellings added unsupported details. No single explanation accounts for every component. Those statements can all be simultaneously accurate. This mirrors broader anomalistics, where investigators often separate observational reliability from explanatory confidence rather than assuming that uncertainty about one dimension invalidates all the others. [ufology news.com] ufology news.com Bilyk A. Anomalous Phenomena methodology and practice of research 2020 Anomalous phenomena17 Jan 2014 — We call it Fortean phenomena after the American Charles Hoy Fort (1874…1932) who wrote several books abo... A sample record for a difficult anomaly Consider an imaginary case involving strange lights above woodland, unusual animal behaviour and subsequent rumours of missing time. A layered record might appear as follows. Primary report Two independent witnesses describe a silent, hovering light lasting approximately three minutes before disappearing behind trees. Evidence profile Independent witness interviews conducted within 48 hours. One blurred photograph with uncertain distance. No confirmed radar data. Animal disturbance reported but undocumented. No verified physical traces recovered. Cultural and transmission history The earliest newspaper article mentions only an unusual light. Six months later, magazine features introduce claims of electromagnetic effects. Years afterwards, online retellings add alleged military involvement that cannot be traced to contemporary sources."},{"title":"Big Cat Labels","url":"/big-cat-labels/","description":"Phantom big cat reports show how a label can hold ecological questions, witness fear, media spread, and local legend together.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"Big Cat Labels Are Phantom Big Cats Animals or Stories? Big Cat Labels Big Cat Labels Are Phantom Big Cats Animals or Stories? Phantom big cat reports show how a label can hold ecological questions, witness fear, media spread, and local legend together. Phantom big cat reports show how a label can hold ecological questions, witness fear, media spread, and local legend together. population context Escapee, misidentification, breeding population, or legend How local fear shapes repeat sightings Why one label can carry several claims forteana taxonomies big cat labels Introduction Phantom big cats occupy one of Forteana 's most intriguing borderlands because the label does not describe a single claim. Instead, it gathers together several different possibilities: escaped exotic pets, misidentified domestic animals, genuine but isolated wild felids, rumours amplified through local communities, and stories that gradually become regional folklore. That mixture explains why reports continue to attract attention despite the absence of convincing evidence for established breeding populations in places such as Britain. Rather than asking simply whether the animals are \"real\", the more useful question is what kind of claim each sighting actually represents and what evidence would distinguish one explanation from another. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Within Fortean classification, phantom big cats are therefore valuable because they demonstrate how a single category can contain ecological questions, witness testimony, media narratives and folklore simultaneously. The mystery lies not only in the reported animals but also in the mechanisms by which uncertainty persists. Why one label carries several different claims Unlike reports of an obviously impossible creature, a phantom big cat begins with something that is biologically plausible. Large felids exist. Some are legally or illegally kept in captivity, some have escaped in documented cases, and some have been recovered after roaming free for short periods. The uncertainty comes from deciding whether a particular report belongs to that ordinary zoological history or to something else. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats The phrase \"phantom big cat\" therefore bundles together several distinct propositions: An escaped or released captive animal. This is well documented in individual cases and requires no unknown species. A misidentified familiar animal. Distance, poor light, vegetation and expectations can all exaggerate size or alter shape. A hidden breeding population. This is a much stronger claim because it requires sustained reproduction, prey availability and recoverable physical evidence. A regional legend. Here the animal functions as a recurring cultural figure regardless of whether any individual sighting has a zoological explanation. Confusion often arises because public discussion moves between these possibilities without recognising that they demand very different standards of evidence. Are the Phantom Big Cat's of Britain Real? Channel: Animal Educate Open on YouTube Escapees are real, but that does not prove a hidden population The strongest zoological foundation beneath phantom big cat reports is the documented escape or release of non native felids. Britain has repeatedly recovered individual exotic cats, including a puma captured alive in Scotland in 1980, a Eurasian lynx captured in London in 2001, and more recent illegally released lynx recovered in the Scottish Highlands. Government records have also documented escapes of captive non native cats over several decades. These cases demonstrate that unusual felids can occasionally appear outside captivity without implying that they have established self sustaining wild populations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats The popular explanation linking many British sightings to the Dangerous Wild Animals Act 1976 reflects this distinction. The law introduced licensing requirements for keeping dangerous species, leading to widespread speculation that owners released animals rather than comply. While this theory remains plausible for some isolated animals, researchers have found little direct evidence that it created enduring breeding populations across Britain. [Wikipedia+2bornfree.org.uk] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats This difference is important. Demonstrating that exotic cats have escaped is not the same as demonstrating that generations of them continue breeding unnoticed. Why so many sightings can have ordinary causes Most phantom big cat investigations ultimately turn on perception rather than zoology. Estimating the size of an unfamiliar animal without nearby reference points is surprisingly difficult. A domestic cat moving through long grass, seen at an unusual angle or from considerable distance, can appear much larger than it really is. Black coats obscure body proportions, while long tails and low, fluid movement encourage comparisons with leopards or pumas. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Other factors reinforce these errors: Brief encounters leave witnesses relying on memory rather than prolonged observation . Fear encourages attention to movement while reducing attention to precise anatomical details. Existing local stories provide ready made interpretations for ambiguous sightings. News coverage makes particular descriptions—such as \"black panther\"—more available in memory, influencing later reports. None of these mechanisms imply dishonesty. Witnesses can sincerely report what they believe they saw while still misjudging size, distance or species. Why experts remain sceptical about breeding populations A persistent population of large predators would leave more than eyewitness accounts. Breeding carnivores produce carcasses, road casualties, DNA, tracks, scat, camera trap images, prey remains with characteristic injuries and repeated observations by trained field workers. Although individual traces have occasionally prompted investigation, wildlife authorities and most zoologists argue that the cumulative evidence remains insufficient to demonstrate an established breeding population of large exotic cats in Britain. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats This does not mean every report is dismissed as fabrication. Instead, investigators generally separate three questions: 1. Did the witness genuinely observe an unusual animal? 2. Can the animal be identified confidently? 3. Does the observation imply a resident population? The first question may often receive a positive answer while the third remains unsupported. Merrily Harpur The 1976 Dangerous Wild Animals Act Channel: The Biome Project Open on YouTube How local fear shapes repeated sightings The folklore dimension becomes visible when reports cluster geographically. Across Britain, named creatures such as the Beast of Exmoor, Beast of Bodmin Moor and Beast of Cumbria have become regional identities rather than isolated incidents. Once a locality acquires a recognised \"beast\", later ambiguous encounters are more likely to be interpreted through that existing narrative. The landscape itself becomes associated with expectation. [Wikipedia+2The Guardian] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats Folklorists studying similar reports in France found that mystery cat narratives often reflected wider social anxieties rather than simply unexplained zoological observations. Rumours spread through newspapers and local conversation, attaching symbolic meanings to predators that represented wilderness, danger or changing relationships between people and the countryside. The stories became culturally meaningful even when no animal was conclusively identified. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net 241722290 Appearances of Beasts and Mystery Cats in France Appearances of Beasts and Mystery Cats in FrancePDF On Jan 1, 1992, Véronique Campion Vincent published Appearances of Beas... This helps explain why reports frequently surge after dramatic media coverage. The folklore does not replace observation; it provides a framework through which later observations are interpreted. Big cat captured on video Channel: Onlooker Open on YouTube Media amplification without deliberate invention Media influence does not necessarily create sightings from nothing. Instead, it changes which experiences become reportable. Someone who briefly glimpses a large dark animal may previously have dismissed the encounter. After repeated newspaper stories about a local \"panther\", the same experience becomes meaningful enough to report. As similar accounts accumulate, they reinforce one another, producing feedback between witnesses, journalists and local identity. Researchers have described this process as capable of generating a self sustaining rumour tradition without requiring organised hoaxes or deliberate deception. [Wikipedia+2ResearchGate] Wikipedia British big cats British big cats"},{"title":"UAP Data Gaps","url":"/uap-data-gaps-1722c2/","description":"Modern UAP work shows that many mysteries persist because sensors, metadata, calibration, and repeat measurements are missing.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Taxonomies","level":3,"text":"UAP Data Gaps When Unidentified Means Not Enough Data UAP Data Gaps UAP Data Gaps When Unidentified Means Not Enough Data Modern UAP work shows that many mysteries persist because sensors, metadata, calibration, and repeat measurements are missing. Modern UAP work shows that many mysteries persist because sensors, metadata, calibration, and repeat measurements are missing. dataset evidence What poor records make impossible to test Sensors, metadata, and baseline measurements Why better data does not pre solve every case forteana taxonomies uap data gaps Introduction Within modern Fortean studies, unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) occupy an unusual position. They are not simply \"mysteries in the sky\"; they are also demonstrations of how easily incomplete evidence can prevent reliable identification. NASA's 2023 Independent Study did not set out to prove or disprove extraordinary explanations. Instead, it asked a more fundamental question: what quality of evidence is actually available, and what would scientists need before any confident conclusion could be reached? The report's central message is remarkably restrained. Many UAP remain unidentified not because they necessarily represent exotic technology, but because the original observations often lack the measurements required for scientific reconstruction. Missing calibration records, incomplete metadata, single sensor observations and absent baseline comparisons leave investigators unable to distinguish unusual physical events from ordinary objects, atmospheric effects or sensor artefacts. That emphasis on evidence rather than speculation makes NASA's work particularly important for a Fortean taxonomy that seeks to preserve genuine uncertainty without exaggerating it. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportTo date, in the peer reviewed scientific literature, there is no conclusive evidence suggesting... What Poor Records Make Impossible to Test NASA repeatedly stresses that \"unidentified\" is not a permanent category. It often reflects an information problem rather than a property of the object itself. Scientific identification normally depends upon reconstructing an observation after the event. Investigators want to know where the sensor was pointed, how it was configured, what environmental conditions existed, whether other instruments recorded the same event and whether known aircraft, satellites, balloons or astronomical objects occupied the same region at the same time. If those pieces are missing, many competing explanations remain equally plausible. This distinction matters because a blurry video alone cannot reliably answer questions about distance, speed or acceleration. An object appearing to make impossible manoeuvres may instead reflect unknown viewing geometry, camera motion, autofocus changes or image processing artefacts. Without sufficient contextual information, even experienced analysts cannot calculate basic physical quantities with confidence. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportTo date, in the peer reviewed scientific literature, there is no conclusive evidence suggesting... For Fortean classification, this means that \"currently unidentified\" should not be confused with \"currently inexplicable\". A case may remain unresolved simply because the available evidence is too incomplete to discriminate between ordinary and extraordinary hypotheses. Replay! NASA's Release of the Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Report Channel: VideoFromSpace · Views: 71.9K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Sensors, Metadata and Baseline Measurements NASA's report identifies four recurring weaknesses that limit almost every difficult UAP investigation: Poor sensor calibration , meaning investigators cannot fully characterise how accurately an instrument recorded the event. Lack of multiple measurements , leaving only one perspective instead of independent confirmation. Missing sensor metadata , including details such as observing mode, timing, location and instrument settings. Lack of baseline data , making it difficult to compare an unusual observation with what the same sensor normally records. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportTo date, in the peer reviewed scientific literature, there is no conclusive evidence suggesting... These sound like technical details, but they determine whether an observation can be analysed scientifically. NASA UFO report finds no evidence UAP have extraterrestrial origins FULL Channel: Global News · Views: 37.7K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why calibration matters Every imaging system introduces distortions. Cameras produce lens artefacts, infrared sensors respond differently under varying temperatures, radar systems generate characteristic returns and software applies processing before images are displayed. If calibration records are unavailable, analysts cannot distinguish genuine features from instrument behaviour. NASA notes that several apparent UAP have ultimately proved to be sensor artefacts once calibration information and metadata were examined carefully. In other words, better knowledge of the instrument changed the interpretation of the observation rather than the observation itself. [Wikisource] en.wikisource.org Page:UAP Independent Study Team Page:UAP Independent Study Team Final Report.pdf/1512 Nov 2023 — Indeed, several apparent UAP have been demonstrated to be se... Why metadata is often more valuable than another photograph Metadata includes information such as: precise timestamps; geographic position; viewing direction; sensor configuration; exposure settings; operating mode; environmental conditions. Without these details, even high resolution imagery can become scientifically limited. Investigators cannot reconstruct geometry, estimate distances or compare observations against aircraft databases, weather information or astronomical catalogues. NASA therefore treats metadata as evidence rather than administrative paperwork. Context frequently determines whether an object appears anomalous at all. [Wikisource] en.wikisource.org Responses to Statement of Task NASA Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Independent...14 Oct 2023 —... Independent Study Team Report (2023) National... UAP i... Why multiple sensors change the problem One optical camera records only an angular position on the sky. It does not automatically reveal range. If infrared imaging, radar, optical cameras and independent observers all detect the same event simultaneously, investigators gain the triangulation needed to estimate altitude, velocity and trajectory. Agreement across different sensor types also reduces the chance that the anomaly originates within a single instrument. NASA therefore recommends systematic collection of multimodal observations instead of relying on isolated videos or eyewitness testimony. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportTo date, in the peer reviewed scientific literature, there is no conclusive evidence suggesting... Why Better Data Does Not Pre solve Every Case Improving data quality does not guarantee that every future UAP will receive an ordinary explanation . Instead, better observations reduce uncertainty. Some cases that currently appear extraordinary may eventually prove to involve atmospheric optics, balloons, drones, birds, astronomical objects or sensor effects. Others may remain unidentified because the available evidence genuinely does not favour one explanation over another. Importantly, NASA does not argue that unexplained cases automatically indicate advanced technology, nor does it argue that all reports have conventional explanations. Its position is methodological: conclusions should follow from evidence rather than precede it. The study explicitly states that it found no evidence supporting an extraterrestrial origin for reported UAP, while also acknowledging that some observations remain unidentified because existing datasets are inadequate. [NASA Science+2NASA] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportTo date, in the peer reviewed scientific literature, there is no conclusive evidence suggesting... This is an important distinction within Fortean studies. An unresolved case is not necessarily evidence for a remarkable phenomenon, but neither is it automatically evidence that witnesses were mistaken. Sometimes the most accurate classification is simply that the available information cannot support a reliable identification. Inside NASA’s UAP Report with Commission Chair David Spergel & Neil deGrasse Tyson Channel: StarTalk · Views: 385.2K · Uploaded: August 2023 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube Governance Rather Than Speculation One of the report's less discussed contributions is its emphasis on scientific governance. NASA recommends creating transparent procedures for collecting,"},{"title":"Ghosts","url":"/ghosts/","description":"Haunting stories often mix place, grief, sleep, fraud, architecture, and genuine uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Ghosts What Makes a Ghost Story Fortean? Ghosts Ghosts What Makes a Ghost Story Fortean? Haunting stories often mix place, grief, sleep, fraud, architecture, and genuine uncertainty. Haunting stories often mix place, grief, sleep, fraud, architecture, and genuine uncertainty. population context The reported experience Ordinary explanations to test How places become haunted forteana ghosts Introduction A ghost story becomes Fortean when it is treated not simply as entertainment or proof, but as a report to be examined: who saw what, under what conditions, what the place already meant, and how the story changed as it was retold. Haunting reports sit in the awkward middle ground between folklore , witness memory , grief, architecture, environmental discomfort, fraud, and events that remain personally powerful even when they are not externally verified. That middle ground matters because ghost reports are common enough to be socially significant. Pew Research found that 18% of American adults said they had seen or been in the presence of a ghost, while 29% said they had felt in touch with someone who had died; a later Pew survey found that 53% of US adults reported some form of dream or other encounter with a dead family member. In Britain, YouGov reported in 2025 that 38% of Britons believed ghosts could exist. These figures do not prove ghosts, but they do show why ghost reports cannot be dismissed as rare eccentricities. They are part of how many people interpret place, loss, fear and memory. [Pew Research Center+2Pew Research Center] pewresearch.org Pew Research Center18% of Americans say they've seen a ghost Pew Research Center18% of Americans say they've seen a ghost The reported experience The first mistake in examining a ghost report is to treat all hauntings as the same kind of claim. A person who wakes unable to move and senses a threatening figure in the room is not reporting the same thing as a family that hears repeated knocks in a wall, a tourist who feels watched in a reputedly haunted vault, or a bereaved person who senses a dead parent nearby. All may be called “ghosts” in ordinary speech, but they differ in timing, witnesses, repeatability and possible explanation. Older psychical researchers recognised this variety. The Society for Psychical Research, founded in 1882, made apparitions and “hallucinations” one of its central early interests, including the large late Victorian Census of Hallucinations. The SPR’s own historical summary treats that census as a landmark attempt to collect unusual experiences systematically rather than as isolated fireside tales. Its archive category on spontaneous apparitions includes crisis apparitions, haunting reports and near death experiences, showing how broad the apparent “ghost” category became once investigators began sorting reports by context. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk Open source on spr.ac.uk. A Fortean reading therefore begins with the shape of the experience : Single apparition: a figure, voice or presence seen once, often in liminal conditions such as darkness, illness, fatigue, grief or waking from sleep. Repeated haunting: sounds, sensations or sightings attached to a building, room, road, ruin or landscape. Crisis or bereavement encounter: a felt contact with someone dying or already dead, often interpreted through family memory rather than local legend. Poltergeist style disturbance: knocks, object movement, electrical oddities or apparent physical effects, often involving households under stress. Reputation led experience: an uneasy feeling or ambiguous perception in a place already labelled haunted. That last category is especially important. A “haunted” place is rarely neutral. Visitors may arrive primed to notice draughts, shadows, temperature changes, odd acoustics and bodily unease. In a major investigation at Hampton Court Palace and the Edinburgh South Bridge Vaults, Richard Wiseman and colleagues found that participants reported more unusual experiences in areas already reputed to be haunted, and the study examined psychological variables alongside physical factors such as magnetic fields. The result did not establish ghosts, but it did show that place reputation and experience can reinforce each other. [Richard Wiseman+2PubMed] richardwiseman.com Richard Wiseman An investigation into alleged 'hauntings Richard Wiseman An investigation into alleged 'hauntings Why witness memory is not the same as witness honesty A sceptical account of ghost reports should not assume that witnesses are lying. Human memory is not a recording device, and sincere testimony can still be reshaped by expectation, later discussion, media coverage, leading questions and the desire to make sense of an ambiguous moment. This is not special pleading against ghost witnesses; it is a basic feature of memory research. The National Academies, reviewing eyewitness identification in criminal cases, warned that memory can be contaminated by many influences, including later interaction with investigators. Elizabeth Loftus’s work on false memory and suggestive questioning has shown that people can develop confident recollections that are distorted or partly constructed after the event. [National Academies+2American Psychological Association] nationalacademies.org Open source on nationalacademies.org. Ghost reports are particularly vulnerable to this because the first interpretation often arrives after the perception. A witness may first hear a thud, glimpse a figure, feel a presence or wake in terror; only later does the event acquire a name: ghost, apparition, warning, visitation, poltergeist. Once that label is attached, memory may become tidier and more story shaped. The shadow becomes “a woman in white”; the sound becomes “footsteps”; the cold patch becomes “the place where she stood”. This does not make the experience worthless. It changes the question. Instead of asking only, “Did a ghost appear?”, a Fortean investigator asks: 1. What was recorded before the witness heard local lore? 2. Were accounts written independently or after group discussion? 3. Did the witness describe perception first and interpretation second? 4. Did later retellings add detail, confidence or narrative structure? 5. Are there physical, medical, architectural or social conditions that match the report? The most valuable ghost testimony is usually early, specific, boring in parts, and resistant to dramatic improvement. The more a report becomes polished into a perfect legend, the harder it is to separate memory from folklore. How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube Ordinary explanations to test The strongest Fortean handling of ghost reports does not begin with debunking. It begins with controls. A claim becomes more interesting, not less, when obvious explanations have been tested carefully and recorded fairly. Sleep, waking and the sensed presence Many bedroom hauntings resemble sleep paralysis : a state in which a person wakes or partly wakes unable to move, often with vivid visual, auditory or tactile experiences and a strong sense that someone is present. Sleep paralysis research describes a hybrid state between dreaming and waking, with paralysis, fear and “presence” experiences that can be interpreted through local supernatural beliefs. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Sleep Paralysis: phenomenology, neurophysiology and treatment arXiv Sleep Paralysis: phenomenology, neurophysiology and treatment This matters because the content of the experience is culturally flexible. One person may describe a ghost at the bed, another a demon, another an intruder, another an alien presence. The bodily event may be similar; the interpretation changes with expectation, fear, religious background, popular media and family stories. A haunting report that begins in bed, at night, with immobility and pressure on the chest should therefore be checked against sleep paralysis before it is treated as evidence of an external entity. Sound, vibration and old buildings Some places feel wrong before anything visibly happens. Old houses, cellars, vaults and ruins contain draughts, settling timber, distant traffic noise, pipe vibration, animal movement, distorted echoes and low frequency sound. These do not explain every ghost report, but they can create unease that later becomes ghostly. Research into alleged hauntings has repeatedly considered infrasound and electromagnetic fields as possible contributors to uncanny sensations. Chris French and colleagues attempted to build a “haunted” room by manipulating complex electromagnetic fields and infrasound; the study found that unusual experiences were reported, but not in a way that cleanly supported the idea that those environmental manipulations alone caused haunt like effects. More recent infrasound work reported mood and stress effects from low frequency exposure, suggesting a plausible route by which a"},{"title":"After loss","url":"/after-loss/","description":"Sensing a dead loved one can be part of grief, family memory and meaning-making rather than a simple haunted-house claim.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"After loss Are Bereavement Ghosts Different From Hauntings? After loss After loss Are Bereavement Ghosts Different From Hauntings? Sensing a dead loved one can be part of grief, family memory and meaning making rather than a simple haunted house claim. Sensing a dead loved one can be part of grief, family memory and meaning making rather than a simple haunted house claim. population context What bereavement encounters usually feel like Why family meaning matters more than place legend How to handle testimony without dismissing grief forteana ghosts after loss Introduction Bereavement ghost experiences differ from the classic haunted house story because they are centred on a relationship rather than a location. Instead of recurring phenomena tied to a building, they typically involve a recently deceased family member, partner or close friend whose presence is sensed during the grieving process. Reports range from vividly seeing the deceased, hearing their voice or smelling a familiar perfume to simply feeling that they are nearby. For many people these experiences are comforting rather than frightening, and they often become part of family memory instead of public ghost lore. Within Fortean studies, bereavement encounters occupy an unusual position. They are among the most frequently reported forms of apparent ghost experience , yet they are also among the least compatible with simple \"haunting\" narratives. Psychological research, anthropology and psychical research all recognise that such experiences are common, sincere and personally meaningful, while disagreeing over whether they represent grief related perception, an aspect of continuing emotional bonds, or evidence for survival after death. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sensory and Quasi Sensory Experiences of the Deceased in...by KS Kamp · 2020 · Cited by 106 — Bereavement among elderly people: grief... What bereavement encounters usually feel like Unlike dramatic ghost stories, bereavement encounters are often quiet and deeply personal. Many people report them in the weeks or months after a death, although some describe experiences years later. Common forms include: Feeling the deceased standing or sitting nearby without clearly seeing them. Brief visual sightings, often in ordinary surroundings rather than supposedly haunted places. Hearing a familiar voice, footsteps or a characteristic phrase. Smelling a distinctive perfume, tobacco, flowers or another scent strongly associated with the deceased. Feeling a touch on the shoulder, a hand held, or the impression that someone has sat on the bed. Vivid dreams in which the deceased appears unusually lifelike and seems to communicate reassurance. Researchers increasingly refer to these as sensory and quasi sensory experiences of the deceased , recognising that many involve a powerful \"felt presence\" without a conventional visual hallucination. People often describe knowing someone is there without being able to explain exactly how they know. [PMC+2Pure York] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sensory and Quasi Sensory Experiences of the Deceased in...by KS Kamp · 2020 · Cited by 106 — Bereavement among elderly people: grief... An important feature is emotional tone. Although distressing experiences do occur, many bereaved individuals report calm, reassurance or a sense that the deceased is \"all right\". Rather than provoking fear, the experience may reduce loneliness or anxiety during intense grief. Studies of widowed spouses dating back to physician Dewi Rees's influential work found that such encounters were often regarded as helpful or emotionally neutral rather than pathological. Later research has broadly confirmed that these experiences are widespread across different bereaved populations. [PMC+2OUP Academic] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The phenomenology and impact of hallucinations concerning...by E Elsaesser · 2021 · Cited by 55 — In this study, we revisited Rees's... The Phenomenology and Impact of Perceived Spontaneous After Death Communications Callum E. Cooper Channel: Parapsychological Association Open on YouTube Why family meaning matters more than place legend Traditional haunting stories are usually anchored to a location: an old manor, battlefield, abandoned hospital or reputedly haunted road. Bereavement encounters follow a different pattern. The setting is often entirely ordinary. People describe sensing a deceased spouse while preparing breakfast, hearing a parent's voice in the family home, or briefly seeing a loved one in a familiar street. The significance comes not from where the event occurred but from who was experienced. Because of this, these reports often remain private. Families may discuss them among themselves without ever presenting them as evidence of a haunted building or supernatural hotspot. A single encounter can become part of shared family history, reinforcing memories of the deceased rather than creating a local legend. Anthropological research also shows that many cultures interpret continuing experiences of the dead as normal aspects of mourning rather than extraordinary paranormal events. Cross cultural comparisons suggest that people may describe remarkably similar experiences while explaining them through very different religious or cultural frameworks. Some regard them as genuine visits, others as ancestors maintaining relationships, while still others see them as expected features of grief. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Cultural research on post bereavement perception or...by P Sabucedo · 2023 · Cited by 41 — Experiencing the continued presence of... This difference is significant for Fortean analysis. A haunted house investigation asks whether multiple witnesses report recurring events at the same place. Bereavement reports instead focus on the continuing relationship between two people, making family context more informative than architectural history. After Death Communication: Life Changing Encounters with the Departed Channel: IANDS Open on YouTube Why psychologists no longer automatically treat these experiences as illness During much of the twentieth century, seeing or sensing someone who had died was frequently described simply as a bereavement hallucination. While that term is still used in clinical literature, many researchers now argue that it can be misleading because it implies pathology where none may exist. Modern bereavement research has found several consistent observations: Such experiences are relatively common among otherwise healthy people. They occur without other symptoms of psychosis in most cases. Many bereaved individuals do not disclose them because they fear ridicule. The experiences frequently provide comfort and help people adapt to loss. They are not, by themselves, considered evidence of mental illness. This shift reflects broader changes in grief theory. Instead of assuming that healthy mourning requires emotionally \"letting go\", many psychologists now discuss continuing bonds —the idea that maintaining an inner relationship with the deceased can be a normal part of adaptation. Bereavement encounters may represent one expression of that continuing relationship rather than a failure to accept death. [Taylor & Francis Online+2PMC] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineThe impact of continuing bonds following bereavementby H Hewson · 2024 · Cited by 127 — Following bereavement, con... At the same time, clinicians distinguish ordinary grief experiences from situations where persistent hallucinations occur alongside severe confusion, dangerous behaviour or other psychiatric symptoms that require medical assessment. Why they remain important in Fortean research Psychical researchers became interested in bereavement experiences long before modern psychology began studying grief systematically. Nineteenth century investigators collected reports of apparitions appearing around the time of death, often calling them crisis apparitions when the experience coincided with an unexpected death or serious danger. From a survival of consciousness perspective, these cases are potentially significant because some reports involve witnesses who claim they learned of a death only afterwards. Researchers have argued that apparently veridical details deserve careful documentation rather than immediate dismissal . Sceptical investigators, however, point to several difficulties: Memories are often recorded long after the event. Families naturally reinterpret ambiguous experiences after learning someone has died. Coincidences become memorable while countless non matching impressions are forgotten. Independent corroboration is frequently absent. As a result, bereavement encounters occupy an enduring middle ground within Fortean literature. They are treated neither as simple folklore nor as decisive proof of survival after death. Instead, they illustrate how extraordinary personal testimony can remain psychologically meaningful while resisting straightforward verification. [Psi"},{"title":"Memory drift","url":"/memory-drift/","description":"Sincere witnesses can remember a ghost report differently as later discussion, questions and local lore reshape the event.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"Memory drift Why Honest Ghost Witnesses Still Change Stories Memory drift Memory drift Why Honest Ghost Witnesses Still Change Stories Sincere witnesses can remember a ghost report differently as later discussion, questions and local lore reshape the event. Sincere witnesses can remember a ghost report differently as later discussion, questions and local lore reshape the event. mechanism Why memory is not a recording device How labels turn perceptions into legends What early testimony is most useful forteana ghosts memory drift Introduction People often assume that if a ghost story changes over time, the original witness must have exaggerated or invented it. Memory research suggests a different possibility. Honest people can remember the same unusual experience differently after repeated retelling, conversations with family and friends, exposure to local legends, or even well intentioned questioning. The emotional reality of the experience may remain genuine while specific details gradually shift. Within Fortean research, this distinction matters. A ghost report is not automatically strengthened because it accumulates vivid detail over time. In many cases, the earliest account—before discussion, media attention or folklore become involved—is the most valuable evidence because it is least affected by later reconstruction. Research in cognitive psychology consistently shows that human memory is reconstructive rather than a literal recording of events, making gradual memory drift an expected feature of sincere testimony rather than proof of deception. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect by DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monit... Why memory is not a recording device Remembering is an active process. Each time a person recalls an event, the brain rebuilds the memory from stored fragments rather than replaying an untouched recording. During this reconstruction, later information can become woven into the recollection without the witness recognising that anything has changed. This principle has been demonstrated repeatedly in eyewitness research. Exposure to misleading information after an event can alter later recall, a phenomenon known as the misinformation effect. The altered memory often feels just as convincing as the original because the witness is no longer consciously aware of where particular details came from. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect by DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monit... For ghost reports, the same mechanisms can operate after an ambiguous experience. A person who initially reports \"seeing a dark shape\" may later remember \"a woman in Victorian clothing\" after hearing neighbours describe the site's famous ghost. The witness is not necessarily fabricating the figure. Instead, original perception, later information and attempts to make sense of the event may have become integrated into a single coherent memory. An influential illustration of reconstructive memory comes from the psychologist Frederic Bartlett. In his classic serial recall experiments, stories changed with each retelling as people unconsciously reshaped unfamiliar details into forms that better matched their expectations and cultural knowledge. Although Bartlett was not studying paranormal experiences, his findings remain highly relevant to how haunting narratives evolve over repeated retellings . [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness testimony Eyewitness testimony How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube How labels turn perceptions into legends Ghost reports rarely exist in isolation. They are usually discussed within families, neighbourhoods or paranormal communities, where names and explanations quickly become attached to otherwise uncertain experiences. This process often follows a recognisable pattern: An unusual experience occurs that is only partly understood. Someone proposes an interpretation, such as \"that was the Grey Lady.\" Later conversations repeat the label. The label becomes intertwined with the witness's own recollection. Future retellings become increasingly consistent with the local legend. Psychologists describe one part of this process as source monitoring . People usually remember the content of information more easily than its origin. Over time, it becomes difficult to distinguish what was directly perceived from what was later suggested by another person, read in a book, or heard during discussion. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect by DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monit... This helps explain why ghost stories from well known haunted locations often become more detailed over the years. Architectural features, historical rumours and previous accounts provide ready made frameworks into which ambiguous memories can gradually fit. The witness may sincerely believe every detail while unknowingly blending perception with later interpretation. Why repeated retelling changes stories Retelling does not merely communicate a memory; it can also reshape it. Every time someone recounts an experience, certain details receive emphasis while others fade. Listeners ask questions. They express surprise at particular elements. The witness reflects on aspects previously overlooked. During later recall, these newly emphasised features become easier to retrieve than details that were never discussed. Research on memory reconsolidation suggests that recalling an event temporarily makes the memory more open to updating before it is stored again. This updating normally helps people keep memories relevant, but it also creates opportunities for subtle distortion, especially when new information is introduced between recollections. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness testimony Eyewitness testimony In ghost narratives, common influences include: repeated interviews; conversations among multiple witnesses; reading local ghost histories; paranormal television programmes; internet discussions; attempts to identify historical figures connected with a location. None of these influences require deliberate dishonesty. They simply provide additional material that memory can absorb. How Memory can be manipulated, with Elizabeth Loftus, PhD Speaking of Psychology Channel: American Psychological Association · Views: 62.4K · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube When several witnesses begin to agree Agreement between witnesses is often treated as evidence that a haunting is genuine. However, investigators distinguish between independent agreement and co developed memory . If witnesses discuss an event extensively before providing separate accounts, they may unintentionally influence one another. Shared conversations can gradually produce increasingly similar stories, even when initial recollections differed. This phenomenon, sometimes called co witness contamination in eyewitness research, has been demonstrated outside paranormal settings and is one reason investigators prefer independent initial statements. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness memory Eyewitness memory For Fortean researchers, convergence after prolonged discussion carries less evidential weight than agreement recorded immediately after the experience. False Memories and the Misinformation Effect Channel: Bite Sized Psychology · Views: 14.3K · Uploaded: July 2025 · Length: 2 minutes 37 seconds Open on YouTube What early testimony is most useful The earliest account is usually the least contaminated by later interpretation. Investigators therefore place particular value on records created before the witness has been exposed to extensive discussion or folklore. Useful early material includes: handwritten notes made immediately after the event; diary entries; timestamped messages; contemporaneous audio recordings; first interviews conducted with neutral questioning; independent statements from multiple witnesses recorded separately. These early records may appear less dramatic than later versions. They often contain uncertainty, incomplete descriptions and admissions such as \"I don't know what I saw.\" Paradoxically, that uncertainty can increase their evidential value because it reflects memory before later reconstruction has imposed a coherent narrative. Research on eyewitness recall similarly finds that prompt interviews reduce opportunities for post event information to reshape memory. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The impact of recall timing on the preservation of eyewitness...by C Chevroulet · 2021 · Cited by 15 — As a witness' memory of the ev... Why memory drift does not"},{"title":"Place reputation","url":"/place-reputation/","description":"A famous haunted place can prime visitors to notice cold spots, shadows and sounds that fit the legend they already know.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"Place reputation Can a Haunted Reputation Create Experiences? Place reputation Place reputation Can a Haunted Reputation Create Experiences? A famous haunted place can prime visitors to notice cold spots, shadows and sounds that fit the legend they already know. A famous haunted place can prime visitors to notice cold spots, shadows and sounds that fit the legend they already know. mechanism How legends prime attention before anything happens What Hampton Court and vault studies tested How folklore becomes part of the evidence chain forteana ghosts place reputation Introduction A haunted reputation can change what people notice long before anything unusual happens. Visitors who enter a castle, palace or underground vault already expecting ghostly activity often pay closer attention to faint sounds, changing temperatures, shadows and bodily sensations that would pass unnoticed in an ordinary building. This does not mean that every reported experience is imagined, nor does it prove that the place is genuinely haunted. Instead, it highlights an important mechanism in the study of ghost reports: reputation influences perception, memory and interpretation. Within Fortean research, this mechanism matters because legends are not merely background stories. They become part of the evidence chain itself. Every new witness arrives carrying expectations shaped by folklore , media coverage, tour guides, friends or previous visitors. Those expectations affect what is noticed, remembered and later reported, making it difficult to separate the physical environment from the cultural story attached to it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov An investigation into alleged 'hauntings'by R Wiseman · 2003 · Cited by 120 — The two studies reported here explored the psychologi... How legends prime attention before anything happens Psychologists often describe attention as selective rather than passive. People cannot consciously process every sound, movement and sensation around them, so the brain constantly filters information according to what seems important. In a location known for hauntings, that filter changes. Visitors may become unusually alert to: sudden draughts or cool patches of air; echoes, footsteps or distant voices; shadows created by uneven lighting; unfamiliar building noises; feelings of unease caused by darkness, confined spaces or silence. The same sensations in an office block or railway station might simply be ignored. Inside a famous haunted location, however, they are more likely to be interpreted as meaningful because visitors have already been told that unusual events occur there. Psychologists refer to this broad process as expectation or perceptual priming: previous knowledge influences the interpretation of ambiguous information. [Goldsmiths, University of London] gold.ac.uk chris french halloween ghosts smiths, University of LondonGhost busting with Chris French26 Oct 2015 — Chris French discusses some of the factors that might lead s... Importantly, this mechanism concerns interpretation rather than dishonesty. A witness may report exactly what they sincerely experienced while still being influenced by the reputation of the setting. Halloween Special: I ain’t afraid of no ghosts! Richard Wiseman and Chris French Channel: Skeptics in the Pub Online Open on YouTube What Hampton Court and the Edinburgh vaults tested Some of the best known investigations into this question were led by psychologist Richard Wiseman and colleagues at two locations already famous for ghost stories: Hampton Court Palace and the South Bridge Vaults beneath Edinburgh. Rather than asking whether ghosts existed, the researchers asked a narrower question: where do visitors report unusual experiences, and what factors might influence those reports? Participants walked through the sites and recorded experiences such as: feeling watched; sudden cold sensations; dizziness; sensing a presence; hearing unexplained sounds; seeing unusual shadows or figures. The researchers compared these reports with the specific locations' reputations and also examined possible physical influences, including local magnetic fields and environmental conditions. Across the studies, participants consistently reported more unusual experiences in areas already regarded as haunted. Remarkably, this pattern appeared even when participants were not specifically aware which individual rooms or passages carried the strongest ghost traditions. [PubMed+2Richard Wiseman] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov An investigation into alleged 'hauntings'by R Wiseman · 2003 · Cited by 120 — The two studies reported here explored the psychologi... The earlier Hampton Court investigation also explored suggestion directly. Some volunteers received information implying that particular areas had recently produced many unusual experiences, while others received the opposite message. The interaction between belief and suggestion influenced how experiences were reported, although the effects were not simple or uniform across all participants. [Hertfordshire Research Profiles] researchprofiles.herts.ac.uk an investigation into the alleged haunting of hampton court palac Hertfordshire Research ProfilesAn investigation into the alleged haunting of Hampton...by R Wiseman · 2002 · Cited by 72 — Hampton Court... These findings are often misunderstood. They did not conclude that ghost reports are \"all in the mind\", nor did they demonstrate paranormal activity. Instead, they showed that psychological expectations and environmental features can combine in ways that increase reports of unusual experiences. Why famous haunted places become self reinforcing Once a location gains a haunted reputation, several reinforcing processes begin to operate simultaneously. Stories guide attention. Visitors often arrive already knowing which staircase, corridor or bedroom is supposed to be haunted. Their attention naturally concentrates on those locations. Ambiguous events become memorable. A creaking floorboard experienced elsewhere is forgotten. The same sound beside the \"haunted gallery\" becomes part of a personal ghost story. Reports influence later visitors. Every published account, television programme or guided tour provides fresh expectations for future witnesses. Silence disappears from the record. Thousands of visitors who notice nothing unusual rarely become part of the folklore, while dramatic experiences are retold repeatedly. Over time this creates a record dominated by memorable accounts rather than typical visits. This process resembles a feedback loop. Reputation encourages heightened attention, heightened attention produces more unusual reports, and those reports strengthen the reputation further. None of these stages requires deliberate fabrication. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Hampton Court as a case study in reputation Hampton Court Palace illustrates how history and folklore intertwine. [hrp.org.uk] hrp.org.uk historic hauntings at hampton court palace Historic Royal PalacesHistoric hauntings at Hampton Court PalaceIn May 2000, the noted psychologist Richard Wiseman conducted an experime... Published: May 2000 The palace is associated with legends surrounding figures such as Catherine Howard, whose reported apparition is said to run screaming through what became known as the Haunted Gallery. Whether or not visitors believe these stories, they provide a powerful interpretive framework before anyone enters the building. The palace itself openly presents these legends as part of its historical visitor experience while distinguishing documented history from later ghost traditions. [Historic Royal Palaces] hrp.org.uk historic hauntings at hampton court palace Historic Royal PalacesHistoric hauntings at Hampton Court PalaceIn May 2000, the noted psychologist Richard Wiseman conducted an experime... Published: May 2000 For researchers, this makes Hampton Court especially valuable. It combines: centuries of accumulated folklore; a steady stream of visitors; clearly defined locations associated with specific legends; an environment rich in unusual lighting, architecture and acoustics. Because the setting already possesses a strong narrative identity, it offers an opportunity to study how expectation shapes experience without needing to create artificial laboratory conditions. How folklore becomes part of the evidence chain One of the most distinctive insights from Fortean studies of hauntings is that folklore is not simply an explanation added afterwards. It actively shapes future testimony. When investigators collect ghost reports from a famous location, they are rarely examining independent observations made in isolation. Instead, each account may already have been influenced by previous books, television documentaries, local guides, online discussions and conversations between visitors. This"},{"title":"Old houses","url":"/old-houses/","description":"Draughts, pipes, traffic vibration, animals and acoustics can make old buildings feel active even when nothing paranormal is proven.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"Old houses The Ordinary Noises Behind Haunted Houses Old houses Old houses The Ordinary Noises Behind Haunted Houses Draughts, pipes, traffic vibration, animals and acoustics can make old buildings feel active even when nothing paranormal is proven. Draughts, pipes, traffic vibration, animals and acoustics can make old buildings feel active even when nothing paranormal is proven. population context Common building sources of knocks and footsteps How vibration and low sound create unease A practical checklist for haunted rooms forteana ghosts old houses Introduction Many reports of hauntings begin not with apparitions but with ordinary sounds: footsteps overhead, knocks inside walls, doors that seem to move on their own, or a persistent feeling that someone is walking through an empty house. Within Fortean research, these reports are worth taking seriously as witness experiences, but they do not automatically point to paranormal causes. Old buildings are complex mechanical systems. As they expand and contract with changing temperatures, respond to wind and traffic, or transmit sound through hidden voids, they can generate noises that are difficult to identify and surprisingly easy to misinterpret. This does not mean every haunting claim has a simple explanation. Rather, many famous \"haunted house\" experiences occur in environments where ordinary physical causes are genuinely difficult to trace. Understanding those causes helps distinguish between unexplained events and the well documented ways that ageing buildings can seem mysteriously alive. Common building sources of knocks and footsteps Historic buildings rarely remain acoustically stable. Timber, masonry, metal pipework and roofing materials all respond differently to temperature, humidity and mechanical stress. The resulting noises are often intermittent, making them seem purposeful rather than mechanical. Some of the most common sources include: Timber expansion and contraction. Wooden floorboards, roof beams and wall framing expand during warm or humid conditions and contract as temperatures fall. The resulting pops, cracks and groans often occur at night, when cooling is most rapid, creating the impression of footsteps or movement above occupied rooms. [Reddit] reddit.com ELI5 What is a house \"settling\" and what noses is it making ELI5 What is a house \"settling\" and what noses is it making...April 29, 2015 — It is called thermal expansion and contraction, eac... Published: April 29, 2015 Water pipes and heating systems. As hot water circulates, metal pipes lengthen slightly. If pipes are tightly fixed or pass through joists, they can produce sharp clicks, bangs or repeated knocking. Steam systems may produce even louder \"water hammer\" effects. Building settlement. Older structures continue to shift by tiny amounts over decades. While dramatic settling is uncommon, small structural movements can generate isolated creaks, especially after heavy rain, drought or seasonal changes. Wind loading. Chimneys, roof timbers, sash windows and loose flashing may vibrate in particular wind directions. Sounds often appear to move through the building because cavities carry vibration surprisingly efficiently. Wildlife. Rodents, squirrels, birds, bats and insects commonly occupy lofts, wall cavities and chimneys. Their movement can sound uncannily like slow walking, scratching or deliberate tapping because plaster and timber amplify small impacts. One reason these noises become associated with hauntings is that they seldom repeat on command. A sound may occur once every few hours or only under a particular combination of weather and heating conditions, making systematic investigation difficult. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Why old houses distort sound Older buildings often confuse listeners because they transmit sound in unexpected ways. Solid brick walls, timber framing, chimney flues and suspended floors can carry vibration far from its source. A tap made in one room may seem to originate from another floor. Water flowing through hidden pipes may produce knocking several metres away from the actual plumbing. Exterior noises—including passing lorries, railway traffic or construction work—can resonate through foundations or steel elements and emerge as low rumbles inside otherwise quiet rooms. Large empty spaces also alter perception. Long corridors, stairwells and vaulted ceilings create echoes and reflections that make ordinary sounds difficult to locate. In darkness, when visual information is reduced, people naturally rely more heavily on hearing, but human directional hearing becomes less accurate for low frequency or reflected sounds. Because many historic buildings were constructed before modern acoustic design, they often contain interconnected voids behind walls and beneath floors that unintentionally function as sound channels. How vibration and low sound create unease Not every reported haunting centres on audible noises. Many witnesses instead describe a feeling that \"something is wrong\" before hearing or seeing anything unusual. One proposed contributor is infrasound —sound below roughly 20 hertz, generally beneath normal human hearing. Sources can include ventilation systems, ageing machinery, large pipes, wind interacting with architectural openings, or vibrations transmitted from nearby traffic. Engineer Vic Tandy became interested in this possibility after experiencing anxiety and seeing a vague grey shape while working alone in a laboratory reputed to be haunted. He later traced an approximately 19 Hz vibration to an extractor fan that produced a standing wave within the room. Tandy suggested that low frequency vibration may have contributed both to feelings of unease and to visual disturbances in at least some environments. His paper proposed a natural explanation for certain haunting reports without claiming it explained all of them. [Richard Wiseman] richardwiseman.com Richard Wiseman The Ghost in the Machine Using the first author's own experience as an example, we...Read more... Subsequent work by psychologist Richard Wiseman explored whether infrasound could influence subjective experiences. In one experiment, volunteers listening to music containing very low frequency components reported unusual feelings—including chills, sadness and anxiety—more often than listeners who heard the same music without the added infrasound. These findings suggest that low frequency vibration can influence emotional experience even when participants cannot consciously identify the sound itself. [Richard Wiseman] wordpress.com Richard Wiseman Ghosts and Hauntings Richard WisemanGhosts and Hauntings Richard Wiseman WordPress.comApril 13, 2020 — Some researchers have suggested that infrasound (ve... More recent laboratory research has strengthened the case that infrasound can measurably affect mood and stress. Participants exposed to 18 Hz vibrations showed increased irritation and elevated cortisol, a stress hormone, even though most could not consciously detect the sound. Researchers suggested that ageing pipes, ventilation systems and similar infrastructure found in older buildings may contribute to feelings later interpreted as supernatural, while cautioning that these findings do not explain reports involving clear visual apparitions or moving objects. [The Times] thetimes.com The Times Sensed a bump in the night? It's not ghosts just bad vibrationsApril 27, 2026 — A recent study published in Frontiers in Behavioural Neuroscience suggests that e... Published: April 27, 2026 Your House Isn't Haunted: Here's What The Science Says Channel: Trace Elements Open on YouTube Why haunted reputations reinforce ordinary sounds Once a building acquires a reputation for being haunted, ordinary environmental events become easier to interpret as meaningful. Psychologists describe this as expectation shaping perception rather than dishonesty. Visitors who have been told that footsteps are frequently heard may pay greater attention to every creak. Random noises become grouped into apparent patterns, and isolated events are remembered more vividly than long periods of silence. This effect is particularly strong in buildings that already encourage heightened awareness: dim lighting, unfamiliar layouts, uneven floors, cold air currents and prolonged quiet all increase attention to ambiguous sensory information. Importantly, recognising the influence of expectation does not imply that witnesses invent experiences. Most people reporting haunted house sounds genuinely heard something. The uncertainty lies in identifying the source, not in whether the experience itself occurred. A practical checklist for investigating haunted rooms Before treating repeated noises as evidence of haunting, investigators—whether sceptical, paranormal or simply curious—can systematically"},{"title":"Poltergeist signs","url":"/poltergeist-signs/","description":"Knocks, moving objects and electrical oddities become more useful to examine when investigators separate timing, witnesses and household pressures.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"Poltergeist signs When Knocks and Moving Objects Become Poltergeists Poltergeist signs Poltergeist signs When Knocks and Moving Objects Become Poltergeists Knocks, moving objects and electrical oddities become more useful to examine when investigators separate timing, witnesses and household pressures. Knocks, moving objects and electrical oddities become more useful to examine when investigators separate timing, witnesses and household pressures. population context What makes a disturbance report different Why repeatability and witnesses matter How stress, fraud and environment should be tested forteana ghosts poltergeist signs Introduction Poltergeist reports occupy a distinctive place within ghost lore because they describe apparently physical disturbances rather than apparitions alone. Witnesses report repeated knocking, objects moving, electrical oddities, unexplained impacts or items apparently thrown without an obvious cause. Within Fortean research, these claims are treated differently from traditional hauntings because they can, at least in principle, be compared with physical evidence, witness testimony and the timing of events. Over more than a century of investigation, researchers have noticed recurring patterns without reaching a single accepted explanation. Some investigators have argued that episodes cluster around periods of family conflict or psychological stress, while sceptics point to hoaxes , perceptual error, structural problems in buildings and selective memory. The most productive investigations therefore focus less on asking whether a disturbance is \"real\" or \"supernatural\" and more on separating observable events from interpretation, identifying who witnessed what, and testing ordinary explanations before considering more unusual ones. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi EncyclopediaPsychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia24 May 2019 — William Roll's surveys found many cases associ... Published: May 2019 What makes a disturbance report different? Unlike a conventional haunting account centred on a ghostly figure or sensed presence, a poltergeist report is built around claims of physical interaction with the environment. Commonly reported features include: Repeated knocks, bangs or impacts occurring over days or weeks. Small objects apparently moving, falling or being thrown. Furniture shifting or vibrating. Lights flickering or electrical devices behaving erratically. Incidents witnessed by more than one household member, although not always simultaneously. The emphasis on physical effects means investigators can ask questions that are less useful in apparition cases. Was anyone in the room? Could the object have been moved naturally? Did anyone photograph or measure the scene? Were there building defects, plumbing problems or electrical faults? Did the activity stop after repairs or after household circumstances changed? Another frequently discussed feature is the apparent concentration of activity around a particular individual rather than a particular room. Parapsychologist William G. Roll argued that many reported cases involved one family member—often an adolescent—during periods of emotional upheaval or domestic tension. Although this observation has become influential within parapsychological literature, it remains a descriptive pattern rather than a demonstrated causal relationship. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi EncyclopediaPsychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia24 May 2019 — William Roll's surveys found many cases associ... Published: May 2019 Richard Wiseman on Lessons from Dale Carnegie, Mentalism, The Psychology of the Paranormal, and More Channel: Tim Ferriss Open on YouTube Why repeatability and witnesses matter Most reported disturbances occur unpredictably, making them difficult to investigate under controlled conditions. Because of this, the quality of documentation often matters more than the dramatic nature of any individual incident. Investigators generally give greater weight to reports that include: Independent witnesses who recorded events separately. Contemporary notes rather than memories written months later. Consistent descriptions over time. Physical traces that can be examined. Precise timelines identifying when activity began, intensified and ended. Equally important is identifying who did not witness an event. Many famous cases include episodes seen by only one person, later retold to others. As stories spread through a household, expectations can influence what people notice and remember, particularly during stressful periods. Memory research consistently shows that sincere witnesses may unintentionally reshape recollections through discussion, repeated retelling and exposure to leading questions rather than deliberate deception. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCWhat Do People Believe About Memory? Implications for the...by SJ Lynn · 2015 · Cited by 53 — We examine the evidence concerning what people believe about memory. We focus o... This is one reason why experienced investigators often construct detailed chronologies instead of relying on dramatic summaries. A timeline can reveal whether reports increased after media attention, whether multiple witnesses actually observed the same event, or whether apparently related incidents occurred under very different circumstances. Why household stress appears so often One of the most persistent observations in the literature is the apparent association between reported poltergeist outbreaks and periods of domestic disruption. Investigators have described households experiencing bereavement, marital conflict, financial pressure, illness, relocation or tension involving adolescents. This pattern has generated several competing interpretations. Stress as a psychological context A conventional psychological interpretation does not argue that stress physically moves objects. Instead, it proposes that stressful environments increase vigilance, emotional arousal and the tendency to interpret ambiguous events as connected. Ordinary household noises become more noticeable, memories become organised into a meaningful narrative, and isolated incidents may gradually be interpreted as part of a single unfolding phenomenon. Stress can also influence attention and memory. People under prolonged pressure are more likely to focus on threatening or unusual events while overlooking uneventful periods, making patterns appear stronger than they may objectively be. [American Psychological Association] apa.org Based on the available data, we American Psychological AssociationThe Effects of Stress on Prospective Memoryby M Piefke · Cited by 50 — In this review, available behavi... Stress within parapsychological theories Some parapsychological researchers have suggested a different possibility: that emotional strain might somehow contribute to genuine physical disturbances through mechanisms not recognised by mainstream science. Roll's influential model proposed that unresolved emotional tension surrounding a particular individual might be associated with recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis, sometimes abbreviated to RSPK. Despite its historical importance within psychical research, this hypothesis has not been experimentally confirmed. Modern reviews generally describe it as speculative because reported outbreaks are unpredictable and have not produced repeatable laboratory evidence. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi EncyclopediaPsychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia24 May 2019 — William Roll's surveys found many cases associ... Published: May 2019 Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube How fraud, environment and ordinary causes should be tested Poltergeist investigations have repeatedly shown that extraordinary claims require careful elimination of ordinary explanations. Several categories deserve systematic testing before attributing events to unknown causes. Deliberate fabrication. A number of famous cases have eventually included admissions of trickery or convincing demonstrations that some incidents were staged. This does not necessarily explain every reported event in a case, but it greatly complicates claims that an entire episode reflects an unexplained phenomenon. Magicians and investigators familiar with deception have shown how apparently impossible events can sometimes be produced using ordinary means. [Richard Wiseman] richardwiseman.wordpress.com Richard Wiseman Academic papers & books Richard WisemanAcademic papers & books Richard Wiseman WordPress.comNovember 23, 2019 — Failure to detect such fraud can lead to seri... Published: November 23, 2019 Building and environmental factors. Old houses naturally produce knocks as materials expand and contract. Loose plumbing, vibration from traffic, faulty wiring,"},{"title":"Sleep ghosts","url":"/sleep-ghosts/","description":"Many bedroom ghost reports begin with waking paralysis, fear and a vivid presence before the story becomes supernatural.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Ghosts","level":3,"text":"Sleep ghosts When a Bedroom Ghost May Be Sleep Paralysis Sleep ghosts Sleep ghosts When a Bedroom Ghost May Be Sleep Paralysis Many bedroom ghost reports begin with waking paralysis, fear and a vivid presence before the story becomes supernatural. Many bedroom ghost reports begin with waking paralysis, fear and a vivid presence before the story becomes supernatural. mechanism What witnesses report during waking paralysis Why culture changes the shape of the visitor Checks before treating a bedroom haunting as external forteana ghosts sleep ghosts Introduction Many reports of a “ghost in the bedroom” begin with a strikingly similar sequence: the person wakes, realises they cannot move, feels overwhelming fear, senses that someone else is in the room, and may see a shadowy figure standing by the bed or pressing on their chest. In Fortean collections these accounts are often presented as hauntings, yet sleep medicine recognises the same pattern as a well described phenomenon known as isolated sleep paralysis . The experience is intensely real to those who undergo it, and understanding its mechanisms does not diminish the emotional impact. Instead, it helps explain why one of the world's most common supernatural experiences so often occurs in bedrooms, at the edge of sleep, and why different cultures describe remarkably similar events while giving the visitor very different identities. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... What witnesses report during waking paralysis Sleep paralysis occurs during the transition between rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and wakefulness. During REM sleep, the brain normally switches off most voluntary muscle movement to prevent people acting out dreams. Occasionally, consciousness returns before this muscle paralysis has ended. The result is an unsettling state in which the person is awake enough to perceive their bedroom but remains unable to move or call for help. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... For witnesses, the paralysis is only part of the experience. Reports frequently include: A powerful sense that another being is present nearby. A dark figure standing in a doorway or beside the bed. Pressure on the chest or difficulty taking a deep breath. The feeling that the figure is watching, approaching or climbing onto the bed. Sounds such as footsteps, whispers, breathing or knocking. In some cases, the sensation of floating, leaving the body or seeing the room from above. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCNightmares or a crippling reality? A review on sleep paralysisby A Honnekeri · 2025 — SP is also largely associated with three types of hallucinations – the 'intruder', the... Researchers studying sleep paralysis have repeatedly found that the \"sensed presence\" is among its most characteristic features. The person often becomes convinced that another conscious being occupies the room, even before seeing any visual image. This feeling can be so compelling that many witnesses remain convinced for years that they experienced an external entity rather than an altered state of consciousness. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... Within Fortean literature this is important because many classic bedroom haunting narratives match this sequence almost exactly: awakening, paralysis, fear, presence and only then interpretation. What Are Incubus And Succubus? Anthropology Insights Channel: Anthropology Insights Open on YouTube Why the experience feels so convincingly external The remarkable realism of sleep paralysis comes from several processes occurring simultaneously rather than from a single hallucination. The sleeper is partially awake and correctly perceives much of the real bedroom. Unlike an ordinary dream, familiar furniture, doors and windows may all appear exactly where they should be. Into this genuine scene, dream imagery and threat related expectations become blended. Because the room itself is real, the added figure often seems equally real. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... Researchers have proposed that the brain's systems for monitoring threats become unusually active during these episodes. The feeling that \"someone is there\" may appear before any visual image forms, after which the brain constructs a figure consistent with that expectation. The hallucinated presence therefore behaves less like a random dream character than like an intruder whose intentions feel immediately dangerous. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) Sleep Paralysis, “The Ghostly Bedroom Intruder” and...28 Feb 2017 — Cheyne and Girard characterize felt presence (FP)... Chest pressure also contributes to supernatural interpretations. During REM sleep, breathing becomes shallower and the muscles involved in deep voluntary breathing remain partly inhibited. When people awaken in this state, they commonly experience the frightening sensation that something heavy is pressing on their chest or preventing them from breathing properly. Throughout history this sensation has been interpreted as an attacking spirit, demon or ghost physically restraining the sleeper. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBISleep Paralysis It tends...Read more... Why culture changes the shape of the visitor One of the strongest findings in sleep paralysis research is that the underlying experience remains remarkably consistent while the identity of the perceived visitor varies across cultures. Historical Europe commonly described an incubus, succubus or \"Old Hag\" sitting upon the sleeper. In parts of East Asia, experiences have been attributed to ghosts or spirit attacks. Egyptian traditions frequently identify a jinn, while many other societies have developed their own supernatural explanations for the same core experience. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Paralysis, a Medical Condition with a Diverse Cultural...by E Olunu · 2018 · Cited by 54 — This review aims to medically descri... Modern accounts show the same pattern of cultural updating. Rather than medieval demons, witnesses may report shadow people, unidentified entities or even alien visitors. The underlying sensations—paralysis, presence and fear—remain stable, while the narrative reflects the beliefs, stories and expectations most available to the witness. [The Cut] thecut.com The Cut Why Is Sleep Paralysis So Terrifying? It affects around 7.6% of the U.S. population. During sleep paralysis, some people report seeing hallucinations, often of shadowy or demo... Some studies suggest that cultural beliefs influence more than interpretation alone. Where people strongly fear supernatural attacks during sleep, anticipation itself may increase anxiety around bedtime, making episodes feel longer, more frightening and potentially more frequent. This creates a feedback loop in which terrifying experiences reinforce supernatural expectations, which in turn heighten fear before future sleep. [Time] time.com The condition occurs when there is a glitch at the transition between wakefulness and REM sleep, leaving individuals paralyzed and often... For researchers interested in Forteana , this illustrates an important distinction. Folklore does not necessarily create the experience from nothing; rather, it appears to shape how an already distinctive neurological event is recognised, remembered and retold. The sleep paralysis demons who reveal your repressed desires Channel: Betwixt: The Story of You Open on YouTube Checks before treating a bedroom haunting as external Sleep paralysis does not explain every report of unusual activity in a house. However, when evaluating an isolated bedroom encounter, investigators commonly ask several practical questions before concluding that an external haunting occurred. Consider whether: The experience happened immediately on falling asleep or upon waking. The witness was unable to move despite feeling awake. A strong sense of terror appeared before any visual figure. The event lasted seconds or a few minutes before suddenly ending. Normal movement returned all at once. The figure disappeared as soon as full movement returned. The experience occurred during periods of stress, disrupted sleep, sleep deprivation or irregular sleep schedules. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... When most of these features are present together, sleep paralysis becomes a strong candidate explanation. In contrast, reports involving multiple witnesses, events"},{"title":"Hoaxes","url":"/hoaxes/","description":"Hoaxes reveal how strange reports can grow through attention, repetition, and weak checking.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Hoaxes How Hoaxes Shape the Fortean Record Hoaxes Hoaxes How Hoaxes Shape the Fortean Record Hoaxes reveal how strange reports can grow through attention, repetition, and weak checking. Hoaxes reveal how strange reports can grow through attention, repetition, and weak checking. mechanism Why hoaxes work How false reports spread What debunking can settle forteana hoaxes Introduction Hoaxes are not a side issue in Fortean history. They are one of the main ways strange reports acquire momentum, authority and staying power. A Fortean hoax may begin as a prank, a money making attraction, a publicity stunt, a revenge plot, an artistic experiment or a deliberate attempt to “test” believers. What makes it important is what happens next: newspapers repeat it, experts are asked to rule on it, believers defend it, sceptics attack it, and the original trick becomes part of a much larger record of contested anomalies. The lesson is not simply that people are gullible. Forteana is full of reports that sit in awkward territory between error, folklore, fraud, misperception and unresolved evidence. Hoaxes reveal the weak points in that territory: the appeal of a good story, the prestige of a famous sponsor, the difficulty of checking images or specimens after the fact, and the way a claim can keep cultural life even after the deception is exposed. The Cottingley fairies, the Cardiff Giant , Piltdown Man, crop circles and the Loch Ness “surgeon’s photograph” show the same pattern in different forms: the false report matters because it teaches how belief, media and evidence interact. [PBS+3Google Arts & Culture+3Smithsonian Magazine] artsandculture.google.com Google Arts & CultureThe Cottingley Fairies: A Study In DeceptionIt contained photographs of fairies that Doyle concluded were real. Expe... Why Hoaxes Work A successful Fortean hoax usually does not ask the public to believe something utterly random. It attaches itself to a story people are already prepared to entertain. The Cardiff Giant appeared in 1869 America, a setting in which biblical literalism, fossil discoveries, travelling exhibitions and commercial showmanship could all meet in one “petrified man”. The object was not merely a statue; it was presented as a possible physical answer to arguments about giants, antiquity and scripture, and it was quickly monetised as an attraction. Smithsonian describes the case as one of nineteenth century America’s most famous hoaxes, made powerful because it looked as though it might be real while remaining open enough for spectators to argue over it. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com cardiff giant was just big hoax 180965274 Smithsonian MagazineThe Cardiff Giant Was Just a Big Hoax16 Oct 2017 — The story, which began on this day in 1869, was classic fake news–... That ambiguity is central. Hoaxes thrive when the claim is just concrete enough to examine but just mysterious enough to resist immediate closure. A carved gypsum giant in a tent, a fairy photograph, a dark shape in Loch Ness , or a circle flattened overnight in a wheat field gives people an object to talk about. The hoaxer does not need everyone to believe. It is often enough for the public to wonder, for journalists to amplify the uncertainty, and for paying audiences or invested believers to keep the debate alive. The Cottingley fairies show how authority can turn a small deception into a public event. Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths took staged fairy photographs in 1917; the images later reached Arthur Conan Doyle , who used them in a 1920 Strand Magazine article on fairies. Leeds University Library’s exhibition material notes that Doyle presented the photographs as real and stated that experts had checked them and found they were not a hoax. The force of the case came partly from the mismatch between Doyle’s reputation as creator of Sherlock Holmes and his spiritualist enthusiasm: a famous rational detective’s author became a guarantor for an extraordinary claim. [Google Arts & Culture] artsandculture.google.com Google Arts & CultureThe Cottingley Fairies: A Study In DeceptionIt contained photographs of fairies that Doyle concluded were real. Expe... The photographs also worked because the test applied to them was too narrow. Investigators looked for photographic manipulation, but the trick had happened in front of the camera: paper figures, arranged in the scene, photographed as though they were living beings. Later accounts from the University of Huddersfield and other institutional sources describe the fairies as line drawings fixed in place, with the full confession coming decades later. The case is a reminder that “no evidence of tampering” is not the same as “the event happened”. [University of Huddersfield] hud.ac.uk Open source on hud.ac.uk. Fortean hoaxes also work because they borrow the emotional charge of the wider subject. Fairies in the aftermath of the First World War, a monster in a deep Scottish loch, a “missing link” in human evolution, or strange circles in the landscape all speak to existing hopes and anxieties. They offer wonder, proof, national prestige, cosmic contact or a crack in ordinary reality. The hoax succeeds when the audience wants the world to be more marvellous, more threatening or more meaningful than the available evidence can safely support. How False Reports Spread False Fortean reports often spread in stages rather than all at once. First comes the apparent evidence: a body, photograph, broadcast, fossil, formation or witness account. Then comes social certification: an expert comments, a newspaper reports, a celebrity endorses, a museum displays, or a researcher declares the object worth taking seriously. Finally comes repetition. Once the claim has been retold often enough, it can survive even after the original basis has collapsed. The Cardiff Giant is a clear example of commercial repetition. After the “giant” was discovered on William Newell’s farm in Cardiff, New York, Newell charged visitors to see it. When P. T. Barnum could not buy the attraction, he had his own version made and promoted that instead. The Public Domain Review notes the irony: Barnum’s copy created a second order hoax, with one fake being advertised against another. The story became less about whether the giant was real and more about spectacle, rivalry and the entertainment value of contested fraud. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org cardiff giant cardiff giant Crop circles show a later, more participatory version of the same mechanism. Doug Bower and Dave Chorley said in 1991 that they had been making crop circles in southern England for years, using simple tools. Smithsonian’s account places their first “flying saucer nest” in Wiltshire in 1976 and stresses that they could not have foreseen how large the phenomenon would become. Once the idea existed, others copied, elaborated and aestheticised it; crop circles evolved from simple marks into complex landscape designs, while paranormal interpretations continued around them. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com crop circles the art of the hoax 2524283 crop circles the art of the hoax 2524283 The spread was helped by the way the formations appeared: overnight, in rural fields, often in areas already rich in ancient monuments, folklore and UFO associations. The Psi Encyclopedia notes sceptical observations that crop circles often appear near roads or tracks and near cultural heritage sites such as Stonehenge, Silbury Hill and chalk figures, features suggestive of human access and human meaning making. That does not make every individual formation equally documented, but it explains why the phenomenon became a perfect Fortean object: visible, mysterious looking, photogenic and culturally located. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Crop Circles Psi Encyclopedia Crop Circles The Loch Ness “surgeon’s photograph” shows the power of a single image. The 1934 photograph, associated with Colonel Robert Wilson, became one of the most famous images of the Loch Ness Monster. Later accounts identify it as a hoax involving a small model mounted on a toy submarine, with Christian Spurling said to have confessed before details were published in the 1990s. PBS’s NOVA summary adds an important caution: although the hoax explanation became widely accepted after the 1994 disclosure, some questions were raised about the confession’s timing and details. That is typical of Fortean debunking: even when a famous piece of evidence falls, debate may continue around the reliability of the exposure itself. [PBS] pbs.org Open source on pbs.org. A hoax can also spread because institutions are slow to correct their own investments. Piltdown Man, announced in 1912 after Charles Dawson claimed to have found remains in Sussex, was treated for decades as a major fossil in human evolution. The Natural History Museum describes Dawson as an amateur archaeologist who claimed to have found a “missing"},{"title":"Cardiff Giant","url":"/cardiff-giant/","description":"The Cardiff Giant became profitable not because everyone believed it, but because argument itself became part of the show.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Cardiff Giant The Cardiff Giant Was a Paying Argument Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant The Cardiff Giant Was a Paying Argument The Cardiff Giant became profitable not because everyone believed it, but because argument itself became part of the show. The Cardiff Giant became profitable not because everyone believed it, but because argument itself became part of the show. entity Why a petrified giant fit its moment How exhibition money kept the story alive What Barnum's rival fake reveals about spectacle forteana hoaxes cardiff giant Introduction The Cardiff Giant became one of the most profitable hoaxes in nineteenth century America not because everyone accepted it as genuine, but because people could not agree on what they were looking at. Presented in 1869 as a newly unearthed “petrified man”, the ten foot figure sat at the intersection of biblical belief, emerging scientific archaeology, commercial entertainment and sensational journalism. Visitors paid not simply to see a giant, but to participate in an argument over whether it represented ancient history, religious proof, Native American art or outright fraud. That uncertainty proved commercially valuable. Even after experts declared it a fake, the controversy itself kept audiences coming, culminating in an extraordinary spectacle where showman P. T. Barnum exhibited his own counterfeit version of an already counterfeit object. The Cardiff Giant therefore illustrates an important pattern in Fortean history: disputed evidence can become more profitable than settled truth. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian Magazine The Cardiff Giant Was Just a Big Hoax Smithsonian MagazineThe Cardiff Giant Was Just a Big HoaxOctober 16, 2017 — 16 Oct 2017 — The story, which began on this day in 1869, was... Published: October 16, 2017 Why a petrified giant fit its moment The Cardiff Giant emerged during a period when Americans were debating geology, evolution and the literal interpretation of the Bible. Fossils were becoming familiar to the public, yet scientific standards for archaeology and palaeontology were still developing. Reports of unusual discoveries circulated widely through newspapers , and many readers were prepared to entertain the possibility that remarkable physical evidence might suddenly rewrite history. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian Magazine The Cardiff Giant Was Just a Big Hoax Smithsonian MagazineThe Cardiff Giant Was Just a Big HoaxOctober 16, 2017 — 16 Oct 2017 — The story, which began on this day in 1869, was... Published: October 16, 2017 George Hull, the tobacconist who organised the deception, later explained that the project grew partly from arguments over the biblical passage in Genesis referring to giants. According to contemporary accounts, Hull wanted both to expose what he regarded as religious credulity and to profit from doing so. He commissioned a gypsum block to be carved into a weathered human figure, artificially aged it with acids and abrasion, and secretly buried it on the farm of his cousin William \"Stub\" Newell before arranging its dramatic \"discovery\" nearly a year later. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant Importantly, the giant was not convincing because it perfectly resembled an ancient human body. Even many early observers noticed anatomical oddities and visible tool marks. Instead, it occupied a plausible middle ground between certainty and mystery. Some visitors interpreted it as a petrified human, others as an ancient statue, while sceptics dismissed it as a recent carving. That ambiguity created discussion rather than immediate dismissal , allowing competing interpretations to coexist long enough for commercial exploitation. [JSTOR] jstor.org 768. It... The Cardiff giant controversy highlighted the problem of fraud and deception across.Read more... 242 The Cardiff Giant Channel: Futility Closet Open on YouTube How exhibition money kept the story alive The financial success of the Cardiff Giant depended on maintaining uncertainty rather than resolving it. Newell quickly erected a tent over the excavation site and initially charged admission before raising the price as crowds increased. Hundreds of visitors arrived daily, and local hotels, restaurants and transport providers also benefited from the sudden influx of tourists. The discovery became a regional economic event as much as a scientific curiosity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant Once the initial excitement peaked, ownership shifted from the original conspirators to investors led by David Hannum, who recognised that the giant's value lay in touring exhibitions. Instead of quietly exposing the fraud or withdrawing the object, the syndicate moved it to larger cities where fresh audiences could continue debating its authenticity. Every newspaper report questioning the giant also served as advertising, encouraging readers to judge the evidence for themselves. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant Scientific criticism did not immediately destroy the attraction. Yale palaeontologist Othniel Charles Marsh pointed out that the statue consisted of soluble gypsum, retained fresh carving marks and could not plausibly have survived centuries underground. Other investigators questioned why a well had been dug at precisely that location. Yet these expert opinions became part of the entertainment rather than an immediate ending. Visitors could compare expert testimony with their own impressions, turning scepticism into another reason to buy a ticket. [HISTORY] history.com the cardiff giant fools the nation 145 years ago The Cardiff Giant Fools the Nation, 145 Years Ago16 Oct 2014 — “It is rather rich,” quipped the Philadelphia Inquirer, “that we sh... This dynamic distinguishes the Cardiff Giant from a simple confidence trick. Instead of disappearing once challenged, it entered a prolonged public debate in which competing authorities—scientists, clergy, journalists, entrepreneurs and spectators—each claimed the right to interpret the evidence. The exhibition profited from the continued existence of disagreement. What Barnum's rival fake reveals about spectacle The most revealing episode came after P. T. Barnum attempted to purchase the giant for exhibition. When the owners refused his offer, Barnum commissioned an almost identical copy and advertised it as the genuine Cardiff Giant, claiming that the original exhibition displayed the fake. Rather than undermining public interest, this created a second layer of deception. Audiences could now choose between two competing giants, each accusing the other of fraud. [HISTORY] history.com the cardiff giant fools the nation 145 years ago The Cardiff Giant Fools the Nation, 145 Years Ago16 Oct 2014 — “It is rather rich,” quipped the Philadelphia Inquirer, “that we sh... This \"fake of a fake\" transformed authenticity into a theatrical performance. Barnum's advertisements asked provocative questions rather than providing answers, encouraging visitors to witness the controversy firsthand. Reports suggest that Barnum's version sometimes attracted larger crowds than the original because the rivalry itself had become the attraction. Newspapers recognised the absurdity of a fraud competing against another fraud, yet that very absurdity generated additional publicity. [HISTORY] history.com the cardiff giant fools the nation 145 years ago The Cardiff Giant Fools the Nation, 145 Years Ago16 Oct 2014 — “It is rather rich,” quipped the Philadelphia Inquirer, “that we sh... The legal dispute that followed further illustrates how commercial spectacle overtook factual resolution. Hannum sued Barnum for claiming the original giant was fake, but the case collapsed into irony because both exhibits were fraudulent. The courts ultimately refused to punish someone for describing a fake object as fake. Meanwhile, public attention remained focused on the competing exhibitions rather than on establishing historical truth. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant The episode is also linked—probably incorrectly—to the famous saying, \"There's a sucker born every minute.\" Contemporary evidence suggests the remark was associated with David Hannum rather than Barnum, while later retellings shifted the quotation to the better known showman. The persistence of this disputed attribution mirrors the giant's own history, where memorable stories often proved more durable than documented fact. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cardiff Giant Cardiff Giant The Cardiff Giant Everything Everywhere Daily Channel: Everything Everywhere (Everything Everywhere) Open on YouTube The Bizarre Story of the Cardiff Giant: The Greatest Hoax Channel: Hugo train Open on YouTube Why the Cardiff Giant remains significant in Fortean history Within the history of Fortean claims, the Cardiff Giant demonstrates that contested evidence can acquire a cultural life independent of belief. The hoax succeeded because it resonated with existing"},{"title":"Cottingley","url":"/cottingley/","description":"The Cottingley photographs show how a staged scene can pass a limited tampering check while still misleading the public.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Cottingley Why the Cottingley Fairies Fooled Experts Cottingley Cottingley Why the Cottingley Fairies Fooled Experts The Cottingley photographs show how a staged scene can pass a limited tampering check while still misleading the public. The Cottingley photographs show how a staged scene can pass a limited tampering check while still misleading the public. dataset evidence How two staged photographs became public evidence Why checking for tampering missed the real trick What the confession changed and what it did not forteana hoaxes cottingley Introduction The Cottingley fairy photographs remain one of the clearest demonstrations that an image can pass a genuine technical examination while still telling a false story. Taken in 1917 by cousins Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths, the photographs appeared to show fairies dancing beside a Yorkshire beck. They were examined by respected photographic specialists, endorsed by influential supporters including Sir Arthur Conan Doyle , and became some of the most famous images in Fortean history. Yet the deception was remarkably simple: the \"fairies\" were paper cut outs positioned in front of the camera rather than alterations made afterwards. The case illustrates an enduring lesson in evidence evaluation. A finding that a photograph has not been retouched is not the same as proof that what it depicts actually existed. [Google Arts & Culture+2Wikipedia] artsandculture.google.com Google Arts & CultureThe Cottingley Fairies: A Study In DeceptionIt contained photographs of fairies that Doyle concluded were real. Expe... How two staged photographs became public evidence The first two Cottingley photographs were created in the summer of 1917 when Elsie Wright, an artistic teenager familiar with photography through her father's darkroom, photographed her younger cousin Frances Griffiths beside apparently dancing fairies. The images originated as a local prank and a way to support the girls' claim that they visited fairies by the stream behind their home. They were not initially presented as the product of sophisticated photographic manipulation. [National Science and Media Museum blog] blog.scienceandmediamuseum.org.uk the story of the cottingley fairies shows that image manipulation is nothing new The images would go on to... The photographs reached members of the Theosophical movement before attracting the attention of Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, who was preparing an article on fairies for The Strand Magazine . In a society still marked by grief after the First World War and widespread interest in spiritualism, the photographs seemed to offer visible confirmation of a hidden world rather than merely an entertaining curiosity. Doyle's reputation gave the images an authority that far exceeded their modest origins. The Public Domain Review+2Google Arts & Culture [publicdomainreview.org] publicdomainreview.org sir arthur and the fairies The Public Domain ReviewSir Arthur and the Fairies12 Jun 2013 — Mary Losure explores how the creator of Sherlock Holmes became convinced... The public response was never unanimous. Contemporary critics questioned the photographs almost immediately, while supporters regarded them as rare physical evidence of supernatural beings. Their cultural importance came from this uncertainty: the photographs became objects to debate rather than evidence that settled the matter. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies Photo Proof of the Cottingley Fairies Existence? Jimmy Akin's Mysterious World Channel: Jimmy Akin Open on YouTube Why checking for tampering missed the real trick The Cottingley case is especially important because the principal technical investigations addressed the wrong question. Photography expert Harold Snelling examined the original negatives and concluded that they showed no signs of darkroom manipulation or composite printing. Kodak's specialists likewise found no evidence that the negatives had been altered, although the company refused to certify the photographs as authentic evidence of fairies. Ilford's assessment was more sceptical, suggesting indications of fakery, yet none of these examinations could establish what had actually stood in front of the camera at the moment of exposure. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies This distinction is fundamental. A photographic negative can be technically genuine while recording an artificial scene. If someone photographs a stage set, a wax figure or a cardboard model, the resulting negative may be completely authentic as a record of that arrangement. The photograph truthfully records what the camera saw, but not necessarily what the viewer assumes the scene represents. That is precisely what happened at Cottingley. The deception occurred before the shutter was pressed. Later confessions described fairy figures copied from illustrations in Princess Mary's Gift Book , cut from card, supported with hatpins and carefully positioned in the landscape before being photographed. Because investigators concentrated on detecting alterations to the negative, they largely overlooked the possibility that the scene itself had been manufactured. Wikipedia+2National Science and Media Museum blog [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies The episode therefore highlights an important principle that extends well beyond Forteana : An authentic negative does not guarantee an authentic event. Absence of photographic manipulation is not evidence that the subject is genuine. Image analysis must be combined with investigation of how the scene was created, who controlled it and whether independent witnesses or corroborating evidence exist. These principles have become standard practice in modern forensic image analysis, but the Cottingley photographs demonstrate why they were necessary in the first place. [Google Arts & Culture] artsandculture.google.com Google Arts & CultureThe Cottingley Fairies: A Study In DeceptionIt contained photographs of fairies that Doyle concluded were real. Expe... What the confession changed and what it did not In the early 1980s, more than sixty years after the photographs were taken, Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths publicly admitted that the fairy figures in the photographs had been created from cardboard illustrations. Both explained that what had begun as a youthful prank became increasingly difficult to retract after respected adults accepted the images as genuine. Elsie later remarked that admitting the truth while Conan Doyle remained alive would have been deeply embarrassing for everyone involved. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies The confession resolved much of the historical mystery, but not every point of debate. Frances Griffiths continued to insist that the fifth and final photograph represented a genuine fairy encounter, while Elsie maintained that all five images had been staged. Geoffrey Crawley's detailed investigation for the British Journal of Photography concluded that the photographs were fraudulent and explored possible explanations for the disputed fifth image, including accidental photographic effects rather than supernatural activity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies This lingering disagreement is itself instructive. Confessions do not always erase every uncertainty surrounding famous cases. Instead, they often shift attention from whether the phenomenon occurred to how belief persisted despite mounting contradictory evidence. Their Fib Convinced The World Episode 03: The Cottingley Fairies Channel: A Thousand Words Open on YouTube The Cottingley Fairies: A Study in Deception Channel: Leeds University Library Galleries Open on YouTube The wider lesson for evaluating extraordinary photographs The Cottingley fairy photographs continue to be cited because they expose a recurring weakness in evidence assessment. Investigators can become highly skilled at detecting one category of deception while overlooking another. The early experts were competent at identifying common photographic tricks such as double exposures or darkroom manipulation. Their mistake was assuming that if the negative was technically genuine, the depicted event must also be genuine. The actual deception exploited a different point in the chain of evidence entirely: the construction of the scene before the photograph was taken. [Google Arts & Culture] artsandculture.google.com Google Arts & CultureThe Cottingley Fairies: A Study In DeceptionIt contained photographs of fairies that Doyle concluded were real. Expe... For Fortean investigations, the case offers several enduring questions that should accompany any striking photograph: Who controlled the scene before the image was captured? Could ordinary physical props produce the same appearance? Is there independent evidence beyond the photograph itself? Does the technical examination answer the same question that the extraordinary claim depends upon? These"},{"title":"Crop Circles","url":"/crop-circles-2ea348/","description":"Crop circles grew from simple human-made marks into a participatory mystery shaped by copying, artistry and paranormal expectation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Crop Circles When Crop Circles Became a Movement Crop Circles Crop Circles When Crop Circles Became a Movement Crop circles grew from simple human made marks into a participatory mystery shaped by copying, artistry and paranormal expectation. Crop circles grew from simple human made marks into a participatory mystery shaped by copying, artistry and paranormal expectation. mechanism Bower, Chorley and the first saucer nests Why roads, fields and ancient sites mattered How copycats turned a hoax into folk art forteana hoaxes crop circles Introduction Crop circles occupy a distinctive place in Fortean history because they demonstrate how a hoax can evolve into a self sustaining cultural movement. What began as simple flattened circles in English fields developed into increasingly elaborate geometric designs, attracting paranormal speculation, scientific investigation, tourism and artistic imitation. Even after the best known originators admitted creating many of the early formations, the phenomenon did not disappear. Instead, copycats expanded it into an international form of landscape art while believers and sceptics continued to disagree over individual cases. The crop circle story therefore illustrates an important Fortean mechanism: once a convincing mystery captures public imagination, exposing one hoax does not necessarily end the phenomenon—it can encourage new participants, new interpretations and new myths. [Wikipedia+2Smithsonian Magazine] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Bower, Chorley and the first saucer nests The modern crop circle phenomenon is closely associated with Doug Bower and Dave Chorley, two English men who publicly confessed in 1991 to creating circles in southern England over many years using remarkably simple equipment. Their method relied on a wooden plank attached to ropes for flattening crops, together with strings and improvised sighting devices to keep designs symmetrical. They demonstrated their technique in front of journalists, showing that apparently mysterious formations could be produced overnight without specialised machinery. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle According to Bower, the original inspiration came from reports of so called \"saucer nests\" in Australia during the 1960s, where circular patches of flattened reeds had been linked to UFO stories. Rather than attempting to imitate extraterrestrial visitors directly, Bower and Chorley exploited an atmosphere in which unusual circular marks were already associated with mysterious aerial phenomena. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Their confession was significant not because it explained every crop formation ever reported, but because it demonstrated beyond reasonable doubt that convincing examples could be manufactured by people. This shifted the burden of proof. Instead of asking whether humans were capable of producing the formations, investigators now had to explain why any particular formation should be considered exceptional. An especially revealing episode occurred when crop circle researcher Pat Delgado examined a freshly made demonstration circle and initially judged it authentic before learning that Bower and Chorley had created it. The incident exposed how expectations could influence interpretation even among experienced investigators. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle How are Crop Circles Made? Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Why roads, fields and ancient sites mattered Crop circles did not appear randomly across Britain. They became concentrated in southern England, especially Wiltshire and neighbouring counties, where several practical and cultural factors reinforced one another. From a practical perspective, cereal crops provided a flexible medium that could be bent without being destroyed, allowing patterns to remain visible for days or weeks. Fields beside roads made discoveries more likely because passing motorists could easily spot fresh formations. Research into the geographical distribution of crop circles has found strong associations with accessible locations rather than isolated landscapes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle The symbolic landscape mattered just as much. Wiltshire contains prehistoric monuments such as Stonehenge and Avebury, long associated with folklore , earth mysteries, ley lines and New Age spirituality. A geometric pattern appearing near these sites seemed more meaningful than an identical design in an ordinary agricultural district. The landscape itself encouraged supernatural interpretation before any evidence was examined. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian MagazineCrop Circles: The Art of the Hoax15 Dec 2009 — Crop circles are a lens through which we can explore the nature and ap... Media attention amplified this effect. Once newspapers regularly photographed circles against famous prehistoric backdrops, the region became identified with unexplained mysteries. Visitors travelled specifically to search for new formations, creating a cycle in which artists knew that circles placed near recognised landmarks would receive greater publicity than those created elsewhere. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian MagazineCrop Circles: The Art of the Hoax15 Dec 2009 — Crop circles are a lens through which we can explore the nature and ap... How copycats turned a hoax into folk art The most remarkable development came after Bower and Chorley's confession. Instead of ending the phenomenon, it inspired hundreds of other makers. Many newcomers regarded crop circles less as deception than as anonymous public art. Designs rapidly evolved beyond simple circles into intricate fractals, spirals, pictograms and mathematically inspired geometries that required planning, teamwork and increasingly sophisticated surveying methods. Physicists and observers have suggested that later groups sometimes employed GPS, measuring equipment and careful geometric planning to achieve a level of precision impossible in the earliest circles. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle This transformation changed the social meaning of crop circles. Early phase: simple circles intended to provoke mystery. Copycat phase: increasingly elaborate designs intended to astonish audiences. Artistic phase: organised groups openly embraced crop circle making as temporary landscape art while others continued working anonymously. Commercial phase: some makers produced commissioned formations for advertising campaigns, films and promotional events. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle The movement also developed its own community. Enthusiasts documented formations, published guidebooks, organised tours and debated authenticity. Researchers known as \"cereologists\" catalogued designs, while sceptics demonstrated construction techniques and investigated claims of paranormal features. Rather than eliminating belief, proven hoaxes simply became another point of disagreement. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Crop Circles the myth, the legend, the history Channel: The History Guy: History Deserves to Be Remembered Open on YouTube Why the hoax survived exposure Crop circles illustrate an unusual feature of successful Fortean hoaxes: exposure did not destroy public interest because the phenomenon had become larger than its creators. Several factors helped sustain it. First, Bower and Chorley never claimed responsibility for every formation worldwide. Believers therefore argued that genuine examples still existed alongside human imitations. This distinction allowed the broader mystery to survive despite convincing demonstrations of hoaxing. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Second, increasingly elaborate formations appeared after the confession, leading some observers to conclude that later designs were too sophisticated for pranksters. Sceptics countered that larger teams, better planning and improved technology naturally produced more complex artwork without requiring paranormal explanations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle Third, many claimed physical anomalies—such as unusual plant growth, magnetic effects or radiation—proved that some circles were different. Although a small number of researchers reported unusual findings, these claims have not achieved broad scientific acceptance, and critics have argued that studies often relied on unverified assumptions about which formations were \"genuine\". The scientific consensus remains that crop circles are adequately explained as human creations, whether made as pranks, art or commercial projects. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Crop circle Crop circle A Fortean lesson in participatory mystery Crop circles demonstrate that hoaxes do not always collapse when exposed. Sometimes they become traditions. Bower and Chorley's original circles encouraged others to imitate, improve and reinterpret the idea. Artists embraced the medium, tourists sought out new"},{"title":"Doyle","url":"/doyle/","description":"Arthur Conan Doyle turned a local fairy prank into a national controversy by lending it fame, confidence and spiritualist meaning.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Doyle How Conan Doyle Made Fairies News Doyle Doyle How Conan Doyle Made Fairies News Arthur Conan Doyle turned a local fairy prank into a national controversy by lending it fame, confidence and spiritualist meaning. Arthur Conan Doyle turned a local fairy prank into a national controversy by lending it fame, confidence and spiritualist meaning. entity How the photographs reached a famous sponsor Why Doyle's reputation mattered to believers How celebrity endorsement changed the case forteana hoaxes doyle Introduction Arthur Conan Doyle did not create the Cottingley fairy photographs, but he transformed them from a local curiosity into one of the most famous paranormal controversies of the twentieth century. His reputation as the creator of Sherlock Holmes, combined with his public commitment to spiritualism, gave the images a level of credibility they would probably never have achieved on their own. By publishing them in The Strand Magazine , defending them in lectures and later expanding the case in The Coming of the Fairies (1922), Doyle helped turn a private prank into an international debate about the reality of unseen worlds. His involvement remains one of the clearest examples in Fortean history of how celebrity endorsement can amplify a weak claim far beyond the strength of its evidence. [Wikipedia+2Arthur Conan Doyle Encyclopedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies How the photographs reached a famous sponsor The photographs first circulated within a small circle interested in fairy lore and spiritual ideas. Elsie Wright's mother, Polly, showed the pictures to members of the Theosophical movement, where they attracted the attention of Edward Gardner, a prominent Theosophist with a deep interest in nature spirits. Gardner recognised their potential significance and arranged for photographic examinations before introducing the material to Doyle. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies The timing was ideal from Doyle's perspective. He had already accepted a commission to write an article on fairies for the Christmas 1920 edition of The Strand Magazine . Instead of relying solely on folklore or personal testimony, he suddenly had what appeared to be photographic evidence. Letters from the period show that Doyle immediately regarded the images as potentially \"epoch making\" if their circumstances could be verified. Rather than treating them as an amusing curiosity, he saw them as possible proof of a wider spiritual reality. [Museum Crush] museumcrush.org arthur conan doyle and the cottingley fairies Museum CrushArthur Conan Doyle and the Cottingley Fairies4 Dec 2020 — Among the fascinating haul is the letter Conan Doyle penned to Gard... This convergence of Gardner's enthusiasm and Doyle's publishing opportunity was crucial. Without either man, the photographs might have remained an obscure local anecdote. Why Doyle's reputation mattered to believers Doyle's endorsement carried unusual weight because of his public identity. Millions of readers associated him with Sherlock Holmes, the fictional detective who solved mysteries through careful observation and logical reasoning. Although Holmes was fictional, many readers naturally associated Doyle himself with analytical thinking. By 1920, however, Doyle had become one of Britain's most prominent advocates of spiritualism. Following the immense losses of the First World War—including the deaths of close family members—he increasingly argued that psychic phenomena offered genuine evidence of survival after death. He believed fairies formed part of the same broader unseen reality as spirits and psychic communication. [Retrospect Journal] retrospectjournal.com the cottingley fairies and the spiritualist movement Retrospect JournalThe Cottingley Fairies and the Spiritualist Movement19 Nov 2023 — As a long time spiritualist, Conan Doyle prompted the... To believers, Doyle's support suggested that: a respected public intellectual had carefully examined the evidence; photography had independently confirmed the girls' claims; the fairy photographs fitted into a larger body of spiritualist evidence rather than standing alone. For sceptics, exactly the opposite lesson emerged. Doyle's commitment to spiritualism appeared to shape his interpretation of the evidence, making him more willing to accept extraordinary conclusions from ambiguous photographs. The contrast between the creator of the world's most famous fictional detective and his enthusiastic defence of fairy photographs has remained one of the defining ironies of the entire episode. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies The Cottingley Fairies: A Study in Deception Channel: Leeds University Library Galleries Open on YouTube How Doyle presented the photographs Doyle did more than simply express private belief. He actively promoted the photographs through influential media. His December 1920 article, \"Fairies Photographed\", appeared in The Strand Magazine , one of Britain's best known illustrated magazines. The publication reproduced the photographs in high quality and presented them as serious evidence deserving public consideration. The issue reportedly sold out rapidly, ensuring that the images reached an audience far beyond spiritualist circles. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies Doyle also emphasised expert opinion. Gardner had arranged for photography specialist Harold Snelling to inspect the negatives. Snelling concluded that the negatives showed no evidence of darkroom manipulation but carefully avoided saying the photographs genuinely depicted fairies. Kodak likewise found no proof of photographic tampering yet declined to certify them as authentic evidence of fairies, while Ilford considered there to be indications of fakery. Doyle nevertheless treated the combined assessments as broadly supportive of authenticity. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cottingley Fairies Cottingley Fairies This illustrates a critical misunderstanding that later became central to discussions of photographic evidence: proving that a negative has not been altered is not the same as proving that what stood before the camera was genuine. How celebrity endorsement changed the case Doyle's influence extended well beyond publication. His lectures, books and public statements encouraged newspapers around the world to report the story. What had begun as two girls entertaining their family became a matter of national and international discussion. Supporters debated fairy folklore, psychical research and photography, while critics questioned both the images and Doyle's judgement. [Arthur Conan Doyle Encyclopedia] arthur conan doyle.com Arthur Conan Doyle EncyclopediaThe Coming of the FairiesThis book contains reproductions of the famous Cottingley photographs, and gives... His endorsement also altered the burden of proof. Instead of asking why two children might create paper fairies, many discussions shifted towards explaining why an internationally respected author believed them. The authority attached to Doyle became part of the evidence in the public imagination, even though it was not evidence in the scientific sense. This mechanism appears repeatedly in Fortean history. Once a recognised figure publicly endorses an extraordinary claim, later discussion often centres as much on the reputation of the sponsor as on the original evidence itself. Sir Arthur Conan Doyle and the Cottingley Fairies Channel: National Science and Media Museum Open on YouTube Why Doyle remained convinced Doyle was not simply gullible. His interpretation reflected a broader worldview that had developed over many years. He believed humanity was entering a period in which psychic phenomena would become increasingly accepted through cumulative evidence. Fairies, in his view, represented one element of a larger invisible universe that spiritualism was gradually revealing. The Cottingley photographs therefore appeared to confirm beliefs he already held rather than forcing him to adopt an entirely new position. [Arthur Conan Doyle Encyclopedia] arthur conan doyle.com Arthur Conan Doyle EncyclopediaThe Coming of the FairiesThis book contains reproductions of the famous Cottingley photographs, and gives... Modern historians generally see this as an example of confirmation bias: the tendency to give greater weight to evidence that supports existing convictions while discounting conflicting information. Doyle genuinely attempted to gather supporting testimony and expert opinion, but he interpreted ambiguous findings through a framework that already assumed supernatural explanations were plausible. Why Conan Doyle Really Believed in Fairies (Cottingley, Part 2) Channel: The DreamForge Channel Open on YouTube The lasting lesson for Fortean history The Cottingley affair demonstrates that extraordinary claims often spread because of trusted intermediaries rather than because of compelling evidence alone. Doyle supplied the"},{"title":"Surgeon s Photo","url":"/surgeon-s-photo/","description":"The surgeon's photograph shows how one compelling image can define a monster legend even after its main evidence collapses.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Surgeon s Photo The Loch Ness Photo That Would Not Sink Surgeon s Photo Surgeon s Photo The Loch Ness Photo That Would Not Sink The surgeon's photograph shows how one compelling image can define a monster legend even after its main evidence collapses. The surgeon's photograph shows how one compelling image can define a monster legend even after its main evidence collapses. dataset evidence How the 1934 image became iconic The model submarine hoax explanation Why debate survived the exposure forteana hoaxes surgeon s photo Introduction The so called surgeon's photograph of the Loch Ness Monster is one of the most influential pieces of evidence in Fortean history precisely because it failed. Published in 1934, the image appeared to show the head and neck of an unknown creature rising from the waters of Loch Ness. For roughly six decades it was widely treated as the strongest visual evidence that the monster might exist. After the photograph was exposed as an elaborate hoax, however, belief in Nessie did not disappear. Instead, the image became a classic demonstration of how a single compelling photograph can shape public imagination long after its evidential value has collapsed. Within the history of Fortean claims, the surgeon's photograph is less important as evidence for a monster than as evidence for the extraordinary durability of iconic images. [The Guardian] theguardian.com fake by one of his co conspirators in a deathbed confession. 1934 Loch Ness monster photograph. This photograph reporting to be of the Lo... How the 1934 image became iconic When the photograph appeared in April 1934, it arrived at exactly the right cultural moment. Reports of mysterious sightings in and around Loch Ness had already generated intense newspaper interest. Unlike earlier eyewitness accounts, however, this claim appeared to offer something objective: a photograph. Its credibility rested on several reinforcing factors: It was associated with London physician Colonel Robert Kenneth Wilson, whose professional status suggested reliability. Wilson himself avoided publicity, leading newspapers to refer to it simply as the \"surgeon's photograph\", giving the impression of reluctant testimony rather than self promotion. The image was visually simple. Against calm water, a dark neck and small head stood out clearly enough for viewers to recognise a familiar \"plesiosaur like\" silhouette while remaining indistinct enough to invite interpretation. Newspapers reproduced the photograph repeatedly, making it the standard visual representation of Nessie for generations. [The Guardian] theguardian.com fake by one of his co conspirators in a deathbed confession. 1934 Loch Ness monster photograph. This photograph reporting to be of the Lo... Its composition also encouraged misjudgement. The version most readers encountered was tightly cropped, making the object appear much larger than it actually was. Later examination of the uncropped negative showed the supposed creature occupied only a tiny portion of the frame, dramatically altering perceptions of scale. Researchers also noted that the pattern of ripples matched a small nearby object rather than a large animal swimming at distance. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster The photograph therefore illustrates an important feature of visual evidence: viewers rarely estimate size directly from an image. Instead, they infer scale from framing and expectation, making carefully composed photographs surprisingly persuasive. Loch Ness Monster The Surgeon's Photo Channel: Naked Science Open on YouTube The model submarine hoax explanation The accepted explanation emerged gradually rather than through a single dramatic revelation. Doubts had circulated for decades, but the fullest account became widely known after Christian Spurling confessed to participating in the deception, confirming details that were later examined in specialist research on the photograph. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Loch Ness Monster Loch Ness Monster According to Spurling's account, the hoax originated with big game hunter Marmaduke Wetherell. Earlier, Wetherell had claimed to discover monster footprints around Loch Ness while working for the Daily Mail . Investigators soon determined that the tracks had been made using a hippopotamus foot umbrella stand, leaving Wetherell publicly embarrassed. The famous photograph was allegedly conceived as revenge against the newspaper that had ridiculed him. [The Guardian] theguardian.com fake by one of his co conspirators in a deathbed confession. 1934 Loch Ness monster photograph. This photograph reporting to be of the Lo... The reported construction was remarkably modest: A small toy submarine, purchased from Woolworth's, served as the floating base. Spurling sculpted a head and neck from wood putty attached to the toy. The model was photographed close to shore under conditions that concealed its true size. Robert Wilson presented the photograph to the press, allowing attention to focus on the image rather than on those who had created it. [The Guardian] theguardian.com fake by one of his co conspirators in a deathbed confession. 1934 Loch Ness monster photograph. This photograph reporting to be of the Lo... Ironically, the model's small size became one of the hoax's greatest strengths. Because little detail was visible, viewers mentally completed the missing information, projecting a much larger creature beyond the limits of the frame. Why debate survived the exposure The exposure of the hoax did not end belief in the Loch Ness Monster. Instead, it separated one famous piece of evidence from the broader legend. Several factors explain why the debate endured. First, believers often argued that exposing one fraudulent photograph did not invalidate every eyewitness report or every other alleged photograph. The surgeon's photograph became merely one data point within a much larger collection of claims. Second, the photograph had become culturally independent of its evidential status. By the time its origins were widely accepted, it had appeared in books, documentaries, tourist advertising and popular culture for decades. For many people, it represented Nessie regardless of whether it represented reality. [PBS] pbs.org The Beast of Loch Ness Birth of a Legend (3 1934, became the very image of the Loch Ness Monster. Birth of a Legend... Marmaduke Wetherell of hippo foot fame) claimed the surgeon's... Third, confessions themselves became subjects of debate. Although Spurling's account is now accepted by most historians and sceptical investigators, some enthusiasts questioned aspects of the reconstruction or suggested that not every detail could be independently verified. Such secondary disputes allowed discussion to continue even after the central explanation gained broad acceptance. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org nessie hoax redux Skeptical InquirerNessie Hoax ReduxMar 1, 1996 — (Wetherall had perpetrated an earlier Loch Ness hoax in which a set of “monster tracks”... This illustrates a recurring pattern in Fortean controversies: once a striking image acquires symbolic importance, disproving it rarely removes it from the cultural record. The photograph survives because it functions simultaneously as historical evidence, popular icon and cautionary tale. The Enigma of the Second Surgeon's Photograph (A Cryptozoology Report) Channel: Omni Viewer Open on YouTube The Loch Ness Monster Explained: Saints, Hoaxes, DNA Tests, and Why Nessie Endures Channel: Terra Glitch Open on YouTube What the case reveals about fragile iconic evidence The surgeon's photograph demonstrates several recurring weaknesses in visual evidence for extraordinary claims. Authority can outweigh technical scrutiny. Much of the image's initial credibility came from the apparent respectability of the man associated with it rather than from detailed analysis of the photograph itself. [British Online Archives] britishonlinearchives.com British Online Archives90 years: The first photo of the Loch Ness Monster12 Nov 2023 — The truth was that Wilson's image had been an elab... Photographs encourage certainty beyond what they actually show. The picture appears more informative than it is. In reality, it records only an ambiguous dark shape on water, with viewers supplying much of the interpretation themselves. Repetition creates credibility. Once reproduced in newspapers, books and television programmes, the image became familiar. Familiarity often increases perceived authenticity, even when supporting evidence remains weak. A famous image can outlive its factual foundation. Today the surgeon's photograph is remembered less as proof of a monster than as proof of how persuasive a single photograph can be. Its importance within Fortean history lies not in confirming Nessie, but in demonstrating how fragile iconic evidence can become the defining symbol of an enduring legend. As a result, the surgeon's photograph occupies a"},{"title":"Piltdown","url":"/piltdown/","description":"Piltdown Man shows how institutions can preserve a false anomaly when it fits a desired story too neatly.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Hoaxes","level":3,"text":"Piltdown Why Piltdown Man Lasted So Long Piltdown Piltdown Why Piltdown Man Lasted So Long Piltdown Man shows how institutions can preserve a false anomaly when it fits a desired story too neatly. Piltdown Man shows how institutions can preserve a false anomaly when it fits a desired story too neatly. entity Why the missing link claim seemed attractive How institutional prestige delayed correction What better testing did to the fossil story forteana hoaxes piltdown Introduction Piltdown Man occupies a distinctive place in Fortean history because it demonstrates that an extraordinary claim can survive not through overwhelming evidence, but through the authority of the institutions and experts that endorse it. Announced in 1912 as the long sought \"missing link\" between apes and humans, the Piltdown fossils appeared to solve one of the biggest scientific questions of the day. For more than four decades, despite persistent doubts from some researchers, the specimen remained influential before being exposed as a deliberate forgery in 1953. [Smithsonian Magazine+2Natural History Museum] smithsonianmag.com piltdown man paleoanthropologys april fools 176401927 Smithsonian MagazinePiltdown Man, Paleoanthropology's April Fool'sApr 1, 2011 — Scientists finally exposed Piltdown Man as a hoax, compos... Within the history of anomalous claims, Piltdown is not simply a scientific embarrassment. It illustrates a recurring prestige trap: when evidence fits prevailing expectations and is championed by respected authorities, weak or contradictory evidence can receive far more protection than it otherwise deserves. For Fortean studies, the case is valuable because it shifts attention from whether a claim is strange to how institutions decide what deserves belief. The Missing Link That Wasn’t Channel: PBS Eons Open on YouTube Why the missing link claim seemed attractive At the beginning of the twentieth century, the search for early human ancestors was one of science's most competitive fields. Researchers expected that a transitional form would combine an ape like jaw with a relatively modern human braincase. The Piltdown remains appeared to offer exactly that combination, making them seem to confirm influential evolutionary ideas rather than challenge them. [Science History Institute] sciencehistory.org Science History Institute The Problem of Piltdown Man Science History InstituteThe Problem of Piltdown ManMay 4, 2023 — Uncover the Piltdown Man hoax, the fossil that fooled scientists for de... Published: May 4, 2023 The discovery also carried national prestige. Britain had contributed enormously to evolutionary science through Charles Darwin, yet spectacular fossil human ancestors were increasingly being discovered elsewhere. A remarkably ancient \"English\" ancestor appealed not only because it filled an evolutionary gap but because it placed Britain at the centre of humanity's story. This coincidence between scientific expectation and national pride made the find unusually persuasive. [Science History Institute] sciencehistory.org Science History Institute The Problem of Piltdown Man Science History InstituteThe Problem of Piltdown ManMay 4, 2023 — Uncover the Piltdown Man hoax, the fossil that fooled scientists for de... Published: May 4, 2023 Importantly, the fossils were not accepted without criticism. Some anatomists questioned the unusual combination of skull and jaw almost immediately, arguing that the pieces did not naturally belong together. However, these objections competed against the authority of prominent supporters and against the broader desire for a convincing missing link. The case therefore became a prolonged debate rather than an immediate rejection. [johnhawks.net+2Science History Institute] johnhawks.net Contemporary skeptics could not openly Scientists doubted the Piltdown hoax from the beginningDecember 28, 2017 — 27 Dec 2017 — The hoax should have been obvious even in 1912... Published: December 28, 2017 Piltdown Man The Biggest Archaeological Mystery Channel: The Rest Is History Open on YouTube How institutional prestige delayed correction Piltdown demonstrates that prestige can shape how anomalies are evaluated. The fossils were introduced by respected figures associated with leading scientific institutions, particularly Arthur Smith Woodward of the British Museum (Natural History). Their endorsement encouraged museums, textbooks and public lectures to treat the discovery as an important reference point, even while technical disagreements continued. [Science History Institute] sciencehistory.org Science History Institute The Problem of Piltdown Man Science History InstituteThe Problem of Piltdown ManMay 4, 2023 — Uncover the Piltdown Man hoax, the fossil that fooled scientists for de... Published: May 4, 2023 Several reinforcing factors helped the forgery endure: Authority substituted for certainty. The reputation of recognised experts encouraged others to treat unresolved questions as minor rather than fundamental. The evidence matched prevailing theories. Because Piltdown appeared to support existing evolutionary expectations, many researchers attempted to interpret inconsistencies instead of asking whether the specimen itself might be fabricated. The original material remained unusually influential. Access to the fossils was limited compared with modern standards, slowing independent re examination. Alternative discoveries created confusion rather than immediate rejection. Genuine hominin fossils discovered in Africa and Asia increasingly conflicted with Piltdown, but many researchers initially tried to reconcile the competing evidence instead of discarding the English specimen. [Royal Society Publishing+2Science History Institute] royalsocietypublishing.org Royal Society PublishingNew genetic and morphological evidence suggests a single...by I De Groote · Cited by 72 — The Piltdown hoax stan... This does not mean that science simply accepted authority without question. Scepticism persisted throughout the Piltdown years. The lesson is subtler: prestige increased the burden of proof required to overturn an accepted narrative, allowing contradictory evidence to accumulate for decades before decisive testing occurred. [johnhawks.net] johnhawks.net Contemporary skeptics could not openly Scientists doubted the Piltdown hoax from the beginningDecember 28, 2017 — 27 Dec 2017 — The hoax should have been obvious even in 1912... Published: December 28, 2017 For Fortean readers, this distinction matters. Institutional endorsement can preserve an anomalous claim just as effectively as popular enthusiasm, particularly when the claim satisfies cultural or intellectual expectations. What better testing did to the fossil story The decisive change came not from rhetoric but from improved methods. After the Second World War, Kenneth Oakley applied fluorine testing to estimate the relative age of the bones. The results showed that the remains were far younger than expected and were inconsistent with a genuinely ancient ancestor. Further examination by Oakley, Joseph Weiner and Wilfrid Le Gros Clark revealed additional signs of fraud. [Natural History Museum+2Linda Hall Library] nhm.ac.uk Natural History MuseumPiltdown Manthe Piltdown remains were only 50,000 years old. Piltdown Man was an audacious fake and a sophisticated... Investigators found multiple lines of physical evidence pointing to deliberate fabrication: The jaw belonged to an orangutan rather than an early human. The teeth had been artificially filed to imitate human wear. Chemical staining had been applied to make bones resemble ancient fossils from the Piltdown gravels. Different pieces came from different origins rather than from a single prehistoric individual. [Natural History Museum+2Smithsonian Magazine] nhm.ac.uk Natural History MuseumPiltdown Manthe Piltdown remains were only 50,000 years old. Piltdown Man was an audacious fake and a sophisticated... The exposure of the hoax in 1953 transformed Piltdown from a celebrated fossil into one of the best known examples of scientific fraud. Rather than undermining science itself, the case illustrated the corrective value of independent testing, new analytical techniques and repeated examination of accepted evidence. [PBS+2Natural History Museum] pbs.org People and Discoveries: Piltdown Man is revealed as fakePiltdown man, one of the most famous fossil skulls in the world, has been decl... Later forensic work strengthened this picture. A comprehensive 2016 study combining DNA analysis, high resolution imaging and detailed morphological comparison concluded that the forged material was prepared with remarkable consistency, supporting the view that Charles Dawson was probably the principal architect of the fraud, although debate over possible assistance has not disappeared entirely. [Royal Society Publishing] royalsocietypublishing.org Royal Society PublishingNew genetic and morphological evidence suggests a single...by I De"},{"title":"Miracles","url":"/miracles/","description":"Miracle reports ask where testimony, religious meaning, medical uncertainty, and sceptical review meet.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Miracles How Should Miracle Claims Be Read? Miracles Miracles How Should Miracle Claims Be Read? Miracle reports ask where testimony, religious meaning, medical uncertainty, and sceptical review meet. Miracle reports ask where testimony, religious meaning, medical uncertainty, and sceptical review meet. policy intervention What is being claimed Witnesses, documents, and institutions Faith meaning versus proof forteana miracles Introduction Miracle claims enter Forteana at the point where an event is reported not merely as moving, symbolic or lucky, but as public evidence that ordinary explanation has failed. A cure after prayer, a statue said to weep blood, a vision witnessed by crowds, or an incorrupt body displayed for devotion may be meaningful inside a faith tradition, yet it also raises practical questions: who recorded it, who examined it, who benefits from it, and what would count as a non miraculous explanation? The central issue is not whether every miracle report is fraud or every believer is credulous. The stronger question is how miracle claims should be read when testimony, medical uncertainty, institutional judgement and sceptical review all meet. In Fortean terms, miracles are unusual because they are not only anomalous reports; they are also governed reports. Churches, doctors, journalists, police, courts, pilgrims, sceptical investigators and online audiences may all compete to decide whether a claim is a sign, a mystery, a mistake, a scam, or an unresolved event. What is being claimed? A miracle claim usually contains two layers. The first is the reported event: a tumour disappears, a paralysed person walks, a liquid appears on a statue, an image seems to move, a person reports a vision, or a prayer is followed by an unexpected recovery. The second is the interpretation: the event is taken to show divine action, saintly intercession, a sacred warning, or the truth of a devotional message. That distinction matters because many disputes arise when the two layers are blurred. A doctor may be willing to say that a cure is “medically unexplained” according to available knowledge, while a bishop, pilgrim or family may call the same cure a miracle. At Lourdes, for example, the official medical process is designed to examine whether a reported healing is medically credible and unexplained, but the religious judgement that a cure is miraculous belongs to Church authority rather than to the medical assessors themselves. The Sanctuary of Lourdes says its medical bureau was established in 1883 so that claimed cures would undergo “rigorous and collegiate medical assessment”, not simply leave the shrine as unexamined stories. [Lourdes France] lourdes france.org Lourdes France The Medical Bureau of the Sanctuary Lourdes FranceThe Medical Bureau of the Sanctuary Lourdes France.OrgDr Georges Fernand Dunot de Saint Maclou established the Bureau des... This makes miracle claims different from many other Fortean reports. A cryptid sighting may fade into folklore if no photograph or specimen appears. A miracle claim can become part of worship, pilgrimage, fundraising, canonisation, local identity and ecclesiastical authority. The event is not only assessed for factual accuracy; it is also placed inside a moral and devotional system. That is why miracle investigations often ask two questions at once: “What happened?” and “What should the faithful be allowed or encouraged to do with this story?” Why testimony is powerful but unstable Miracle reports often begin with testimony. A person says they were healed, a group says they saw a statue weep, or a family says a dying relative recovered after invoking a saint. Testimony is not worthless. Courts, medicine, history and journalism all rely on human reports. But miracle testimony carries extra pressure because the claimed event is usually rare, emotionally charged and surrounded by expectation. The classic philosophical problem was sharpened by David Hume, who argued that testimony for a miracle must be weighed against the ordinary experience that nature behaves regularly. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy summarises the debate as a question about whether testimony can ever be strong enough to justify belief in an event that appears to conflict with established regularities. Later philosophers have disputed Hume’s exact argument, but the practical problem remains: the rarer the claimed event, the more carefully testimony must be checked against error, exaggeration, coincidence, misdiagnosis and fraud. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy] plato.stanford.edu Stanford Encyclopedia of PhilosophyMiracles Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophyby T McGrew · 2010 · Cited by 83 — The argument for a mi... Modern psychology adds a less dramatic but important point. Eyewitness memory is not a perfect recording device. General reviews of eyewitness testimony note that people can misremember details, absorb suggestions, and reconstruct events in ways that feel sincere but are inaccurate. That does not mean miracle witnesses are lying. It means that sincerity and accuracy are not the same thing, especially when an event is interpreted in a religious setting where expectation, fear, hope and group attention are intense. [eCampusOntario Pressbooks] ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub Eyewitness Testimony and Memory Biases – PSYC 100Eyewitnesses can provide very compelling legal testimony, but r... For a Fortean reading, the most useful questions are concrete: Was the event recorded before it became famous, or only after a devotional story had formed? Are there medical notes, images, lab results, photographs, timestamps or independent documents? Did witnesses describe the same thing without prompting? Were alternative explanations investigated while the evidence was still fresh? Did the claim change as it passed through sermons, newspapers, social media or pilgrimage networks? A miracle claim becomes stronger when testimony is early, independent, consistent and attached to records that can be examined. It becomes weaker when the claim depends on memory alone, when details improve with retelling, or when investigators cannot separate the event from the later religious narrative. Medical cures: the hardest cases are often about uncertainty Healing claims are among the most persistent miracle reports because they involve high human stakes. A person is gravely ill, medical treatment seems insufficient, prayer or pilgrimage occurs, and the person recovers in a way that feels impossible to those involved. These stories are emotionally powerful because they are not abstract anomalies; they concern fear, pain, gratitude and survival. Lourdes is the most important modern case because it has built an unusually formal boundary between devotion and medical review. A 2012 academic re examination of Lourdes cures notes that hundreds of cures were historically reported and that the medical credibility of older cases depends heavily on retrospective diagnosis, record quality and changing standards of evidence. The authors argue that some religious healing experiences may be better approached with modern medical and neuropsychiatric methods rather than treated simply as either supernatural proof or nonsense. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Lourdes Medical Cures Revisited PMC NIHby B François · 2012 · Cited by 53 — In this study, we reexamine the Lourdes cures usin... The criteria used in Lourdes style assessment show why the process is strict. A cure must be tied to a serious and verified diagnosis, be unexpected, rapid, complete and lasting, and lack an adequate explanation according to current medical knowledge. The Catholic Medical Quarterly’s account of the Lourdes Medical Bureau describes the core questions as whether the person was truly sick, whether the person is truly cured, and whether a possible explanation exists. [cmq.org.uk] cmq.org.uk ording to current medical knowledge ». The...Read more... The sceptical counterpoint is not that all such recoveries are imaginary. It is that “unexplained” is not the same as “inexplicable in principle”. Medicine contains rare recoveries, diagnostic uncertainty, variable disease courses, immune effects, treatment interactions and incomplete records. Spontaneous remission of cancer, for instance, is rare but documented in medical literature, and researchers continue to study possible mechanisms rather than treating every unexpected regression as a supernatural event. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights This creates a genuine grey zone. A believer may see timing, prayer and personal transformation as religiously decisive. A clinician may see an unusual but medically open case. A sceptic may ask whether the original diagnosis was secure, whether treatments were still active, whether remission is known in that disease, and whether the recovery was really permanent. The same"},{"title":"Lourdes Cures","url":"/lourdes-cures/","description":"Lourdes shows how a healing can be medically unusual without automatically becoming public proof of divine intervention.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Lourdes Cures When Does a Cure Become a Miracle? Lourdes Cures Lourdes Cures When Does a Cure Become a Miracle? Lourdes shows how a healing can be medically unusual without automatically becoming public proof of divine intervention. Lourdes shows how a healing can be medically unusual without automatically becoming public proof of divine intervention. entity How Lourdes separates medical review from religious judgement What makes a cure rapid, complete and lasting Why unexplained recovery is not the same as proof forteana miracles lourdes cures Introduction Lourdes occupies a distinctive place in discussions of miracle claims because it deliberately separates two different questions: has an unexpected recovery occurred that medicine cannot currently explain, and does that recovery therefore count as a miracle? The answer to the first question is sought through medical investigation, while the second belongs to religious judgement. This distinction makes Lourdes one of the few settings where a major religious tradition has built a formal process intended to prevent every dramatic recovery from being immediately labelled miraculous. For students of Forteana , this distinction is especially important. Lourdes demonstrates that an event can remain medically unusual without automatically becoming public proof of divine intervention. The continuing debate is therefore not simply about whether remarkable cures occur, but about what level of evidence is required before an unexplained recovery can reasonably be interpreted as a miracle. How Lourdes separates medical review from religious judgement Since the nineteenth century, people reporting cures at Lourdes have been encouraged to present themselves to the Sanctuary's Medical Bureau rather than simply leave with a personal testimony. The purpose is not to prove miracles but to document diagnoses, collect medical records, examine patients and determine whether a reported recovery withstands clinical scrutiny. Over time this process has become increasingly structured, involving the Lourdes Medical Bureau and, for the most difficult cases, the International Medical Committee of Lourdes (CMIL), whose members include specialists from different countries and medical disciplines. [Lourdes France+2Lourdes France] lourdes france.org Lourdes FranceThe Medical Bureau of the SanctuaryThe members of the International Medical Committee of Lourdes have the task of assessing... A crucial feature of the system is that the medical investigators do not declare miracles. Their role is limited to deciding whether a cure is medically inexplicable according to current scientific knowledge. Only after that stage can the bishop responsible for the healed person's home diocese decide whether the event should be recognised by the Catholic Church as miraculous. That ecclesiastical decision may take into account spiritual testimony and wider pastoral considerations in addition to the medical findings. A medically unexplained cure therefore does not automatically become an official miracle. [PMC+2Lourdes France] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLourdes: A uniquely Catholic approach to medicine NIHby TJ Dichoso · 2015 · Cited by 5 — According to the Lambertini criteria, the disease must be serious and impossible to cure by... This institutional separation matters because it prevents medicine from being asked to answer theological questions while also preventing theology from bypassing medical examination. ‘Miracle Doctor’ of Lourdes Steps Down After 17 Years of Investigation SG News Channel: SG News Open on YouTube What makes a cure rapid, complete and lasting? The Lourdes process is intentionally conservative. Cases are expected to satisfy criteria derived from the eighteenth century work of Prospero Lambertini, later Pope Benedict XIV, which continue to influence Catholic evaluations of alleged miracles. Among the principal expectations are: the original diagnosis must be well established; the illness must have been serious, with little realistic prospect of spontaneous recovery; the recovery must be rapid or sudden rather than gradual in the expected course of treatment; the recovery must be complete, without significant remaining disability; the improvement must persist over many years rather than prove temporary; no conventional medical explanation should adequately account for the outcome. [PMC+2Lourdes France] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLourdes: A uniquely Catholic approach to medicine NIHby TJ Dichoso · 2015 · Cited by 5 — According to the Lambertini criteria, the disease must be serious and impossible to cure by... Long term follow up is therefore essential. Some investigations continue for many years before any conclusion is reached because relapses, revised diagnoses or new medical knowledge may alter how a case is understood. A dramatic improvement observed over days or weeks is not considered sufficient by itself. [PMC+2Lourdes France] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLourdes: A uniquely Catholic approach to medicine NIHby TJ Dichoso · 2015 · Cited by 5 — According to the Lambertini criteria, the disease must be serious and impossible to cure by... Why so few reported healings become recognised miracles The contrast between reported cures and recognised miracles illustrates how demanding the process has become. Thousands of healings have been reported since the apparitions associated with Lourdes, yet only a small fraction have ultimately been recognised by the Catholic Church as miracles after medical and ecclesiastical review. The large difference reflects strict filtering rather than an assumption that every testimony is either true or false. [Lourdes France+2Lourdes France] lourdes france.org Lourdes FranceLes miracles de LourdesMore than 80% of the cures recognised as miraculous concern women. The youngest person whose healing... Historical studies also show that standards have tightened considerably over time. Earlier investigations often relied on limited documentation, whereas twentieth century and modern cases increasingly incorporate imaging, laboratory testing, specialist review and prolonged observation . As diagnostic methods improved, the proportion of claims judged medically inexplicable fell sharply. This trend reflects both better medicine and greater caution rather than evidence that unusual recoveries have disappeared. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Lourdes Medical Cures Revisited PMC NIHby B François · 2012 · Cited by 53 — In the six years from 1933 through 1938, 48 cures... Why unexplained recovery is not the same as proof Perhaps the most important lesson from Lourdes is epistemological rather than theological. \"Unexplained\" and \"miraculous\" are not equivalent statements. An unexplained recovery may reflect: incomplete understanding of the disease; uncertainty about the original diagnosis; an exceptionally rare spontaneous remission; interactions between treatment and biological processes that are not fully understood; psychological or physiological mechanisms that remain difficult to measure. Medicine has repeatedly revised its understanding of diseases once thought impossible to survive. Consequently, medical investigators at Lourdes define their conclusions in deliberately limited terms: they judge whether current medical knowledge adequately explains the recovery, not whether supernatural intervention has been demonstrated. [PMC+2Sage Journals] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLourdes: A uniquely Catholic approach to medicine NIHby TJ Dichoso · 2015 · Cited by 5 — According to the Lambertini criteria, the disease must be serious and impossible to cure by... The Catholic Church likewise avoids claiming that every medically unexplained cure is miraculous. Even after favourable medical findings, bishops may decline to issue a formal declaration of miracle. This layered process acknowledges that theological interpretation involves different standards and questions from clinical investigation. [Lourdes France] lourdes france.org Lourdes FranceRecognition of a miracleThe support of the Lambertini criteria will assure that the cure has been found complete and lastin... Sanctuary of Our Lady of Lourdes: Investigating medically unexplained cures 60 Minutes Channel: 60 Minutes Open on YouTube What sceptics and supporters each find persuasive Lourdes remains a point of disagreement because the same evidence supports different interpretations. Supporters argue that the value of Lourdes lies precisely in its reluctance to proclaim miracles. They note that independent physicians, including doctors with no religious commitment, may participate in reviewing cases, and they view the extensive documentation and long term follow up as evidence of institutional caution rather than credulity. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCLourdes: A uniquely Catholic approach to medicine NIHby TJ Dichoso · 2015 · Cited by 5 — According to the Lambertini criteria, the disease must be serious and impossible to cure by... Sceptics respond that"},{"title":"Early Records","url":"/early-records/","description":"A miracle report is stronger when the first records are early, independent and less shaped by sermons, media or pilgrimage claims.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Early Records Did the Miracle Story Change in the Telling? Early Records Early Records Did the Miracle Story Change in the Telling? A miracle report is stronger when the first records are early, independent and less shaped by sermons, media or pilgrimage claims. A miracle report is stronger when the first records are early, independent and less shaped by sermons, media or pilgrimage claims. dataset evidence Why first reports matter most How newspapers, sermons and social media reshape claims A checklist for comparing early and late versions forteana miracles early records Introduction A miracle report is usually strongest at the moment it first enters the historical record, before it has been retold in sermons, devotional books, newspapers , pilgrim literature or social media. That does not mean later accounts are automatically false. Rather, historians, investigators and many religious authorities recognise that stories often become more detailed, emotionally powerful and theologically meaningful as they circulate. The key question is whether the original testimony can still be separated from the later narrative built around it. Within Fortean investigations, this distinction is especially important because the same event may exist in two forms: an early report describing what witnesses claimed to have seen, and a later devotional tradition interpreting the event as evidence of divine intervention. Comparing those layers helps reveal which details remained stable, which appeared only later, and whether changes reflect improved documentation, normal memory processes, community storytelling or deliberate embellishment. [The Australian National University] researchportalplus.anu.edu.au The Australian National UniversityMiracles, historical testimonies, and probabilitiesby A Tucker · 2005 · Cited by 35 — The topic and met... Why first reports matter most Historical investigation generally gives greater weight to testimony that is close in time to the alleged event. Early documents are less likely to have absorbed decades of interpretation, harmonisation or local legend. Several factors make early testimony particularly valuable: It is closer to witnesses' original memories. It predates organised campaigns promoting a shrine, saint or pilgrimage. It is less likely to incorporate details borrowed from earlier miracle stories. It allows later versions to be checked for additions and omissions. This approach is not unique to miracle research. It reflects standard principles of source criticism used throughout historical scholarship, where independent and contemporary evidence is generally considered stronger than later retellings . Historians also distinguish between an eyewitness statement, second hand testimony and accounts compiled generations later. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Historical method Historical method Importantly, an early record does not prove that a miracle occurred. It only reduces one category of uncertainty: the possibility that the account itself evolved substantially before anyone documented it. What often changes over time When researchers compare early and late versions of the same miracle claim, several recurring patterns appear. Early accounts frequently contain uncertainty. Witnesses disagree about times, numbers of observers or precise circumstances. They may describe an unexpected recovery, an unusual light or an unexplained event without assigning a definite religious meaning. Later devotional versions often become more structured. They may include: clearer dialogue; precise timings that were absent initially; symbolic numbers or biblical parallels; statements about conversions or mass emotional responses; claims that everyone present recognised the event immediately as miraculous. Such developments do not necessarily indicate fraud. Communities naturally organise stories into memorable forms, especially when the stories become part of religious teaching or local identity. Historians therefore ask whether these additions can be traced to independent evidence or appeared only after the event acquired devotional importance. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage Journals Miracles and Methods Two central concepts for this article are miracle and source critical strength. The concept of a miracle is the...Read more... Can Historians Investigate Miracles? Mike Licona Responds. Channel: Mike Licona Open on YouTube How newspapers, sermons and social media reshape claims A miracle narrative rarely remains confined to its original witnesses. Every stage of transmission introduces opportunities for reinterpretation. Newspapers Newspapers often provide some of the earliest independent records, particularly from the nineteenth century onwards. Contemporary reporting may preserve details later forgotten by believers and sceptics alike. However, newspapers also introduce editorial pressures. Headlines simplify complex events, journalists compress interviews, and competing publications may exaggerate unusual elements to attract readers. Comparing newspaper reports with private letters, parish documents or medical records can reveal whether dramatic details originated with witnesses or with editors. Sermons and devotional literature Once a reported event becomes a focus of devotion, retelling serves a different purpose from journalism. A sermon seeks spiritual meaning rather than forensic reconstruction. This shift often changes emphasis. Ambiguous observations become examples of faith, while contradictory testimony receives less attention. Stories may also be adapted to fit familiar religious themes, making them easier to remember but harder to compare with the earliest documentation. Researchers studying miracle traditions therefore distinguish between testimony intended to report events and literature intended to inspire belief or devotion. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMedicine and the Inquiry on Miracles in Early Modern by A Laverda · 2019 · Cited by 3 — The inquiry on miracles in the early modern canonization process revealed a fundamental cooperation... Social media Modern miracle claims can evolve far more quickly than historical ones. Videos, screenshots and personal testimonies may circulate globally within hours, often detached from their original context. Online sharing introduces additional complications: edited or shortened clips; repeated copying without attribution; claims that grow more dramatic as they are reposted; mixing verified information with speculation; algorithms favouring emotionally engaging narratives. Research on online rumours consistently finds that rapidly spreading claims require careful verification because repetition alone does not establish accuracy. Independent confirmation remains more valuable than viral popularity. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Determining the Veracity of Rumours on Twitter Determining the Veracity of Rumours on TwitterNovember 19, 2016... Published: November 19, 2016 Miracle, Luck or Chance? The Story of God Channel: National Geographic Open on YouTube Case patterns from miracle investigations Although every alleged miracle is different, investigators repeatedly encounter similar contrasts between early testimony and mature tradition. One common pattern begins with an unexpected medical recovery. The earliest documentation may simply state that a patient's improvement surprised physicians. Years later, the same recovery may be retold as an instantaneous cure accompanied by extraordinary signs, even though those additional details do not appear in the first medical records. Another pattern involves reported apparitions or weeping statues . Initial witnesses sometimes disagree about exactly what they observed. As pilgrimage develops, later accounts often become increasingly consistent, reflecting a shared community narrative rather than independent recollection. Established investigation procedures recognise this possibility. For example, the medical process associated with reported healings at Lourdes deliberately attempts to preserve early clinical evidence before theological interpretation proceeds, separating questions of medical explanation from later declarations of miracle. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCMedicine and the Inquiry on Miracles in Early Modern by A Laverda · 2019 · Cited by 3 — The inquiry on miracles in the early modern canonization process revealed a fundamental cooperation... Why memory and storytelling naturally change testimony Changes in miracle stories do not always require deception. Modern research on memory shows that recollection is reconstructive rather than photographic. People often remember the central meaning of emotionally significant events while unintentionally altering peripheral details. Discussion with family members, exposure to media coverage or repeated public retelling can gradually reshape individual memories until witnesses sincerely recall details absent from their earliest statements. This process becomes especially powerful"},{"title":"Witness Memory","url":"/witness-memory/","description":"Sincere witnesses can still misremember details when expectation, fear and group attention shape a reported vision.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Witness Memory Why Miracle Witnesses Can Be Sincere but Wrong Witness Memory Witness Memory Why Miracle Witnesses Can Be Sincere but Wrong Sincere witnesses can still misremember details when expectation, fear and group attention shape a reported vision. Sincere witnesses can still misremember details when expectation, fear and group attention shape a reported vision. mechanism How group settings affect remembered miracles Early testimony versus later devotional retellings Questions that test whether accounts stayed stable forteana miracles witness memory Introduction Reports of miracles witnessed by crowds often begin with honest testimony rather than photographs, measurements or formal investigations. That does not mean the witnesses are dishonest, nor does it automatically validate the reported event. Modern research on eyewitness memory shows that people can sincerely remember experiences inaccurately, especially when they are emotionally aroused, surrounded by others, and trying to interpret an unexpected event. Memory is reconstructive rather than a literal recording, so later conversations, expectations and repeated retellings can gradually reshape what individuals believe they saw. [PMC+2Frontiers] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 268 — Findings from basic psychological researc... Within debates about miracle claims, this psychological perspective offers a competing explanation for some crowd vision reports. It does not claim that every reported vision is mistaken. Instead, it explains why multiple sincere witnesses may eventually produce accounts that appear mutually reinforcing while still containing distortions introduced after the original experience . How group settings affect remembered miracles Crowds create conditions that can influence both perception at the time of an event and memory afterwards. Unlike isolated observation , a public religious gathering often includes shared expectations, emotional intensity and constant interaction among participants. Several well established psychological mechanisms are relevant: Selective attention. During emotionally significant events, people often focus on one striking feature while overlooking surrounding details. Stress and heightened arousal can narrow attention rather than improve complete recall. [Frontiers] frontiersin.org full The present review will discuss two such important factors, Expectation effects. Pilgrims attending a site where visions or miraculous signs are anticipated may interpret ambiguous sensory information in similar ways because they share background beliefs and expectations. Memory conformity. After witnessing an event, people naturally discuss what happened. Research shows that details mentioned by one witness can later become incorporated into another witness's memory, even when those details were not originally observed. [DigitalCommons+2Frontiers] digitalcommons.unl.edu Memory Conformity Between Eyewitnessesby F Gabbert · 2012 · Cited by 39 — In summary, memory conformity effects are often d... Source monitoring errors. People may later remember a detail but forget whether they personally saw it, heard it from another witness or read it in a later account. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect by DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monit... These processes are especially important for reports involving distant lights, changing cloud formations, subtle movements of statues or brief visionary experiences where the original sensory evidence may already have been ambiguous. Scott Fraser: The problem with eyewitness testimony Channel: TED · Views: 246.2K · Uploaded: September 2012 · Length: 20 minutes Open on YouTube Why agreement between witnesses is not always independent evidence One common argument for a miracle vision is that many witnesses reported similar experiences. While independent agreement can strengthen historical evidence, psychological research warns that apparent agreement is not necessarily independent. Studies of co witness discussions repeatedly demonstrate that people influence each other's recollections. After conversation, witnesses often produce accounts that become increasingly alike. Importantly, participants usually do not experience themselves as inventing details. They commonly report altered memories with genuine confidence. [DigitalCommons+2Goldsmiths, University of London] digitalcommons.unl.edu Memory Conformity Between Eyewitnessesby F Gabbert · 2012 · Cited by 39 — In summary, memory conformity effects are often d... This has two implications for crowd miracle reports: Similar testimony collected after extensive discussion may partly reflect shared reconstruction rather than independent observation. The longer witnesses exchange stories before formal documentation, the harder it becomes to separate original perception from later consensus. For historians, the timing of interviews therefore matters almost as much as the content of the testimony. Early testimony versus later devotional retellings Miracle traditions often develop over months or years. During that period, accounts may become more detailed, internally consistent and theologically meaningful. Memory research predicts several reasons why this can happen without deliberate deception. Immediately after an unusual event, witnesses frequently describe uncertainty, fragmented observations or incomplete impressions. As time passes, later retellings tend to become more coherent because people reconstruct memories using repeated narration, community discussion and existing beliefs. Every retrieval of a memory creates an opportunity for modification before the memory is stored again. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 268 — Findings from basic psychological researc... Devotional communities may also unintentionally reinforce particular versions of events by repeating certain details in sermons, pilgrim accounts, newspapers or books. Once these details become familiar, witnesses may later experience them as personal memories rather than learned information—a classic example of source confusion. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCA Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect by DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — One cognitive account of the misinformation effect is that memory errors are due to source monit... This does not imply fraud. It reflects the ordinary way autobiographical memory operates under repeated rehearsal. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Emotion, stress and certainty A common misunderstanding is that emotionally powerful experiences produce perfectly accurate memories. Research suggests a more complicated picture. Emotion often increases confidence that an event occurred while reducing accuracy for peripheral details. Witnesses may remain highly certain that something extraordinary happened yet disagree about timing, colours, positions, spoken words or the sequence of events. Acute stress also affects attention during encoding, making later reconstruction more likely. [Frontiers+2PMC] frontiersin.org full The present review will discuss two such important factors, Confidence should therefore not be treated as a direct measure of historical accuracy. Highly confident witnesses can still make sincere memory errors. “It’s NONSENSE” — Alex O’Connor ENDS Pastor’s Career Live on Stage Channel: Skeptic Scriptura Open on YouTube Questions that test whether accounts stayed stable Researchers and historians examining miracle reports often look beyond whether witnesses believed what they said. They ask whether the testimony remained stable before opportunities for memory contamination arose. Useful questions include: Were witnesses interviewed separately before discussing the event together? Are the earliest written accounts available, or only later summaries? Did new details appear gradually in later retellings? Do independent witnesses describe the same central event while differing on secondary details, or do later accounts become unusually uniform? Were interviews conducted soon after the event, before extensive publicity or devotional interpretation? These questions do not determine whether a miracle occurred. Instead, they help assess how much confidence should be placed in the historical reliability of specific descriptions. What eyewitness memory research can and cannot explain Psychological mechanisms explain how sincere people may misremember or reshape unusual experiences, particularly in emotionally charged group settings. They account for many features commonly found in crowd vision reports, including increasing agreement after"},{"title":"Rare Recovery","url":"/rare-recovery/","description":"Rare recoveries matter because they force investigators to ask whether a cure is impossible, undocumented or just not yet explained.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Rare Recovery Can Rare Recovery Explain a Miracle Cure? Rare Recovery Rare Recovery Can Rare Recovery Explain a Miracle Cure? Rare recoveries matter because they force investigators to ask whether a cure is impossible, undocumented or just not yet explained. Rare recoveries matter because they force investigators to ask whether a cure is impossible, undocumented or just not yet explained. mechanism Why spontaneous remission complicates miracle claims The role of diagnosis, treatment and timing How sceptics and believers read the same recovery forteana miracles rare recovery Introduction Rare recoveries occupy a unique place in debates about miracle healing because they force the same basic question every time: did something truly impossible happen, or did medicine encounter an uncommon natural event that it cannot yet fully explain? The existence of spontaneous remission does not prove that miracle claims are false, but it does mean that an unexpected recovery cannot, by itself, demonstrate supernatural intervention. Equally, the fact that a remission is medically unexplained does not mean it has no natural cause. This tension makes spontaneous remission one of the strongest sceptical tests applied to healing claims, especially in Fortean discussions where extraordinary recoveries are often presented as evidence of divine or paranormal action. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights by G Radha · 2021 · Cited by 129 — Spontaneous remission (SR) of cancer is a rare but well documented phenomenon. Mechanisms of SR are... Why spontaneous remission complicates miracle claims In medicine, spontaneous remission refers to the partial or complete disappearance of a disease without treatment, or after treatment considered insufficient to account for the improvement. The term is most commonly discussed in cancer, although unexpected remissions have also been reported in autoimmune disorders, infectious diseases and some neurological conditions. These events are rare, but they are well documented enough that physicians accept they occur, even if the biological mechanisms remain uncertain. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights by G Radha · 2021 · Cited by 129 — Spontaneous remission (SR) of cancer is a rare but well documented phenomenon. Mechanisms of SR are... For investigators of miracle claims, this creates an important logical challenge. If a disease occasionally improves without a known medical explanation, then any claimed miraculous cure must first rule out the possibility that it belongs to this small but genuine category of natural recoveries. The sceptical argument is therefore not that spontaneous remission explains every miracle claim. Rather, it demonstrates that surprising recoveries are part of the natural history of disease. Before invoking supernatural intervention, investigators ask whether the case is exceptional because: the original diagnosis was wrong; the disease naturally fluctuated; an earlier treatment worked after an unexpected delay; the recovery represents a genuine spontaneous remission; [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous remission Spontaneous remission or the recovery remains unexplained even after careful investigation. Only after these alternatives have been examined does the question of a miracle become meaningful. The role of diagnosis, treatment and timing The strongest healing claims depend less on the recovery itself than on the quality of the medical documentation surrounding it. Was the disease correctly diagnosed? Many apparent miracles have later proved to involve diagnostic uncertainty. Imaging may have been misinterpreted, laboratory results may have been incomplete, or later knowledge may reveal that the patient never had the disease originally believed to be present. Because of this, sceptical investigators place enormous weight on records made before any claimed healing occurred. Biopsy results, pathology reports and repeated clinical examinations generally provide stronger evidence than retrospective testimony alone. Inside Cancer's Medical Miracle Dr. Sharmin on Spontaneous Remission and Immunotherapy Channel: Health Wealth & Lifestyle Open on YouTube Could treatment have worked after all? Another frequent complication is delayed therapeutic effect. A patient may have received surgery, antibiotics, chemotherapy, radiotherapy or immune based treatment weeks or months before the apparent miracle. If the disease subsequently disappears after prayer or pilgrimage, believers may naturally associate the timing with the religious event. Physicians, however, must also consider whether the earlier treatment produced a slower than expected response. Modern cancer therapy has reinforced this caution. Some immunotherapies can trigger delayed tumour regression long after treatment begins, making simple chronological associations unreliable evidence of causation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights by G Radha · 2021 · Cited by 129 — Spontaneous remission (SR) of cancer is a rare but well documented phenomenon. Mechanisms of SR are... Does timing create a false impression? Human beings naturally connect dramatic events occurring close together. If recovery follows a prayer service, blessing or visit to a shrine, it is psychologically compelling to treat one event as causing the other. Yet spontaneous remission demonstrates that recoveries sometimes occur unexpectedly even without any religious intervention. The mere sequence of \"prayer followed by recovery\" therefore cannot distinguish miracle from coincidence. This does not disprove religious interpretations, but it means timing alone cannot establish them. What medicine knows about spontaneous remission Although spontaneous remission remains uncommon, researchers have proposed several mechanisms that may contribute to it in at least some diseases. The immune system is the leading candidate. Numerous documented cancer regressions have occurred after severe infections accompanied by high fever, suggesting that unusually intense immune activation may occasionally recognise and destroy malignant cells. This historical observation helped inspire later research into cancer immunotherapy. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights by G Radha · 2021 · Cited by 129 — Spontaneous remission (SR) of cancer is a rare but well documented phenomenon. Mechanisms of SR are... Other proposed contributors include: changes in tumour blood supply; programmed cell death within cancer cells; hormonal changes; alterations in the tumour microenvironment; unknown genetic or immune interactions. Importantly, none of these mechanisms explains every documented remission. Researchers generally regard spontaneous remission as a descriptive label rather than a single disease process. It identifies an unexpected outcome without claiming a single universal cause. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCThe spontaneous remission of cancer: Current insights by G Radha · 2021 · Cited by 129 — Spontaneous remission (SR) of cancer is a rare but well documented phenomenon. Mechanisms of SR are... When Modern Medicine Meets Spontaneous Healing: Dr. Jeff Rediger Channel: OncoDaily TV Open on YouTube How sceptics and believers read the same recovery Perhaps the most interesting feature of spontaneous remission is that exactly the same medical event can support very different interpretations. A sceptical investigator may argue: [Spontaneous remission] Wikipedia Spontaneous remission on is rare but real; unexplained does not mean supernatural; incomplete medical knowledge leaves room for future explanation. A religious believer may instead argue: the remission occurred immediately after prayer; similar recoveries cluster around particular shrines or devotions; divine action may work through natural biological processes rather than against them. Neither interpretation changes the underlying medical facts. Instead, they differ over what counts as the best explanation once those facts have been established. This distinction is especially important because medicine is generally cautious about declaring anything impossible. A physician may honestly conclude that a recovery is medically inexplicable while making no judgement about whether it was miraculous. Why recognised miracle investigations demand more than an unexplained cure Formal investigations into miracle claims often incorporate spontaneous remission directly into their assessment procedures. For example, investigations associated with healing claims at Lourdes require independent confirmation of the diagnosis, evidence that the illness was serious, complete and lasting recovery, and careful consideration of whether any recognised medical explanation—including spontaneous remission—could account for the improvement. Medical experts may conclude that a cure is scientifically"},{"title":"Vatican Rules","url":"/vatican-rules/","description":"The Vatican's newer approach shows how institutions may permit devotion while avoiding a firm declaration that a claim is supernatural.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Vatican Rules Why the Church Rarely Says Supernatural Now Vatican Rules Vatican Rules Why the Church Rarely Says Supernatural Now The Vatican's newer approach shows how institutions may permit devotion while avoiding a firm declaration that a claim is supernatural. The Vatican's newer approach shows how institutions may permit devotion while avoiding a firm declaration that a claim is supernatural. policy intervention What the newer norms changed about approval Why devotion can be allowed without certification Hoaxes, exploitation and online amplification forteana miracles vatican rules Introduction Reports of visions, apparitions and other alleged miracles now spread worldwide within hours through social media, livestreams and messaging apps, often attracting pilgrims, donations and intense public debate before any formal investigation has begun. In response, the Vatican fundamentally changed its approach in 2024. Rather than focusing on declaring whether a reported event is genuinely supernatural, the new rules emphasise careful discernment, pastoral guidance and protection of the faithful from deception, manipulation and financial exploitation. This marks an important shift within the wider study of miracle claims: the Church now accepts that modern communications have changed both the speed and the risks of alleged supernatural phenomena, making governance as important as theological judgement. [Vatican] vatican.va rc ddf doc 20240517 norme fenomeni soprannaturali en Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith promulgates the following Norms for Proceeding in the Discernment of Alleged Supernatural Phenome... Why the Church Rarely Says \"Supernatural\" Now The Vatican's new Norms for Proceeding in the Discernment of Alleged Supernatural Phenomena , issued by the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith in May 2024, replaced guidance that had remained largely unchanged since 1978. The earlier norms assumed investigations could proceed before reports became internationally famous. That assumption no longer reflects reality. The new document explicitly notes that alleged visions, apparitions and miraculous signs now circulate rapidly through modern communications, often generating widespread devotion, commercial activity or controversy long before Church authorities have interviewed witnesses or examined evidence. The Vatican therefore concluded that older procedures were no longer adequate for the internet age. [Vatican] vatican.va rc ddf doc 20240517 norme fenomeni soprannaturali en Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith promulgates the following Norms for Proceeding in the Discernment of Alleged Supernatural Phenome... Perhaps the most striking change is procedural rather than theological. As a general rule, neither a diocesan bishop nor even the Holy See will formally declare that an alleged phenomenon is of supernatural origin. Such declarations become exceptional rather than expected. Instead, the emphasis shifts towards determining whether devotion surrounding the event is spiritually beneficial and free from serious doctrinal or moral problems. [Vatican News] vaticannews.va dicastery doctrine faith supernatural phenomena norms Vatican NewsVatican releases new norms on alleged supernatural...16 May 2024 — The Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith releases a do... Published: May 2024 This reflects an important distinction. The Church is no longer asking primarily, \"Can we prove this came directly from God?\" Instead, it more often asks, \"Can Catholics safely participate in devotion associated with this claim without being misled?\" Why the Vatican Changed Rules for Evaluating Supernatural Phenomena Jimmy Akin's Mysterious World Channel: Jimmy Akin Open on YouTube What the Newer Norms Changed About Approval Rather than offering a simple approval or rejection system, the 2024 norms establish several possible outcomes that allow for more nuanced responses. The most positive ordinary judgement is nihil obstat (\"nothing stands in the way\"). This does not certify that visions or apparitions are supernatural. Instead, it indicates that no significant doctrinal, moral or pastoral problems have been found and that devotional practices may continue under appropriate oversight. [Vatican Press+2America Magazine] press.vatican.va Vatican PressNorms of the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith...17 May 2024 — The document of the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the... Published: May 2024 Other possible conclusions permit increasingly cautious responses, including: recognising positive spiritual fruits while identifying aspects needing clarification; requiring correction of problematic teachings or messages; restricting promotion of the phenomenon; prohibiting public devotion when serious concerns exist; declaring that the reported events clearly should not be promoted because evidence indicates fraud, manipulation or serious error. [Vatican Press] press.vatican.va Vatican PressNorms of the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith...17 May 2024 — The document of the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the... Published: May 2024 The Vatican also strengthened central oversight. Local bishops still conduct initial investigations, but the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith must review and approve their conclusions before they become official. This is intended to improve consistency between dioceses and reduce contradictory local decisions. [Vatican+2EWTN News] vatican.va rc ddf doc 20240517 norme fenomeni soprannaturali en Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith promulgates the following Norms for Proceeding in the Discernment of Alleged Supernatural Phenome... Why Devotion Can Be Allowed Without Certification One aspect of the new policy surprises many observers. The Church now explicitly accepts that people may benefit spiritually from prayer, pilgrimage and devotion associated with a reported apparition even when no judgement has been made that the underlying event was supernatural. This distinction rests on longstanding Catholic teaching regarding private revelations. Catholics are never obliged to believe alleged apparitions or visions, even when they receive favourable treatment from Church authorities. Public revelation is considered complete with Christ; later private revelations may encourage faith but cannot add new doctrine. [USCCB] usccb.org vatican publishes new norms discern alleged supernatural phenomena Vatican publishes new norms to discern alleged...17 May 2024 — The Vatican has published new norms for the church to discern allege... Published: May 2024 Consequently, the Vatican now separates two questions: whether the reported event can be verified as supernatural; whether the associated devotional practices help people live the Christian faith without doctrinal error or harmful behaviour. Under this approach, a shrine or pilgrimage may receive pastoral support because of its spiritual fruits while the supernatural character of the original reports remains officially unresolved. This avoids presenting uncertain historical claims as established fact while recognising that prayer and religious practice may still bear positive results. [Vatican News+2America Magazine] vaticannews.va dicastery doctrine faith supernatural phenomena norms Vatican NewsVatican releases new norms on alleged supernatural...16 May 2024 — The Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith releases a do... Published: May 2024 Vatican reveals new rules for the supernatural (Vatican/Global) 17/May/2024 Channel: Mark 1333 Open on YouTube Hoaxes, Exploitation and Online Amplification The internet is not treated merely as a faster way of spreading news. In the Vatican's analysis, digital media changes the entire environment in which miracle claims emerge. A dramatic video of a weeping statue or a visionary's livestream can reach millions before investigators inspect the object, interview witnesses or examine possible natural explanations. Viral attention may generate donations, merchandise, organised pilgrimages and devoted online communities that become difficult to moderate even if later evidence proves the claim unreliable. [AP News] apnews.com The updated guidelines, replacing those from 1978, aim to protect the faithful from potential hoaxes and abuse, emphasizing that exploiti... For this reason, the norms repeatedly warn against: financial exploitation of believers; manipulation of vulnerable people; sectarian movements centred on charismatic personalities; fabricated messages presented as divine commands; behaviour that divides Church communities. The document goes further by stating that the use of alleged supernatural experiences to control people or facilitate abuse constitutes a particularly grave moral matter that may carry canonical consequences. [Vatican] vatican.va rc ddf doc 20240517 norme fenomeni soprannaturali en Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith promulgates the following Norms for Proceeding in the Discernment of Alleged Supernatural Phenome..."},{"title":"Weeping Statues","url":"/weeping-statues/","description":"Claims about bleeding or weeping statues rise or fall on whether investigators can examine the object before devotion rewrites the story.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Miracles","level":3,"text":"Weeping Statues What Should Count as Evidence for a Weeping Statue? Weeping Statues Weeping Statues What Should Count as Evidence for a Weeping Statue? Claims about bleeding or weeping statues rise or fall on whether investigators can examine the object before devotion rewrites the story. Claims about bleeding or weeping statues rise or fall on whether investigators can examine the object before devotion rewrites the story. dataset evidence What is actually claimed when liquid appears Why timing and access matter for testing Fraud, contamination and devotional pressure forteana miracles weeping statues Introduction Reports of weeping or bleeding statues are among the most visually compelling miracle claims because they appear to produce physical evidence rather than relying solely on testimony. If a statue is said to shed tears, blood, oil or another liquid, it seems that investigators should be able to collect samples, examine the object and determine what happened. In practice, however, the strongest evidence is often available only for a short period. Once pilgrims, clergy, journalists and curious visitors arrive, the scene changes, the object may be cleaned, moved or enclosed, and the original conditions can no longer be reconstructed. For that reason, the central question is often less \"Did the statue weep?\" than \"What evidence was preserved before the story overtook the event?\" Across many reported cases, the timing of scientific access has proved as important as the laboratory results themselves. What is actually claimed when liquid appears? A weeping statue claim normally contains several distinct assertions that should be evaluated separately: A liquid genuinely emerged from the statue rather than being applied externally. The liquid appeared without an ordinary physical cause such as condensation, seepage or damage. The substance itself is unusual, for example human blood rather than coloured water or oil. The appearance of the liquid is interpreted as evidence of supernatural intervention. These claims require different kinds of evidence. Establishing that a liquid is human blood, for example, does not establish how it reached the statue. Likewise, showing that no obvious hidden reservoir exists inside a statue does not exclude external application or more subtle physical mechanisms. Investigators therefore need evidence about both the material and the circumstances under which it appeared. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Weeping statue Weeping statue Why timing and access matter for testing Fresh physical evidence is unusually important in these cases because many potentially decisive observations disappear quickly. An ideal investigation would document the object before anyone touches it, photograph the surrounding environment, record temperature and humidity, preserve the liquid using forensic procedures, and maintain a clear chain of custody. The statue would then undergo careful imaging and material examination before repeated public handling altered its condition. Instead, many famous reports follow the opposite sequence. The liquid is first noticed by devotees, news spreads rapidly, large numbers of visitors gather, and only afterwards do investigators attempt to reconstruct events. By then: surfaces may have been wiped or kissed; candles, flowers or oils may have contaminated the area; numerous people may have handled the object; environmental conditions have changed; the liquid has dried or been removed; later observations cannot recreate the original circumstances. Because these events often occur in active places of worship, preserving a pristine forensic scene can conflict with devotional practice. The very behaviour inspired by belief may unintentionally reduce the quality of later evidence. [The Guardian] theguardian.com The GuardianThe crying game8 Dec 2000 — When villagers at Civitavecchia witnessed tears of blood falling down the face of a souvenir Mado... Joe Nickell Why Investigate the Paranormal For Good Reason Channel: JamesRandiFoundation Open on YouTube What can science actually test? Laboratory analysis can answer some questions quite well while leaving others unresolved. Scientists may examine: The liquid itself. Chemical testing can identify water, oil, blood or other substances. Biological material. Blood typing or DNA analysis may determine whether the material is human and potentially identify its source. The statue's construction. X rays, CT imaging or other non destructive methods can look for cavities, tubes or mechanical devices. Surface structure. Microscopy may reveal cracks, porous materials or pathways through which moisture can travel. Environmental conditions. Humidity and temperature measurements help assess whether condensation or capillary action could explain the appearance. These methods are powerful, but they cannot directly answer whether an unexplained event was supernatural. They evaluate physical mechanisms, not theological interpretations. The Civitavecchia case: how evidence became contested The reported weeping Madonna at Civitavecchia, Italy, illustrates both the strengths and limitations of physical investigation. In 1995 a small plaster statue was reported to have wept tears of blood before numerous witnesses, attracting intense public attention. Authorities arranged scientific examinations. Radiological imaging reportedly found no concealed mechanical devices, while laboratory testing identified the liquid as human blood of male origin. Investigators requested DNA samples from members of the owner's household for comparison, but these were not provided. The absence of a complete forensic chain meant that the source of the blood could not be conclusively established. [Port Mobility Civitavecchia+2The Guardian] civitavecchia.portmobility.it Port Mobility Civitavecchia The Madonna of Civitavecchia Port Mobility CivitavecchiaThe Madonna of CivitavecchiaSeptember 16, 2015 — the Madonnina, in fact, began suddenly to weep blod in his ha... Published: September 16, 2015 The case demonstrates an important distinction. Finding genuine blood answers only one question. It does not determine whether the blood appeared naturally, was deliberately applied, or resulted from another process. Once early opportunities for controlled observation passed, later debate centred largely on interpretation rather than new physical evidence. Natural mechanisms investigators consider Claims of weeping statues are examined against several ordinary physical explanations before more extraordinary interpretations are considered. Condensation. Temperature differences between materials can produce droplets that appear in predictable locations, especially around smoother or cooler surfaces such as painted eyes. Capillary action. Porous plaster, stone or ceramic can draw moisture through microscopic channels. Small defects in glaze or paint may cause liquid to emerge at apparently significant points. Chemist Luigi Garlaschelli has argued that some reported cases can be reproduced using these principles. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Weeping statue Weeping statue Material degradation. Ageing varnishes, adhesives or restoration materials may release oils or coloured fluids under changing environmental conditions. External contamination. Liquids can be transferred unintentionally through repeated touching, cleaning products, devotional oils or candle residues. These explanations are not assumptions that every case is mundane. Rather, they are hypotheses that can often be tested directly if investigators gain prompt access. Our Lady of Sorrows: Seven Sorrows, Akita & the Tears of Mary Channel: Sensus Fidelium Open on YouTube Fraud, contamination and devotional pressure Some reported weeping statues have later been shown to involve deliberate human intervention. Investigations have uncovered examples in which blood, paint or other substances were applied to religious images. In several cases, forensic testing linked biological material to specific individuals or identified ordinary substances such as scented olive oil rather than unexplained fluids. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Weeping statue Weeping statue Intentional fraud is not the only concern. Even sincere believers can unintentionally complicate an investigation: enthusiastic touching introduces contamination; repeated cleaning removes microscopic traces; protective relocation changes environmental conditions; widespread publicity encourages retrospective embellishment of witness accounts; institutional caution may delay or restrict independent examination. These factors mean that weak evidence does not necessarily imply deception, but it does make later conclusions less certain. Why churches are often cautious Contrary to popular assumptions, many church authorities treat reported weeping statues with considerable scepticism. Within the Roman Catholic tradition, local bishops commonly order investigations precisely because false claims can damage public trust and"},{"title":"Mistakes","url":"/mistakes/","description":"Many Fortean stories begin when ordinary objects, animals, or lights are seen under confusing conditions.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Mistakes When Ordinary Things Look Impossible Mistakes Mistakes When Ordinary Things Look Impossible Many Fortean stories begin when ordinary objects, animals, or lights are seen under confusing conditions. Many Fortean stories begin when ordinary objects, animals, or lights are seen under confusing conditions. mechanism Bad light, distance, and surprise Animals, aircraft, and weather How a mistake becomes a legend forteana mistakes Introduction Misidentification is one of the main ways ordinary events become Fortean mysteries. A light is seen at dusk, a large bird crosses a road in headlights, a cloud holds an unnervingly artificial shape, or a distant aircraft moves in a way the witness cannot place. For a few seconds, the normal category fails. That gap is where many strange reports are born. This does not mean witnesses are foolish or dishonest. It means human perception works under limits. Bad light, distance, surprise, emotion and incomplete context can make familiar things look impossible. Official UAP investigations, eyewitness research and long running monster folklore all point to the same mechanism: many reports begin as sincere observations of real things, then change as people describe, repeat, label and connect them to existing stories. Project Blue Book files and later UAP work repeatedly identify balloons, aircraft, astronomical objects, birds, drones, satellites and weather effects among reports first experienced as anomalous. [Pieces of History+2AARO] prologue.blogs.archives.gov saucers over washington the history of project blue book Pieces of HistorySaucers Over Washington: the History of Project Blue Book19 Dec 2019 — Upon further inspection, the majority of these re... Why misidentification matters in Forteana Forteana lives in the space between observation and explanation. A Fortean report usually starts with a claim that something happened in the real world: a creature was seen, lights crossed the sky, objects fell from above, a figure appeared, a sound came from nowhere. Misidentification matters because it explains how a report can be honest, vivid and still wrong about what was seen. That distinction is important. A hoax begins with deception. A hallucination begins with an experience not anchored to an external object. Misidentification often begins with a real stimulus: Venus, a satellite train, a weather balloon, an owl, a crane, a fox, a cloud formation, a meteor, a drone, an aircraft light, or a reflection. The witness is not inventing the event; they are trying to classify it under poor conditions. The UAP field provides the clearest modern evidence because many reports are collected by institutions. The US National Archives’ account of Project Blue Book notes that most investigated reports were eventually found to be misidentified objects such as weather balloons or aircraft, even while Cold War officials still treated reports seriously because of possible defence implications. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov saucers over washington the history of project blue book Pieces of HistorySaucers Over Washington: the History of Project Blue Book19 Dec 2019 — Upon further inspection, the majority of these re... AARO’s official case imagery page shows the same process in miniature: several puzzling videos are assessed with high confidence as balloons after analysts compare shape, movement and wind behaviour. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP ImageryAARO bases its assessment on the object's strong morphological consistency with other resolved imagery depicting balloons... That mechanism also applies outside UFO cases. In cryptid and haunting stories, the “object” may be an animal, a shadow, a sound, a building effect, or a remembered glimpse. The birth of mystery is often not the sighting alone, but the moment a witness or community gives the sighting a name. Bad light, distance and surprise A strange report often begins with a sensory problem. The witness sees something briefly, at night, through rain, across a field, from a moving car, under stress, or against a bright sky. Those conditions strip away the cues people normally use to identify size, speed, distance and shape. Research on eyewitness identification shows how sharply accuracy can fall as distance increases and light worsens. One study using live targets found that, even without facial masking, target present identification accuracy fell from.69 at 5 metres to.34 at 12.5 metres and.17 at 20 metres across all participants; under some combinations of distance, lighting and masking, performance approached chance level. [CentAUR] centaur.reading.ac.uk We investigated their combined effect on.Read more the effects of facial masking, distance, lighting, and eyewitness...August 25, 2023 — by TJ Nyman · 2023 · Cited by 11 — Distance... Published: August 25, 2023 Another review of eyewitness failure describes misidentification as a problem of both perception and memory: people may misperceive what they saw, then later remember that interpretation rather than the raw visual information. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCWhy eyewitnesses fail PMCWhy eyewitnesses fail Fortean reports add an extra difficulty: the witness is often not identifying a face but classifying an unfamiliar, moving or partly hidden object. A hovering light gives few cues to distance. A large bird seen in headlights may seem much larger than it is. A meteor may look nearby because the sky offers no scale. A balloon drifting with the wind may appear to manoeuvre if the observer is also moving. Surprise then locks in the first interpretation. When someone expects a plane, bird or cloud, perception tends to settle quickly. When the object violates expectation, attention narrows. The witness may remember the emotional force of the moment more strongly than its measurable details: how far away it was, how long it lasted, what direction it moved, whether there was sound, or whether other known objects were nearby for comparison. This is why many investigations depend on context rather than confidence. A witness may be completely sincere and still misjudge altitude, speed or size. A good explanation asks what was visible from that place at that time, not only how certain the witness felt. Debunking Earth's Most Infamous UFO Sightings Channel: Spark · Views: 15.8K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube Animals, aircraft and weather The most common misidentified things in Fortean reports are not exotic. They are everyday objects seen under unusual viewing conditions or in unexpected places. In aerial cases, recurring candidates include balloons, aircraft, drones, satellites, planets, meteors, flares, contrails and unusual clouds. NASA’s UAP independent study report stresses that many sightings lack the high quality data needed for firm analysis, and frames the problem as one of better observation: calibrated sensors, metadata, context and transparent methods. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) are one of our planet's greatest mysteries. Observations... AARO’s more recent official examples show how mundane objects can look strange when recorded at poor resolution or without obvious scale, especially balloons drifting in wind. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP ImageryAARO bases its assessment on the object's strong morphological consistency with other resolved imagery depicting balloons... Astronomy produces especially durable mistakes. Venus is a classic example because it can appear extremely bright in twilight and seem to hover rather than move. Popular astronomy sources repeatedly note that Venus, Jupiter, meteors, the Moon, the International Space Station and satellite passes can all be reported as UFOs when observers lack sky context. [Space+2Discover Magazine] space.com Jupiter and Venus 'Could Be Mistaken for UFOs Jupiter and Venus 'Could Be Mistaken for UFOs Recent satellite constellations have added a new form of misidentification: a 2024 case study reconstructed a 2022 incident in which multiple commercial pilots reported UAP over the Pacific, and concluded that a recently launched Starlink satellite train could account for the observations. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Weather has its own catalogue of false mysteries. Lenticular clouds are a useful example because they can look startlingly artificial: smooth, lens shaped, sometimes stacked, and often stationary looking because they form in standing waves of air near mountains. The Met Office describes unusual cloud forms such as arcus clouds through ordinary atmospheric processes, while meteorological explainers note that lenticular clouds are often mistaken for UFOs because of their distinctive oval shape. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Open source on metoffice.gov.uk. Animal misidentification works differently. Here the witness usually sees a living body but assigns it to the wrong category because of size, posture,"},{"title":"Balloon UAPs","url":"/balloon-uaps/","description":"Official UAP examples show how balloons can seem uncanny when video lacks scale, resolution and wind context.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Balloon UAPs How Balloons Become Official UAP Cases Balloon UAPs Balloon UAPs How Balloons Become Official UAP Cases Official UAP examples show how balloons can seem uncanny when video lacks scale, resolution and wind context. Official UAP examples show how balloons can seem uncanny when video lacks scale, resolution and wind context. dataset evidence Why balloons look strange on camera Shape, drift and wind as clues What official case imagery can and cannot prove forteana mistakes balloon uaps Introduction Official unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) imagery has repeatedly demonstrated how ordinary balloons can appear extraordinary when recorded under difficult viewing conditions. Low resolution infrared sensors, long focal lengths, uncertain range, moving camera platforms and incomplete weather data can combine to create objects that seem to hover, accelerate or manoeuvre in ways that initially resist explanation. In recent years, the U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has used several publicly released cases to illustrate that an object can remain officially \"unidentified\" for a time without implying anything exotic, and that additional analysis often resolves such cases as balloons or other commonplace airborne objects. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP ImageryOfficial UAP Imagery. AARO assesses, with high confidence, that the footage depicts the presence of a physical object. UAP... Within the wider history of Forteana , these cases are valuable because they show the mechanics of misidentification in unusually well documented form. Rather than relying only on eyewitness recollection, investigators can compare video, aircraft telemetry, sensor geometry and atmospheric data to reconstruct what the camera was actually recording. Why balloons look strange on camera A balloon is deceptively difficult to identify from military imagery. Unlike aircraft, it often has no obvious propulsion, leaves little or no visible exhaust and drifts with the surrounding air mass. Through a stabilised infrared targeting camera, these characteristics can produce behaviour that appears intelligent or unconventional. Several factors contribute to this illusion: Lack of scale: Without a known reference object, viewers cannot reliably estimate size or distance. Compression and low resolution: Small airborne objects may occupy only a handful of pixels, masking familiar shapes. Tracking systems: Military targeting pods continually adjust zoom, stabilisation and pointing angle, creating apparent motion unrelated to the object's own movement. Observer motion: A fast moving aircraft observing a slow moving object creates strong parallax effects, making a drifting balloon appear to race across the landscape. These effects are not unique to UAP investigations; they are familiar challenges in aerial surveillance. However, once footage is detached from its operational context and viewed without accompanying telemetry, ordinary objects can acquire an aura of mystery. ‘Most UFO sightings are explainable but…’: Pentagon AARO chief tells Senate Armed Services Committee Channel: The Economic Times · Views: 15.6K · Uploaded: November 2024 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Shape, drift and wind as clues One of AARO's recurring findings is that balloons share a recognisable combination of visual and behavioural characteristics when viewed through military sensors. Instead of following powered flight profiles, balloons typically: move at speeds consistent with local winds; show gradual rather than abrupt changes in direction; lack visible propulsion signatures; maintain relatively constant orientation despite apparent camera movement; and exhibit shapes consistent with inflated lighter than air objects when analysed frame by frame. In 2025 AARO publicly released multiple case resolution videos from Europe recorded in 2022, assessing with high confidence that the recorded objects were almost certainly balloons. The office stated that its conclusions rested on two complementary lines of evidence: the objects' morphology matched other resolved balloon imagery, while their measured movement closely followed expected wind driven behaviour rather than powered flight. [DVIDS] dvidshub.net pr 009 uap report resolved balloon europe 2022 DVIDSPR 009, UAP Report Resolved as a Balloon, Europe 2022AARO assesses, with high confidence, that the object depicted in the video is a... This combination is significant because neither appearance nor motion alone is always decisive. A blurred object may resemble several different things, but when its track also matches atmospheric drift, confidence in a balloon explanation increases substantially. What official case imagery can and cannot prove Official UAP footage often circulates long before investigators possess enough contextual information to identify the object. The imagery itself rarely contains sufficient information to determine distance, speed or size. A useful example is the well known GOFAST video recorded by a U.S. Navy aircraft. For years the footage was widely interpreted as showing an object travelling at extraordinary speed just above the ocean surface. Later AARO analysis reconstructed the encounter using aircraft geometry, trigonometry and sensor data, concluding that the object was actually much higher—around 13,000 feet—and moving at roughly wind speed. Its dramatic apparent velocity resulted primarily from motion parallax caused by the observing aircraft's own movement. [AARO+2DefenseScoop] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution Card Methodology Final AARO GoFast Case Resolution6 Feb 2025 — The distance between these two points (red arrows) is the perceived distance the. UAP travell... Although AARO's published GOFAST resolution does not identify the object with complete certainty, the reconstructed speed and altitude are compatible with passive windborne objects such as balloons, illustrating how incomplete geometric information can produce misleading visual impressions. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution Card Methodology Final AARO GoFast Case Resolution6 Feb 2025 — The distance between these two points (red arrows) is the perceived distance the. UAP travell... The important lesson is methodological rather than categorical. A striking video does not automatically measure what viewers think it measures. Sensor recordings require reconstruction before claims about extraordinary performance can be evaluated. UAP FILES Footage Released of Object Tracked over Europe in 2022 Channel: Media Magik Entertainment · Views: 3.3K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Balloon resolutions in the wider UAP record Recent AARO reporting shows that balloon identifications are not isolated exceptions but part of a broader pattern in official investigations. The office's Fiscal Year 2024 report states that it resolved 118 reported cases during the reporting period, identifying prosaic explanations including balloons, birds, drones and other conventional objects. Many remaining cases were not judged anomalous because they displayed impossible behaviour, but because available information was insufficient for confident identification. [U.S. Department of War] media.defense.gov FY24 CONSOLIDATED ANNUAL REPORT ON UAP 508 Department of WarFiscal Year 2024 Consolidated Annual Report on...14 Nov 2024 — AARO resolved 118 cases during the reporting period, all... This distinction matters. \"Unidentified\" is a temporary analytical category rather than evidence for an extraordinary explanation. As additional radar data, weather information, flight records or sensor calibration become available, previously puzzling imagery may be reclassified without any change to the underlying footage. Why these cases matter in Forteana Balloon related UAP cases demonstrate a recurring theme in Fortean history: mystery often arises from missing context rather than impossible phenomena. The videos remain compelling because they genuinely look unusual when viewed in isolation. Military equipment, specialised sensors and official investigation lend them an appearance of authority that can encourage extraordinary interpretations. Yet the same official investigations increasingly show that careful reconstruction—adding wind fields, camera geometry, aircraft motion and environmental data—can transform an apparently inexplicable object into an ordinary one. That does not mean every UAP report is resolved as a balloon, nor that every unresolved case has a mundane explanation. Instead, balloon cases illustrate a broader evidential principle: imagery alone is rarely enough. Reliable interpretation depends on restoring the physical context that the camera cannot capture by itself, making these official examples among the clearest demonstrations of how sincere observation can become enduring mystery until missing information is recovered. The Science of Alien Sightings StarTalk Channel: National Geographic · Views: 219.2K ·"},{"title":"Bird Monsters","url":"/bird-monsters/","description":"Owls, cranes and herons can become monster reports when night roads, headlights and eyeshine exaggerate size, posture and threat.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Bird Monsters When Birds Become Monsters at Night Bird Monsters Bird Monsters When Birds Become Monsters at Night Owls, cranes and herons can become monster reports when night roads, headlights and eyeshine exaggerate size, posture and threat. Owls, cranes and herons can become monster reports when night roads, headlights and eyeshine exaggerate size, posture and threat. mechanism Eyeshine, wingspan and startled witnesses Roadside encounters and false scale Why bird explanations fit some creature reports forteana mistakes bird monsters Introduction Many famous creature reports begin with a fleeting encounter in poor light rather than a prolonged observation . Among the most convincing natural explanations are large birds seen at night or in the glare of vehicle headlights. An owl perched beside a road, a heron lifting off unexpectedly, or a crane standing upright in marshland can briefly appear much larger, stranger and more human like than they really are. Combined with bright reflective eyes, unfamiliar postures and the stress of a surprise encounter, these birds can produce sightings that later evolve into reports of unknown monsters. This explanation does not fit every cryptid claim, nor does it dismiss witnesses as dishonest. Instead, it shows how normal visual perception can produce extraordinary impressions when size, distance and shape are difficult to judge. Within Fortean studies, large birds offer one of the clearest examples of how sincere observations can become enduring mysteries. [Audubon+2Skeptical Inquirer] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — More than 50 years after the first sighting, the best expl... Published: October 26, 2018 Eyeshine, Wingspan and Startled Witnesses The feature that often dominates monster reports is not the wings or body but the eyes. Witnesses frequently describe bright red or orange eyes shining from the darkness. Such descriptions feel supernatural because the eyes appear self luminous, yet in many situations they are simply reflecting a light source. When headlights or a torch are close to the observer's line of sight, reflected light travels directly back towards the source. This creates the impression of glowing eyes even though the animal is merely reflecting light. The colour depends on viewing angle, lighting conditions and the anatomy of the eye, with reddish reflections being particularly striking under vehicle headlights. [American Bird Conservancy+2Discover Magazine] abcbirds.org owl be seeing you amazing facts about owl eyes American Bird Conservancy\"Owl\" Be Seeing You: Amazing Facts About Owl Eyes4 Mar 2022 — Behind an owl eye's rod packed retina is another l... The effect becomes more dramatic because large nocturnal birds often remain motionless until the last possible moment. A driver may notice only two bright points floating above roadside vegetation before the bird suddenly spreads its wings and takes flight. That rapid transition—from stationary eyes to a large silhouette—can make the animal appear to transform into something much bigger than it is. Owls are especially well suited to producing this impression because they possess exceptional low light vision and large forward facing eyes. Their broad facial discs can also resemble a face more than a beak when briefly illuminated. [Audubon+2American Bird Conservancy] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — More than 50 years after the first sighting, the best expl... Published: October 26, 2018 It is worth noting that discussions of bird eyeshine are more complicated than popular summaries sometimes suggest. Scientific surveys have found that birds generally lack the classic tapetum lucidum—the reflective layer responsible for the strong eyeshine seen in cats and many mammals—although some birds can still produce noticeable eye reflections through other optical mechanisms and retinal structures. The important point for eyewitness reports is the visual effect itself rather than the precise anatomy, since observers experience reflected eyes as apparently glowing regardless of the underlying cause. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Comparative morphology of the tapetum lucidum (among...by FJ Ollivier · 2004 · Cited by 428 — Some species (primates, squirrels, b... Owl Adaptations: Vision, Hearing & Silent Flight Explained! Channel: BuschWildlife Open on YouTube Roadside Encounters and False Scale Night roads create ideal conditions for misjudging an animal's size. Drivers have little opportunity to establish distance because darkness removes familiar reference points. A bird standing on a raised bank may appear level with the car. If it opens its wings while the vehicle is moving, the apparent size increases dramatically. Several perceptual effects combine at once: Poor distance cues. Darkness makes nearby birds seem farther away, leading the brain to infer that they must be much larger. Unexpected posture. Herons and cranes stand unusually upright compared with most birds, making them appear almost human. Sudden wing extension. A folded bird can double its visible width in an instant when startled into flight. Moving observer. A witness travelling at speed has only seconds to interpret an unfamiliar shape before it disappears. Large wading birds are particularly effective at producing these illusions. Great blue herons and cranes have exceptionally long necks and legs, while some species can reach heights exceeding one metre with wingspans approaching two metres. Seen from an unfamiliar angle, these proportions can appear unlike any bird the witness expects to encounter. [Skeptical Inquirer+2Phys.org] skepticalinquirer.org The data revealed that there indeed were sightings of sandhill cranes in and around the Point Pleasant area prior to 1967... Why Some Bird Species Become \"Monsters\" Not every bird generates monster reports. Certain species repeatedly appear in sceptical investigations because their appearance matches recurring witness descriptions. Owls often explain reports involving glowing eyes, broad shoulders, silent flight and encounters near woodland or buildings. Their habit of turning the head without moving the body can make them seem unnaturally fixed on an observer. A perched owl viewed from below also hides much of its body beneath feathers, exaggerating the impression of a bulky torso. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — More than 50 years after the first sighting, the best expl... Published: October 26, 2018 Sandhill cranes are frequently proposed in North American cryptid cases because they are unusually tall birds with long legs, broad wings and reddish skin around the eyes. Their height surprises observers unfamiliar with them, particularly where they appear outside their expected range during migration. [Skeptical Inquirer] skepticalinquirer.org The data revealed that there indeed were sightings of sandhill cranes in and around the Point Pleasant area prior to 1967... Great blue herons offer another plausible explanation for some reports. Their elongated necks can appear surprisingly thick or almost absent depending on whether the neck is extended or folded into an S shape. During take off, the combination of long wings, dangling legs and awkward movement can produce an unfamiliar silhouette. Research comparing historical sightings with bird distributions has suggested that herons may fit some reports even better than cranes in particular regions. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Owl Eyes Are Shaped Like Tubes, Here's Why Channel: Seeker Open on YouTube Creature Reports That Fit Bird Explanations Perhaps the best known example is the series of sightings associated with the Mothman legend near Point Pleasant, West Virginia, during 1966 and 1967. The original newspaper accounts described a \"man sized bird\" before later retellings increasingly emphasised supernatural elements. Over time, investigators have proposed several bird species—including barred owls, great blue herons and sandhill cranes—as explanations for different reports rather than assuming a single animal lay behind every sighting. Modern analyses argue that multiple ordinary birds observed under varying conditions are more consistent with the diversity of witness descriptions than one unknown creature. [Wikipedia+2Skeptical Inquirer] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Importantly, this interpretation does not require every witness to have seen the same species. One report may involve an owl reflecting headlights, another a heron taking flight near a road, and another an unusually placed crane during migration. As the stories merge through conversation and media coverage, they become remembered as sightings of a single mysterious being rather than"},{"title":"Cloud UFOs","url":"/cloud-ufos/","description":"Lenticular clouds can look engineered or disc-shaped because smooth standing-wave formations appear still, stacked and unusually clean.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Cloud UFOs The Clouds That Look Too Perfect Cloud UFOs Cloud UFOs The Clouds That Look Too Perfect Lenticular clouds can look engineered or disc shaped because smooth standing wave formations appear still, stacked and unusually clean. Lenticular clouds can look engineered or disc shaped because smooth standing wave formations appear still, stacked and unusually clean. mechanism Why lenticular clouds form Why stillness and symmetry feel artificial Separating weather effects from aerial objects forteana mistakes cloud ufos Introduction Lenticular clouds are among the most convincing examples of how ordinary weather can produce extraordinary looking sights. Smooth, lens shaped and often apparently motionless, they can resemble hovering craft so closely that they have featured in countless UFO reports and popular photographs. Within the study of Forteana , they are a classic illustration of how a genuine atmospheric phenomenon can trigger sincere reports of something seemingly impossible. Unlike vague lights or distant objects, lenticular clouds possess features that many people instinctively associate with engineering rather than nature: precise curves, layered symmetry and an uncanny ability to remain fixed in one part of the sky. Understanding how they form helps explain why they have repeatedly become part of UFO folklore without requiring deception or unusual atmospheric anomalies. [Met Office+2National Weather Service] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met OfficeUnusual cloud formations... UFO sightings across the world. How do lenticular clouds form? When air... Lenticular clouds are a... Why lenticular clouds form Lenticular clouds develop when stable, moist air flows across mountains or other substantial terrain barriers. As the air is forced upwards it cools, allowing water vapour to condense. Downwind of the mountain, the airflow often settles into a series of standing atmospheric waves, sometimes called mountain waves. A key point is that the wave remains fixed relative to the landscape even though the air itself is moving rapidly through it. Moist air continually condenses at one point in the wave and evaporates further downstream. The cloud therefore appears almost stationary despite strong winds passing through it. Meteorologists sometimes describe this as a cloud being continuously rebuilt in the same location rather than physically sitting still. [National Weather Service+2Met Office] weather.gov National Weather ServiceAltocumulus Standing Lenticular CloudsWhen sufficient moisture is present above mountain top level, ACSL clouds d... The resulting cloud often has characteristics unlike the ragged appearance people expect from everyday clouds: Smooth, sharply defined edges produced by relatively laminar (orderly) airflow. A flattened lens or saucer profile. Multiple stacked layers where several standing waves exist. Remarkably little apparent movement over periods of minutes or even hours. Because these clouds reveal the shape of invisible air currents, they often look less like drifting vapour than like solid objects suspended in the sky. [Mount Washington Observatory+2National Weather Service] mountwashington.org Mount Washington ObservatoryA Closer Look at Lenticular CloudsThese visually striking clouds have been variously described to appear as l... Lenticular Clouds Channel: Alan Sealls · Views: 11.3K · Uploaded: January 2023 · Length: 3 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Why stillness and symmetry feel artificial Human perception relies heavily on familiar patterns. Most clouds change shape constantly, drift across the sky and have irregular edges. Lenticular clouds violate all three expectations. Their symmetry is especially important. People often associate circles, smooth curves and layered geometry with manufactured objects. Aircraft, satellites and buildings contain repeated, regular forms, whereas natural landscapes usually appear rough and asymmetrical. A lenticular cloud unexpectedly displays those engineered looking qualities. The illusion is strengthened by its apparent stillness. Since the cloud remains anchored over one location while neighbouring clouds continue moving, observers may conclude that an object is hovering rather than recognising that they are seeing a stationary wave in moving air. The lack of obvious motion also removes one of the easiest visual clues people use to classify clouds. Lighting adds another layer of confusion. Around sunrise or sunset, low angle sunlight can illuminate the upper surface while leaving the underside in shadow, giving the impression of a metallic disc with a darker base. Photographs can exaggerate this effect because cameras compress depth and contrast, making the cloud appear even more solid than it looked to the eye. [Met Office+2Mount Washington Observatory] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met OfficeUnusual cloud formations... UFO sightings across the world. How do lenticular clouds form? When air... Lenticular clouds are a... Why they became linked with UFO reports Since the late 1940s, saucer shaped aerial reports have often been compared with lenticular clouds. Meteorologists have long recognised that their distinctive appearance makes them one of the most common weather phenomena to be mistaken for unidentified flying objects. This association is particularly strong in mountainous regions where standing wave conditions occur frequently. Well known examples include areas around: The Rocky Mountains in North America. California's Mount Shasta. Japan's Mount Fuji. The European Alps. The Andes. These landscapes regularly produce striking photographs that circulate online with captions suggesting impossible or alien looking skies before being identified as ordinary meteorology. [AccuWeather+2Mount Washington Observatory] accuweather.com Lenticular clouds, sometimes mistaken for UFOs, are in a...25 Sept 2024 — Lenticular clouds, sometimes mistaken for UFOs, are... Within broader UFO investigations, weather phenomena have consistently appeared among the ordinary explanations for reports initially considered mysterious. Historical investigations such as Project Blue Book concluded that many sightings were ultimately attributable to conventional aircraft, astronomical objects or meteorological phenomena rather than unknown technology. Although lenticular clouds represented only one category among many, they became culturally significant because they so closely matched the popular \"flying saucer\" image. [Wikipedia+2Pieces of History] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Spotted a UFO near the mountains? It was likely a lenticular cloud Channel: CBS 13 News · Views: 309 · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 1 minute 29 seconds Open on YouTube Separating weather effects from aerial objects Observers can often distinguish a lenticular cloud from a physical airborne object by considering its behaviour rather than its shape. [lenticular] weather.gov clouds Big Island 11/25/200325 Nov 2003 — Some people have mistaken them for UFOs due to their smooth saucer like shape.... National Wea... ar cloud typically: Remains fixed relative to nearby mountains or high ground. Changes gradually through growth and evaporation rather than rapid manoeuvres. Develops soft, vapour like edges on close inspection. Exists alongside other cloud features associated with mountain wave conditions. Persists despite strong winds because new droplets continually replace old ones. By contrast, an aircraft or other solid object changes position independently of the terrain and does not continually dissolve and reform. Pilots also recognise lenticular clouds for a practical reason unrelated to UFO folklore. They are visible indicators of mountain wave activity, which can produce powerful updraughts, downdraughts and rotor turbulence beneath the smooth looking cloud. The attractive appearance therefore signals potentially hazardous flying conditions rather than calm skies. [National Weather Service+2Met Office] weather.gov National Weather ServiceAltocumulus Standing Lenticular CloudsWhen sufficient moisture is present above mountain top level, ACSL clouds d... What lenticular clouds reveal about Fortean mystery Lenticular clouds demonstrate that a convincing mystery does not require an unusual object—only an unusual combination of appearance, expectation and viewing conditions. Their significance within Forteana lies less in the clouds themselves than in the way people interpret them. A witness encountering an apparently motionless, perfectly symmetrical form in an unexpected place may reasonably conclude that they are seeing something engineered rather than meteorological. The experience is genuine, but the initial classification may not be. This makes lenticular clouds an excellent example of the broader pattern behind many Fortean reports: ordinary natural processes can produce scenes so visually unexpected that they momentarily escape familiar categories. The mystery begins not because nature"},{"title":"Naming Legends","url":"/naming-legends/","description":"A vague report becomes easier to repeat, connect and believe once a community gives it a familiar Fortean name.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Naming Legends How a Strange Glimpse Gets a Name Naming Legends Naming Legends How a Strange Glimpse Gets a Name A vague report becomes easier to repeat, connect and believe once a community gives it a familiar Fortean name. A vague report becomes easier to repeat, connect and believe once a community gives it a familiar Fortean name. mechanism From encounter to first telling How labels sharpen ambiguous details Why named categories attract later reports forteana mistakes naming legends Introduction Many Fortean mysteries do not become enduring legends at the moment of the sighting. They become legends when someone gives the experience a recognisable name. An uncertain glimpse of a large animal becomes a \"Big Cat\". An odd light becomes a \"UFO\". A shadowy figure becomes a \"ghost\". Once a label exists, later witnesses have a ready made category into which they can place their own ambiguous experiences. The result is that isolated reports begin to look like connected evidence, even when the original observations were unrelated or poorly understood. This process does not require fraud or mass delusion. It emerges from ordinary human habits of categorisation, memory and storytelling, all of which shape how unusual events are recalled and shared. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov These failures and distortions are influenced by several... From encounter to first telling A strange experience usually begins as something difficult to classify rather than something obviously paranormal. A witness may notice movement in poor light, hear an unfamiliar sound or glimpse an unusual silhouette. At that stage, the experience often contains uncertainty rather than a complete narrative. The first retelling is therefore crucial. As the witness searches for words, they naturally compare the event with familiar ideas. Instead of saying \"I saw something I could not identify,\" they may say, \"It looked like a ghost,\" or \"It reminded me of a flying saucer.\" These comparisons are not necessarily claims of certainty. They are attempts to communicate an unfamiliar experience using shared language. Folklorists distinguish between a personal experience narrative and a fully developed legend. A first person account, often called a memorate, may begin as an individual's sincere description of an unusual event. As that account is repeated, simplified and linked to other stories, it can gradually lose its individuality and become part of a wider legendary tradition. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Urban Legends With Real World Origins — 49 Creepy Cases Channel: Brainex Open on YouTube How labels sharpen ambiguous details Naming does more than organise stories. It changes how people remember them. Research on eyewitness memory consistently shows that recollection is reconstructive rather than photographic. Memories are influenced by later information, discussions with other people and the concepts available to describe an event. Once a witness adopts a label, later recall often becomes organised around that category instead of around the original sensory experience. [PMC+2ojs.pensamultimedia.it] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov These failures and distortions are influenced by several... This helps explain why later retellings often appear more detailed than the original account. A witness who initially recalled only \"a large dark shape\" may later remember wings, glowing eyes or impossible movement because those features fit the accepted image associated with the chosen label. The process is usually unconscious rather than deceptive. Recent cognitive research also suggests that verbal labels become integrated with memory during learning and consolidation. Giving an event a meaningful title or category can influence how it is later reconstructed, making the remembered event more consistent with the concept than with the original perception. [Pure York] pure.york.ac.uk influences of learned verbal labels and sleep on temporal event m Pure YorkInfluences of learned verbal labels and sleep on temporal...by Y Wang · 2024 · Cited by 3 — Conceptual knowledge is known to mo... Devil's Punchbowl PA: The Truth About the Sunken Train Legend Channel: History of the Northeast Open on YouTube Why named categories attract later reports Once a community recognises a category, later ambiguous sightings become easier to classify in the same way. This creates a feedback loop: A report receives a memorable label. The label circulates through conversation, newspapers or online communities. People encountering similarly ambiguous events recognise the label. New reports reinforce the apparent existence of the category. The growing collection of reports makes the category appear increasingly established. The important point is that the label itself becomes part of the environment through which later witnesses interpret their experiences. Instead of confronting an entirely unknown event, they confront something that already has a cultural identity. This mechanism helps explain why reports often cluster around well known Fortean categories rather than producing an unlimited variety of unique mysteries. People are more likely to describe a mysterious light as a UFO than invent an entirely new class of aerial phenomenon, because \"UFO\" already provides a familiar framework for interpretation. The same pattern appears with lake monsters, phantom animals, haunted buildings and other recurring subjects in Fortean culture. [Academia] academia.edu It confronts psychological knowledge about Memorates and memory: A re evaluation of Lauri Honko's theory...This paper deals with the phenomena where culture and society in... When official language changes the legend Names introduced by investigators can also reshape public perception. One notable example is the replacement of the popular phrase \"flying saucer\" with the more neutral term \"unidentified flying object\". During investigations such as Project Blue Book , officials preferred terminology that described an object as unidentified rather than assuming its shape or origin. The new label broadened the category dramatically, allowing reports of lights, discs, triangles and many other aerial phenomena to be grouped together under a single heading. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book The choice of words mattered. \"Flying saucer\" encouraged witnesses to imagine a particular form, whereas \"UFO\" invited almost any unexplained aerial observation into the same reporting tradition. More recently, the term \"unidentified anomalous phenomena\" (UAP) has similarly expanded the language used in official investigations, showing how changing terminology can alter what people consider relevant to report. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Folklore How they upheld norms. And still do. Channel: Annoyed Kitten Open on YouTube Why repeated names create the illusion of independent evidence A named category can make separate reports appear more closely related than they really are. If ten witnesses independently report \"a strange animal\", investigators may initially treat the cases cautiously because the descriptions differ. If the same ten reports are all described publicly as sightings of a specific cryptid, readers may perceive them as mutually supporting evidence, even when the observations vary greatly in appearance, behaviour and circumstances. This is not simply a matter of media exaggeration. Human beings naturally group similar experiences into categories. Once enough reports share the same label, the category itself becomes a lens through which future evidence is interpreted. Folklore researchers have long observed similar processes in the development of legends. Personal accounts become linked to established narratives, while familiar narrative patterns encourage people to interpret new experiences in comparable ways. Over time, the legend becomes more stable than any individual sighting that originally contributed to it. [Academia+2Academia] academia.edu It confronts psychological knowledge about Memorates and memory: A re evaluation of Lauri Honko's theory...This paper deals with the phenomena where culture and society in... Naming as the bridge between perception and legend Within Forteana , naming is one of the most powerful mechanisms connecting ordinary perception to extraordinary belief. The original sighting may last only seconds and remain deeply uncertain. The name, however, can persist for decades. By giving an ambiguous encounter a recognised identity, communities make it easier to remember, compare and repeat. Each new report is then interpreted against earlier named examples, creating an expanding tradition that often feels more coherent than the underlying evidence alone. In this way, legends frequently grow not because later witnesses see exactly the same thing, but because they learn to describe different experiences using the same familiar name. [Academia+2PMC] academia.edu It confronts"},{"title":"Starlink Trains","url":"/starlink-trains/","description":"Satellite trains can turn routine orbital traffic into dramatic UAP reports when witnesses see clustered lights moving together at night.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Starlink Trains When Satellite Trains Look Like Visitors Starlink Trains Starlink Trains When Satellite Trains Look Like Visitors Satellite trains can turn routine orbital traffic into dramatic UAP reports when witnesses see clustered lights moving together at night. Satellite trains can turn routine orbital traffic into dramatic UAP reports when witnesses see clustered lights moving together at night. event time window What satellite trains look like from the ground The 2022 Pacific pilot case How launch timing and sky tracks test a sighting forteana mistakes starlink trains Introduction Starlink satellite trains have created a new and unusually persuasive source of modern UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) reports. Unlike a single satellite drifting steadily across the sky, a newly launched Starlink group can appear as a chain of bright lights travelling together in formation, sometimes producing reflections that seem to brighten, fade or move in unexpected ways. For observers unfamiliar with the phenomenon—including experienced pilots—these displays can appear genuinely extraordinary rather than obviously artificial. This makes Starlink trains an important example within Forteana of how a real, physical object can generate sincere reports of something apparently inexplicable. Recent investigations have shown that careful reconstruction of satellite positions, launch timing and cockpit viewing geometry can resolve at least some well known pilot UAP cases without questioning the honesty or competence of the witnesses. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... What satellite trains look like from the ground A Starlink train forms immediately after a SpaceX launch, when dozens of satellites are released into nearly the same orbit before gradually spreading apart over days or weeks. During this early phase they appear as a procession of evenly spaced points of light moving silently across the sky. Several features distinguish these trains from conventional aircraft: The lights move at a constant apparent speed with no engine noise. Individual objects maintain almost perfect spacing. They are usually visible only shortly after sunset or before sunrise, when the satellites remain illuminated by the Sun while observers on the ground are in darkness. The formation gradually disperses over subsequent days as each satellite raises its orbit independently. [Space+2SatelliteMap.space] space.com These satellites initially travel in a tight, bright line resembling a \"train,\" captivating skywatchers and often being mistaken for UFOs... These characteristics are striking enough that thousands of first time observers have reported them as UFOs. The effect is strongest within the first few days after launch, when the satellites remain close together and relatively bright. Once they reach their operational altitude, the familiar \"train\" largely disappears, leaving much fainter individual satellites. [Space] space.com These satellites initially travel in a tight, bright line resembling a \"train,\" captivating skywatchers and often being mistaken for UFOs... The situation becomes more complicated because satellite brightness is not constant. Reflections from solar panels and spacecraft surfaces depend on the changing geometry between the Sun, the satellite and the observer. A satellite may brighten dramatically for only a few seconds before fading again, making it appear to blink or manoeuvre even though it continues on a predictable orbit. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... STARLINK satellites train seen from earth SpaceX Elon Musk Channel: ViralVideoLab · Views: 4.9M · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 2 minutes 19 seconds Open on YouTube The 2022 Pacific pilot case One of the best documented examples occurred on 10 August 2022 over the Pacific Ocean, when pilots on two commercial airliners reported unusual lights ahead of their aircraft. In total, five pilots independently described luminous objects that appeared difficult to identify. The incident gained attention because multiple experienced witnesses observed the phenomenon from separate aircraft, and photographs and video were recorded from one cockpit. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Initially, the reports appeared noteworthy precisely because they involved trained aviation professionals. Pilots are accustomed to recognising stars, planets, aircraft lighting and ordinary satellite motion. The witnesses also reported that the lights did not resemble previous Starlink observations they had seen. A detailed reconstruction published in 2024 reached a different conclusion. Researchers combined several independent datasets: the orbital elements of the Starlink satellites launched earlier that same day; [space.com] space.com x starlink satellites Starlink satellites: Facts, tracking and impact on astronomy1 Jun 2026 — Starlink satellites are easier to see a day or two after their l... aircraft positions reconstructed from Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) flight tracking; the precise viewing geometry from the cockpit; and simulations of satellite reflections. The reconstructed sky closely matched the appearance, direction and timing of the reported lights. Rather than a single unidentified craft, the evidence indicated that the pilots were observing newly deployed Starlink satellites whose reflections created unfamiliar visual patterns from their particular vantage point. The authors argued that the event demonstrates how unusual illumination geometry—not abnormal flight behaviour—produced the apparent anomaly. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Importantly, this interpretation does not suggest the pilots imagined the event. They observed real luminous objects in the sky. The investigation instead concerns classification: identifying what those objects were. How launch timing and sky tracks test a sighting The Starlink example illustrates how investigators can test a UAP report using measurable evidence rather than relying solely on eyewitness confidence. The first question is whether a Starlink launch occurred recently. Satellite trains remain tightly grouped only for a limited period after deployment, so the launch date immediately constrains what should have been visible. The second question is orbital visibility. Satellites can only be seen when they remain sunlit while the observer is already in darkness. This typically limits visibility to the period after sunset or before sunrise. A midnight report, for example, usually requires a different explanation because low Earth orbit satellites are generally in Earth's shadow. [Space] space.com These satellites initially travel in a tight, bright line resembling a \"train,\" captivating skywatchers and often being mistaken for UFOs... Investigators can then compare: the observer's location; the exact time; the aircraft or witness heading; known satellite orbital data; and predicted reflection geometry. If these independent datasets converge, the explanation becomes testable rather than speculative. This approach mirrors methods long used in aviation accident investigation, where multiple sources of physical evidence are compared against witness testimony. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Pilot Shares Videos Of Strange UFO Sightings In Skies Over The US Channel: TODAY Open on YouTube Starlink satellites, the string of lights in the night sky. Channel: Brad Panovich (wxbrad) · Views: 251.7K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Why this matters for Forteana Starlink trains represent a distinctly modern source of Fortean mystery. Earlier generations commonly misidentified Venus, meteors or weather balloons. Today's observers also encounter rapidly growing satellite constellations whose appearance changes with launch schedules, orbital manoeuvres and illumination angles. The result is a new category of sincere mystery: witnesses accurately report unusual lights, but the unfamiliarity lies in recent changes to the night sky rather than in unknown technology. As satellite constellations continue to expand, investigators increasingly need space situational awareness—the ability to know what artificial objects occupy the sky at a given moment—to distinguish genuinely unexplained observations from predictable orbital traffic. Researchers studying the 2022 Pacific case have argued that providing pilots with better satellite"},{"title":"Planet UFOs","url":"/planet-ufos/","description":"Bright planets can look like hovering craft when twilight, distance and a blank sky remove the usual cues for scale and motion.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mistakes","level":3,"text":"Planet UFOs Why Venus So Often Becomes a UFO Planet UFOs Planet UFOs Why Venus So Often Becomes a UFO Bright planets can look like hovering craft when twilight, distance and a blank sky remove the usual cues for scale and motion. Bright planets can look like hovering craft when twilight, distance and a blank sky remove the usual cues for scale and motion. mechanism Why bright planets fool the eye Twilight, motion and missing scale Checking sky context before naming a mystery forteana mistakes planet ufos Introduction Among all the ordinary objects that become extraordinary in UFO reports , no celestial body has a stronger reputation than Venus. Within Forteana, many accounts of mysterious hovering lights begin not with a hidden spacecraft but with the brightest planet in the evening or morning sky. When viewed against a darkening twilight, without familiar landmarks or a sense of scale, Venus can appear uncannily bright, stationary and even intelligent in its behaviour. Similar mistakes also occur with Jupiter and, less often, other bright planets. Understanding why these worlds are so often reported as UFOs helps explain one of the most common mechanisms through which genuine observations become enduring mysteries. Why bright planets fool the eye Venus is the brightest planet visible from Earth, reaching an apparent brightness that far exceeds any star. Shortly after sunset or before sunrise it often dominates an otherwise empty sky, shining steadily near the horizon where atmospheric effects are strongest. NASA's Night Sky Network notes that Venus has been reported as a UFO many times because of its exceptional brightness and position during twilight. [Night Sky Network] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Night Sky Network Identifying UFOs and UAPs Do you think you have spotted a UFO? We have some tips to help you identify that...Read more... Several characteristics combine to make the planet seem unusual: Exceptional brightness. Venus can outshine every object in the night sky except the Moon, drawing immediate attention. Low altitude. When viewed close to the horizon, atmospheric turbulence can make it shimmer, flash different colours or appear distorted. Long visibility. Unlike a meteor or aircraft, Venus may remain in roughly the same place for hours, creating the impression of a hovering object. Isolation. During twilight, relatively few stars are visible, removing the celestial context that would normally identify it as an astronomical object. [Night Sky Network+2Discover Magazine] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Night Sky Network Identifying UFOs and UAPs Do you think you have spotted a UFO? We have some tips to help you identify that...Read more... Jupiter can produce similar reports. Although usually less brilliant than Venus, it is bright enough to attract attention and can appear unusually large or colourful when atmospheric conditions are poor. Modern guidance from the U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) specifically lists bright planets such as Venus and Jupiter among the celestial objects most commonly misperceived as anomalous aerial phenomena. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO HomeCelestial objects: Bright planets like Venus and Jupiter, meteors, and other astronomical bodies can be misperceived as hove... UFO & UAP. Your Astronomy Sucks 3 Channel: science out there · Views: 2.6K · Uploaded: July 2021 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Twilight, motion and missing scale The mystery is not created by the planet itself but by the way human vision interprets it. A blank sky offers almost no reliable cues for judging distance or size. A brilliant point of light therefore becomes psychologically detached from its true location, making it seem closer than it really is. Without trees, buildings or clouds nearby for comparison, observers often struggle to estimate whether the object is hundreds of metres away or tens of millions of kilometres away. Twilight intensifies the problem. As the sky darkens, Venus remains bright while surrounding landmarks become difficult to see. The contrast makes the planet appear unusually prominent, almost spotlight like. Atmospheric refraction near the horizon may also make the light flicker or appear to pulse, reinforcing impressions that it is changing shape or activity. [Night Sky Network] nightsky.jpl.nasa.gov Night Sky Network Identifying UFOs and UAPs Do you think you have spotted a UFO? We have some tips to help you identify that...Read more... Another important effect is apparent motion . Although Venus is essentially stationary over short periods, people frequently perceive it as moving because of changes in their own viewpoint. Common situations include: Driving along winding roads, where the changing perspective makes the planet appear to follow the vehicle. Watching from a moving aircraft or boat. Shifting attention between foreground objects and the sky. Experiencing the visual illusion known as autokinesis, in which a stationary light appears to drift when viewed against a dark background for an extended period. These effects can create sincere reports of objects pacing cars, hovering above fields or slowly manoeuvring without producing any actual motion in the sky. Investigators have documented such perceptual errors for decades. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Identification studies of UFOs Project Blue Book) to provide a serious scientific investigation into UFOs.... Witnesses may believe the \"UFO\" was following them even t... Why witnesses often describe intelligent behaviour One striking feature of many Venus reports is that observers attribute purposeful movement to an object that is not moving at all. Several ordinary processes contribute to this impression: Observer motion makes the planet seem to maintain a constant distance from a vehicle. Atmospheric scintillation causes rapid changes in brightness and colour that resemble flashing lights. Cloud layers passing in front of the planet may briefly hide or reveal it, making it appear to accelerate or disappear. Expectation encourages the brain to interpret ambiguous visual information as meaningful behaviour. Once an observer concludes they are watching an aircraft or unknown craft rather than a planet, later perceptions are often interpreted within that framework. A brief flicker becomes a signal, while ordinary perspective changes become manoeuvres. Jupiter and Venus 'could be mistaken for UFOs' Channel: The Telegraph · Views: 22.9K · Uploaded: March 2012 · Length: 1 minute 57 seconds Open on YouTube Famous UFO investigations involving Venus The connection between Venus and UFO reports is not merely theoretical. It appears repeatedly throughout the history of official investigations. Project Blue Book , the U.S. Air Force programme that investigated thousands of UFO reports between 1947 and 1969, regularly identified astronomical objects among reported sightings. Modern summaries of the project note that bright planets and stars accounted for a significant share of reports initially considered mysterious. [U.S. Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue BookFrom 1947 to 1969, the Air Force investigated Unidentified Flying Obj... One of the best known examples is the 1966 Portage County UFO chase in Ohio. Police officers pursued what they believed was a low flying luminous object across multiple jurisdictions. Project Blue Book concluded that parts of the pursuit involved the planet Venus after the officers had already followed another object earlier in the event. That explanation became controversial because critics argued it failed to account for every stage of the chase, illustrating an important point: a real investigation may involve several ordinary stimuli rather than a single all encompassing explanation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book The history of Blue Book therefore demonstrates both sides of the issue. Venus genuinely explains many reports, but invoking it automatically without matching the timing, direction and geometry of a sighting risks replacing one weak explanation with another. Serious investigators compare astronomical positions with witness observations rather than assuming every bright light must be the planet. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Checking sky context before naming a mystery Because planets follow predictable paths, they are among the easiest UFO candidates to verify after the event. Useful questions include: Was Venus or Jupiter above the horizon at that location and time? Was the object visible for tens of minutes or even hours? Did it remain fixed relative to the stars while the observer moved? Did its apparent motion disappear when viewed from a stationary position? Were atmospheric conditions likely to produce strong scintillation near the horizon? Modern astronomy apps, planetarium software and official sky charts can answer these"},{"title":"Disappearances","url":"/disappearances/","description":"Missing-person stories can become Fortean when uncertainty, fear, weak records, and speculation fill the gaps.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Disappearances When Disappearances Become Fortean Stories Disappearances Disappearances When Disappearances Become Fortean Stories Missing person stories can become Fortean when uncertainty, fear, weak records, and speculation fill the gaps. Missing person stories can become Fortean when uncertainty, fear, weak records, and speculation fill the gaps. event time window What is known versus retold Search records and missing context Why uncertainty invites speculation forteana disappearances Introduction Missing person stories become Fortean when the absence of a clear ending is treated as evidence of a stranger reality. A person walks into woodland, boards a bus, works a lighthouse shift, crosses a border, or leaves home during a crisis; the record then breaks. In that gap, later retellings may add patterns, ominous details, symbolic geography, suppressed evidence or paranormal hints. The result can be compelling folklore , but it is also ethically risky because the “mystery” usually began as a real investigation, a family’s unresolved loss, and a set of records that may be incomplete rather than uncanny. Charles Fort helped define this territory by collecting anomalous reports that seemed rejected or “damned” by official explanation, including disappearances among many other oddities. Modern Fortean reading is most useful when it separates the documented case from the retold legend: what is known, what is inferred, what is rumour, and what has simply become memorable through repetition. Fort’s own method was literary and provocative, not a modern missing person protocol, so his legacy needs careful handling when real people are involved. [skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org Open source on skepticalinquirer.org. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube What is known versus retold A missing person case has two timelines. The first is the event window: the last confirmed sighting, the delay before alarm, the search, the police decisions, the weather , the terrain, and any physical evidence. The second is the retelling timeline: newspaper summaries, anniversary pieces, podcasts, folklore books, online maps, documentaries and speculative threads. Fortean stories often emerge when the second timeline becomes more famous than the first. The difference matters because small details can harden into “facts” after decades of repetition. A clean room becomes an “interrupted meal”; an official record gap becomes a “cover up”; a failed search becomes proof that the person could not have died nearby. The Flannan Isles lighthouse case shows this clearly. The Northern Lighthouse Board records that three keepers disappeared in December 1900 and that the discovery was made when the lighthouse tender Hesperus reached the island on 26 December. The Board’s own history points to severe damage at the west landing, supporting the practical possibility that the men were swept away while attending to storm damage. Later retellings, however, often emphasise eerie domestic details and supernatural atmosphere far more than the official maritime evidence. [Northern Lighthouse Board+2Open Book] nlb.org.uk Open source on nlb.org.uk. Paula Jean Welden’s disappearance near Bennington , Vermont, in 1946 is another useful example. The grounded version is already troubling enough: an 18 year old Bennington College student went for a walk towards the Long Trail on 1 December, was seen by witnesses, and was never found. The case exposed weaknesses in local law enforcement and helped spur the creation of the Vermont State Police. In later folklore, however, Welden is folded into the “Bennington Triangle”, a cluster of disappearances around Glastenbury Mountain, where the geography itself becomes part of the mystery. Vermont Public’s 2025 reporting notes that of the five people usually included in the Bennington Triangle story, only Welden appears on the current Vermont State Police missing person list; that does not solve the other cases, but it shows how a folklore cluster can outgrow the official record. [Vermont Public+2NamUs] vermontpublic.org Vermont Public How the 'Bennington Triangle' developed a cult following Vermont Public How the 'Bennington Triangle' developed a cult following This is the central Fortean tension: retellings preserve cases that might otherwise be forgotten, but they can also flatten them into motifs. A person becomes “the girl on the trail”, “the vanished lighthousemen”, “the missing writer”, or “one of the cluster”. The story becomes easier to remember, and sometimes less accurate. Search records and missing context Modern missing person work is built around risk, welfare and verification. In UK policing guidance, a person is treated as missing when their whereabouts cannot be established and their wellbeing cannot be confirmed; risk assessment is required for every missing report and should be based on the current circumstances rather than assumptions from the past. That is a very different frame from the Fortean habit of asking whether a case “defies explanation”. The operational question is not whether the story feels strange, but what action may find the person or establish what happened. [College of Policing] college.police.uk missing persons risk assessment missing persons risk assessment The scale of ordinary missing person work also changes the meaning of rarity. In the United States, the FBI’s 2024 National Crime Information Center report recorded 533,936 missing person records entered during the year, while 537,446 were removed, often because the person was located, returned home, or the record was otherwise cleared. At the end of 2024, 93,447 active missing person records remained in the system. These figures do not make unresolved cases less serious; they show why isolated anecdotes can mislead when they are detached from the large baseline of disappearances, returns, duplicate reports, jurisdictional differences and data limitations. [FBI] fbi.gov 2024 ncic missing and unidentified person statistics 2024 ncic missing and unidentified person statistics Databases help, but they do not remove uncertainty. NamUs, the US National Missing and Unidentified Persons System, describes itself as a national repository and resource centre for missing, unidentified and unclaimed person cases, designed to help investigators match long term missing people with unidentified remains. Its existence is a corrective to purely anecdotal mystery making, yet it also shows why records can be patchy: not every old case was documented to modern standards, and not every jurisdiction historically entered information in the same way. [NamUs] namus.nij.ojp.gov Open source on ojp.gov. Wilderness cases are especially vulnerable to Fortean inflation because search failure can look more mysterious than it is. Search and rescue research treats lost person behaviour as a practical field of study, not a paranormal puzzle. A 2022 modelling study using wilderness search and rescue data notes that thousands of people are reported lost in US wilderness areas each year and that rapid location depends on understanding how people move when lost. The same study reports that, in US national parks alone between 2004 and 2014, 46,609 people became lost and required a search and rescue campaign. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAn agent based model reveals lost person behavior PMCAn agent based model reveals lost person behavior Terrain also hides evidence. Dense canopy, steep ground, water, snow, animal activity, weather and searcher limits can all leave a missing person undiscovered even when the area was searched. Recent search and rescue research into drone assisted detection under forest canopy stresses that ordinary aerial views can miss people because vegetation conceals the ground level cues rescuers need. That kind of technical limitation is less dramatic than “vanished without a trace”, but it is often more useful. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. The Flannan Isles Lighthouse Disappearance Matt's Mysteries 14 Channel: Matt's Mysteries Open on YouTube Why uncertainty invites speculation Uncertainty is not an empty space; people fill it. Families need answers, investigators need leads, journalists need a narrative, and audiences are drawn to patterns. In Fortean retellings, three pressures often turn unresolved absence into uncanny story. First, there is the pressure of narrative neatness. Real missing person files may contain partial sightings, contradictory witness statements, poor weather, delayed reporting and dead ends. Retellings often trim that mess into a clean sequence: last seen, impossible clue, failed search, enduring mystery. The cleaner the story becomes, the more supernatural it can feel. Second, there is the pressure of pattern making. Fort’s famous question about Ambrose Bierce and Ambrose Small — whether “somebody” was collecting Ambroses — is a perfect"},{"title":"Ethics","url":"/ethics-e7c627/","description":"Unresolved disappearances are not just mysteries; they are living losses that can be harmed by careless supernatural framing.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Ethics When Mystery Retellings Hurt the Living Ethics Ethics When Mystery Retellings Hurt the Living Unresolved disappearances are not just mysteries; they are living losses that can be harmed by careless supernatural framing. Unresolved disappearances are not just mysteries; they are living losses that can be harmed by careless supernatural framing. mechanism What ambiguous loss means for families How entertainment framing can distort grief How to retell cases with care forteana disappearances ethics Introduction Missing person stories occupy a difficult place in Fortean culture. They invite speculation because the ending is unknown, yet that very uncertainty is also what makes them emotionally and ethically fraught. Behind many famous disappearances are relatives who continue living without confirmation of death, survival or return. Psychologist Pauline Boss described this state as ambiguous loss : a form of loss in which a person is physically absent but psychologically present because certainty never arrives. Rather than being a dramatic narrative device, ambiguous loss is a recognised framework for understanding the unique strain placed on families of missing people. [PubMed+2Missing People] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Author Pauline G Boss 1. Department of Family Social Science, St. Paul, MN, USA Ambiguous loss: working with families of the missingby PG Boss · 2002 · Cited by 248 — Ambiguous loss: working with families of the... Within Fortean retellings , this distinction matters. Supernatural explanations, conspiracy theories or folklore motifs may keep a case in public memory, but they can also reshape real people into symbols while obscuring the lived experience of those still searching for answers. Ethical retelling begins by recognising that unresolved disappearance is not simply a mystery to solve but an ongoing human condition. What ambiguous loss means for families Ambiguous loss differs fundamentally from bereavement after a confirmed death. A death certificate, funeral and recognised mourning rituals provide social acknowledgement that a loss has occurred, even if grief remains profound. Families of missing people often receive none of these markers. Instead, they may experience contradictory emotions simultaneously: Hope that the missing person is still alive. Fear that they have died. Guilt about imagining either possibility. Pressure from others to \"move on\". Anxiety whenever new information appears, even years later. Boss argues that the central problem is not a failure to grieve correctly but the absence of reliable knowledge. The uncertainty itself becomes the source of chronic stress. Therapeutic work therefore focuses less on achieving \"closure\" than on helping families live with unanswered questions while maintaining meaningful lives. [PubMed+2guildhumanservices.org] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Author Pauline G Boss 1. Department of Family Social Science, St. Paul, MN, USA Ambiguous loss: working with families of the missingby PG Boss · 2002 · Cited by 248 — Ambiguous loss: working with families of the... Research and support organisations working specifically with missing person families echo this point. The charity Missing People notes that relatives often struggle because society expects grief to follow familiar patterns that simply do not fit disappearance. Family members may disagree about what they believe happened, creating additional tension within households already coping with uncertainty. [Missing People] missingpeople.org.uk Missing People What does ambiguous loss mean? Missing PeopleWhat does ambiguous loss mean? Missing PeopleA researcher named Pauline Boss developed the concept of 'ambiguous loss', n... This perspective also explains why anniversaries, renewed searches, unidentified human remains or media coverage can reopen emotional wounds decades after the disappearance. Unlike conventional bereavement, the uncertainty never fully recedes. Ambiguous Loss 101 Channel: The Missed Foundation (Missed) Open on YouTube How entertainment framing can distort grief Fortean retellings frequently emphasise mystery because mystery attracts audiences. That emphasis can unintentionally alter how disappearances are understood. One common mechanism is narrative simplification . Complex investigations involving weather , search logistics, mental health, criminal possibilities or incomplete records become compressed into a memorable supernatural question. The individual becomes \"the vanished hiker\", \"the missing lighthouse keeper\" or \"one of the triangle disappearances\", while the ordinary details of their life recede. Another mechanism is pattern creation . Unrelated disappearances may be grouped into geographical \"hotspots\" or paranormal clusters. Such groupings can become culturally influential even when investigators find no evidence linking the cases. The resulting folklore may persist far longer than the original investigative record. Entertainment formats also reward unresolved endings. Podcasts, documentaries and online discussions often conclude with competing theories because uncertainty sustains audience engagement. Yet every speculative ending carries an ethical cost when presented without clear distinctions between documented evidence and imaginative interpretation. This does not mean mystery writing is inherently harmful. Public attention has occasionally generated new witnesses, revived investigations or preserved historical records that might otherwise have disappeared. The ethical question is therefore not whether people discuss unresolved cases, but how they distinguish established facts from speculation and how they portray the people involved. Why supernatural explanations deserve particular care Within Forteana , paranormal explanations often emerge because conventional answers appear incomplete. Missing evidence, unsuccessful searches or unusual circumstances can encourage interpretations involving portals, unknown creatures, alien abduction or other extraordinary causes. The ethical difficulty is that extraordinary explanations can change the meaning of a family's experience in several ways. First, they may shift attention away from verified evidence toward details selected primarily because they appear mysterious. Ordinary investigative uncertainties become interpreted as evidence of hidden forces. Second, supernatural framing can unintentionally invalidate relatives' own experiences. Families are frequently navigating police processes, legal complications, financial consequences and emotional uncertainty. Public fascination with paranormal theories may overshadow these practical realities. Third, speculative narratives often become remarkably persistent online. Even if investigators later identify more probable explanations or recover additional evidence, earlier paranormal versions may continue circulating because they are more memorable. Responsible Fortean discussion therefore requires recognising the difference between exploring folklore and making claims about what happened to identifiable people. Ambiguous Loss: Why Savannah Guthrie’s Grief Feels Personal Channel: Dr. Suzette Glasner Open on YouTube How to retell disappearance cases with care Ethical retelling does not require abandoning mystery. It requires treating uncertainty honestly and remembering that unresolved cases involve living communities rather than fictional characters. Useful practices include: Separate evidence from interpretation. Clearly distinguish confirmed facts, investigative findings, witness testimony and later speculation. Avoid treating absence as proof. An unexplained gap in evidence is not itself evidence of paranormal causes. Recognise ongoing ambiguity. Resist language implying certainty where none exists. Keep the missing person human. Include who they were, not only how they disappeared. Acknowledge surviving relatives. Their experience continues regardless of public fascination. Correct persistent myths where possible. Repeated inaccuracies can become more influential than original records. Avoid sensational titles or imagery. Dramatic framing may increase attention while diminishing empathy. These practices reflect broader principles used by organisations supporting families of missing people, which emphasise respectful language, careful media engagement and awareness that publicity can simultaneously assist investigations and deepen emotional strain. [Missing People] missingpeople.org.uk Missing People What does ambiguous loss mean? Missing PeopleWhat does ambiguous loss mean? Missing PeopleA researcher named Pauline Boss developed the concept of 'ambiguous loss', n... Why \"closure\" is often the wrong goal Popular culture frequently speaks about finding closure, but ambiguous loss theory challenges the assumption that closure is always possible or even desirable. Boss argues that insisting upon closure can become another burden placed upon families. When certainty does not exist, expecting"},{"title":"Flannan Isles","url":"/flannan-isles/","description":"The Flannan Isles case shows how maritime damage records can be overshadowed by eerie lighthouse folklore.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Flannan Isles Did the Flannan Keepers Vanish in a Storm? Flannan Isles Flannan Isles Did the Flannan Keepers Vanish in a Storm? The Flannan Isles case shows how maritime damage records can be overshadowed by eerie lighthouse folklore. The Flannan Isles case shows how maritime damage records can be overshadowed by eerie lighthouse folklore. event time window What the lighthouse records actually say How later retellings added eerie details Why the storm damage explanation still matters forteana disappearances flannan isles Introduction The disappearance of the three Flannan Isles lighthouse keepers in December 1900 remains one of Britain's most enduring maritime mysteries. In Fortean retellings , the case is often presented as an inexplicable vanishing surrounded by ominous details and supernatural suggestions. Yet the surviving official records tell a more grounded story. They point towards extreme weather , severe damage at the island's west landing and the hazards faced by lighthouse keepers working on one of the most exposed coastlines in the North Atlantic. While no definitive proof survives, the storm damage explanation remains the conclusion reached by the official investigation and is supported by physical evidence documented at the scene. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... Understanding the Flannan Isles case therefore requires separating what was recorded in 1900 from what later entered popular folklore . The mystery lies not in an absence of evidence, but in the fact that the available evidence can support only a probable explanation rather than a certain one. What the lighthouse records actually say The disappearance came to light on 26 December 1900, when the lighthouse tender Hesperus reached Eilean Mòr after bad weather had delayed its scheduled visit. Relief keeper Joseph Moore climbed to the lighthouse expecting to be greeted by the resident crew but found the buildings deserted. The lamps had been cleaned and refilled, indicating that routine duties had been completed before the men vanished. One set of waterproof oilskins remained inside, suggesting that one keeper had gone outside without full protective clothing. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... The missing men were: James Ducat, principal keeper. Thomas Marshall, second assistant. Donald McArthur, serving temporarily in place of an ill colleague. No signs of violence or struggle were discovered inside the lighthouse. The doors were closed, equipment appeared orderly and extensive searches failed to locate the keepers anywhere on the island. Bodies were never recovered, despite searches of the surrounding coastline. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... A further clue existed even before the Hesperus arrived. On 15 December, the passing steamer Archtor noted that the lighthouse was not showing its light. That report did not reach the Northern Lighthouse Board until after the disappearance had already been discovered because communications from passing ships were much slower than they are today. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... What most likely happened to the vanished keepers at Flannan Isles Lighthouse (mini documentary) Channel: Bad Things: True Crime Open on YouTube Why investigators focused on the west landing The strongest physical evidence was not inside the lighthouse but at the island's west landing, where supplies were normally unloaded. Robert Muirhead, the Northern Lighthouse Board superintendent who investigated the case, found remarkable storm damage: Storage equipment high above sea level had been smashed open. Iron railings had been bent. Sections of the railway used to move supplies had been torn from their concrete foundations. A massive rock had been displaced. Turf had been ripped away from the cliff top well above the shoreline. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Flannan Isles Lighthouse Flannan Isles Lighthouse Muirhead considered some of this destruction almost unbelievable without seeing it firsthand. The evidence suggested that exceptionally powerful seas had struck the western side of the island. His official conclusion was that the keepers had probably gone to secure equipment or inspect storm damage, where they were overwhelmed by an unusually large wave and swept into the sea. He believed two men had gone to the landing first and that the third had subsequently left the lighthouse, contrary to regulations requiring someone to remain with the light, perhaps after realising his colleagues were in danger. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Flannan Isles Lighthouse Flannan Isles Lighthouse Why the storm explanation remains credible To modern readers, the idea that experienced lighthouse keepers could all be swept away may seem unlikely. The geography of Eilean Mòr helps explain why investigators considered it plausible. The west landing sits within a narrow inlet where Atlantic swells can become concentrated. Powerful waves entering the confined space can rebound explosively against cliffs and surge far above what observers might expect from normal sea conditions. Even today, rogue waves—rare but exceptionally large waves formed through the interaction of swell systems, currents and coastal topography—are recognised hazards in exposed maritime environments. Several features strengthen the storm hypothesis: The documented damage demonstrates that exceptional wave action occurred. The missing equipment was located precisely where dangerous conditions were later observed. No evidence of criminal activity, fire or internal accident was found. The disappearance coincided with a period of severe weather affecting the region. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... The absence of recovered bodies does not seriously weaken this explanation. The Atlantic around the Outer Hebrides is characterised by strong currents, heavy seas and rocky coastlines that have historically prevented the recovery of many people lost overboard. The Flannan Isles Lighthouse Mystery Reprise Channel: Bedtime Stories Open on YouTube How later retellings added eerie details The Flannan Isles story gradually shifted from a maritime tragedy into one of Britain's best known supernatural mysteries. Among the most frequently repeated claims are: an untouched meal left on the table; an overturned chair suggesting someone fled in panic; clocks mysteriously stopped at the moment of disappearance; inexplicable diary entries describing terrifying storms, weeping keepers and desperate prayers before the event. These details have proved memorable because they create a dramatic narrative of sudden interruption. However, they have much weaker documentary support than the official investigation. Perhaps the best known example concerns the supposed lighthouse logbook. Popular accounts often quote entries claiming that experienced keeper Thomas Marshall described unprecedented storms, that Donald McArthur was crying, and that all three men prayed together before writing \"God is over all.\" These passages have appeared repeatedly in books, documentaries and online articles. Research into the surviving records has shown that these diary entries were not part of the original official documentation. Later investigators traced them to fictional embellishments introduced years after the disappearance rather than authentic lighthouse logs. The weather records also do not support the dramatic sequence described in those supposed entries. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Flannan Isles Lighthouse Flannan Isles Lighthouse Similarly, some famous domestic details appear to have grown in the retelling. Contemporary reports emphasise that the lighthouse had been left in orderly condition rather than portraying a room frozen in the middle of a dramatic event. [Northern Lighthouse Board] nlb.org.uk Northern Lighthouse BoardFlannan IslesTheir disappearance was only discovered as a result of the routine visit by Lighthouse Tender HESPE... Why the storm damage explanation still matters Within Fortean literature, the Flannan Isles disappearance illustrates how an unresolved event can evolve into folklore without requiring fabricated evidence. The genuine mystery is compelling enough: three experienced men disappeared from an isolated island and were never found. Yet the official record consistently points towards a hazardous working"},{"title":"Missing 411","url":"/missing-411/","description":"Missing 411-style storytelling often turns incomplete records and failed searches into stronger claims than the evidence can bear.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Missing 411 When Missing Person Patterns Go Too Far Missing 411 Missing 411 When Missing Person Patterns Go Too Far Missing 411 style storytelling often turns incomplete records and failed searches into stronger claims than the evidence can bear. Missing 411 style storytelling often turns incomplete records and failed searches into stronger claims than the evidence can bear. mechanism What the pattern claims usually rely on Why absence is not proof of anomaly How to read clusters without overclaiming forteana disappearances missing 411 Introduction The appeal of Missing 411 lies in a simple but powerful idea: that apparently unrelated disappearances in wilderness areas form a hidden pattern that ordinary explanations cannot account for. Within modern Fortean culture, these stories have become one of the best known examples of how missing person cases are transformed into a larger mystery. The central problem is not that disappearances never contain puzzling features—they often do—but that incomplete records, unsuccessful searches and selective comparisons can be presented as evidence for an underlying phenomenon without demonstrating that such a phenomenon actually exists. Independent analyses have repeatedly found a substantial gap between the strength of the claims and the evidence available to support them. [skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org An Investigation of the Missing411 ConspiracyPaulides has classified over 1,440 missing persons cases under the Missing411 label... Understanding that evidence gap is important because genuine missing person investigations are already difficult. Rugged terrain, changing weather , delayed reporting, wildlife, water, injury and simple bad luck frequently leave investigators with incomplete information. An unexplained outcome does not automatically imply an unexplained cause. What the pattern claims usually rely on The Missing 411 series, created by David Paulides, argues that numerous disappearances in North American wilderness areas share recurring characteristics. Rather than advancing one explicit explanation, the books and documentaries emphasise clusters of unusual details and encourage readers to conclude that ordinary search and rescue explanations are insufficient. [skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org An Investigation of the Missing411 ConspiracyPaulides has classified over 1,440 missing persons cases under the Missing411 label... The recurring features often highlighted include: People disappearing close to trails or campsites. Searches that fail despite apparently favourable conditions. Human remains later being found in places already searched. Young children or experienced outdoorspeople vanishing unexpectedly. Cases occurring near water, granite formations or berry patches. Sudden weather changes interfering with searches. Search dogs reportedly failing to follow scent trails. Taken individually, many of these details are genuine elements of particular investigations. The difficulty arises when they are assembled into a profile after the fact. Without knowing how often these same features occur in ordinary missing person cases, it is impossible to determine whether they represent meaningful signals or simply common characteristics of wilderness incidents. For example, bad weather naturally complicates searches, water attracts both visitors and wildlife, and mountainous terrain often contains granite. These features are common across national parks regardless of whether a disappearance is later considered mysterious. I Investigated the Strangest Missing People Cases (Missing 411) Channel: GroovyGavin Open on YouTube Why absence is not proof of anomaly The largest evidence gap concerns the treatment of missing information itself. A failed search does not establish that a person could not have been in the searched area. Search and rescue specialists have long documented that vegetation, terrain, weather, fatigue, limited visibility and imperfect search coverage mean people are sometimes overlooked, even during extensive operations. Human remains have also been found months or years later in areas previously searched, not because they appeared there later, but because difficult environments make complete detection extraordinarily hard. Similarly, the absence of an identified cause is not evidence for an extraordinary cause. Many wilderness disappearances end without a definitive explanation because: no witnesses observed the critical event; environmental exposure destroys physical evidence; scavengers disperse remains; waterways transport bodies far from the disappearance point; injuries may prevent a missing person from signalling rescuers. Each missing fact increases uncertainty, but uncertainty itself is not positive evidence for a paranormal or unknown mechanism. This distinction is fundamental in both scientific reasoning and criminal investigation. A hypothesis requires evidence supporting it; it cannot be confirmed simply because competing explanations remain incomplete. [skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org An Investigation of the Missing411 ConspiracyPaulides has classified over 1,440 missing persons cases under the Missing411 label... How to read clusters without overclaiming Humans are exceptionally good at recognising patterns, even when none exist. This tendency becomes especially powerful when examining hundreds or thousands of cases after they have already occurred. If enough variables are available—weather, age, terrain, season, water, clothing, occupation, geology or countless others—some combinations will inevitably recur. This is sometimes described as a multiple comparisons problem. Once investigators begin searching retrospectively for common features, apparent clusters become much easier to discover than they would be in advance. A stronger demonstration would require questions such as: How frequently does this feature occur in all wilderness disappearances? Is the claimed feature significantly more common than expected? Were the selection criteria defined before examining the cases? Are contradictory examples included or excluded? Without this type of baseline comparison, recurring characteristics may simply reflect what is already common among people who become lost in wilderness environments. Independent statistical reviews of available Missing 411 datasets have concluded that the reported patterns do not clearly exceed what would be expected from ordinary wilderness accidents and disappearances. [skepticalinquirer.org+2skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org An Investigation of the Missing411 ConspiracyPaulides has classified over 1,440 missing persons cases under the Missing411 label... The Missing 411 Mystery Has A Solution. You Won't Like It. Channel: Joe Scott Open on YouTube Selection bias and changing case narratives Another recurring criticism concerns case selection. The books do not analyse every wilderness disappearance. Instead, they select cases considered to fit a developing profile. This creates a risk of selection bias: examples matching the proposed pattern receive attention, while ordinary looking cases remain outside the dataset. Researchers have also identified instances where later discoveries or official findings altered the understanding of individual disappearances but did not always receive equal prominence in later retellings . Some people featured in Missing 411 discussions were eventually found alive or had conventional explanations emerge after publication. Including resolved and unresolved cases together can make the overall collection appear more mysterious than the underlying evidence warrants. [skepticalinquirer.org] skepticalinquirer.org Assessing the Missing 411 Conspiracy Theory29 Mar 2023 — This conspiracy theory is the invention of a single individual: Bigfoot proponen... The same issue affects descriptive details. Different retellings may emphasise particular facts—such as unusual weather, unusual clothing or unexpected locations of remains—while giving less attention to evidence that points towards accident, exposure, drowning or medical emergency. None of this proves deliberate distortion in every instance. It illustrates how narrative emphasis can gradually reshape readers' impressions of the underlying investigations. David Paulides is Lying About Missing 411 Channel: The Lore Lodge Open on YouTube The National Park database debate One element of the Missing 411 story that attracted legitimate public attention concerns record keeping. Paulides argued that the US National Park Service lacked a comprehensive, easily accessible database of missing persons across all parks. Journalists confirmed that record management between parks and agencies had historically been inconsistent, prompting broader discussion about how disappearances should be tracked nationally. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia David PaulidesMissing 411 is a series of self published books and films, which document cases of people"},{"title":"Data Checks","url":"/data-checks/","description":"Large missing-person datasets help separate memorable anecdotes from the scale and messiness of real disappearance records.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Data Checks What Databases Do to Mystery Stories Data Checks Data Checks What Databases Do to Mystery Stories Large missing person datasets help separate memorable anecdotes from the scale and messiness of real disappearance records. Large missing person datasets help separate memorable anecdotes from the scale and messiness of real disappearance records. dataset evidence What NCIC figures show about scale How Nam US helps connect missing and unidentified cases Why databases still leave room for uncertainty forteana disappearances data checks Introduction Fortean retellings often gain their power from the impression that a disappearance is unique, inexplicable or ignored by official systems. In reality, missing person investigations take place within large national databases that record hundreds of thousands of reports, many of which are resolved quickly while others remain open for years. The United States' National Crime Information Center (NCIC) and the National Missing and Unidentified Persons System (NamUs) do not explain individual mysteries, but they provide an important reality check. They replace anecdote with documented records, show how common missing person reports actually are, and create opportunities to connect missing people with unidentified remains. At the same time, they reveal that incomplete records and unresolved cases are genuine features of real investigations, not automatic evidence of hidden conspiracies or paranormal causes. [NamUs] namus.nij.ojp.gov NamUs: HomeNamUs is a national centralized repository and resource center for missing, unidentified, and unclaimed person cases acro... What NCIC figures show about scale The NCIC, operated by the FBI, is the principal national law enforcement database for missing person records in the United States. It is not a public storytelling archive but an operational system used by police agencies to enter, update and clear missing person reports. For readers encountering Fortean claims that disappearances are unusually frequent in one location or that authorities systematically ignore missing people, NCIC statistics provide essential context. Every year, hundreds of thousands of missing person records are entered into the database. The overwhelming majority are later cleared when the individual is found or the case is otherwise resolved. For example, the FBI reported that 498,038 missing person records were entered into NCIC during 2025, while 503,409 records were cleared during the same year. At the end of that year, around 90,000 active records remained. These figures illustrate both the enormous scale of missing person reporting and the fact that unresolved cases represent only a fraction of annual reports. [FBI] fbi.gov 2025 ncic missing person and unidentified person statistics 2025 NCIC MISSING AND UNIDENTIFIED PERSONFeb 4, 2026 — During 2025, 498,038 missing person records were entered into NCIC. Missing Per... This distinction matters because folklore frequently cites the annual number of reports without mentioning how many are resolved. A claim that \"hundreds of thousands of people vanish every year\" is technically derived from reporting statistics, but it becomes misleading if presented as though all of those people disappeared permanently. NCIC data demonstrate that missing person reports include a wide range of circumstances, from vulnerable adults and people experiencing mental health crises to children who quickly return home, alongside the comparatively smaller number of long term unexplained disappearances. [FBI] fbi.gov 2025 ncic missing person and unidentified person statistics 2025 NCIC MISSING AND UNIDENTIFIED PERSONFeb 4, 2026 — During 2025, 498,038 missing person records were entered into NCIC. Missing Per... Another useful corrective is that NCIC records are administrative rather than narrative. The database does not record whether a disappearance feels mysterious or resembles a folklore pattern. It records legally significant facts that can support investigation, identification and recovery. The National Missing and Unidentified Persons System (NamUs) Channel: WebsEdgeSociety Open on YouTube How NamUs helps connect missing and unidentified cases Where NCIC functions primarily as a law enforcement information system, NamUs was designed to solve one of the biggest practical problems in missing person investigations: connecting separate records that may describe the same individual. NamUs is a national repository supported through the U.S. Department of Justice that contains missing person cases, unidentified human remains and unclaimed person records. Unlike NCIC alone, it integrates forensic information such as dental records, fingerprints, DNA profiles, anthropological analysis and photographs where available. It also provides analytical tools that help investigators compare missing persons with unidentified remains across jurisdictional boundaries. [NamUs+2NamUs] namus.nij.ojp.gov NamUs: HomeNamUs is a national centralized repository and resource center for missing, unidentified, and unclaimed person cases acro... This capability has direct relevance to Fortean retellings. Many mystery narratives assume that a vanished person simply \"disappeared without trace.\" In practice, unidentified remains may exist in another county or state, under a different case number and investigated by a different agency. NamUs exists precisely because such fragmented record keeping has historically prevented cases from being linked. Since its creation, NamUs has assisted in tens of thousands of case resolutions and facilitated thousands of identifications through forensic services including DNA comparison, fingerprint examination, forensic odontology and, more recently, forensic genetic genealogy. These successes illustrate that apparently impossible disappearances are sometimes resolved through improved data sharing rather than dramatic new evidence. [RTI] rti.org namus national missing unidentified persons system NAMUS: Forensic Genealogy & Missing Persons StatisticsJun 19, 2024 — NamUs currently houses records for more than 54,000 active missin... For readers interested in Forteana , this is an important shift in perspective. A mystery can persist for decades not because it is supernatural but because records, biological evidence and investigative jurisdictions remained disconnected until new databases or forensic methods brought them together. Why databases still leave room for uncertainty Neither NCIC nor NamUs should be treated as complete or infallible records of every disappearance. NCIC depends on information entered and maintained by law enforcement agencies. NamUs also relies on agencies and authorised users to submit and update cases. Although some U.S. states require eligible cases to be entered into NamUs, nationwide participation has historically varied, meaning that not every missing person or unidentified decedent appears in the public facing system. [NamUs] namus.nij.ojp.gov NamUs: HomeNamUs is a national centralized repository and resource center for missing, unidentified, and unclaimed person cases acro... The databases also serve different purposes. NCIC includes operational records that are not all publicly accessible, whereas NamUs selectively publishes unresolved cases and supports investigations with forensic services. Consequently, the absence of a case from NamUs should not automatically be interpreted as evidence that authorities ignored it or concealed information. Equally, an unresolved listing does not imply paranormal circumstances. It may reflect missing biological evidence, unidentified remains lacking usable DNA, incomplete historical documentation, jurisdictional complexity or the simple fact that no confirmed lead has yet emerged. Modern forensic techniques continue to resolve cases that remained open for decades, demonstrating that uncertainty often reflects investigative limits rather than inexplicable events. [RTI] rti.org namus national missing unidentified persons system NAMUS: Forensic Genealogy & Missing Persons StatisticsJun 19, 2024 — NamUs currently houses records for more than 54,000 active missin... Introducing NamUs 2.0 Channel: Office of Justice Programs Open on YouTube Overview of NamUs the National Missing and Unidentified Persons System Channel: National Institute of Justice Open on YouTube Reading mystery stories alongside official records For anyone examining missing person folklore, NCIC and NamUs are best understood as baseline reference points rather than final authorities. Before accepting claims that a disappearance was uniquely mysterious, several questions become useful: Was the individual officially reported missing and entered into national systems? [reveilleadvisors.com] reveilleadvisors.com national missing persons database NamUs helps report and find missing persons and unidentified remains... Does the case remain active, or was it later resolved? Has it been compared against unidentified"},{"title":"Bennington","url":"/bennington/","description":"Paula Jean Welden's disappearance shows how one documented case can become the anchor for a larger folklore cluster.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Bennington How One Trail Case Became a Triangle Bennington Bennington How One Trail Case Became a Triangle Paula Jean Welden's disappearance shows how one documented case can become the anchor for a larger folklore cluster. Paula Jean Welden's disappearance shows how one documented case can become the anchor for a larger folklore cluster. entity The confirmed Long Trail sighting How the Bennington Triangle formed around the case What official records do and do not support forteana disappearances bennington Introduction Paula Jean Welden's disappearance is one of the best examples of how a documented missing person case can become the centrepiece of a larger Fortean legend. On 1 December 1946, the 18 year old Bennington College student left campus for what appeared to be an afternoon walk on Vermont's Long Trail. Multiple witnesses saw her during the journey, yet she was never seen again. The investigation exposed weaknesses in Vermont's law enforcement system and ultimately contributed to the creation of the Vermont State Police. Decades later, however, the case acquired a second life as the most famous episode in the folklore of the \"Bennington Triangle\", a supposed zone of mysterious disappearances centred on Glastenbury Mountain. Understanding the difference between the historical investigation and the later legend is essential to understanding why the case continues to occupy such an important place in Fortean writing. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden The confirmed Long Trail sighting The documented sequence of events is comparatively well established. After finishing work in the Bennington College dining hall, Welden changed clothes and left campus during the afternoon. She hitchhiked towards the Long Trail, a popular hiking route that crossed Vermont Route 9 near Woodford Hollow. Several independent witnesses placed her along this route. One motorist gave her a lift for part of the journey. Hikers later recalled speaking with her after she asked about the trail, and the final widely accepted sighting placed her continuing north along what is now Harbour Road towards the wooded section of the Long Trail. Witnesses reported that she disappeared around a bend in the road, after which nobody could account for her movements. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden What makes the case so enduring is not a lack of witnesses but the abrupt end of reliable evidence. There were no confirmed sightings after she entered the more isolated section of the trail, no verified belongings recovered, and no physical evidence that clearly indicated whether she became lost, suffered an accident, or encountered another person. The subsequent search was extensive for its time. Volunteers, National Guard personnel, local officials and investigators from neighbouring states searched the Long Trail, nearby roads and surrounding woodland. Despite weeks of searching and numerous reported leads, nothing conclusively established what happened to Welden. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Lost in the Wilderness: The Unexplained Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Channel: Uncharted Enigmas Open on YouTube How the Bennington Triangle formed around the case The phrase \"Bennington Triangle\" did not exist when Paula Welden disappeared. It was coined decades later by Vermont author and broadcaster Joseph A. Citro, who grouped several disappearances between 1945 and 1950 into a single geographical mystery centred on Glastenbury Mountain. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Bennington Triangle Bennington Triangle Within that framework, Welden's disappearance became the defining case because it possessed several qualities that lend themselves to folklore: a young person vanishing during daylight; multiple credible witnesses establishing a clear last known route; an intensive but unsuccessful search; an absence of physical evidence; an unresolved ending that left room for speculation. Once these features were combined with other disappearances in the wider region, later retellings increasingly shifted attention away from the individual investigation and towards the idea that the landscape itself possessed unusual properties. Paranormal books, documentaries and internet discussions frequently describe disappearances, strange lights, unusual wildlife or unexplained phenomena as if they formed parts of a single continuing mystery, even though the cases differ greatly in circumstance. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Bennington Triangle Bennington Triangle From a Fortean perspective, this illustrates how a genuine historical mystery can become the anchor for a broader narrative pattern. The unanswered question of what happened to one individual is gradually reframed as evidence for an anomalous location. What official records do and do not support Officially, Paula Jean Welden remains an unsolved missing person case. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden The historical record supports several core facts: she travelled towards the Long Trail, was observed by multiple witnesses, disappeared on 1 December 1946, and was never located despite an extensive search. Contemporary criticism of the investigation also appears well founded. Vermont lacked a statewide police force, coordination between agencies proved difficult, and the handling of the search attracted significant public criticism. Within months, the state established the Vermont State Police, with Welden's case widely recognised as a major catalyst for that reform. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden The official record does not establish that the disappearance was connected to any paranormal phenomenon or that the various disappearances later grouped into the Bennington Triangle shared a common cause. Likewise, there is no verified evidence demonstrating murder, accidental death, suicide or voluntary disappearance. Numerous theories have circulated over the decades—including becoming lost, dying from exposure, abduction or homicide—but none has been supported by conclusive evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden This distinction matters because later accounts often present the Bennington Triangle as though it were an established investigative category rather than a retrospective folklore label. The legend connects cases through geography and atmosphere, whereas official investigations treated each disappearance on its own evidence. The Enduring Mystery of the Paula Welden Case Channel: Bennington Museum Open on YouTube The Bennington Triangle: One Mountain, Five Vanishings, and a Wilderness That Took Them One by One Channel: Chalkline File Open on YouTube Why the case remains central to Fortean retellings Paula Jean Welden occupies a distinctive position in Fortean literature because her disappearance satisfies two different audiences simultaneously. For historians and investigators, it represents a significant unsolved case that exposed institutional weaknesses and influenced policing in Vermont. For Fortean writers, it provides an unresolved narrative that naturally invites pattern seeking. The absence of a known ending allows later storytellers to connect it with regional folklore, abandoned settlements around Glastenbury Mountain and other disappearances without needing definitive evidence linking them. The case has also influenced popular culture beyond paranormal writing. Novelists and documentary makers have repeatedly drawn upon the mystery, helping preserve public awareness of Welden long after many comparable missing person cases faded from memory. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Bennington Triangle Bennington Triangle Seen through the lens of Forteana , Paula Jean Welden's disappearance demonstrates how folklore often develops not because extraordinary evidence accumulates, but because an unresolved historical event acquires symbolic weight. The documented disappearance remains unchanged; what evolves over time is the interpretive framework surrounding it. The Bennington Triangle legend is therefore best understood not as an officially recognised mystery zone, but as a cultural narrative built around one of New England's most enduring unsolved disappearances. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Disappearance of Paula Jean Welden Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How One Trail Case Became a Triangle. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Book of the Damned By Charles Fort Connects the case to the wider tradition of anomaly collecting. See on Amazon Book The vanishing hitchhiker By Jan Harold Brunvand Explains how narratives grow beyond documented events. See on Amazon Book The Vermont Ghost Guide By"},{"title":"Search Limits","url":"/search-limits/","description":"Failed searches can look impossible in retellings, but terrain, weather, canopy and human limits often explain why evidence stays hidden.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Disappearances","level":3,"text":"Search Limits Why Searched Ground Still Hides People Search Limits Search Limits Why Searched Ground Still Hides People Failed searches can look impossible in retellings, but terrain, weather, canopy and human limits often explain why evidence stays hidden. Failed searches can look impossible in retellings, but terrain, weather, canopy and human limits often explain why evidence stays hidden. population context How terrain changes what searchers can see Why woods and weather distort certainty How search limits become Fortean claims forteana disappearances search limits Introduction One of the most persistent claims in Fortean retellings of wilderness disappearances is that a person \"vanished despite extensive searches\". Presented without context, this can sound almost impossible. In reality, search and rescue professionals have long documented that even intensive operations can fail to locate someone who is relatively close to search teams. Dense vegetation, broken terrain, weather, limited visibility, fatigue, imperfect search coverage and the unpredictable behaviour of lost people all reduce the probability of detection. Search failure is therefore not unusual in wilderness incidents, even when trained personnel, dogs, aircraft and volunteers are involved. Studies of search and rescue operations have led to increasingly sophisticated models of lost person behaviour precisely because simple assumptions about where someone \"must be\" repeatedly proved wrong. [alpine rescue.org+2PMC] alpine rescue.org Lost Person Behavior – The Science of Search and RescueWhat are the probabilities? • 25% and 50% rings shown. • 9% within 100 meters. • 5... Within Fortean literature, however, unsuccessful searches often become transformed into evidence that conventional explanations cannot account for the disappearance. Understanding how real searches work helps explain why this narrative develops without requiring a paranormal cause. Why searched ground can still hide people The phrase \"they searched the area\" often creates a misleading mental picture of every square metre being examined. Wilderness searches rarely work like that. Search teams instead work from probabilities. Incident commanders estimate where a missing person is most likely to be based on the person's age, experience , health, likely decisions, terrain, available tracks, watercourses and elapsed time. Resources are then concentrated where detection is most likely rather than evenly covering an entire landscape. Modern search planning uses statistical models developed from thousands of previous incidents because exhaustive searches are usually impossible within the available time. [alpine rescue.org+2ResearchGate] alpine rescue.org Lost Person Behavior – The Science of Search and RescueWhat are the probabilities? • 25% and 50% rings shown. • 9% within 100 meters. • 5... Even when searchers physically pass through an area, this does not guarantee they will detect someone. Detection depends on numerous interacting factors, including: vegetation density and overhead canopy; slopes, gullies, rock outcrops and fallen timber; lighting conditions and shadows; weather , rain or fresh snowfall; searcher spacing; the posture and visibility of the missing person; whether the person is moving, unconscious or concealed. Search theory therefore distinguishes between searching an area and successfully detecting everything within it. Probability of detection is almost never 100%. [alpine rescue.org+2pure.port.ac.uk] alpine rescue.org Lost Person Behavior – The Science of Search and RescueWhat are the probabilities? • 25% and 50% rings shown. • 9% within 100 meters. • 5... Lost Person Behaviour BC AdventureSmart Winter Series Channel: BC Search and Rescue Association Open on YouTube How terrain changes what searchers can see Forests frequently appear much more open in photographs or aerial imagery than they do at ground level. Mature woodland may permit visibility of only a few metres in some directions, while ravines, boulder fields and dense undergrowth create numerous blind spots. Research into wilderness search behaviour has shown that terrain strongly influences both where lost people travel and how easily they can be found. People often follow paths of least resistance, drift downhill, follow streams or continue along linear features, but they may also become trapped in vegetation, fall into depressions or remain concealed beneath natural cover. Modern Bayesian search models explicitly incorporate terrain characteristics because they measurably affect both movement and detection. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net September 2010; Computational...Read more... Published: September 2010 These realities help explain why remains are occasionally discovered months or years later in locations that had previously received search attention. Such discoveries are frequently described in folklore as proof that someone \"appeared where they could not have been before\". Investigators are usually more cautious, recognising that difficult terrain and incomplete detection provide a simpler explanation. Why woods and weather distort certainty Weather changes both the landscape and the evidence left within it. Heavy rain removes footprints, alters scent conditions for search dogs and changes water levels. Snow may conceal tracks while simultaneously preserving others. Wind disperses scent unpredictably through valleys and forests. Fog dramatically reduces visibility for both aircraft and ground teams. Time also works against investigators. As hours pass, the potential search area expands rapidly because a mobile person may continue walking for many kilometres. Search resources, however, remain finite. This mismatch between an expanding search radius and limited personnel is one reason why early assumptions are often revised as new information emerges. [ResearchGate+2AIDR Knowledge Hub] researchgate.net September 2010; Computational...Read more... Published: September 2010 Retellings commonly compress this evolving process into a single statement such as \"hundreds searched everywhere\". The operational reality is far more complicated, involving changing priorities, probability maps and repeated reassessments. Why human behaviour complicates searches A second source of misunderstanding is the assumption that lost people behave logically. Search and rescue databases have shown that different categories of missing people display different patterns. Experienced hikers, children, people with dementia and injured individuals often make very different decisions after becoming lost. Some remain close to the point where they realised they were disoriented. Others continue walking for many hours. Some intentionally avoid steep ground, while others descend into drainages or follow streams because they believe these will lead to civilisation. [alpine rescue.org+2socialsciencespace.com] alpine rescue.org Lost Person Behavior – The Science of Search and RescueWhat are the probabilities? • 25% and 50% rings shown. • 9% within 100 meters. • 5... These behavioural differences explain why search planning increasingly relies on statistical evidence rather than intuition. They also undermine a common Fortean claim that a person was found \"too far away\" or \"in an impossible location\". Sometimes the individual simply behaved differently from what relatives, witnesses or later commentators expected. How search limits become Fortean claims Many recurring motifs in wilderness mystery literature originate from ordinary features of search operations rather than unexplained events. A typical narrative progression looks like this: When hikers vanish, how modern searches really work Policing Matters Channel: Police1 Open on YouTube 1. A difficult search produces no immediate result. 2. Public reporting simplifies the search into \"the whole area was searched\". 3. Later retellings omit operational limitations such as weather, darkness or search spacing. 4. The absence of evidence becomes treated as evidence that the person could not have been present. 5. The disappearance acquires paranormal interpretations involving portals, unknown predators or other anomalous forces. This pattern illustrates how uncertainty gradually hardens into apparent impossibility. The key transformation occurs not during the search itself but during years of retelling. Introduction to Search and Rescue Channel: Ionia County Search And Rescue Open on YouTube Case patterns rather than impossible events Several well known disappearance stories frequently cited in Fortean discussions rely heavily on the claim that victims were \"missed\" during earlier searches. Yet subsequent recoveries often occurred in environments known to present poor visibility, heavy vegetation or complex topography. Search and rescue professionals generally regard these recoveries as reminders that negative search results must be interpreted cautiously rather than as evidence that the missing person"},{"title":"Mothman","url":"/mothman/","description":"Mothman shows how sightings can become folklore, tourism, and local identity as well as an evidence debate.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Mothman How Did Mothman Become More Than a Sighting? Mothman Mothman How Did Mothman Become More Than a Sighting? Mothman shows how sightings can become folklore, tourism, and local identity as well as an evidence debate. Mothman shows how sightings can become folklore, tourism, and local identity as well as an evidence debate. entity The 1966 sighting wave Disaster, prophecy, and retelling Museums, festivals, and town identity forteana mothman Introduction Mothman became more than a sighting because Point Pleasant, West Virginia, turned a brief wave of frightening reports into a durable local legend: first through newspaper attention and eyewitness storytelling, then through the trauma shadow of the 1967 Silver Bridge disaster, and later through museums, festivals, statues, tours, films and cryptid tourism . For Forteana , the case matters because it shows how an anomaly can survive even when the evidence remains disputed. Mothman is not just a question of whether witnesses saw an unknown creature, an owl, a crane, a prank, or something misremembered. It is also a case study in how a community can convert uncertainty into identity. The legend now belongs simultaneously to local history, paranormal culture, sceptical debate and the visitor economy, which is why Point Pleasant remains central to the story nearly six decades after the original reports. [West Virginia Encyclopedia] wvencyclopedia.org West Virginia Encyclopediae WV West Virginia Encyclopediae WV The 1966 Sighting Wave The classic Mothman story begins on 15 November 1966, when Roger and Linda Scarberry and Steve and Mary Mallette reported a strange figure near the former wartime munitions area north of Point Pleasant, commonly called the TNT area . Accounts vary in details, but the core image became fixed quickly: a tall, winged, humanoid or bird like figure, with striking red eyes, seen by car headlights in a lonely industrial landscape. The next day’s local newspaper headline captured the uncertainty better than later retellings often do: the witnesses had seen a “man sized bird”, a “creature”, or “something”. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu Open source on si.edu. That uncertainty is one reason the case became so effective as folklore. The first reports did not arrive as a polished monster myth. They were awkward, partial and hard to classify. Was the figure a bird? A man with wings? A misperceived animal? A frightening encounter exaggerated under stress? The early language left room for belief, scepticism and imaginative expansion. The West Virginia Encyclopedia summarises the sighting wave as 26 reports over roughly a year, centred on Point Pleasant and often near the abandoned TNT plant, with sceptics at the time and since proposing explanations such as a stray sandhill crane. [West Virginia Encyclopedia] wvencyclopedia.org West Virginia Encyclopediae WV West Virginia Encyclopediae WV The setting did a great deal of narrative work. The TNT area was not an ordinary country lane; it was a landscape of bunkers, industrial remains, woods and wildlife habitat. Later sceptical discussion has repeatedly pointed to the area’s birds and night time conditions as plausible contributors to misidentification. Audubon’s investigation, for example, treats owl eyeshine and owl body shape as serious natural explanations, noting how a nocturnal bird caught in headlights can produce precisely the kind of unnerving eye effect that witnesses remembered. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Audubon Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Audubon At the same time, reducing the legend to a bird does not explain why this particular “bird” became Mothman. Many people misidentify animals; few misidentifications become town mascots. What turned Point Pleasant’s reports into Forteana was the way the sightings accumulated around a recognisable image, a repeated place, and a community mood. Newspaper coverage gave scattered claims a public frame. Night drives to the TNT area became a kind of legend trip. New reports were interpreted in light of earlier ones, so each account helped teach the next witness, reader or visitor what Mothman was supposed to look and feel like. The resulting pattern is typical of strong Fortean cases. There is enough testimony to make the story socially real, but not enough stable evidence to settle it scientifically. The witnesses’ sincerity, the eerie setting, the striking red eye motif and the absence of a captured animal all kept the story open. Sceptical explanations became part of the legend rather than ending it, because they gave believers something to resist and undecided readers something to weigh. Disaster, Prophecy and Retelling Mothman’s transformation from local scare to enduring legend cannot be separated from the Silver Bridge collapse on 15 December 1967. The bridge connected Point Pleasant, West Virginia, with Kanauga, Ohio; when it failed at about 5 p.m., 46 people died, nine were injured, and 31 of the 37 vehicles on the bridge fell with the structure. The official engineering explanation is not paranormal: the National Transportation Safety Board determined that the collapse began with a fracture in eyebar 330 at joint C13N, caused by a critical flaw that developed over the bridge’s life through stress corrosion and corrosion fatigue. [NTSB] ntsb.gov Open source on ntsb.gov. That official explanation is important because it marks the boundary between tragedy and prophecy. The bridge did not collapse because a winged figure appeared in the sky. It collapsed because a vulnerable bridge component failed in a way that inspection methods of the period could not detect without disassembling the joint. Yet folklore often attaches meaning to disaster after the fact, especially when a community has already been living with rumours, anxiety and anomalous reports. In Point Pleasant, the timing gave later storytellers a powerful structure: a year of sightings, then catastrophe, then silence. [West Virginia Encyclopedia] wvencyclopedia.org West Virginia Encyclopediae WV West Virginia Encyclopediae WV John Keel’s 1975 book The Mothman Prophecies did more than preserve the legend; it reshaped it. The West Virginia Encyclopedia notes that Keel connected Mothman to other strange reports, including alleged electronic disturbances, unidentified flying objects, “men in black” anecdotes and the Silver Bridge collapse. The 2002 film adaptation then carried that version of the story to a much wider audience, making prophecy, dread and impending disaster central to the popular understanding of Mothman. [West Virginia Encyclopedia] wvencyclopedia.org West Virginia Encyclopediae WV West Virginia Encyclopediae WV This is where Mothman becomes especially useful for understanding Forteana. A sceptical reader may see the prophecy layer as retrospective pattern making: a disaster gave older sightings new significance. A believer may see the sightings as warnings, omens or manifestations. A folklorist may focus less on deciding between those positions and more on how the connection works: a monster story becomes a disaster story, and a disaster story becomes a place memory carried through popular culture. The legend’s later growth also depended on disputed source material. Folklore scholar David Clarke describes the revival of the Mothman legend as a process involving believers, sceptics and people in between, and notes that later reassessments have proposed prank activity, media influence, large birds and other non extraordinary explanations. Clarke also points out that some auxiliary stories around the wider Mothman/UFO complex were later treated as invented or dubious, which makes the case a good example of how Fortean narratives can gather both testimony and embellishment over time. [SHURA] shura.shu.ac.uk Open source on shu.ac.uk. That does not make the legend worthless. It makes it layered. The eyewitness reports, the bridge disaster, Keel’s narrative, the film, the sceptical responses and the town’s later embrace are not the same kind of evidence. They answer different questions. Eyewitness accounts concern what people believed they saw. Engineering reports explain why the bridge fell. Folklore and media history explain how the two became linked in memory. Mothman survives because all three layers remain active. The Silver Bridge Disaster: 46 People Died Because of a Defect in One Piece of Steel Channel: The Fault Line Open on YouTube Why Mothman Fits Forteana So Well Mothman is often described as a cryptid, but the Point Pleasant case is broader than a possible unknown animal. It also includes urban legend, disaster memory, UFO era anxiety, media feedback, local tourism and the psychology of perception. That hybridity is exactly why it belongs in Forteana. The case does not sit neatly inside zoology, ghost lore, religious omen tradition or engineering history; it crosses all of them without being fully explained by any one"},{"title":"Bird Theory","url":"/bird-theory/","description":"Natural explanations do not end the Mothman story, but owls, cranes and night-time perception remain central to the debate.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"Bird Theory Was Mothman Really a Misidentified Bird? Bird Theory Bird Theory Was Mothman Really a Misidentified Bird? Natural explanations do not end the Mothman story, but owls, cranes and night time perception remain central to the debate. Natural explanations do not end the Mothman story, but owls, cranes and night time perception remain central to the debate. mechanism Eyeshine, silhouettes and headlights Owls, cranes and the TNT wildlife setting Why a plausible bird still became folklore forteana mothman bird theory Introduction The most widely discussed natural explanation for the original Mothman reports is not an undiscovered creature but a misidentified bird seen under difficult conditions. Since the first Point Pleasant sightings in November 1966, sceptics, wildlife experts and folklore researchers have repeatedly suggested that large owls, sandhill cranes or other unusually large birds could account for at least some of the eyewitness descriptions. This idea does not claim that every report involved the same animal or that all witnesses were mistaken in the same way. Instead, it argues that darkness, surprise, car headlights and an unfamiliar setting can transform an ordinary encounter into an extraordinary memory. The debate remains central to Forteana because the bird hypothesis is plausible enough to challenge paranormal interpretations while still leaving genuine questions about why the legend became so powerful. [Audubon+2wvencyclopedia.org] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 Eyeshine, silhouettes and headlights One of the strongest parts of the bird explanation concerns the famous glowing red eyes. Many nocturnal birds exhibit intense eyeshine when illuminated by vehicle headlights or bright torches. Rather than producing light themselves, their eyes reflect incoming light from specialised tissue behind the retina, making them appear to glow dramatically in darkness. Depending on the viewing angle, colour balance and surrounding conditions, witnesses may perceive this reflection as orange or red. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 Night time perception is also much less reliable than people often assume. Human observers estimate size, distance and speed poorly in unfamiliar, low light environments. A bird perched on a post, tree or embankment can appear much larger if only partly illuminated. If it suddenly takes flight as a car approaches, its expanding wingspan may create the impression that a human shaped figure has unfolded into the air. Audubon's review of the case notes that barred owls, in particular, combine forward facing eyes, broad wings and striking eyeshine in ways that can appear surprisingly unsettling when encountered unexpectedly. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 Witnesses also reported that the creature seemed to follow their car without flapping its wings. Large birds often alternate short bursts of flapping with gliding, especially when benefiting from air currents or moving downhill. Under stress, brief observations can easily be remembered as continuous silent flight. Why owls became the leading explanation Owls feature prominently because they naturally match several recurring elements of the earliest descriptions. They are: Active after dark, when nearly all classic Mothman encounters occurred. Capable of producing intense reflected eyeshine. Broad winged and capable of very quiet flight. Difficult to identify accurately when viewed briefly in poor light. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 Joe Nickell and other sceptical investigators have argued that investigators should begin with known wildlife before proposing unknown creatures. Their approach does not accuse witnesses of fabrication. Instead, it assumes that sincere people can honestly describe what they believed they saw while unintentionally misidentifying an animal under unusual conditions. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 However, the owl theory also has limitations. Several witnesses described a figure considerably taller than even the largest North American owls. Others reported long legs, an almost human outline or wings attached to a humanoid body rather than a recognisable bird. These inconsistencies mean that even supporters of ordinary explanations do not necessarily argue that every reported encounter involved an owl. Mothman: Harbinger of Doom or Cryptid Legend? Channel: J. Horton Films Open on YouTube The sandhill crane hypothesis The second major natural explanation is the sandhill crane. This proposal dates back to the original sighting wave rather than being a modern reinterpretation. Wildlife specialists noted that an out of range crane could have appeared unfamiliar to many local residents, especially at night. Contemporary newspaper reports even referred to the possibility of a \"monster bird\" being a crane. [wvencyclopedia.org] wvencyclopedia.org Mothman22 Feb 2024 — Skeptics offered various explanations, some arguing that the creature was a stray sandhill crane. As sightings incre... A sandhill crane possesses several features that resemble parts of the classic Mothman description: It can stand almost as tall as an adult person. Its wingspan approaches two metres. Adults have conspicuous reddish bare skin around the eyes. Long legs and an elongated neck produce an unusual silhouette when seen unexpectedly. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Sandhill crane Sandhill crane Because cranes are relatively uncommon in the Point Pleasant area, especially outside normal migration patterns, an isolated individual could have appeared both unfamiliar and alarming. This explanation became influential enough that the sandhill crane is now routinely mentioned in summaries of the Mothman legend. [wvencyclopedia.org] wvencyclopedia.org Mothman22 Feb 2024 — Skeptics offered various explanations, some arguing that the creature was a stray sandhill crane. As sightings incre... The TNT Area made misidentification more likely The abandoned TNT Area was an unusually favourable environment for confusing wildlife encounters. Built during the Second World War and later left largely abandoned, it contained woodland, wetlands, old storage bunkers and isolated roads. Such habitats support numerous bird species, particularly nocturnal predators and wetland birds. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu Its eyes are glowing red.Read more Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — A large mothlike figure, flying above... Equally important was the way visitors experienced the landscape. Most sightings occurred while people were driving at night through an unfamiliar industrial ruin. Headlights created narrow cones of illumination separated by deep darkness. Objects appeared suddenly, disappeared just as quickly and were viewed from moving vehicles. Modern research into eyewitness perception consistently shows that stress, surprise and poor visibility increase the likelihood of confident but inaccurate identifications, especially when observers encounter something unexpected. This does not prove that every witness saw a bird, but it provides a realistic mechanism by which ordinary wildlife could become extraordinary. Why the bird theory does not settle the case The bird explanation answers several features of the reports but leaves others open to debate. Supporters argue that it explains: The glowing eyes through reflected light. [audubon.org] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 The winged appearance. Silent flight. The concentration of sightings in wildlife habitat. Variation between eyewitness descriptions. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — Barred Owl's exhibit extreme eyeshine when caught by a bea... Published: October 26, 2018 Critics respond that it does not fully explain: Reports describing a"},{"title":"First Report","url":"/first-report/","description":"The Scarberry and Mallette sighting became powerful because its early wording left room for a bird, a creature or something harder to name.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"First Report What Did the First Witnesses Actually Describe? First Report First Report What Did the First Witnesses Actually Describe? The Scarberry and Mallette sighting became powerful because its early wording left room for a bird, a creature or something harder to name. The Scarberry and Mallette sighting became powerful because its early wording left room for a bird, a creature or something harder to name. event time window The 15 November 1966 encounter Why early newspaper language stayed uncertain How one report fixed the red eyed image forteana mothman first report Introduction The best known Mothman story begins not with a fully formed monster but with a hesitant eyewitness report. Late on 15 November 1966, two young couples from Point Pleasant, West Virginia, told police that they had encountered an unfamiliar figure near the abandoned TNT area , a former wartime munitions complex. Their descriptions were striking, but also inconsistent enough to leave open several possibilities: an unusually large bird, an unidentified animal, a human shaped figure with wings, or something they could not classify. That uncertainty proved crucial. Rather than presenting a clearly defined creature, the first report created a mystery that allowed later retellings to fix the now famous image of the red eyed Mothman while leaving the original evidence open to debate. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Point Pleasant Registry: “Couples See Man Sized Bird...Creature...Something.” The stereotypical inclination for a small town to form an... The 15 November 1966 encounter Roger and Linda Scarberry and Steve and Mary Mallette were driving through the isolated TNT area shortly before midnight when they noticed what they later described as a large, dark figure standing near an abandoned power facility. According to their statements, the creature appeared roughly man sized or taller, with folded wings and two bright red eyes reflecting the car's headlights. As they drove away, they said the figure took flight and followed their vehicle for several miles before disappearing. Sheriff's deputies later searched the area but found nothing unusual. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. One feature often overlooked in later retellings is how tentative the witnesses' own language initially was. They did not immediately claim to have discovered a supernatural being. Instead, they struggled to describe an encounter that did not match any familiar animal. Early reports mention a \"bird like creature\", a \"man with wings\", and simply \"something\". Different witnesses emphasised different details, including its height, posture and manner of movement. Linda Scarberry later recalled seeing a muscular, human like body, while other descriptions stressed its avian qualities. These variations appeared from the very beginning rather than emerging decades later. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The setting also mattered. The abandoned TNT area combined derelict industrial buildings, woodland, ponds and abundant wildlife. At night, headlights illuminating an unfamiliar animal against ruined structures could easily create an unsettling visual impression, even before any folklore had attached itself to the location. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — More than 50 years after the first sighting, the best expl... Published: October 26, 2018 Monstrous \"Moth Man\" Terrifies West Virginia Town Ancient Aliens History Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube Why early newspaper language stayed uncertain The following day's local newspaper captured this ambiguity with a headline that has become almost as famous as the encounter itself: \"Couples See Man Sized Bird...Creature...Something.\" Rather than declaring the arrival of a monster, the report preserved the witnesses' uncertainty. The wording deliberately avoided deciding whether the object was a bird, an animal, or an entirely unknown creature. [themothman.fandom.com] themothman.fandom.com Couples See Man Sized Bird...Creature...SomethingTwo Point Pleasant couples said today they encountered a man sized, bird like creature i... This cautious framing reflected the evidence available at the time. The newspaper reported that the witnesses appeared genuinely frightened and that police had investigated without confirming their claims, but it stopped short of endorsing any extraordinary explanation. The article also noted that the witnesses insisted they had not been drinking, addressing one obvious sceptical question without claiming that their interpretation was necessarily correct. [Yahoo] yahoo.com 55th...20 Dec 2021 — Two Point Pleasant couples said today they encountered a man sized, bird like creature in the TNT area about midnig... That early restraint is significant because later popular culture often gives the impression that the Mothman legend emerged fully formed overnight. In reality, the first published account documented confusion rather than certainty. The creature's identity remained unresolved, allowing believers, sceptics and curious readers alike to project different explanations onto the same event. How one report fixed the red eyed image Although the first report was cautious, it introduced several visual elements that became permanent features of the legend. The most enduring was the glowing red eyes. Whether caused by reflected headlights, remembered under stress, or accurately observed as described, this single feature distinguished the encounter from countless ordinary wildlife sightings. Later books, films and artwork often exaggerated the eyes, but they were already present in the witnesses' original account. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. The report also established a blend of human and bird characteristics rather than describing a conventional animal. Witnesses variously referred to wings folded behind a tall figure, difficulty seeing a face because of the eyes, and a creature capable of pursuing a moving car through the air. These details became the template against which subsequent sightings were interpreted. Later witnesses often described creatures resembling the original report, even when individual features differed. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Equally important was what the first report did not establish. It did not use the name \"Mothman\", did not connect the sighting to prophecy, and did not associate it with later disasters. Those elements entered the story only as the legend expanded through continuing reports and later authors. The initial encounter remained simply an account of four witnesses trying to describe something they believed they had seen. Mothman: America's Notorious Winged Monster Monstrum Channel: Storied Open on YouTube The Legend of the Mothman Channel: Bedtime Stories Open on YouTube What the evidence can and cannot show From an evidential standpoint, the first sighting remains impossible to resolve conclusively because no physical evidence accompanied the testimony. There were no photographs, recovered feathers, footprints capable of scientific examination, or independent recordings. What survives is a contemporaneous newspaper report, police involvement, and the witnesses' statements. [themothman.fandom.com] themothman.fandom.com Couples See Man Sized Bird...Creature...SomethingTwo Point Pleasant couples said today they encountered a man sized, bird like creature i... Natural explanations have therefore focused on misidentification rather than fabrication. Ornithologists and sceptical investigators have suggested that a large bird—particularly an owl or an out of range sandhill crane—could account for many reported features. Headlights can produce vivid red eyeshine in nocturnal birds, while poor lighting, distance and surprise can distort estimates of height and shape. Audubon has argued that these optical effects deserve serious consideration because they closely resemble aspects of the original testimony. [Audubon] audubon.org Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl? Is the Mothman of West Virginia an Owl?October 26, 2018 — 26 Oct 2018 — More than 50 years after the first sighting, the best expl... Published: October 26, 2018 Yet these explanations do not entirely erase the historical importance of the report. Whether the witnesses encountered an unknown creature, misidentified wildlife or an unusual combination of circumstances, the first account illustrates how ambiguous evidence can become remarkably durable folklore. The power of the initial report lay not in proving what was seen, but in preserving enough uncertainty that competing explanations have remained plausible for decades. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Did the First Witnesses Actually Describe?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond"},{"title":"Keel Version","url":"/keel-version/","description":"John Keel turned scattered sightings, rumors and disaster memory into the darker story that later audiences recognized as Mothman prophecy.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"Keel Version How John Keel Rewrote Mothman's Meaning Keel Version Keel Version How John Keel Rewrote Mothman's Meaning John Keel turned scattered sightings, rumors and disaster memory into the darker story that later audiences recognized as Mothman prophecy. John Keel turned scattered sightings, rumors and disaster memory into the darker story that later audiences recognized as Mothman prophecy. entity From local reports to a wider strange events complex UFOs, men in black and electronic disturbances How the film spread the prophecy version forteana mothman keel version Introduction John Keel did not invent the Mothman reports, but he fundamentally changed what they meant. Before his involvement, the Point Pleasant sightings were largely understood as a local mystery centred on reports of a strange winged creature. Through his 1975 book The Mothman Prophecies , Keel reframed those sightings as only one part of a much larger pattern involving UFOs, mysterious visitors, strange telephone calls, apparent psychic experiences and what he believed were warnings preceding disaster. That reinterpretation proved enormously influential. For many later readers and film audiences, Mothman ceased to be simply an unidentified creature and instead became a symbol of omen, prophecy and pervasive supernatural uncertainty. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies From local reports to a wider strange events complex Keel arrived in Point Pleasant as an experienced journalist and UFO investigator rather than a cryptozoologist. His interests already extended beyond reports of unknown animals to recurring patterns in paranormal claims. Instead of treating the winged figure as a biological mystery, he looked for connections between apparently unrelated events occurring in the same place and time. In The Mothman Prophecies , the creature becomes only one element within a network of unusual experiences. Keel assembled eyewitness testimony about the red eyed figure alongside reports of unidentified flying objects, unexplained lights, prophetic dreams, odd coincidences and encounters with enigmatic strangers. His argument was not that every report proved the existence of a literal monster, but that Point Pleasant represented an outbreak of interconnected anomalies that conventional explanations failed to address. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies This represented a major shift in emphasis. Earlier newspaper accounts focused on what witnesses thought they had seen. Keel instead asked why so many different forms of strangeness appeared to cluster together. That change moved the story from local folklore into the wider tradition of Forteana , where unusual phenomena are often interpreted collectively rather than in isolation. [SHURA] shura.shu.ac.uk Clarke Mothman Of West(AM The Mothman of West Virginia: A case study in Legendary...by D Clarke · 2022 · Cited by 6 — some of the Mothman and UFO witnesses b... The Mothman Prophecies by John Keel Book Review Channel: Crizzybooks Open on YouTube UFOs, men in black and electronic disturbances One of Keel's most distinctive contributions was expanding the Mothman story far beyond the creature itself. His book describes repeated reports of UFO sightings, bizarre telephone calls, electronic malfunctions and encounters with mysterious individuals who later became associated with the \"Men in Black\" tradition. Keel had already written influential articles about Men in Black before publishing The Mothman Prophecies , portraying them as unsettling figures who appeared around UFO witnesses under mysterious circumstances. Rather than presenting them as government agents, he increasingly suggested they were part of the same unexplained intelligence behind many paranormal experiences. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia John Keel John Keel The result was a very different narrative from a straightforward cryptid investigation. In Keel's version: Mothman was only one manifestation of a broader outbreak of unexplained events. UFO reports , strange visitors and psychic experiences were treated as interconnected rather than separate mysteries. Apparent electrical disturbances, unusual telephone activity and other anomalies became important supporting details instead of unrelated curiosities. Witness testimony gained significance because of recurring patterns across multiple types of reports rather than because any individual encounter could be independently verified. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies This approach also reflected Keel's evolving rejection of the simple extraterrestrial explanation for UFOs. He increasingly argued that many paranormal experiences might originate from what he later described as \"ultraterrestrials\"—entities capable of manipulating perception and folklore rather than visitors from another planet. Mothman therefore became evidence for a broader theory about anomalous phenomena instead of merely an unidentified animal. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia John Keel John Keel Why Keel linked Mothman with prophecy Perhaps Keel's most enduring innovation was connecting the Point Pleasant experiences with the collapse of the Silver Bridge in December 1967. Importantly, Keel did not claim that Mothman literally predicted the disaster through clear warnings. Instead, he argued that numerous unsettling experiences, dreams, impressions and unexplained incidents seemed to precede major tragedies. Looking back after the bridge collapse , these scattered experiences acquired the appearance of failed or misunderstood warnings. The sense of prophecy therefore emerged largely through retrospective interpretation rather than explicit prediction. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies This distinction is often lost in later retellings . Popular culture frequently presents Mothman itself as a supernatural harbinger of doom. Keel's own argument was more ambiguous. His interest lay in recurring patterns of apparent forewarning and symbolic communication rather than in portraying the creature as a conscious messenger. That ambiguity nevertheless proved powerful. By tying the sightings to a real tragedy that claimed 46 lives, Keel transformed Mothman from a local monster story into a narrative about anxiety, coincidence and the possibility that disasters might cast mysterious shadows before they occur. Official engineering investigations later concluded that the bridge failed because of stress corrosion cracking in a suspension eyebar, entirely independent of paranormal claims, but Keel's narrative permanently linked the two events in public imagination. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies Introduction To The Mothman Prophecies by John Keel Channel: Plan9Crunch Open on YouTube How the film spread the prophecy version The 2002 film The Mothman Prophecies amplified Keel's reinterpretation for an international audience. Although inspired by his book, the film streamlined its complex web of paranormal speculation into a psychological supernatural thriller centred on dread, premonitions and impending catastrophe. The cinematic Mothman appears only sparingly. Instead, the emphasis falls on mysterious phone calls , impossible knowledge, prophetic fragments and the emotional consequences of trying to understand events that resist ordinary explanation . This reinforced the public impression that Mothman was fundamentally an omen rather than an unknown creature. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies (film The Mothman Prophecies (film For many viewers, the film became their first encounter with the legend. As a result, Keel's interpretive framework—not the original 1966 newspaper reports—became the dominant version of the story in popular culture. Modern discussions often begin with prophecy, Men in Black and supernatural warning before mentioning the original eyewitness descriptions of a winged figure. Debate over Keel's legacy Keel's influence remains significant but controversial. Within Fortean writing, many regard The Mothman Prophecies as a landmark because it challenged simple explanations and encouraged researchers to examine relationships between different categories of anomalous reports rather than treating each in isolation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia John Keel John Keel Sceptics, however, argue that Keel's method blurred the distinction between independently documented events and speculative connections. Critics have questioned whether later editions and retellings strengthened links that were less apparent during the original investigation, while researchers have pointed to discrepancies between contemporary records and Keel's later narrative. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman Prophecies The Mothman Prophecies Regardless of where one stands on those debates, Keel's cultural impact is difficult to dispute. Without his reinterpretation, Mothman might have remained one more regional"},{"title":"Tourism","url":"/tourism/","description":"Point Pleasant turned a disputed anomaly into civic identity through a statue, museum, festival culture and cryptid tourism.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"Tourism How Point Pleasant Made Mothman a Mascot Tourism Tourism How Point Pleasant Made Mothman a Mascot Point Pleasant turned a disputed anomaly into civic identity through a statue, museum, festival culture and cryptid tourism. Point Pleasant turned a disputed anomaly into civic identity through a statue, museum, festival culture and cryptid tourism. population context The statue, museum and public face of the legend Festivals, tours and visitor economy Why local embrace keeps the case alive forteana mothman tourism Introduction Point Pleasant, West Virginia, has done something unusual with the Mothman legend: rather than trying to distance itself from a disputed paranormal story, it has embraced it as part of the town's identity. Today, Mothman functions as a cultural mascot, tourism brand and community symbol at the same time. Visitors arrive not because the mystery has been solved, but because it remains unresolved. The town has built attractions around that uncertainty while generally presenting the legend with humour, local pride and historical context rather than insisting that visitors accept a supernatural explanation. This transformation has made Point Pleasant one of the best known \"cryptid towns\" in the United States and illustrates how Fortean folklore can become a durable form of place making and heritage tourism. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2JSTOR] folklife.si.edu Its eyes are glowing red Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...Jun 6, 2021 — A large mothlike figure, flying abov... How the town gave Mothman a public face The most recognisable symbol of modern Mothman tourism is the polished stainless steel statue that stands on Main Street in Point Pleasant. Unveiled in 2003 and created by artist Bob Roach, the sculpture deliberately presents an athletic, comic book inspired version of the creature rather than attempting to reproduce the inconsistent descriptions given by witnesses in 1966. Its exaggerated wings, glowing eyes and muscular form have become the image most visitors associate with Mothman, even though it differs markedly from many original sighting reports. The statue performs an important cultural function. It provides a friendly and photographable icon that allows families, paranormal enthusiasts and curious travellers to participate in the legend without requiring belief in it. For many visitors, posing with the statue has become a ritual comparable to photographing famous roadside attractions elsewhere in America. The legend is therefore encountered first as public art and civic branding before it is encountered as an unsolved mystery. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu Its eyes are glowing red Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...Jun 6, 2021 — A large mothlike figure, flying abov... Nearby, the Mothman Museum reinforces this public image by presenting eyewitness accounts, newspaper coverage, memorabilia and artefacts connected with both the sightings and later popular culture. Rather than functioning as a conventional natural history museum, it occupies a space between local history, folklore and paranormal interpretation. Visitors encounter original reporting, displays related to the 1966–67 events, material from The Mothman Prophecies , and an extensive retail component that reflects the commercial success of the legend. The museum's continued operation and year round opening underline that Mothman tourism is no longer limited to annual events but supports regular visitation throughout the year. [mothmanmuseum.com] mothmanmuseum.com Mothman MuseumStop by and visit our museum and retail shop which is located in the heart of Point Pleasant, WV. We are open 7 days a week... World's ONLY Mothman Museum! NOW is the Best Time to Visit Point Pleasant's Best Attraction! Channel: Cashew Paul Open on YouTube Festivals, tours and the visitor economy The annual Mothman Festival, first held in 2002, transformed the legend from a niche paranormal interest into one of Point Pleasant's defining public celebrations. Scheduled each September near the anniversary of the original sightings, it combines guest lectures, vendors, costume competitions, live entertainment, food stalls and community events with a distinctly playful atmosphere. Organisers describe it as a celebration of the local legend rather than an attempt to prove its reality. [MOTHMAN FESTIVAL®] mothmanfestival.com MOTHMAN FESTIVAL®MOTHMAN FESTIVAL® MOTHMAN FESTIVAL®Mothman Festival is an annual event held every third weekend in September that commemorates the 1966 Point Pleasant, Wes... Attendance has grown dramatically over the years. Recent festivals have attracted well over 10,000 visitors, with reported attendance exceeding 15,000 in 2023—far larger than the town's permanent population. This seasonal influx fills hotels across the surrounding region, increases restaurant and retail trade and has become one of the area's most important tourism events. Local officials and organisers consistently describe the festival as a significant contributor to the regional economy. [Wikipedia+2West Virginia Public Broadcasting] Wikipedia Mothman Festival Mothman Festival The festival also broadens the appeal of the legend beyond paranormal enthusiasts. Visitors include horror fans, folklore researchers, photographers, families, cosplay communities and travellers interested in unusual Americana. Guided visits to the former TNT area , historical talks and local heritage attractions place the cryptid narrative alongside genuine regional history, allowing multiple audiences to engage with Point Pleasant for different reasons. [West Virginia Public Broadcasting] wvpublic.org West Virginia Public Broadcasting Mothman Festival Celebrates W.Va Legend, Packs...Sep 19, 2024 — Bellamy says the Mothman legend has fueled an economic boom in Point Pleasant, making it the most filmed... Branding a mystery without resolving it Point Pleasant's handling of Mothman is notable because it rarely asks visitors to choose between belief and scepticism. Official tourism material generally presents the legend as part of the town's cultural heritage while leaving its interpretation open. This strategy has several advantages: Believers can treat Point Pleasant as a pilgrimage site connected with an enduring mystery. Sceptics can appreciate the folklore, local history and social phenomenon without accepting paranormal claims. Casual tourists can simply enjoy an unusual destination with distinctive public art, museums and events. Because the attractions emphasise experience rather than proof, disagreement over what witnesses actually saw does little to diminish tourism. Indeed, the continuing uncertainty helps sustain interest by encouraging repeated discussion and reinterpretation. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu Its eyes are glowing red Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...Jun 6, 2021 — A large mothlike figure, flying abov... Commercial branding has expanded accordingly. Mothman appears on shop signs, restaurants, souvenirs, murals, clothing, craft products and seasonal promotions. The creature has become shorthand for Point Pleasant itself, much as other towns identify with historic figures or regional landmarks. This commercial use has gradually detached Mothman from its original role as an ominous figure associated with fear and transformed it into a recognisable civic mascot. [Medium] medium.com The Mothman Economy by The AwlThere was a new stage by the river in Point Pleasant, built mostly for a small summer music festiva... Mothman Festival 2025 Was So Weird and Fun! Channel: Hannah Dreaming Open on YouTube Visiting the Mothman Museum and BigFoot Travel Vlog Channel: CoreniaBug Open on YouTube Why local embrace keeps the legend alive Many folklore traditions fade when the original witnesses disappear. Point Pleasant has largely avoided that pattern because the community continually renews the legend through institutions rather than relying solely on oral tradition. Each generation of visitors encounters the story through visible public landmarks, museum displays, annual festivals and local businesses. Media coverage of these attractions introduces new audiences, while documentaries, books and films encourage further tourism. The result is a self reinforcing cycle in which tourism supports preservation, and preservation generates more tourism. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Mothman of Point Pleasant The Mothman of Point Pleasant Importantly, local promotion has also shifted the emotional tone of Mothman. In the late 1960s the creature was widely portrayed as frightening and, after the Silver Bridge collapse , sometimes as a harbinger of disaster. Contemporary Point Pleasant generally presents Mothman with humour, affection and civic pride. Plush toys, humorous artwork and family friendly events coexist with more"},{"title":"Bridge Collapse","url":"/bridge-collapse/","description":"The Silver Bridge disaster gave Mothman a tragic afterlife, even though the official cause was structural failure, not prophecy.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"Bridge Collapse How Disaster Changed the Mothman Story Bridge Collapse Bridge Collapse How Disaster Changed the Mothman Story The Silver Bridge disaster gave Mothman a tragic afterlife, even though the official cause was structural failure, not prophecy. The Silver Bridge disaster gave Mothman a tragic afterlife, even though the official cause was structural failure, not prophecy. event time window What happened on 15 December 1967 The engineering cause and the folklore boundary Why tragedy encouraged prophetic retellings forteana mothman bridge collapse Introduction The collapse of the Silver Bridge on 15 December 1967 transformed the Mothman story from a regional curiosity into one of the best known legends in modern Forteana . In historical terms, the bridge disaster and the Mothman sightings are separate events: official investigations concluded that the bridge failed because of a catastrophic structural defect, not because of any supernatural influence. In folklore , however, the disaster reshaped how earlier sightings were remembered. What had been strange reports of a winged figure became retrospectively interpreted by many as warnings, omens or prophecies of the tragedy to come. The tension between documented engineering failure and retrospective supernatural interpretation remains central to understanding why the Mothman legend has endured. [NTSB+2ASCE] ntsb.gov Collapse of U.S 35 Highway Bridge, Point Pleasant, West...The Safety Board finds that the cause of the bridge collapse was the cleavage fracture in the... What happened on 15 December 1967 On the afternoon of 15 December 1967, the Silver Bridge, officially known as the Point Pleasant Bridge, was carrying heavy rush hour and Christmas shopping traffic across the Ohio River between Point Pleasant, West Virginia, and Gallipolis, Ohio. Without warning, the bridge collapsed into the river. Thirty one vehicles fell with the structure, and 46 people died. Two victims were never recovered. The suddenness of the failure shocked engineers and the public alike because there had been no obvious warning that the bridge was about to fail. [NTSB] ntsb.gov Collapse of U.S 35 Highway Bridge, Point Pleasant, West...The Safety Board finds that the cause of the bridge collapse was the cleavage fracture in the... The disaster had consequences well beyond the immediate tragedy. It prompted nationwide changes in bridge inspection standards and became one of the defining engineering failures of twentieth century America. The Federal Aid Highway Act of 1968 introduced the first national bridge inspection programme, making the collapse a turning point in infrastructure safety. [ASCE] asce.org Silver Bridge Collapse and Creation of National...Investigation of the wreckage determined that the collapse was the result of a sma... Silver Bridge Memorial and the Mothman (Gallipolis) Channel: Traveling around Ohio Open on YouTube The engineering cause and the folklore boundary The official explanation for the collapse is one of the clearest parts of the story. Investigators determined that failure began with a single steel eyebar in one suspension chain, known as eyebar 330. Over decades, a tiny crack developed through the combined effects of stress corrosion and corrosion fatigue. When that eyebar fractured, the bridge's design left virtually no redundancy. The neighbouring eyebar slipped free, the suspension chain failed, and the entire structure collapsed within moments. [NTSB+2transportation.wv.gov] ntsb.gov Collapse of U.S 35 Highway Bridge, Point Pleasant, West...The Safety Board finds that the cause of the bridge collapse was the cleavage fracture in the... Several engineering lessons emerged from the investigation: A microscopic defect proved catastrophic. The flaw that initiated failure was extremely small yet located where stress was concentrated. The bridge lacked redundancy. With only two eyebars in each chain link, the failure of one rapidly overloaded the other. Inspection methods of the era were limited. Detecting the internal crack would have required dismantling parts of the bridge, something routine inspections could not realistically accomplish at the time. The bridge was not overloaded. Investigators found that traffic on the day of the collapse remained within the bridge's intended design capacity. [NTSB+2Wikipedia] ntsb.gov Collapse of U.S 35 Highway Bridge, Point Pleasant, West...The Safety Board finds that the cause of the bridge collapse was the cleavage fracture in the... These findings establish an important boundary between engineering history and folklore. No official investigation found evidence linking reported Mothman sightings to the bridge's failure. The disaster has a documented mechanical explanation supported by metallurgical analysis and structural engineering. The supernatural connection belongs to later interpretation rather than to the accident investigation itself. [NTSB] ntsb.gov Collapse of U.S 35 Highway Bridge, Point Pleasant, West...The Safety Board finds that the cause of the bridge collapse was the cleavage fracture in the... Why tragedy encouraged prophetic retellings The association between Mothman and prophecy emerged largely after the collapse rather than before it. Throughout late 1966 and 1967, residents had reported encounters with an unusual winged figure around the former TNT area near Point Pleasant. At the time, these reports were treated as local mysteries rather than predictions of disaster. Only after the bridge collapsed did many people begin looking backwards through the sightings for hidden meaning. [West Virginia Public Broadcasting] wvpublic.org That's where he made the connection between the Silver West Virginia Public BroadcastingFrom Mothman To The Silver Bridge: 13 Months In The Life...Oct 20, 2025 — John Keel published his book... This process reflects a common feature of folklore. When an unexpected catastrophe occurs, communities often revisit earlier unusual events and reinterpret them as warnings that were misunderstood at the time. Psychologists describe this tendency as hindsight bias, while folklorists note that legends frequently acquire prophetic elements after traumatic events because doing so creates a more coherent narrative from otherwise unrelated experiences. [USU Institutional Repository] digitalcommons.usu.edu USU Institutional RepositoryNarrative Hijacking: Mothman and the Silver Bridge Collapseby J Daly · 2020 — This paper examines the commemo... In the Mothman case, several versions of the prophecy narrative developed: The warning interpretation , in which Mothman appeared as an omen attempting to signal impending disaster. The harbinger interpretation , where the creature did not cause the collapse but appeared wherever tragedy was imminent. The supernatural causation theory , a much less widely accepted claim suggesting the creature somehow influenced the disaster itself. Only the first two became widespread within popular retellings . None is supported by the engineering evidence explaining why the bridge failed. [Popular Mechanics+2USU Institutional Repository] popularmechanics.com silver bridge collapse mothman Popular MechanicsMothman and the Collapse of Point Pleasant's Silver Bridge31 Aug 2023 — The story of how a risky, cost cutting bridge de... The Collapse of West Virginia's Silver Bridge Atlas of Cursed Places Channel: National Geographic Open on YouTube How the prophecy connection entered popular culture The prophetic interpretation became especially influential through later books rather than contemporary reporting. The decisive step came with journalist and paranormal writer John Keel's 1975 book The Mothman Prophecies . Keel presented the Point Pleasant sightings as part of a broader pattern of unusual phenomena and framed the Silver Bridge collapse as the tragic event foreshadowed by those reports. His interpretation reached a far larger audience than the original local newspaper coverage ever had. [West Virginia Public Broadcasting] wvpublic.org That's where he made the connection between the Silver West Virginia Public BroadcastingFrom Mothman To The Silver Bridge: 13 Months In The Life...Oct 20, 2025 — John Keel published his book... Subsequent documentaries, television programmes and the 2002 film adaptation reinforced this narrative. Popular culture often compresses chronology and uncertainty into a clearer dramatic arc: mysterious sightings occur, disaster follows, and the creature becomes remembered as a prophet of doom. While effective storytelling, this presentation tends to blur the distinction between documented history and retrospective folklore. [ChasingtheFrog.com] chasingthefrog.com Mothman Prophecies True Story John Keel, Silver Bridge...Watch John Keel speak about the creature, the Silver Bridge collapse and his... Why the bridge collapse changed the Mothman story permanently Without the Silver Bridge disaster, the Mothman sightings might have remained an unusual"},{"title":"TNT Area","url":"/tnt-area/","description":"The abandoned TNT area gave the first Mothman reports an eerie, repeatable place where wildlife, darkness and rumor could meet.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Mothman","level":3,"text":"TNT Area Why the TNT Area Made Mothman Believable TNT Area TNT Area Why the TNT Area Made Mothman Believable The abandoned TNT area gave the first Mothman reports an eerie, repeatable place where wildlife, darkness and rumor could meet. The abandoned TNT area gave the first Mothman reports an eerie, repeatable place where wildlife, darkness and rumor could meet. entity The wartime landscape behind the sightings Night drives, bunkers and repeat encounters How place turned reports into legend trips forteana mothman tnt area Introduction The abandoned TNT Area near Point Pleasant became the defining stage for the first Mothman reports because it combined an unusual physical landscape with the conditions that make extraordinary stories memorable. Former ammunition facilities, concrete storage bunkers, wetlands, woodland and isolated roads created an environment where unfamiliar wildlife, darkness and expectation could easily combine. Rather than being a random backdrop, the location gave multiple witnesses a shared reference point, allowing apparently separate encounters to accumulate into a coherent local legend. Whether the sightings reflected an unknown creature, misidentified birds or something else entirely remains disputed, but the setting itself played a crucial role in making the reports believable, repeatable and enduring. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... The wartime landscape behind the sightings The place known locally as the TNT Area originated as the West Virginia Ordnance Works, a vast World War II explosives complex constructed in 1942. During the war it manufactured and stored munitions across thousands of acres. After production ceased in 1945, much of the site was abandoned or repurposed, leaving behind concrete \"igloo\" storage bunkers, derelict industrial structures, overgrown roads and extensive tracts of woodland and marsh. Parts of the former complex later became the McClintic Wildlife Management Area, while other sections remained contaminated industrial land. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia West Virginia Ordnance Works West Virginia Ordnance Works This mixture of industrial ruins and recovering nature mattered because it was unlike ordinary rural countryside. Visitors encountered silent concrete domes half hidden by vegetation, abandoned buildings, drainage ponds and isolated access roads. Such places naturally encouraged exploration while also limiting visibility, especially after dark. For witnesses trying to describe an unexpected encounter, the surroundings reinforced the sense that something unusual had happened before any discussion of the creature itself. [Atlas Obscura] atlasobscura.com tnt area Atlas ObscuraTNT Area in Point Pleasant6 Jun 2009 — TNT Area. These rows of World War II era bunkers were the scene of some of the infamo... Mothman: the Legend, the Museum, the TNT Area Channel: Slone's Wilderness Expeditions Open on YouTube Night drives, bunkers and repeat encounters The best known sighting occurred on the night of 15 November 1966 when two young couples reported seeing a large winged figure with glowing red eyes near the former power plant and armory area before it appeared to pursue their car. The following day's newspaper deliberately reflected uncertainty, describing a \"man sized bird\", \"creature\" or \"something\" rather than presenting a fixed monster. That ambiguity proved important because later witnesses interpreted their own experiences against an already emerging narrative. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... Unlike a single isolated report, many later claims came from the same general landscape. Drivers travelled the deserted roads at night, hunters entered wooded sections, and local residents visited the abandoned complex after hearing rumours. Each new account reinforced the idea that the TNT Area itself was where one should expect strange encounters. The location therefore acted as a stable stage onto which different experiences could be projected, even when descriptions varied in height, wingspan or behaviour. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... The physical layout also encouraged repeated observation attempts. Long straight roads illuminated animals suddenly caught in headlights, while pools of standing water and thick woodland reduced depth perception. Concrete bunkers and industrial remains created unexpected silhouettes and echoes. These environmental features cannot explain every reported detail, but they help explain why witnesses repeatedly described startling, fleeting encounters rather than prolonged, clear observation. [Atlas Obscura] atlasobscura.com tnt area Atlas ObscuraTNT Area in Point Pleasant6 Jun 2009 — TNT Area. These rows of World War II era bunkers were the scene of some of the infamo... Why the setting encouraged both belief and scepticism The same characteristics that made the TNT Area seem mysterious also provided natural explanations favoured by sceptics. The wetlands and ponds support a wide range of large birds, and wildlife experts have long suggested that unfamiliar species viewed under poor lighting could account for some reports. Sandhill cranes have frequently been proposed because of their height, broad wings and reddish facial markings, although great blue herons, owls and other birds have also been suggested in different analyses. Headlights reflecting from nocturnal birds' eyes can produce an intense red or orange eyeshine that closely resembles witness descriptions. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Importantly, these explanations depend on the landscape rather than dismissing it. The abandoned industrial site had become productive wildlife habitat by the 1960s, meaning that unusual bird encounters were more plausible there than in an urban environment. The setting therefore supports both sides of the debate: believers point to the concentration of reports in one recognisable place, while sceptics point to environmental conditions that increase the likelihood of misidentification . [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mc Clintic Wildlife Management Area Mc Clintic Wildlife Management Area Exploring the Abandoned TNT Bunkers of Point Pleasant, WV! Channel: Free4All Open on YouTube The Mothman TNT Area Exploration Point Pleasant, West Virginia Channel: M L Outdoors Open on YouTube How place turned reports into legend trips The TNT Area did more than host sightings; it became a destination. Almost immediately after the first newspaper coverage, curious residents drove through the area hoping to experience the phenomenon themselves. This transformed the location from an abandoned military site into a landscape of expectation, where every unusual sound, reflected light or large bird could be interpreted through the developing Mothman story. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... Folklorists often describe such journeys as \"legend trips\"—visits to places already associated with extraordinary stories. The TNT Area fits this pattern closely. Because the site was accessible yet isolated, people could physically enter the same roads and clearings where earlier witnesses claimed to have seen the creature. Shared geography helped preserve the legend because later visitors were not simply hearing a story; they were experiencing the same environment that had generated it. [Smithsonian Folklife Center] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of Point Pleasant...6 Jun 2021 — The creature has risen from local app... Over time, the location itself became inseparable from the Mothman narrative. Even as the legend expanded through books, films and tourism , the abandoned wartime landscape remained the point of origin. Its combination of military history, environmental transformation and atmospheric isolation gave the original reports a tangible setting that audiences could imagine, visit and debate. That enduring connection between place and story is one of the chief reasons the TNT Area occupies such a central position in the folklore surrounding Mothman. [Smithsonian Folklife Center+2Atlas Obscura] folklife.si.edu mothman point pleasant west virginia Smithsonian Folklife CenterAn Ode to a Hometown Creature: Mothman of"},{"title":"Newspapers","url":"/newspapers/","description":"Old newspapers preserve many strange reports, but they also carry exaggeration, duplication, and missing context.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Newspapers Can Old Newspapers Prove Strange Events? Newspapers Newspapers Can Old Newspapers Prove Strange Events? Old newspapers preserve many strange reports, but they also carry exaggeration, duplication, and missing context. Old newspapers preserve many strange reports, but they also carry exaggeration, duplication, and missing context. dataset evidence Why Fort used published sources Local journalism and repetition Tracing the earliest account forteana newspapers Introduction Old newspaper clippings can make Fortean stories feel unusually solid: a dated page, a named town, a headline, perhaps even a witness or magistrate. They matter because much of classic Forteana survives through exactly this route. Charles Fort built his books from published scraps, including newspapers, magazines and scientific journals, treating rejected reports as “damned” data rather than as settled proof. His method turned the clipping into a kind of evidence, but a slippery one: newspapers preserve traces of strange claims, not automatic confirmation that the strange event happened as reported. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science The central question is therefore not “Was it printed?” but “What kind of printed thing is it?” A clipping may be a local observation , a syndicated filler item, a joke, a hoax, a misreported scientific story, or a later retelling detached from its first source. For Forteana , old newspapers are strongest when they help establish chronology, geography and circulation. They are weakest when repeated clippings are mistaken for independent witnesses. Why Fort Used Published Sources Fort’s choice of newspapers was practical as well as philosophical. He was not running field investigations into every fall of fish, uncanny footprint or aerial light. He was working in libraries, combing through printed records for reports that orthodox explanation had ignored or filed away too quickly. Scholar Charlotte Sleigh describes Fort’s mid 1910s shift into collecting unusual phenomena from scientific journals and newspapers, material that fed into the lost manuscripts X and Y and then into his four published books. [Kent Academic Repository] kar.kent.ac.uk Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science Kent Academic Repository Charles Hoy Fort and the uncanny voices of science That approach suited Forteana because newspapers sit between private testimony and official record. A strange thing told at a pub or family table can vanish; once printed, it becomes dateable, searchable and comparable. Fort’s surviving reputation rests partly on that paper trail. The New York Public Library catalogue records Fort related material as including trays of notes on occult phenomena, and other descriptions of his working habits emphasise the small slips, cards and scraps on which he gathered anomalies. [NYPL Archives] archives.nypl.org Open source on nypl.org. But Fort’s use of newspapers also exposes a permanent weakness in Fortean evidence. A published clipping is not the same as a sworn deposition, a specimen, a photograph, a weather record, or a chain of physical custody. Fort often used publication itself as a challenge to exclusion: if respectable journals and newspapers had printed these things, why were they ignored? Yet the answer might be that the report was garbled, copied, satirical, exaggerated, or simply too thin to support the weight placed on it. This is why newspaper clippings are best read as evidence of reporting, circulation and belief before they are treated as evidence of the anomaly itself. A clipping can prove that a story existed in a particular newspaper on a particular date. It may suggest that witnesses, editors or readers took it seriously. It does not, by itself, prove that frogs fell from a cloud, a ghost crossed a lane, or an impossible craft flew over a town. Local Journalism and Repetition The most seductive feature of newspaper evidence is volume. A Fortean story may appear in dozens of papers, giving the impression of a widespread evidential base. In nineteenth and early twentieth century press culture, however, repetition often means circulation rather than confirmation. Newspapers routinely reprinted material from other newspapers, agencies and exchange networks. Smithsonian Magazine notes that before modern feature syndicates matured, editors commonly filled space by reusing material found elsewhere; later syndication made repeated items even more systematic. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian Magazine How Syndicated Columns, Comics and Stories Forever Smithsonian Magazine How Syndicated Columns, Comics and Stories Forever This matters because a Fortean researcher can easily mistake one story printed twenty times for twenty stories. A report of a strange light might originate in a single local column, then be shortened, embellished, renamed and printed across a region. The apparent pattern may partly be a map of newspaper exchange, not a map of sightings. The 1896 97 American “mystery airship” wave is a useful example. Readex’s survey of historical newspaper coverage shows how reports, denials and sceptical commentary moved through the press, including stories of a secret inventor and lawyerly detail that later proved suspect. [readex.com] readex.com ufo fever americas historical newspapers mysterious airships ufo fever americas historical newspapers mysterious airships The case is Fortean not because every airship clipping is reliable, but because the clippings show how rumours, technological expectation, local pride and newspaper competition could produce a national anomaly before powered flight was ordinary. Repetition can still be useful when handled carefully. It can show where a story first appeared, how quickly it spread, what details changed, and when sceptical counter reporting entered the record. Those changes are often more revealing than the headline. A copied paragraph that gains a named witness in one town and loses a location in another may indicate editorial embroidery. A report that appears first as humour and later as sober news may show how genre confusion turns entertainment into “evidence”. Tracing the Earliest Account For Fortean newspaper work, the earliest recoverable account matters more than the most colourful one. Later retellings often smooth away uncertainty, combine separate reports, or add the dramatic label by which a case becomes famous. The task is to move backwards: from the familiar clipping to its source, from the source to local context, and from local context to possible non Fortean explanations. A good clipping audit asks several questions: Where did the item first appear? A local report close to the alleged event is usually more valuable than a distant reprint. Does it name witnesses, officials or locations? Specific names and addresses make cross checking possible. Is the tone news, satire, filler, folklore , sermon, advertisement or letter? Newspapers mixed genres on crowded pages. Do later versions copy the same wording? Identical phrasing usually means dependence, not corroboration. Did any nearby paper contradict it? Silence is not disproof, but local denial or absence can matter. Can the report be checked against weather, court, police, shipping, astronomical or scientific records? A clipping becomes stronger when it connects to evidence outside journalism. The “Devil’s Footprints” of Devon in February 1855 show why this matters. The famous story concerns hoof like marks reported after snow around the Exe estuary, later associated with the devil and repeated in mystery literature. The case is often remembered as a single eerie event, but newspaper treatment involved local reports, letters, speculation and illustrations, each with its own evidential value. The Fortean question is not simply whether “the newspapers reported it”, but which newspapers reported what, how close they were to the scene, and whether the later demonic framing overtook the physical details. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Tracing the earliest account can also downgrade a classic. The 1835 Great Moon Hoax began as a newspaper series in The Sun of New York, falsely attributing lunar discoveries to Sir John Herschel and a fictitious companion. It was printed as if derived from scientific authority and widely discussed, but it was a fabricated press event, not a failed astronomical observation. [HISTORY] history.com The Great Moon Hoax\" is published in the \"New York Sun The Great Moon Hoax\" is published in the \"New York Sun For Forteana, it is a warning: impressive period newspaper coverage can document public excitement while proving nothing about the alleged phenomenon. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length,"},{"title":"Cross Checks","url":"/cross-checks/","description":"A strange clipping becomes stronger when names, weather, court, police, shipping or astronomical records can test the printed claim.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"Cross Checks How to Test a Strange Old Clipping Cross Checks Cross Checks How to Test a Strange Old Clipping A strange clipping becomes stronger when names, weather, court, police, shipping or astronomical records can test the printed claim. A strange clipping becomes stronger when names, weather, court, police, shipping or astronomical records can test the printed claim. policy intervention Which details make a clipping checkable Outside records that can confirm or weaken a report How to preserve uncertainty without overstating proof forteana newspapers cross checks Introduction A strange newspaper clipping is not the end of a Fortean investigation but the beginning of one. A clipping can establish that a story was printed at a particular time and place, yet its value rises or falls when it is compared with independent records created for different purposes. A report of mysterious lights, an unexplained explosion or an extraordinary animal sighting becomes more informative when the names, dates, weather , court proceedings, shipping movements or astronomical conditions can be checked against contemporary documentation. Equally, a clipping may be weakened if those records contradict key details or show that the report was copied, exaggerated or misunderstood. The aim is not to force every mystery into either belief or scepticism, but to distinguish between what the newspaper demonstrably recorded and what the underlying event may actually have been. [guides.lib.fsu.edu] guides.lib.fsu.edu Researching with Historical Newspapers10 Apr 2026 — Guide to local, national, and international news sources, including recent and histor... Which details make a clipping checkable? Many old reports contain enough factual anchors to permit independent testing. The more specific the information, the more opportunities there are to verify or challenge it. The most useful details include: Exact or approximate dates. Named people, witnesses, police officers, doctors or magistrates. Named towns, villages, farms, roads or landmarks. Precise times of day. References to ships, trains, businesses or institutions. Weather descriptions. References to legal proceedings, coroners' inquests or public meetings. Descriptions of astronomical objects such as the Moon, bright planets or meteor showers. These details matter because they were often recorded independently by organisations with no interest in promoting a strange story. A shipping register existed to record vessel movements, not to support reports of sea monsters. A weather office measured rainfall and wind regardless of rumours about falls of fish. That independence makes such records especially valuable as corroborating—or contradicting—evidence. A clipping that merely says \"locals reported a strange creature\" offers little to test. By contrast, one stating that \"Constable John Smith attended the scene near High Street at 10.15 p.m. during heavy rain on 14 October\" provides several independent avenues of investigation. Outside records that can confirm or weaken a report No single external source settles every case. Instead, different record types answer different questions about the clipping. Weather observations Weather records are among the strongest external checks because they were collected systematically. If a clipping describes: unusual snowfall, violent thunderstorms, dense fog, hail, strong winds, flooding, lightning, then historical meteorological observations can establish whether those conditions occurred locally at the stated time. They cannot prove an anomalous event happened, but they can show whether the reported environment matches known conditions. Weather data are particularly useful in assessing reports of mysterious aerial lights, unusual sounds and animal falls. A clipping claiming clear skies while official observations recorded continuous cloud and rain deserves closer scrutiny rather than automatic acceptance. Library and Archives Workshop: Using Historical Newspapers for Your Research Channel: Tennessee Secretary of State · Views: 1.6K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 58 minutes Open on YouTube Police, court and coroner's records Many Fortean stories involve alleged deaths, assaults, disappearances or public disturbances. If a newspaper reports that: police investigated, an arrest occurred, a coroner opened an inquest, witnesses testified in court, then surviving official records may preserve names, testimony, verdicts or procedural details omitted from the newspaper. Court records are especially useful because newspapers often shortened proceedings or paraphrased testimony. Comparing the two may reveal that sensational headlines overstated what witnesses actually said, or that later retellings quietly removed mundane explanations discussed during the hearing. Civil registration and census material Named individuals can often be checked through birth, marriage, death and census records. This helps answer questions such as: Did the named witness actually exist? Was the person living where the clipping claimed? Was the reported victim alive after the supposed incident? Did family relationships match the newspaper account? Sometimes this reveals simple reporting mistakes . At other times it identifies two people with similar names whose stories later became merged. Shipping and maritime records Sea mysteries frequently become more testable than they first appear. If a clipping mentions: a named vessel, a collision, an unexplained disappearance, unusual cargo, harbour arrivals, shipping registers, port records and maritime databases may establish whether the vessel was genuinely present, delayed, wrecked or operating elsewhere. Digital humanities researchers have demonstrated that historical shipping records and newspaper archives can be linked systematically because both contain overlapping factual information such as ship names, dates and ports. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate Linking Historical Ship Records to a Newspaper Archive Linking Historical Ship Records to a Newspaper ArchiveNovember 10, 2014 — 15 Dec 2016 — In this paper, we focus on discoverin... Published: November 10, 2014 This does not eliminate mystery, but it narrows speculation by replacing assumptions with documented movements. Astronomical records Many historical reports describe: brilliant lights, fireballs, strange celestial objects, unusual moons, eclipses, comets. Astronomical calculations are exceptionally reliable for reconstructing past skies. Checking: Moon phase, moonrise and moonset, planetary positions, meteor showers, eclipses, bright comet appearances, can determine whether a seemingly mysterious observation coincided with an ordinary but striking astronomical event. Conversely, if the clipping reports a full Moon on a night when astronomical records show a new Moon, confidence in the report's accuracy decreases. How to evaluate the reliability of historical sources Channel: History Skills Open on YouTube Looking for independent confirmation rather than repetition One of the easiest mistakes is treating multiple newspaper reports as multiple witnesses. Nineteenth and early twentieth century newspapers routinely copied stories from one another, sometimes shortening, rewriting or embellishing them. Twenty similar clippings may therefore represent a single original report rather than twenty separate investigations. Cross checking should therefore ask: Which newspaper printed the story first? Which papers simply reprinted it? Did later papers add genuinely new witnesses? Are later versions introducing details absent from the original? The goal is to distinguish independent confirmation from ordinary newspaper circulation. Historical newspaper research guides similarly recommend broad searching followed by careful narrowing because identical stories often appear across different publications for reasons unrelated to independent reporting. [thesocialhistoryarchive.com] thesocialhistoryarchive.com ectively and interpret your results with greater accuracy... Reconciling contradictions External records will not always agree. A clipping might report: heavy snow where weather logs show only frost; an inquest that official records cannot locate; a ship in one harbour while maritime records place it elsewhere. Such contradictions should not automatically be treated as proof of fraud. Alternative explanations include: typographical errors; incorrect dates; confusion between neighbouring towns; later editorial corrections; mistaken witness memories; abbreviated newspaper reporting. Researchers should therefore test whether changing the date by a day, checking neighbouring jurisdictions or searching for variant spellings resolves the discrepancy before concluding that the story is false. Understanding historical sources & analysis Channel: veritas et caritas Open on YouTube How to preserve uncertainty without overstating proof The strongest clipping audits resist the temptation to turn"},{"title":"Devil Tracks","url":"/devil-tracks/","description":"The Devon footprints case shows how local snow reports, letters, illustrations and demonic labels can carry different evidential weight.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"Devil Tracks How Hoofprints Became Devil Evidence Devil Tracks Devil Tracks How Hoofprints Became Devil Evidence The Devon footprints case shows how local snow reports, letters, illustrations and demonic labels can carry different evidential weight. The Devon footprints case shows how local snow reports, letters, illustrations and demonic labels can carry different evidential weight. event time window What local reports said about the snow marks How letters and illustrations changed the story Where later folklore overtook physical details forteana newspapers devil tracks Introduction The 1855 Devil's Footprints of Devon are often presented as a straightforward mystery: after a night of snow, hoof like tracks appeared across parts of the county, and newspapers declared that the Devil himself might have walked the countryside. The surviving newspaper record tells a more complicated story. Contemporary reporting did not simply preserve evidence; it actively shaped what counted as evidence. Local news items, readers' letters, editorial commentary and engraved illustrations gradually transformed scattered observations of unusual snow marks into one of Britain's best known Fortean cases. For anyone interested in newspaper clippings as Fortean evidence, the value of the Devil's Footprints lies less in whether the tracks were supernatural than in watching how reporting changed over time. The surviving press shows a shift from local descriptions of physical marks, through national debate illustrated with sketches and expert correspondence, to later retellings in which the phrase \"Devil's Footprints\" came to overshadow many of the original uncertainties. [Peter Moore] peter moore.co.uk Peter Moore The Curious Case of the Devil's Foot Prints (or Peter Moore9 Feb 2015 — It happened exactly 160 years ago, on the night of 8 February 1855. It is a mystery of Sherlockian proportions. I... Published: February 1855 What local reports said about the snow marks The first newspaper reports emerged after heavy snowfall on the night of 8–9 February 1855. Local papers described rows of hoof like impressions found in several communities around the Exe Estuary. While later accounts often speak of a single uninterrupted trail stretching for scores of miles, the earliest reporting was necessarily local. Individual communities described what residents had actually seen in their own fields, gardens, roads and churchyards rather than documenting one continuous route. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Those local descriptions share several recurring features: The impressions appeared roughly hoof shaped rather than being positively identified as hooves. Reports frequently noted that the marks formed a single file track. Fresh snow provided an unusually clear recording surface, making otherwise ordinary animal traces appear more striking. Distances, spacing and exact dimensions varied between witnesses rather than remaining perfectly consistent. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints These inconsistencies matter because they reveal that the newspaper record preserves multiple observations rather than one perfectly standardised phenomenon. Modern summaries often merge them into a single idealised trail, but the contemporary reports preserve a more fragmented picture. The dramatic claims that the tracks crossed walls, rooftops, rivers and enclosed gardens also deserve careful reading. Newspapers repeated such observations because they made the story remarkable, yet they generally reflected separate local reports instead of one observer tracing an impossible route from beginning to end. Later investigators have argued that no contemporary witness actually followed a continuous line across the entire reported distance. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Did the Devil Visit Devon in 1855? The Devil's Hoof Prints Mystery Channel: Well, I Never Open on YouTube How letters and illustrations changed the story As the story spread beyond Devon, newspapers changed from reporting observations to hosting a public debate. The national illustrated press became especially influential. The Illustrated London News published engravings showing the supposed footprints, giving readers across Britain a standard visual image of the mystery. These illustrations were not photographs but artists' interpretations based on witness material. Once reproduced nationally, they helped establish what readers expected the mysterious tracks to look like, even though individual descriptions had varied. [Wikimedia Commons] commons.wikimedia.org Commons File:The \"Devil's footprints Original: (right): \"Professor Owen on the Foot Marks in the Snow in Devon\". Illustrated London News, 3 March 1855, p.Read more... Published: March 1855 The newspaper also opened its pages to correspondence, producing several important shifts in framing. Expert opinion competed with supernatural explanations Rather than endorsing a demonic explanation outright, the correspondence mixed natural history with sensational reporting. The anatomist Richard Owen proposed that badgers might account for at least some of the impressions, emphasising known animal behaviour instead of supernatural causes. His contribution demonstrates that Victorian newspapers frequently presented scientific opinion alongside popular speculation rather than treating them as mutually exclusive. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Other correspondents explored different possibilities, including rodents whose hopping gait could create paired impressions resembling cloven hooves in certain snow conditions. Although this explanation became more widely discussed in modern scholarship, its roots also appeared in nineteenth century newspaper correspondence rather than being an entirely modern invention. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Letters also created new stories Some of the most memorable elements entered the tradition through correspondence rather than first hand reporting. One celebrated example concerns rumours that escaped kangaroos from a private menagerie had made the tracks. Later evidence indicates that the Reverend G. M. Musgrave introduced this explanation partly to divert parishioners from fears that the Devil had literally visited the district. The episode illustrates how letters to newspapers could generate fresh folklore while simultaneously attempting to calm it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Equally revealing is the discovery of surviving papers belonging to the Reverend Henry Thomas Ellacombe. These include private letters, tracings of footprints and a draft letter marked \"not for publication\". Together they show that discussions about the evidence extended beyond what eventually appeared in print. The newspaper archive therefore represents only one layer of the surviving documentation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints The Devil's Footprints: A Mystery 150 Years In The Making Channel: Curious History Open on YouTube Where later folklore overtook physical details As the decades passed, the balance between physical evidence and narrative gradually reversed. Later retellings increasingly emphasised dramatic features: the Devil walking through Devon in a single night; an uninterrupted trail extending extraordinary distances; impossible crossings over rivers, roofs and high walls; widespread public panic. These elements all have roots in nineteenth century reporting, but repetition gradually detached them from the patchwork of individual local observations that originally produced them. The result is a cleaner and more dramatic story than the newspapers initially presented. [Peter Moore] peter moore.co.uk Peter Moore The Curious Case of the Devil's Foot Prints (or Peter Moore9 Feb 2015 — It happened exactly 160 years ago, on the night of 8 February 1855. It is a mystery of Sherlockian proportions. I... Published: February 1855 Researchers such as Mike Dash have argued that the surviving evidence is better understood as a collection of different incidents than as proof of one enormous continuous track. Some reported marks may have been made by ordinary animals, others by separate causes, while embellishment and repetition during newspaper circulation encouraged readers to perceive them as one unified mystery. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints This evolution also explains why later folklore often treats \"Devil's Footprints\" as the original name of the event. In reality, the demonic label emerged because the shape of the marks encouraged comparisons with cloven hooves. The supernatural interpretation became progressively more dominant as newspapers and later writers preferred the memorable title over more cautious descriptions such as unusual footprints or snow marks. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Devil's Footprints Devil's Footprints Devil's"},{"title":"First Account","url":"/first-account/","description":"The first recoverable clipping often matters more than the most dramatic later version because it fixes what the story originally claimed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"First Account Why the First Clipping Matters Most First Account First Account Why the First Clipping Matters Most The first recoverable clipping often matters more than the most dramatic later version because it fixes what the story originally claimed. The first recoverable clipping often matters more than the most dramatic later version because it fixes what the story originally claimed. dataset evidence How later retellings reshape the original claim What a first report can and cannot establish How to compare early versions against local context forteana newspapers first account Introduction For anyone investigating a classic Fortean claim, the most valuable newspaper clipping is usually not the most widely circulated or the most dramatic one. It is the earliest recoverable report. That first account fixes what was actually claimed before later editors shortened it, embellished it, miscopied it or detached it from its original context. In practical terms, locating the earliest newspaper version is one of the strongest tests that can be applied to historical anomaly stories. It cannot prove that an extraordinary event happened, but it can establish when the claim entered the public record, what details were present at the start, and how the story changed as it spread through the press. This chronological approach fits Charles Fort 's reliance on printed sources while applying a more rigorous historical method to the same material. [Wikipedia+2Research Guides] Wikipedia Charles Fort Charles Fort Why the first clipping matters most The earliest newspaper report serves as a reference point against which every later version can be measured. Once an item begins circulating through regional exchanges or syndication networks, it often accumulates changes that are difficult to distinguish from the original narrative. Three questions make the first report especially valuable: What was originally claimed? Later retellings frequently add details that were absent from the first publication. Who made the claim? The earliest version is more likely to identify witnesses, locations, dates or officials before those details disappear in abbreviated reprints. How close was the newspaper to the event? A local newspaper published within days of the alleged incident generally deserves more evidential weight than a distant paper reproducing the story weeks later. This reflects proximity to sources, not automatic accuracy. [Research Guides+2storybench.org] guides.loc.gov Research Guides Historical Newspapers Newspaper Collections at the...This research guide gathers together a collection of topical guides that tell various stories of America... For Fortean research, this distinction is crucial because many famous anomalies survive only as newspaper reports. Establishing the first known appearance allows researchers to separate the history of the report from the history of the alleged event. How later retellings reshape the original claim Historical newspapers routinely copied one another. Before modern reporting practices, editors exchanged material extensively, filling columns with items lifted from other publications. A story appearing in dozens of newspapers may therefore represent one originating report rather than dozens of independent investigations. [storybench.org+2Wikipedia] storybench.org how newspaper stories went viral in the 19th century Looking through more than 40,000 issues of 132 different...Read more... As stories travelled, several kinds of change commonly appeared: witness names removed or simplified; locations generalised from a precise village to a county or country; dates omitted, making chronology harder to reconstruct; sensational headlines added despite restrained original text; editorial commentary merged into factual description; numerical details, distances or quantities altered during copying. These changes matter because later versions often become the ones quoted in paranormal books or online collections. A spectacular twentieth century retelling may therefore rest upon a comparatively modest nineteenth century local report. The earliest clipping acts as a control document. Every later variation should be checked against it before being treated as new evidence. The Book of the Damned (1/2) 🌟 By Charles Fort. FULL Audiobook Channel: Audiobooks. Full length, free Open on YouTube What a first report can and cannot establish Finding the first known newspaper account strengthens documentation, but it does not authenticate the underlying phenomenon. A first report can reasonably establish: that the story existed by a particular date; how contemporaries initially described the event; which newspaper first published the account, so far as surviving archives show; whether later versions faithfully reproduced or altered the original. It cannot, by itself, establish: that witnesses were accurate; that editors verified the claims independently; that no earlier unpublished or now lost report existed; that the reported anomaly actually occurred. This distinction is especially important in Forteana , where surviving documentation is often fragmentary. Newspapers preserve evidence of reporting and public circulation. They are only indirect evidence for the anomalous event itself. [Research Guides+2OAPEN] guides.loc.gov Research Guides Historical Newspapers Newspaper Collections at the...This research guide gathers together a collection of topical guides that tell various stories of America... How to compare early versions against local context The earliest clipping becomes significantly more informative when read alongside ordinary local reporting from the same period rather than in isolation. Useful comparisons include: Coverage elsewhere in the same issue. Reports of storms, earthquakes, fairs, accidents or public meetings may confirm the broader setting described in the anomaly report. Neighbouring newspapers. Independent local papers may ignore the event, contradict it or provide additional factual details. Editorial style. Some newspapers regularly published humorous fillers, travellers' tales or curious anecdotes. Understanding that editorial culture helps judge whether an item was intended as hard news, entertainment or speculation. Timing. Reports appearing immediately after the alleged event generally deserve closer attention than recollections published months or years later. Reading the newspaper as a complete historical document often reveals whether an anomaly was treated as routine local news, sensational curiosity or obvious amusement. How & Where to Research Newspapers for Genealogy and Family History Channel: Family Tree Support Open on YouTube Practical method for identifying the earliest account Modern digitised newspaper archives make it easier than ever to work backwards through a story, although they introduce new challenges such as incomplete collections and imperfect optical character recognition (OCR), the automated conversion of scanned pages into searchable text. [OAPEN] library.oapen.org The digital evolution not only...Read more... A practical workflow is: London Fortean Society: The Birth of the Hoax with Ian Keable Channel: Conway Hall Open on YouTube 1. Begin with the commonly cited version. 2. Search for distinctive phrases rather than only the headline. 3. Sort results chronologically. 4. Examine every earlier appearance rather than assuming the oldest search hit is the origin. 5. Check whether the earliest version itself credits another newspaper with phrases such as \"from the...\" or \"our correspondent.\" 6. Continue tracing references until the reporting trail either reaches a local source or disappears into missing archival coverage. Researchers should also remember that the earliest surviving clipping is not necessarily the first publication. Many historical newspapers have not survived, while others remain undigitised or have OCR errors that prevent straightforward discovery. [OAPEN+2NEH] library.oapen.org The digital evolution not only...Read more... Why this is one of the strongest Fortean tests Among the many ways to evaluate historical anomaly reports, tracing the newspaper trail backwards is unusually productive because it addresses questions that can actually be answered from surviving evidence. Instead of asking immediately whether a rain of animals, mysterious light or impossible creature was real, it first asks a more manageable historical question: what did the earliest identifiable report actually say? That shift often changes the interpretation of the entire case. Some stories become stronger because the original local report contains precise names, dates and observations that later summaries omitted. Others become weaker because dramatic details turn out to have been introduced only after repeated reprinting. For this reason, the earliest recoverable newspaper account functions as a practical benchmark in Fortean research. It does not resolve whether an anomaly occurred, but it"},{"title":"Moon Hoax","url":"/moon-hoax/","description":"The 1835 Moon Hoax proves that impressive newspaper detail can document public excitement while revealing a fabricated press event.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"Moon Hoax When Scientific Authority Was Faked in Print Moon Hoax Moon Hoax When Scientific Authority Was Faked in Print The 1835 Moon Hoax proves that impressive newspaper detail can document public excitement while revealing a fabricated press event. The 1835 Moon Hoax proves that impressive newspaper detail can document public excitement while revealing a fabricated press event. event time window How the hoax borrowed astronomical credibility Why wide discussion did not confirm the claim What Fortean readers can learn from fabricated authority forteana newspapers moon hoax Introduction The Great Moon Hoax of 1835 is one of the clearest historical warnings against treating newspaper publication as proof. For readers interested in Forteana , it demonstrates an important distinction: a newspaper can provide excellent evidence that a remarkable claim circulated widely, while simultaneously providing no evidence that the claim itself was true. The episode is valuable precisely because it separates documentation from verification. The hoax succeeded not through crude fantasy but by borrowing the language, reputation and presentation of genuine science. It showed how printed authority could be manufactured from respected names, technical detail and repeated publication. For anyone using historical newspaper clippings as Fortean evidence, the Moon Hoax remains a reminder that impressive documentation may record a cultural event—the spread of belief—rather than the reality of the reported phenomenon. [JSTOR] jstor.org The observers were wondering... 20All three newspaper articles are quoted in: Locke, Moon Hoax, 61 62. 21.Read more... Exeter History Minute Great Moon Hoax Channel: Exeter Historical Society Open on YouTube How the hoax borrowed astronomical credibility When the New York Sun began publishing its six part series on 25 August 1835, readers were told they were seeing extracts from an established scientific publication describing astonishing discoveries by the celebrated British astronomer Sir John Herschel. According to the articles, Herschel had used a revolutionary telescope at the Cape of Good Hope to observe forests, temples, strange animals and intelligent winged humanoids living on the Moon. Linda Hall Library+2The Library of Congress [lindahall.org] lindahall.org richard adams locke Linda Hall LibraryRichard Adams Locke25 Aug 2022 — On Aug. 25, 1835, the New York Sun, a fledgling newspaper less than 2 years old, ran a... The deception worked because almost every element contained a grain of reality. Sir John Herschel was a genuine and internationally respected astronomer. Herschel really was conducting observations in southern Africa. The reports adopted the formal language and descriptive style of contemporary scientific writing. The articles claimed to be reprinted from an overseas publication, placing the supposed evidence beyond the immediate reach of most readers for verification. [JSTOR+2Linda Hall Library] jstor.org The observers were wondering... 20All three newspaper articles are quoted in: Locke, Moon Hoax, 61 62. 21.Read more... The fabricated technical descriptions were especially effective. Rather than beginning with bat winged lunar beings, the articles first spent considerable effort explaining an extraordinary telescope and its optical capabilities. By establishing an apparently scientific foundation before introducing sensational conclusions, the series encouraged readers to accept later claims because the earlier framework appeared authoritative. Modern historians have noted that the technical apparatus functioned rhetorically: if the instrument sounded plausible, the observations seemed plausible too. [Unbound] blog.library.si.edu the great moon hoax or was it the jokes on who UnboundThe Great Moon Hoax or Was It — The Joke's on Who?4 Sept 2013 — As an aside on the telescope, Locke described Herschel as designin... The hoax also exploited the public's enthusiasm for astronomy. The early nineteenth century was an era in which new telescopes, planetary discoveries and speculation about life beyond Earth attracted widespread attention. Popular writers had already suggested that inhabited worlds were scientifically reasonable possibilities. Richard Adams Locke therefore exaggerated existing expectations rather than inventing an entirely alien idea. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Great Moon Hoax Great Moon Hoax The Folklorist: The Great Moon Hoax Channel: The Folklorist Open on YouTube Why wide discussion did not confirm the claim One reason the Great Moon Hoax remains so instructive is that the story spread rapidly without becoming any more reliable. Newspapers discussed it, reprinted extracts and argued over its implications. Readers debated the discoveries, while rival publications commented on the reports. The volume of discussion created the appearance of overwhelming documentary support. Yet almost all of that apparent evidence ultimately traced back to the same fabricated source. [The New Yorker] newyorker.com The New Yorker Moon Shot: Race, a Hoax, and the Birth of Fake News This hoax, concocted by Richard Adams Locke, greatly boosted the paper’s circulation and dominated public conversation. The hoax coincide... This illustrates an enduring problem in historical newspaper research. A researcher might discover: dozens of newspaper references, reports from several cities, apparently independent commentary, extensive public excitement. None of these necessarily demonstrates that the original claim was independently confirmed. They may instead document how efficiently one compelling story circulated through nineteenth century press networks. For Fortean researchers, this distinction is critical. Multiple newspaper clippings often measure dissemination rather than corroboration. The Moon Hoax therefore serves as a textbook example of why source genealogy matters. Before counting reports, it is necessary to identify whether they represent separate witnesses or merely repeated publication. Another revealing feature is the delay before definitive correction. Because Herschel was working thousands of miles away, immediate verification was difficult. The geographical distance that lent authenticity also slowed contradiction. Printed authority could therefore persist long enough to generate extensive secondary reporting before the fabrication became widely recognised. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Great Moon Hoax Great Moon Hoax What Fortean readers can learn from fabricated authority The Great Moon Hoax should not be read simply as a curiosity about Victorian gullibility. It is better understood as a lesson in evaluating documentary evidence. For Fortean investigations, newspaper clippings remain extremely valuable because they can establish: when a story entered public circulation; how it changed over time; which communities encountered it; how editors framed unusual claims. What they cannot establish on their own is that the reported anomaly occurred. The Moon Hoax demonstrates that authority can be assembled from entirely recognisable ingredients: respected scientific figures; realistic technical language; identifiable dates and places; serial publication; confident editorial presentation. None of these features guarantees factual accuracy. Indeed, the success of the hoax depended on using authentic markers of credibility to support fictional observations. [JSTOR+2Linda Hall Library] jstor.org The observers were wondering... 20All three newspaper articles are quoted in: Locke, Moon Hoax, 61 62. 21.Read more... This is particularly relevant because many Fortean reports survive only in newspapers. The lesson is not to dismiss those reports automatically, but to classify them correctly. A clipping may provide strong evidence that a remarkable claim existed in print at a particular moment, while remaining weak evidence that the extraordinary event itself happened. The Great Moon Hoax Channel: University of West Georgia Open on YouTube A lasting warning about printed authority The enduring importance of the Great Moon Hoax lies less in its imaginary lunar creatures than in its demonstration of how easily institutional credibility can be staged on the printed page. The articles did not persuade readers merely by being imaginative; they persuaded because they resembled trustworthy scientific reporting and attached themselves to genuine scientific prestige. Within the study of Forteana, this makes the episode a useful methodological caution. Newspapers are indispensable historical sources, but they are records of communication before they are records of reality. The Great Moon Hoax reminds readers that the strongest question to ask of any remarkable clipping is not whether it was widely printed, but whether the authority it projects can be traced beyond the printed page itself. [Wikipedia+2The Library of Congress] Wikipedia Great Moon Hoax Great Moon Hoax Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to"},{"title":"Airship Wave","url":"/airship-wave/","description":"The 1896-97 airship wave shows how local pride, invention rumours and press competition could turn scattered claims into a national mystery.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"Airship Wave Did Newspapers Build the Mystery Airship? Airship Wave Airship Wave Did Newspapers Build the Mystery Airship? The 1896 97 airship wave shows how local pride, invention rumours and press competition could turn scattered claims into a national mystery. The 1896 97 airship wave shows how local pride, invention rumours and press competition could turn scattered claims into a national mystery. event time window How airship reports spread across American papers Rumours of inventors, denials and suspect details What the case teaches about press driven anomalies forteana newspapers airship wave Introduction The American mystery airship wave of 1896–97 is one of the clearest examples of how newspapers could transform scattered, ambiguous observations into a nationwide Fortean mystery. Months before practical powered flight became an everyday reality, readers encountered reports of cigar shaped craft carrying brilliant searchlights, mechanical noises and even human occupants. Some accounts described secret inventors testing revolutionary machines, while others claimed encounters with mysterious crews or spectacular crashes. Although many reports may have originated from genuine observations of unusual lights or experimental balloons, the way newspapers copied, embellished and competed over the story helped create an anomaly that became larger than any single sighting. [cdn.centerforinquiry.org+2readex.com] cdn.centerforinquiry.org November 17 to mid December 1896, and the second, January 22 to May. 1897 (Bullard 1982a:207, 211)...Read more... Published: December 1896 For students of Forteana , the value of the airship wave lies not only in asking whether any extraordinary aircraft existed, but in examining how nineteenth century journalism shaped public belief. The surviving newspaper clippings document the rapid spread of an idea as much as they record unusual events. How airship reports spread across American papers The first major cluster of reports appeared over northern California in November 1896, particularly around Sacramento, San Francisco and Oakland. Witnesses described an illuminated object moving purposefully through the night sky, often with a powerful searchlight. Because electric lighting, balloons and experimental engineering were all familiar but still novel technologies, the reports sounded just plausible enough to capture public attention. [KQED] kqed.org 1896 mystery airship bay area ufo history victorian aliens The 1896 UFO That Stunned Bay Area VictoriansMay 10, 2024 — For weeks, witnesses in San Francisco, Oakland and Sacramento saw a myste... Published: May 10, 2024 Over the following months the story migrated eastward through newspaper exchange networks. By early 1897 reports appeared throughout the Midwest and eventually across much of the United States. Editors frequently reprinted articles from distant papers, sometimes shortening them, combining separate accounts or adding fresh commentary. To readers, dozens of apparently independent reports suggested a widespread phenomenon even when many stories traced back to relatively few original publications. [readex.com+2cdn.centerforinquiry.org] readex.com ufo fever americas historical newspapers mysterious airships The Mysterious Airships of 1896 9712 Sept 2014 — For five months beginning in November 1896, newspapers across the country described stra... Published: November 1896 This pattern reflected normal newspaper practice rather than deliberate deception. Nineteenth century editors routinely filled columns by borrowing stories from other publications, often with minimal verification. Once the mystery airship became popular copy, every new sighting encouraged further reporting, creating a feedback loop between witnesses, editors and readers. (1896, 4K, 60fps) A day in XIX Century life. Channel: Nineteenth century videos. Back to life. Open on YouTube Rumours of inventors, denials and suspect details One striking feature of the airship wave is that many newspapers assumed the objects were not supernatural but technological. Reports repeatedly suggested that an unknown inventor had secretly perfected a revolutionary flying machine and was conducting night time trials before revealing the invention publicly. The idea matched contemporary fascination with rapid advances in electricity, engineering and transportation. [HowStuffWorks] science.howstuffworks.com How Stuff Works Airships in America Airships in America Aliens & UFOsThe UFO wave of 1896 and 1897 sparked great interest as well as many hoaxes. A Chicago ne... The press eagerly published rumours identifying possible inventors. Various engineers, mechanics and wealthy businessmen found themselves credited with creating the mysterious craft. Many publicly denied involvement, while others allowed speculation to continue because it generated publicity. The repeated cycle of anonymous claims followed by emphatic denials gave the story an appearance of investigative journalism even when little evidence existed. [cdn.centerforinquiry.org] cdn.centerforinquiry.org November 17 to mid December 1896, and the second, January 22 to May. 1897 (Bullard 1982a:207, 211)...Read more... Published: December 1896 As the reports multiplied, some became increasingly elaborate. Newspapers printed accounts of: conversations with airship crews; [wedentondoit.com] wedentondoit.com the 1897 texas airship invasion 21 Apr 2017 — The earliest reported Airship sighting in Texas came from rural Denton on April 13 of 1897, when a curious local out starga... descriptions of propellers, wings and electric lights; miraculous speeds and manoeuvres; landings in rural districts; requests for water or repairs; anonymous inventors refusing to reveal their identities. These details often varied dramatically between locations. Rather than converging on a consistent description, the stories accumulated local colour, suggesting that journalists and witnesses alike adapted the central narrative to fit regional expectations. [cdn.centerforinquiry.org+2readex.com] cdn.centerforinquiry.org November 17 to mid December 1896, and the second, January 22 to May. 1897 (Bullard 1982a:207, 211)...Read more... Published: December 1896 Press competition and the making of a national anomaly The airship wave emerged during an intensely competitive period in American journalism. Newspapers competed aggressively for circulation, and unusual stories attracted readers. Editors therefore had incentives to print dramatic eyewitness accounts while also publishing sceptical commentary, allowing both believers and doubters to remain engaged. Importantly, many newspapers did not simply endorse the reports. Contemporary editorials frequently mocked witnesses, ridiculed extravagant claims and accused rival publications of printing nonsense. Yet even hostile coverage kept the mystery alive. Every attempt to debunk a new report reminded readers that another airship story had appeared somewhere else. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mystery airshipSome newspapers denounced the entire airship story as nonsense and openly mocked and ridiculed the witnesses and believers... This mixture of belief and ridicule helped sustain public interest. A completely credulous press would likely have lost credibility, while universal dismissal would have ended the story quickly. Instead, disagreement between newspapers encouraged readers to continue following developments. Famous reports that illustrate newspaper amplification Several well known episodes reveal how newspaper narratives could become detached from reliable evidence. The alleged crash near Aurora, Texas, in April 1897 became famous only because newspapers reported that a strange airship had collided with a windmill and that its non human pilot had been buried locally. Later investigations found little contemporary physical evidence, and historians generally regard the story as a likely journalistic fabrication or local publicity effort rather than a documented accident. Nevertheless, repeated reprinting transformed it into one of the most enduring legends associated with early UFO history. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mystery airshipSome newspapers denounced the entire airship story as nonsense and openly mocked and ridiculed the witnesses and believers... Another example is the colourful Kansas story in which farmer Alexander Hamilton claimed that an airship carried away one of his cattle. The tale later became associated with a local \"Liars' Club\" and is widely regarded as an intentional tall tale, yet it continues to appear in compilations of unexplained mysteries because the original newspaper clipping survives. [The Guardian] theguardian.com 0,, 2556,00 The GuardianNovember 1876 to May 1897 mysterious large airships...Most famous of the hoaxes were Alexander Hamilton's March 1897 cow nap... Published: November 1876 These cases demonstrate that preservation in print can give fictional or humorous stories a misleading appearance of"},{"title":"Reprint Trap","url":"/reprint-trap/","description":"Repeated clippings can make one strange report look like many unless wording, dates and newspaper exchange patterns are checked.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Newspapers","level":3,"text":"Reprint Trap When Twenty Clippings Are One Story Reprint Trap Reprint Trap When Twenty Clippings Are One Story Repeated clippings can make one strange report look like many unless wording, dates and newspaper exchange patterns are checked. Repeated clippings can make one strange report look like many unless wording, dates and newspaper exchange patterns are checked. mechanism How newspaper exchange networks spread odd reports Signs that repeated articles share one source Why circulation maps are not sighting maps forteana newspapers reprint trap Introduction A stack of old newspaper clippings can create a powerful illusion: if twenty newspapers printed the same account of a phantom airship, mysterious creature or unexplained fall of objects, it is tempting to read them as twenty separate confirmations. In many cases, however, they are only twenty copies of a single report travelling through newspaper exchange networks or commercial syndication. For Fortean research, this distinction is critical. The number of surviving clippings often reflects the efficiency of nineteenth and early twentieth century news circulation rather than the number of independent witnesses. Historians of the press have repeatedly cautioned that newspapers routinely copied one another, shared exchange copies and republished agency material, making repetition an unreliable measure of corroboration. [OAPEN+2City Research Online] library.oapen.org Digitised Newspapers – A New Eldorado for Historians?January 30, 2023 — by A Fickers — The application of digital technologies to ne... Published: January 30, 2023 How newspaper exchange networks spread odd reports Long before modern digital media, newspapers operated in a dense ecosystem of exchanges. Editors received copies of other papers, clipped items that might interest local readers and reprinted them with little or no rewriting. Later, wire services and newspaper syndicates made this process even more systematic by distributing identical stories to many publications. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Print syndication Print syndication This system was especially favourable to unusual stories. Strange happenings filled otherwise empty columns, entertained readers and cost almost nothing to reproduce. A curious report from a rural weekly might therefore appear: 1. in its original local paper; 2. in neighbouring regional newspapers; 3. in papers hundreds of miles away through exchange arrangements; 4. in later compilations of oddities. By the time researchers encounter these surviving clippings in digital archives, the chain of copying is often invisible. The archive displays many publications, but not necessarily many independent investigations. For Fortean material, this means that newspaper distribution should be treated as evidence for the spread of a narrative, not automatically as evidence that multiple communities observed the same anomaly. Edinburgh Fortean Society Weird World of Charles Fort Channel: Gordon Rutter Open on YouTube Signs that repeated articles share one source The safest assumption is not that matching articles are independent, but that they require comparison before they can be counted separately. Several features commonly reveal a shared origin: Nearly identical wording. Unusual phrases, identical spelling errors or matching sentence order strongly suggest copying rather than independent reporting. The same witness details. If every paper repeats the same names, occupations, quotations and measurements without variation, they are probably drawing from one original account. Simultaneous publication. Newspapers in different towns printing virtually identical stories within days of one another usually indicates wire service or exchange circulation rather than parallel reporting. Shared editorial features. Headings may differ because local editors rewrote titles, while the body text remains identical. Missing local reporting. A newspaper may print a remarkable event supposedly occurring elsewhere without adding any independent investigation or correspondent's observations. Historians using digitised newspaper collections increasingly stress that similarity alone cannot establish multiple sources. Large searchable archives make duplication easier to discover, but they also make duplicated stories appear deceptively abundant if researchers count hits rather than tracing textual relationships. [OAPEN] library.oapen.org Digitised Newspapers – A New Eldorado for Historians?January 30, 2023 — by A Fickers — The application of digital technologies to ne... Published: January 30, 2023 London Fortean Society: The Birth of the Hoax with Ian Keable Channel: Conway Hall Open on YouTube When twenty clippings are really one story A common mistake in Fortean catalogues is treating every clipping as an additional witness. Imagine a report of mysterious lights over a village appearing in one local newspaper. A regional paper republishes it. A metropolitan daily copies the regional version. Smaller weeklies then reprint the metropolitan account. Decades later, a digital search returns twenty surviving newspaper records. Nothing in that search result alone demonstrates: twenty investigations; twenty eyewitness groups; twenty editors confirming the event; twenty independent interviews. Instead, it may represent one reporter, one editor and one original witness account reproduced repeatedly. This is not merely a theoretical concern. Newspaper historians have shown that exchange practices and later syndication routinely produced identical content across geographically distant publications. The appearance of widespread agreement can therefore emerge from printing practices rather than from repeated observation . [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Print syndication Print syndication Why circulation maps are not sighting maps One of the most misleading visual effects in historical Fortean research occurs when newspaper locations are plotted on a map. If identical reports appear in newspapers published across a country, the resulting pattern can resemble a wave of sightings. Yet the map may simply trace newspaper distribution routes. A circulation map answers the question: \"Where was this story printed?\" A sighting map asks: \"Where did independent observers report the phenomenon?\" Those are fundamentally different questions. Commercial newspaper networks frequently ignored geographical proximity. A report originating in one town could appear first in a distant city if exchange agreements or wire services connected those publications. Consequently, the spatial pattern of printed reports may reflect editorial relationships instead of the movement of an unexplained phenomenon. For Fortean analysis, mapping publication locations without reconstructing source relationships risks transforming media circulation into apparent physical evidence. 67. Fortean Times Talk (with Ian Simmons) Channel: Chinwag Podcast Open on YouTube Separating corroboration from replication The strongest newspaper evidence comes from reports that genuinely increase informational independence rather than merely increase publication count. Researchers should look for additional value such as: separate interviews with different witnesses; local follow up investigations; corrections or disagreements between newspapers; independently recorded times or locations; references to official records, weather observations or physical evidence unavailable to the first article. Conversely, identical reports with no new factual content should usually be treated as a single evidential unit regardless of how many newspapers carried them. This approach mirrors broader historical source criticism. Independent testimony depends on separate lines of information, not simply multiple appearances in print. Historians therefore distinguish between numerous surviving copies of one report and genuinely independent attestations of the same event. [OAPEN] library.oapen.org Digitised Newspapers – A New Eldorado for Historians?January 30, 2023 — by A Fickers — The application of digital technologies to ne... Published: January 30, 2023 Why the reprint trap matters for Fortean evidence Many classic Fortean cases survive only because newspapers preserved them. That preservation is invaluable. However, the evidential weight of a case depends less on the number of clippings than on the number of independent reporting chains behind those clippings. Recognising syndicated reprints does not debunk anomalous reports. A strange event may still have occurred exactly as described. The point is methodological: repeated publication should not be mistaken for repeated observation. Before counting newspaper stories as separate witnesses, researchers should establish whether they represent independent reporting or simply the remarkable efficiency with which unusual stories travelled through historical newspaper networks. [OAPEN] library.oapen.org Digitised Newspapers – A New Eldorado for Historians?January 30, 2023 —"},{"title":"Parapsychology","url":"/parapsychology/","description":"Parapsychology overlaps with Forteana, but it asks narrower questions about claimed psychic phenomena.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Parapsychology Where Does Parapsychology Fit in Forteana? Parapsychology Parapsychology Where Does Parapsychology Fit in Forteana? Parapsychology overlaps with Forteana, but it asks narrower questions about claimed psychic phenomena. Parapsychology overlaps with Forteana, but it asks narrower questions about claimed psychic phenomena. policy intervention Shared interest in anomalies Different evidence standards Why boundaries remain contested forteana parapsychology Introduction Parapsychology fits inside Forteana as a narrower, more rule bound attempt to study alleged psychic phenomena: telepathy, clairvoyance, precognition, psychokinesis, remote viewing , apparitional experiences and related claims. Forteana keeps many anomalous reports in view, often across folklore, journalism, science, hoaxing and cultural memory. Parapsychology asks a more specific question: can information, influence or experience occur through means not currently recognised by ordinary psychology, biology or physics? That narrower focus matters because it creates a boundary problem. Parapsychology shares Forteana’s interest in reports that sit awkwardly outside accepted explanation, but it also tries to govern those reports through experiments, controls, journals, professional associations and statistical arguments. The dispute is therefore not only whether “psi” exists. It is also about who gets to set evidence standards for anomalies, what counts as replication, and when a strange report should remain a Fortean curiosity rather than become a scientific claim. The Society for Psychical Research describes itself as the first organisation devoted to scholarly investigation of experiences challenging contemporary scientific models, while the Parapsychological Association defines its field around the study of psi experiences such as telepathy, clairvoyance, psychokinesis, psychic healing and precognition. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk Society for Psychical ResearchFounded in 1882, the SPR was the first organisation to conduct scholarly research into human experiences th... The shared territory: anomalous experience, not guaranteed paranormal fact Parapsychology and Forteana meet at the point where people report experiences that feel evidential but do not fit ordinary categories. A person dreams of a death before hearing the news; a medium appears to know private details; a subject in a laboratory seems to identify a hidden target; a “remote viewer” sketches a distant location; a poltergeist case includes moving objects, frightened witnesses and confused local authorities. These are recognisably Fortean because they are reported as real world anomalies. They are recognisably parapsychological when the claim is narrowed to mind, perception, intention or survival after death. That overlap is older than the modern word “Forteana”. The Society for Psychical Research, founded in 1882, investigated mediumship, apparitions , telepathy and other extraordinary claims using committees, reports and journals rather than leaving them solely to séance culture or newspaper marvels. Cambridge University Library’s description of the SPR archive notes that the society initially focused on investigating, exposing and reproducing fake psychic effects, while also creating administrative and methodological systems for paranormal investigation. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk Open source on cam.ac.uk. Charles Fort ’s own writing also crossed into psychical territory. His final book, Wild Talents , collected reports of apparent human powers, poltergeist like disturbances, dowsing, telekinesis and other claims that later readers would recognise as close to parapsychology. But Fort’s method differed from laboratory parapsychology. He piled up anomalies to unsettle confident explanation; he did not usually try to reduce them to a testable psychic mechanism. That difference is the first boundary: Forteana often preserves the strangeness of a report, while parapsychology tries to decide whether a narrower psychic hypothesis can survive controlled examination. [Internet Sacred Text Archive] sacred texts.com Internet Sacred Text Archive Wild Talents Index Internet Sacred Text Archive Wild Talents Index The distinction is useful because it prevents two common mistakes . The first is to treat every Fortean story about ghosts, coincidences or mysterious forces as if it were a formal parapsychology claim. The second is to treat laboratory parapsychology as if it were merely a more technical version of ghost hunting. In practice, parapsychology has developed its own institutions, vocabulary and disputes: “psi” as a broad label, “ESP” for alleged information transfer without normal senses, “psychokinesis” for alleged mind matter influence, and experimental formats such as card guessing, ganzfeld telepathy tests and remote viewing protocols. [parapsychological.org] parapsychological.org Parapsychological Association – A professional organization...The Parapsychological Association is an international professional organiz... Why parapsychology is more tightly governed than general Forteana Forteana can tolerate unresolved cases. Parapsychology cannot do so as easily, because it presents some claims as experimentally investigable. Once a claim is put into a laboratory or a government funded programme, it must meet standards about blinding, randomisation, sensory leakage, selective reporting, fraud prevention and replication. The governance question is therefore central: how do investigators stop an anomalous result from being produced by chance, cueing, poor controls, experimenter expectancy, data selection or ordinary human error? The early SPR shows the double role that has followed parapsychology ever since. It was not simply a believers’ club, because exposure of fraud and methodological testing were part of its remit. But it was also not a conventional sceptical organisation, because many members took seriously the possibility that some claims might be genuine. That mixed posture made it institutionally unusual: open to anomalies, but formally concerned with records, testimony, controls and publication. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk Open source on cam.ac.uk. Modern parapsychology’s governance structure is visible in its professional organisations and journals. The Parapsychological Association, founded in 1957 and affiliated with the American Association for the Advancement of Science since 1969, presents itself as an international organisation for scientists and scholars studying psi experiences. The SPR continues to publish the Journal of the Society for Psychical Research , a serial that began in 1884 and is still listed as active by the University of Pennsylvania’s Online Books Page. [parapsychological.org] parapsychological.org Parapsychological Association – A professional organization...The Parapsychological Association is an international professional organiz... Yet formal institutions do not settle the evidence question. They only define where the argument can happen. A Fortean magazine or archive may preserve a strange case because it is culturally persistent or unresolved. A parapsychology paper must answer a harder question: does the result exceed chance under conditions strong enough to rule out normal information transfer, bias and error? That is why parapsychology has often become a test case for demarcation: not simply “science versus superstition”, but the harder issue of what happens when a marginal field adopts scientific looking procedures yet remains rejected by most mainstream scientists. Spooks and spoofs: psychologists and psychical research in the inter war years Channel: The Royal Society Open on YouTube Different evidence standards: case files, experiments and replication The clearest boundary between Forteana and parapsychology is the kind of evidence each can use. Forteana is comfortable with case files: old newspaper reports, witness testimony, local legends, police notes, odd coincidences and recurring motifs. These can be valuable for historical and cultural analysis even when they do not prove the claimed phenomenon. Parapsychology may use cases too, but its strongest bid for scientific recognition has usually come from experiments and meta analyses. The ganzfeld debate shows the problem. In a typical ganzfeld telepathy experiment, a “receiver” is placed in mild sensory homogenisation and later chooses which of several images or clips was the target allegedly being sent by another person. Advocates have argued that aggregated results show above chance scoring. Critics have replied that early studies had weaknesses involving randomisation, documentation, sensory leakage, target handling and selective interpretation. The Psi Encyclopedia’s account of ganzfeld meta analysis notes that Ray Hyman began evaluating 42 ganzfeld studies in the early 1980s because they were thought to represent relatively sophisticated parapsychological research, but his critique focused heavily"},{"title":"Apparitions","url":"/apparitions/","description":"Apparitional experiences sit between ghost folklore and psychical research when investigators ask whether they imply survival after death.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Apparitions Are Apparitions Evidence or Experience? Apparitions Apparitions Are Apparitions Evidence or Experience? Apparitional experiences sit between ghost folklore and psychical research when investigators ask whether they imply survival after death. Apparitional experiences sit between ghost folklore and psychical research when investigators ask whether they imply survival after death. population context Why apparitions belong to both ghost lore and psychical research How testimony becomes a case file Where survival claims exceed the evidence forteana parapsychology apparitions Introduction Apparitions occupy a distinctive position within Forteana because they can be treated either as ghost stories or as evidence bearing case reports. The difference lies in the question being asked. Folklore asks what people believe they encountered; parapsychology asks whether any reported encounter provides evidence that consciousness can survive bodily death. That shift transforms an anecdote into an investigation. For more than a century, psychical researchers have collected thousands of first hand reports, especially those in which an apparition allegedly appeared at the time of a person's death or conveyed information unknown to the witness. These reports remain central to debates about survival after death, yet they have never produced a scientific consensus. The strongest cases are intriguing because they appear to contain independently verifiable details, while the weakest resemble experiences that psychology can explain through perception, memory, expectation or grief. The continuing importance of apparitions therefore lies less in proving survival than in testing how extraordinary claims should be evaluated. Why apparitions belong to both ghost lore and psychical research Across cultures, apparitions are usually understood as the visible or otherwise perceptible presence of someone who is absent, dying or dead. In folklore , they often become part of local haunting traditions or religious narratives. Psychical research narrows the question considerably: was the experience merely subjective, or did it involve information that could not reasonably have been acquired through ordinary means? [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk ghosts and apparitions psi research overview Psi EncyclopediaGhosts and Apparitions in Psi Research (Overview)17 Oct 2016 — It treats apparitions as methodologically diverse phenomen... This distinction explains why early psychical researchers separated spontaneous apparitional experiences from legends about haunted places. They concentrated on witnesses who had no expectation of seeing a ghost and who reported an apparently normal looking person before learning that the individual had died or suffered a crisis. Such reports became known as crisis apparitions and were considered more valuable than traditional haunting stories because they could sometimes be checked against independent timelines. [Psi Encyclopedia+2SPR] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk ghosts and apparitions psi research overview Psi EncyclopediaGhosts and Apparitions in Psi Research (Overview)17 Oct 2016 — It treats apparitions as methodologically diverse phenomen... Researchers also distinguished between several broad categories: Crisis apparitions: experiences coinciding with death or severe danger. Post mortem visitations: encounters reported after a person's death, often during bereavement. Haunting apparitions: repeated experiences associated with particular locations. Collective apparitions: events claimed to have been perceived simultaneously by more than one witness. These categories matter because each raises different evidential questions. A haunting associated with a centuries old building relies heavily on accumulated testimony, whereas a crisis apparition can potentially be evaluated against independently recorded events. Frederic Myers & Psychical Research with Terence Palmer Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube How testimony becomes a case file Psychical researchers recognised early that memory is unreliable, especially after dramatic events. Consequently, organisations such as the Society for Psychical Research attempted to standardise investigations rather than simply collecting ghost stories. [Cambridge University Library] lib.cam.ac.uk bridge University LibrarySociety for Psychical Research Cambridge University LibraryThe six initial areas of study were Thought Tran... A stronger apparitional case typically includes several features: the witness documented the experience before learning of the death or crisis; the timing can be independently verified; the witness had no prior expectation of an apparition; multiple independent witnesses reported comparable experiences; ordinary communication can reasonably be excluded. Investigators also looked for weaknesses. They examined whether witnesses had heard rumours beforehand, whether memories had changed over time, whether the experience occurred during illness or emotional strain, and whether later retellings had unintentionally become more dramatic. The emphasis on documentation represented an important departure from ordinary ghost lore. Rather than asking whether a witness sincerely believed the experience, researchers asked whether independent evidence supported the chronology and whether alternative explanations remained plausible. The Census of Hallucinations One of the most ambitious attempts to evaluate apparitional reports scientifically was the Census of Hallucinations , conducted by members of the Society for Psychical Research during the late nineteenth century. More than 17,000 people were questioned about vivid sensory experiences occurring while apparently awake. Investigators were especially interested in reports that coincided with another person's death or crisis. [IAPSOP+2JSTOR] iapsop.com Proceedings of the Society for Psychical Research335 Collective Auditory Hallucinations. 315 317. Collective Hallucinations with different Percepts... 317,318,344,374,383. Collective Tactil... The researchers argued that apparent coincidences between experiences and deaths occurred more often than simple chance would predict. Critics, however, questioned the statistical assumptions, retrospective reporting and possible selection effects. Although the census became a landmark in psychical research methodology, it did not persuade the wider scientific community that apparitions demonstrated survival after death. [JSTOR] jstor.org Numbers, Narratives, and the Census of Hallucinationsby C Keep · 2019 · Cited by 3 — Abstract: This article examines the Society for... Why some cases are considered stronger than others Not all apparition reports carry the same evidential weight. Psychical researchers have consistently regarded veridical cases —those apparently involving accurate information unknown to the witness—as the most significant. A classic example involves a person unexpectedly seeing or hearing a distant relative shortly before discovering that the relative died at approximately the same time. If the timing was recorded independently and no normal communication was possible, proponents argue that the coincidence deserves explanation beyond simple chance. However, even these cases leave multiple possibilities open. Depending on one's theoretical assumptions, the same event might be interpreted as: coincidence combined with selective memory; an unusual but entirely psychological hallucination; an instance of telepathy between living minds rather than communication from the dead; evidence consistent with some form of post mortem survival. Importantly, the survival interpretation is only one hypothesis among several. Early psychical researchers themselves frequently debated whether crisis apparitions, if genuine, pointed more naturally towards telepathy than towards surviving personalities. [ResearchGate+2Sage Journals] researchgate.net 364785913 Phantasms of the Living Phantasms of the Living... Society for Psychical Research, was first published in 1886. It documents over 700 case studies of... Scientific Evidence of PSI and Survival of Consciousness Electricity of Life Channel: The Thunderbolts Project Open on YouTube Where survival claims exceed the evidence The central dispute is not whether people genuinely experience apparitions. Modern psychology accepts that vivid sensory experiences can occur in mentally healthy individuals, particularly during bereavement, fatigue, stress or altered states of consciousness. The disagreement concerns what these experiences mean. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk ghosts and apparitions psi research overview Psi EncyclopediaGhosts and Apparitions in Psi Research (Overview)17 Oct 2016 — It treats apparitions as methodologically diverse phenomen... Several limitations prevent apparitions from becoming decisive evidence for survival after death. First, most reports are retrospective. Witnesses"},{"title":"Ganzfeld Tests","url":"/ganzfeld-tests/","description":"Ganzfeld experiments show why above-chance results can remain controversial when controls, bias, and replication are still disputed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Ganzfeld Tests Why Ganzfeld Telepathy Still Divides Researchers Ganzfeld Tests Ganzfeld Tests Why Ganzfeld Telepathy Still Divides Researchers Ganzfeld experiments show why above chance results can remain controversial when controls, bias, and replication are still disputed. Ganzfeld experiments show why above chance results can remain controversial when controls, bias, and replication are still disputed. dataset evidence How a Ganzfeld test is meant to work What critics found in early studies Why replication matters more than surprise results forteana parapsychology ganzfeld tests Introduction Ganzfeld telepathy experiments occupy a distinctive place in parapsychology because they represent one of the field's most sustained attempts to test alleged telepathy under controlled laboratory conditions. Unlike anecdotal reports of psychic experiences, Ganzfeld studies were designed to produce measurable, repeatable data using standardised procedures and statistical analysis. Yet they have also become a textbook example of the replication problem: some experiments and meta analyses report small but statistically significant effects above chance, while critics argue that methodological weaknesses, inconsistent replication, selective reporting and analytical choices prevent those results from establishing telepathy as a genuine phenomenon. The continuing debate is therefore not simply about whether participants sometimes score above chance, but whether those scores represent evidence for an unknown form of information transfer or the accumulated effects of ordinary experimental problems. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2024 · Cited by 24 — Milton & Wiseman (1999) meta analyzed further 30 studies collected for the period 1987 to 1997... How a Ganzfeld test is meant to work The Ganzfeld method was developed in the 1970s from the idea that reducing ordinary sensory input might make any genuine extrasensory perception easier to detect. \"Ganzfeld\" refers to a uniform field of stimulation: participants typically recline in a comfortable chair wearing halved table tennis balls over their eyes while listening to continuous white or pink noise through headphones under soft red lighting. The aim is not hypnosis but sensory homogenisation, reducing visual and auditory distractions. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2021 · Cited by 9 — This meta analysis is an investigation into anomalous perception (i.e., conscious identification... A standard experiment involves two participants: The sender views a randomly selected target, often a photograph or short video. The receiver remains in the Ganzfeld environment and verbally reports any impressions that come to mind. After the session, the receiver is shown several possible targets—commonly four—and attempts to identify which one was actually viewed by the sender. With four equally likely targets, chance alone predicts a correct choice roughly 25% of the time. Researchers therefore examine whether many trials collectively produce a hit rate significantly above that level. Because individual successes could easily occur by luck, the method depends heavily on accumulating data across many experiments. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2024 · Cited by 24 — Milton & Wiseman (1999) meta analyzed further 30 studies collected for the period 1987 to 1997... The procedure was progressively refined through computer randomisation, automated target selection and stricter isolation measures after early criticisms identified opportunities for unintentional information leakage. These later \"autoganzfeld\" systems were intended to reduce experimenter influence and standardise protocols across laboratories. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ganzfeld experiment Ganzfeld experiment The Woo of Wu Part 7 Replicable Results and the Ganzfeld Experiment Channel: Martymer 81 Open on YouTube What critics found in early studies The first major controversy centred not on statistics but on experimental quality. During the early 1980s, psychologist Ray Hyman reviewed the available Ganzfeld literature and argued that every study contained at least one methodological weakness. The concerns included inadequate randomisation, insufficient documentation, opportunities for sensory leakage, inconsistent judging procedures and weaknesses in experimental blinding. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ganzfeld experiment Ganzfeld experiment Rather than simply disagreeing, Hyman and parapsychologist Charles Honorton undertook an unusually collaborative review of the evidence. Their 1986 joint communiqué acknowledged that many early studies suffered from genuine methodological limitations while also agreeing on a set of improved standards for future experiments. These recommendations included stronger randomisation, better isolation of participants, automated procedures where possible and clearer reporting standards. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ganzfeld experiment Ganzfeld experiment The subsequent autoganzfeld studies were widely presented by supporters as meeting these higher standards. Some analyses reported hit rates above chance, particularly when dynamic video targets rather than still images were used. Critics, however, questioned whether subtle sensory cues or procedural artefacts had been eliminated completely and argued that improvements in methodology had not resolved all possible alternative explanations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ganzfeld experiment Ganzfeld experiment Why replication matters more than surprise results Replication lies at the centre of the Ganzfeld controversy because scientific confidence depends less on isolated positive findings than on whether independent researchers repeatedly obtain the same outcome under comparable conditions. The debate intensified after two influential meta analyses reached different conclusions. Bem and Honorton combined earlier studies and concluded that the overall database showed a statistically significant effect consistent with anomalous information transfer. They argued that improved experimental protocols had strengthened the evidence compared with earlier work. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Does psi exist? Comments on Milton and Wiseman's (1999...by L Storm · 2001 · Cited by 145 — Milton and R. Wiseman (1999) attempted to replicate D. Bem a... Several years later, Julie Milton and Richard Wiseman analysed a newer collection of 30 Ganzfeld studies conducted by a broader range of investigators. Their analysis found an overall effect that was not statistically different from chance, leading them to argue that the earlier findings had not replicated successfully. [koestlerunit.wordpress.com] koestlerunit.wordpress.com Does Psi Exist? Lack of Replication of an Anomalous...by J Milton · 1999 · Cited by 332 — Honorton that appeared to support the existence of a communica... This disagreement shifted the discussion away from whether one experiment had succeeded and towards broader questions: Should only the most methodologically rigorous studies be included? How should unsuccessful replications be weighted? Do differences between laboratories matter? Can a small statistical effect be considered reliable if it appears inconsistently? These questions are not unique to parapsychology; they are central to evidence evaluation across many scientific disciplines. In the Ganzfeld literature, however, they determine whether the cumulative evidence is interpreted as a genuine anomaly or as statistical noise. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2024 · Cited by 24 — Milton & Wiseman (1999) meta analyzed further 30 studies collected for the period 1987 to 1997... Science, Telepathy And The Unexplained Power Of The Human Mind Channel: Progress Science Documentaries Open on YouTube Why meta analyses continue to disagree Supporters of Ganzfeld research often point to later meta analyses that continue to report small positive overall effects. Reviews by Lance Storm and colleagues combined larger databases and concluded that the aggregate results remained statistically significant despite methodological improvements over time. More recent reviews extending the database into the twenty first century similarly report modest effects under both frequentist and Bayesian statistical approaches. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2024 · Cited by 24 — Milton & Wiseman (1999) meta analyzed further 30 studies collected for the period 1987 to 1997... Critics respond that statistical significance alone is insufficient. They argue that combining heterogeneous studies through meta analysis can conceal important differences in quality, protocol and laboratory performance. If only a subset of researchers consistently obtain positive findings while"},{"title":"Psi Statistics","url":"/psi-statistics/","description":"Parapsychology shows why a statistically unusual result may still fall short of mainstream scientific acceptance.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Psi Statistics Why Significant Psi Results Are Not Enough Psi Statistics Psi Statistics Why Significant Psi Results Are Not Enough Parapsychology shows why a statistically unusual result may still fall short of mainstream scientific acceptance. Parapsychology shows why a statistically unusual result may still fall short of mainstream scientific acceptance. mechanism What above chance scoring can and cannot show Publication bias and flexible analysis Why predictable replication remains the key test forteana parapsychology psi statistics Introduction Parapsychology is unusual because many of its most debated experiments do produce results that are reported as statistically significant, yet those results have not led to broad scientific acceptance of psi. This apparent contradiction often confuses readers. A statistically significant result simply indicates that an observed pattern would be unlikely under a particular chance model if all the assumptions of the analysis are correct. It does not, by itself, establish that a proposed explanation—such as telepathy or precognition—is correct. In mainstream science, extraordinary claims require not only statistical evidence but also consistent independent replication, robust methodology, and convincing exclusion of ordinary explanations . Those additional standards are where the debate over psi has remained unresolved. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2021 · Cited by 9 — A second major meta analysis on a set of 'autoganzfeld' studies was performed by Bem & Honorton... What above chance scoring can and cannot show Many laboratory psi studies use a simple statistical question: did participants score above the level expected by chance? In a typical ganzfeld telepathy experiment, for example, a participant choosing correctly 32% of the time when chance predicts 25% may produce a statistically significant result. Meta analyses combining many such studies have sometimes reported small but positive average effects, leading some parapsychologists to argue that psi is detectable only through accumulated statistics rather than dramatic individual demonstrations. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2021 · Cited by 9 — A second major meta analysis on a set of 'autoganzfeld' studies was performed by Bem & Honorton... However, statistical significance answers only one question: whether the observed data are unlikely under a specified null hypothesis. It does not answer several other questions that are crucial for scientific acceptance: Whether the experimental controls ruled out every plausible conventional explanation. Whether the effect appears consistently across independent laboratories. Whether the estimated effect size is stable rather than fluctuating with methodology. Whether the statistical model itself reflects how the data were actually collected and analysed. A small but statistically significant deviation from chance can therefore represent several possibilities. It could indicate a genuine unknown phenomenon, a subtle methodological weakness, selective reporting, underestimated sources of bias, or simply an overestimate produced by random variation. Statistics alone cannot distinguish among these alternatives. [aiprinc.org] aiprinc.org 1985). Meta analysis of psi ganzfeld research: A response to. Hyman. Journal of Parapsychology, 49, 51–91. Hyman, R. (1957). Review of M... This distinction explains why supporters and critics often agree on the numerical calculations while disagreeing sharply about what those calculations mean. Remote Viewing and Statistical Validation Channel: Beach TV CSULB · Views: 147.7K · Uploaded: March 2018 · Length: 28 minutes Open on YouTube Publication bias and flexible analysis One reason mainstream researchers remain cautious is that small statistical effects are especially vulnerable to biases that can accumulate across many experiments. Publication bias—sometimes called the \"file drawer problem\"—occurs when positive findings are more likely to be published than negative ones. If unsuccessful psi experiments remain unpublished, published literature may overstate the apparent strength of an effect. This problem affects many scientific disciplines and is not unique to parapsychology, but it becomes particularly important when expected effects are already very small. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Publication bias Publication bias Flexible analysis presents another challenge. Researchers may unintentionally make numerous analytical decisions after viewing the data, including: choosing among several outcome measures; excluding particular observations; stopping data collection after apparently promising results; testing multiple statistical models before reporting one. Each individual decision may seem reasonable, but together they increase the chance of finding apparently significant results that are difficult to reproduce later. Modern metascience recognises these practices as a general reproducibility problem across psychology and other behavioural sciences rather than something exclusive to psi research. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. The history of ganzfeld research illustrates these concerns. During the 1980s, psychologist Ray Hyman argued that many published studies suffered from methodological weaknesses, including optional stopping, inadequate randomisation, incomplete documentation and possible sensory leakage. Charles Honorton disputed many of those conclusions while agreeing that stronger protocols were needed. Their subsequent joint communiqué identified methodological improvements that both sides accepted before stronger claims could be justified. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Ganzfeld ESP Psi EncyclopediaGanzfeld ESP Psi Encyclopedia7 Mar 2017 — Honorton and Hyman Meta Analyses. By 1982, 47 ganzfeld studies had been carri... Why predictable replication remains the key test The central issue separating statistical significance from accepted scientific evidence is replication. Science rarely treats a single significant experiment as decisive. Confidence grows when different research groups, working independently and using rigorous protocols, obtain similar results under conditions that minimise opportunities for bias. In psi research, this has proved difficult. Successive meta analyses have reached different conclusions depending on which studies were included, how methodological quality was evaluated and which statistical models were applied. Some reviews report a small but persistent above chance effect, whereas critics argue that the apparent signal weakens when higher quality studies and unsuccessful replications receive greater weight. [PMC+2aiprinc.org] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCAnomalous perception in a Ganzfeld condition by PE Tressoldi · 2021 · Cited by 9 — A second major meta analysis on a set of 'autoganzfeld' studies was performed by Bem & Honorton... The debate therefore shifted from asking whether isolated experiments reach conventional significance thresholds to asking whether researchers can reliably predict when psi effects will occur. Mainstream science generally expects a genuine phenomenon to produce dependable results under clearly specified conditions. If an effect appears only intermittently or varies substantially between laboratories without a well supported explanatory theory, confidence remains limited. This is especially important because a statistically significant effect can be extremely small. An effect size may be mathematically distinguishable from chance while still being too inconsistent for practical prediction. From the perspective of scientific acceptance, reproducibility matters more than the existence of isolated positive findings. The Replication Crisis: Why Studies Fail and How Psychology Is Fixing Itself PSYC1040 Week 13A Channel: SocialNeuro Open on YouTube Why meta analysis has not settled the debate Parapsychology has relied heavily on meta analysis because individual experiments often lack sufficient statistical power to detect very small effects. Combining many studies can increase sensitivity and reveal patterns that are difficult to see in single experiments. Supporters argue that repeated small effects appearing across decades deserve serious consideration. Critics respond that meta analysis cannot compensate for weaknesses in the underlying studies. If the original literature contains publication bias, inconsistent methodology or questionable analytical choices, combining those studies may simply produce a more precise estimate of a biased result rather than stronger evidence for psi. [aiprinc.org] aiprinc.org 1985). Meta analysis of psi ganzfeld research: A response to. Hyman. Journal of Parapsychology, 49, 51–91. Hyman, R. (1957). Review of M... The disagreement is therefore less about whether the statistical calculations are correct than about"},{"title":"Leakage Risks","url":"/leakage-risks/","description":"Many psychic tests fail or become disputed because ordinary cues, poor handling, or fraud can look like anomalous information.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Leakage Risks When Psychic Evidence Leaks Ordinary Clues Leakage Risks Leakage Risks When Psychic Evidence Leaks Ordinary Clues Many psychic tests fail or become disputed because ordinary cues, poor handling, or fraud can look like anomalous information. Many psychic tests fail or become disputed because ordinary cues, poor handling, or fraud can look like anomalous information. mechanism Sensory leakage in laboratory tests Fraud prevention in mediumship and experiments Why stronger controls change the claim forteana parapsychology leakage risks Introduction Claims of psychic ability become scientifically interesting only after ordinary explanations have been excluded. Within parapsychology , this means that a seemingly impressive result is not enough on its own. Researchers must show that information could not have reached the participant through normal sensory channels, subtle interpersonal cues, experimental mistakes or deliberate deception. This requirement distinguishes laboratory parapsychology from anecdotal Fortean reports: the central question is not simply whether an unusual event occurred, but whether conventional explanations have been ruled out to a standard that other researchers can independently assess. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov This work has obtained mixed results and provoked a considerable amount of methodological debate... The history of psychic research shows why these controls matter. Many celebrated demonstrations have later been explained by sensory leakage, poor blinding, biased judging or outright fraud. At the same time, supporters of psi argue that increasingly strict protocols sometimes leave residual effects that deserve further investigation. The debate therefore centres less on extraordinary stories than on the quality of the experimental controls. When ordinary clues become \"psychic\" information Sensory leakage refers to any situation in which information reaches a participant through normal means while appearing to have paranormal origins. The leakage may be obvious, such as overhearing a conversation, or so subtle that neither participant nor experimenter realises it has occurred. In psychic research, leakage can arise through several mechanisms: Visual cues: reflections, visible markings, body language, facial expressions or accidental glimpses of targets. Auditory cues: overheard conversations, sounds from adjacent rooms or changes in an experimenter's voice. Handling cues: differences in the way target materials are shuffled, stored or presented. Procedural cues: experimenters unconsciously signalling correct answers through timing, posture or emphasis. Information contamination: participants learning details before testing through conversation, public records or social media. These possibilities matter because humans are exceptionally good at detecting patterns and extracting information from weak signals. A participant may sincerely believe they are relying on intuition while unknowingly responding to ordinary cues. Sensory leakage in laboratory tests Laboratory parapsychology has repeatedly revised its methods in response to concerns about leakage. Early card guessing experiments using Zener cards became controversial because critics demonstrated numerous opportunities for information transfer. Participants might glimpse card backs, see reflections, hear handling noises or detect subtle differences in shuffling. These weaknesses did not necessarily prove fraud, but they made positive results difficult to interpret. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Sensory leakage Sensory leakage The Ganzfeld telepathy experiments were specifically designed to reduce normal sensory input by placing a \"receiver\" in a state of mild sensory homogenisation while another participant attempted to mentally transmit a target image. However, methodological reviews identified potential weaknesses including inadequate soundproofing, handling cues, insufficient randomisation and opportunities for experimenters to learn target identities. Ray Hyman's influential reviews argued that these procedural weaknesses prevented the studies from conclusively demonstrating psi, while Charles Honorton accepted many criticisms and advocated stronger protocols in later work. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Sensory leakage Sensory leakage Later automated Ganzfeld systems reduced several earlier vulnerabilities by computerising target selection and limiting experimenter involvement. Even so, debate continued over whether all possible leakage routes had been eliminated and whether apparent positive findings replicated reliably across independent laboratories. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Ganzfeld experiment Ganzfeld experiment Experiments And Investigations Into Psychic Fraud! 40 MINUTE COMPILATION Derren Brown Channel: Derren Brown Open on YouTube Fraud prevention in mediumship and experiments Mediumship presents additional challenges because successful readings often depend on complex interactions between mediums, sitters and investigators. Modern research attempts to prevent ordinary information transfer through layered controls such as: Separating mediums and sitters into different rooms. Preventing anyone in contact with the medium from knowing the sitter's identity. Using anonymous identification codes instead of names. Randomising reading order. Employing double blind or triple blind procedures so neither participants nor evaluators know which reading belongs to which sitter. Using independent judges to assess matches after all readings have been completed. A frequently cited example is the controlled study by Ciarán O'Keeffe and Richard Wiseman. Professional mediums gave readings while physically separated from sitters, with identities concealed throughout the procedure. Statements were later scored under blind conditions. The investigators concluded that, under these controls, the evidence did not support claims of genuine mediumistic ability, although they also argued that the methodology could serve as a model for testing similar claims in the future. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov This work has obtained mixed results and provoked a considerable amount of methodological debate... Other researchers, including Julie Beischel and colleagues, have reported positive findings using increasingly elaborate blinded protocols. These studies remain debated, with critics questioning statistical interpretation, replication and residual sources of bias rather than necessarily alleging fraud. The disagreement illustrates that stronger controls do not automatically settle the question; they shift attention towards increasingly subtle methodological issues. [googlegroups.com+2ResearchGate] googlegroups.com achieving positive findings regarding the anomalous reception of information... Why cold reading complicates psychic claims Not all apparently accurate psychic performances involve conscious deception. One of the best studied alternative explanations is cold reading : a collection of conversational techniques that create the impression of extraordinary knowledge. Cold reading commonly involves: Broad statements that fit many people. Gradually refining guesses based on reactions. Interpreting silence or emotion as feedback. Allowing clients to supply missing information. Remembering apparent \"hits\" while overlooking numerous misses. Because both clients and performers often remember successful statements more vividly than incorrect ones, sincere belief can coexist with entirely conventional information gathering. Controlled experiments therefore attempt to eliminate all feedback during readings. If mediums cannot observe facial expressions, hear verbal responses or know anything about the intended recipient, cold reading opportunities become much more limited. [cdn.centerforinquiry.org] cdn.centerforinquiry.org Schwartz et al.'s After Death Communication Studiesby R WISEMAN · 2001 · Cited by 13 — Let us suppose that the medium is not in contact w... How TV's Favourite Psychic Got Exposed as a Fraud Channel: Nollid Open on YouTube Historical lessons from exposed fraud The history of psychical research contains numerous cases in which impressive demonstrations collapsed after investigators discovered concealed methods. Investigations into physical mediumship during the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries exposed hidden assistants, fake ectoplasm, concealed props, manipulated furniture and other stage techniques. Later investigators also documented instances in which performers relied on advance research, private files or information gathered before public demonstrations. These exposures encouraged psychical researchers to adopt stricter observation , independent witnesses and tighter physical controls rather than relying on impressive performances alone. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Importantly, exposing one fraudulent performer does not logically disprove every paranormal claim. Equally, identifying a few carefully"},{"title":"Remote Viewing","url":"/remote-viewing/","description":"Remote viewing became a rare case where a Fortean-style claim entered government testing, funding, and national-security debate.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Remote Viewing How Remote Viewing Became a State Problem Remote Viewing Remote Viewing How Remote Viewing Became a State Problem Remote viewing became a rare case where a Fortean style claim entered government testing, funding, and national security debate. Remote viewing became a rare case where a Fortean style claim entered government testing, funding, and national security debate. policy intervention What remote viewing claimed to test Why governments became interested What the controversy says about evidence standards forteana parapsychology remote viewing Introduction Remote viewing is one of the most unusual episodes in the history of parapsychology because it briefly moved from the margins of Fortean speculation into the centre of government funded research. The claim was straightforward but extraordinary: that some people could describe distant or hidden locations, objects or events without using the ordinary senses. During the Cold War, this idea attracted enough attention within the United States intelligence community to receive sustained funding, classified oversight and operational testing. Yet the same programme ultimately became a case study in how governments evaluate extraordinary claims, weighing statistical anomalies against practical usefulness and scientific credibility. Rather than proving or disproving psychic abilities once and for all, the history of remote viewing reveals how national security institutions sometimes investigate unlikely ideas when the perceived strategic stakes appear high. [CIA] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... What remote viewing claimed to test The term \"remote viewing\" emerged in the early 1970s during research led by physicist Harold Puthoff and engineer Russell Targ at the Stanford Research Institute (now SRI International). They deliberately avoided older terms such as \"clairvoyance\" or \"ESP\", hoping to frame the work as a testable information gathering process rather than a spiritual or occult practice. Remote viewers were asked to describe a distant target—perhaps a building, landscape or object—without any normal sensory access to it. Their verbal descriptions and sketches were then compared with the actual target. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Remote viewing Remote viewing Researchers attempted to reduce opportunities for fraud or sensory leakage by introducing blind protocols. In stronger experimental designs, neither the participant nor the experimenter interacting with them knew the target during the session. Independent judges later matched the descriptions against multiple possible targets to determine whether performance exceeded chance expectations. Supporters argued that repeated statistical departures from chance suggested an anomalous source of information, even if no accepted physical mechanism could explain it. Critics responded that methodological weaknesses, subtle cueing, selective reporting and subjective judging could generate apparently impressive results without requiring paranormal explanations. [CIA+2ResearchGate] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... The resulting debate shifted away from spectacular anecdotes and towards questions familiar to experimental science: Were the protocols genuinely blind? Could independent laboratories reproduce the findings? Were statistical effects large enough to matter in practical applications? Scientific and Spiritual Implications of Psychic Abilities Russell Targ Channel: Science and Nonduality · Views: 217.7K · Uploaded: February 2015 · Length: 32 minutes Open on YouTube Why governments became interested Remote viewing reached government attention largely because of Cold War uncertainty. During the 1970s, reports circulated that the Soviet Union was investing resources in psychic research with possible military applications. Whether those reports accurately reflected Soviet capabilities mattered less than the possibility that an adversary might discover an unexpected intelligence advantage. Intelligence agencies have historically explored unconventional technologies whenever the potential strategic payoff appears large enough, even if the probability of success seems low. [ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu] ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu ere trained to acquire such 'Remote Viewing' capabilities for collecting...Read more... This climate led to a succession of classified American programmes that evolved through several organisational names before becoming known collectively as the Stargate Project. Funding and management shifted among military and intelligence organisations over roughly two decades, while research moved from SRI to Science Applications International Corporation (SAIC). Investigators explored both controlled laboratory experiments and operational tasks in which remote viewers attempted to describe foreign facilities, hostages, missing aircraft or other intelligence targets. [CIA] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... The programme's existence demonstrates that governments do not require certainty before investigating unconventional ideas. Intelligence work routinely deals with incomplete information, and agencies sometimes pursue speculative research if the expected cost is modest relative to a potentially transformative capability. Remote viewing therefore became a policy problem rather than merely a scientific curiosity. Remote viewing, CIA psychic spies, and Project Stargate Jimmy Akin's Mysterious World Channel: Jimmy Akin · Views: 6.9K · Uploaded: June 2020 · Length: 1 hour 3 minutes Open on YouTube From laboratory experiment to intelligence programme Operational use proved far more demanding than statistical research. A laboratory experiment only needed to demonstrate performance above chance under controlled conditions. Intelligence agencies required information that was timely, specific, accurate and reliable enough to influence real decisions. Former participants described attempts to integrate remote viewing reports into broader intelligence assessments rather than treating them as stand alone evidence. In practice, however, the material often consisted of symbolic impressions, partial sketches and ambiguous descriptions requiring substantial interpretation. This ambiguity made it difficult to distinguish genuine successes from retrospective matching, in which vague statements appear meaningful only after the true outcome becomes known. [CIA] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... The gap between statistical significance and operational usefulness became increasingly important. Even if an experiment suggested performance slightly above chance across many trials, intelligence officers needed dependable information on individual missions. An intelligence method that occasionally produces intriguing results but cannot reliably indicate when it is correct has limited operational value. Why the programme ended In 1995, responsibility for the programme shifted to the CIA, which commissioned the American Institutes for Research (AIR) to conduct an independent evaluation. The review became notable because it deliberately included experts representing contrasting positions on parapsychology. Jessica Utts, a statistician, concluded that the accumulated experimental evidence indicated a genuine anomaly that could not easily be dismissed as chance or methodological error. She argued that the statistical effects deserved continued scientific investigation. Ray Hyman, a psychologist and long time critic of paranormal claims, accepted that some experiments appeared stronger than many earlier parapsychology studies but argued that the evidence remained insufficient to establish psychic functioning because independent replication, theoretical understanding and methodological certainty were still lacking. [CIA+2CIA] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... The broader AIR assessment focused on a different question from the existence of psi itself: whether the programme had produced useful intelligence. Its conclusion was largely negative. Reviewers found no persuasive evidence that remote viewing had generated operationally valuable intelligence beyond what conventional methods could provide, and they recommended ending the programme. The CIA subsequently terminated it and declassified much of its documentation. [CIA] cia.gov AN EVALUATION OF THE REMOTE VIEWING PROGRAMUtts and Hyman were asked to prepare independent reports based on their review. In this rev... Joe McMoneagle CIA's Project Stargate SRS"},{"title":"Spr Boundary Institu","url":"/spr-boundary-institu/","description":"The SPR matters because it treated strange reports as neither automatic proof nor mere entertainment, but as records to test and preserve.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Parapsychology","level":3,"text":"Spr Boundary Institu The Society That Tried to Police the Strange Spr Boundary Institu Spr Boundary Institu The Society That Tried to Police the Strange The SPR matters because it treated strange reports as neither automatic proof nor mere entertainment, but as records to test and preserve. The SPR matters because it treated strange reports as neither automatic proof nor mere entertainment, but as records to test and preserve. entity Why the SPR was founded Fraud exposure and serious investigation How archives shaped paranormal evidence forteana parapsychology spr boundary institu Introduction The Society for Psychical Research (SPR), founded in London in 1882, occupies an unusual place in the history of Forteana . Rather than treating reports of ghosts, telepathy, mediumship or hauntings as either unquestionable proof or mere entertainment, it attempted to turn them into subjects for organised investigation. Its founders argued that extraordinary claims deserved neither automatic belief nor automatic dismissal , but systematic examination using methods borrowed from science and scholarship. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk The Society is still in existence and is...Read more... That ambition made the SPR a classic boundary institution. It stood between the scientific establishment, which often regarded paranormal claims with scepticism, and the Spiritualist movement, whose members frequently expected confirmation of survival after death or psychic powers. The Society became a place where anomalous reports were collected, tested, criticised, archived and debated. In doing so, it shaped how later generations of parapsychologists, sceptics and Fortean researchers approached unusual evidence, even when they rejected its conclusions. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Psychical research and the origins of American psychologyby A Sommer · 2012 · Cited by 129 — These researchers viewed mediumistic frau... Why the SPR was founded The late nineteenth century saw rapid scientific advances alongside widespread public fascination with séances, mesmerism and alleged supernatural phenomena. Newspapers reported remarkable psychic claims, while Spiritualist organisations promoted communication with the dead. The founders of the SPR believed neither unquestioning acceptance nor outright ridicule offered an adequate response. Henry Sidgwick, Frederic W. H. Myers, Edmund Gurney, William F. Barrett and their colleagues established committees devoted to specific topics, including thought transference (later called telepathy), apparitions , haunted houses, mesmerism and mediumship. Their founding statement promised to investigate these subjects \"without prejudice or prepossession\" and with the same disciplined enquiry applied to recognised scientific problems. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk The Society is still in existence and is...Read more... This institutional structure distinguished the Society from earlier enthusiasts. Instead of relying on individual investigators or newspaper stories, it created: specialist committees with defined research areas; published journals and proceedings subject to criticism by members; formal case documentation; correspondence networks extending across Britain and overseas; and procedures for interviewing witnesses and preserving records. These practices helped transform scattered reports into an organised body of evidence, regardless of whether individual cases ultimately supported paranormal explanations. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk The Society is still in existence and is...Read more... InPresence 0149: William James and Psychical Research Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube Fraud exposure and serious investigation One reason the SPR became a boundary institution was its willingness to expose deception, even when doing so angered people sympathetic to psychic claims. Many early investigations concluded that celebrated mediums relied on sleight of hand, hidden assistants or carefully staged conditions. Richard Hodgson became particularly well known for examining fraudulent practices, while S. J. Davey demonstrated how apparently convincing séance phenomena could be reproduced through ordinary conjuring techniques. These demonstrations were designed not simply to debunk particular performers but to educate investigators about how easily sincere observers could be misled. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Society for Psychical Research Society for Psychical Research This sceptical approach created tension within the Society itself. Some Spiritualist members resigned after repeated fraud investigations, believing the organisation had become too critical of mediumship. Decades later, Arthur Conan Doyle and many supporters left after disputes surrounding the exposure of fraudulent spirit photography and physical mediumship. These disagreements illustrated the SPR's difficult position: it sought evidence wherever it led rather than serving as an advocacy organisation for Spiritualism. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Society for Psychical Research Society for Psychical Research At the same time, exposing fraud did not mean rejecting every unusual report. Investigators sometimes concluded that particular experiences deserved further study because witness testimony, experimental controls or repeated observations appeared difficult to explain immediately. This willingness to separate individual cases from broader belief systems became one of the Society's defining characteristics. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk rmined to discourage interest in...Read more... How archives shaped paranormal evidence Perhaps the SPR's most enduring contribution was not proving paranormal phenomena but preserving an extraordinary documentary record. From its earliest years the Society collected witness statements, correspondence, experimental notes, committee reports, photographs and investigation files. Today these archives are largely housed at Cambridge University Library alongside continuing collections in London. They include material associated with figures such as Oliver Lodge, Arthur Conan Doyle and William Crookes, together with thousands of case files documenting alleged psychic experiences over many decades. [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk The Society is still in existence and is...Read more... These archives mattered because they changed the status of strange reports. Instead of disappearing after newspaper publication or local gossip, cases became permanent records that could be revisited by later researchers using new methods or different assumptions. Examples include: repeated investigations of particular mediums over many years rather than isolated exposures; comparative collections of apparition reports used to search for recurring patterns; experimental records allowing later scholars to evaluate methodology rather than relying on second hand summaries; and correspondence revealing disagreements among investigators instead of presenting a single institutional viewpoint. For Fortean researchers, this archival habit was especially important. Even if a case remained unexplained, preserving the original testimony allowed future investigators to distinguish between contemporary evidence and later retellings . [lib.cam.ac.uk] lib.cam.ac.uk The Society is still in existence and is...Read more... Frederic Myers & Psychical Research with Terence Palmer Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube A meeting place between science and anomaly The SPR never fully belonged to either orthodox science or popular paranormal culture. Many respected scientists and scholars participated at various points, including psychologists, philosophers and physicists, while mainstream scientific institutions often remained unconvinced by its conclusions. Conversely, committed believers sometimes regarded the Society as excessively cautious because it questioned cherished claims and publicised fraudulent practices. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Psychical research and the origins of American psychologyby A Sommer · 2012 · Cited by 129 — These researchers viewed mediumistic frau... This intermediate position influenced several important developments. SPR researchers helped popularise concepts such as \"telepathy\", contributed early studies of hallucinations and eyewitness reliability, and encouraged more systematic thinking about experimental design in the investigation of unusual claims. Historians of psychology have argued that some of these methodological innovations influenced emerging psychological science, even where paranormal interpretations were rejected. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Psychical research and the origins of American psychologyby A Sommer · 2012 · Cited by 129 — These researchers viewed mediumistic frau... Rather than defining itself through a fixed conclusion, the Society defined itself through a process: document first, investigate carefully, expose fraud where found, preserve unresolved material and continue debate."},{"title":"Poltergeists","url":"/poltergeists/","description":"Poltergeist cases test how investigators handle sounds, movement, witnesses, hoaxes, and household stress.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Poltergeists Why Poltergeist Cases Are So Hard to Prove Poltergeists Poltergeists Why Poltergeist Cases Are So Hard to Prove Poltergeist cases test how investigators handle sounds, movement, witnesses, hoaxes, and household stress. Poltergeist cases test how investigators handle sounds, movement, witnesses, hoaxes, and household stress. mechanism What the claims usually include Fraud, psychology, and physics What stronger evidence would require forteana poltergeists Introduction Poltergeist cases are hard to prove because their “evidence” is usually noisy, sudden and domestic: knocks in walls, objects found displaced, furniture said to move, glass breaking, lights flickering, voices, water, stones, fires, and frightened witnesses trying to describe events that rarely happen on command. Within Forteana , they are a stress test for evidence itself. A case can involve sincere witnesses, real disturbances and careful notes, yet still fall short of proving a paranormal mechanism because the crucial event happened off camera, in poor conditions, or around people who may have motives to exaggerate, misperceive or play tricks. The better question is not simply “are poltergeists real?” It is: what would count as good evidence for a claim built from bangs, movements and witness clusters? Poltergeist reports sit at the awkward boundary between household physics, fraud, psychology , folklore and psychical research. That is exactly why they remain classic Fortean material: they are dramatic enough to grip a street, but unstable enough to resist clean proof. The Society for Psychical Research notes that such activity is often rare, short lived, domestic, tense, anecdotal and difficult to observe under good conditions. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia What the claims usually include Most poltergeist reports are not quiet apparitions. They are “noisy” cases: sounds and physical effects are the core claim. A useful working definition is a recurrent disturbance in which percussive sounds and object movements are reported, often in a house, often around a particular person or family, and often with no agreed ordinary cause. The German word is commonly glossed as a “noisy” or “rumbling” spirit, but modern investigators have often treated the label less as a proven spirit category than as a shorthand for a case family. Across case collections, two features keep returning: knocks or raps, and movement of objects. A recent review of case collection work notes that Alan Gauld and A. D. Cornell’s large 1979 study treated “percussive sounds” and “object movements” as defining features; a later 54 case German collection likewise found striking sounds in 70% of cases and object movement in 87%. [anomalistik.de] anomalistik.de zfa2022 1 076 huesmann schriever zfa2022 1 076 huesmann schriever Barrie Colvin’s report on the Andover case also notes that rapping sounds appeared in almost half of the poltergeist cases listed by Gauld and Cornell. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping Poltergeist spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping Poltergeist spr.ac.uk The physical catalogue is broad, but the most memorable claims tend to fall into a few recurring types: Raps, knocks and bangs: Sounds from walls, floors, furniture or ceilings, sometimes alleged to answer questions. Small object movement: Crockery, bottles, toys, stones, books or household items said to fly, fall, shift or appear in odd places. Furniture disturbance: Chairs, beds, shelves or cupboards reported as moving, shaking, overturning or opening. Environmental oddities: Lights flickering, electrical failures, water appearing, doors opening, cold breezes or smells. Centred outbreaks: Activity said to cluster around a child, adolescent, employee, distressed person or particular household. The Seaford case in Long Island, New York, shows the classic pattern. From 3 February to 10 March 1958, the Herrmann family reported opened bottles, loud noises, flying objects, an exploding sugar bowl and an overturned bookcase. It became a media event as well as a mystery, with police, journalists and investigators trying to make sense of disturbances that seemed physical but would not settle into a repeatable demonstration. [library.umbc.edu] library.umbc.edu Seaford Poltergeist – Garrett Collection Seaford Poltergeist – Garrett Collection The Andover case shows the other classic form: apparently meaningful rapping. In 1974, Colvin investigated a Hampshire council house where the main claim was not flying furniture but coded raps that seemed to give meaningful responses. The report says attempts were made to exclude normal causes, including observing objects closely while raps appeared to transfer to them. That sort of case is especially tempting because it seems to add intention to noise: a bang becomes more impressive if it appears to answer a question. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping Poltergeist spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping Poltergeist spr.ac.uk Why witness evidence becomes unstable Poltergeist cases often begin with witnesses who are not trying to launch a theory. A family hears a bang, finds a bottle open, sees a picture fallen, or notices a child frightened by events in a bedroom. The problem is that the evidential value of these observations changes quickly once a “case” forms. Attention increases. Neighbours, clergy, police, journalists, ghost hunters and sceptics may arrive. Children may realise adults are watching. Journalists may reward drama. Investigators may form loyalties. Ordinary household accidents may be reinterpreted as part of the pattern. This does not mean witnesses are lying. It means the social setting becomes part of the evidence. Domestic cases are especially difficult because homes are not laboratories. They contain loose objects, hidden spaces, pipes, pets, tired people, family tensions, night time noises, visitors, arguments and private motives. The SPR’s overview is unusually blunt about these difficulties: cases may be over before researchers arrive, access is complicated by family life and unsocial hours, media involvement can interfere, and investigators must judge both first hand observation and the reliability of what they are told. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia The “focus person” problem adds another layer. Many parapsychological interpretations have treated poltergeist outbreaks as centred on a living person, sometimes an adolescent under stress, rather than a dead agent. William Roll’s term “recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis” described the idea that distress could be externalised as physical effects. The SPR overview notes that this “living agent” model became favoured by many professional parapsychologists, especially where reported phenomena correlated with a person showing anger or distress. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview) – Psi Encyclopedia For a critic, however, the same pattern points in another direction. If the disturbances happen around a particular child, teenager or unhappy adult, that person may be the source not of unconscious psychokinesis but of deliberate trickery, attention seeking, dissociation, mischief or accidental cueing. The evidence can point to “person centred” without deciding the mechanism. Fraud, psychology and physics Poltergeist evidence is vulnerable because many of its headline effects can be produced by ordinary means. A thrown object is impressive if nobody saw it leave a hand. A knock is impressive if nobody checked the pipework, floorboards, neighbours or prankster. A voice is impressive if it seems disembodied, less so if the witness cannot rule out ventriloquism, throat speech or someone speaking from another room. A moving lamp is impressive until strings, vibration or a rigged fitting become possible. Fraud does not need to explain every report to weaken a case. It only needs to show that some key events were staged, or that investigators failed to control the obvious routes by which staging could occur. In the Enfield case, centred on a north London council house between 1977 and 1979, the dispute has never been just “believers versus sceptics”. Even sympathetic investigators Maurice Grosse and Guy Lyon Playfair accepted that some incidents were faked, while critics such as Anita Gregory and John Beloff found the children’s behaviour suspicious and were unconvinced by the stronger claims. Searchable archive records at Cambridge University show that the SPR’s Enfield material includes correspondence, tapes, press cuttings and exchanges involving Gregory and Grosse, reflecting the"},{"title":"Andover Raps","url":"/andover-raps/","description":"The Andover case asks whether raps that seem to answer questions are stronger evidence or just more ambiguous noise.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"Andover Raps Can Knocks Really Answer Questions? Andover Raps Andover Raps Can Knocks Really Answer Questions? The Andover case asks whether raps that seem to answer questions are stronger evidence or just more ambiguous noise. The Andover case asks whether raps that seem to answer questions are stronger evidence or just more ambiguous noise. event time window Responsive rapping in the Hampshire house Controls used to exclude ordinary causes Why intention is difficult to prove from sound forteana poltergeists andover raps Introduction The Andover rapping case is often cited as one of the more intriguing modern British poltergeist investigations because the central claim was not merely that unexplained knocks occurred, but that the knocks appeared to answer questions intelligently. If genuine, this would move the evidence beyond unexplained noise towards apparent communication. Yet this same feature also creates the case's greatest evidential weakness. Determining whether a sequence of knocks genuinely conveys intention is far more difficult than establishing that sounds occurred at all. The Andover investigation therefore occupies an important place in discussions of poltergeist evidence: it illustrates how reports of \"meaningful\" responses can simultaneously strengthen a paranormal claim and make it more vulnerable to alternative interpretations. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Responsive rapping in the Hampshire house The reported events took place in a council house in Andover, Hampshire, during the spring of 1974. Barrie G. Colvin investigated the family over a series of visits after local newspaper reports drew attention to persistent rapping sounds. According to his later published account, the household had gradually developed a simple code by which knocks could represent answers to questions, allowing apparently meaningful exchanges with an unseen source. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Unlike many famous poltergeist cases, the principal claim was not that furniture flew across rooms or objects were violently displaced. Instead, the remarkable feature was the alleged responsiveness of the sounds. Witnesses reported that knocks came from walls, bedheads and, at times, other nearby objects, with the number or sequence of raps corresponding to requested answers. This focus on communication makes Andover unusual even within the already specialised category of \"rapping\" poltergeist reports . [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Colvin regarded the apparent intelligence of the responses as the defining characteristic of the case. In one reported experiment, a concealed number was presented while another family member was asked to indicate it solely through raps. According to the published report, the correct number of knocks was produced repeatedly under those test conditions. Such episodes formed the strongest evidential core of his investigation. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... The Great Spiritualist Hoax That Changed America The Fox Sisters Channel: Moxxy Herstory Open on YouTube Controls used to exclude ordinary causes Because ordinary knocking can be produced by people, buildings or hidden mechanical means, Colvin attempted a variety of informal controls intended to reduce obvious normal explanations. These included: observing walls and furniture closely while raps occurred; attempting to determine whether sounds shifted between different physical objects; varying who asked questions; introducing simple tests in which the expected response was not obvious from casual observation ; recording detailed notes over repeated visits rather than relying on a single dramatic incident. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... One aspect frequently highlighted by proponents is the reported transfer of the apparent sound source. Colvin described occasions when raps seemed to originate not only from walls but also from objects under direct observation, making hidden manual production appear less straightforward than if every sound had come from a single fixed location. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Even so, these controls fell well short of modern experimental standards. The investigation occurred in an occupied family home rather than a laboratory. Continuous audio recording, multiple independent observers, blind protocols and comprehensive physical monitoring were not available throughout the reported events. As a result, the controls could reduce some conventional explanations without conclusively eliminating them. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Why intention is difficult to prove from sound The central problem raised by the Andover case is philosophical as much as investigative: when does a sequence of knocks become evidence of intention rather than coincidence? Several difficulties arise. Pattern recognition. Humans are exceptionally good at finding meaning in ambiguous events. Once a simple code exists, listeners naturally interpret uncertain sounds as fitting expected answers, particularly if they already believe communication may be occurring. Flexible questioning. Many questions posed during alleged rapping sessions permit broad interpretation. Slight uncertainty over when counting begins or ends can change the apparent message without anyone deliberately manipulating the outcome. Observer expectation. Investigators and witnesses who hope for meaningful responses may unintentionally reinforce a communication system by asking leading questions, repeating unclear responses or accepting approximate answers. Limited information content. A series of knocks generally conveys very little information. A correct \"yes\", \"no\" or small number may seem impressive, but statistically such responses require relatively little information to match expectations compared with longer, independently verifiable messages. These problems do not demonstrate fraud or error in any particular instance. Rather, they explain why responsive rapping is inherently more difficult to evaluate than it first appears. Apparent intelligence can emerge from surprisingly small amounts of ambiguous information. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Poltergeists: Noisy Ghosts or Household Spirits? Channel: Icy Sedgwick Open on YouTube Why the case remains debated Supporters argue that Andover deserves attention because the observations extended across multiple visits and included attempts, however limited, to test the responsiveness of the phenomena rather than merely collecting anecdotes. They also point to similarities with historical responsive rapping cases documented in psychical research literature, suggesting that Andover fits a recurring pattern rather than standing alone. spr.ac.uk+2Southwest Ghost Hunters Association [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... Sceptics reach a different conclusion. They note that the strongest evidence depends heavily on witness testimony collected by a single principal investigator and lacks the level of independent replication expected for extraordinary claims. The apparent success of individual tests cannot easily be separated from unconscious cueing, selective reporting, coincidence or subtle human involvement because the original conditions cannot now be recreated. [spr.ac.uk] spr.ac.uk The Andover Case: A Responsive Rapping PoltergeistAfter the fourth rap, I continued to hold the card in place in case any further raps we... A Haunting Experience: The Enfield Poltergeist with Jerry Rothwell Channel: Factual America Podcast Open on YouTube What Andover contributes to the study of noisy evidence Within the broader study of poltergeist reports, the Andover case is valuable less because it settles the existence of paranormal communication than because it exposes a recurring evidential dilemma. A loud unexplained bang may indicate only an unexplained noise. A bang that seems to answer a question"},{"title":"Enfield","url":"/enfield/","description":"Enfield remains powerful because it had many witnesses and records, but also pranks, media attention and investigator disagreement.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"Enfield Why Enfield Is Still So Disputed Enfield Enfield Why Enfield Is Still So Disputed Enfield remains powerful because it had many witnesses and records, but also pranks, media attention and investigator disagreement. Enfield remains powerful because it had many witnesses and records, but also pranks, media attention and investigator disagreement. event time window Witnesses, recordings and press attention Admitted fakery and suspicious incidents How more evidence can create more doubt forteana poltergeists enfield Introduction The Enfield Poltergeist remains one of the most disputed cases in modern Forteana because it combines an unusually rich body of evidence with equally strong reasons to question that evidence. Between 1977 and 1979, investigators, neighbours, police officers, journalists and researchers all reported unusual events at a council house in north London. Hundreds of hours of audio recordings, photographs, witness statements and investigator notes survive. Yet the same archive also contains admitted hoaxes , contradictory testimony, media influence and disagreements among the investigators themselves. Rather than settling the case, the abundance of material has made it harder to interpret. Supporters argue that a small core of incidents remains unexplained despite exposed trickery. Critics respond that once the investigation became contaminated by deception, publicity and expectation, separating genuine events from staged ones became almost impossible. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist Witnesses, recordings and press attention Unlike many alleged hauntings, Enfield was documented almost continuously for months rather than days. The case attracted investigators from the Society for Psychical Research (SPR), especially Maurice Grosse and Guy Lyon Playfair, who spent long periods inside the Hodgson family home. Grosse accumulated hundreds of hours of tape recordings, while Playfair later published a detailed narrative based on interviews, observations and field notes. These records remain one of the largest surviving collections for any alleged poltergeist case. [Wikipedia+2Psi Encyclopedia] Wikipedia Maurice Grosse Maurice Grosse The witness list was also unusually broad. Besides the Hodgson family, neighbours described hearing unexplained knocks, police officers reported seeing furniture move, photographers captured dramatic images, and journalists regularly visited the house. To believers, this diversity suggests the events cannot be dismissed as a simple family fabrication. To sceptics, however, the growing audience changed the social environment around the case. Every new visitor introduced additional expectations, interpretations and opportunities for misunderstanding. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist Media attention became part of the evidence rather than merely reporting it. As newspapers , television crews and curiosity seekers arrived, the children at the centre of the case became aware that their behaviour was being watched and recorded. Investigators therefore faced a classic observational problem: attempts to document the phenomenon also altered the circumstances in which it occurred. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist The Enfield Poltergeist Channel: 210015allan Open on YouTube Admitted fakery and suspicious incidents The greatest source of evidential contamination is that trickery was not merely alleged—it was directly observed on multiple occasions. Janet Hodgson, around whom much of the reported activity centred, was filmed bending spoons, attempting to bend an iron bar and engaging in other deceptive behaviour. Maurice Grosse himself reportedly witnessed her using a broom handle to create noises and hiding equipment during parts of the investigation. Janet and her sister Margaret also admitted to journalists that they had faked some incidents, although they later withdrew those admissions after discussions with the investigators. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist These admissions created two competing interpretations. Supporters' view: the girls occasionally staged phenomena because genuine activity had become less frequent or because they enjoyed the attention, but this did not explain every reported incident. Sceptics' view: once deliberate deception had been demonstrated, all subsequent claims required a much higher standard of proof that the investigation never achieved. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Guy Lyon Playfair Psi EncyclopediaGuy Lyon Playfair Psi Encyclopedia30 Mar 2021 — He argued that trickery explained only a small part of the Enfield even... Photographic evidence illustrates the disagreement particularly well. Images that believers described as showing Janet levitating above her bed have been interpreted by critics as photographs of an athletic child jumping from the mattress. The same photographs therefore function as evidence for opposite conclusions depending on assumptions about the surrounding circumstances. Joe Nickell and other sceptical investigators argued that the images resemble ordinary gymnastics rather than unexplained suspension in mid air. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist The famous \"Bill\" voice, supposedly produced by the spirit of a deceased former resident, also remains contentious. Investigators regarded the harsh, elderly sounding speech as one of the strongest pieces of evidence, while critics argued that the sounds could be produced using the false vocal cords or ventriloquial techniques. Later broadcasts uncovered historical information linking the name and biography to a real former occupant, but this did not resolve whether the voice itself had a paranormal origin or whether ordinary means could account for it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist Investigator disagreement became part of the evidence One reason Enfield remains controversial is that disagreement emerged within psychical research itself rather than only between believers and sceptics. Guy Lyon Playfair consistently argued that some fraud had occurred but maintained that trickery could not explain the entire sequence of events. His position was nuanced rather than absolute: he accepted exaggeration and deception while insisting that a residue of apparently genuine phenomena remained. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Guy Lyon Playfair Psi EncyclopediaGuy Lyon Playfair Psi Encyclopedia30 Mar 2021 — He argued that trickery explained only a small part of the Enfield even... Other researchers associated with the Society for Psychical Research were much less convinced. Anita Gregory regarded several episodes as suspicious and believed the girls had staged incidents, particularly when journalists were present. John Beloff also concluded that ordinary deception, including ventriloquism, provided a better explanation for many of the reported events. These disagreements meant that even investigators working within broadly sympathetic organisations failed to reach consensus. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist Outside psychical research, sceptical investigators such as Joe Nickell argued that the investigation repeatedly accepted weak evidence, overlooked simple explanations and underestimated children's ability to improvise convincing tricks. Nickell also criticised interpretations of technical incidents, including tape recorder malfunctions that some investigators considered paranormal but which he argued were consistent with known mechanical problems in older recording equipment. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist The TRUE Haunting of The Enfield Poltergeist (FULL PARANORMAL HORROR DOCUMENTARY) Channel: The Tape Library Open on YouTube How more evidence created more doubt Enfield demonstrates an important principle in evaluating extraordinary claims: a larger archive does not automatically produce stronger evidence. Several forms of contamination accumulated simultaneously: Observer effects: investigators, journalists and visitors changed the household environment simply by being present. Confirmed deception: exposed trickery forced every subsequent claim to be assessed under increased suspicion. Mixed quality documentation: eyewitness testimony, photographs, tape recordings and personal recollections varied greatly in reliability. Confirmation bias: believers and sceptics often interpreted the same recordings or photographs through different assumptions. Narrative development: as the case became internationally famous, later retellings increasingly selected dramatic episodes while omitting contradictory details. [Wikipedia+2The Guardian] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist This mixture makes statistical or cumulative reasoning difficult. Instead of independent pieces of evidence reinforcing one another,"},{"title":"Focus Person","url":"/focus-person/","description":"Person-centred outbreaks can support very different readings, from unconscious psychokinesis to stress, mischief or attention seeking.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"Focus Person Why Poltergeists Often Follow One Person Focus Person Focus Person Why Poltergeists Often Follow One Person Person centred outbreaks can support very different readings, from unconscious psychokinesis to stress, mischief or attention seeking. Person centred outbreaks can support very different readings, from unconscious psychokinesis to stress, mischief or attention seeking. mechanism Children, adolescents and distressed households Recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis as an explanation Ordinary alternatives to the living agent model forteana poltergeists focus person Introduction One of the most distinctive ideas in modern poltergeist research is that the reported disturbances often appear to follow a living person rather than a haunted building. Instead of asking whether a ghost inhabits a house, investigators noticed that some outbreaks seemed to cluster around a particular child, teenager or emotionally distressed adult. This observation gave rise to the concept of the focus person —sometimes called the agent —and, within parapsychology , to the living agent explanation. The focus person model does not settle the question of whether unusual events are genuinely paranormal. Rather, it shifts attention from places to people. Depending on the investigator's assumptions, the same pattern can support very different interpretations: unconscious psychokinesis, deliberate trickery, heightened attention to ordinary events, or the effects of family conflict and psychological stress. This person centred approach remains one of the central debates in the study of reported poltergeist phenomena. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Why investigators look for a focus person A recurring feature in many classic reports is that unusual incidents are said to occur primarily when one individual is present or nearby. The reported activity may lessen when that person leaves the house, attends school, stays with relatives or is otherwise absent. Investigators therefore began asking not only what happened , but also who was present when it happened. The focus person is not necessarily presented as the cause of the events in an ordinary sense. Even investigators who accepted paranormal possibilities often regarded the individual as the centre around which the disturbances occurred, rather than as someone consciously producing them. This distinction was important because many of the people identified as agents were themselves frightened by the events and appeared to gain little from them. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases The idea also explained why many alleged hauntings seemed unusually mobile. Reports sometimes described disturbances following a person between rooms, homes or even temporary accommodation, rather than remaining tied to one supposedly haunted location. Whether interpreted as evidence of paranormal agency or not, this pattern encouraged researchers to treat the human participant as the primary object of investigation. Paranormal Term of the Day: Recurrent Spontaneous Psychokonesis (RSPK) Channel: Exploring the Supernatural Open on YouTube Children, adolescents and distressed households Many well known cases involve children or adolescents, especially during periods of family tension, illness, bereavement or major life change. This has led researchers from very different traditions to notice similar demographic patterns while disagreeing sharply about what those patterns mean. Parapsychologists have argued that adolescence may coincide with periods of heightened emotional conflict, making it more likely—if psychokinesis exists—for unconscious effects to appear. Psychoanalytic investigators instead viewed the same age group as especially vulnerable to expressing stress through symbolic behaviour or unconscious acting out. Sceptics point to another possibility: young people possess both the opportunity and motivation to play pranks that adults may initially overlook. [Psi Encyclopedia+2Wikipedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Importantly, the association is statistical rather than absolute. Many reported focus persons have been adults, and not every case involves obvious psychological distress. Even supporters of the focus person model acknowledge that no single personality profile consistently predicts who becomes the centre of an alleged outbreak. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis as an explanation The best known living agent hypothesis within parapsychology is recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis (RSPK) , a term introduced by researchers J. Gaither Pratt and William G. Roll in the late 1950s. The name deliberately replaced \"poltergeist\" because it avoided assuming that spirits or ghosts were involved. Under the RSPK model, the reported physical disturbances are interpreted as unconscious psychokinetic effects generated by a living individual under emotional strain. The process is proposed to be involuntary rather than intentional. Instead of consciously moving objects with the mind, the focus person is thought to externalise psychological tension through an unknown mind–matter interaction. [Psi Encyclopedia+2Wikipedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Supporters argue that this framework fits several recurring observations: activity appears concentrated around one individual; incidents are often brief rather than lifelong; emotional stress frequently precedes reported outbreaks; witnesses sometimes describe disturbances occurring close to the focus person rather than throughout an entire property. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases The theory, however, faces a major scientific obstacle. Psychokinesis itself has not been established as a reproducible physical phenomenon under controlled experimental conditions. Consequently, mainstream science does not accept RSPK as an evidence based explanation for reported poltergeist cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Ordinary alternatives to the living agent model The same concentration of events around one person can also be explained without invoking paranormal forces. This is why investigators increasingly examine ordinary mechanisms before considering extraordinary ones. One possibility is intentional mischief or deception . If a child or teenager throws objects, creates noises or manipulates household items, other family members may witness only the apparent result rather than the act itself. Once expectations develop, unrelated accidents can also become incorporated into the growing narrative of a \"poltergeist\". Researchers such as Frank Podmore and later Joe Nickell argued that many famous outbreaks are better understood in this way. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frank Podmore Frank Podmore Another explanation involves attention and interpretation . In households already experiencing stress, ordinary noises, appliance failures or misplaced objects may be noticed more frequently and linked together into a meaningful pattern. Once neighbours, journalists or paranormal investigators become involved, increased observation can unintentionally reinforce this process through expectation and confirmation bias. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Psychological approaches offer a further perspective. Rather than assuming deliberate fraud, some researchers have suggested that emotional conflict, family dynamics and interpersonal tensions shape both behaviour and perception during alleged outbreaks. In this view, the focus person is psychologically central to the case without necessarily producing paranormal effects. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases Psi Encyclopedia Psychological Aspects of Poltergeist Cases What Is Recurrent Spontaneous Psychokinesis (RSPK)? Channel: All About Myths and Conspiracies Open on YouTube What famous cases reveal Several frequently discussed cases illustrate why the focus person concept remains controversial. The 1958 Seaford case became influential because William Roll interpreted some events as fitting the RSPK model while acknowledging that certain incidents might have involved pranks. Later sceptical reanalysis argued that the evidence was better explained by the"},{"title":"House Noises","url":"/house-noises/","description":"Pipes, floorboards, vibrations, pets and hidden spaces can turn ordinary homes into misleading evidence machines.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"House Noises When a Haunted House Is Just a House House Noises House Noises When a Haunted House Is Just a House Pipes, floorboards, vibrations, pets and hidden spaces can turn ordinary homes into misleading evidence machines. Pipes, floorboards, vibrations, pets and hidden spaces can turn ordinary homes into misleading evidence machines. mechanism Common domestic sources of knocks and bangs Why night time settings distort judgment Checks that separate physics from mystery forteana poltergeists house noises Introduction Many reported poltergeist cases begin with a simple observation : unexplained knocking, creaking, footsteps overhead or sharp bangs somewhere inside an ordinary home. Because these sounds often occur without an obvious source, particularly at night, they can become the first \"evidence\" that a house is haunted. In practice, however, buildings are complex mechanical systems. Timber expands and contracts, plumbing changes pressure, heating systems cycle on and off, appliances vibrate, and animals move through hidden spaces. Once a supernatural explanation takes hold, ordinary noises may be reinterpreted as part of an escalating pattern rather than as unrelated household events. This does not mean every unexplained sound has an immediate explanation. Rather, investigations into alleged hauntings consistently show that domestic noises deserve careful examination before they are treated as evidence of paranormal activity. The challenge is not that houses are silent until ghosts arrive; it is that occupied buildings produce an astonishing variety of sounds that are easily misidentified under conditions of uncertainty. Common domestic sources of knocks and bangs Most houses generate intermittent noises without any fault or supernatural cause. Many occur only under particular temperature, weather or occupancy conditions, making them seem mysterious because they resist easy repetition. Some of the most common mechanisms include: Heating pipes and plumbing. Hot water causes metal pipes to expand against joists, brackets and wall cavities. As they cool, they contract again. This can produce loud single knocks, rapid tapping or sequences of bangs that appear to move through the building. Water hammer—pressure waves created when valves close suddenly—can create particularly sharp impacts. Floorboards and timber framing. Wood absorbs and releases moisture, expanding in humid conditions and shrinking in dry ones. Seasonal changes or overnight temperature drops can make floorboards, roof trusses and wall studs creak or crack loudly. Central heating and boilers. Boilers, radiators and ductwork cycle automatically. Metal ducts flex as warm air passes through them, while pumps and valves generate intermittent clicks and vibrations. Appliances and vibration. Washing machines, refrigerators, freezers, extractors and air conditioning equipment can transmit vibrations into floors and walls, making sounds appear to originate somewhere entirely different. Roof spaces and wall cavities. Birds, rodents, squirrels and other animals often move through lofts or cavity walls. Scratching, thumping or brief running sounds can easily resemble footsteps, especially when the exact location is hidden. Wind and weather. Loose roof tiles, gutters, fencing, branches, chimneys and exterior cladding all respond to changing wind conditions. Because gusts vary unpredictably, the resulting noises may seem intelligent or selective. Buildings also conduct sound surprisingly well. A knock produced several rooms away may emerge from a wall or ceiling because timber framing, metal pipework and masonry transmit vibration more efficiently than air. As a result, witnesses often misjudge both the direction and distance of the original sound. Is Your Home Haunted? Common Spooky Signs and Their Real Explanations! Channel: Diane Cardano:The Real Deal in Real Estate Open on YouTube Why night time settings distort judgement The classic \"bump in the night\" is not simply a cultural cliché. Several well understood psychological and environmental factors make nocturnal sounds more likely to be interpreted as meaningful. At night, background traffic, conversation and household activity decline. The relative quiet makes ordinary structural noises far more noticeable than during the day. Sleepiness also reduces attention and increases uncertainty about what has just been heard. Darkness removes visual information that normally helps identify a sound's source. A radiator settling after heating may sound remarkably like footsteps when the listener cannot simultaneously see that nothing is moving. Expectation amplifies the effect. Once residents begin discussing possible paranormal activity, confirmation bias encourages them to notice every unusual creak while overlooking the hundreds of ordinary noises that occur without incident. Investigators of alleged hauntings have repeatedly noted that expectation changes how ambiguous events are interpreted, particularly when witnesses already know a property's haunted reputation . [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Haunted house Haunted house Emotional states matter as well. Stress, bereavement, anxiety, conflict within a household and prolonged sleep disruption can increase vigilance, making ambiguous sounds seem unusually significant without implying dishonesty on the part of witnesses. When vibration feels like a presence Not every unsettling experience involves an audible bang. Some buildings contain very low frequency vibrations, known as infrasound, generated by ventilation systems, boilers, traffic or large mechanical equipment. Recent laboratory research found that exposure to infrasound increased irritation and physiological stress, even when participants could not consciously hear it. Researchers argue that these subtle bodily sensations may contribute to reports that an old basement or house \"feels wrong\" before any obvious noise is noticed. Scientists caution, however, that this work offers a plausible explanation for feelings of unease rather than for dramatic claims such as furniture moving or objects flying across rooms. [Smithsonian Magazine] smithsonianmag.com Smithsonian Magazine Worried Your House Is Haunted? The Spooky Sensation...April 28, 2026 — 28 Apr 2026 — It's “plausible” that feelings caused by these low frequency noises might lead som... Published: April 28, 2026 The distinction is important. Feeling watched, uncomfortable or tense inside an old building may have an environmental component without providing evidence for a poltergeist. Why ordinary noises become convincing evidence Domestic sounds rarely remain isolated. Instead, witnesses often assemble them into a narrative. A sequence might develop like this: 1. A loud unexplained bang wakes someone during the night. 2. Days later, another unrelated noise occurs elsewhere in the house. 3. An object is discovered where nobody remembers leaving it. 4. Family members begin discussing whether the events are connected. 5. Future noises are interpreted through that new framework. Humans are exceptionally good at detecting patterns, even when events are unrelated. Once separate incidents become linked into a single story, the perceived strength of the haunting increases despite each individual event having an ordinary explanation . This process explains why many famous poltergeist investigations devote considerable effort to separating genuinely connected events from the normal background activity of occupied homes. How to Fix Water Hammer Noise FAST! (Stop Banging Pipes for Good) Anytime Plumbing Guide Channel: Anytime Plumbing, Inc. Open on YouTube Checks that separate physics from mystery Investigators—whether sceptical or sympathetic to paranormal claims—often begin with practical environmental checks before considering more unusual explanations. Useful steps include: Recording exactly when noises occur and whether they correlate with heating cycles, plumbing use or weather. Checking lofts, crawl spaces and wall cavities for wildlife. Inspecting loose pipe brackets, radiators, ductwork and floorboards. Comparing sounds heard inside with those produced outside by neighbouring properties, traffic or industrial equipment. Determining whether multiple independent listeners identify the same source location or whether perceptions differ. Controlled observation is especially valuable because many supposed \"mysterious\" noises disappear once their physical origin is identified. Equally important, if sounds continue after common causes have been ruled out, investigators can define the remaining problem more precisely rather than beginning with a paranormal assumption. Why houses are effective \"evidence machines\" Ordinary homes unintentionally create ideal conditions for mistaken poltergeist reports . They contain concealed spaces, changing temperatures, moving water, flexible materials, hidden animals and countless sources of intermittent vibration. Their internal structure also"},{"title":"Seaford","url":"/seaford/","description":"The Seaford case shows how bottles, noises and flying objects can create a famous mystery without producing repeatable proof.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"Seaford What Did the Seaford Poltergeist Prove? Seaford Seaford What Did the Seaford Poltergeist Prove? The Seaford case shows how bottles, noises and flying objects can create a famous mystery without producing repeatable proof. The Seaford case shows how bottles, noises and flying objects can create a famous mystery without producing repeatable proof. event time window The 1958 Herrmann family outbreak Police, press and investigator involvement Why physical claims remained hard to prove forteana poltergeists seaford Introduction The Seaford Poltergeist remains one of the best known modern American poltergeist cases because it appeared to involve repeated physical disturbances witnessed by family members, police officers, journalists and paranormal investigators. Between early February and mid March 1958, the Herrmann family in Seaford, Long Island, reported bottles opening and spilling by themselves, loud unexplained noises, objects apparently flying through rooms, and furniture overturning. Despite the unusually large number of witnesses and the attention the case received, it never produced the kind of repeatable, controlled physical evidence that could establish a paranormal explanation. Instead, Seaford became an enduring example of how dramatic events can generate extensive testimony while remaining evidentially unresolved. [library.umbc.edu] library.umbc.edu n exploding bowl of sugar, an overturned bookcase, loud noises...Read more... The 1958 Herrmann family outbreak The reported disturbances began on 3 February 1958 in the home of the Herrmann family and continued for roughly five weeks. Accounts described an assortment of apparently physical events rather than ghostly apparitions . Bottles containing household liquids were said to uncap and spill, a sugar bowl reportedly shattered, books and other objects moved unexpectedly, and at one point a bookcase was said to overturn. Loud knocks and crashes were also repeatedly reported. Investigators later catalogued dozens of separate incidents during the outbreak. [library.umbc.edu+2Psi Encyclopedia] library.umbc.edu n exploding bowl of sugar, an overturned bookcase, loud noises...Read more... What distinguished Seaford from many lesser known reports was not the type of phenomena but the number of observers. Friends, neighbours, police officers and members of the press visited the house while the disturbances were still being reported. Some witnesses stated that they observed unusual movements or heard unexplained sounds, lending the case an appearance of credibility beyond a family's private testimony. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Seaford Poltergeist Psi EncyclopediaSeaford Poltergeist Psi Encyclopedia24 Jan 2022 — The Seaford 'poltergeist' was a widely publicised series of disturban... Even so, almost every reported event shared a crucial limitation. The alleged phenomena occurred unexpectedly, lasted only moments, and could not be reproduced under controlled conditions. No single incident produced an unambiguous chain of evidence showing that an object had moved without any ordinary physical cause. Popper the Poltergeist (Seaford, Catholic Family Poltergeist, RSPK) Jimmy Akin's Mysterious World Channel: Jimmy Akin Open on YouTube Police, press and investigator involvement The Seaford case quickly became a media story. Newspaper coverage attracted visitors, curious neighbours and paranormal researchers, transforming what began as a household disturbance into a nationally discussed mystery. The publicity also increased the number of people who claimed to witness unusual events, but it simultaneously made objective investigation more difficult because the home was no longer a controlled environment. [library.umbc.edu] library.umbc.edu n exploding bowl of sugar, an overturned bookcase, loud noises...Read more... Among the best known investigators were parapsychologists J. G. Pratt and W. G. Roll, working in association with researchers connected to J. B. Rhine's laboratory at Duke University. They documented the reported incidents and argued that the pattern resembled what parapsychologists called recurrent spontaneous psychokinesis (RSPK): the hypothesis that unconscious mental processes, often associated with an emotionally stressed young person, might produce physical effects without deliberate intention. They regarded 12 year old James Herrmann as the apparent focus of the disturbances, not because they believed he consciously caused them, but because many reported events clustered around him. [survivalafterdeath.info+2Psi Encyclopedia] survivalafterdeath.info IS S: The Seaford Poltergeist: J G. PrattThe pages that follow give a detailed summary of our findings on the case as these were finally assembled and reported in the Jou... The investigators attempted to separate family members, observe rooms and record incidents as carefully as circumstances allowed. However, the activity remained unpredictable. Although some events reportedly occurred while observers were present, they still unfolded too quickly and under conditions that left room for competing explanations. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Seaford Poltergeist Psi EncyclopediaSeaford Poltergeist Psi Encyclopedia24 Jan 2022 — The Seaford 'poltergeist' was a widely publicised series of disturban... Why the physical claims remained hard to prove From an evidential perspective, Seaford illustrates an important distinction between many reported physical events and strong physical proof . Several factors limited the strength of the evidence: No repeatable demonstration. Investigators never succeeded in producing the alleged effects reliably under controlled observation . Brief and unexpected events. Most incidents lasted only seconds, making detailed observation difficult. Lack of comprehensive recording. The outbreak occurred before continuous domestic video surveillance was feasible, leaving investigators dependent largely on witness accounts and written notes. Crowded investigative conditions. As publicity grew, the increasing number of visitors complicated efforts to exclude accidental disturbance or deliberate interference. Ordinary objects rather than unique traces. Spilled bottles, overturned furniture and broken household items were genuine physical outcomes, but they did not by themselves identify what caused them. [Psi Encyclopedia+2survivalafterdeath.info] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Seaford Poltergeist Psi EncyclopediaSeaford Poltergeist Psi Encyclopedia24 Jan 2022 — The Seaford 'poltergeist' was a widely publicised series of disturban... This distinction explains why the case continues to attract discussion. Physical effects certainly appear more compelling than reports of feelings or apparitions, yet physical outcomes alone do not automatically demonstrate an unknown force. A bottle found open, for example, establishes that it opened; it does not establish how it opened. The Seaford Haunting and Cheesman Park Two True Stories that Allegedly Inspired \"Poltergeist\" Channel: Tilly CXZ Open on YouTube Competing interpretations Supporters of the paranormal interpretation argued that the number of independent witnesses and the variety of reported disturbances made deliberate fraud an inadequate explanation. They noted that some incidents allegedly occurred while multiple observers were present or when family members appeared separated from the affected area. Pratt and Roll therefore considered psychokinesis the explanation that best fit the pattern they documented. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Seaford Poltergeist Psi EncyclopediaSeaford Poltergeist Psi Encyclopedia24 Jan 2022 — The Seaford 'poltergeist' was a widely publicised series of disturban... Sceptics reached a different conclusion. They argued that the evidence depended overwhelmingly on testimony rather than controlled observation and that apparently impossible events could result from unnoticed human actions, misperception or selective reporting. Later critical examinations suggested that the young James Herrmann had both opportunity and motive to create at least some of the disturbances through practical jokes, although no decisive proof of fraud was ever established. Because neither side produced conclusive evidence, the competing interpretations have remained unresolved. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Seaford Poltergeist Psi EncyclopediaSeaford Poltergeist Psi Encyclopedia24 Jan 2022 — The Seaford 'poltergeist' was a widely publicised series of disturban... The case therefore occupies an unusual position. Believers cannot point to a definitive experiment proving paranormal agency, while sceptics cannot point to a documented confession or reconstruction explaining every reported event. What Seaford ultimately proved The Seaford Poltergeist did not prove that poltergeists exist, nor did it conclusively"},{"title":"Witness Pressure","url":"/witness-pressure/","description":"Once neighbours, clergy, police, journalists and investigators arrive, the social setting becomes part of the evidence.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Poltergeists","level":3,"text":"Witness Pressure How a Household Mystery Becomes a Public Case Witness Pressure Witness Pressure How a Household Mystery Becomes a Public Case Once neighbours, clergy, police, journalists and investigators arrive, the social setting becomes part of the evidence. Once neighbours, clergy, police, journalists and investigators arrive, the social setting becomes part of the evidence. population context How attention changes witness behaviour Children, reporters and incentives for drama Why domestic access complicates investigation forteana poltergeists witness pressure Introduction Poltergeist cases rarely remain private for long. Once neighbours, police officers, clergy, journalists or paranormal investigators begin visiting a household, the social environment becomes part of the evidence itself. Reports are no longer shaped only by the original witnesses, but also by expectations, repeated questioning, media attention and the practical challenges of managing a crowded home. For investigators—whether sceptical or sympathetic—this creates a central problem: it becomes increasingly difficult to distinguish between an unusual event, an honest recollection, a mistaken interpretation and behaviour influenced by an audience. Researchers across psychical research, psychology and sceptical inquiry have repeatedly identified this shift from domestic incident to public spectacle as one of the defining difficulties of evaluating poltergeist claims. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview Psi EncyclopediaPoltergeists (Overview) Psi Encyclopedia1 Mar 2015 — Noisy outbreaks of thrown objects, raps, fires and apparent mischi... How attention changes witness behaviour A typical poltergeist report begins with a small number of closely connected witnesses: usually members of the same household who experience unexplained noises or moving objects. As publicity grows, however, the witness pool expands dramatically. Friends arrive to watch, neighbours compare stories, police investigate complaints, journalists seek interviews and paranormal researchers organise observations. Every additional observer changes the social dynamics. This matters because witness testimony is not collected under laboratory conditions. People discuss what they have seen before formal interviews take place. Memories are revised as individuals compare experiences. Incidents that originally seemed unrelated may later be grouped together into a single \"outbreak\". Conversely, ambiguous events may be forgotten if they do not fit the emerging narrative. Psychological research on eyewitness testimony has long shown that memory is reconstructive rather than photographic. Repeated discussion, leading questions and knowledge of other people's experiences can alter later recollections without deliberate dishonesty. Poltergeist investigations create precisely these conditions: emotionally charged situations, repeated interviews and strong expectations that something remarkable is occurring. [Küre Encyclopedia] kureansiklopedi.com Küre EncyclopediaEnfield Poltergeist Case KÜRE EncyclopediaThe house was not a laboratory: the placement of cameras and microphones, ch... Children, reporters and incentives for drama Many of the best known twentieth century poltergeist cases centred on children or adolescents. This does not establish that children cause the reported phenomena, but it introduces distinctive social pressures. Children quickly become aware that adults are paying close attention. Investigators may spend hours asking questions. Newspapers seek dramatic quotations. Television crews may request demonstrations or re enactments. Even without conscious deception, the incentive structure changes because unusual behaviour receives immediate attention and ordinary behaviour does not. The widely publicised Enfield case illustrates this tension. Over eighteen months, neighbours, journalists, photographers and investigators reported witnessing unusual events, while extensive media coverage transformed an ordinary council house into an international paranormal story. At the same time, investigators documented occasions on which the children admitted to pranks or were observed producing at least some effects themselves. Supporters argued that these episodes did not explain every reported incident, whereas critics concluded that publicity encouraged exaggeration and performance. Even investigators who believed aspects of the case were genuine acknowledged that not every reported event could be accepted uncritically. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Enfield poltergeist Enfield poltergeist Media attention can also reinforce witness confidence. Once newspapers present a household as haunted, later visitors often arrive expecting to witness extraordinary events. Expectations influence both interpretation and reporting, increasing the likelihood that ambiguous sounds or movements will be viewed as paranormal rather than ordinary. The TRUE Haunting of The Enfield Poltergeist (FULL PARANORMAL HORROR DOCUMENTARY) Channel: The Tape Library Open on YouTube Why domestic access complicates investigation Unlike controlled experiments, most poltergeist investigations occur in lived in homes that cannot simply be sealed off. Daily domestic life continues while an investigation proceeds. Family members cook meals, children attend school, neighbours visit, relatives stay overnight and repair workers enter the property. Journalists, photographers and curious visitors may also move through rooms where later disturbances are reported. These practical realities create several investigative difficulties: It becomes hard to establish exactly who had access to particular rooms or objects. Potential sources of accidental disturbance multiply as visitor numbers increase. Continuous observation is almost impossible without disrupting ordinary family life. Privacy concerns prevent investigators from monitoring every space at every moment. Researchers associated with the Society for Psychical Research have repeatedly noted that poltergeist cases are unusually resistant to controlled observation because they occur in ordinary households rather than experimental settings. The environment itself limits the degree of scientific control that can realistically be imposed. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Poltergeists (Overview Psi EncyclopediaPoltergeists (Overview) Psi Encyclopedia1 Mar 2015 — Noisy outbreaks of thrown objects, raps, fires and apparent mischi... Witness clusters are not independent evidence A large number of witnesses often appears persuasive at first glance. However, investigators distinguish between many people independently observing the same event and many people participating in the same social environment. In famous cases, dozens of witnesses may have visited the house over months or years, yet relatively few actually observed the most dramatic alleged events firsthand. Others may have heard stories from family members, seen the aftermath of an incident or witnessed only minor disturbances. From an evidential perspective, witness clusters therefore require careful analysis: Independence: Did witnesses observe events separately or after discussing them? Timing: Were accounts recorded immediately or reconstructed later? Opportunity: Did each witness personally observe the claimed phenomenon? Consistency: Do accounts agree on important details without becoming suspiciously identical? Large witness numbers can increase confidence when observations are genuinely independent. They do not automatically eliminate the possibility of shared misunderstanding, social influence or selective memory. 1977: The ENFIELD POLTERGEIST Nationwide Weird and Wonderful BBC Archive Channel: BBC Archive Open on YouTube The role of journalists Journalists occupy an unusual position in many celebrated poltergeist cases. They are neither neutral laboratory observers nor household participants. News organisations naturally favour dramatic developments: flying objects, mysterious voices or official involvement by police. Routine periods in which nothing happens receive much less attention. This reporting pattern can unintentionally amplify the apparent intensity of an outbreak. Coverage may also alter behaviour inside the household itself. Families experience public scrutiny, neighbours become more attentive to unusual noises and additional investigators arrive after reading newspaper reports. The investigation becomes part of the story, creating feedback between reporting and the events being reported. The 1958 Seaford case demonstrates this process. Reports of unexplained disturbances attracted police officers, journalists and psychical researchers, turning a local household problem into a widely discussed public mystery. Later assessments reached sharply different conclusions about the significance of the evidence, illustrating how extensive publicity does not necessarily"},{"title":"Dismissal","url":"/dismissal/","description":"Forteana asks when institutions are reasonably sceptical and when they reject awkward observations too quickly.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Dismissal When Does Scepticism Become Dismissal? Dismissal Dismissal When Does Scepticism Become Dismissal? Forteana asks when institutions are reasonably sceptical and when they reject awkward observations too quickly. Forteana asks when institutions are reasonably sceptical and when they reject awkward observations too quickly. policy intervention Fort's attack on authority Why weak evidence gets rejected Handling anomalies responsibly forteana dismissal Introduction Forteana is often treated as a shelf of oddities, but its sharper question is institutional: when is scepticism a healthy filter, and when does it become a way of discarding awkward observations before they have been properly examined? Charles Fort ’s phrase “damned” facts named reports that science had, in his view, excluded because they did not fit accepted categories. His complaint was not that every strange report was true, but that authority could turn uncertainty into dismissal too quickly. The Book of the Damned is described by Project Gutenberg as a 1919 catalogue of anomalous reports that mainstream science had excluded or “damned”, including strange falls, sky sightings and poltergeist claims. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Project Gutenberg The Book of the Damned by Charles Fort Project Gutenberg Project Gutenberg The Book of the Damned by Charles Fort Project Gutenberg The modern answer is not to abandon scepticism. It is to govern it. Responsible inquiry needs a way to reject weak evidence without shaming observation itself. That means separating three things that are often confused: a witness may be sincere, a report may be worth recording, and a dramatic explanation may still be unsupported. NASA’s 2023 UAP study made exactly this distinction, calling for rigorous evidence, better data acquisition, calibrated sensors, systematic reporting and reduced stigma rather than either credulous acceptance or blanket ridicule. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA Science... Fort’s attack on authority Fort’s lasting influence came from making a social criticism sound like a catalogue of marvels. He gathered reports of anomalous falls, lights, disappearances and other odd events, then used them to accuse scientific culture of protecting its boundaries. The charge was not simply “scientists are wrong”. It was subtler: institutions may decide in advance what kinds of facts are respectable, then judge reports partly by whether they fit that prior map. That criticism has obvious dangers. A system that treats every rejected claim as suppressed truth becomes impossible to correct. Fort’s own writing often played with extravagant explanations, and later Fortean readers have had to distinguish his useful suspicion of premature certainty from the temptation to turn anomaly into belief. Anomalistics, the more disciplined heir to this impulse, is defined by the Psi Encyclopedia as taking unexplained claims seriously without committing itself to any outcome, treating anomalies as opportunities for inquiry rather than immediate belief or dismissal. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics – Psi Encyclopedia The governance problem is that science must filter. Journals, laboratories, grant panels and public agencies cannot pursue every strange report with equal effort. Yet scientific ideals also include organised scepticism: the norm that claims should be open to critical scrutiny. Robert Merton’s widely cited scientific ethos included universalism, communality, disinterestedness and organised scepticism, meaning that knowledge claims should be evaluated through shared critical processes rather than protected by rank or taste. [bitss.org] bitss.org Open source on bitss.org. Forteana matters here because it asks whether “organised scepticism” is actually organised, or merely social. A well governed sceptical system asks: What exactly was observed? By whom? Under what conditions? With what instruments? What ordinary explanations have been tested? What data would change the assessment? A dismissive system jumps faster: the topic is embarrassing, the witness is not expert, the claim resembles folklore, therefore the report is not worth preserving. Why weak evidence gets rejected Most anomalous reports are rejected for good reasons. They are single events, often unrepeatable, usually filtered through memory, language, local rumour and media incentives. Even credible witnesses can misjudge distance, speed, scale, sound, light, time and probability. A bright object in the sky can be a planet, aircraft, satellite, balloon, drone, military platform, weather effect, camera artefact or reflection. A report of something falling from the sky can involve storms, birds, waterspouts, prank, misidentification or later exaggeration. NASA’s UAP report is useful because it shows what responsible rejection looks like. It does not say witnesses are worthless. It says witness reports can reveal patterns, but without calibrated sensor data they cannot provide conclusive evidence or support repeatable analysis. It also notes that UAP analysis is hampered by poor sensor calibration, lack of multiple measurements, missing sensor metadata and lack of baseline data. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA Science... [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA Science... That principle applies across Forteana. Weak evidence is not weak because it is strange. It is weak because it is hard to test. The key failure modes are familiar: Thin observation: one witness, one angle, no preserved object, no instrument record. Ambiguous classification: a report says what something was like, not what it was. No baseline: investigators do not know how often similar “ordinary” events occur in the same setting. Retrospective storytelling: later accounts become more coherent, dramatic or symbolically loaded than the first report . High explanatory leap: the observation may be real, but the proposed cause is far beyond what the evidence supports. This is why “awkward observation” and “extraordinary explanation” must be kept apart. A pilot may honestly report an unidentified object; that does not establish alien technology. A family may report a haunting; that does not establish spirits. A local paper may report frogs after a storm; that does not establish a supernatural fall. Good scepticism preserves the observation while refusing the leap. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube The meteorite lesson The history of meteorites is the classic Fortean warning against overconfident dismissal. In the eighteenth century, reports of stones falling from the sky sounded absurd to many learned observers. Ron Westrum’s study of meteorites as scientific anomalies argues that scientists judged alleged anomalous events by both prior plausibility and the credibility of reports, and that recognition of meteorites required better ways of evaluating reports, a theory to explain them and highly credible eyewitness testimony. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Open source on sagepub.com. The lesson is not that every ridiculed claim will become tomorrow’s science. Most will not. The lesson is that institutions can confuse “I do not yet have a mechanism” with “the observation cannot be real”. Before meteorites were accepted, testimony from ordinary observers could be treated as folklore or superstition. Once specimens, witnessed falls and explanatory theory aligned, the same class of report became scientifically tractable. That shift shows what Forteana can contribute without becoming anti science. It reminds institutions to keep raw reports, samples, dates, locations and contextual details even when the explanation seems unlikely. Dismissal can be rational at the level of belief while still being irresponsible at the level of record keeping. A scientist may be right not to accept a claim; an institution may still be wrong to destroy, ignore or stigmatise the data trail. Semmelweis and the risk of hindsight Ignaz Semmelweis is often used as a simple morality tale: genius discovers handwashing, arrogant doctors reject it. The real case is more useful and more uncomfortable. A 2020 article in the British Journal for the History of Science argues that one major objection to Semmelweis’s evidence was methodological: critics said that the fall in maternal mortality after chlorine hand disinfection did not by itself prove causation, because mortality from childbed fever fluctuated. The article also notes that Friedrich Wieger’s 1849 account reported a fall from more than 7 per cent in 1840–1846 to 1.27 per cent in 1848, the first full year of the intervention. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Open source on cambridge.org. This matters because institutional dismissal can be both understandable and harmful. The critics were not wrong to ask"},{"title":"Meteorites","url":"/meteorites/","description":"Meteorites show how strange reports can be rejected rationally at first, then become science when specimens, witnesses and theory align.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"Meteorites When Falling Stones Became Real Science Meteorites Meteorites When Falling Stones Became Real Science Meteorites show how strange reports can be rejected rationally at first, then become science when specimens, witnesses and theory align. Meteorites show how strange reports can be rejected rationally at first, then become science when specimens, witnesses and theory align. event time window Why falling stones once sounded impossible What changed the evidential threshold The Fortean lesson for preserving awkward reports forteana dismissal meteorites Introduction Reports of stones falling from the sky once occupied an uncomfortable space between folklore and natural philosophy. To many educated observers in the eighteenth century, such stories sounded impossible because they conflicted with prevailing ideas about the heavens. The transformation of meteorites from objects of ridicule into accepted scientific evidence is therefore one of the clearest historical examples of how apparently absurd reports can become legitimate science when observation , physical specimens and theory converge. It also illustrates an important Fortean lesson: dismissing eyewitness accounts too quickly can delay discovery, but accepting them uncritically is equally hazardous. Meteorites became accepted not because scepticism disappeared, but because scepticism was redirected towards testing evidence instead of rejecting reports outright. [American Museum of Natural History] amnh.org ernst chladni meteoritics American Museum of Natural HistoryErnst Chladni and Meteoritics: Rocks from the SkyThen in 1794, the German physicist Ernst Chladni publi... Why falling stones once sounded impossible For centuries, people across Europe and elsewhere reported fireballs followed by stones landing on the ground. Many of these accounts were preserved in local records, church chronicles and eyewitness testimony. Yet most natural philosophers regarded them as misunderstandings or superstition. The resistance was not simply prejudice. It reflected the best scientific framework available at the time. Aristotelian cosmology treated the heavens as fundamentally different from the Earth, while later Enlightenment science largely assumed that space contained planets, comets and stars, but not countless small rocky bodies capable of reaching the ground. If rocks could not exist between planets, reports of falling stones demanded terrestrial explanations instead. [American Museum of Natural History] amnh.org ernst chladni meteoritics American Museum of Natural HistoryErnst Chladni and Meteoritics: Rocks from the SkyThen in 1794, the German physicist Ernst Chladni publi... Several alternative explanations were proposed: lightning fused ordinary rocks into unusual forms; volcanic eruptions threw stones across great distances; atmospheric processes somehow created rocky masses inside clouds; witnesses had exaggerated or invented dramatic events. From the perspective of contemporary science, these explanations appeared more reasonable than the suggestion that rocks travelled through space before striking Earth. The problem was therefore one of theoretical plausibility rather than a simple refusal to examine evidence. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Meteorites in history: An overview from the Renaissance to...They dismissed reports of fallen stones or irons as tales told... Murchison Meteorite: Witnessing the fall of the most famous space rock Channel: GeoscienceAustralia Open on YouTube What changed the evidential threshold The decisive change did not come from a single spectacular event but from several different kinds of evidence reinforcing one another over roughly a decade. German physicist Ernst Chladni's 1794 treatise argued that reported fireballs, iron masses and falling stones all described the same natural phenomenon. Rather than dismissing eyewitness testimony, he compared independent reports from different countries and suggested that these objects originated in space. His proposal was widely criticised because it contradicted accepted astronomical thinking, yet it provided a coherent hypothesis that could be tested. [American Museum of Natural History] amnh.org ernst chladni meteoritics American Museum of Natural HistoryErnst Chladni and Meteoritics: Rocks from the SkyThen in 1794, the German physicist Ernst Chladni publi... The next shift came through repeated, well observed meteorite falls. Incidents such as Siena in 1794 and Wold Cottage in England in 1795 produced physical specimens witnessed by numerous observers. Instead of isolated anecdotes, investigators now had multiple events with comparable characteristics occurring in different places. [meteorite times.com] meteorite times.com Siena, Wold Cottage, L'Aigle, Soldani, Chladni, Baptiste ...1 Jan 2023 — Historians have often argued that L'Aigle in the French provinc... Laboratory work also strengthened the case. British chemist Edward Howard and French mineralogist Jacques Louis de Bournon analysed meteorite specimens and found remarkably consistent chemical compositions, including nickel rich iron that differed from ordinary terrestrial rocks. Similar materials appearing in geographically separate falls suggested they belonged to a distinct class of objects rather than local geological accidents. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Meteorite fall Meteorite fall Meanwhile, astronomical discoveries helped close the theoretical gap. The discovery of the first asteroids between 1801 and 1807 made the existence of numerous small bodies in the Solar System increasingly plausible, reducing one of the main conceptual objections to Chladni's proposal. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net ResearchGate(PDF) The meteorite fall at L'Aigle and the Biot report7 Apr 2016 — Five advances leading to the acceptance of meteorites wer... The L'Aigle investigation that changed minds The event most often identified as the turning point occurred near L'Aigle, France, on 26 April 1803. Thousands of stones fell over a broad area in daylight, witnessed by large numbers of people from different communities. The sheer scale of the event made simple dismissal increasingly difficult. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia L'Aigle (meteorite April 26, 2026 — Ernst Chladni had theorised and published a book in 1794 saying that meteorites originated beyond Earth. Although some o... Published: April 26, 2026 The French Academy of Sciences sent Jean Baptiste Biot to investigate. His importance lay less in discovering something entirely new than in demonstrating how extraordinary claims could be investigated systematically. Biot's inquiry combined several independent lines of evidence: interviews with numerous witnesses whose accounts could be compared; mapping of the distribution of recovered stones across the landscape; examination of specimens that differed from local geology; comparison between physical evidence and reported fireball observations. Rather than relying on dramatic testimony alone, he treated the event as a field investigation. The convergence of independent witnesses, recoverable objects and geographical patterning produced a standard of evidence that many sceptical scientists found persuasive. His report became a landmark in the acceptance of meteorites as genuine extraterrestrial objects. [Wikipedia+2Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jean Baptiste Biot Jean Baptiste Biot Importantly, no single witness convinced science. No single stone did either. Acceptance emerged because multiple forms of evidence pointed towards the same explanation. This Space Rock is 4.5 Billion Years Old. Here's Its Secret... Channel: American Museum of Natural History Open on YouTube The cost of dismissing awkward reports From a Fortean perspective, meteorites demonstrate a subtle form of scientific failure. The principal mistake was not that scientists demanded evidence. The mistake was allowing theoretical expectations to reduce the credibility of observations before adequate investigation had occurred. Had every report of falling stones simply been ignored, researchers would have lost opportunities to collect fresh specimens, compare independent cases and recognise recurring patterns. The delay in accepting meteorites illustrates how valuable observations can remain invisible when existing theories define them as impossible in advance. [Lyell Collection] lyellcollection.org Lyell CollectionMeteorites in history: an overview from the Renaissance to...The decade between 1794 and 1804 witnessed a dramatic advan... At the same time, the history also warns against romanticising rejected claims. Many extraordinary reports throughout history have indeed proved mistaken, exaggerated or fraudulent. Meteorites succeeded because the evidence became progressively stronger through repeatability, physical recovery, chemical analysis and careful documentation—not because disbelief itself became evidence of truth. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Meteorite fall Meteorite fall The Road to Recognition Channel: UCLA"},{"title":"Scepticism","url":"/scepticism-b4393c/","description":"Healthy scepticism tests claims through shared standards, while social dismissal rejects reports because they feel embarrassing or disreputable.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"Scepticism When Scepticism Becomes Dismissal Scepticism Scepticism When Scepticism Becomes Dismissal Healthy scepticism tests claims through shared standards, while social dismissal rejects reports because they feel embarrassing or disreputable. Healthy scepticism tests claims through shared standards, while social dismissal rejects reports because they feel embarrassing or disreputable. mechanism What organised scepticism is supposed to do How embarrassment becomes an evidence filter Questions that keep rejection accountable forteana dismissal scepticism Introduction Organised scepticism is one of science's central safeguards. It requires claims to be tested against shared standards of evidence, transparent methods and open criticism before they are accepted. Social dismissal is something different. It rejects or ignores reports because they are associated with embarrassment, low status, ridicule or reputational risk rather than because they have failed careful examination. Within Fortean discussions, this distinction matters because the central question is not whether unusual claims are true, but whether they are being evaluated by scientific criteria or by social instinct. Science depends on filtering weak evidence, yet those filters can drift from methodological judgement towards cultural habit. A report may be discarded because it resembles folklore , paranormal belief or conspiracy culture before anyone asks what was actually observed. Understanding this mechanism helps explain why debates over anomalous observations often become arguments about reputation as much as evidence. [bitss.org] bitss.org Merton's norms and the Scientific EthosMerton discussed four idealized norms: Universalism, Communality, Disinterestedness, and Organized... What organised scepticism is supposed to do The sociologist Robert K. Merton described organised scepticism as one of the norms supporting scientific inquiry. The principle does not require disbelief. Instead, it requires the temporary suspension of judgement while claims are examined through public, critical and repeatable methods. Every claim, whether ordinary or extraordinary, is supposed to face the same standards rather than being accepted or rejected because of who presents it or how surprising it sounds. [bitss.org+2Wikipedia] bitss.org Merton's norms and the Scientific EthosMerton discussed four idealized norms: Universalism, Communality, Disinterestedness, and Organized... In practice, organised scepticism asks questions such as: What exactly was observed? How reliable is the observation ? What alternative explanations have been tested? Can the evidence be independently examined? What evidence would change the current assessment? These questions are procedural rather than ideological. They do not assume that an unusual claim is correct, nor do they assume that it must be mistaken. Their purpose is to separate uncertainty from error. This distinction is especially important in areas associated with Forteana because many reports are genuinely ambiguous. A witness may honestly describe an event while remaining mistaken about its cause. Organised scepticism protects against both premature belief and premature rejection by insisting that explanation follows evidence rather than preceding it. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube How embarrassment becomes an evidence filter Social dismissal emerges when the topic itself begins influencing judgement before the evidence is considered. Instead of asking whether a report has been properly investigated, observers ask whether it belongs to a socially respectable category. Several mechanisms contribute to this shift. Reputational risk. Researchers, officials or witnesses may avoid discussing certain subjects because they fear professional damage more than factual error. The result is fewer reports, fewer investigations and less reliable data. Category contamination. Once a subject becomes associated with sensational claims, fraudulent cases or popular entertainment, even stronger evidence may inherit that negative reputation. New observations become judged partly by their association with previous cultural stereotypes. Ridicule as quality control. Humour and mockery can function as informal gatekeepers. Although sceptical criticism is valuable, ridicule can discourage careful reporting before any evaluation occurs. Potential witnesses may decide that remaining silent is safer than risking public embarrassment. Availability bias. Dramatic hoaxes and mistaken identifications receive disproportionate attention, making all similar reports appear equally unreliable even when individual cases differ in quality. None of these mechanisms demonstrates that extraordinary claims are true. Instead, they explain how evidence may become selectively absent because people anticipate social costs before scientific assessment begins. A contemporary example: reducing stigma without lowering standards The distinction between scepticism and dismissal appears clearly in NASA's 2023 independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP). The report concluded that there is no convincing evidence that observed UAP represent extraterrestrial technology. At the same time, it argued that stigma surrounding reporting has reduced both the quantity and quality of available observations. Rather than recommending greater belief, the study recommended better science: calibrated sensors, improved metadata, standardised reporting, transparent analysis and the removal of unnecessary ridicule that discourages data collection. Its position illustrates organised scepticism in practice. Better evidence—not greater enthusiasm—is presented as the solution. [NASA Science+2NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... This approach is notable because it rejects two opposite errors simultaneously. It rejects unsupported extraordinary conclusions while also rejecting the assumption that socially awkward reports should simply disappear from consideration. Why dismissal can weaken science Scientific institutions cannot investigate every unusual observation with equal effort. Resources are limited, and most anomalous reports ultimately receive conventional explanations. Filtering is therefore unavoidable. The problem arises when the filtering process itself becomes socially rather than evidentially driven. Several consequences may follow: Potential witnesses become less willing to report unusual observations. Researchers avoid controversial topics regardless of data quality. Poor reporting systems produce poorer evidence, reinforcing the belief that the subject lacks reliable evidence. Legitimate anomalies that could reveal instrument failures, new natural phenomena or unexpected observational effects remain under documented. This creates a feedback loop. Weak evidence justifies neglect, while neglect prevents stronger evidence from being gathered. The absence of evidence may partly reflect an absence of systematic observation rather than the complete absence of unusual events. Recognising this possibility does not imply that hidden discoveries are waiting to be confirmed. It simply highlights that institutional behaviour can influence the quality of available evidence. Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Questions that keep rejection accountable Organised scepticism remains accountable when rejection itself can be examined. Instead of treating dismissal as self evidently justified, it asks whether the investigative process has been fair. Useful questions include: Was the claim evaluated using explicit evidential criteria? Would the same evidence receive similar treatment in a less controversial field? Were alternative explanations actually investigated rather than merely assumed? Was the witness criticised personally instead of assessing the observation? Is the conclusion based on available evidence or on expectations about what \"cannot\" happen? These questions do not lower scientific standards. Instead, they test whether those standards have been applied consistently. The Fortean lesson One enduring contribution of Fortean thought is the insistence that anomalies deserve careful classification before they deserve explanation. Most unusual reports will prove mistaken, incomplete or mundane. Some may reveal gaps in observation, instrumentation or existing understanding. The challenge is distinguishing between these outcomes without allowing social discomfort to become a substitute for investigation. Healthy scepticism is therefore neither belief nor disbelief. It is a disciplined willingness to ask difficult questions"},{"title":"Semmelweis","url":"/semmelweis/","description":"Semmelweis shows why practical stakes can justify provisional action before a theory has won full scientific acceptance.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"Semmelweis When Doubt Becomes Too Dangerous Semmelweis Semmelweis When Doubt Becomes Too Dangerous Semmelweis shows why practical stakes can justify provisional action before a theory has won full scientific acceptance. Semmelweis shows why practical stakes can justify provisional action before a theory has won full scientific acceptance. entity What Semmelweis's mortality evidence showed Why critics questioned causation How risk changes the threshold for action forteana dismissal semmelweis Introduction The story of Ignaz Semmelweis is often reduced to a simple morality tale: a brilliant doctor was ignored, and history eventually proved him right. The reality is more instructive. Semmelweis did not possess a complete scientific theory of infection, nor could he explain precisely why his intervention worked. What he did have was unusually strong practical evidence that a simple change in behaviour dramatically reduced maternal deaths. His case illustrates a crucial principle for evaluating controversial claims: when the human cost of inaction is high and a proposed intervention is low risk, the threshold for taking provisional action may be lower than the threshold for accepting a complete scientific explanation. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCIgnaz Phillip Semmelweis' studies of death in childbirth by I Loudon · 2013 · Cited by 71 — The maternal mortality rate in the first clinic was 98.4 per 1000 births, while the rate in the sec... Within the broader history of scientific dismissal , Semmelweis is therefore important not because he proves that rejected ideas are usually correct—they are not—but because he demonstrates that evidence of benefit sometimes deserves action before a settled theory exists. Ignaz Semmelweis My Favourite Scientist Channel: FavScientist Open on YouTube What Semmelweis's mortality evidence showed When Semmelweis became assistant physician at the Vienna General Hospital in 1846, he confronted a striking puzzle. Women admitted to the First Obstetrical Clinic, staffed by doctors and medical students, died from puerperal (childbed) fever at far higher rates than women treated in the neighbouring Second Clinic, where midwives trained. The patients were allocated between the clinics by schedule rather than medical condition, creating an unusually informative comparison. Historical analyses estimate mortality in the doctors' clinic at around 9–10%, compared with roughly 3–4% in the midwives' clinic over several preceding years. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCIgnaz Phillip Semmelweis' studies of death in childbirth by I Loudon · 2013 · Cited by 71 — The maternal mortality rate in the first clinic was 98.4 per 1000 births, while the rate in the sec... Semmelweis searched systematically for explanations. Differences in climate, overcrowding, birth position and religious practices failed to account for the gap. The turning point came after the death of his colleague Jakob Kolletschka, who developed a fatal illness after being accidentally cut during an autopsy. Semmelweis recognised similarities between Kolletschka's post mortem findings and those of women dying from childbed fever. He concluded that medical students were carrying infectious material from cadavers directly to labouring mothers on their hands. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCIgnaz Phillip Semmelweis' studies of death in childbirth by I Loudon · 2013 · Cited by 71 — The maternal mortality rate in the first clinic was 98.4 per 1000 births, while the rate in the sec... In May 1847 he required everyone entering the First Clinic after autopsy work to scrub their hands with a chlorinated lime solution. The effect was immediate and dramatic. Mortality in the doctors' clinic fell to levels comparable with those in the midwives' clinic, dropping from the previously elevated rates to around 1–2% during the following year. Later historical reviews have described this as one of medicine's earliest large scale natural experiments demonstrating the effectiveness of infection control. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCIgnaz Phillip Semmelweis' studies of death in childbirth by I Loudon · 2013 · Cited by 71 — The maternal mortality rate in the first clinic was 98.4 per 1000 births, while the rate in the sec... The important point is that Semmelweis's evidence was primarily empirical rather than theoretical. He had observed a reproducible association, introduced a targeted intervention and documented a substantial reduction in deaths. Ep2 Ignaz Semmelweis and the Fight to Save Mothers Channel: Let's Learn Public Health Open on YouTube Why critics questioned causation Modern readers sometimes assume that Semmelweis's contemporaries rejected overwhelming evidence out of simple prejudice. The historical record is more complicated. The first difficulty was conceptual. Germ theory had not yet been developed. Most physicians explained epidemics through miasma, constitutional weakness or multiple interacting causes. Semmelweis's idea that invisible material transferred by physicians could produce fatal disease lacked an accepted biological mechanism. His own explanation in terms of \"cadaverous particles\" proved incomplete because puerperal fever could also spread from living infected patients, not only from corpses. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Ignaz Phillip Semmelweis: The Unrecognized Pioneer of...by NR Dash · 2024 · Cited by 2 — In the era of “miasma theory,” Ignaz Semmelweis... A second objection concerned scientific inference rather than ideology alone. Historians examining contemporary documents have shown that some critics argued the evidence demonstrated only that mortality fell after handwashing was introduced, not necessarily because of it. In modern terminology, they worried about confusing correlation with causation—a concern that is ordinarily legitimate in scientific reasoning. The problem was that, in this instance, they gave insufficient weight to the magnitude, consistency and practical consequences of the observed change. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] cambridge.org Cambridge University Press & AssessmentWhy Semmelweis's doctrine was rejected: evidence from...by N KADAR · 2020 · Cited by 7 — The main... Professional and social factors reinforced these intellectual objections: Accepting Semmelweis's conclusion implied that physicians themselves had been unintentionally killing patients. Medical authority was invested in existing theories of disease. Semmelweis delayed publishing a full account of his work, limiting wider scrutiny of his data. His increasingly combative style alienated potential supporters, even among colleagues sympathetic to his observations. [PMC+2Cambridge University Press & Assessment] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Ignaz Phillip Semmelweis: The Unrecognized Pioneer of...by NR Dash · 2024 · Cited by 2 — In the era of “miasma theory,” Ignaz Semmelweis... These factors interacted. Scientific uncertainty, professional identity and communication failures all contributed to the slow acceptance of his findings. How risk changes the threshold for action Semmelweis's story has become influential because it highlights an important distinction between accepting a theory and adopting a precaution. A scientific theory normally demands strong evidence, independent replication and an explanation consistent with broader knowledge. A practical intervention can sometimes justify a different standard when three conditions are met: The observed benefit is large. The reduction in maternal mortality was not subtle but dramatic. The proposed intervention carries little cost or danger. Handwashing with disinfectant imposed inconvenience rather than substantial risk. The consequences of delay are severe. Continuing existing practice meant preventable deaths would almost certainly continue. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCIgnaz Phillip Semmelweis' studies of death in childbirth by I Loudon · 2013 · Cited by 71 — The maternal mortality rate in the first clinic was 98.4 per 1000 births, while the rate in the sec... This does not mean every surprising claim deserves immediate implementation. Many proposed interventions later prove ineffective or harmful. The lesson is narrower: decisions about action should consider expected harm as well as scientific certainty. Where the intervention is inexpensive, reversible and plausibly beneficial, waiting for perfect theoretical understanding may itself become the riskier choice. Semmelweis therefore occupies an unusual place in the history of scientific scepticism. His case is not an argument against demanding evidence. Rather, it shows that evidence relevant to action is not always identical to evidence sufficient for complete theoretical acceptance. Why We Reject New Evidence That Could Directly Help Us: The Semmelweis Effect Channel: PsyberSpace® Podcast Open on YouTube What the case contributes to scientific dismissal Within discussions of awkward observations and Fortean themes, Semmelweis is valuable precisely because he"},{"title":"Sensor Gaps","url":"/sensor-gaps/","description":"Unidentified events often stay unresolved because the available record lacks calibration, metadata, multiple angles or baseline comparisons.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"Sensor Gaps Why Better Data Changes Strange Cases Sensor Gaps Sensor Gaps Why Better Data Changes Strange Cases Unidentified events often stay unresolved because the available record lacks calibration, metadata, multiple angles or baseline comparisons. Unidentified events often stay unresolved because the available record lacks calibration, metadata, multiple angles or baseline comparisons. dataset evidence What missing metadata hides Why single angle records mislead How baseline data makes anomalies testable forteana dismissal sensor gaps Introduction Many reports of strange aerial or environmental phenomena remain unresolved for a mundane reason: the available data are not good enough to determine what happened. In modern anomaly research, the decisive question is often not whether a witness was honest, but whether the observation can be reconstructed from reliable measurements. A single dramatic image, an isolated radar return or a brief eyewitness account may be intriguing, yet still fail to support a firm conclusion because essential information is missing. NASA's 2023 independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) argued that the greatest obstacle to scientific progress is not necessarily the rarity of unusual events, but poor quality observations lacking calibration, metadata, multiple sensor perspectives and baseline comparisons. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... Within Forteana , this shifts the discussion away from belief versus disbelief. The more practical issue is whether a reported anomaly leaves behind a data trail that allows competing explanations to be tested rather than merely debated. What Missing Metadata Hides A photograph or video rarely speaks for itself. Every sensor records an image within a context, and that context is often more important than the image. Metadata typically includes information such as: The exact time and location of the observation. Camera model, lens, zoom level and exposure settings. Whether image stabilisation or digital enhancement was active. Sensor orientation and pointing direction. Weather conditions and atmospheric visibility. Observer movement and platform speed. Without these details, investigators cannot reliably estimate distance, velocity or object size. A bright point recorded by a mobile phone, for example, may appear to accelerate dramatically simply because the camera is moving or continually refocusing. NASA's study specifically identified the lack of sensor metadata as one of the primary reasons many reports remain scientifically inconclusive. The report argues that collecting standardised contextual information is as important as collecting the imagery itself because it allows independent researchers to reproduce measurements and eliminate ordinary explanations . [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... This is one reason apparently spectacular footage can remain permanently unresolved. The recording survives, but the information needed to interpret it has already been lost. REPLAY! NASA Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Update Channel: The Launch Pad · Views: 6.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why Single Angle Records Mislead One camera records only a two dimensional projection of a three dimensional event. Without another viewpoint, investigators cannot directly measure: Distance. True speed. Physical size. Altitude. Direction of travel. This creates opportunities for ordinary optical effects to imitate extraordinary motion. A familiar example is parallax. When an observer or aircraft moves, nearby objects shift across the background much faster than distant ones. Without independent distance measurements, an object can appear to move at remarkable speed even if it is stationary or travelling slowly. Computer vision and photogrammetry routinely exploit multiple viewpoints precisely because one viewpoint alone cannot reliably recover three dimensional geometry. [Milvus] milvus.io What is the parallax effect in the computer vision?The parallax effect in computer vision refers to the apparent shift in the posit... Likewise, a single infrared image may show an unusual heat signature without revealing whether the apparent target is a distant aircraft, atmospheric reflection or instrument artefact. Radar, optical imaging and infrared each observe different physical properties. Agreement between them is generally much more informative than any one sensor operating alone. NASA therefore recommends combining optical imagery, infrared observations, radar and other independent measurements whenever possible. In practice, relatively few reported anomalies contain this complete set of evidence. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... How Baseline Data Makes Anomalies Testable An anomaly can only be recognised against an established baseline. Baseline data answers questions such as: What normally appears in this location? How frequently do known aircraft pass through? What atmospheric conditions are typical? How does the sensor behave when recording ordinary targets? What kinds of false positives occur regularly? Without this reference frame, unusual observations cannot easily be distinguished from uncommon but expected events. NASA highlighted the lack of baseline data alongside poor calibration and missing metadata. A camera that occasionally produces lens flare or electronic noise may generate images that appear mysterious until investigators compare them with many ordinary recordings made under similar conditions. Likewise, atmospheric conditions such as temperature inversions, reflections or ice crystals may create recurring visual effects that seem exceptional only because there is no systematic comparison set. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... This principle is common throughout science. A laboratory identifies anomalies only after characterising normal instrument behaviour. Fortean investigations increasingly apply the same logic to unusual sightings. Why Better Sensors Do Not Automatically Produce Better Answers Modern equipment has become dramatically more capable, yet ambiguity often remains because measurements are collected opportunistically rather than through controlled observation. Many reported sightings originate from systems designed for completely different purposes: Aircraft targeting sensors. Security cameras. Mobile phones. Dashcams. Wildlife cameras. Consumer drones. These instruments are optimised for their intended tasks, not for measuring unidentified objects under controlled scientific conditions. NASA observed that many UAP detections are effectively accidental, captured by sensors that were never designed or calibrated for anomaly investigation. Consequently, even sophisticated military or civilian equipment may provide incomplete evidence if critical contextual information was not recorded alongside the imagery. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... Better technology therefore improves investigations only when accompanied by systematic data collection protocols. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why Some Cases Stay Officially \"Unresolved\" In official investigations, \"unresolved\" does not necessarily imply that an event is inexplicable. It often means one of three things: The available evidence is insufficient to distinguish between several ordinary explanations. Key measurements are missing and cannot be recovered. Existing data are internally inconsistent or too poor in quality for rigorous analysis. This distinction matters because an unresolved classification reflects evidential limits rather than confirmation of an extraordinary phenomenon. The U.S. All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has repeatedly stated that many unresolved reports lack sufficient technical information for definitive assessment. Its public reports argue that additional, higher quality sensor data would likely allow many currently unidentified cases to be attributed to ordinary objects or known physical phenomena. [AARO+2U.S. Department of War] aaro.mil The US Defense Department & The UAP MissionMany cases in AARO's holdings remain unresolved because of a lack of verifiable data. Case... From a Fortean"},{"title":"UAP Stigma","url":"/uap-stigma/","description":"NASA's UAP work reframes unidentified sightings as a data problem shaped by poor sensors, missing metadata and witness stigma.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"UAP Stigma Can UAP Reports Be Taken Seriously? UAP Stigma UAP Stigma Can UAP Reports Be Taken Seriously? NASA's UAP work reframes unidentified sightings as a data problem shaped by poor sensors, missing metadata and witness stigma. NASA's UAP work reframes unidentified sightings as a data problem shaped by poor sensors, missing metadata and witness stigma. policy intervention Why ridicule damages reporting quality What calibrated sensors can add How to separate witnesses from explanations forteana dismissal uap stigma Introduction NASA's work on unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) represents an important shift in how unusual aerial reports are framed. Rather than treating every unexplained sighting as evidence of something extraordinary, or dismissing all reports as mistakes , the agency argues that the central problem is scientific: there is too little reliable data. The quality of evidence is weakened by uncalibrated sensors, incomplete metadata, inconsistent reporting and, crucially, a longstanding stigma that discourages people from documenting what they observe. This reframing is significant within the broader Fortean debate because it separates the act of reporting from the interpretation of the event. A witness may have seen something genuinely unidentified without that implying an exotic explanation. NASA's proposed remedy is therefore not belief, but better governance of observation through standardised reporting, improved instrumentation and a scientific culture that distinguishes curiosity from credulity. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... Why ridicule damages reporting quality One of the strongest conclusions of NASA's 2023 Independent Study Team is that social attitudes can become a scientific obstacle. The report argues that many potential witnesses, particularly pilots and other professionals, hesitate to report unusual observations because they fear ridicule, damage to their careers or association with sensational UFO claims. This reluctance creates a selection problem: potentially useful observations never enter the record, while the reports that do emerge often arrive years later with diminished evidential value. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... This is an important distinction. NASA does not claim that witnesses are necessarily correct about what they observed. Instead, it argues that suppressing reports prevents investigators from collecting the information needed to determine ordinary explanations . Scientific inquiry depends upon observations being recorded before they are interpreted. The agency therefore recommends treating UAP reporting more like aviation safety reporting than paranormal testimony. In aviation, near misses, equipment failures and unexpected events are valuable precisely because they reveal gaps in knowledge. A similar reporting culture for unidentified observations would increase the amount of usable information without assuming any particular cause. Media coverage of the report highlighted this recommendation, noting NASA's emphasis on reducing the \"tin foil hat\" stereotype attached to unexplained aerial sightings. [NASA Science+2Sky News] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube What calibrated sensors can add NASA's report repeatedly stresses that most current UAP evidence is scientifically weak not because it is mysterious, but because it was never collected for scientific investigation. Many reports rely upon: cameras that were not calibrated for precise measurement; single viewpoints with no independent confirmation; missing information about camera settings, location or timing; absent environmental data; incomplete archiving of the original recordings. Without these details, investigators often cannot distinguish between a genuine external object, an optical illusion, an atmospheric effect or a sensor artefact. The report therefore frames unidentified phenomena primarily as a data quality problem rather than an anomaly problem. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... NASA recommends using multiple well calibrated sensors whenever possible. Combining optical imagery with infrared observations, radar, satellite information and accurate metadata allows investigators to cross check competing explanations instead of relying upon a single ambiguous video. The report also points out that careful analysis has already shown some apparent UAP to be artefacts created by sensor limitations or processing effects. Better calibration does not merely help discover unusual events—it also helps eliminate false anomalies more quickly, improving confidence in the cases that remain unexplained. [Wikisource] en.wikisource.org Responses to Statement of Task NASA Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Independent...14 Oct 2023 — Indeed, several apparent UAP have been demonstrated to be s... REPLAY! NASA Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Update Channel: The Launch Pad · Views: 6.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube How to separate witnesses from explanations Perhaps the report's most useful conceptual contribution is its insistence that observation and explanation should be evaluated independently. A witness can be: sincere; experienced; careful; still mistaken about the cause of what they observed. Conversely, a report may deserve careful investigation even if its eventual explanation proves entirely ordinary. This distinction addresses a common weakness in public discussion. UFO debates frequently collapse into two opposing assumptions: if the witness appears credible, the extraordinary explanation must also be credible; if the explanation is ordinary, the witness must have been dishonest or foolish. NASA rejects both positions. Instead, it recommends preserving observations while remaining agnostic about their interpretation until sufficient evidence is available. This approach allows investigators to respect witnesses without granting evidential weight to conclusions that exceed the available data. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... For Fortean questions, this distinction is especially important. Many historical controversies have become polarised because criticism of an explanation was perceived as criticism of the observer. NASA's framework attempts to separate those issues. A governance problem rather than a belief problem Within the wider discussion of scientific dismissal , NASA's report treats stigma as an institutional design problem rather than a psychological curiosity. Its recommendations include: normalised reporting channels; standardised data collection; transparent evaluation methods; use of artificial intelligence and machine learning tools to identify conventional objects before highlighting genuine anomalies; collaboration across civilian, commercial and government data sources. The objective is not to prove extraordinary claims but to improve the quality of evidence entering scientific analysis. NASA explicitly states that its study found no evidence that reported UAP are extraterrestrial in origin. Equally, it argues that poor evidence should not be confused with evidence that nothing unusual was observed. Unknown simply means that the available information is insufficient for confident identification. [NASA Science+2NASA] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... NASA discusses findings from UFO study full video Channel: CBS News · Views: 34.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why this matters for Forteana Charles Fort criticised institutions for allowing social assumptions to determine which observations deserved attention. NASA's UAP study does not embrace Fort's conclusions, but it does acknowledge a related institutional risk: ridicule can distort the evidence base before scientific analysis even begins. Its proposed solution remains firmly within mainstream scientific practice. Instead of lowering evidential standards, NASA argues for raising them through better reporting systems, calibrated instruments, richer metadata and transparent evaluation. In that"},{"title":"Witnesses","url":"/witnesses-7c648d/","description":"Honest witnesses can preserve valuable clues, but memory, distance, speed and context can still distort what was seen.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Dismissal","level":3,"text":"Witnesses Why Sincere Witnesses Can Still Be Wrong Witnesses Witnesses Why Sincere Witnesses Can Still Be Wrong Honest witnesses can preserve valuable clues, but memory, distance, speed and context can still distort what was seen. Honest witnesses can preserve valuable clues, but memory, distance, speed and context can still distort what was seen. population context What eyewitness reports can capture well Where perception and memory fail How investigators can record without endorsing forteana dismissal witnesses Introduction Many anomalous claims begin with someone reporting something they genuinely believe they saw. Within Forteana, that simple fact is often overlooked in two opposite ways: sceptics may dismiss witnesses because the claim sounds unusual, while believers may treat sincerity as proof that an extraordinary event occurred. Neither conclusion follows. A person can be completely honest and still misperceive distance, speed, scale, lighting or sequence, while an unusual observation can also contain accurate details worth preserving. The challenge is not deciding whether a witness is truthful or deceptive, but separating the reliability of the observation from the interpretation placed upon it. Modern psychology , investigative practice and recent guidance on unidentified anomalous phenomena all support the same principle: careful documentation should come before confident explanation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... What eyewitness reports can capture well Eyewitnesses remain indispensable because they often provide the first record of unexpected events. Before instruments arrive or physical traces are collected, human observation may be the only available evidence. Even when witnesses disagree about interpretation, they frequently preserve useful information such as: The approximate time and location of an event. The sequence in which visible changes occurred. Weather , lighting and environmental conditions. The number of objects or people observed. Immediate reactions before rumours or media coverage spread. For investigators of unusual aerial sightings, mysterious lights or other Fortean reports, these descriptive details are often more valuable than a witness's explanation of what they believe they saw. A report that carefully distinguishes between \"I observed a bright object changing direction\" and \"I saw an alien craft\" preserves evidence while avoiding premature conclusions. NASA's independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena explicitly recommends systematic reporting, improved observational data and reduced stigma surrounding reporting. The report argues that collecting observations consistently is more productive than dismissing witnesses or accepting extraordinary interpretations without supporting evidence. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... Where perception and memory fail The limitations of eyewitness testimony are not signs of dishonesty. They arise because human perception was never designed to function as a precision recording device. How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube Perception during the event Observers rarely experience ideal viewing conditions. Distance compresses depth, darkness removes visual cues, atmospheric effects alter apparent motion, and unexpected events unfold quickly. A bright light against a dark sky provides few reference points, making size, speed and altitude particularly difficult to judge. Stress can also narrow attention. In criminal psychology this is illustrated by the well studied \"weapon focus\" effect, where attention becomes concentrated on a threatening object at the expense of surrounding details. Although anomalous sightings differ greatly from crimes, the broader principle remains relevant: surprising events often reduce rather than improve overall observational accuracy. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness identification Eyewitness identification Memory after the event Remembering is an active reconstruction rather than a replay of stored footage. Every recollection can be influenced by later conversations, media reports, repeated questioning or new assumptions. Research led by psychologists such as Elizabeth Loftus has repeatedly demonstrated the misinformation effect: subtle wording changes or misleading information introduced after an event can alter later recollections. People may become highly confident in details that were never originally observed. Confidence therefore cannot be treated as a direct measure of accuracy. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness testimony Eyewitness testimony Memory can also become contaminated through social processes. Witnesses who discuss events together often converge on shared narratives, even when individual memories initially differed. More recently, studies have shown that searching social media after an event may unintentionally introduce new faces, images or assumptions into memory, making later identification less reliable. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net The Contaminating Effect of Social Media on Eyewitness...Social media searches can alter eyewitness memory and reduce the re... Why sincere witnesses often reach different conclusions Two honest observers may report incompatible accounts because they experienced different perspectives rather than because one is lying. Several factors contribute: Viewing angle: Objects hidden from one observer may be visible to another. Reference points: An aircraft viewed against stars appears differently from one viewed against hills or buildings. Expectation: Existing beliefs shape interpretation more readily than raw perception. Duration: A five second glimpse rarely supports detailed reconstruction. Retelling: Each retelling encourages memories to become organised into a coherent narrative, sometimes replacing uncertainty with apparent certainty. This distinction is particularly important in Fortean investigations. The descriptive portions of two reports may agree closely while their explanations differ dramatically. One witness may describe a structured craft, another an atmospheric phenomenon, and a third an experimental aircraft, despite all observing the same stimulus. Recognising this difference allows investigators to compare observations without forcing agreement about interpretation. How Reliable is Eyewitness Testimony? Channel: National Science Foundation News · Views: 173.1K · Uploaded: July 2015 · Length: 4 minutes Open on YouTube How investigators can record without endorsing Good investigation neither validates extraordinary claims automatically nor discards them because they sound unusual. Instead, it attempts to preserve the original observation before later influences reshape it. Established interviewing methods encourage investigators to: Record testimony as soon as practical. Ask open ended rather than leading questions. Separate direct observations from interpretations. Document uncertainty instead of forcing definite answers. Keep independent witnesses separate until statements have been recorded. Preserve environmental information such as weather, lighting, timing and viewing position. The cognitive interview, developed from psychological research on eyewitness memory, aims to maximise accurate recall while reducing interviewer influence. Rather than supplying possible explanations, investigators encourage witnesses to reconstruct the original context and describe events in their own words. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Cognitive interview Cognitive interview Within Forteana , these practices are especially valuable because many reports concern singular, unrepeatable events. Once memory becomes contaminated, the original observation may never be recoverable. Why witness testimony still matters The weaknesses of eyewitness evidence do not make it worthless. Scientific history contains many examples where careful observations preceded satisfactory explanations. Early reports of unusual natural phenomena, rare atmospheric optics and previously unknown astronomical events were initially based on human observation before instruments provided confirmation. The appropriate lesson is therefore balanced. Witness testimony alone rarely establishes an extraordinary claim, but it frequently identifies events deserving further investigation. A sincere witness contributes a data point rather than a verdict. This balanced position reflects both modern cognitive psychology and contemporary approaches to anomalous reports. Honest observation deserves respectful documentation, yet every account remains subject to the ordinary limits of human perception and memory. Those limits do not prove that an anomalous event"},{"title":"Scepticism","url":"/scepticism/","description":"The best Fortean reading tests reports seriously without turning every unexplained story into belief.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Scepticism How to Be Sceptical Without Being Dismissive Scepticism Scepticism How to Be Sceptical Without Being Dismissive The best Fortean reading tests reports seriously without turning every unexplained story into belief. The best Fortean reading tests reports seriously without turning every unexplained story into belief. policy intervention Suspended belief as a method Questions before conclusions Unresolved versus paranormal forteana scepticism Introduction Scepticism without dismissal is the working discipline that lets Forteana be examined seriously without turning every odd report into a belief claim. It means neither “accept the paranormal until disproved” nor “laugh the witness out of the room”. A Fortean report is usually a mixture of event, memory, context, interpretation and later retelling. The sceptical task is to separate those layers carefully: what was actually reported, what evidence survives, what ordinary explanations fit, and what remains genuinely unresolved. That balance matters because premature belief turns anomalies into folklore dressed as fact, while premature dismissal can destroy useful testimony, discourage future reporting and miss the social or scientific reason a case became strange in the first place. Modern anomalistics frames this as a method of inquiry: anomalous claims are assessed with scientific caution, but the existence of an unexplained residue is not treated as proof of the paranormal. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... Suspended belief is a method, not a mood The most useful Fortean stance is temporary non belief: hold the story open long enough to investigate it, but do not promote it into a conclusion before the evidence earns that move. This is close to the “zetetic” approach associated with sociologist Marcello Truzzi, who argued that real scepticism begins with doubt before inquiry, not with a negative verdict announced in advance. In that sense, scepticism without dismissal is less a personality trait than an operating rule: keep curiosity active, keep standards high, and avoid confusing “not yet explained” with “therefore paranormal”. [newdualism.org] newdualism.org Truzzi on Scientific UnorthodoxyTrue skepticism which is a part of science consists of doubt preceeding inquiry, and that essentially tak... This matters especially in Forteana because many cases begin as sincere reports rather than formal claims. A person may report a light, apparition, animal, fall of objects, strange sound or coincidence without claiming to know what caused it. If the investigator immediately treats the witness as gullible, deceptive or irrational, the case is no longer being examined; it is being socially managed. But if the investigator treats sincerity as proof, the opposite error appears: honest perception is mistaken for accurate description of external reality. A practical Fortean reading therefore separates three questions that are often blurred together: Did someone really report this? This concerns documentation, provenance, witnesses, dates and whether later versions have inflated the account. Did the reported experience happen as described? This concerns perception, memory, environment, instrumentation and independent corroboration. What caused it? This is the final question, not the first one. That order is crucial. A haunting report may be genuine as an experience while mistaken as a ghost. A UFO report may be sincere while caused by aircraft, balloons, birds, atmospheric effects, camera artefacts or insufficient sensor data. A cryptid sighting may preserve valuable local testimony while still failing to establish an unknown animal. Scepticism without dismissal protects the report from ridicule while protecting the conclusion from overreach. Questions before conclusions The first intervention in a Fortean case is not an explanation but a better set of questions. Forteana attracts claims that cross domains, so the question set must cross domains too: astronomy for aerial lights, zoology for unknown animals, psychology for apparitions, meteorology for strange falls, folklore for recurring motifs, and media history for stories that grow through repetition. The Society for Psychical Research’s overview of anomalistics stresses that anomalous cases are often multidisciplinary because it is not obvious at the start which discipline will resolve them. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Anomalistics Psi EncyclopediaAnomalistics Psi Encyclopedia13 Apr 2015 — Anomalistics is generally governed by the Mertonian norms of science: commun... Good sceptical questioning begins with the least dramatic facts. What time was it? Where exactly was the witness standing? What direction were they facing? How long did the event last? Was there weather, traffic, aircraft activity, wildlife, machinery, a festival, military activity, a known astronomical object, or a prior expectation shaping what was noticed? Were there photographs, logs, radar, call records, medical notes, local news reports or other witnesses? How soon after the event was the account recorded? The point is not to bury wonder under bureaucracy. It is to prevent a common Fortean failure: letting the most exciting interpretation organise the evidence. Once a case is labelled “ghost”, “monster” or “alien craft”, later readers tend to filter details through that label. A better method keeps several explanations alive at once and lets each one make predictions. A balloon explanation should fit wind, altitude, appearance and duration. A sleep related explanation should fit timing, paralysis, fear, bedroom setting and dreamlike presence. A hoax explanation should fit motive, opportunity and trace evidence. A genuinely unresolved classification should fit the absence of adequate data, not the investigator’s impatience. This is where “extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence” is useful but easily misused. The maxim, popularised by Carl Sagan and developed from earlier formulations associated with Laplace and Truzzi, reminds readers that claims with major implications need stronger support than ordinary claims. But as a method, it should mean proportionate evidence, not an excuse to ignore reports before inquiry begins. Philosophers and scientists have criticised the phrase when “extraordinary” becomes subjective or when the standard is applied so vaguely that no unusual claim could ever receive a fair hearing. [Wikipedia+2PMC] Wikipedia Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence Why witnesses deserve respect even when reports fail A central lesson of sceptical Forteana is that false conclusions do not require foolish witnesses. Human perception is active, selective and context sensitive. Under uncertainty, people infer shape, agency, intention and meaning from incomplete signals. That is not a defect found only in “believers”; it is part of normal cognition. Research on paranormal belief and perception shows why this distinction matters. Studies have linked paranormal belief with greater susceptibility to illusory pattern perception, agency detection and attention biases, but the findings do not show that every witness is deluded or dishonest. They show that ambiguous stimuli can be interpreted differently depending on expectation, context and individual differences. Pareidolia — perceiving meaningful forms, such as faces or figures, in ambiguous patterns — is especially relevant to ghost photographs, faces in windows, shapes in smoke and “figures” in low light images. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Sleep paralysis is another useful example because it shows how an experience can be terrifying, vivid and culturally meaningful without requiring a paranormal cause. During sleep paralysis, a person may wake while unable to move and may experience visual, auditory or tactile hallucinations, often including a sensed presence. Researchers describe it as a hybrid state between dreaming and waking, and public explainers note its frequent connection with ghost, demon or intruder interpretations. A respectful sceptical reading does not say, “Nothing happened.” Something did happen: the question is whether the best explanation is neurological, environmental, cultural, paranormal, or some combination of perception and interpretation. [arXiv+2cashp.columbian.gwu.edu] arxiv.org arXiv Sleep Paralysis: phenomenology, neurophysiology and treatment arXiv Sleep Paralysis: phenomenology, neurophysiology and treatment The same discipline applies to aerial reports. Pilots, police officers, soldiers and airport staff can be competent observers and still encounter conditions in which distance, speed, size and identity are hard to judge. Modern UAP investigations repeatedly"},{"title":"Evidence Standard","url":"/evidence-standard/","description":"The famous evidence maxim is useful only when it raises standards without turning scepticism into automatic rejection.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"Evidence Standard How Much Evidence Is Enough? Evidence Standard Evidence Standard How Much Evidence Is Enough? The famous evidence maxim is useful only when it raises standards without turning scepticism into automatic rejection. The famous evidence maxim is useful only when it raises standards without turning scepticism into automatic rejection. policy intervention What the maxim gets right How extraordinary can become subjective Using proportionate evidence in Fortean cases forteana scepticism evidence standard Introduction The familiar maxim that “extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence” has become one of the best known principles of scientific scepticism. Within Fortean inquiry, however, its value depends on how it is applied. Used well, it reminds investigators that claims which challenge well established knowledge should be supported by correspondingly strong evidence before they are accepted. Used poorly, it becomes a conversation ending slogan that dismisses unusual reports before they have even been examined. A balanced Fortean approach treats the maxim as a guide to evidence, not a licence for disbelief. Reports of unexplained lights, apparitions , cryptid sightings or other anomalies should neither be accepted at face value nor rejected simply because they sound improbable. The appropriate question is always: what evidence exists, how reliable is it, and does it justify the conclusion being proposed? What the maxim gets right The principle is most closely associated with Carl Sagan, although versions of it appeared much earlier in the writings of David Hume, Pierre Simon Laplace and sociologist Marcello Truzzi. Its enduring strength is that it expresses a basic rule of rational inquiry: the more a claim conflicts with well tested knowledge, the stronger the evidence should be before established understanding is revised. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do Extraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence?by D Deming · 2016 · Cited by 59 — In 1979 astronomer Carl Sagan popularized th... This is not a special rule reserved for paranormal topics. Science routinely applies different evidential thresholds depending on what is at stake. For example: A claim that someone saw an unfamiliar bird may reasonably rely on a careful eyewitness description and photographs. A claim that a previously unknown large mammal inhabits a densely populated region demands much stronger evidence, such as physical remains, genetic material or multiple independent observations. A claim that established physical laws have been violated requires evidence capable of surviving intensive independent testing. The important point is that evidence standards rise with the consequences of accepting the claim, not because investigators dislike surprising ideas. For Fortean research, this principle encourages careful documentation. Multiple independent witnesses, contemporaneous records, environmental measurements, original photographs, chain of custody for physical samples and opportunities for replication all increase confidence. Weak evidence does not become strong merely because the reported event is dramatic. Dr. Rodney Schmaltz says Scientific Skepticism can Save Society Channel: Centre for Inquiry Canada Open on YouTube How \"extraordinary\" can become subjective The greatest weakness of the maxim is that neither \"extraordinary\" nor \"evidence\" has an agreed objective definition. What one investigator considers extraordinary may seem entirely plausible to another, depending on their background knowledge and assumptions. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do Extraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence?by D Deming · 2016 · Cited by 59 — In 1979 astronomer Carl Sagan popularized th... This subjectivity creates two common errors. The first is inflating the claim . A witness may simply report seeing an unusual aerial object. Later discussion transforms this into \"proof of extraterrestrial spacecraft\", making the evidential burden far greater than the original observation justified. The report itself may be ordinary evidence for an unidentified object while offering no evidence at all for its ultimate cause. The second is inflating the evidential standard . Some sceptics invoke the maxim as though any claim outside current consensus deserves automatic rejection, regardless of the quality of the available evidence. Critics have argued that this use turns the phrase into a rhetorical device rather than a genuine methodological principle because the threshold can be raised indefinitely whenever inconvenient evidence appears. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Do Extraordinary Claims Require Extraordinary Evidence?by D Deming · 2016 · Cited by 59 — In 1979 astronomer Carl Sagan popularized th... Marcello Truzzi, whose work strongly influenced anomalistics, argued that genuine scepticism should suspend judgement until evidence has been evaluated. He distinguished this from what he called \"pseudoscepticism\", where investigators begin from a negative conclusion and require impossible standards only for claims they already reject. That criticism remains influential within Fortean circles because it emphasises that scepticism should test evidence rather than protect existing beliefs. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Marcello Truzzi Marcello Truzzi Using proportionate evidence in Fortean cases Rather than treating every anomaly as either convincing proof or obvious error, Fortean investigation benefits from proportional reasoning. The first step is separating the report from the interpretation . Someone may genuinely experience something unusual without correctly identifying its cause. A witness can honestly report seeing an unfamiliar creature while being mistaken about its species. Likewise, an unexplained aerial observation does not automatically establish an extraordinary origin. The second step is matching the evidence to the specific claim. A photograph may adequately support the claim that an object was present in the sky. It may not support conclusions about its distance, speed, origin or physical nature. Similarly, consistent witness testimony may demonstrate that several people observed something unusual while still leaving its explanation unresolved. Finally, investigators should ask whether alternative explanations have been examined with equal care. This does not mean assuming that ordinary explanations are always correct. It means comparing competing hypotheses against the same body of evidence. A proportionate approach therefore recognises several possible outcomes: the evidence strongly supports a conventional explanation; the evidence supports that an unusual event occurred but not its cause; the evidence is too weak to justify any confident conclusion; the evidence remains genuinely anomalous and deserves continued investigation. Only the final category represents a persistent Fortean mystery, and even then \"unexplained\" should not be confused with \"paranormal\". Do extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence? Channel: Majesty of Reason Open on YouTube Why unresolved cases still matter A common misunderstanding is that a case loses all value if it fails to prove an extraordinary hypothesis. Fortean researchers have often argued the opposite. Well documented unresolved cases can reveal weaknesses in observation, instrumentation, reporting practices or existing explanatory frameworks. Some ultimately receive conventional explanations years later as new information becomes available. Others remain unexplained simply because the available evidence is incomplete rather than because the phenomenon itself is necessarily extraordinary. Maintaining an archive of unresolved reports therefore has scientific value. It preserves observations that may later be reinterpreted as knowledge improves, while avoiding premature conclusions in either direction. History contains many examples of initially disputed observations later becoming accepted through accumulated evidence, alongside many celebrated anomalies eventually explained through better investigation. The lesson is not that every strange report will be vindicated, but that careful inquiry is more productive than immediate belief or immediate dismissal . Evidence standards without closed minds The most useful reading of the famous maxim is therefore procedural rather than ideological. Strong claims deserve strong evidence because accepting them may require revising established knowledge. That does not imply that unusual reports are unworthy of investigation, nor that existing theories are beyond challenge. For Fortean inquiry, the practical standard is straightforward: treat witnesses respectfully, preserve original evidence carefully, distinguish observation from interpretation, and allow conclusions to remain provisional until the evidence justifies greater confidence. High evidential standards and intellectual openness are not competing virtues. They are complementary safeguards against both credulity and"},{"title":"GEIPAN","url":"/geipan/","description":"France's GEIPAN model shows how cases can be identified, weakly evidenced, or left open without becoming paranormal proof.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"GEIPAN How an Official File Can Stay Open GEIPAN GEIPAN How an Official File Can Stay Open France's GEIPAN model shows how cases can be identified, weakly evidenced, or left open without becoming paranormal proof. France's GEIPAN model shows how cases can be identified, weakly evidenced, or left open without becoming paranormal proof. policy intervention What case categories are for Why lack of data is its own result How revisiting cases protects uncertainty forteana scepticism geipan Introduction France's GEIPAN offers one of the clearest examples of how an official investigation body can treat unusual reports without forcing them into either belief or dismissal . Rather than dividing cases into simply \"explained\" and \"unexplained\", GEIPAN uses a classification system that distinguishes between well identified events, probable explanations, cases with too little information, and cases that remain unidentified after investigation. That distinction is important for Fortean investigation because it recognises that uncertainty comes in different forms. A report may remain open because the evidence is inadequate, or because the available evidence has resisted explanation. Neither outcome is presented as proof of extraordinary phenomena. Instead, both are treated as legitimate investigative results that may change if better evidence appears. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... What case categories are for GEIPAN, the investigation unit within the French space agency CNES, receives reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena from witnesses and works with police, aviation authorities, weather specialists and other experts when appropriate. Its aim is to document, analyse and publish cases rather than to endorse any particular explanation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & GeipanIts mission is to collect, analyze, investigate, publish, and archive reports of UAP (Unidentified Aerial/Aerospace... Its four principal categories reflect different levels of evidential confidence: Category A: the phenomenon has been clearly identified after investigation. Category B: the most likely explanation has been identified, although some uncertainty remains. Category C: the case cannot be identified because the available information is insufficient. Category D: the phenomenon remains unidentified even after investigation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... The crucial feature is that these categories describe the outcome of an investigation rather than the nature of the phenomenon itself. A Category D case is not labelled \"paranormal\", \"extraterrestrial\" or \"inexplicable forever\". It simply records that, given the available evidence and current methods, investigators could not reach a satisfactory conventional identification. Likewise, a Category C case does not imply mystery; it indicates that the evidence is too incomplete for a reliable conclusion. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... For scepticism without dismissal, this is a valuable distinction. It separates \"we do not know because we lack evidence\" from \"we do not know despite having investigated adequate evidence\". Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why lack of data is its own result Many discussions of unusual reports assume that every case should end with a definite explanation. GEIPAN's Category C shows why that expectation is unrealistic. Witnesses may provide only a brief description, report an event long after it occurred, or lack photographs, precise times, weather information or corroborating observations. Sometimes investigators simply cannot reconstruct the circumstances well enough to test competing explanations. Rather than guessing, GEIPAN records the investigation as inconclusive because of insufficient information. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... This approach has two important consequences. First, it discourages overconfident scepticism. A poor quality report should not be declared solved merely because one ordinary explanation seems possible. Second, it discourages overconfident belief. An unresolved report produced by missing information is not evidence that an extraordinary event occurred. The absence of decisive evidence is itself part of the case record. For Fortean researchers, this distinction encourages careful archival work. Better witness interviews, contemporaneous notes, environmental records or additional observers may matter more than imaginative theories. The investigative priority becomes improving the evidence rather than filling evidential gaps with speculation. Why a Category D case is not paranormal proof Category D often attracts the most public attention because it contains reports that remain unidentified after investigation. However, GEIPAN's own methodology treats these as unresolved investigations, not confirmations of extraordinary causes. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... Several factors can produce a Category D outcome: observations that contain internally consistent witness testimony but resist conventional identification; combinations of environmental conditions that cannot be reconstructed completely; limitations in available physical or instrumental evidence; competing explanations that remain equally plausible. None of these automatically favours a paranormal interpretation. Instead, Category D acknowledges that investigation sometimes reaches the limits of available information without producing a secure answer. This is an example of suspended judgement in practice. The investigation stops because the evidence stops, not because investigators have chosen an extraordinary explanation. Ovnis inexpliqués près de Rennes : reconstitution filmée du cas d'Étrelles 🛸 Channel: Le Télégramme Open on YouTube How revisiting cases protects uncertainty One of the most distinctive features of GEIPAN's system is that classifications are not necessarily permanent. According to its published methodology, Category C and Category D cases may be re examined if new information becomes available after the original investigation. A newly discovered witness, improved photographic analysis, additional radar information or better astronomical or meteorological reconstruction can justify reopening a case. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... This policy embodies an important scientific principle: conclusions should remain proportional to evidence, and evidence can improve over time. Revisiting cases also guards against two opposite errors. Premature closure. An apparently solved case can sometimes prove more complicated if overlooked evidence emerges. Permanent mystery inflation. A famous unexplained case should not become untouchable folklore simply because it has entered popular culture. Instead of treating classifications as fixed verdicts, GEIPAN treats them as the best available assessment at a particular moment. What the model contributes to Fortean investigation The greatest value of GEIPAN's classification system is methodological rather than ideological. It demonstrates that an official archive can preserve anomalous reports without requiring investigators to choose between credulity and debunking. Its categories distinguish between explanation, probability, insufficient evidence and genuine residual uncertainty. That framework encourages investigators to ask different questions of different cases rather than forcing every report into a single narrative. For Fortean research, this is an important lesson. A carefully documented unresolved case deserves continued attention because it remains unresolved—not because it has become evidence for the paranormal. Equally, a case lacking sufficient information deserves neither ridicule nor romanticisation. It simply remains unfinished until better evidence, if any, changes the balance. [Geipan+2Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMethodologyClassification C: Phenomenon not identified due to lack of data or information. Classification D: Phenomenon not identif... GEIPAN : qui étudie les ovnis ? Science En Questions Channel: L'Esprit Sorcier TV · Views: 13.3K · Uploaded: May 2025 · Length: 1 hour 3 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks"},{"title":"Pareidolia","url":"/pareidolia/","description":"Faces in windows, smoke, shadows, and grainy photos often reveal how pattern-seeking shapes paranormal interpretation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"Pareidolia Why We See Faces in the Strange Pareidolia Pareidolia Why We See Faces in the Strange Faces in windows, smoke, shadows, and grainy photos often reveal how pattern seeking shapes paranormal interpretation. Faces in windows, smoke, shadows, and grainy photos often reveal how pattern seeking shapes paranormal interpretation. mechanism How pattern perception works Common Fortean photo traps When images deserve closer investigation forteana scepticism pareidolia Introduction Pareidolia is the tendency to perceive meaningful patterns in ambiguous visual information, especially faces and human figures where none have been deliberately created. Within Forteana , it provides one of the most important mechanisms for understanding why ghost photographs, mysterious figures in windows, shadowy shapes and indistinct creature images can appear deeply convincing without requiring deception or mental illness. Rather than dismissing witnesses, pareidolia explains how normal human perception operates when confronted with incomplete, noisy or emotionally significant visual information. Research shows that the human brain is strongly biased towards detecting faces quickly because missing a real face or potential threat has historically been more costly than occasionally seeing one that is not really there. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPareidolia in a Built Environment as a Complex by C Wang · 2022 · Cited by 22 — Pareidolia is an illusion caused by ambiguous stimuli [1], and the ambiguous forms are perceived as v... Viewed through the lens of scepticism without dismissal , pareidolia is not an accusation that \"nothing happened\". It is a reminder that an image and its interpretation are different things. A photograph may genuinely contain an unusual shape while the conclusion that it depicts a ghost, cryptid or supernatural presence remains open to investigation. How pattern perception works Human vision is not a passive recording device. The brain constantly combines incoming visual information with expectations, memory and prior experience to produce a coherent interpretation of the world. This predictive process usually helps us recognise people and hazards almost instantly, even under poor viewing conditions. Faces receive especially privileged treatment. Neuroscience studies have found that face like arrangements of eyes, a nose and a mouth activate specialised visual processing remarkably early, even when the \"face\" is simply a pattern in clouds, wood grain or household objects. The brain therefore errs on the side of detection rather than caution. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCPareidolia in a Built Environment as a Complex by C Wang · 2022 · Cited by 22 — Pareidolia is an illusion caused by ambiguous stimuli [1], and the ambiguous forms are perceived as v... Several conditions increase the likelihood of pareidolia: Low light that obscures detail. Heavy image compression or digital noise. Motion blur and long exposures. Smoke, mist, foliage or textured surfaces. Emotional expectation, especially in allegedly haunted locations. Prior suggestion, such as being told where a ghost should appear before viewing an image. None of these conditions guarantee a false perception, but they make ambiguous images much easier to interpret as meaningful figures. Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Common Fortean photo traps Ghost photography has changed enormously since the nineteenth century, yet many recurring visual features remain surprisingly consistent because they arise from ordinary photographic limitations rather than the era's technology. Faces in windows and dark interiors Old buildings often produce reflections from interior lighting, exterior scenery and uneven glass. Combined with curtains, dirt, scratches or weathering, these reflections can resemble human faces looking outward. Expectation also matters. Visitors who already know a location's haunted reputation often inspect photographs for unusual details, giving ambiguous shapes greater psychological significance. Smoke, mist and breath Smoke, fog and visible breath naturally form irregular shapes that briefly resemble heads, bodies or flowing robes. Once someone identifies a figure, other viewers frequently perceive the same outline, even though the image contains no objectively defined person. This process explains why different observers often agree about seeing \"the face\" once it has been pointed out. Shadows and tree branches Branches crossing a wall, uneven illumination or multiple light sources can generate surprisingly human silhouettes. At night, small shifts in viewpoint may transform an ordinary shadow into something apparently standing in a doorway. Photographs freeze these fleeting alignments, removing the movement that would normally reveal their mundane origin. Digital \"orbs\" Among the most common paranormal photographs are bright circular \"orbs\". Photography specialists have repeatedly demonstrated that these usually result from flash illumination reflecting from dust, pollen, raindrops or insects close to the camera lens. Similar effects arise from lens flare, condensation and other optical artefacts. Because the particles are outside the camera's focus, they appear as luminous discs rather than recognisable objects. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spirit photography Spirit photography Why ambiguous images feel so convincing One reason ghost photographs remain persuasive is that the perception itself feels immediate. People often report that they \"saw it instantly\", giving the impression that the image contains an obvious figure. In reality, ambiguous images frequently depend on top down perception: the brain uses expectation to complete incomplete information. Once a face has been recognised, it becomes difficult to see the image as random texture again. This is why internet discussions commonly include annotated versions with circles or arrows showing viewers where to look. Emotional context strengthens this effect. Images taken after bereavement, during ghost investigations or at historically significant locations carry personal meaning that naturally encourages interpretation. Respecting that emotional experience does not require concluding that the photograph documents a supernatural event. Pareidolia: Seeing Faces in Things Channel: Duncan Clarke · Views: 1.6M · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 18 minutes Open on YouTube When images deserve closer investigation Recognising pareidolia should not become an excuse to dismiss every unusual photograph automatically. A careful Fortean investigation begins by asking whether ambiguity alone explains the image before moving towards more extraordinary possibilities. Useful questions include: Is the original, unedited image available? Are multiple photographs from the same sequence consistent? Can lighting, reflections or camera settings explain the effect? Does the apparent figure disappear when viewed from another angle? Did witnesses notice the phenomenon before or only after reviewing the photographs? Is independent contextual evidence available beyond the image itself? These questions separate examination from assumption. Some photographs remain genuinely puzzling because the available information is incomplete, but an unexplained image is not automatically evidence of paranormal activity. Pareidolia and respectful scepticism Within Forteana, pareidolia is valuable precisely because it protects both witnesses and evidence. It allows investigators to acknowledge that someone sincerely perceived a face, apparition or figure without accusing them of fabrication. At the same time, it reminds researchers that perception is not identical to external reality. Many famous ghost images have ultimately been explained through reflections, double exposures, camera artefacts or ordinary environmental features. Others remain unresolved because too little information survives to test competing explanations. In neither situation does the witness's honesty determine whether the interpretation is correct. Scepticism without dismissal therefore treats pareidolia as a starting point rather than a verdict. The experience is accepted as real, the interpretation remains open, and the photograph is examined with the same balance of curiosity and critical thinking that characterises the strongest investigations in Forteana. What it means if you can see faces in objects Susan G. Wardle Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why We See Faces in the Strange. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Believing Brain By Michael Shermer Explains pattern recognition and belief formation. See on Amazon Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Supports careful interpretation of visual evidence. See on Amazon Book Thinking, Fast"},{"title":"Sleep Paralysis","url":"/sleep-paralysis/","description":"Sleep paralysis shows how a terrifying presence can be real as an experience while needing no ghost or demon explanation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"Sleep Paralysis When a Haunting Happens in the Body Sleep Paralysis Sleep Paralysis When a Haunting Happens in the Body Sleep paralysis shows how a terrifying presence can be real as an experience while needing no ghost or demon explanation. Sleep paralysis shows how a terrifying presence can be real as an experience while needing no ghost or demon explanation. mechanism What sleep paralysis feels like Why sensed presences become ghost stories How to evaluate bedroom apparition reports forteana scepticism sleep paralysis Introduction Sleep paralysis is one of the clearest examples of why scepticism need not become dismissal . People who experience it often describe an overwhelming certainty that someone—or something—is in the room. They may see a shadowy figure, feel crushing pressure on their chest, hear footsteps or voices, or believe they are under attack. These experiences are genuine in the sense that they are vividly and often terrifyingly perceived. What respectful scepticism questions is not whether the person had the experience, but whether the experience accurately identifies an external ghost, demon or other supernatural visitor. For students of Forteana , sleep paralysis is an important test case because it shows how sincere testimony and natural explanation can coexist. Rather than accusing witnesses of fabrication or demanding belief in the paranormal, it asks a more careful question: what happened in the brain and body that made the experience feel so unquestionably real? Modern sleep research provides well supported mechanisms that explain many classic \"bedroom haunting\" reports without reducing the emotional reality of those who experienced them. [PMC+2Psychiatry Online] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... What sleep paralysis feels like Sleep paralysis occurs during the transition between rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and wakefulness. The person becomes conscious while the temporary muscle paralysis that normally prevents people acting out dreams is still active. They are awake enough to perceive their surroundings but temporarily unable to move or speak. Episodes usually last seconds to a few minutes. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... The paralysis alone would be alarming, but many episodes also include vivid hallucinations that blend dream imagery with waking perception. Researchers consistently describe three broad patterns: An intruder experience: the overwhelming feeling that an unseen person or threatening presence is nearby. An incubus experience: chest pressure, difficulty breathing and the conviction that something is sitting on or restraining the body. Vestibular or movement experiences: sensations of floating, falling, leaving the body or unusual movement despite remaining physically still. [PubMed+2PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations during sleep...by JA Cheyne · 1999 · Cited by 409 — One factor, labeled Intruder, consis... Importantly, these experiences do not usually feel dreamlike while they are happening. People often report being certain they were fully awake because they recognised their bedroom and could remember trying unsuccessfully to move or call for help. That conviction explains why many witnesses sincerely reject suggestions that they \"must have been dreaming\". From their perspective, they were awake—and in one important sense they were. The experience is a hybrid state in which waking awareness and REM sleep features overlap. [PMC+2Psychiatry Online] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... Sleep Paralysis: The Science of Waking Up Dead 😱 Channel: AltReality Science Open on YouTube Why sensed presences become ghost stories One of the most striking features of sleep paralysis is the \"sensed presence\"—the conviction that another being occupies the room even when no one is visible. This feeling is so common that it has become a central focus of sleep paralysis research. Studies by psychologist J. Allan Cheyne found that the sensed presence is closely associated with intense fear and hypervigilance. Rather than simply inventing a random image, the brain appears to generate a threat detection state in which ambiguous sensory information is interpreted as the presence of an intruder. Once that expectation exists, dream imagery may provide a matching figure: a shadow person, demon, witch, ghost or human attacker. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Hypnagogic and hypnopompic hallucinations during sleep...by JA Cheyne · 1999 · Cited by 409 — One factor, labeled Intruder, consis... The chest pressure reported in many cases has a similarly understandable basis. During REM sleep, breathing naturally becomes shallower and skeletal muscles remain inhibited. When awareness returns before normal muscle control, the unusual sensation of restricted breathing can easily be interpreted as an external force pressing on the chest. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBISleep Paralysis StatPearls NCBI Bookshelfby M Farooq · 2023 · Cited by 28 — The possible explanation for Incubus hallucination during an episode of sle... This combination produces a psychologically compelling narrative: 1. You awaken unable to move. 2. Your brain detects danger. 3. You struggle to breathe normally. 4. Dream imagery intrudes into waking awareness. 5. Your mind identifies an apparent attacker to explain these sensations. The resulting story feels externally caused because the perceptions themselves are experienced as real. The mistake, if there is one, lies in attributing the source outside the body rather than recognising the interaction between REM sleep and waking consciousness. Why cultures describe different visitors Sleep paralysis demonstrates an important principle in Forteana: experience may be universal while interpretation varies. Across cultures, remarkably similar reports appear of nocturnal visitors who immobilise sleepers. The details, however, reflect local beliefs. In some traditions the visitor is interpreted as a demon, in others a witch, ghost, spirit or supernatural attacker. Modern accounts may instead describe shadow figures, extraterrestrial abductors or mysterious entities. Medical reviews argue that these differing explanations often represent cultural interpretations layered onto a common physiological experience rather than evidence for many unrelated supernatural beings. The core features—immobility, fear, sensed presence and chest pressure—remain surprisingly consistent despite changing folklore . [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Paralysis, a Medical Condition with a Diverse Cultural...by E Olunu · 2018 · Cited by 52 — This review aims to medically descri... This does not imply that culture invents the experience. Instead, culture supplies a ready made explanation for sensations that are already frightening and difficult to understand. The terrors of sleep paralysis Ami Angelowicz Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube How to evaluate bedroom apparition reports Respectful scepticism neither dismisses nor automatically validates reports of bedroom apparitions . Instead it asks whether the circumstances match a recognised pattern before considering more extraordinary explanations. Questions worth asking include: Did the experience occur while falling asleep or immediately after waking? Was the witness unable to move or speak? Was there an overwhelming feeling of a nearby presence? Were chest pressure, breathing difficulty or intense fear reported? Did the figure disappear as movement returned? Were there contributing factors such as sleep deprivation, irregular sleep schedules or another sleep disorder? [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... The presence of these features does not prove sleep paralysis occurred, but together they form a well documented clinical pattern that should be considered before invoking paranormal explanations. Conversely, reports that occur during ordinary daytime wakefulness, involve multiple independent observers simultaneously, or include evidence extending beyond personal perception require separate evaluation rather than being casually labelled sleep paralysis. Respectful scepticism avoids replacing one reflexive explanation with another. What this teaches about scepticism Sleep paralysis illustrates a broader lesson for investigating anomalous experiences. Witness sincerity and causal accuracy are different questions. A person may honestly report seeing a terrifying figure standing beside the bed. The emotional impact, memory and fear are"},{"title":"UAP Data","url":"/uap-data/","description":"Modern UAP reports show why unidentified often means under-documented, not necessarily extraordinary.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"UAP Data Unidentified Is Not the Same as Alien UAP Data UAP Data Unidentified Is Not the Same as Alien Modern UAP reports show why unidentified often means under documented, not necessarily extraordinary. Modern UAP reports show why unidentified often means under documented, not necessarily extraordinary. dataset evidence Why stigma affects reporting How weak sensor data limits conclusions What unresolved UAP cases can and cannot prove forteana scepticism uap data Introduction Modern reports of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP), formerly more commonly called UFOs, illustrate an important principle of sceptical inquiry: an event can remain unidentified because the available evidence is incomplete, not because it is extraordinary. This distinction is central to a balanced Fortean approach. A report may deserve investigation without implying that any particular explanation—whether extraterrestrial, secret technology or an unknown natural phenomenon—is justified. Recent government and scientific reviews have repeatedly reached the same broad conclusion. The greatest obstacles to understanding unresolved UAP cases are not necessarily mysterious objects but inconsistent reporting, poor quality sensor data, missing contextual information and the long standing stigma surrounding witnesses. Rather than proving an extraordinary explanation, many unresolved cases highlight the limits of the evidence available for scientific investigation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFinally, NASA's very involvement in gathering future data will play an important role in reducin... Why stigma affects reporting For decades, pilots, military personnel and commercial aircrew often regarded reporting unusual aerial observations as professionally risky. Fear of ridicule, concern about career consequences and association with sensational UFO culture discouraged many observers from filing detailed reports or discussing incidents openly. NASA's 2023 Independent Study Team identified this stigma as a genuine obstacle to scientific progress. The report argued that reluctance to report observations reduces both the quantity and quality of available information. Witnesses who hesitate may delay reporting, omit details or never report an incident at all, making later investigation much harder. NASA concluded that reducing stigma is itself a scientific necessity because better reporting creates better datasets. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFinally, NASA's very involvement in gathering future data will play an important role in reducin... This point is often misunderstood. Reducing stigma does not mean encouraging belief in extraordinary explanations. Instead, it encourages routine reporting of unusual observations in the same way aviation safety systems encourage reporting of equipment failures, weather hazards or near misses. Treating unusual observations as data rather than as entertainment or taboo allows investigators to examine them using ordinary scientific methods. The United States Department of Defense has similarly encouraged structured reporting through the All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO), particularly from military personnel and civilian pilots. Standardised reporting increases the chances that investigators receive accurate timing, location, weather conditions, radar information and other contextual details before evidence is lost. [AARO] aaro.mil Submit A ReportCivilian pilots are encouraged to promptly report UAP sightings to air traffic control. AARO receives UAP related Pilo... How weak sensor data limits conclusions Many popular discussions focus on dramatic videos, but investigators consistently point to a less exciting problem: most unresolved UAP reports contain too little reliable information to support strong conclusions. NASA's study identified several recurring weaknesses: single camera observations with no independent confirmation poorly calibrated sensors missing metadata describing camera settings or aircraft position absence of simultaneous radar, infrared and visual observations limited environmental information such as weather or atmospheric conditions short duration recordings with no observations before or after the event These limitations make even ordinary objects surprisingly difficult to identify. A distant aircraft, balloon, bird or atmospheric effect can appear highly unusual when viewed through a narrow sensor with unknown settings and no supporting measurements. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFinally, NASA's very involvement in gathering future data will play an important role in reducin... A key scientific principle is that multiple independent measurements provide far stronger evidence than any single observation . Ideally, investigators would like simultaneous optical imagery, infrared data, radar tracking and accurate positional information. In practice, very few publicly discussed UAP cases include this combination. Sensor artefacts further complicate interpretation. Modern military imaging systems are designed primarily for targeting or surveillance rather than scientific measurement. Camera motion, image stabilisation, infrared processing, zoom changes and viewing geometry can produce visual effects that appear remarkable without representing unusual motion by the observed object. Analysing such recordings requires access to calibration data that is often unavailable outside the originating organisation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFinally, NASA's very involvement in gathering future data will play an important role in reducin... Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why unresolved does not mean inexplicable An unresolved case occupies an important but limited category. It simply means investigators cannot identify the observed phenomenon with confidence using the available evidence. This differs from several stronger claims that are often incorrectly treated as equivalent: Unresolved is not unexplained forever. Additional evidence can later identify a previously mysterious observation. Unresolved is not evidence of extraterrestrial technology. Multiple explanations may remain plausible simultaneously. Unresolved is not proof that investigators failed. Sometimes the available information is simply insufficient to support any reliable conclusion. A useful comparison comes from accident investigation. Some aviation accidents remain officially undetermined because crucial evidence was destroyed or never collected. Investigators do not infer extraordinary causes from missing information; they recognise the limits imposed by incomplete evidence. The same reasoning applies to many UAP reports. Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube What official reviews have found Recent official reviews have consistently distinguished between cases with identified explanations and those lacking enough evidence for resolution. AARO's historical assessments state that although many reports remain unresolved, most contain limited or poor quality information. The office assesses that many unresolved cases would probably become identifiable if more complete and higher quality observations were available. Rather than treating unidentified reports as evidence of extraordinary phenomena, AARO describes data quality itself as the principal limiting factor. [U.S. Department of War] media.defense.gov DOPSR 2024 0263 AARO HISTORICAL RECORD REPORT VOLUME U.S. Department of WarAARO Historical Record Report Volume 18 Mar 2024 — AARO Investigating Unresolved Historical Nuclear Related UAP Cas... NASA reached a closely related conclusion. Its independent study found no evidence that current UAP datasets require extraterrestrial explanations, while emphasising that the existing evidence base is too inconsistent to permit strong scientific conclusions about many cases. The report therefore recommended improving data collection instead of drawing broad inferences from limited observations. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFinally, NASA's very involvement in gathering future data will play an important role in reducin... These findings illustrate an important methodological distinction. Saying \"we do not know\" reflects scientific caution rather than scepticism toward witnesses. Investigators can simultaneously regard a witness as sincere while recognising that sincerity alone cannot determine what was observed. Better data changes the question Modern UAP research increasingly focuses less on debating individual sightings and more on improving observation systems. Proposed improvements include:"},{"title":"Witnesses","url":"/witnesses-967c81/","description":"Honest witnesses can misread ambiguous events, so a careful page separates sincerity, memory, and external evidence.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Scepticism","level":3,"text":"Witnesses Can a Sincere Witness Still Be Wrong? Witnesses Witnesses Can a Sincere Witness Still Be Wrong? Honest witnesses can misread ambiguous events, so a careful page separates sincerity, memory, and external evidence. Honest witnesses can misread ambiguous events, so a careful page separates sincerity, memory, and external evidence. population context Why sincerity is not proof How memory changes strange reports Respectful questions that preserve testimony forteana scepticism witnesses Introduction Many Fortean accounts begin with a person describing something they genuinely believe they experienced. That sincerity deserves respect, but it is not the same thing as independent evidence that the event occurred exactly as remembered. A careful investigator can accept that a witness is being honest while still asking whether perception, memory, later discussion, or missing context may have altered the account. This distinction is central to scepticism without dismissal : it protects witnesses from unfair accusations of deceit while protecting investigations from treating memory itself as proof. Research on eyewitness testimony has repeatedly shown that memory is reconstructive rather than a perfect recording. People can recall real experiences inaccurately without intending to mislead, and confidence alone is not a reliable guide to accuracy. These findings matter far beyond criminal courts. They offer a practical framework for reading unusual reports with empathy and intellectual caution rather than either belief or ridicule. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 263 — Findings from basic psychological researc... Why sincerity is not proof A sincere witness answers one question: whether they are honestly reporting what they believe they experienced. It does not answer another, separate question: whether their recollection perfectly matches an external event. In Fortean investigations these questions are often blurred together. If someone reports an unexplained light, an apparition, or an unfamiliar animal, listeners may jump immediately to one of two extremes. They either conclude that the witness must be mistaken or dishonest, or they assume honesty proves the extraordinary interpretation. Neither conclusion follows logically. Instead, investigators benefit from separating three distinct possibilities: The witness is deliberately inventing the story. The witness experienced something genuine but misunderstood what caused it. The witness accurately perceived an event that remains unexplained. Only the first possibility concerns honesty. The other two involve perception and interpretation rather than character. Modern research on eyewitness evidence reinforces this distinction. Scientific reviews consistently find that many ordinary influences—including distance, lighting, stress, viewing angle and the passage of time—affect what people later remember without implying deception. [National Academies+2National Academies] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesIdentifying the Culprit: Assessing Eyewitness Identification...Throughout this report, the term identification denotes... Psychology Eyewitness Testimony Channel: Cognito · Views: 2.6K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube How memory changes strange reports Memory is an active reconstruction rather than a stored video recording. Every recall involves rebuilding an event from fragments, expectations and later knowledge. That reconstruction is usually good enough for everyday life but becomes more vulnerable when the original experience was brief, surprising or ambiguous. For Fortean reports, several well established mechanisms are especially relevant. Ambiguous events invite interpretation Many unusual experiences occur under poor viewing conditions: distant lights, darkness, fog, fleeting movement or unexpected sounds. When sensory information is incomplete, the brain naturally fills gaps using prior knowledge and expectations. This does not mean witnesses invent experiences. It means that perception itself already contains interpretation before memory even begins. Later certainty may reflect the brain settling on one explanation rather than preserving uncertainty. Research reviewed by the National Academies identifies limited viewing time, poor illumination, stress and distance as important influences on later eyewitness accuracy. [National Academies] nationalacademies.org National AcademiesIdentifying the Culprit: Assessing Eyewitness Identification...Throughout this report, the term identification denotes... Memories absorb later information Witnesses rarely remain isolated after an unusual event. They discuss it with friends, compare stories, read newspaper articles, revisit the location or encounter online explanations. Each encounter creates opportunities for new information to become incorporated into recollection. Laboratory research on the misinformation effect has demonstrated that misleading post event information can alter later memories while leaving people genuinely convinced they remember the modified version. [PMC+2Wiley Online Library] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect PMCby DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 34 — This research has focused on both basic (... For Fortean cases, this means investigators should distinguish between: the earliest recorded account; later expanded versions; interpretations added after publicity; folklore or community traditions that become attached to the report. The earliest documentation often preserves valuable uncertainty that later retellings smooth away. Common Law S4 E8: The Psychology of Eyewitness Memory Channel: University of Virginia School of Law · Views: 2.1K · Uploaded: May 2022 · Length: 29 minutes Open on YouTube Confidence grows more easily than accuracy People often assume that confident witnesses must possess accurate memories. Decades of research show that the relationship is far more complicated. Confidence can increase after social reinforcement, repeated retelling or confirming feedback even when memory itself has not become more accurate. Consequently, an emotionally compelling narrative delivered years later should not automatically outweigh a hesitant contemporary account made soon after the event. [Judicature+2PMC] judicature.duke.edu A Clearer View: The Impact of the National Academy of...Overconfidence may be the most pernicious problem with eyewitness test... Respectful questions that preserve testimony Treating witnesses respectfully does not require accepting every conclusion they draw. In fact, neutral interviewing often preserves better evidence than enthusiastic agreement or aggressive scepticism. Useful questions focus first on observation before interpretation. Instead of asking, \"Do you think it was paranormal?\", an investigator might ask: What did you directly see or hear? What was the lighting like? Approximately how long did the event last? What drew your attention? When did you first write or tell someone about it? Did you discuss it with other witnesses before recording your own account? These questions preserve the witness's experience while reducing opportunities to unintentionally reshape it. Research on investigative interviewing has shown that carefully structured approaches such as the Cognitive Interview can improve the amount of accurate information recalled without relying on leading questions. Equally important is avoiding suggestive questioning that pressures witnesses towards a particular explanation. [assets.college.police.uk+2Simply Psychology] assets.college.police.uk It uses different retrieval techniques to prompt the...Read more... A Fortean approach to testimony Within Forteana , witness testimony often represents the starting point rather than the conclusion of an investigation. A compelling report can identify an event worth examining even when it cannot establish what objectively occurred. This perspective also explains why many classic cases remain debated for decades. The testimony may be entirely genuine while the surviving physical evidence is sparse, contradictory or absent. Rather than forcing certainty, careful investigators separate the documented experience from later interpretations. The most useful question therefore becomes neither \"Did the witness lie?\" nor \"Should we believe them?\" Instead it is: \"What does this testimony reliably establish, and where does memory end and independent evidence begin?\" That distinction allows sincere witnesses to be treated with dignity while keeping extraordinary conclusions tied to evidence that can be examined beyond memory alone. THE COGNITIVE INTERVIEW Geiselman (1984) & Fisher (1987) Channel: Psychology Unlocked Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can a Sincere Witness Still Be Wrong?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper"},{"title":"Combustion","url":"/combustion/","description":"Spontaneous human combustion cases reveal how dramatic deaths can become mysteries when evidence is incomplete.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Combustion What Explains Spontaneous Human Combustion Claims? Combustion Combustion What Explains Spontaneous Human Combustion Claims? Spontaneous human combustion cases reveal how dramatic deaths can become mysteries when evidence is incomplete. Spontaneous human combustion cases reveal how dramatic deaths can become mysteries when evidence is incomplete. mechanism The claim and its pattern Fire science and scene evidence Why the phrase remains powerful forteana combustion Introduction Spontaneous human combustion claims sit at the darker edge of Forteana because they begin with a real and often tragic fact: a person is found dead, badly burned, while the surrounding room appears strangely intact. The Fortean claim is that the human body can ignite and burn from within without an external flame. The stronger forensic reading is less supernatural but still unsettling: many classic cases look like slow, localised fires in which clothing, chair fabric, bedding, body fat and incapacity combine to produce a “human candle” effect after some ordinary ignition source has been missed, destroyed or wrongly discounted. Forensic pathologist Roger Byard has described spontaneous combustion as a scientifically discredited mythology, while medical and fire investigation sources continue to discuss the wick effect as the mechanism that can make these scenes look impossible. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Forensic Sci Med Pathol. 2016 Sep;12(3):350 2. doi: 10.1007/s12024 016 9748 2. Epub 2016 Feb 12.Read more... The claim and its pattern The usual spontaneous human combustion story has a recognisable shape. A victim, often elderly, isolated, asleep, unwell or otherwise unable to escape, is found with the torso almost entirely consumed. Feet or lower legs may remain recognisable. Damage to the room is limited compared with the damage to the body. There may be soot, greasy deposits, heat damage above the body, and a burned chair or patch of flooring, but not the room wide destruction many people expect from a fire hot enough to reduce a body to ash. That contrast is what gives the claim its power: the scene looks selective, almost targeted. [The Irish Times] irishtimes.com The Irish TimesExplanation overdue for combustion of humans20 Oct 2011 — The room in which the victim dies usually shows few, if any, sig... The phrase “spontaneous human combustion” also smuggles in a conclusion. It does not merely say “unusual burning death”; it says the fire began without an external ignition source. That is the key weakness. In many historical accounts, candles, fireplaces, cigarettes, lamps or other mundane fire sources were present but treated as irrelevant, omitted in retellings , or impossible to evaluate because the fire itself destroyed the earliest evidence. Nickell and Fischer’s well known investigation of alleged cases argued that supposed spontaneous combustion deaths often occurred near plausible ignition sources and involved circumstances that could leave the victim unable to react. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Mary Reeser and the “cinder lady” problem Mary Reeser ’s death in St Petersburg, Florida, in July 1951 became one of the defining modern cases. She was found after her landlady noticed heat at the door; most of Reeser’s body and chair had been consumed, while much of the small apartment remained comparatively undamaged. The case drew police and FBI attention, and later reporting notes that investigators found no evidence of lightning or accelerants such as petrol or alcohol. The more plausible reconstruction is that Reeser, who had taken sleeping pills and was known to smoke, dropped a cigarette that ignited flammable clothing; her body fat and the chair then sustained a slow localised fire. [Tampa Bay Times] tampabay.com Open source on tampabay.com. The case remains memorable because some details do not feel intuitive. A body can be badly destroyed while nearby objects survive; a fire can be lethal without becoming a room engulfing blaze; and the absence of an obvious ignition source after the event is not the same as proof that no ignition source existed. Reeser’s case shows why spontaneous combustion is a Fortean subject rather than a simple hoax category: the death was real, the scene was unusual, and the mystery grew in the gap between fire science and public expectation. [northeastjournal.org] northeastjournal.org Spontaneous Combustion? The Curious Case of Mary Spontaneous Combustion? The Curious Case of Mary Spontaneous Human Combustion Debunking Historic Mysteries Channel: Lady of the Library Open on YouTube Michael Faherty and the modern coroner’s verdict A much later case renewed the debate. In December 2010, Michael Faherty, aged 76, was found burned in his home in Galway, Ireland. At the 2011 inquest, the coroner said there was no adequate explanation other than spontaneous combustion; fire officers reportedly said the nearby open fire was not the cause, and no accelerant or sign of forced entry was found. The Guardian reported the verdict as the first such conclusion in the coroner’s 25 year career. [The Guardian] theguardian.com The Guardian Spontaneous combustion killed Irish pensioner, inquest rules The Guardian Spontaneous combustion killed Irish pensioner, inquest rules The Faherty case is important because it shows how “spontaneous combustion” can appear in an official setting without settling the scientific question. A coroner’s verdict of no adequate explanation is not a demonstrated mechanism for internal ignition. It marks the limit of that investigation’s reconstruction. Irish Times coverage later noted that Faherty was found near an open fireplace and that the only real fire damage was to the body, the floor beneath and the ceiling above — exactly the kind of pattern that keeps these cases alive in public memory. [The Irish Times] irishtimes.com ashes and cinders a spontaneous combustion on prussia street 1.3369596 ashes and cinders a spontaneous combustion on prussia street 1.3369596 How fire science changes the mystery The main forensic explanation is the wick effect, sometimes called the candle effect. The basic idea is simple: clothing, bedding or upholstery acts like a wick, while melted body fat acts as fuel. Once an external flame has started the process, a small, persistent fire can burn for hours. It does not need dramatic flames. It needs time, available fat, absorbent material, oxygen and a victim who is dead, asleep, unconscious, intoxicated, immobile or otherwise unable to stop it. Medscape’s forensic pathology overview describes the wick effect as a smouldering fire perpetuated by melting body fat, like a candle feeding a small flame. [eMedicine] emedicine.medscape.com 1975728 overview Forensic Pathology of Thermal Injuries2 Jul 2025 — The wick effect is seen when a smoldering fire is perpetuated by melting fat... This helps explain the most “impossible” features of the scenes: The torso is often worst affected because it contains more fat and rests against clothing, bedding, chair fabric or carpet that can absorb melted fat. Lower legs and feet may survive because they contain less fat, may be positioned away from the main heat column, and may not be wrapped in the same fuel soaked material. The room may not burn down because a small, localised fire burns upward more readily than sideways and may not spread far if nearby combustibles are limited. The ignition source may disappear because a cigarette, match, ember, fabric edge or small flame may be consumed early in the event. Experiments and forensic observations support the broad plausibility of this mechanism. A BBC linked pig carcass experiment often cited in discussions of the wick effect showed that a body wrapped in material could burn for hours, destroy much of the fatty central body area, and still leave the surrounding room less damaged than expected. Popular Science’s 2025 explainer summarised forensic scientist John DeHaan’s experiment in similar terms: the pig’s midsection and bones were largely consumed while the less fatty legs remained. [Popular Science] popsci.com Popular Science Can humans spontaneously combust? The baffling cases Popular Science Can humans spontaneously combust? The baffling cases The wick effect does not mean every unusual fire death is solved in advance. It is a mechanism for how a body can be extensively consumed after ignition, not a magic answer for where the first spark came from. That distinction matters. In a good investigation, “wick effect” should prompt detailed questions about cigarettes, heaters, fireplaces, candles, clothing fibres, mobility, medication, alcohol, oxygen equipment, bedding and furniture — not become a lazy replacement for scene work. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Can You Spontaneously Combust? Earth Science Channel: BBC Earth Science Open on YouTube Spontaneous Human Combustion: The"},{"title":"Alcohol Myths","url":"/alcohol-myths/","description":"Older spontaneous combustion stories often linked burning deaths to alcohol, revealing how moral panic helped a medical myth survive.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Alcohol Myths How Alcohol Became Part of the Myth Alcohol Myths Alcohol Myths How Alcohol Became Part of the Myth Older spontaneous combustion stories often linked burning deaths to alcohol, revealing how moral panic helped a medical myth survive. Older spontaneous combustion stories often linked burning deaths to alcohol, revealing how moral panic helped a medical myth survive. event time window Early medical claims about alcohol and combustion Moral storytelling in older case reports Why modern forensic explanations moved away from the myth forteana combustion alcohol myths Introduction One of the most persistent ideas in the history of spontaneous human combustion is that alcohol somehow made the human body capable of bursting into flames. For more than two centuries, heavy drinking appeared repeatedly in medical reports, newspaper stories and popular fiction as an apparent risk factor. Yet the role assigned to alcohol was rarely based on experimental evidence. Instead, it reflected a mixture of limited medical knowledge, selective reporting and powerful moral attitudes towards drunkenness. Modern fire science and forensic pathology have rejected the notion that alcohol saturates the body sufficiently to make it spontaneously combustible. Instead, investigators explain many historical cases through ordinary ignition sources, prolonged burning and the so called wick effect , while recognising that intoxication may have increased the likelihood of an accidental fire rather than causing combustion itself. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion How alcohol became linked to spontaneous combustion The association between alcohol and spontaneous combustion emerged during the eighteenth century, when physicians attempted to explain a handful of unusual burning deaths without understanding the behaviour of prolonged, low temperature body fires. One of the earliest influential examples was the reported death of Countess Cornelia Zangari Bandi in the early eighteenth century. Paul Rolli's 1746 account, published in the Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society , described the case while noting reports that the victim regularly consumed spirits. The implication that excessive drinking somehow altered the body into a combustible substance became highly influential, despite the absence of experimental proof. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion By the early nineteenth century, medical texts frequently repeated earlier cases instead of critically re examining them. Works on medical jurisprudence listed habitual drunkenness as a common characteristic among alleged victims, gradually transforming an observation from a small collection of anecdotes into what appeared to be an established medical pattern. The repetition itself gave the claim an undeserved authority. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Importantly, physicians of the period often confused correlation with causation. Many reported victims were elderly, infirm, socially isolated or intoxicated—factors that also increased the likelihood of accidental fires involving candles, fireplaces, oil lamps or tobacco. Moral storytelling shaped the myth The alcohol connection survived not simply because of medical speculation but because it served a broader moral purpose. Throughout the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, excessive drinking was widely portrayed as both a physical and moral corruption. Stories of people literally consumed by internal fire became powerful cautionary tales. Rather than presenting combustion as a random tragedy, many accounts implied that victims had brought disaster upon themselves through vice. This moral framing made the stories memorable and easy to repeat. As temperance movements gained influence during the nineteenth century, spontaneous combustion narratives could function almost as parables about the dangers of drunkenness, regardless of whether the evidence supported such conclusions. This pattern is visible in many early case reports. Details about alcohol consumption were often highlighted, while equally important circumstances—such as fireplaces, candles, smoking materials or limited mobility—received comparatively little attention or disappeared entirely in later retellings . Modern investigators have noted that the omission of ordinary ignition sources helped reinforce the illusion that the fires originated inside the body. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion The Baffling Cases of Spontaneous Human Combustion Channel: Popular Science Open on YouTube Literature reinforced the association The medical myth spread well beyond scientific circles because popular writers adopted it. Perhaps the best known literary example is Charles Dickens's Bleak House (1852–1853), in which the alcoholic rag and bottle dealer Mr Krook dies by spontaneous combustion. Dickens defended his decision after critics argued that he had promoted an impossible phenomenon, insisting that he had relied on published case histories then considered respectable. The episode illustrates how medical belief and popular culture reinforced one another. Readers encountered spontaneous combustion not only in newspapers and medical books but also in one of the most widely read novels of the Victorian period. Because Krook is portrayed as chronically intoxicated, the fictional death strengthened the perceived connection between alcoholism and combustion for generations of readers. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Other nineteenth century fiction likewise portrayed drunken characters as especially vulnerable to \"animal combustion\" or similar internal fires, reflecting contemporary assumptions rather than experimental evidence. Why alcohol was a plausible—but incorrect—explanation The theory initially appeared reasonable because alcohol is flammable in concentrated form. If distilled spirits burn readily outside the body, some physicians reasoned that a body saturated with alcohol might also become combustible. Modern chemistry shows why this idea fails. The human body remains mostly water, even after heavy drinking. Alcohol absorbed into tissues is rapidly metabolised and never approaches the concentrations needed for spontaneous ignition. Blood alcohol levels that are medically dangerous are still far below those required to sustain combustion. There is therefore no known biological mechanism by which drinking alcohol could make a living person's tissues ignite without an external flame. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Occasionally, speculative modern hypotheses have suggested that metabolic products such as acetone might contribute to unusual fires under rare circumstances. These ideas remain unsupported as explanations for alleged spontaneous human combustion and have not displaced the forensic consensus that external ignition is required. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Can Spontaneous Human Combustion REALLY Happen? Channel: Math and Science Open on YouTube Why modern forensic explanations moved away from the myth Twentieth century fire investigation transformed understanding of these deaths by focusing on combustion rather than folklore . Detailed analyses by fire investigators and forensic specialists showed that many historical cases shared ordinary characteristics: nearby candles, fireplaces or cigarettes capable of providing ignition; victims who were elderly, asleep, ill or intoxicated and therefore less able to escape; clothing that absorbed liquefied body fat during burning; prolonged, localised fires that destroyed much of the body while leaving the wider room comparatively intact. This process became known as the wick effect. In this model, clothing acts like the wick of a candle while body fat supplies the fuel after ignition from an ordinary external source. The result can appear remarkably selective, producing scenes that earlier observers interpreted as mysterious internal combustion. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Modern investigators therefore distinguish sharply between alcohol as a behavioural risk factor and alcohol as a combustion mechanism. Heavy intoxication may increase the chances of falling asleep with a lit cigarette, collapsing near a fire or failing to react after clothing ignites. It does not make the body chemically capable of spontaneous ignition. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion The legacy of the alcohol myth The historical link between alcohol and spontaneous human combustion reveals how scientific uncertainty can merge with cultural attitudes. Early observers tried to explain baffling fire scenes using the medical knowledge available to them, but their interpretations were strongly"},{"title":"Intact Rooms","url":"/intact-rooms/","description":"Limited room damage is often the detail that makes these deaths seem impossible, but slow upward burning can leave a misleading scene.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Intact Rooms Why Did the Room Not Burn Down? Intact Rooms Intact Rooms Why Did the Room Not Burn Down? Limited room damage is often the detail that makes these deaths seem impossible, but slow upward burning can leave a misleading scene. Limited room damage is often the detail that makes these deaths seem impossible, but slow upward burning can leave a misleading scene. population context How slow local fires spread upward What soot and heat damage can reveal Why public expectations misread burn scenes forteana combustion intact rooms Introduction One of the most striking features of alleged spontaneous human combustion cases is not simply the condition of the body, but the condition of the room. Readers often expect that a fire capable of destroying much of a human body should also consume furniture, walls and the entire building. When that does not happen, the scene appears to defy ordinary fire behaviour. Modern fire investigation offers a different explanation. In many cases, the contrast between a severely burned body and a largely intact room is exactly what investigators would expect from a slow, localised fire rather than a rapidly developing house fire. The proposed \" wick effect \" produces a relatively small but sustained flame that consumes body fat and nearby clothing over many hours. Such a fire can cause intense damage within a limited area while never generating the large flames needed to ignite the rest of the room. This mismatch between public expectation and actual fire dynamics is one reason spontaneous human combustion remains a persistent Fortean mystery rather than a forensic one. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Forensic Sci Med Pathol. 2016 Sep;12(3):350 2. doi: 10.1007/s12024 016 9748 2. Epub 2016 Feb 12.Read more... How a slow local fire can leave most of a room standing The common mental picture of fire is a fast moving blaze that spreads rapidly from curtains to furniture before engulfing an entire room. Fatal fires associated with alleged spontaneous human combustion rarely resemble this pattern. Instead, forensic investigators describe a slow burning process in which: clothing absorbs melted body fat and behaves like a candle wick; [reddit.com] reddit.com TIL About the Wick Effect, when a human body is destroyed...A human body is destroyed by fire due to their clothing having soaked up mel... combustion remains concentrated around the torso, where body fat is greatest; flames are comparatively small, although temperatures immediately around the body remain high; the fire progresses over several hours rather than minutes. Because the burning area remains limited, much of the heat is consumed in continuously evaporating moisture from tissue and burning the available fuel immediately around the body. There may simply not be enough radiant heat or flame contact to ignite more distant furnishings. Experimental work has repeatedly shown that sustained combustion under these conditions can remain remarkably localised. [Wiley Online Library+2ResearchGate] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibrarySustained Combustion of Bodies: Some Observations 4 May 2012 — The combustion then takes place where the body fat bur... Published: May 2012 This helps explain why investigators sometimes find a chair almost completely destroyed while nearby shelves, books or curtains remain only lightly scorched. Can Spontaneous Human Combustion REALLY Happen? Channel: Math and Science Open on YouTube Why bodies and furniture can burn differently The apparent contradiction becomes less surprising when different fuels are compared. A human body is mostly water and does not burn easily on its own. However, once an external ignition source starts a fire and clothing becomes saturated with liquefied fat, the combination resembles an inside out oil lamp. The clothing provides the wick, while rendered fat provides a continuing fuel source. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Spontaneous human combustion in the light of the 21st...by V Koljonen · 2012 · Cited by 12 — A tear in the skin has to occur for t... Furniture elsewhere in the room may never receive sufficient heat for long enough to ignite. Many household materials require sustained exposure to high temperatures before flaming combustion begins. If flames remain only a few tens of centimetres high and are concentrated around one location, ignition of distant objects may never occur despite many hours of burning. Fire dynamics research emphasises that fire spread depends not simply on maximum temperature but on heat transfer, ventilation, fuel arrangement and duration of exposure. [NIST] nist.gov fire dynamics Fire Dynamics NISTNovember 17, 2010 — Dynamics is the study of how fires start, spread and develop. (NFPA 921). Human skin temperat... Published: November 17, 2010 What soot and heat damage reveal Although dramatic photographs often focus on what survived, investigators also pay close attention to what changed. Typical observations in these cases include: heavy soot staining above the body; greasy or oily deposits on nearby walls and ceilings; localised charring of flooring beneath the body; heat damage directly overhead rather than across the entire room; smoke contamination extending further than flame damage. These patterns are consistent with prolonged smouldering and slow combustion. Heat and smoke naturally rise, producing vertical staining above the point of burning. A ceiling may become heavily blackened while furniture only a short distance away escapes ignition. Fire investigators regard these patterns as clues to a confined fire rather than evidence that flames somehow ignored surrounding combustible materials. [USFA Applications] apps.usfa.fema.gov R. Thermal damage on the sides of furnishings close to the fire may be USFA ApplicationsFire Investigation: First Responders Student ManualThermal damage is limited to the areas where the fire or hot gases ar... Historical reports often mention sticky soot or greasy residues coating the room. Such deposits are compatible with incomplete combustion of fats and other organic materials during a long burning, oxygen limited fire rather than requiring an unusual or unknown process. [MDPI] mdpi.com The Phenomenon of Spontaneous Human Combustion in...by A Pérez Pérez · 2025 — The room was covered with a thick, sticky and foul sme... Why expectations often misread these scenes The impression that \"the room should have burned down\" largely comes from comparing these deaths with dramatic building fires seen in films or television. In reality, a room reaches full involvement only if enough heat is produced to ignite multiple surrounding fuels. Investigators refer to this transition as flashover. Many alleged spontaneous human combustion scenes never show evidence that flashover occurred. Without it, a fire may remain surprisingly restricted despite lasting for many hours. [NIST] nist.gov fire dynamics Fire Dynamics NISTNovember 17, 2010 — Dynamics is the study of how fires start, spread and develop. (NFPA 921). Human skin temperat... Published: November 17, 2010 Another source of confusion is cremation. Modern crematoria use controlled temperatures of roughly 800–1,000°C together with forced airflow and specialised equipment to reduce a body to bone fragments over several hours. A slow domestic fire operates under very different conditions. It can destroy soft tissue and severely damage bone locally without reproducing the complete cremation process familiar to the public. [cfitrainer.net] cfitrainer.net Fatal Fires: Fire Effects on the Body This module provides a foundational Fatal Fires: Fire Effects on the BodyThis module provides a foundational introduction to the dynamic fire effects on the human body, incl... Because most people have little experience with real fire behaviour, they intuitively assume that severe damage to one object must imply catastrophic damage to everything nearby. Fire investigations repeatedly show that this assumption is unreliable. Spontaneous Human Combustion Debunking Historic Mysteries Channel: Lady of the Library Open on YouTube Experimental evidence supporting localised burning Several experiments have explored whether a body can burn extensively without destroying an entire room. Laboratory demonstrations of the wick effect have shown that cloth impregnated with fat burns slowly with a relatively modest flame, producing considerable soot over an extended period. Larger demonstrations using pig carcasses wrapped in blankets likewise produced prolonged burning with limited flame spread, while leaving much of the test room structurally intact. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect A more detailed forensic study of cadavers found that sustained combustion under wick effect conditions could continue for six to seven hours. Destruction was concentrated in the torso, where subcutaneous fat is greatest, while the limbs often remained comparatively preserved. This closely resembles the damage"},{"title":"Mary Reeser","url":"/mary-reeser/","description":"Mary Reeser's 1951 death became a defining case because a destroyed body, an intact apartment, sleeping pills, and smoking fit both mystery and fire science.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Mary Reeser Why Mary Reeser Became the Cinder Lady Mary Reeser Mary Reeser Why Mary Reeser Became the Cinder Lady Mary Reeser's 1951 death became a defining case because a destroyed body, an intact apartment, sleeping pills, and smoking fit both mystery and fire science. Mary Reeser's 1951 death became a defining case because a destroyed body, an intact apartment, sleeping pills, and smoking fit both mystery and fire science. entity What investigators found in the apartment Why smoking and sedation mattered How later retellings changed the mystery forteana combustion mary reeser Introduction Mary Reeser's death in St Petersburg, Florida, on 2 July 1951 became the defining modern case in debates over alleged spontaneous human combustion because it combined an extraordinary fire scene with an apparently ordinary ignition source. Reeser, a 67 year old widow, was found almost completely reduced to ash while much of her apartment remained standing. The contrast between the destruction of her body and the limited damage to the room encouraged decades of paranormal speculation. Yet the official investigation followed a different line: that a dropped cigarette, combined with sedative medication, flammable clothing and a slow burning \" wick effect \", could account for the scene without invoking an unknown phenomenon. The enduring fascination with the case lies not in a single unexplained fact but in the tension between what seems intuitively impossible and what fire investigators argue can occur under unusual conditions. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser March 31, 2026 — Mary Hardy Reeser (March 8, 1884 – July 2, 1951) of St. Petersburg, Florida, was a woman whose unusual death by burning... Published: March 31, 2026 What investigators found in the apartment When Reeser's landlady arrived with a telegram on the morning of 2 July 1951, she found the apartment door closed and the metal doorknob unusually hot. Police entering the flat discovered that Reeser had been almost entirely consumed by fire while seated in an upholstered chair. Only part of her left foot, sections of her spine and portions of her skull remained. Nearby plastic objects had softened from heat, but the fire had not spread through the apartment as a conventional house fire would. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser March 31, 2026 — Mary Hardy Reeser (March 8, 1884 – July 2, 1951) of St. Petersburg, Florida, was a woman whose unusual death by burning... Published: March 31, 2026 The unusual appearance of the scene prompted the St Petersburg Police Department to seek outside assistance. Physical evidence, including carpet fragments, glass, the surviving slipper and other recovered material, was sent to the FBI for laboratory examination. Local investigators specifically asked whether science could explain how a human body could be so extensively destroyed while the fire remained confined to such a small area. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mary Reeser Mary Reeser Several details quickly entered popular folklore but deserve careful treatment: The apartment was not untouched. The chair in which Reeser had been sitting was destroyed, heat damaged nearby objects, and soot and greasy residues were present. The mystery arose because the damage was localised rather than room wide. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser March 31, 2026 — Mary Hardy Reeser (March 8, 1884 – July 2, 1951) of St. Petersburg, Florida, was a woman whose unusual death by burning... Published: March 31, 2026 The famous \"shrunken skull\" became controversial. Contemporary reports claimed the skull had shrunk dramatically. Later fire researchers argued that skulls do not contract in that manner under heat, suggesting that early descriptions were inaccurate or misunderstood, making this one of the most repeated but least reliable elements of the story. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser March 31, 2026 — Mary Hardy Reeser (March 8, 1884 – July 2, 1951) of St. Petersburg, Florida, was a woman whose unusual death by burning... Published: March 31, 2026 These features made the case appear extraordinary to the public while simultaneously presenting a challenging but recognisable fire investigation for forensic specialists. The Spontaneous Human Combustion Of Mary Reeser Channel: BuzzFeed Unsolved Network Open on YouTube Why smoking and sedation mattered The most influential non paranormal explanation is often called the cigarette theory. Rather than suggesting that a cigarette could instantly cremate a person, investigators proposed a sequence of events in which the cigarette merely served as the initial ignition source. According to this reconstruction, Reeser had taken barbiturate sleeping medication before retiring for the night and was also known to smoke. If a lit cigarette fell onto her nightdress or the upholstery of the chair after she became drowsy or unconscious, the clothing could begin smouldering without provoking an effective response. Smoke inhalation, sedation or both could then have prevented escape before flames developed. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion From that point, investigators argued that the fire could transition into what later became widely known as the wick effect . In this process: Clothing functions as a wick. Melted body fat becomes the fuel. The fire burns slowly for many hours. Heat is concentrated close to the body rather than spreading rapidly throughout the room. This mechanism produces a long duration, relatively low flame fire capable of destroying much of the body while leaving surprisingly limited damage elsewhere. Experimental work and later forensic literature have shown that such slow burning conditions can reproduce many of the characteristics once thought unique to spontaneous human combustion claims. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Importantly, the cigarette theory does not claim that a cigarette alone possesses enough energy to reduce a body to ash. Instead, it proposes that the cigarette provides only the initial spark, after which the body's own fat and nearby combustible materials sustain combustion for many hours. This distinction is often lost in popular retellings . Why the apartment did not become an inferno One reason the Reeser case continues to surprise readers is that many people assume a fire capable of destroying a human body must also destroy the building. Fire investigators point out that this expectation is based on everyday experience with rapidly spreading fires rather than slow, fuel limited combustion. In the proposed reconstruction, several factors restricted the fire: Reeser remained in one location rather than moving through the room. The chair and clothing absorbed liquefied fat, concentrating combustion around the body. The concrete floor beneath the chair limited downward fire spread. Limited oxygen reduced the likelihood of flashover, the rapid ignition of an entire room. As a result, temperatures immediately around the body could remain high for extended periods while temperatures elsewhere never reached the levels needed to ignite furniture across the apartment. This selective pattern has since become one of the principal arguments supporting the wick effect explanation in similar cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Samozapłon ciała. Prawda czy mit? Channel: OdwróconaLekcja Open on YouTube How later retellings changed the mystery The Reeser case has accumulated layers of embellishment over more than seventy years. Newspaper headlines quickly dubbed her the \"Cinder Lady\", and later books, television programmes and paranormal publications increasingly presented the death as evidence for spontaneous human combustion. Several changes in emphasis shaped public memory: The FBI's interest was sometimes portrayed as evidence that investigators believed in spontaneous combustion, when in reality laboratory staff were asked to evaluate an unusual fire scene rather than endorse a paranormal explanation. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Mary Reeser Mary Reeser Reports often highlighted the remarkable preservation of the apartment while minimising the documented damage around the chair and floor area. [St Pete Catalyst] stpetecatalyst.com St Pete Catalyst Vintage St. Pete: The strange case of Mary Reeser St Pete Catalyst Vintage St. Pete: The strange case of Mary Reeser The disputed \"teacup sized skull\" became one of the case's most repeated details despite later criticism from fire researchers who questioned the original description. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser March 31, 2026 — Mary Hardy Reeser (March 8, 1884 – July 2, 1951) of St. Petersburg, Florida, was a woman whose unusual death by burning... Published: March 31, 2026 Some later accounts implied that investigators found no possible ignition source, even though Reeser's smoking habit formed a central part of the official reconstruction. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion"},{"title":"Faherty Verdict","url":"/faherty-verdict/","description":"Michael Faherty's inquest shows how an official spontaneous combustion verdict can mark investigative uncertainty rather than prove internal ignition.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Faherty Verdict What Did the Faherty Verdict Really Prove? Faherty Verdict Faherty Verdict What Did the Faherty Verdict Really Prove? Michael Faherty's inquest shows how an official spontaneous combustion verdict can mark investigative uncertainty rather than prove internal ignition. Michael Faherty's inquest shows how an official spontaneous combustion verdict can mark investigative uncertainty rather than prove internal ignition. event time window The Galway fire scene and inquest finding Why a coroner's verdict is not a mechanism How the fireplace detail complicates the story forteana combustion faherty verdict Introduction The death of Michael Faherty in Galway, Ireland, is frequently cited as the strongest modern example of an official finding of spontaneous human combustion . That description is accurate only in a limited legal sense. At the 2011 inquest, West Galway coroner Dr Ciarán McLoughlin recorded that Faherty's death fitted the category of spontaneous human combustion because investigators could not identify an adequate explanation after ruling out several obvious possibilities. The verdict did not establish that a person can ignite from within, nor did it resolve the physical mechanism of the fire. Instead, the case illustrates how a coroner's verdict can express the limits of an investigation when available evidence cannot confidently support a more conventional conclusion. [The Irish Times] irishtimes.com court finds pensioner s death in fire caused by spontaneous combustion 1.606542 Mr Faherty (76), originally from Connemara, died...Read more... The Galway fire scene and the inquest finding Michael Faherty, aged 76, died in his home at Clareview Park, Ballybane, on 22 December 2010. Emergency responders found his badly burned body in the sitting room, lying on his back with his head nearest an open fireplace. According to evidence presented at the inquest, the fire damage was unusually localised. The principal damage was to Faherty's body, the floor beneath him and the ceiling directly above, while the remainder of the house suffered mainly smoke damage. No evidence of forced entry or foul play was identified, and investigators reported finding no accelerants. [The Irish Times] irishtimes.com court finds pensioner s death in fire caused by spontaneous combustion 1.606542 Mr Faherty (76), originally from Connemara, died...Read more... Assistant Chief Fire Officer Gerry O'Malley told the inquest that the fire service did not believe the fire burning in the fireplace had directly caused the fatal blaze. Garda investigator Gerard O'Callaghan likewise described an unusual scene that he had not previously encountered. Faced with these findings, Dr McLoughlin concluded: \"This fire was thoroughly investigated and I'm left with the conclusion that this fits into the category of spontaneous human combustion, for which there is no adequate explanation.\" He also noted that it was the first such verdict he had returned in 25 years as a coroner. The ruling attracted international attention precisely because an official legal authority had used the controversial term rather than avoiding it. [The Guardian] theguardian.com irish pensioner killed spontaneous combustion The GuardianSpontaneous combustion killed Irish pensioner, inquest rules23 Sept 2011 — The West Galway coroner, Kieran McLoughlin, said t... Samozapłon ciała. Prawda czy mit? Channel: OdwróconaLekcja Open on YouTube Why a coroner's verdict is not a mechanism The unusual wording of the verdict is often misunderstood. A coroner's role is to determine who died, and when, where and, so far as the evidence allows, how they died. That legal function does not require proving a scientific mechanism if the available evidence is incomplete. In Faherty's case, the post mortem examination could not establish the precise cause of death because of the extensive burns. The absence of accelerants or obvious criminal involvement narrowed the range of explanations but did not identify how ignition began. The verdict therefore reflected investigative uncertainty rather than experimental confirmation that spontaneous human combustion exists as a recognised physical process. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect This distinction is important because later reporting often shortened the story to \"a coroner proved spontaneous human combustion.\" That is not what the inquest established. The legal conclusion acknowledged that investigators had exhausted the explanations they considered available without reaching a conventional answer. Whether the fire ultimately began from an unnoticed ember, a dropped ignition source or another accidental cause remained unresolved. [Center for Inquiry] centerforinquiry.org Center for Inquiry Irish “Spontaneous Human Combustion”? Blarney!27 Sept 2011 —... coroner has proclaimed “spontaneous combustion” as the cause of death of a 76 year old man, Michael Faherty, w... How the fireplace detail complicates the story One of the most debated aspects of the case is the open fireplace. The inquest heard that the fire in the grate was not considered to be the direct source of the blaze. This finding has often been repeated as evidence supporting spontaneous human combustion. However, the fireplace's presence also complicates the case because it demonstrates that an external ignition source existed within the room, even if investigators concluded that the visible fire itself had not spread in an ordinary way. [The Irish Times] irishtimes.com court finds pensioner s death in fire caused by spontaneous combustion 1.606542 Mr Faherty (76), originally from Connemara, died...Read more... Fire investigators and forensic commentators have noted that proving a fireplace did not directly ignite surrounding furnishings is different from proving that no ember, spark or secondary ignition occurred. Once a body has burned extensively, much of the evidence capable of identifying the original ignition point may be destroyed. As a result, excluding one pathway does not necessarily exclude every accidental mechanism. [Center for Inquiry] centerforinquiry.org Center for Inquiry Irish “Spontaneous Human Combustion”? Blarney!27 Sept 2011 —... coroner has proclaimed “spontaneous combustion” as the cause of death of a 76 year old man, Michael Faherty, w... The coroner himself reportedly consulted forensic pathology literature before reaching his conclusion. Ironically, some of the literature he referenced also observed that many alleged spontaneous human combustion cases historically occurred near fireplaces or chimneys, highlighting how often ordinary ignition sources appear in otherwise puzzling incidents. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Is Spontaneous Human Combustion A Real Phenomenon? Channel: Heart Starts Pounding w/ Kaelyn Moore Open on YouTube The Fire from Within: Spontaneous Human Combustion Channel: Dark Histories Open on YouTube What the Faherty verdict really proved The lasting significance of the Faherty case lies less in fire science than in legal interpretation. It demonstrated that: an official inquest can adopt an unusual descriptive category when conventional explanations cannot be established; such a verdict does not amount to scientific proof of a new combustion mechanism; the absence of accelerants, obvious foul play or an immediately identifiable ignition source is not equivalent to evidence that no external ignition occurred; unusual burn patterns can leave investigators with genuine uncertainty even after thorough forensic examination. [The Guardian+2The Irish Times] theguardian.com irish pensioner killed spontaneous combustion The GuardianSpontaneous combustion killed Irish pensioner, inquest rules23 Sept 2011 — The West Galway coroner, Kieran McLoughlin, said t... For that reason, the Faherty case continues to occupy an unusual position in discussions of spontaneous human combustion. Supporters point to it as the only modern instance in which a coroner explicitly recorded such a finding. Skeptics point to the same record as an illustration of the difference between an unexplained death and evidence for an unexplained physical phenomenon. The verdict remains historically important because it documented uncertainty in an exceptional fire investigation—not because it settled the long running scientific debate over spontaneous human combustion. [The Guardian+2Center for Inquiry] theguardian.com irish pensioner killed spontaneous combustion The GuardianSpontaneous combustion killed Irish pensioner, inquest rules23 Sept 2011 — The West Galway coroner, Kieran McLoughlin, said t... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Did the Faherty Verdict Really Prove?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Encourages evidence based interpretation of unusual verdicts. See on Amazon Book Spontaneous human combustion By Jenny Randles, Peter Hough"},{"title":"Missing Spark","url":"/missing-spark/","description":"The hardest question is often not whether a body can keep burning, but whether a cigarette, ember, candle, or match vanished before investigators arrived.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Missing Spark Where Did the First Flame Go? Missing Spark Missing Spark Where Did the First Flame Go? The hardest question is often not whether a body can keep burning, but whether a cigarette, ember, candle, or match vanished before investigators arrived. The hardest question is often not whether a body can keep burning, but whether a cigarette, ember, candle, or match vanished before investigators arrived. dataset evidence Why small ignition sources disappear Common fire sources in classic cases How absence of evidence becomes a paranormal claim forteana combustion missing spark Introduction One of the strongest arguments used in support of spontaneous human combustion is deceptively simple: investigators never found the thing that started the fire. If no match, cigarette, candle or ember remains, some writers conclude that no ordinary ignition existed. Fire investigators, however, approach the problem differently. The earliest stage of a fire is often the least well preserved because the origin is exactly where burning is most intense. A missing ignition source is therefore a genuine evidential problem, but not, by itself, evidence that a fire began without an external flame. Modern forensic reviews of unusual burn deaths consistently distinguish between an unidentified ignition source and no ignition source , arguing that these are not equivalent conclusions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Spontaneous human combustion in the light of the 21st...by V Koljonen · 2012 · Cited by 12 — \"spontaneous human combustion\" refers... Why small ignition sources disappear The object that starts a fatal fire is frequently tiny, fragile and highly combustible. A cigarette burns to ash within minutes. A wooden match may be entirely consumed. A candle can melt into unrecognisable wax residue or be destroyed when surrounding furnishings collapse. An ember from a fireplace may leave no distinctive trace once mixed with general fire debris. Several features of unusual burn deaths make this especially important: The fire may smoulder for hours before being discovered. The origin is often beneath or immediately beside the victim, where later combustion is most destructive. Fire suppression, rescue attempts and scene disturbance can destroy delicate evidence. Heat, collapsing furniture and falling debris can move or obscure the original ignition point. Consequently, investigators often reconstruct the probable origin from burn patterns, witness testimony, smoking habits, electrical appliances and the surrounding environment rather than expecting to recover the original spark itself. This reconstruction is standard fire investigation practice and does not require the physical ignition object to survive. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion What started the Palisades fire? Channel: Howtown Open on YouTube Common fire sources in classic cases Many famous cases associated with spontaneous human combustion share a notable feature: plausible ignition sources were present even when none could later be definitively identified. Smoking materials. Cigarettes are repeatedly considered the most likely accidental ignition source in historical cases involving elderly or sleeping victims. A cigarette dropped onto loose clothing, blankets or upholstered furniture can smoulder before developing into open flame, especially if the victim is incapacitated. By the time investigators arrive, the cigarette itself has long disappeared. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Fireplaces and open hearths. Older reports frequently involve homes heated by coal fires, wood burning stoves or open fireplaces. Escaping embers may ignite clothing without leaving an obvious trace once the body and nearby furnishings have burned. In discussing alleged spontaneous combustion cases, forensic literature has repeatedly noted the recurring proximity of fireplaces or chimneys. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Candles, oil lamps and matches. Before electric lighting became universal, naked flames were common in homes. Historical accounts often mention candles or lamps somewhere within the room even if no direct causal link could later be established. Because these objects themselves burn, investigators may have little physical evidence remaining. Electrical faults. In more recent cases, electrical appliances sometimes become possible candidates. Even when forensic examination excludes wiring as the primary cause, the initial suspicion illustrates how difficult origin reconstruction can become after prolonged burning. Mary Reeser and the problem of the vanished cigarette The death of Mary Reeser remains the best known illustration of how an apparently missing ignition source can evolve into a paranormal narrative. When Reeser was found in 1951, much of her body and the chair in which she had been sitting had been consumed, while much of the apartment remained comparatively intact. Investigators ruled out accelerants and lightning, but they could not identify the precise object that initiated the fire. This uncertainty became central to later claims of spontaneous combustion. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Death of Mary Reeser Death of Mary Reeser Yet contemporaneous investigation never concluded that there had been no ignition source. Instead, investigators considered Reeser's known smoking habit and the possibility that a dropped cigarette ignited her highly flammable clothing before the slow \" wick effect \" sustained the fire. By the time the body was discovered many hours later, any cigarette would almost certainly have been completely consumed. The missing cigarette therefore became consistent with the proposed reconstruction rather than evidence against it. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion Why investigators rarely expect to recover the first flame Fire investigation is fundamentally an exercise in reconstruction rather than direct observation . Investigators often work backwards from what survived instead of forwards from what has vanished. Several reasons explain why certainty is frequently impossible: Fire destroys the very area where ignition occurs. Victims may have been alone, leaving no eyewitnesses. Medical events such as strokes or heart attacks can prevent escape, making the ignition appear more mysterious than it was. Household fires may continue smouldering for many hours before discovery. As a result, coroners and fire investigators commonly describe the ignition as undetermined or most probably caused by a particular source rather than claiming absolute certainty. This cautious language reflects evidential limits rather than endorsement of paranormal explanations. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Spontaneous human combustion in the light of the 21st...by V Koljonen · 2012 · Cited by 12 — \"spontaneous human combustion\" refers... Launch of Fire Investigations by BRE and FIUK – Workplace Fire Demonstration & Investigation Channel: BRE Group Open on YouTube How absence of evidence becomes a paranormal claim The logical leap in many spontaneous human combustion accounts occurs when a missing ignition source is transformed into proof that no ignition source existed. Investigators such as Joe Nickell and John Fischer, after reviewing numerous historical cases, argued that published retellings often omitted mundane details including smoking, candles, fireplaces or lamps that were documented in original records. As these ordinary details disappeared from later versions, the cases appeared increasingly inexplicable. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Spontaneous human combustion Spontaneous human combustion This pattern reflects a common evidential problem in historical mysteries. The less complete the surviving documentation, the easier it becomes for uncertainty to be interpreted as positive evidence for extraordinary causes. In unusual burn deaths, the destruction caused by the fire itself naturally enlarges those evidential gaps. 3 Unexplained Mysteries: Spontaneous Combustion & The 11,000 Ft Fall Weird or What? Channel: BingeWorthy Documentaries Open on YouTube What the missing spark really tells us The absence of an identifiable ignition source is one of the most intriguing features of classic spontaneous human combustion claims, but it is also one of the weakest foundations for concluding that combustion began internally. Modern forensic understanding of the wick effect addresses how a body can continue burning for many hours after ignition, not how ignition begins. In a minority of cases, the precise initiating flame genuinely cannot be identified. That reflects the destructive nature of fire and the limitations of post event reconstruction rather than evidence that no ordinary ignition ever occurred. The \"missing spark\" therefore remains an unresolved detail in some individual investigations, but it is not regarded by forensic science as positive"},{"title":"Wick Effect","url":"/wick-effect/","description":"The wick effect explains how clothing and body fat can feed a slow fire that looks far stranger than an ordinary ignition event.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Combustion","level":3,"text":"Wick Effect Can a Body Burn Like a Candle? Wick Effect Wick Effect Can a Body Burn Like a Candle? The wick effect explains how clothing and body fat can feed a slow fire that looks far stranger than an ordinary ignition event. The wick effect explains how clothing and body fat can feed a slow fire that looks far stranger than an ordinary ignition event. mechanism How the wick effect starts after ignition Why the torso burns while the room survives What the mechanism can and cannot prove forteana combustion wick effect Introduction The wick effect is the leading forensic explanation for many deaths that have been described as spontaneous human combustion . Rather than proposing that a human body ignites from within, it suggests that a small external flame—such as a dropped cigarette, an ember from a fireplace, or another local ignition source—starts a slow burning fire. Once clothing is damaged and body fat begins to melt, the fat is absorbed into the fabric, allowing the clothing to function much like the wick of a candle while the fat becomes the fuel. The resulting fire can burn for many hours, destroying much of the torso while leaving large parts of the room relatively untouched. This mechanism does not explain every unusual burning death, but it provides a well supported physical process that accounts for many of the features once regarded as mysterious. [PubMed+2PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Spontaneous human combustion in the light of the 21st...by V Koljonen · 2012 · Cited by 11 — The purpose of this review article wa... How the wick effect starts after ignition The crucial point is that the wick effect is not an ignition mechanism. It requires an existing flame. A typical sequence proposed by forensic investigators is: 1. A small external flame ignites clothing, bedding or another combustible material. 2. The heat burns through the skin and begins melting the layer of fat beneath it. 3. Liquid fat seeps into surrounding clothing or fabric. 4. The fabric absorbs the fat and burns slowly, continuously drawing more melted fat into the fire, much like a candle wick draws molten wax. 5. The process continues for hours, sometimes until the available fuel is exhausted or the fire extinguishes itself. This is very different from an ordinary house fire with high flames spreading rapidly through a room. Instead, the fire is usually localised, relatively small and sustained over a long period. Because the burning rate is slow, much of the heat is concentrated around the body rather than being released quickly into the surrounding space. [PubMed+2PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov 95 kg) was wrapped in a cotton blanket and placed on a carpet covered plywood panel.Read more... The body's composition helps explain why this process is possible. Human fat contains a large amount of chemical energy, while clothing provides an absorbent material capable of transporting that liquefied fat to the combustion zone. Water rich muscle tissue is comparatively difficult to ignite directly, so the fire depends on this combination of melted fat and fabric rather than on flesh simply bursting into flames. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Experiments in the combustibility of the human bodyby AM Christensen · 2002 · Cited by 68 — This paper provides possible explanatio... Can You Spontaneously Combust? Earth Science Channel: BBC Earth Science Open on YouTube Why the torso burns while the room survives Perhaps the most striking feature of alleged spontaneous human combustion cases is the contrast between severe destruction of the body and limited damage to the surrounding room. Wick effect( endnote 1 \"Endnote 1\") fect offers several reasons for this pattern. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect First, most body fat is concentrated around the torso, abdomen and upper thighs. These areas therefore provide the greatest fuel supply. The lower legs, hands and feet contain much less fat, so they frequently survive relatively intact. Second, the flames produced by a wick effect fire are often modest in height. The fire releases heat over many hours instead of generating the intense, room filling flames associated with rapidly developing house fires. Nearby furniture may be scorched or partially burned, but objects even a short distance away can remain largely undamaged. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained combustion of bodies: some observationsby JD DeHaan · 2012 · Cited by 33 — Two intact, unembalmed human cadavers were exp... Third, hot gases rise. Most heat travels upward rather than sideways, producing damage above the body while limiting the spread across the room. Investigators often report soot deposits, greasy residues from vaporised fat and localised heat damage on ceilings or nearby walls without evidence of a fully developed room fire. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained combustion of bodies: some observationsby JD DeHaan · 2012 · Cited by 33 — Two intact, unembalmed human cadavers were exp... This behaviour can seem counter intuitive because people naturally assume that a fire capable of consuming much of a human body must also engulf the entire room. In reality, fire behaviour depends not only on temperature but also on fuel availability, oxygen supply, duration and how heat is distributed. Experimental evidence for the human candle model The wick effect is not based solely on theory. Several experimental studies have explored whether a body can burn in this way. Early laboratory demonstrations showed that cloth wrapped around animal or human fat could sustain slow combustion once adequately ignited. These tests illustrated that fat alone is difficult to ignite, but fat absorbed into fabric can maintain a prolonged flame resembling a candle. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Wick effect Wick effect Larger scale experiments strengthened this interpretation. One widely discussed demonstration used a pig carcass because pigs have body composition and fat distribution broadly comparable to humans. Wrapped in a blanket and given an external ignition source, the carcass burned slowly for many hours. The surrounding furnished room suffered surprisingly limited damage despite extensive destruction of the carcass itself. The experiment reproduced several characteristics commonly reported in alleged spontaneous human combustion cases, including localised burning and incomplete fire spread. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov 95 kg) was wrapped in a cotton blanket and placed on a carpet covered plywood panel.Read more... Subsequent forensic studies using donated human remains have likewise shown that sustained, body fed combustion can occur under accidental fire conditions. These experiments documented relatively small fires, limited radiant heat and burning patterns that differed markedly from conventional room fires while still producing extensive destruction of the body. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained combustion of bodies: some observationsby JD DeHaan · 2012 · Cited by 33 — Two intact, unembalmed human cadavers were exp... The Mystery Of Spontaneous Human Combustion Channel: Grunge Open on YouTube What the mechanism can and cannot prove The wick effect explains how an apparently impossible fire can occur without requiring a mysterious internal source of ignition. It accounts for several features repeatedly seen in unusual burning deaths: Extensive destruction of the torso. Preservation of the lower legs or extremities. Limited spread of fire through the room. Oily or greasy residues around the scene. Long duration, low flame combustion. These observations are consistent with established principles of fire science rather than requiring unknown biological or physical processes. [PubMed+2PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained combustion of bodies: some observationsby JD DeHaan · 2012 · Cited by 33 — Two intact, unembalmed human cadavers were exp... However, the mechanism also has clear limits. It does not identify the original ignition source in a particular death. If the initial flame has been destroyed or left little trace, investigators may never determine whether it was a cigarette, fireplace ember, electrical fault or another accidental source. It also does not explain every fire involving a human body. Cases involving accelerants, deliberate burning, explosions or extensive structural fires require different forensic analysis. Each death must be investigated individually rather than assuming every unusual burning pattern results from the same process. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sustained combustion of bodies: some observationsby JD DeHaan · 2012 · Cited by 33 — Two intact, unembalmed human cadavers were exp... Finally, the wick effect does not prove that every historical claim of spontaneous human combustion has been fully resolved. Some older reports remain poorly documented or impossible to reconstruct because evidence was lost or investigative standards were limited. What it demonstrates is that a natural mechanism"},{"title":"UFO Reports","url":"/ufo-reports/","description":"UFO reports sit between astronomy, aircraft recognition, secrecy, psychology, and modern myth-making.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"UFO Reports Why UFO Reports Are Perfect Fortean Cases UFO Reports UFO Reports Why UFO Reports Are Perfect Fortean Cases UFO reports sit between astronomy, aircraft recognition, secrecy, psychology, and modern myth making. UFO reports sit between astronomy, aircraft recognition, secrecy, psychology, and modern myth making. dataset evidence What a sighting report contains Misidentification and secrecy Why unresolved does not mean alien forteana ufo reports Introduction UFO sightings are perfect Fortean cases because they are not simply “mysteries in the sky”. They are evidence problems: brief observations, partial sensor traces, classified aviation activity, cultural expectation, honest witness error, and sometimes genuinely unresolved records all mixed together. A UFO, or the newer official term UAP, means an unidentified aerial or anomalous phenomenon; it does not mean an alien craft. That distinction is the whole point. The strongest UFO material is interesting precisely because it sits between ordinary explanation and incomplete evidence, not because “unidentified” automatically points to extraterrestrial visitors. Modern investigations repeatedly find the same pattern. Many reports resolve into balloons, drones, birds, satellites, aircraft, astronomical objects, camera artefacts or sensor effects. A smaller residue remains unresolved, often because the data are too thin, not because the object has been shown to be extraordinary. NASA’s 2023 independent UAP study found no evidence of extraterrestrial origin and stressed that the field needs better, standardised data rather than more dramatic speculation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAll domain Anomaly Resolution Office. The cavitation is likely a sensor... no conclusive eviden... What a sighting report contains A UFO report is usually a bundle of claims rather than a single fact. At minimum, it may include who saw the object, where and when it appeared, how long it lasted, what direction it moved, what shape or colour it seemed to have, whether it made noise, whether other witnesses were present, and whether a camera, radar, infrared sensor or flight instrument recorded anything. Each of those details can help, but each can also mislead. This makes UFO evidence unusually fragile. A witness may be sincere and still misjudge distance, height or speed. A bright object that seems to “hover” may be a planet, an aircraft approaching head on, a balloon drifting at altitude, or a satellite reflecting sunlight. A fast object in a video may be genuinely fast, or it may look fast because the camera is moving, zoomed in, stabilised, or lacking a reliable distance reference. The US intelligence community’s 2021 preliminary assessment named sensor limitations and sociocultural stigma as collection obstacles, meaning reports are often both technically incomplete and socially awkward to file. [DNI] dni.gov Preliminary Assessment: Unidentified Aerial Phenomena Sociocultural stigmas and sensor limitations remain obstacles to collecting data on UAP. Although some technical...Read more... The best reports therefore are not necessarily the strangest sounding ones. They are the ones with independent measurements that can be cross checked: exact time, location, weather , aircraft traffic, satellite tracks, radar data, sensor metadata, original image files, and multiple witnesses who were not simply repeating one another. Without those anchors, the report may preserve a real experience but still fail as strong evidence. A Fortean reading keeps both halves in view. It does not dismiss the witness as foolish, and it does not treat the witness’s interpretation as proof. It asks what the report can actually support. Misidentification is not the same as fabrication One reason UFO reports endure is that many of them come from serious people: pilots, police officers, military personnel, scientists, air traffic observers and large groups of ordinary witnesses. That makes them socially compelling. But credibility of character is not the same as accuracy of identification. A trained observer can be excellent at recognising many aviation hazards while still being surprised by an unusual lighting angle, an unfamiliar satellite train, a balloon, a classified aircraft, or an optical effect. Historical official studies show this pattern clearly. The US Air Force’s Project Blue Book investigated 12,618 sightings from 1947 to 1969; 701 remained “unidentified” when the project ended. The Air Force did not present that residue as proof of alien craft, and the project’s closure followed the University of Colorado’s Condon study and a National Academy of Sciences review. [U.S. Air Force] af.mil Open source on af.mil. That number is still Forteanly important. A residue of unexplained cases shows that official classification does not eliminate uncertainty. But it also shows why percentages can mislead. A case may remain unidentified because the object was extraordinary, because the report was incomplete, because no one collected the right data at the time, or because the relevant information was classified or unavailable. Modern reporting produces a similar structure. The US Department of Defence said AARO received 757 UAP reports for its May 2023 to June 2024 reporting cycle, with many resolved or queued for closure and some requiring more investigation. AARO officials said resolved cases commonly turned out to be balloons, birds, drones, satellites and aircraft, while only a small percentage were potentially anomalous. [U.S. Department of War] war.gov Open source on war.gov. (https://www.war.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/3964824/department of defense releases the annual report on unidentified anomalous phen/) Secrecy makes ordinary objects look extraordinary UFO sightings are unusually vulnerable to secrecy because the sky is full of things people are not meant to recognise: military tests, reconnaissance aircraft, drones, training exercises, missile launches, sensor platforms and surveillance balloons. A witness can see something real, and the official explanation may be vague not because the object is alien, but because the underlying programme is secret. Cold War history matters here. US Navy historical material, drawing on intelligence histories, notes that U 2 and later OXCART flights accounted for more than half of all UFO reports during parts of the late 1950s and 1960s. The aircraft flew at altitudes unfamiliar to many observers, sometimes catching sunlight in ways that made them look strange from the ground. [Naval History and Heritage Command] history.navy.mil u2s ufos and operation blue book u2s ufos and operation blue book That is a classic Fortean trap. The public sees an anomaly; officials cannot tell the whole truth; rumours fill the gap; later disclosure shows that at least some “impossible” sightings were secret but human technology. This does not prove that every unresolved case is a spy plane, but it explains why secrecy can manufacture mystery even when nothing paranormal is present. Recent balloon incidents show the same mechanism in a different form. When governments increase surveillance sensitivity, they find more objects. Wired reported after the 2023 Chinese surveillance balloon episode that broader searches and changed detection thresholds can produce more UFO reports without requiring a sudden increase in exotic phenomena. [WIRED] wired.com The More You Look for Spy Balloons, the More UFOs You'll Find The More You Look for Spy Balloons, the More UFOs You'll Find UAP Independent Study Event Post Meeting Media Teleconference (May 31, 2023) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 30.3K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 1 hour 2 minutes Open on YouTube Why cameras and sensors do not settle the question Modern UFO debate often treats video as decisive: if there is footage, the event must be stronger than testimony. In practice, video can create a new evidence problem. A camera records light, motion and contrast, but interpretation depends on distance, lens behaviour, frame rate, stabilisation, sensor type, compression, platform movement and metadata. Without those, a clip can look more informative than it is. The best known recent military videos illustrate the problem. Some Navy UAP footage became famous because it appeared to show objects moving in striking ways, yet later analysis has focused on perspective, parallax, sensor rotation and missing range information. In 2024, AARO’s director told Congress that the well known “ GOFAST ” object appeared fast partly because of parallax, assessing that it was much higher and slower than many viewers had assumed, while still not necessarily identifying the exact object. [New York Post] nypost.com Open source on nypost.com. That distinction is crucial. Explaining a motion illusion does not always identify the object. It may only downgrade the claim from “physically astonishing craft” to “unknown object seen under"},{"title":"AARO Reports","url":"/aaro-reports/","description":"Recent AARO reports show that many cases become balloons, drones or birds, while a smaller residue remains data-poor and unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"AARO Reports What Official UFO Reports Really Resolve AARO Reports AARO Reports What Official UFO Reports Really Resolve Recent AARO reports show that many cases become balloons, drones or birds, while a smaller residue remains data poor and unresolved. Recent AARO reports show that many cases become balloons, drones or birds, while a smaller residue remains data poor and unresolved. policy intervention How modern UAP reports are collected Common explanations behind closed cases Why unresolved cases still need better data forteana ufo reports aaro reports Introduction The United States' All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) was created to investigate reports of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) using a structured, evidence based process rather than relying on anecdote or speculation. Within the broader Fortean question of UFO sightings, AARO is significant because it demonstrates both what official investigations can achieve and where they reach their limits. Its reports consistently show that many initially puzzling sightings are eventually identified as ordinary objects such as balloons, drones, birds, satellites or conventional aircraft. At the same time, a substantial number of reports remain unresolved—not because they have been shown to represent extraordinary technology, but because the available evidence is too incomplete to support a confident conclusion. This distinction is central to understanding modern official UFO investigations. [U.S. Department of War+2AARO] media.defense.gov FY24 CONSOLIDATED ANNUAL REPORT ON UAP 508 Department of WarFiscal Year 2024 Consolidated Annual Report on...14 Nov 2024 — AARO resolved 118 cases during the reporting period, all... How modern UAP reports are collected AARO was established within the US Department of Defense to receive reports from military personnel and other government sources, analyse available evidence and determine whether unusual observations pose concerns for aviation safety, intelligence or national security. Unlike earlier programmes that often relied heavily on witness testimony, AARO attempts to combine multiple forms of evidence whenever possible, including: eyewitness accounts; radar tracking; infrared and electro optical imagery; flight and sensor data; intelligence reporting; environmental information such as weather conditions. The office's emphasis is not simply on identifying unusual objects but on reconstructing events through independently verifiable evidence. A sighting supported by several calibrated sensors is generally more valuable than one based only on memory or a single mobile phone recording. AARO also encourages standardised reporting procedures so that future cases contain enough information for meaningful analysis. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO HomeUAP Cases. Official UAP Imagery · UAP Case Resolution Reports · UAP Reporting Trends · UAP Records · Congressional/Press Pro... This reflects a broader shift away from treating UFO reports primarily as isolated mysteries. Instead, the goal is to transform scattered observations into comparable datasets that can be analysed using consistent methods. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Common explanations behind closed cases One of AARO's most important contributions is demonstrating how apparently mysterious sightings often become ordinary once sufficient evidence is assembled. Its Fiscal Year 2024 report states that it resolved 118 cases during the reporting period, with every resolved case attributed to commonplace causes including balloons, birds and unmanned aerial systems. Officials have also described satellites, conventional aircraft and other familiar aerial objects as recurring explanations across their wider archive. U.S. Department of War+2U.S. Department of War [media.defense.gov] media.defense.gov FY24 CONSOLIDATED ANNUAL REPORT ON UAP 508 Department of WarFiscal Year 2024 Consolidated Annual Report on...14 Nov 2024 — AARO resolved 118 cases during the reporting period, all... These outcomes illustrate several recurring features of UFO investigations: Limited perspective. Objects viewed without reliable distance references can appear to move faster or behave more unusually than they actually do. Sensor ambiguity. Infrared cameras, targeting systems and optical sensors may produce misleading impressions when viewed outside their operational context. Changing airspace. Civilian drones, satellite constellations and specialised aircraft have increased the number of unfamiliar objects that observers encounter. Incomplete reporting. Early descriptions often omit information that later proves essential for identification. For Fortean studies, these resolutions are important because they show that \"unidentified\" is usually a temporary category rather than evidence for an exotic explanation. A report may begin as puzzling but become routine once additional evidence is obtained. Why unresolved cases still need better data The unresolved portion of AARO's archive is often misunderstood. Remaining unexplained does not imply confirmation of extraordinary technology, nor does it necessarily indicate that investigators have encountered something beyond current science. Instead, many cases remain open because the available information is insufficient for reliable attribution. Missing radar data, incomplete video recordings, uncertain locations, absent metadata or delayed reporting can prevent investigators from reconstructing what actually occurred. The 2024 report notes that hundreds of reports remain active because they lack enough scientific or technical information for definitive analysis. Officials have emphasised that these cases may later be resolved if additional evidence becomes available. [U.S. Department of War+2DefenseScoop] war.gov dod examining unidentified anomalous phenomena Department of WarDOD Examining Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena14 Nov 2024 —... AARO has discovered no verifiable evidence of extraterre... This distinction matters because an unresolved case is fundamentally different from a demonstrated anomaly. In scientific and intelligence work, \"insufficient evidence\" is not equivalent to \"evidence of something extraordinary\". AARO has also identified practical obstacles that reduce resolution rates, including: delayed submission of reports; [twz.com] twz.com highly anticipated government report on ufos thin on new evidence Highly Anticipated Government Report On UFOs Very Thin...12 Jan 2023 — The new report, which has been delayed for months now, offers lit... inconsistent sensor coverage; classified systems whose data cannot always be widely shared; varying reporting standards between organisations; incomplete preservation of original recordings and metadata. These limitations mean that many investigations end not with certainty but with a judgement about the quality of the available evidence. Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube What official investigations can—and cannot—establish AARO's reports are careful about the claims they make. The office has repeatedly stated that it has found no verified evidence of extraterrestrial technology, non human intelligence or breakthrough aerospace capabilities within the cases it has resolved. At the same time, it avoids claiming that every report has already been fully explained. [AARO+2U.S. Department of War] aaro.mil AARO HomeUAP Cases. Official UAP Imagery · UAP Case Resolution Reports · UAP Reporting Trends · UAP Records · Congressional/Press Pro... This middle position is sometimes unsatisfying to both sceptics and enthusiasts. Those expecting definitive debunking may overlook the genuine uncertainty created by incomplete evidence. Those expecting confirmation of extraordinary craft may mistake unresolved cases for positive evidence rather than recognising that uncertainty can arise simply because the data are inadequate. NASA's independent UAP study reached a closely related conclusion. Rather than proposing dramatic explanations, it argued that progress depends on obtaining higher quality, standardised observations through better sensors , better reporting practices and more transparent data sharing. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportFor any scientific analysis purposes, including UAP analysis, it is essential that the data used... UAP FILES Footage Released of Object Tracked over Europe in 2022 Channel: Media Magik Entertainment · Views: 3.3K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Why AARO matters for Fortean evidence From a Fortean perspective, AARO's greatest contribution is methodological rather than sensational. It demonstrates that official investigation can reduce uncertainty, but it also exposes the practical"},{"title":"GOFAST","url":"/gofast/","description":"The GOFAST debate shows how a military video can look extraordinary while missing the range data needed to judge speed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"GOFAST Did GOFAST Really Move That Fast? GOFAST GOFAST Did GOFAST Really Move That Fast? The GOFAST debate shows how a military video can look extraordinary while missing the range data needed to judge speed. The GOFAST debate shows how a military video can look extraordinary while missing the range data needed to judge speed. dataset evidence What the famous video appears to show How parallax changes the speed claim Why downgrading motion is not full identification forteana ufo reports gofast Introduction The GOFAST video is one of the best known military UFO recordings released by the US Department of Defense, and it is often presented as showing an object racing just above the ocean at extraordinary speed. A closer technical examination, however, reaches a more cautious conclusion. The video itself does not contain enough information to determine the object's true speed directly because it lacks an independent measurement of its distance from the observing aircraft. That missing range information allows a well known visual effect—motion parallax—to create the impression of extreme velocity. Rather than demonstrating impossible flight, the GOFAST debate has become an important example of how incomplete sensor data can make an ordinary object appear extraordinary. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution AARO GoFast Case ResolutionFebruary 24, 2025 — 6 Feb 2025 — In January 2015, a U.S. Navy F/A 18F pilot recorded an object using a For... Published: February 24, 2025 Within discussions of Fortean evidence, GOFAST illustrates a recurring problem: a striking image can survive long after its strongest interpretation has weakened. The video remains genuinely unidentified in the limited sense that the object's exact identity has not been established, but that is different from demonstrating unusual or impossible performance. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution AARO GoFast Case ResolutionFebruary 24, 2025 — 6 Feb 2025 — In January 2015, a U.S. Navy F/A 18F pilot recorded an object using a For... Published: February 24, 2025 What the famous video appears to show GOFAST was recorded in January 2015 by the targeting pod of a US Navy F/A 18F operating off the east coast of Florida. The infrared video shows a small object apparently streaking low across the sea while aircrew express surprise after the targeting system locks onto it. When the footage became public in 2018 and was officially released by the Department of Defense in 2020, many viewers concluded that it depicted an object travelling at remarkable speed just above the waves. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution AARO GoFast Case ResolutionFebruary 24, 2025 — 6 Feb 2025 — In January 2015, a U.S. Navy F/A 18F pilot recorded an object using a For... Published: February 24, 2025 That interpretation is understandable because the camera view creates several strong visual impressions at once: the object appears to skim close to the ocean surface; the background water seems to rush beneath it; the observing aircraft is itself travelling at high speed; and the infrared image provides almost no visual clues about distance or size. Human observers naturally combine these cues into a mental picture of a fast moving object. The crucial question is whether that picture matches the underlying geometry. Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick West Channel: Cool Worlds · Views: 365.2K · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube How parallax changes the speed claim Motion parallax is the apparent movement of an object caused by the observer's own motion rather than the object's actual speed. Everyone experiences it when travelling in a car: nearby trees seem to race past, while distant hills hardly appear to move. Exactly the same principle applies to an aircraft travelling hundreds of knots while tracking a distant airborne object. If the target is much farther away than viewers intuitively assume, much of the apparent sideways motion comes from the fighter jet's movement rather than from the object itself. Without knowing the object's true range, apparent motion across the camera frame cannot be converted reliably into real speed. [PBS] pbs.org 3 ways scientists use math to help debunk UFO videosSemeter helped analyze one particular video called \"GO FAST,\" where an object seen... Independent analysts pointed out this possibility soon after the video's release by reconstructing the geometry from the targeting pod's displayed data. Their calculations suggested that the object could be travelling at speeds broadly consistent with winds at altitude rather than exhibiting extraordinary acceleration. [Metabunk] metabunk.org go fast footage from tom delonges to the stars academy bird balloon.9569 Metabunk\"GO FAST\" Footage from Tom DeLonge's To The Stars...9 Mar 2018 — 25,000 feet altitude, the speed of the object could be anything... This explanation gained additional weight when the US Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) published its own analysis. Using geospatial modelling, trigonometry and historical weather data, AARO concluded with high confidence that the object was not close to the sea surface but was instead approximately 13,000 feet above it. Depending on its direction relative to the wind, the object's airspeed was estimated to fall within a range compatible with an ordinary wind driven object rather than anomalous flight. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution AARO GoFast Case ResolutionFebruary 24, 2025 — 6 Feb 2025 — In January 2015, a U.S. Navy F/A 18F pilot recorded an object using a For... Published: February 24, 2025 Why the missing range matters The GOFAST recording includes telemetry such as aircraft attitude, viewing angle and targeting information, but it does not provide a direct, continuously reliable measurement of the target's distance that would settle the speed question on its own. That limitation is fundamental. To calculate an object's true velocity, investigators normally require at least: an accurate distance to the target; changes in that distance over time, or independent radar tracking; precise aircraft position and motion; and synchronised sensor information. Without these measurements, a video can only show apparent angular motion across the camera's field of view. The same angular movement can correspond to dramatically different real world speeds depending on how far away the object actually is. [PBS] pbs.org 3 ways scientists use math to help debunk UFO videosSemeter helped analyze one particular video called \"GO FAST,\" where an object seen... For this reason, a visually dramatic infrared clip is not, by itself, sufficient evidence for extraordinary performance. Quantum physicists REanalyze GOFAST UFO video footage released by the US Navy and Pentagon Channel: NYU Quantum Technology Lab · Views: 22.0K · Uploaded: June 2021 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Why downgrading the motion is not full identification An important point often lost in public discussion is that reducing the estimated speed does not automatically identify the object. AARO's published assessment does not claim to know exactly what GOFAST depicts. Instead, it concludes that the available evidence shows no anomalous performance characteristics. The office judged the object to be moving in a manner broadly compatible with atmospheric conditions while acknowledging that its precise identity could not be established from the available data alone. [AARO] aaro.mil Go Fast Case Resolution AARO GoFast Case ResolutionFebruary 24, 2025 — 6 Feb 2025 — In January 2015, a U.S. Navy F/A 18F pilot recorded an object using a For... Published: February 24, 2025 Several conventional possibilities have therefore remained under discussion, including: a balloon or other wind driven object; a small airborne object with limited propulsion; [youtube.com] youtube.com Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick WestThe pilots said it was a 40 foot tic tac object with no visible form of propulsion. Mi... another ordinary aerial object that cannot be distinguished because of the infrared imagery and missing range information. These possibilities differ in detail, but they share an important feature: none requires extreme acceleration or impossible flight characteristics. What GOFAST shows about UFO evidence The GOFAST case has become influential not because it conclusively explains a UFO, but because it demonstrates how easily compelling military imagery can be over interpreted when critical measurements are missing. From a Fortean perspective, the case highlights several broader lessons: military sensors are sophisticated but still produce ambiguous data when essential measurements are absent; eyewitness reactions by trained personnel do not substitute for geometric analysis; \"unidentified\" describes the limits of available evidence rather than proving an extraordinary origin; and resolving one claimed anomaly, such as apparent speed, is different"},{"title":"Pilot Reports","url":"/pilot-reports/","description":"Pilot sightings can be compelling without being conclusive, especially when shared viewing geometry turns ordinary objects into surprises.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"Pilot Reports Why Pilots Can Misread Strange Lights Pilot Reports Pilot Reports Why Pilots Can Misread Strange Lights Pilot sightings can be compelling without being conclusive, especially when shared viewing geometry turns ordinary objects into surprises. Pilot sightings can be compelling without being conclusive, especially when shared viewing geometry turns ordinary objects into surprises. population context What pilot testimony can and cannot prove Shared viewing angles and group misidentification What records make an aviation sighting stronger forteana ufo reports pilot reports Introduction Pilot UFO reports occupy a distinctive place in Fortean discussions because they come from people trained to observe the sky. A pilot's testimony deserves careful attention, particularly when it concerns an unexpected object that could affect flight safety. At the same time, aviation experience does not eliminate the limits of human perception. Distance, darkness, unusual viewing angles, atmospheric conditions and incomplete contextual information can all produce sincere but mistaken identifications. This balance is important. Pilot reports can be stronger than many casual eyewitness accounts because they often include precise times, routes, radio communications and, occasionally, radar or cockpit sensor data. Yet history also shows that even experienced aviators sometimes misidentify satellites, balloons, astronomical objects or ordinary aircraft under unusual conditions. The evidential value therefore lies not in the witness's profession alone, but in how well the observation can be independently verified. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceUAPJune 16, 2022 — 9 Jun 2022 — A study team to examine unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAPs) – that is, observations of ev... Published: June 16, 2022 What pilot testimony can and cannot prove Pilots are generally skilled at recognising conventional aircraft, judging weather conditions and maintaining situational awareness. They are also accustomed to noticing anything unusual that could present a hazard. That makes their reports valuable starting points for investigation. However, pilots are not immune to the same perceptual constraints that affect everyone else. Many aerial observations occur: at night, with few distance references; through cockpit windscreens that can introduce reflections; while both observer and object are moving; over featureless terrain or open ocean where scale is difficult to judge. An object that appears to accelerate dramatically may instead be distant and moving slowly while the observing aircraft changes course. Likewise, a bright light may seem stationary when approaching almost directly head on, only to appear to move abruptly once the viewing geometry changes. Aviation safety training explicitly warns pilots about visual illusions and the need to rely on instruments rather than perception alone under difficult conditions. [Federal Aviation Administration+2FAASafety] faa.gov Spatial D Federal Aviation AdministrationSpatial Disorientation English18 Sept 2024 — False Visual Reference Illusions may cause you to orient your... For Fortean evidence, this means pilot expertise increases the credibility of the observation itself—\"I saw something unusual\"—without automatically validating the interpretation—\"it was an extraordinary craft.\" ‘Most UFO sightings are explainable but…’: Pentagon AARO chief tells Senate Armed Services Committee Channel: The Economic Times · Views: 15.6K · Uploaded: November 2024 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Shared viewing angles and group misidentification One reason pilot UFO reports often appear especially persuasive is that several crews may report the same object independently. Multiple witnesses certainly strengthen the claim that something was seen, but they do not necessarily establish that the object was extraordinary. Commercial aircraft frequently travel similar routes at similar altitudes. If several crews share nearly identical viewing geometry, they may all experience the same optical conditions. A recent example illustrates this well. In August 2022, five pilots on two commercial flights over the Pacific independently reported an unusual formation of lights that initially appeared anomalous. Subsequent reconstruction using aircraft position data and orbital information concluded that the phenomenon was consistent with a newly launched train of Starlink satellites reflecting sunlight at an unexpected angle. The researchers argued that improving pilots' awareness of satellite constellations could reduce future misidentifications without dismissing the sincerity of the witnesses. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... This case demonstrates an important evidential principle: agreement among trained observers confirms that an unusual visual event occurred, but not necessarily that its first interpretation was correct. Why unfamiliar lights become convincing Several well understood visual effects can make ordinary aerial objects appear extraordinary. Autokinesis. A stationary light viewed against a dark background can appear to drift or manoeuvre after prolonged fixation. Pilots are specifically trained to avoid staring continuously at isolated lights because this illusion has contributed to aviation accidents. [AOPA] aopa.org Tricked by IllusionsAutokinesis: At night, a stationary dim light against a dark background will appear to move if a pilot visually f... Loss of distance cues. At cruising altitude, especially over water or at night, there may be almost no reliable reference points. Without known distances, apparent speed and size become difficult to estimate. [Flight Safety Foundation] flightsafety.org in the dark Flight Safety FoundationIn the Dark20 Jan 2017 — Most night vision–related accidents, however, occur because pilots misperceive visual cu... Head on geometry. Aircraft approaching nearly head on can appear almost motionless despite travelling hundreds of kilometres per hour. A subsequent turn can make them seem to accelerate suddenly, even though their speed has not changed. Satellite reflections. Modern satellite constellations can produce unfamiliar patterns of bright lights or sudden flashes. Pilots who trained before the widespread deployment of large satellite constellations may encounter visual phenomena that simply did not exist earlier in their careers. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... None of these mechanisms implies that every pilot report has an ordinary explanation. They simply illustrate why honest observers can produce detailed descriptions that nevertheless misidentify the source. Modern investigations favour evidence over reputation Recent official UAP investigations have increasingly focused on measurable evidence rather than witness status alone. NASA's independent UAP study concluded that improving data collection is more valuable than relying on anecdotal reports, however sincere. Better sensor calibration, synchronised observations and complete metadata allow investigators to distinguish genuinely unusual cases from those arising through incomplete information. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceUAPJune 16, 2022 — 9 Jun 2022 — A study team to examine unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAPs) – that is, observations of ev... Published: June 16, 2022 Similarly, the US Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has resolved numerous reported UAP cases as balloons, birds, drones or other conventional objects after detailed review of sensor information. In some well publicised military pilot cases, additional analysis found that apparently extraordinary motion resulted from perspective, infrared imaging characteristics or limited observational data rather than exotic flight behaviour. [TWZ] twz.com eglin afb pilot likely saw a lighting balloon not a ufo pentagon concludes Eglin AFB Pilot Likely Saw A Lighting Balloon, Not A UFO...24 Apr 2024 — AARO assessed that based on information provided by the airc... These findings do not suggest that pilots fabricate reports. Instead, they show that careful technical analysis frequently changes the initial interpretation. What records make an aviation sighting stronger Within the broader evidence problems surrounding UFO reports, aviation cases become significantly more persuasive when they include information that can be independently checked. The strongest cases typically combine several forms of evidence: precise time and geographic position; flight path and altitude; cockpit voice or air traffic control recordings; weather and astronomical conditions; original sensor or radar data; observations from independent aircraft at different locations; photographs or video with complete"},{"title":"Blue Book","url":"/blue-book-75a50c/","description":"Project Blue Book's 701 unresolved cases matter because they preserve uncertainty without proving alien visitation.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"Blue Book What Did Blue Book Leave Unexplained? Blue Book Blue Book What Did Blue Book Leave Unexplained? Project Blue Book's 701 unresolved cases matter because they preserve uncertainty without proving alien visitation. Project Blue Book's 701 unresolved cases matter because they preserve uncertainty without proving alien visitation. dataset evidence What Project Blue Book counted Why unidentified did not mean extraterrestrial How old official archives shape modern UFO belief forteana ufo reports blue book Introduction Project Blue Book remains one of the most important official UFO investigations because it produced a result that is often misunderstood. Between 1947 and 1969, the US Air Force examined 12,618 reported UFO sightings. Most were eventually attributed to conventional causes, but 701 cases remained officially classified as \"unidentified\". Those unresolved reports have become a central feature of Fortean discussions, not because they prove extraterrestrial visitors, but because they demonstrate that even a large government investigation could not explain every case with the evidence available. The significance of Blue Book therefore lies less in what it claimed than in what it could not confidently determine. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying ObjectsFrom 1947 to 1969, a total of 12, 618 sightings were reported to Project... Within the broader study of UFO sightings as evidence problems, Project Blue Book illustrates an important distinction: an unresolved case is not the same as a confirmed extraordinary event. The surviving unidentified residue represents uncertainty preserved in official records rather than proof of any particular explanation. What Project Blue Book counted Project Blue Book was the final and longest running public US Air Force UFO investigation, operating from 1952 until its closure in December 1969 while incorporating reports dating back to 1947. Its stated objectives were straightforward: determine whether reported UFOs posed a threat to national security; [facebook.com] facebook.com US Air Force's Project Blue Book UFO studiesProject Blue Book had two goals: To determine if UFOs were a threat to national security, and... analyse reports using scientific and technical methods where possible; identify conventional explanations whenever sufficient evidence existed. [U.S. Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book... reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The d... Investigators received reports from military personnel, commercial pilots, police officers, scientists and members of the public. Each file varied considerably in quality. Some contained multiple witness statements, photographs or radar information, while others consisted only of brief descriptions with little supporting evidence. By the project's conclusion: 12,618 reports had been catalogued. Approximately 94% received conventional explanations. 701 remained classified as unidentified after investigation. [U.S. Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book... reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The d... That final figure has become one of the most frequently quoted statistics in UFO literature. However, it is easy to misunderstand what the category actually meant. 'Project Blue Book' Ep. 1 Official Clip UFO SHOWTIME Documentary Series Channel: SHOWTIME · Views: 290.8K · Uploaded: August 2021 · Length: 1 minute 11 seconds Open on YouTube Why \"unidentified\" did not mean extraterrestrial The Air Force consistently emphasised that an unidentified classification described the outcome of an investigation, not the nature of the object itself. A report entered the unidentified category when investigators lacked sufficient evidence to assign a confident conventional explanation. An unresolved file could reflect many different situations: observations lasting only a few seconds; contradictory witness testimony; poor weather or darkness; missing radar or photographic evidence; insufficient technical information to distinguish between competing explanations. In other words, \"unidentified\" often meant that the available evidence had reached its limit. When Project Blue Book ended, the Air Force stated three principal conclusions: [archives.gov] archives.gov National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying ObjectsFrom 1947 to 1969, a total of 12, 618 sightings were reported to Project... no investigated UFO demonstrated a threat to US national security; no case showed technology beyond contemporary scientific knowledge; no evidence indicated extraterrestrial vehicles. [U.S. Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book... reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The d... These conclusions did not eliminate uncertainty. Instead, they separated uncertainty from extraordinary claims. From a Fortean perspective, that distinction remains crucial. The residue preserves genuine unanswered questions without automatically supporting alien hypotheses. Why the unidentified residue still matters The 701 unresolved cases remain historically significant because they illustrate how incomplete evidence accumulates in large observational datasets. Several features make the residue valuable for later researchers. First, the cases survived a formal investigative process rather than casual speculation. They were examined by an official programme with access to military reporting channels, even if its methods and resources varied over time. Second, the unresolved files remind researchers that complex observational problems rarely produce perfect closure. Weather, instrumentation, human perception and incomplete documentation can all prevent definitive identification. Third, the residue provides an opportunity to revisit historical reports using modern knowledge. Some sightings once considered mysterious have later been linked to previously classified aircraft programmes, improved astronomical understanding or better atmospheric modelling. Others remain unresolved simply because too little original information survives to support any firm conclusion. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book For historians of science and Fortean researchers alike, the unidentified category is therefore best understood as a measure of evidential limitation rather than mystery alone. Project Blue Book Channel: Daily Dose Documentary · Views: 397 · Uploaded: October 2021 · Length: 3 minutes 36 seconds Open on YouTube How the Condon Report shaped Blue Book's ending Project Blue Book did not close because every report had been solved. Instead, it ended after the publication of the University of Colorado's Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects , commonly called the Condon Report. Edward Condon's research team concluded that more than two decades of UFO investigation had contributed little to scientific knowledge and found no persuasive evidence that UFO reports represented visitors from other worlds. The National Academy of Sciences reviewed the report and agreed that further large scale investigation was unlikely to produce significant scientific advances. Those assessments formed the basis for the Air Force's decision to terminate Blue Book in December 1969. [U.S. Air Force+2National Geographic] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue Book... reported to Project Blue Book, 701 remained \"unidentified.\" The d... Critics, including astronomer J. Allen Hynek, argued that some individual cases deserved more careful scientific attention than they had received. Those criticisms helped keep interest in the unidentified residue alive long after the programme itself had ended. Even so, criticism of the investigation's methods did not itself demonstrate that the unresolved cases represented extraordinary phenomena. Project Blue Book: America's Obsession with UFOs Channel: Origins OSU · Views: 1.6K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube How official archives continue to shape UFO belief Blue Book's archives remain influential because they are unusually accessible. The National Archives preserves thousands of case files, allowing historians, sceptics, UFO researchers and the general public to examine many of the original documents themselves. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying ObjectsFrom 1947 to 1969, a total of 12, 618 sightings were reported to Project... This openness has had two contrasting effects. For sceptical researchers, the files show how many apparently mysterious reports"},{"title":"Secret Tests","url":"/secret-tests/","description":"U-2 and OXCART history shows how real military secrecy can turn human technology into lasting UFO mystery.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"Secret Tests When Spy Planes Became UFOs Secret Tests Secret Tests When Spy Planes Became UFOs U 2 and OXCART history shows how real military secrecy can turn human technology into lasting UFO mystery. U 2 and OXCART history shows how real military secrecy can turn human technology into lasting UFO mystery. event time window Why high altitude aircraft surprised observers How secrecy blocked clear explanations What later disclosure teaches UFO investigators forteana ufo reports secret tests Introduction Secret military aircraft provide one of the clearest historical examples of how genuine government secrecy can create enduring UFO mysteries without requiring an extraterrestrial explanation. During the Cold War, reconnaissance aircraft such as the U 2 and Lockheed A 12 OXCART routinely flew at altitudes and speeds unfamiliar to both the public and many professional aviators. Witnesses accurately reported seeing unusual objects, yet officials often could not explain what they had observed without revealing classified programmes. The result was a peculiar evidential trap: authentic sightings of real but secret technology became part of the UFO record, while official silence encouraged speculation that ranged far beyond the available evidence. Declassified documents now allow historians to reconstruct this process in considerable detail, making it one of the strongest examples of how secrecy itself can distort the interpretation of unexplained aerial phenomena. [CIA+2sgp.fas.org] cia.gov U 2S, UFOS, AND OPERATION BLUE BOOKto the letter writers the true cause of the UFO sightings. U 2 and later. OXCART flights accounted... Why high altitude aircraft surprised observers The U 2 entered testing in the mid 1950s with a capability that was extraordinary for its era. While commercial and military aircraft commonly operated around 20,000 feet, the U 2 routinely exceeded 60,000 feet. Most pilots, air traffic controllers and civilians simply had no reason to expect an aircraft at such altitudes. This created several observational effects that naturally appeared mysterious: Aircraft flying far above expected operating heights appeared to move unusually slowly because of their great distance. Sunlight could illuminate a high altitude aircraft long after lower aircraft and observers had entered darkness, making the object appear as a bright glowing point in an otherwise dark sky. Lack of familiar reference points made estimates of size, speed and distance highly unreliable. The aircraft often left unusual looking condensation trails that behaved differently from those produced by lower flying aircraft. The CIA's official history notes that many reports occurred during evening flights when airline crews travelling west saw the U 2 still reflecting sunlight above the Earth's shadow. To witnesses unfamiliar with the optical geometry involved, the aircraft appeared as a brilliant object performing inexplicable feats. [CIA] cia.gov U 2S, UFOS, AND OPERATION BLUE BOOKto the letter writers the true cause of the UFO sightings. U 2 and later. OXCART flights accounted... The later Lockheed A 12 OXCART, developed as the successor to the U 2, added further confusion. Flying faster and higher than almost any publicly acknowledged aircraft, it introduced flight characteristics that again exceeded normal expectations. Because the entire programme remained deeply classified, even experienced observers had no obvious frame of reference for what they were seeing. [National Security Archive] nsarchive2.gwu.edu National Security Archive The Secret History of the U 2 National Security ArchiveThe Secret History of the U 2 and Area 5115 Aug 2013 — CIA history reveals newly declassified material on U 2... Project Blue Book Channel: Daily Dose Documentary · Views: 397 · Uploaded: October 2021 · Length: 3 minutes 36 seconds Open on YouTube How secrecy blocked clear explanations The most revealing aspect of the historical record is not that secret aircraft generated UFO reports , but that investigators often knew—or strongly suspected—the real explanation while being unable to say so publicly. Project Blue Book investigators regularly compared reported sightings against classified flight schedules through secure channels. When timings matched U 2 or OXCART operations, investigators could internally resolve many cases. However, security restrictions prevented them from informing witnesses that they had observed a classified reconnaissance aircraft. Instead, responses were often vague, incomplete or attributed to less sensitive explanations. [Naval History and Heritage Command] history.navy.mil From Gregory W.Read more Naval History and Heritage CommandU 2s, UFOs, and Operation Blue Book24 Jan 2024 — U 2 and later OXCART flights accounted for more than o... This produced several unintended consequences: Witnesses remained convinced they had seen something unexplained. Official explanations sometimes appeared evasive or implausible. Public suspicion grew that authorities were concealing important information. Later conspiracy theories interpreted genuine secrecy about aircraft programmes as evidence of secrecy about extraterrestrial craft. In other words, the government really was withholding information—but not necessarily the information that many members of the public imagined. This distinction is important in Fortean studies because it separates documented institutional secrecy from unsupported claims about what that secrecy concealed. The CIA's \"half of all UFO reports\" claim One frequently quoted statement deserves careful treatment. A CIA historical review concluded that U 2 and later OXCART flights \"accounted for more than one half of all UFO reports during the late 1950s and most of the 1960s.\" The statement has been widely repeated in media coverage following the publication of declassified documents. [CIA+2The Guardian] cia.gov U 2S, UFOS, AND OPERATION BLUE BOOKto the letter writers the true cause of the UFO sightings. U 2 and later. OXCART flights accounted... However, historians caution against reading this figure too broadly. The estimate refers to reports generated during particular periods in which these aircraft were actively flying, not to every UFO report across all decades or countries. Furthermore, not every unexplained sighting involved secret aircraft, and the CIA's estimate itself has been questioned by some researchers who argue that the available reporting data do not support such a precise proportion. [Space] space.com 28256 ufo sightings cia u2 aircraft CIA About UFOs of the 1950s and '60s: 'It Was Us'Jan 14, 2015 — The CIA tweet has sparked its own UFO flap: Several analysts dispute... The historical significance therefore lies less in the exact percentage than in the demonstrated mechanism: classified aviation programmes unquestionably generated substantial numbers of sincere UFO reports. The Secrets of Area 51 What the Government Doesn’t Want You to Know Channel: The Mysteria Archive Open on YouTube Area 51 and the growth of myth The later public fascination with Area 51 illustrates how classified aviation can acquire an almost supernatural reputation. For decades, the Nevada test facility was officially unacknowledged while hosting development of the U 2, A 12 OXCART and later advanced aircraft. Because local residents occasionally observed unfamiliar aircraft performing unusual manoeuvres without official explanation, rumours naturally accumulated around the site. [CIA+2National Security Archive] cia.gov ask molly what really went on at area 51 Ask Molly: What really went on at Area 51?1 Jul 2022 — Area 51 (also known as “Groom Lake,” “Paradise Ranch,” and several other names)... Once secrecy becomes established, every subsequent unexplained observation is interpreted within that existing framework. New aircraft tests reinforce old rumours, while the inability to discuss classified programmes publicly encourages increasingly elaborate alternative explanations. The eventual acknowledgement of Area 51's historical role demonstrated that extraordinary secrecy did exist—but primarily around reconnaissance technology rather than recovered alien spacecraft. That disclosure explains part of the mythology while leaving separate claims to be evaluated on their own evidence. What later disclosure teaches UFO investigators The declassification of Cold War reconnaissance programmes offers several methodological lessons for evaluating UFO evidence today. First, an unidentified object is not necessarily an extraordinary object. The witnesses who reported high altitude spy aircraft often described exactly what they genuinely observed. Their mistake lay in identifying the cause, not in inventing the observation itself. Second, official denials require careful interpretation. Governments may withhold information for conventional national security reasons rather than because they possess evidence of non human technology. Historical records show that both"},{"title":"Starlink","url":"/starlink/","description":"Starlink trains show how a changing sky can create sincere multi-witness UFO reports before public recognition catches up.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UFO Reports","level":3,"text":"Starlink When Satellites Look Like UFOs Starlink Starlink When Satellites Look Like UFOs Starlink trains show how a changing sky can create sincere multi witness UFO reports before public recognition catches up. Starlink trains show how a changing sky can create sincere multi witness UFO reports before public recognition catches up. mechanism Why satellite trains look unfamiliar from aircraft The 2022 Pacific pilot sighting reconstruction Better warnings for pilots and skywatchers forteana ufo reports starlink Introduction Starlink satellite trains have become one of the clearest modern examples of how sincere UFO reports can arise from unfamiliar technology rather than deception or extraordinary phenomena. Since SpaceX began launching large batches of low Earth orbit satellites in 2019, observers have encountered patterns of moving lights that did not previously exist in the night sky. For people who have never seen a newly launched satellite train—or who encounter unusual reflections from satellites already spreading into operational orbits—the appearance can seem unlike any familiar aircraft or astronomical object. The result is a growing number of genuine, often multi witness reports that initially appear mysterious but can later be reconstructed using satellite tracking data and orbital modelling. This is an important Fortean lesson: the sky itself has changed faster than public recognition has. Why satellite trains look unfamiliar from aircraft The classic Starlink \"train\" appears shortly after launch, when dozens of satellites occupy nearly the same orbit and follow one another in an evenly spaced line. Seen shortly after sunset or before sunrise, they remain illuminated by the Sun while the ground below is already dark. To an observer, the satellites resemble a silent procession of bright stars moving together across the sky rather than a single satellite or aircraft. [Space] space.com Starlink satellite train: how to see and track it in the night skyJune 1, 2026 — 1 Jun 2026 — The Starlink satellite train is often... Published: June 1, 2026 From the cockpit of an airliner, the geometry becomes even more unfamiliar. Pilots cruise above much of the atmosphere, extending the distance to the visible horizon and allowing them to see satellites under lighting conditions that people on the ground cannot. Depending on the viewing angle, satellites may brighten suddenly, fade away, or appear in repeated sequences as sunlight reflects from their surfaces. These changing reflections can create the impression of manoeuvring lights despite the satellites following ordinary orbital paths. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Another complication is scale. Human observers naturally judge an object's motion using assumptions developed from aircraft and weather balloons. Satellites hundreds of kilometres away violate those assumptions. Without a reliable distance reference, apparent speed, spacing and altitude become difficult to estimate accurately, even for experienced observers. Train of Satellites in the Sky over California Looks like UFOs SpaceX Starlink Train 2023 Channel: Attractions 360° · Views: 50.2K · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 2 minutes 7 seconds Open on YouTube The 2022 Pacific pilot sighting reconstruction One of the best documented examples occurred on 10 August 2022 over the Pacific Ocean. Pilots on two commercial flights independently reported unusual lights, with five flight crew members observing the phenomenon. The event was significant because it involved multiple experienced witnesses, photographs and video rather than a single anecdotal account. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Researchers later reconstructed the event using several independent datasets: Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) records showing the aircraft positions. Two line element (TLE) orbital data describing the locations of recently launched Starlink satellites. Computer simulations recreating the pilots' viewpoint. The original photographs and video recorded from the cockpit. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... The reconstruction found that a Starlink launch earlier the same day had produced a train of satellites whose changing reflection geometry matched both the timing and appearance of the reported lights. What initially looked anomalous from the cockpit could be reproduced using known satellite positions and expected sunlight reflections. The case therefore became an example of how apparently extraordinary sightings can sometimes be resolved only after combining aviation data with modern space tracking information. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Importantly, this does not imply that the pilots were careless or incompetent. Rather, it illustrates that they encountered an entirely new visual phenomenon created by thousands of recently deployed satellites—a situation for which traditional aviation experience offered little preparation. Why some Starlink sightings do not resemble a simple train Public attention often focuses on the straight \"string of pearls\" appearance visible soon after launch, but this is only one stage in the satellites' evolution. As satellites climb towards their operational orbits, their spacing changes and their reflective surfaces present different angles to the Sun. Under certain geometries, observers—particularly airline crews looking towards the hidden Sun below the horizon—may see repeated flashes or groups of lights that brighten and fade instead of a continuous line. These effects have sometimes been described by pilots as \"racetrack\" patterns because individual reflections appear repeatedly in roughly the same region of sky. [Sky & Telescope+2arXiv] skyandtelescope.org starlink flares can fool anyone even airline pilots Sky & TelescopeStarlink Flares Can Fool Anyone — Even Airline Pilots10 Jun 2024 — The flare from numerous Starlink satellites, launched b... The important point is that the unusual appearance comes from changing illumination rather than unusual motion. The satellites remain in predictable orbits, while the reflected sunlight reaching the observer changes continuously as both the satellites and the aircraft move. Line of lights in the sky: Starlink satellite train seen over south central Pa. Channel: FOX43 News · Views: 55.2K · Uploaded: June 2023 · Length: 1 minute 43 seconds Open on YouTube Better warnings for pilots and skywatchers The growing number of satellite constellations has led researchers to argue that aviation needs improved \"space situational awareness\"—practical information about visible satellites similar to the weather or air traffic information already available to crews. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Suggested improvements include: Predictive software showing when recently launched satellite trains will be visible from an aircraft's position. Better integration of satellite ephemerides into aviation planning tools. Educational material explaining how satellite reflections differ from aircraft navigation lights. Public skywatching resources that identify upcoming Starlink passes before they generate unnecessary reports. [arXiv+2Space] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... These measures would not eliminate every unidentified sighting, but they would reduce confusion created by entirely predictable orbital events. What Starlink teaches about UFO evidence From a Fortean perspective, Starlink demonstrates how technological change alters the background against which extraordinary claims are judged. A phenomenon that genuinely did not exist before 2019 rapidly became common enough to generate reports from ordinary observers, amateur astronomers, police officers and commercial pilots alike. [SYFY] syfy.com spacex satellites are now being mistaken for ufos and making astronomers rage SpaceX satellites are now being mistaken for UFOs—and...1 Jan 2020 — Montana residents recently reported sightings of what they thou... The episode reinforces several broader lessons about UFO evidence: Multiple witnesses do not guarantee an exotic explanation. Independent observers can all experience the same unfamiliar but ordinary phenomenon. Observer expertise has limits. Pilots are highly trained to identify aviation hazards, not necessarily the latest orbital deployment strategies of"},{"title":"UF Ology","url":"/uf-ology/","description":"UFOlogy studies aerial mysteries directly, while Forteana treats them alongside other unstable reports.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"UF Ology Is UF Ology Part of Forteana? UF Ology UF Ology Is UF Ology Part of Forteana? UFOlogy studies aerial mysteries directly, while Forteana treats them alongside other unstable reports. UFOlogy studies aerial mysteries directly, while Forteana treats them alongside other unstable reports. population context Aerial sightings as anomaly reports Specialist UFO evidence traditions What Forteana adds forteana uf ology Introduction UFOlogy can be part of Forteana, but it is not simply the same thing. UFOlogy narrows attention to aerial sightings, sensor tracks, alleged craft, close encounters and official files. Forteana treats those reports as one branch of a wider anomaly culture, alongside strange falls, cryptids, apparitions , poltergeist claims and other unstable reports that sit awkwardly between evidence, folklore, misperception, hoax, secrecy and genuine uncertainty. The difference matters because the two traditions ask different questions: UFOlogy often asks “what was in the sky?”, while Forteana also asks “why does this kind of report recur, mutate and survive?” Forteana, in that sense, does not dilute UFO reports . It changes the frame, putting them beside older sky wonders and other “damned” facts rather than forcing every case into either debunking or extraterrestrial belief. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Why UFOlogy became its own evidence tradition Modern UFOlogy grew around a relatively specific problem: people, sometimes including pilots, radar operators, police officers and military personnel, reported things in the sky that they could not identify. That focus produced its own evidence habits. A UFO case is typically sorted by date, location, witness position, duration, angular size, weather , aircraft traffic, radar correlation, photographic material, physical traces and possible conventional explanations. The United States Air Force’s Project Blue Book records, now declassified and held by the National Archives, show how official UFO investigation became a document heavy practice rather than just a body of strange stories. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National Archives Project BLUE BOOK This is where UFOlogy differs most sharply from broad Forteana . It has often tried to behave like a specialist inquiry into aerospace anomalies. J. Allen Hynek’s 1972 book The UFO Experience : A Scientific Inquiry helped codify that ambition, especially through classifications such as nocturnal lights, daylight discs, radar visual cases and close encounters. Those categories did not prove that UFOs were alien craft; they gave investigators a shared way to separate weak distant light reports from closer, richer, more puzzling claims. [Internet Archive] archive.org Open source on archive.org. Official interest also shaped UFOlogy’s tone. The post war flying saucer era unfolded in a world of radar, jet aircraft, nuclear anxiety, Cold War secrecy and later satellite surveillance. UFO reports could therefore be treated not only as folklore or curiosity, but as possible aviation safety, intelligence or defence matters. That institutional setting continues in the newer language of unidentified anomalous phenomena, or UAP. NASA’s 2023 independent study team report framed the issue as a data problem that could benefit from better collection methods, transparent scientific practice and careful use of existing observational systems. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP Science UAP Where Forteana changes the question A Fortean reading begins from a broader premise: some reports are not just unexplained objects, but unstable events that cross boundaries between observation, interpretation and culture. Forteana is interested in why a claim is rejected, why it persists, how witnesses describe it, how authorities handle it, and how old motifs reappear in new dress. The Society for Psychical Research’s Psi Encyclopedia describes Fortean phenomena as “scientific heresies” in Fort’s sense: claims or events rejected by mainstream science but repeatedly collected by anomaly minded writers and investigators. [Psi Encyclopedia] psi encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Psi Encyclopedia Forteana – Psi Encyclopedia Applied to UFOs, that means a sighting is not reduced to “aliens or nothing”. A Fortean approach can hold several possibilities together: a misidentified aircraft, a planet seen under unusual conditions, a classified military platform, an atmospheric phenomenon, a social rumour, a hoax, a sincere but distorted memory, or a genuinely unresolved report. The point is not to believe all of them equally. It is to resist premature closure, especially when both believers and sceptics are tempted to make a case simpler than the evidence allows. This is why Forteana often feels less tidy than UFOlogy. A specialist UFO investigator may want a clean case file with a probable answer. A Fortean writer may be equally interested in the messy residue: the newspaper language, the witness’s metaphors, the mismatch between official explanation and public trust, and the way one case becomes part of a larger pattern of strange reports. That broader interest is useful, but it can also frustrate people who want a final technical verdict. UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Aerial sightings as anomaly reports The strongest overlap between UFOlogy and Forteana lies in the raw report: someone sees something in the sky, describes it as extraordinary, and the explanation remains uncertain or disputed. Such reports long predate the phrase “flying saucer”. Fortean and folkloric writers have repeatedly noted that people have described strange lights, celestial signs, aerial vessels and sky beings using the language available in their own time. Jacques Vallée’s Passport to Magonia became influential because it argued that modern UFO stories should be compared with older traditions of supernatural visitation, not treated only as reports of spacecraft. [Google Books] books.google.com Books Passport to Magonia: from Folklore to Flying Saucers Books Passport to Magonia: from Folklore to Flying Saucers This comparative move is central to “UFOlogy versus Forteana”. UFOlogy tends to preserve the aerial object as the main unit of study. Forteana notices that the object is often only one part of the experience. A witness may report paralysis, missing time, telepathic messages, animal reactions, burns, religious dread, technological imagery or absurd details that do not fit neat aerospace expectations. These elements may be dismissed by technical investigators as noise, but Forteana asks whether the noise is part of the historical pattern. The comparison is not an argument that medieval fairies were aliens or that modern UFOs are folklore in a dismissive sense. It is a warning about interpretation. A person in 1897 might describe an impossible “airship”; a person in 1952 might describe a flying saucer; a person today might describe a drone, orb or UAP. The words change with technology, media and expectation. Forteana is interested in that translation process. Specialist UFO evidence traditions UFOlogy’s great strength is that it developed practical filters for better and worse cases. It asks whether a report has multiple witnesses, independent observation, radar or infrared data, photographs, physical traces, contemporaneous notes, and enough environmental information to test mundane explanations. This evidential discipline separates it from loose strange story collecting. Recent official UAP material shows both the value and the limits of this approach. AARO’s official imagery page includes cases resolved as balloons, birds or prosaic aircraft, alongside cases still marked unresolved or undergoing analysis. Several unresolved entries remain limited not because they obviously show exotic technology, but because the available data is insufficient to determine exactly what was seen. [AARO] aaro.mil Official UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... That distinction is important. In UFOlogy, “unidentified” is a status, not a conclusion. Reuters’ summary of AARO’s 2024 historical report noted that US government investigations since the end of the Second World War found no confirmed evidence of extraterrestrial technology, while also acknowledging that some reports remain unsolved and that better data would probably resolve many of them. [Reuters] reuters.com Open source on reuters.com. Modern research groups have tried to respond to this weakness by designing dedicated observation systems rather than relying on after the fact witness accounts. The Galileo Project, for example, has proposed multimodal ground based observatories using optical, infrared, radio and other sensors to"},{"title":"AARO Cases","url":"/aaro-cases/","description":"AARO's public imagery shows why unresolved does not mean extraordinary when birds, balloons and aircraft remain plausible comparisons.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"AARO Cases What Does Unresolved Really Mean? AARO Cases AARO Cases What Does Unresolved Really Mean? AARO's public imagery shows why unresolved does not mean extraordinary when birds, balloons and aircraft remain plausible comparisons. AARO's public imagery shows why unresolved does not mean extraordinary when birds, balloons and aircraft remain plausible comparisons. dataset evidence Resolved images and ordinary explanations Why some cases stay uncertain How Forteana handles doubt without closure forteana uf ology aaro cases Introduction Public imagery released by the U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has become one of the most visible pieces of evidence in modern UAP debate. Yet the most important lesson from these images is not that they reveal extraordinary objects. Rather, they show how difficult it can be to identify distant targets from limited sensor data, and why an \"unresolved\" label is not the same as evidence for something exotic. This distinction sits at the heart of the difference between UFOlogy and a Fortean approach. Instead of treating uncertainty as proof of hidden technologies or extraterrestrial craft, the evidence encourages careful attention to ambiguity, incomplete information and the limits of observation . [AARO+2NASA Science] aaro.mil Official UAP Imagery UAP ImageryForward Looking Infrared video footage of an unidentified object recorded by the aircrew of a U.S. Navy F/A 18F in 2015. \"... Resolved images and ordinary explanations AARO's public image library deliberately mixes unresolved material with cases that have been investigated and explained. This is important because it demonstrates how easily ordinary objects can appear remarkable when viewed through military infrared sensors, stabilised optics or long range cameras. One of the clearest examples is the famous GOFAST infrared video recorded by a U.S. Navy aircraft. For years it circulated as evidence of an object racing just above the ocean. After analysing the aircraft's geometry, sensor data and viewing angles, AARO concluded that the apparent speed was largely a result of parallax: the object was much farther away than many viewers assumed and therefore moving far more slowly than it first appeared. The office did not identify the precise object, but it rejected the dramatic interpretation suggested by the video's appearance. [AARO+2U.S. Department of War] aaro.mil Official UAP Imagery UAP ImageryForward Looking Infrared video footage of an unidentified object recorded by the aircrew of a U.S. Navy F/A 18F in 2015. \"... The broader pattern is even more instructive than any individual case. In its annual reporting and public briefings, AARO has repeatedly stated that it has resolved hundreds of reports as familiar objects, including: birds; balloons; drones; satellites; conventional aircraft. Many additional cases are expected to close in the same way once final reviews are complete. U.S. Department of War+2U.S. Department of War [war.gov] war.gov dr jon kosloski director aaro media roundtable on the fy24 consolidated annual Department of WarDr. Jon Kosloski, Director, AARO, Media Roundtable on the...14 Nov 2024 — AARO has successfully resolved hundreds of ca... This demonstrates an important feature of visual evidence. Human observers naturally interpret isolated images according to expectations. A bright point can appear to manoeuvre because the camera moves. Infrared glare can distort an object's apparent shape. Compression artefacts, autofocus changes and incomplete metadata all make confident identification harder than casual viewing suggests. A striking image is therefore not necessarily a reliable guide to what actually occupied the sky. Pentagon Reveals 757 UAP Sightings With Near Miss Safety Concerns Channel: electron media group, inc. · Views: 6.8K · Uploaded: November 2024 · Length: 34 minutes Open on YouTube Why some cases stay uncertain An unresolved case is often misunderstood as an unexplained phenomenon. AARO uses the term much more narrowly. In official practice, a report may remain unresolved because investigators lack enough reliable information to determine which ordinary explanation is correct. Missing radar tracks, incomplete flight records, poor image quality, short observation times or absent sensor calibration can prevent analysts from distinguishing between several plausible possibilities. The result is uncertainty rather than confirmation of anything extraordinary. [AARO+2NASA Science] aaro.mil Official UAP Imagery UAP ImageryForward Looking Infrared video footage of an unidentified object recorded by the aircrew of a U.S. Navy F/A 18F in 2015. \"... This distinction appears repeatedly in AARO's public releases. Some newly released videos remain unresolved because the available imagery is simply insufficient for a confident conclusion. In one publicly released European case, AARO explicitly stated that the footage did not provide enough information to render a determination. Another infrared video was assessed as depicting a genuine physical object whose behaviour appeared unremarkable, making further analysis unnecessary despite the object's remaining unidentified. [DVIDS] dvidshub.net PR 017, Unresolved UAP Report, Europe 2024The video footage associated with this report is insufficient for AARO to render a determinatio... The office has also emphasised that unresolved cases are frequently retained because additional information may emerge later. New sensor data, witness interviews or intelligence reporting can allow previously uncertain events to be revisited and eventually resolved without changing the underlying phenomenon itself. [DefenseScoop] defensescoop.com uap aaro chief unveils pentagon annual caseload analysis new efforts DefenseScoop'The truly anomalous': New AARO chief unveils Pentagon's...14 Nov 2024 — “AARO has successfully resolved hundreds of cases i... NASA's independent UAP study reached a closely related conclusion. The report argues that current evidence is constrained less by mysterious observations than by inconsistent, poorly calibrated and incomplete datasets. Better instrumentation, standardised reporting and transparent scientific methods are expected to reduce uncertainty over time. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... Why public imagery often carries more meaning than it deserves Images released by government agencies inevitably acquire symbolic significance beyond their evidential value. A short infrared clip can become a cultural icon because viewers fill gaps in the available information with assumptions. Online discussions often freeze around individual frames while overlooking factors such as camera tracking, aircraft motion, sensor limitations or missing contextual data. The visual impression becomes stronger than the evidential foundation. Military imagery is especially vulnerable to this problem because it is rarely collected for scientific investigation. Targeting systems are designed to support operational missions rather than careful measurement of unknown objects. Important contextual information—including precise range, atmospheric conditions or complete sensor metadata—may never have been recorded or may remain classified. As NASA's study observed, these limitations make reliable interpretation difficult even for specialists. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... The consequence is that the public often sees compelling imagery without the accompanying information needed for confident identification. The UFO 'cookbook': How the American government investigates the unexplainable Channel: PBS NewsHour · Views: 17.1K · Uploaded: May 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube UAP FILES Visual Evidence from 3 Cases over Africa (2022 2024) Channel: Media Magik Entertainment · Views: 1.3K · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube How Forteana handles doubt without closure Within a Fortean perspective, unresolved imagery is valuable precisely because it resists premature certainty. A believer may interpret an unidentified object as evidence of non human technology. A determined sceptic may insist that every unresolved image must eventually prove mundane. Forteana occupies a less comfortable position. It accepts that some reports remain uncertain because the evidence is genuinely incomplete, while recognising that history shows many apparently mysterious observations eventually acquire ordinary explanations. AARO's imagery reinforces this cautious approach. The office's own archive contains examples where appearances proved misleading and many more where several conventional explanations remain plausible. Birds, balloons and aircraft continue to account for"},{"title":"Blue Book","url":"/blue-book/","description":"Project Blue Book shows how UFO reports became official case files, with dates, witnesses, radar notes and proposed explanations.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"Blue Book How UFO Cases Became Official Files Blue Book Blue Book How UFO Cases Became Official Files Project Blue Book shows how UFO reports became official case files, with dates, witnesses, radar notes and proposed explanations. Project Blue Book shows how UFO reports became official case files, with dates, witnesses, radar notes and proposed explanations. dataset evidence What Project Blue Book recorded Why file structure shaped UF Ology Where Fortean questions begin forteana uf ology blue book Introduction Project Blue Book is central to modern UFOlogy because it transformed thousands of unusual sighting reports into structured official records rather than leaving them as isolated stories. Between 1952 and 1969, the United States Air Force collected, investigated and archived reports from military personnel, pilots, police officers and members of the public. Each case was typically organised around practical questions: when and where the event occurred, who witnessed it, whether radar or photographs existed, what weather conditions prevailed and whether conventional explanations could account for the observation . This documentary approach became one of the defining habits of UFO investigation and continues to influence researchers today. At the same time, Fortean writers often view the same files as evidence not only of unexplained aerial events but also of how institutions classify, interpret and sometimes simplify unusual experiences. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... What Project Blue Book recorded Project Blue Book inherited earlier Air Force efforts, including Project Sign and Project Grudge, but expanded the emphasis on systematic documentation. The goal was twofold: determine whether reported unidentified flying objects posed a national security threat and analyse the reports using consistent investigative procedures. By the programme's closure in December 1969, it had examined 12,618 reports, of which 701 remained officially classified as \"unidentified\" after investigation. The Air Force concluded that none provided evidence of extraterrestrial vehicles or represented a security threat. [Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue BookThe project, headquartered at Wright Patterson Air Force Base, Ohio... The surviving files reveal a remarkably standardised approach. Depending on the case, investigators attempted to collect: Date, time and precise location. Names and backgrounds of witnesses. Weather and astronomical conditions. Flight paths of known aircraft. Radar observations where available. Photographs, sketches or physical samples. Duration, colour, brightness, shape and apparent motion. Proposed explanations and confidence in those explanations. Many files also contain correspondence between investigators, military commands and scientific advisers, allowing later researchers to reconstruct not only the reported event but also the reasoning that led to a final classification. Today these records are preserved by the United States National Archives, with witness identities generally redacted for privacy. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Why file structure shaped UFOlogy Project Blue Book did more than accumulate reports; it established habits that became standard within UFO research. Instead of treating every sighting as equally persuasive, investigators looked for independent forms of corroboration. A report supported by multiple observers, radar returns or photographs generally attracted more attention than a single unexplained light seen briefly by one witness. This emphasis encouraged later civilian researchers to distinguish between anecdotal reports and cases supported by several kinds of evidence. Scientific adviser J. Allen Hynek also encouraged more systematic classification. Rather than speaking simply of \"flying saucers\", investigators increasingly grouped reports according to observable characteristics such as nocturnal lights, daylight discs, radar visual cases and later the various categories of close encounters. These classifications did not demonstrate what UFOs were, but they helped researchers compare similar reports instead of treating every incident as unique. [Internet Archive] archive.org Open source on archive.org. The Blue Book files also demonstrate the importance placed on elimination. Investigators routinely checked whether Venus, bright stars, weather balloons, aircraft, meteors or atmospheric effects could account for an observation before leaving it unresolved. That habit remains one of the defining methodological features of serious UFO investigation, regardless of whether researchers ultimately favour conventional or unconventional explanations. [Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue BookThe project, headquartered at Wright Patterson Air Force Base, Ohio... What the files reveal about evidence habits One of the most valuable aspects of the archive is not the number of unexplained cases but the visible investigative process. Each case file often shows a progression from initial witness statements through technical assessment to a final judgement. This allows readers to examine not only the conclusion but also the evidence available at each stage. Unlike folklore collections, the documents frequently preserve uncertainty, conflicting testimony and alternative hypotheses within the same file. The files also illustrate several recurring evidence principles: Contemporaneous recording: reports made soon after the event were generally considered more reliable than recollections years later. Multiple independent witnesses: agreement between observers was viewed as stronger than repeated versions of a single account. Instrumental support: radar tracks, photographs or physical measurements received particular attention because they could be compared independently of witness memory . Conventional elimination: investigators generally attempted ordinary explanations before considering a case genuinely unexplained. Explicit uncertainty: some reports remained unresolved not because investigators endorsed extraordinary explanations but because the available evidence was insufficient to identify the object confidently. These practices helped create an investigative culture centred on documentation rather than speculation. Project Blue Book Channel: Daily Dose Documentary · Views: 397 · Uploaded: October 2021 · Length: 3 minutes 36 seconds Open on YouTube The limits of the Blue Book record The existence of an official case file does not automatically make a report reliable. The archive contains cases of widely varying quality, from detailed military observations to brief public letters lacking essential information. Many reports were resolved as astronomical objects, balloons, aircraft or other ordinary phenomena once additional evidence became available. [Air Force] af.mil unidentified flying objects and air force project blue book Air ForceUnidentified Flying Objects and Air Force Project Blue BookThe project, headquartered at Wright Patterson Air Force Base, Ohio... Historians and UFO researchers have also debated whether Blue Book's institutional priorities affected its conclusions. Hynek later criticised aspects of the programme, arguing that some investigations became too focused on finding conventional explanations and that genuinely puzzling cases sometimes received limited follow up. Conversely, sceptical commentators argue that the overwhelming proportion of identified cases demonstrates the effectiveness of ordinary investigation rather than evidence for extraordinary phenomena. These competing interpretations arise from many of the same documents rather than from different bodies of evidence. [Internet Archive] archive.org Open source on archive.org. Another limitation is that the files reflect the technology and procedures available at the time. Radar systems, photographic equipment and communication networks were far less capable than modern sensor systems, meaning many cases inevitably lacked the data needed for definitive conclusions. Project Blue Book Exposed (2020) [Documentary] Channel: CiNENET Full movies in English · Views: 17.1K · Uploaded: January 2025 · Length: 1 hour 4 minutes Open on YouTube Where Fortean questions begin For UFOlogy, Project Blue Book represents an archive of evidence. For Forteana , it is also an archive of interpretation. A Fortean perspective asks why certain reports resisted explanation,"},{"title":"Sky Sensors","url":"/sky-sensors/","description":"Dedicated observatories aim to replace fragile witness memory with calibrated sky data, while still facing ambiguity and false positives.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"Sky Sensors Can Instruments Settle Aerial Mysteries? Sky Sensors Sky Sensors Can Instruments Settle Aerial Mysteries? Dedicated observatories aim to replace fragile witness memory with calibrated sky data, while still facing ambiguity and false positives. Dedicated observatories aim to replace fragile witness memory with calibrated sky data, while still facing ambiguity and false positives. policy intervention Why after the fact reports are weak Multimodal sensors and false positives Where Fortean interpretation still matters forteana uf ology sky sensors Introduction One of the biggest weaknesses in traditional UFO investigation is that most cases begin after something unusual has already happened. Investigators are left reconstructing events from memory, mobile phone footage, incomplete radar records or second hand testimony. The Galileo Project was created to reverse that sequence. Instead of starting with dramatic reports and searching for evidence afterwards, it aims to collect continuous, calibrated observations of the sky first and only then ask whether any recorded event deserves further investigation. This shift is important within the broader contrast between UFOlogy and Forteana because it moves the discussion from retrospective storytelling towards prospective measurement, while recognising that even sophisticated instruments cannot eliminate ambiguity altogether. [galileo.hsites.harvard.edu+2arXiv] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu tware and instrumentation system designed to conduct a multimodal census of aerial...Read more... Why after the fact reports are a weak foundation Eyewitness accounts remain valuable historical records, but they suffer from familiar limitations. Human perception varies with lighting, distance, expectation and stress, while memories change over time and often become influenced by later discussion or media coverage. Even honest witnesses may disagree about speed, size, colour or direction. The Galileo Project's central implementation choice is therefore to replace event driven investigation with continuous observation . Fixed observatories watch the sky regardless of whether anyone reports something unusual. This means the project gathers data on ordinary aircraft, birds, satellites, weather phenomena and astronomical objects as well as anything unexpected. The unusual event is interpreted against a much larger baseline of normal activity rather than being treated as an isolated mystery. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. This approach also addresses a recurring criticism of UFOlogy: many famous cases cannot be revisited because the original conditions no longer exist. By recording the environment continuously, researchers hope future anomalies will already have accompanying measurements instead of requiring reconstruction from memory. Scientist explains how he hunts for alien life in space Hot Take with Jesse Weber Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube How multimodal sensors reduce false positives The project's observatories are designed around the idea that no single sensor should decide whether an object is anomalous. Instead, multiple independent instruments observe the same region of sky simultaneously. The proposed sensor suite includes: Wide field visible and infrared cameras that continuously survey the sky. Narrow field tracking instruments for higher resolution follow up imaging. Radio receivers that monitor radio frequency emissions. Passive radar related measurements and antenna arrays where available. Acoustic sensors covering audible, infrasonic and ultrasonic frequencies. Environmental instruments measuring temperature, humidity, wind, pressure and other local conditions. Auxiliary measurements such as electric, magnetic and particle environments where appropriate. [arXiv+2MDPI] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. The key idea is corroboration. A bright reflection seen only by one optical camera may be a lens artefact. An apparent fast moving target detected only in infrared might result from calibration problems or atmospheric effects. If several independent sensors record compatible measurements at the same time, investigators gain much stronger evidence that a real physical object was present. Calibration is equally important. Rather than relying on consumer cameras of unknown characteristics, the observatories attempt to document sensor behaviour, timing accuracy and instrument alignment. Recent engineering papers describe calibration methods using known aircraft positions obtained from Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) transmissions to verify the geometry of infrared camera arrays before searching for unusual events. [arXiv] arxiv.org Commissioning An All Sky Infrared Camera Array for Detection Of Airborne ObjectsNovember 12, 2024... Published: November 12, 2024 Why fresh data still does not guarantee clear answers Better instruments reduce uncertainty but do not eliminate it. The atmosphere produces optical effects including mirages, temperature inversions, reflections and turbulence. Birds, insects, balloons, drones and satellites can produce surprising appearances, especially when observed at unusual angles or under difficult lighting. Instrumental artefacts, software errors and synchronisation problems also remain possible. For that reason, the Galileo Project emphasises an aerial census rather than a search exclusively for extraordinary objects. Machine learning systems are intended first to classify ordinary phenomena and identify probable false positives before highlighting genuine statistical outliers for detailed analysis. An anomaly is therefore defined operationally as something that resists known classifications, not automatically as evidence of non human technology. [galileo.hsites.harvard.edu+2arXiv] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Frequently Asked Questions The Galileo Project26 Jan 2022 — The Galileo Project will apply the scientific method to test hypotheses reg... This philosophy closely matches recommendations made by NASA's independent UAP study, which argued that progress depends primarily on higher quality, standardised observations, careful metadata and transparent scientific methods rather than speculation based on isolated reports. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportThe study of Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP) presents a unique scientific opportunity tha... Avi Loeb on the Scientific Investigation of UAP Channel: Professor Avi Loeb Open on YouTube Massive UAP file release leaves Avi Loeb ‘vindicated’ Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Where Fortean interpretation still matters Even if observatories eventually collect millions of hours of calibrated measurements, interpretation will remain necessary. A sensor can measure position, brightness, spectrum or motion, but deciding whether an event represents a new atmospheric phenomenon, an unfamiliar human technology, an instrumental error or something genuinely unexplained still requires judgement. Scientific analysis always depends on comparison with existing knowledge, and unusual data must survive repeated attempts at conventional explanation before being regarded as genuinely anomalous. This is where a Fortean perspective retains value. Forteana has long argued that unusual reports should neither be accepted uncritically nor dismissed prematurely. The availability of better data changes the quality of the discussion but does not abolish interpretive uncertainty. Instead, it shifts debate away from competing witness narratives towards competing explanations of the same recorded measurements. In other words, the Galileo Project does not promise to prove extraordinary claims. Its more immediate promise is methodological: future debates about aerial mysteries may increasingly revolve around shared datasets collected by calibrated instruments rather than incomplete memories, ambiguous photographs and retrospective reconstruction. Whether those datasets ultimately explain away anomalies or reveal genuinely unexpected phenomena is a question the instruments themselves cannot answer without careful scientific interpretation. [galileo.hsites.harvard.edu+2arXiv] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu tware and instrumentation system designed to conduct a multimodal census of aerial...Read more... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Instruments Settle Aerial Mysteries?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan Explains why calibrated evidence matters. See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience By Joseph Allen Hynek Shows the transition from witness reports toward structured evidence. See on Amazon Book UFOs : Generals, Pilots, and Government Officials Go on the R... By Leslie Kean, Heather Henderson Provides context for modern evidence debates. See on Amazon Book Passport to Magonia: from folklore to flying saucers By Jacques Vallee, Vallee Balances"},{"title":"Hynek Scale","url":"/hynek-scale/","description":"Hynek's categories helped investigators separate distant lights from richer close encounter claims without proving an alien answer.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"Hynek Scale Why Close Encounters Needed Categories Hynek Scale Hynek Scale Why Close Encounters Needed Categories Hynek's categories helped investigators separate distant lights from richer close encounter claims without proving an alien answer. Hynek's categories helped investigators separate distant lights from richer close encounter claims without proving an alien answer. policy intervention Nocturnal lights and daylight discs Radar visual cases and witness quality How Forteana reads the leftover strangeness forteana uf ology hynek scale Introduction J. Allen Hynek's classification system became one of the most influential organisational tools in UFOlogy because it attempted to separate fundamentally different kinds of reports instead of treating every sighting as equally significant. Rather than arguing that unexplained objects were extraterrestrial, Hynek proposed categories that distinguished distant observations from events reported at close range, where witnesses could potentially provide richer detail and where simple misidentification might be less likely. This \"close encounter filter\" was intended as an investigative aid, not as proof that close reports were true. Within the wider debate between UFOlogy and Forteana , the classification is important because it shows how investigators tried to impose order on a highly varied body of testimony while leaving open the possibility that some reports would remain resistant to straightforward explanation. [Smithsonian Institution] si.edu Smithsonian Institution The UFO experience a scientific inquiry [by] J Allen HynekNocturnal lights UFOs seen in the daytime: daylight discs Radar visual... encounters of the second kind Close encou... Why Hynek created categories instead of a single UFO label Hynek's experience as scientific adviser to the US Air Force's Project Sign, Project Grudge and Project Blue Book convinced him that \"UFO\" covered many different kinds of events. A brief glimpse of a moving light at night did not offer the same evidential value as an object observed at close range for several minutes by multiple witnesses. His 1972 book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry therefore introduced a structured classification that encouraged investigators to record comparable information rather than relying on dramatic narratives alone. [Smithsonian Institution] si.edu Smithsonian Institution The UFO experience a scientific inquiry [by] J Allen HynekNocturnal lights UFOs seen in the daytime: daylight discs Radar visual... encounters of the second kind Close encou... The system also reflected a practical concern. Many reports could ultimately be traced to astronomical objects, aircraft, balloons or atmospheric effects. By grouping cases according to observation conditions, Hynek hoped investigators could compare similar reports, identify recurring errors and reserve the greatest attention for the relatively small number of cases that resisted conventional explanations. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Identification studies of UFOs Identification studies of UFOs Nocturnal lights and daylight discs The first part of Hynek's scheme classified observations made at greater distances. Nocturnal lights referred to unusual lights seen in the night sky. These were the most common reports but were often the hardest to interpret because darkness removes visual cues about size, speed and distance. Bright planets, stars, meteors and aircraft lights frequently entered later explanations for these cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Identification studies of UFOs Identification studies of UFOs Daylight discs covered unidentified objects observed during daylight, regardless of whether they literally resembled a flying saucer. Better lighting could reveal shape and movement more clearly than night sightings, but distance still limited certainty. Hynek therefore treated these as observational reports rather than automatically elevating them to stronger evidence. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The distinction between these categories illustrates an important investigative principle. Hynek was not ranking mystery for its own sake; he was recognising that observation conditions strongly influence the quality of testimony. The UFO Experience: Evidence Behind Close… by J. Allen Hynek · Audiobook preview Channel: Google Play Books · Views: 1.5K · Uploaded: January 2024 · Length: 1 hour 8 minutes Open on YouTube Radar visual cases and witness quality Hynek gave particular attention to reports in which radar operators and visual observers independently described what appeared to be the same event. These became radar visual cases. At first glance, combining human testimony with instrument readings seems to produce stronger evidence than either source alone. However, Hynek also recognised that radar returns can be affected by atmospheric propagation, equipment limitations and interpretation errors. Consequently, radar confirmation was treated as potentially valuable corroboration rather than decisive proof. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter This approach anticipated a broader lesson that remains relevant in modern investigations of unidentified aerial phenomena: evidence becomes more persuasive when independent sources converge, but every source has its own limitations. Multiple imperfect observations do not automatically create certainty. How the close encounter filter worked Hynek's best known contribution was dividing close range reports into three progressively richer classes. He suggested that reports occurring within roughly 500 feet (about 150 metres) deserved separate treatment because witnesses should, in principle, have more opportunity to observe details than those watching distant lights. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The original categories were: Close Encounter of the First Kind (CE1): a nearby visual observation without reported physical effects. Close Encounter of the Second Kind (CE2): a nearby observation accompanied by claimed physical traces or environmental effects, such as marks on the ground, electrical interference or effects on vegetation. Close Encounter of the Third Kind (CE3): a nearby observation in which witnesses reported seeing occupants or entities associated with the object. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter The key point is that these were investigative categories rather than endorsements of witness claims. A CE3 report was not considered more believable simply because it involved reported occupants; instead, it was recognised as containing qualitatively different testimony requiring different questions and methods of evaluation. Later researchers introduced fourth and fifth kinds of close encounter, especially to encompass alleged abduction and communication experiences, but these additions were not part of Hynek's original framework and have never achieved universal acceptance among UFO researchers. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Close encounter Close encounter What If You Have A Close Encounter Of The Third Kind? Unveiled Channel: Unveiled · Views: 15.6K · Uploaded: October 2020 Open on YouTube How Forteana reads the leftover strangeness From a Fortean perspective, Hynek's classification is valuable precisely because it does not settle what UFO reports ultimately represent. Instead, it organises experiences while leaving interpretation open. A Fortean reader may note that close encounters often blur the boundary between observation and narrative. As reports become more elaborate, they may incorporate physical claims, unusual psychological experiences, folklore motifs or cultural expectations alongside descriptions of aerial objects. Rather than assuming these elements confirm extraterrestrial visitation, Forteana asks why certain patterns recur across different periods and cultural settings. This produces a subtle contrast with traditional UFOlogy. UFO investigators often use the classification to decide which cases deserve deeper technical analysis. Forteana instead treats the same categories as a way of comparing how extraordinary experiences are described, remembered and transmitted. In that view, Hynek's system becomes less a ladder of proof than a map showing where ordinary observation gives way to increasingly complex human testimony. What the classification achieved—and what it could not Hynek's scale remains influential because it encouraged investigators to collect structured information instead of treating every report as an isolated curiosity. It recognised that observation conditions, proximity and independent corroboration all affect the evidential value of a case. At the same time, the system has clear limits. Classification does not authenticate a report, eliminate misperception or resolve competing explanations. A carefully documented close encounter may still have an ordinary cause, while a distant observation can remain genuinely unidentified because the available information is too"},{"title":"Magonia","url":"/magonia/","description":"Jacques Vallee's folklore comparison asks whether UFO stories repeat older visitation motifs in modern technological language.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"Magonia Are UFO Stories Older Than Saucers? Magonia Magonia Are UFO Stories Older Than Saucers? Jacques Vallee's folklore comparison asks whether UFO stories repeat older visitation motifs in modern technological language. Jacques Vallee's folklore comparison asks whether UFO stories repeat older visitation motifs in modern technological language. entity Airships, fairies and sky visitors Technology as changing witness language Why comparison is not dismissal forteana uf ology magonia Introduction Jacques Vallée's Passport to Magonia , first published in 1969, is one of the most influential books in the Fortean approach to UFOs because it asks a different question from conventional UFOlogy. Rather than arguing simply that unidentified flying objects are either alien spacecraft or mistaken observations, Vallée proposed that modern UFO reports resemble much older traditions about fairies, mysterious sky visitors, supernatural kidnappings and other extraordinary encounters. His argument was not that medieval people were literally describing extraterrestrials. Instead, he suggested that similar patterns of experience recur across centuries while the cultural language used to describe them changes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jacques Vallée Jacques Vallée Within Forteana , this comparison became a major turning point. It shifted attention away from identifying unusual objects alone and towards recurring stories, witness testimony and cultural interpretation. Whether readers ultimately accept Vallée's conclusions or reject them, Passport to Magonia remains a landmark because it reframed UFO reports as part of a much longer history of anomalous experiences rather than as a phenomenon beginning with the flying saucer era of the late 1940s. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jacques Vallée Jacques Vallée Airships, fairies and sky visitors Vallée's central observation was that accounts from different historical periods often contain striking structural similarities despite describing apparently different beings. He assembled examples ranging from European fairy folklore and religious apparitions to nineteenth century mystery airships and twentieth century UFO encounters. The details change, but many narratives include recurring features such as: luminous aerial objects or impossible vehicles; encounters with unusual humanoid figures; missing or distorted time; journeys to strange realms before returning home; messages delivered by mysterious visitors; physical or psychological after effects experienced by witnesses. Rather than treating these similarities as coincidence, Vallée argued that they deserved systematic comparison. He believed UFO investigators had concentrated too narrowly on modern technology while overlooking centuries of reports with comparable narrative structures. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jacques Vallée Jacques Vallée The late nineteenth century American \"mystery airship\" wave became one of his favourite examples. Witnesses described advanced flying machines decades before practical aircraft became commonplace. To Vallée, this suggested that unexplained aerial reports often appear in forms that fit contemporary expectations. In earlier centuries, impossible visitors arrived as angels, demons or fairy beings. During the industrial age they appeared as fantastic dirigibles. In the space age they increasingly resembled extraterrestrial craft. [A Sky of Books and Movies] jeroenthoughts.wordpress.com UFO stories behave exactly like folk tales, fairy faith and religious A Sky of Books and MoviesReview: Passport to Magonia (1969) by Jacques Vallée28 Aug 2024 — It is the quintessential and highest regarded... Passport to Magonia by Jacques Vallee: UFOs, Folklore, and Parallel Worlds Channel: Starseed Films Open on YouTube Technology as changing witness language Perhaps the most influential idea in Passport to Magonia is that technology may function as a cultural vocabulary rather than a literal explanation. Vallée suggested that witnesses describe extraordinary experiences using concepts available within their own society. A medieval observer familiar with stories of enchanted kingdoms might speak of fairies. Someone living after the development of rockets and science fiction might instead describe spacecraft and alien visitors. This does not necessarily mean the underlying experiences are identical. Rather, the interpretation evolves alongside culture. The \"language\" used by witnesses changes even if certain recurring features remain remarkably stable. From a Fortean perspective, this helps explain why reports seem to adapt across historical periods without requiring every account to represent the same objective event. Instead of asking only, \"What object was seen?\", Vallée encouraged readers to ask, \"Why do people repeatedly describe extraordinary encounters in culturally meaningful forms?\" [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Jacques Vallée Jacques Vallée This approach also explains why Vallée became increasingly dissatisfied with the simple extraterrestrial hypothesis. If modern UFO narratives closely resemble older traditions that predate modern astronomy, then any complete explanation should account for both historical continuity and changing cultural imagery rather than focusing exclusively on visitors from another planet. [Goodreads] goodreads.com Passport to Magonia Quotes by Jacques F ValléeIt would be nice to hold on to the common belief that the UFOs are craft from a superior space civilization, because this is a hypo... Why comparison is not dismissal A common misunderstanding is that comparing UFO reports with folklore automatically dismisses them as myths or inventions. That is not Vallée's argument. Instead, he criticised two opposite extremes: reducing all reports to hoaxes , misidentifications or psychological error; assuming every unexplained encounter must involve extraterrestrial spacecraft. His comparative method occupies an intermediate position. Folklore, in this view, is valuable not because it proves supernatural beings exist, but because it preserves recurring patterns of human testimony. Those patterns may reveal something about perception, culture or an unidentified phenomenon that neither conventional scepticism nor straightforward extraterrestrial explanations fully capture. [Medium] medium.com PASSPORT TO MAGONIA. On UFOs, Folklore, and Parallel...Passport to Magonia was unusual in the literature of its subject because it... This distinction has become especially important within Forteana, where folklore is often treated as historical evidence for recurring experiences rather than historical proof of literal supernatural entities. Passport to Magonia: UFOs, Folklore, and Parallel Worlds Jacques Vallée Channel: ProfessorMystic · Views: 141 · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 6 hours 4 minutes Open on YouTube Criticism and debate Passport to Magonia remains influential partly because it raises difficult questions without providing a universally accepted answer. Supporters argue that Vallée broadened UFO research by introducing anthropology, comparative religion and folklore into a field previously dominated by aerospace thinking. They see the historical parallels as too persistent to ignore and credit him with identifying patterns overlooked by conventional investigators. [TALKING THE WEIRD] talkingtheweird.com reviewing the weird passport to magonia TALKING THE WEIRDReviewing the Weird: Passport to MagoniaFeb 18, 2021 — The book is over 50 years old and “outdated” only in the sense th... Critics, however, argue that similarities between stories do not demonstrate a common underlying cause. Human cultures often produce recurring myths involving journeys, mysterious beings and encounters with the unknown. Shared narrative motifs may arise through psychology, storytelling traditions and cultural transmission rather than through encounters with an external phenomenon. Some also argue that Vallée sometimes groups together reports from very different historical contexts, making broad comparisons that are difficult to test empirically. [A Sky of Books and Movies] jeroenthoughts.wordpress.com UFO stories behave exactly like folk tales, fairy faith and religious A Sky of Books and MoviesReview: Passport to Magonia (1969) by Jacques Vallée28 Aug 2024 — It is the quintessential and highest regarded... The debate therefore centres less on whether parallels exist—they plainly do—and more on what those parallels actually mean. Why Passport to Magonia still matters in Forteana Within the broader landscape of Forteana, Passport to Magonia helped redefine UFOs as one expression of a wider family of anomalous experiences rather than an isolated post war mystery. Its lasting influence lies in encouraging investigators to compare witness accounts across centuries instead of treating the flying saucer era as a complete historical beginning. Fairies, miraculous aerial signs, phantom airships and modern UFOs become part of a continuous conversation about how"},{"title":"NASA Data","url":"/nasa-data/","description":"NASA's UAP framing treats unidentified sightings as a data-quality problem, changing the tone from belief to measurement.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / UF Ology","level":3,"text":"NASA Data Can Better Data Change UFO Debates? NASA Data NASA Data Can Better Data Change UFO Debates? NASA's UAP framing treats unidentified sightings as a data quality problem, changing the tone from belief to measurement. NASA's UAP framing treats unidentified sightings as a data quality problem, changing the tone from belief to measurement. policy intervention Why NASA framed UAP as a data problem Transparency, sensors and scientific trust What Forteana says measurement can miss forteana uf ology nasa data Introduction NASA's recent approach to unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) marks a notable shift within the wider landscape of UFOlogy and Forteana . Rather than asking whether extraordinary sightings prove extraordinary claims, NASA frames the problem as one of measurement, data quality and scientific process. In this view, many unresolved cases remain unresolved not because they demonstrate exotic technology, but because the available evidence is incomplete, poorly documented or impossible to verify. That change in emphasis matters because it moves debate away from competing beliefs and towards standards for collecting, sharing and analysing observations. At the same time, Forteana reminds readers that mysterious reports are also cultural artefacts whose meaning cannot always be reduced to sensor readings alone. The contrast is therefore not simply science versus mystery, but two different ideas about what counts as evidence. Why NASA framed UAP as a data problem NASA's 2023 Independent Study Team repeatedly argued that the central obstacle to understanding UAP is not a lack of reports but a lack of usable observations. According to the report, analysis is hindered by poor sensor calibration, missing metadata, the absence of multiple independent measurements and too little baseline information against which unusual events can be compared. Rather than treating every unexplained sighting as a potential breakthrough, the report treats incomplete evidence as the primary scientific challenge. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... This framing changes the tone of investigation. Traditional UFO debates often revolve around individual cases, witness credibility or competing interpretations of dramatic incidents. NASA instead asks questions familiar to astronomy, atmospheric science and Earth observation : Was the sensor properly calibrated? Can the observation be independently confirmed? Are the time, location and environmental conditions recorded? Can the event be compared with known aircraft, satellites, weather or astronomical data? These questions are deliberately unglamorous. Their purpose is to make future observations more comparable and reproducible rather than more sensational. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... The report also makes an important distinction between \"unidentified\" and \"unexplainable\". An event may remain unidentified simply because the available information is inadequate, not because it defies known science. Better evidence, rather than stronger belief, becomes the proposed solution. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Transparency, sensors and scientific trust NASA's recommendations extend beyond collecting more reports. The agency argues for higher quality observations supported by transparent methods that other researchers can examine and reproduce. This reflects standard scientific practice rather than a specialised UFO methodology. Several recommendations illustrate this approach: Standardised sensor metadata , recording exactly how observations were made. Multiple observing systems , combining optical, infrared and radar information where possible. Well calibrated instruments with documented performance characteristics. Open scientific review , allowing methods and conclusions to be scrutinised publicly. Use of artificial intelligence and machine learning to identify unusual events within large observational datasets, provided those systems are trained on reliable information. [NASA Science+2New Space Economy] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... NASA also highlighted an unexpected social issue: stigma. Pilots, scientists and members of the public may hesitate to report unusual observations if they expect ridicule. The agency argued that treating reports as legitimate scientific data, rather than automatically associating them with extraordinary claims, could improve both reporting quality and public confidence. [PBS] pbs.org WATCH: NASA report says more science and less stigma are...But officials said NASA's involvement should help reduce the stigma around... Another notable proposal was exploring smartphone based citizen science. Rather than encouraging anecdotal submissions alone, the report suggested systems capable of collecting images together with automatically recorded location, timing and sensor information. This reflects the broader scientific principle that structured observations are more valuable than isolated stories because they preserve the context needed for later analysis. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... What Forteana says measurement can miss From a Fortean perspective, NASA's programme addresses only one dimension of anomalous reports. Forteana has long collected accounts that blur the boundaries between observation, folklore , psychology and social history. Its interest lies not only in identifying an object but also in understanding why particular narratives emerge, spread and persist. A Fortean collector might preserve: recurring descriptions across decades; local traditions attached to particular places; similarities between modern UFO reports and older sky wonders; witness reactions and changing interpretations over time. These features may have historical or cultural significance even if no physical explanation can ever be confirmed. This produces a different definition of evidence. NASA seeks observations that can be independently measured and reanalysed. Forteana often values testimony precisely because it records unusual human experiences that may never be repeatable. In that sense, a witness narrative can remain worth preserving even when it cannot satisfy scientific standards of proof. The approaches therefore answer different questions. NASA asks whether an event can become reliable scientific data. Forteana asks what the report itself reveals about recurring patterns in human encounters with the unexplained. NASA discusses findings from UFO study full video Channel: CBS News · Views: 34.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Can better data change UFO debates? Improved data cannot guarantee that every mystery will be solved, but it can change the nature of disagreement. Cases supported by multiple calibrated sensors, precise timing and complete metadata are easier to investigate systematically than reports based solely on memory or low quality imagery. At the same time, higher standards are likely to reduce the number of genuinely unresolved cases by eliminating ordinary explanations more efficiently. Some reports may prove to involve atmospheric effects, balloons, satellites, aircraft or sensor artefacts once sufficient contextual information is available. Others may remain unexplained, but with a clearer understanding of exactly what is known and what remains uncertain. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportAt present, analysis of UAP data is hampered by poor sensor calibration, the lack of multiple me... NASA's framework therefore does not promise spectacular discoveries. Instead, it promises better discrimination between poor evidence and genuinely puzzling observations. That is a significant departure from much of classic UFO culture, where debate often centred on dramatic individual stories. Within the broader world of Forteana, however, those stories retain value for different reasons. Scientific measurement may clarify what happened in the sky, while strange story collecting continues to document how people experience , interpret and remember the unknown. Rather than replacing one another, the two traditions illuminate different parts of the same phenomenon: one investigates observable"},{"title":"Coincidences","url":"/coincidences/","description":"Uncanny coincidences show how probability, memory, selection, and storytelling can make chance feel designed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Coincidences Why Do Some Coincidences Feel Impossible? Coincidences Coincidences Why Do Some Coincidences Feel Impossible? Uncanny coincidences show how probability, memory, selection, and storytelling can make chance feel designed. Uncanny coincidences show how probability, memory, selection, and storytelling can make chance feel designed. mechanism What makes a coincidence uncanny Probability and selective attention When meaning outruns evidence forteana coincidences Introduction Some coincidences feel impossible because the mind experiences them as small plots: a thought and a phone call , a name repeated three times in a day, a dream that seems to echo tomorrow’s news. In Forteana , these stories matter because they sit at the border between chance and meaning. They do not have to prove telepathy, fate or hidden design to be worth examining. Their power comes from a recognisable human problem: we are good at noticing patterns, poor at estimating how many possible patterns could have happened, and inclined to retell the striking “hits” while forgetting the uneventful misses. The best explanation is usually not that the event was mathematically impossible. It is that probability, selective attention, memory, social storytelling and personal significance have converged. Statisticians Persi Diaconis and Frederick Mosteller define coincidence as a surprising concurrence of events, perceived as meaningfully related, with no apparent causal connection; they argue that many such cases can be understood through hidden causes, psychology , multiple possible endpoints and the “law of truly large numbers”. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of Statistics What makes a coincidence uncanny? A plain rare event is not always a coincidence. A meteorite falling in a field is unlikely, but it becomes uncanny in the Fortean sense when it seems to answer, mirror or repeat something else: a name, a date, a dream, a warning, a family story. The emotional charge comes from relation, not rarity alone. That is why coincidences often have a compact narrative shape. “I had not thought about her for years, then she called.” “I learnt a word in the morning and saw it twice before dinner.” “Two strangers met and discovered the same birthday, street name and family connection.” These are not merely events; they are events with a felt hinge between them. Diaconis and Mosteller stress that the observer’s psychology enters the definition itself, because coincidence depends on what seems surprising, meaningful and causally unexplained to someone noticing it. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of Statistics Fortean coincidence stories also tend to resist clean classification. A sceptical reader may see memory bias. A believer may see synchronicity . A statistician may see a large search space. A folklorist may see a story becoming memorable because it is easy to repeat. The same incident can travel through all four frames without changing its basic facts. Charles Fort ’s relevance is not that he solved such cases, but that he treated awkward reports as culturally revealing. Fort’s wider project was to collect “damned” or excluded facts: odd reports that seemed to have been dismissed because they did not fit respectable categories. Later commentary on Fort has emphasised that his work often mattered less as proof of anomalies than as a challenge to overconfident explanation. [The Public Domain Review] publicdomainreview.org charles fort and the book of the damned charles fort and the book of the damned Probability is larger than intuition The simplest reason coincidences feel impossible is that people usually estimate the wrong probability. They ask, “What were the odds of this exact thing happening to me today?” after the event has happened. But the real probability question is usually wider: across all the people, days, names, memories, dreams, journeys, searches, messages, overheard words and near matches available, what are the odds that something would later be shaped into a striking story? Diaconis and Mosteller’s classic paper makes this point with unusual clarity. Once obvious causes are set aside, they identify several forces that generate many everyday coincidences: hidden causes, memory and perception, the tendency to count near matches as matches, and the accumulation of enormous numbers of events and interactions over time. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of Statistics The birthday problem is the cleanest demonstration. Many people feel that a shared birthday in a small group is surprising because they imagine one person trying to match one chosen date. But the real question is whether any pair in the group matches. In a group of 23 people, the chance of at least two sharing a birthday is already about one half; in a group of 100 it is close to certain. [Significance magazine] significancemagazine.com Significance magazine The birthday problem: lessons in probability from Significance magazine The birthday problem: lessons in probability from That lesson transfers to Fortean coincidence stories. A person does not have one possible coincidence waiting to happen. They have thousands. Any pair of names, places, numbers, dates, illnesses, dreams, news items or chance meetings can later become the “one” coincidence that is remembered. The more categories are allowed, the more likely a striking overlap becomes. This is why a coincidence can be genuinely rare in its final form and still not be evidence of design. The final form is often selected after the fact. A dream did not have to match exactly; it only had to resemble some later event. A number did not have to recur in one precise place; it could recur on a receipt, clock, address, train platform, phone number or news report. Once the target can move, the odds change dramatically. Probability and Outrageous Events: David J. Hand on The Law of Truly Large Numbers Channel: Samuel Devis Open on YouTube Selective attention turns chance into a pattern Many uncanny coincidences begin when attention changes. The frequency illusion , also called the Baader Meinhof phenomenon, describes the experience of noticing something for the first time and then seeming to encounter it everywhere. Linguist Arnold Zwicky popularised the term “frequency illusion” and linked it to selective attention and confirmation bias: first the mind starts filtering for the thing, then it treats each new sighting as confirmation that something unusual is happening. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion The effect is familiar. Someone considers buying a particular car and suddenly sees that model on every road. A newly learnt word appears to jump out from books, podcasts and conversations. A person hears about an obscure historical group and then spots references to it repeatedly. The world may not have changed much; the filter has. This matters for Forteana because many coincidence claims are built from attention shifts. A symbol, number, animal or phrase becomes personally salient, and then the mind scans for it more efficiently. The resulting experience can feel external and uncanny: “It is following me.” But the mechanism may be internal and ordinary: “I am now tuned to notice it.” Selective attention does not make the experience fake. The car really is on the road; the word really is in the article; the repeated number really is on the ticket. What changes is the meaning assigned to the recurrence. The event is real, but its apparent impossibility may come from failing to count all the times the world offered possible matches and none were noticed. Memory keeps the hits and drops the misses Coincidence stories are rarely recorded like laboratory data. They are remembered, compressed and retold. That gives them force, but it also makes them vulnerable to distortion. The striking match survives; the vague non match disappears. The dream that seemed prophetic is remembered; the many dreams that led nowhere are not. The day a friend called after being thought of becomes a story; the hundred unreturned thoughts do not. Diaconis and Mosteller explicitly include memory and perception among the main psychological sources of perceived coincidence. Their point is not that people are lying, but that ordinary mental processes can make the surprising cases much more available than the background rate of uneventful cases. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of Statistics Storytelling sharpens this effect. A coincidence that is messy in real time becomes cleaner in the telling. Dates are rounded, vague impressions become stronger, near matches become matches, and irrelevant details are removed. The result is often more memorable and more meaningful than the original event felt as it unfolded. This is one reason older coincidence anecdotes are hard to assess. Many are not frauds; they are polished memories. The further a story travels from a"},{"title":"Birthday Odds","url":"/birthday-odds/","description":"The birthday problem shows why a match can feel astonishing even when many hidden comparison points make it likely.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Birthday Odds Why Shared Dates Feel Stranger Than They Are Birthday Odds Birthday Odds Why Shared Dates Feel Stranger Than They Are The birthday problem shows why a match can feel astonishing even when many hidden comparison points make it likely. The birthday problem shows why a match can feel astonishing even when many hidden comparison points make it likely. mechanism The any pair mistake From birthdays to names and numbers What the example does and does not prove forteana coincidences birthday odds Introduction Many coincidence stories begin with a seemingly astonishing match: two strangers share a birthday, two families have children born on the same date, or a memorable number appears in several places at once. The birthday problem explains why these experiences feel far more extraordinary than they usually are. The key insight is that people instinctively calculate the wrong probability. Instead of asking whether any two items in a collection might match, they unconsciously imagine the odds of one specific item finding one specific match. That hidden shift in perspective makes ordinary statistical events appear almost miraculous. The birthday problem has therefore become one of the most useful demonstrations for understanding uncanny coincidences , especially in discussions of Forteana and synchronicity , because it separates genuine rarity from the surprising consequences of large numbers of possible comparisons. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu diaconis mosteller We find the utility of the birthday problem impressive as a tool for thinking about coincidences.Read more... The any pair mistake The birthday problem is often called a paradox, although there is no contradiction in the mathematics. The surprise comes from intuition. If you walk into a room containing 22 other people, the chance that someone shares your birthday is only about 6%. Most people naturally extend that intuition to the whole room and assume that any shared birthday must also be unlikely. The actual question, however, is different: what is the chance that any pair among those 23 people shares a birthday? That probability is just over 50%. With 30 people it rises to around 71%, and with 50 people it exceeds 97%. By the time a group reaches 70 people, a shared birthday is virtually guaranteed under the standard assumptions of the problem. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Birthday problem May 9, 2026 — In probability theory, the birthday problem asks for the probability that, in a set of n randomly chosen people, at least t... Published: May 9, 2026 The reason is simple once the hidden comparisons become visible. Every additional person can match everyone already present. In a group of 23 people there are 253 different pairs, not merely 23 possible comparisons. Each individual comparison is unlikely, but hundreds of opportunities accumulate into a substantial overall chance that at least one match will occur. [betterexplained.com] betterexplained.com Understanding the Birthday ParadoxUnderstanding the Birthday Paradox. 23 people. In a room of just 23 people there's a 50 50 chance of at... This is the \"any pair mistake\": our intuition follows one comparison, while probability counts them all. 23 and Football Birthdays Numberphile Channel: Numberphile Open on YouTube From birthdays to names and numbers The birthday problem is not really about birthdays. Birthdays merely provide an easy example of a much broader mechanism. Whenever people remember an uncanny coincidence, there are often many unnoticed opportunities for a match: Two people may share a first name, surname or initials. House numbers, flight numbers or hotel room numbers may repeat. Dates, anniversaries or ages may coincide. Places of birth, occupations or family names may overlap. Telephone digits, licence plates or lottery numbers may partially match. Viewed separately, each possibility seems improbable. Taken together, they create an enormous search space in which at least one striking overlap becomes much more likely than intuition suggests. This is why coincidence stories often feel compelling after the fact. The storyteller usually highlights the successful match while overlooking the many equally plausible features that did not coincide. Had the birthdays differed, perhaps the matching surname, street name or birth month would have become the remarkable detail instead. The event is selected because it is memorable, not because it was the only possible coincidence. Diaconis and Mosteller identify this expansion of possible outcomes as one of the central reasons that coincidences appear more mysterious than probability alone would predict. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu diaconis mosteller We find the utility of the birthday problem impressive as a tool for thinking about coincidences.Read more... Why coincidence stories feel stronger than the mathematics The birthday problem explains more than the numbers; it also explains the psychology . People naturally experience coincidence from a personal viewpoint. They remember the single remarkable event rather than the countless ordinary encounters that produced nothing unusual. This retrospective focus makes the successful match seem uniquely improbable. Several features strengthen the effect: Emotional significance. A birthday linked to a deceased relative feels more meaningful than one shared with a stranger. Selective memory. Successful matches are retold, while unsuccessful comparisons disappear from memory. Flexible matching. Near misses often count as hits. Sharing a birth month or anniversary may be remembered almost as vividly as sharing an exact date. Story structure. Coincidences become memorable because they fit a narrative with a beginning, surprise and apparent resolution. None of these psychological effects changes the underlying probability, but they change how surprising the event feels. Check your intuition: The birthday problem David Knuffke Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube The Infamous, Brain Bending Birthday Problem Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube What the example does and does not prove The birthday problem is frequently misunderstood in discussions of unexplained phenomena. It does demonstrate that surprisingly small groups can produce apparently extraordinary matches through ordinary probability alone. This makes it an effective corrective to claims that every unusual coincidence requires an extraordinary explanation. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu diaconis mosteller We find the utility of the birthday problem impressive as a tool for thinking about coincidences.Read more... However, it does not show that every coincidence is therefore meaningless. Mathematics answers only one question: how likely a pattern is to arise by chance under specified assumptions. It cannot determine whether a particular coincidence has personal, cultural or psychological significance for those involved. Nor does the birthday problem prove that every coincidence has been correctly modelled. Real populations do not have perfectly uniform birthday distributions, and many coincidence stories involve dependent events, shared environments or hidden causal connections rather than independent random occurrences. The birthday problem is deliberately simplified because its purpose is to reveal how intuition systematically underestimates the number of possible matches, not to model every real world situation exactly. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Birthday problem May 9, 2026 — In probability theory, the birthday problem asks for the probability that, in a set of n randomly chosen people, at least t... Published: May 9, 2026 Within the broader study of coincidences that feel impossible, the birthday problem therefore serves as a mechanism rather than a verdict. It reminds us that before asking whether a coincidence points to fate, synchronicity or an unknown connection, it is worth first asking whether we have counted all the opportunities for an ordinary match to occur. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Shared Dates Feel Stranger Than They Are. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Why People Believe Weird Things By Michael Shermer Connects statistical errors with extraordinary beliefs. See on Amazon Book The Drunkard's Walk By Leonard Mlodinow First published 2008. Subjects: Probabilities, Chance, Random variables, Mathematics, Nonfiction. See on Amazon Book The Improbability Principle By David J. Hand First published 2014. Subjects: Probabilities, Mathematics, popular works, Coincidence. See on Amazon Book Innumeracy By John Allen Paulos First published 1988. Subjects: Mathematical ability, Mathematics, Number concept, Popular works, Ouvrages de vulgarisation. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Why People Believe Weird Things The Drunkard's Walk The Improbability Principle As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks"},{"title":"Coincidence Log","url":"/coincidence-log/","description":"A simple coincidence log can separate what was known beforehand from details sharpened by memory and retelling.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Coincidence Log How to Record a Coincidence Before It Grows Coincidence Log Coincidence Log How to Record a Coincidence Before It Grows A simple coincidence log can separate what was known beforehand from details sharpened by memory and retelling. A simple coincidence log can separate what was known beforehand from details sharpened by memory and retelling. policy intervention What to write down first Dates, witnesses, and exact wording How retelling changes the case forteana coincidences coincidence log Introduction People who experience an extraordinary coincidence often remember the event as a complete story. The difficulty is that the story rarely exists in complete form at the moment it happens. Memory is reconstructive rather than a literal recording, and every retelling can sharpen, simplify or unintentionally alter details. For anyone interested in Fortean reports, the single most valuable step is therefore to make a contemporaneous record before discussing the event widely or trying to interpret it. That first record does not prove that an unusual coincidence has occurred, but it preserves what was actually known at the time and makes later evaluation far more reliable. Research on memory, eyewitness testimony and coincidence perception all points to the same practical lesson: the earlier the record, the more useful it becomes. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness testimony Eyewitness testimony What to write down first A useful coincidence log is not a polished narrative. It is a snapshot taken before memory has had time to reorganise the experience into a satisfying story. Record the following as soon as possible: The exact date and time the coincidence was noticed. What happened first , in chronological order. What you already knew beforehand , including any predictions, dreams, thoughts or conversations recorded before the event. The unexpected event itself , described as literally as possible. What made it feel remarkable , without yet deciding what it means. What did not happen , especially failed expectations or similar thoughts that led nowhere. Including unsuccessful predictions is important because coincidence stories naturally highlight memorable \"hits\" while forgetting the many ordinary misses. Psychologists studying coincidence perception have long argued that overlooking these non events can make unusual matches appear far rarer than they really are. [Scribd] scribd.com International Library of Psychology.) Watt, Caroline Objectively, a close coincidence is large enough sample, any outrageous thing more Scribd(International Library of Psychology.) Watt, CarolineObjectively, a close coincidence is large enough sample, any outrageous thing... Writing in plain language also helps. Instead of \"I knew she would call,\" write \"At 2:15 pm I thought of Jane after seeing her old photograph. At 4:40 pm she telephoned.\" This separates observation from later interpretation. Dates, witnesses, and exact wording The strongest coincidence records preserve context as carefully as content. Preserve original wording If the coincidence involves a dream, prediction or sudden impression, record the exact words you used before any outcome is known. Even small wording changes can greatly widen what appears to count as a successful match. For example, \"I dreamt of a bridge collapse \" is much more specific than \"I dreamt something bad would happen.\" After a news event, memories often drift towards whichever version seems the better fit. Recording the original phrasing prevents this unconscious broadening of the prediction. Research on coincidence judgement notes that people frequently remember a prediction as having been more precise than it originally was, especially after learning the outcome. [Scribd] scribd.com International Library of Psychology.) Watt, Caroline Objectively, a close coincidence is large enough sample, any outrageous thing more Scribd(International Library of Psychology.) Watt, CarolineObjectively, a close coincidence is large enough sample, any outrageous thing... How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube Record independent witnesses separately If more than one person experienced the event, ask each witness to write their own account before comparing notes. Studies of eyewitness memory show that people often influence one another after discussing an event. Shared conversations can create \"memory conformity\", where details remembered by one person become incorporated into another person's recollection, even when they were not originally observed. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Memory conformity Memory conformity Independent accounts are therefore more valuable than a single jointly constructed version. Keep supporting material Where possible, preserve evidence created before the coincidence became significant: diary entries emails text messages photographs with timestamps calendar entries voice notes social media posts phone records Such material provides fixed reference points that are not dependent on later memory. How retelling changes the case Retelling is not simply repeating a memory. Each recollection is an opportunity for the memory to be reconstructed. Classic work by Frederic Bartlett showed that repeated retelling tends to make stories shorter, more coherent and better fitted to existing expectations. Missing details are often replaced with information that feels plausible rather than information that was definitely present. Later research by Elizabeth Loftus and others demonstrated that post event information, suggestive questioning and even subtle changes in wording can alter what people sincerely believe they remember. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness testimony Eyewitness testimony This matters especially for Fortean coincidence reports because many are emotionally meaningful. Emotional importance often increases confidence in a memory without guaranteeing its accuracy. The more often a remarkable coincidence is discussed, the easier it becomes for interpretation and observation to merge. Typical changes during repeated retelling include: removing uncertainty or hesitation making timings appear closer together increasing the specificity of earlier predictions forgetting contradictory details combining several related incidents into one smoother narrative unconsciously adopting details suggested by other witnesses or listeners None of these changes necessarily involve deception. They are normal features of human memory. How Memory can be manipulated, with Elizabeth Loftus, PhD Speaking of Psychology Channel: American Psychological Association · Views: 62.4K · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube Why an early record strengthens a Fortean case Keeping an immediate log does not favour either sceptical or paranormal explanations. Instead, it improves the quality of the evidence available for either interpretation. For those who believe some coincidences may reflect synchronicity or other anomalous processes, an early written record reduces the possibility that the apparent pattern emerged through hindsight alone. For sceptical investigators, the same record allows probability, chronology and alternative explanations to be assessed using the facts as originally reported rather than a later polished narrative. In both cases, the first written account functions as a baseline against which later recollections can be compared. A coincidence that still appears remarkable after comparing the original notes with subsequent retellings deserves more careful attention than one whose most striking features only emerge months later. Scott Fraser: The problem with eyewitness testimony Channel: TED · Views: 246.2K · Uploaded: September 2012 · Length: 20 minutes Open on YouTube A practical coincidence log template A simple structure is usually enough: 1. When did I first notice the coincidence? 2. What exactly happened first? 3. What did I know before the later event occurred? 4. What independent evidence already existed? 5. Who else was present, and have they written their own account? 6. What alternative explanations occurred to me immediately? 7. What details am I uncertain about? Including uncertainty is a strength rather than a weakness. It marks the boundary between direct observation and later interpretation, helping preserve the original event before memory naturally reshapes it into a more compelling story. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How to Record a Coincidence Before It Grows. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Why People Believe Weird Things By Michael Shermer Explains cognitive biases that influence coincidence stories. See on Amazon Book The Book of the Damned By Charles Fort Historical foundation for collecting unusual reports. See on Amazon Book The Believing Brain By Michael Shermer Examines how people form"},{"title":"Frequency Illusion","url":"/frequency-illusion/","description":"The frequency illusion explains why a new word, symbol, car, or number can suddenly seem to appear all around you.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Frequency Illusion Why One New Thing Suddenly Appears Everywhere Frequency Illusion Frequency Illusion Why One New Thing Suddenly Appears Everywhere The frequency illusion explains why a new word, symbol, car, or number can suddenly seem to appear all around you. The frequency illusion explains why a new word, symbol, car, or number can suddenly seem to appear all around you. mechanism How attention changes the filter Everyday examples that feel uncanny When noticing becomes over meaning forteana coincidences frequency illusion Introduction One of the most common experiences behind apparently uncanny coincidences is the feeling that a newly learned word, number, symbol, car model or phrase suddenly starts appearing everywhere. Within discussions of Forteana, this can feel like the world is sending messages or arranging meaningful repetitions. In most cases, however, cognitive psychology offers a well supported explanation known as the frequency illusion , often nicknamed the Baader–Meinhof phenomenon . Rather than the outside world changing, the mind's filtering system changes, making previously ignored information suddenly stand out. Research and cognitive theory suggest that this effect arises from the interaction of selective attention and confirmation bias, creating the impression that the object's frequency has dramatically increased when its actual occurrence may be unchanged. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion Understanding this mechanism does not make repeated sightings any less striking. Instead, it explains why such experiences feel so convincing and why they so often become the basis for stories about fate, synchronicity or hidden connections. How attention changes the filter The brain constantly receives vastly more information than conscious awareness can process. To cope, it filters incoming sights, sounds and ideas, prioritising whatever seems personally relevant. The frequency illusion begins the moment something crosses that threshold of relevance. A person learns an unfamiliar word, notices a particular make of car while shopping, hears an unusual surname, or becomes interested in a repeating number. Once the brain has marked that item as significant, it becomes easier to detect against the background of everyday experience . What previously blended into the environment now attracts attention almost automatically. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion Linguist Arnold Zwicky, who popularised the term \"frequency illusion\", argued that two processes work together: Selective attention makes the newly relevant item easier to notice. Confirmation bias reinforces the belief that the item is genuinely appearing more often because each new encounter is remembered as supporting evidence, while countless non occurrences are ignored. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion This combination is powerful because it changes perception without requiring any change in the external world. Why You Keep Seeing the Same Thing Everywhere Baader Meinhof Phenomenon read in 5 Minutes Channel: SmartScope Learning Open on YouTube Everyday examples that feel uncanny The illusion is remarkably flexible because it can attach itself to almost anything that enters awareness. Common experiences include: learning a new word and hearing it several times within a week; deciding to buy a particular car model and suddenly seeing it on every journey; noticing an unusual surname that then seems to appear repeatedly in books or news stories; becoming aware of a number such as 11:11 and feeling it appears unusually often on clocks or receipts; hearing a song for the first time before encountering it in shops, radio broadcasts and conversations. These experiences often feel surprising because people naturally compare the present with a memory of never having noticed the item before. In reality, the object may have been present all along but was simply filtered out before it became meaningful. [Scribbr] scribbr.com baader meinhof phenomenon The Baader–Meinhof Phenomenon Explained2 Nov 2022 — The Baader–Meinhof phenomenon refers to the false impression that something ha... This explains why different people can inhabit the same environment while noticing entirely different recurring patterns depending on their interests, worries or recent experiences. Why repeated words feel especially meaningful Words occupy a special place because language is processed constantly yet mostly unconsciously. Thousands of spoken and written words pass through attention every day, with only a fraction reaching conscious awareness. When an unfamiliar word is learned, the brain gains a new category. Once that category exists, matching examples become much easier to recognise. The result is the strong impression that newspapers , conversations and television have all started using the word simultaneously. The same effect applies to names, technical terms and expressions that suddenly become memorable because of a conversation, book or film. Unless there has genuinely been a surge in public usage, the apparent increase reflects a change in perception rather than in language itself. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion Why the experience feels external One reason the frequency illusion feels mysterious is that the observer experiences only its outcome, not the internal change that produced it. From the first person perspective, the sequence appears straightforward: 1. I learned about something. 2. The world immediately started presenting it repeatedly. 3. Therefore something unusual seems to be happening. The hidden step is that the observer's attentional system has quietly changed between the first and second events. Because this shift happens automatically, the repeated encounters seem to come from outside rather than from altered perception. This is why people often describe the experience as though reality itself has become synchronised with their thoughts. What is Baader Meinhof Effect? Channel: Psychology Answered Open on YouTube When noticing becomes over meaning Most frequency illusions are harmless curiosities. They become more significant when repeated observations are interpreted as evidence for broader conclusions. Examples include assuming: a number carries supernatural significance because it appears repeatedly; a particular event is becoming dramatically more common without checking objective statistics; repeated mentions confirm a conspiracy or hidden influence; a chance sequence proves destiny or psychic connection. The mechanism does not mean every genuine trend is imaginary. Sometimes an item really is becoming more common because of changing fashions, breaking news or increased media coverage. The crucial question is whether independent evidence shows that frequency has actually changed, rather than relying solely on personal impressions. [Scribbr] scribbr.com baader meinhof phenomenon The Baader–Meinhof Phenomenon Explained2 Nov 2022 — The Baader–Meinhof phenomenon refers to the false impression that something ha... For this reason, psychologists distinguish between subjective frequency—the number of times something feels noticeable—and objective frequency, which can be measured independently. Why it matters in Fortean thinking Within Forteana , repeated encounters often occupy an ambiguous position between psychology and mystery. They are compelling because they possess genuine emotional force while remaining compatible with ordinary cognitive processes. A person who repeatedly encounters the same unusual word after hearing it once may interpret the experience as coincidence, synchronicity, divine guidance or simply a quirk of attention. The observable facts remain identical; only the explanatory framework changes. The frequency illusion therefore provides an important baseline explanation for many reports of repeated names, symbols and numbers. It does not settle broader philosophical questions about meaning or synchronicity, but it demonstrates that an experience can feel profoundly improbable even when it emerges naturally from the way attention filters an information rich world. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Frequency illusion Frequency illusion Why your brain is secretly editing Reality. Channel: 000Outline Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why One New Thing Suddenly Appears Everywhere. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book You are Not So Smart By David McRaney Explains confirmation bias and selective attention behind the frequency illusion. See on Amazon Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman Covers cognitive biases involved in noticing patterns. See on Amazon Book The Invisible Gorilla By Christopher Chabris, Daniel Simons Shows how attention filters what we notice. See on Amazon Book The Improbability Principle By David J. Hand Explains why repeated observations can seem"},{"title":"Synchronicity","url":"/synchronicity/","description":"Jung's synchronicity idea gives language to meaningful coincidence while leaving a difficult gap between significance and proof.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Synchronicity Can a Coincidence Be Meaningful but Not Proof? Synchronicity Synchronicity Can a Coincidence Be Meaningful but Not Proof? Jung's synchronicity idea gives language to meaningful coincidence while leaving a difficult gap between significance and proof. Jung's synchronicity idea gives language to meaningful coincidence while leaving a difficult gap between significance and proof. entity What synchronicity claims Why the idea remains attractive Where meaning outruns evidence forteana coincidences synchronicity Introduction Carl Jung's idea of synchronicity gives one of the best known philosophical and psychological frameworks for coincidences that feel uncannily meaningful. Rather than claiming that every striking coincidence reveals fate, psychic powers or hidden cosmic design, Jung argued that some events are experienced as connected by meaning rather than by ordinary cause and effect . This distinction explains why synchronicity has remained influential in Fortean writing: it provides a language for experiences that feel deeply significant without necessarily supplying scientific proof that anything paranormal has occurred. At the same time, it leaves a difficult question unresolved. Can a coincidence genuinely carry personal meaning while still offering no objective evidence that an unseen force produced it? What synchronicity claims Jung introduced synchronicity gradually during the 1930s before presenting his fullest account in his 1952 essay Synchronicity: An Acausal Connecting Principle . He described synchronicity as the simultaneous occurrence of an inner psychological state and an external event that appear connected by meaning despite lacking an identifiable causal relationship. In his view, meaning itself could sometimes form a genuine pattern alongside physical causation rather than being merely a subjective interpretation. [IAAP] iaap.org Synchronicity: An Acausal Connecting PrincipleSynchronicity was defined by Jung as an acausal connecting principle, whereby internal... This is an important distinction. A synchronistic event is not simply: an unlikely event; a coincidence that later proves useful; or an event that has a hidden ordinary cause waiting to be discovered. Instead, Jung proposed that certain experiences unite two separate events through symbolic resonance. The connection is recognised psychologically rather than established through physical mechanisms. One of Jung's most famous examples occurred during psychotherapy. A patient described dreaming of a golden scarab when, according to Jung's account, a rose chafer beetle struck the consulting room window at that moment. Jung regarded the timing as psychologically transformative because it broke through the patient's rigid scepticism. The importance lay not in the insect itself but in the symbolic convergence between dream and reality. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. For Jung, synchronicity therefore concerned meaningful coincidence , not simply improbable coincidence. Jung's Theory That Proves Coincidences Don't Exist Channel: Kurst Open on YouTube Why the idea remains attractive Synchronicity continues to appeal because it reflects how many people actually experience remarkable coincidences. Often the strongest impression is not that the event was mathematically impossible but that it seemed personally addressed. Examples frequently reported include: thinking intensely about someone immediately before unexpectedly hearing from them; repeatedly encountering the same unusual symbol during an emotionally significant period; finding a book, quotation or conversation that appears uncannily suited to a personal dilemma. These experiences often occur during periods of grief, major life transitions, illness or identity change. In such moments people naturally search for coherence, and coincidences may feel less like isolated accidents than like narrative turning points. Jung believed that synchronistic experiences frequently emerged during periods when unconscious psychological material was becoming conscious. He connected this process with his broader theory of archetypes and the collective unconscious, suggesting that symbolic patterns could appear simultaneously in both inner experience and external events. Whether or not one accepts this theory, it explains why synchronicity has been especially influential in psychotherapy, religious studies and discussions of spiritual experience. [IAAP] iaap.org Synchronicity: An Acausal Connecting PrincipleSynchronicity was defined by Jung as an acausal connecting principle, whereby internal... Unlike deterministic beliefs that every event has been pre planned, synchronicity preserves an element of unpredictability. The coincidence is meaningful precisely because it appears unexpectedly and cannot be manufactured at will. Synchronicity — Why Meaningful Coincidences Intensify During Transformation (Jung + Alchemy) Channel: The Hermetic School Open on YouTube Jung's collaboration with Wolfgang Pauli One reason synchronicity attracted lasting attention is that Jung explored it alongside the Nobel Prize winning physicist Wolfgang Pauli. Their long correspondence examined whether modern physics and depth psychology might eventually converge on a broader understanding of reality in which causality was not the only organising principle. [PhilPapers] philpapers.org Phil Papers C G. JUNG, The Interpretation of Nature and the Psycheby CG JUNG · 1955 · Cited by 707 — The Interpretation of Nature and the Psyche: Synch... This collaboration is often misunderstood. Pauli did not demonstrate synchronicity scientifically, nor did quantum mechanics prove Jung's theory. Instead, both men were interested in philosophical questions about whether mind and matter might share deeper forms of order that existing scientific concepts had not yet explained. Later popular writing frequently invoked quantum physics to justify synchronicity, but mainstream physicists generally regard these extensions as speculative rather than established science. The historical collaboration remains intellectually interesting without providing empirical confirmation of Jung's central claims. [Metanexus] metanexus.net Wolfgang Pauli, Carl Jung, and the Acausal Connecting...Jung's theory of synchronicity posited that certain events often called... Where meaning outruns evidence The greatest strength of synchronicity is also its greatest weakness. A synchronistic event can be intensely convincing to the individual experiencing it while remaining almost impossible to verify objectively. Suppose someone dreams of an old friend and unexpectedly meets them the next day. For that person, the experience may feel life changing. Yet an outside observer cannot measure the subjective significance that makes the coincidence meaningful. The event may be psychologically important regardless of whether chance alone produced it. This creates an important distinction: Personal meaning concerns what the experience contributes to someone's life or psychological development. Objective evidence concerns whether the experience demonstrates a previously unknown principle operating in nature. Jung largely concentrated on the first question while suggesting—without conclusively demonstrating—that the second might also be true. Modern psychology generally accepts that coincidences can have genuine therapeutic or emotional importance without requiring supernatural explanations. A meaningful experience may influence behaviour, memory or recovery regardless of how the coincidence arose. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Why mainstream science remains unconvinced The central scientific difficulty is that synchronicity lacks a reliable method of testing. If meaningful coincidences occur unpredictably, depend upon subjective interpretation and cannot be reproduced under controlled conditions, they are difficult to evaluate using conventional scientific methods. Critics therefore argue that synchronicity functions more as a philosophical or interpretive framework than as a testable theory of nature. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Researchers also point to several well established cognitive processes that make meaningful coincidences more likely to be noticed: selective attention towards emotionally important information; confirmation bias, which emphasises memorable \"hits\" while forgetting countless non events; pattern recognition in noisy environments; hindsight reconstruction that strengthens apparent connections after the fact. These mechanisms do not necessarily explain every individual experience, but they reduce the need to invoke an unknown acausal principle. Statistical reasoning likewise shows that in lives containing millions of perceptions and interactions, highly unusual combinations are expected to occur occasionally. Consequently, mainstream psychology and scientific scepticism generally interpret synchronicity as an"},{"title":"Dream Hits","url":"/dream-hits/","description":"Dream coincidences become persuasive when remembered hits are retold while the many dreams that matched nothing fade away.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Dream Hits Why Prophetic Dreams Need the Missing Misses Dream Hits Dream Hits Why Prophetic Dreams Need the Missing Misses Dream coincidences become persuasive when remembered hits are retold while the many dreams that matched nothing fade away. Dream coincidences become persuasive when remembered hits are retold while the many dreams that matched nothing fade away. dataset evidence Dream records versus remembered stories Near matches and flexible targets How to test a dream claim fairly forteana coincidences dream hits Introduction Dreams are among the most compelling sources of seemingly impossible coincidences . A vivid dream of an accident, a famous death or an unexpected meeting can feel astonishing when something similar later occurs. Yet the persuasive power of these stories depends on a hidden comparison: the memorable \"hits\" are retold for years, while the countless dreams that matched nothing are rarely written down, remembered or shared. This imbalance is central to evaluating claims of prophetic dreams. The question is not simply whether one dream resembled a later event, but how many other dreams did not. Researchers studying coincidences, memory and dream reports consistently argue that fair evaluation requires counting both successes and failures. Without the forgotten misses, even ordinary chance can produce remarkably convincing stories. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu These include data gathering methods (informal.Read more Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 Why remembered dream hits feel so convincing Dreams possess several qualities that make coincidences especially persuasive. First, dreams are emotionally intense. Fear, surprise and strong imagery increase the likelihood that a dream will be remembered after an unusual real world event gives it new significance. A dream about a collapsing bridge may be forgotten within hours unless a bridge accident appears in the news days later, at which point the dream suddenly seems prophetic. Second, dream memories are often reconstructed rather than replayed exactly. Modern dream research shows that remembering dreams is an act of recall, not retrieving a perfect recording. As with ordinary autobiographical memory, details may shift over time, especially after later experiences provide an attractive narrative. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NIHby Y Nir · 2010 · Cited by 866 — Dreams are a most remarkable experiment in psychology and neuroscience, conducted every night i... Finally, people usually dream several times each night, yet remember only a fraction of those dreams. Even frequent dream recall captures only a small sample of total dream experience , meaning the apparent \"success rate\" is calculated from an incomplete record. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by M Schredl · 2022 · Cited by 15 — Dream recall frequency and lucid dream frequency showed large inter individual differences that ar... Dreams, Nightmares, and Neuroscience Dr. Baland Jalal EP 533 Channel: Jordan B Peterson Open on YouTube Dream records versus remembered stories The strongest evidence for any prophetic dream claim comes from dreams recorded before the alleged prediction could be recognised. A contemporaneous dream diary fixes the wording, imagery and timing before later events influence memory. Without such records, it becomes difficult to distinguish between: a genuine detailed prediction; a vague dream that later acquired new meaning; a dream remembered differently after the event occurred; a dream among hundreds of forgotten dreams that never matched anything. This distinction explains why psychologists prefer prospective recording over retrospective storytelling. Prospective methods prevent the dream itself from changing in response to later events. Historically, some researchers interested in precognition have attempted prospective dream studies by asking participants to document dreams before target events were selected or before future news became known. These approaches reduce hindsight bias, although they have generally not produced evidence strong enough to establish precognition as an accepted scientific phenomenon. [Koestler Unit+2Pure Edinburgh] koestlerunit.wordpress.com Koestler Unit Prospectively Reported Spontaneous Precognitive Dreams For instance Besterman (1933) had forty three percipients document their dreams...Read more... Dream Telepathy with Stanley Krippner Channel: New Thinking Allowed with Jeffrey Mishlove Open on YouTube Near matches and flexible targets Another reason prophetic dreams appear common is that dream symbolism is unusually flexible. Dreams are rarely literal. They feature changing locations, blended identities, impossible events and emotionally charged imagery. After something noteworthy happens, many different dream elements can be interpreted as partial correspondences. For example, a dream involving: water may later be linked to flooding, a cruise, heavy rain or emotional upheaval; an unfamiliar stranger may become \"obviously\" a colleague met weeks later; a generic disaster scene may be associated with any major news event. Statisticians refer to this as the problem of multiple possible endpoints. If many different outcomes qualify as successful matches, apparent accuracy increases dramatically. The more interpretative freedom available, the easier it becomes to find convincing correspondences after the fact. Diaconis and Mosteller identified this flexibility, together with near matches and selective perception, as major generators of striking coincidences. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu These include data gathering methods (informal.Read more Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 The forgotten misses The missing comparison is simple but powerful. Imagine someone recalls three dreams that later seemed remarkably accurate over twenty years. That sounds extraordinary until additional information is included: perhaps they remembered thousands of dreams during that period; perhaps dozens of dreams predicted disasters that never happened; perhaps hundreds contained equally vivid but completely unmatched imagery. Once the denominator is restored, the apparent improbability changes. This pattern resembles publication bias in science: successful results receive attention while unsuccessful ones disappear. In dream coincidence stories, the forgotten misses create a similar distortion. Stories naturally preserve emotionally satisfying successes while quietly discarding failures. Psychological studies of precognitive dream belief support this concern. Researchers have shown that selective recall and a greater tendency to identify meaningful correspondences between unrelated dream reports and later events both contribute to stronger belief in prophetic dreams. These mechanisms do not require dishonesty; they arise from normal cognitive processes. [heiJOURNALS] journals.ub.uni heidelberg.de heiJOURNALSPsychological factors in precognitive dream ex periences:...by C Watt · Cited by 17 — Controlling for sensory factors and ch... The Quantum Physics of Synchronicity The Jung Pauli Conjecture Channel: Mirror Open on YouTube How to test a dream claim fairly A fair evaluation does not begin by asking whether one dream resembles one later event. It asks whether the overall pattern exceeds what chance, memory and interpretation would predict. Useful criteria include: Record dreams immediately. Timestamped entries prevent later editing. Define success beforehand. Decide what counts as a prediction before knowing future events. Count every dream. Include apparent failures as well as dramatic successes. Limit interpretation. Avoid expanding vague symbols until almost any outcome qualifies. Use independent judges where possible. If outsiders cannot reliably match dreams to future events, subjective interpretation may be doing most of the work. These principles mirror broader statistical approaches for studying coincidences: reduce hindsight, specify criteria in advance and compare observed successes with all possible opportunities for apparent matches. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu These include data gathering methods (informal.Read more Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 Why the debate remains unresolved Prophetic dreams remain an enduring subject within Forteana because they combine intensely personal experiences with events that sometimes appear astonishingly specific. Individual cases can be emotionally persuasive, particularly when documented before the event or involving highly unusual details. At the same time, attempts to move from compelling"},{"title":"Phone Call","url":"/phone-call/","description":"A phone call after thinking of someone feels uncanny because the remembered hit hides a large background of uncalled thoughts.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Coincidences","level":3,"text":"Phone Call When a Thought Is Followed by a Call Phone Call Phone Call When a Thought Is Followed by a Call A phone call after thinking of someone feels uncanny because the remembered hit hides a large background of uncalled thoughts. A phone call after thinking of someone feels uncanny because the remembered hit hides a large background of uncalled thoughts. population context The memorable hit pattern Counting all the unreturned thoughts Friendship routines as hidden causes forteana coincidences phone call Introduction Few coincidence stories are as familiar as this: you suddenly think about an old friend, and moments later the phone rings or a message arrives from that very person. Within the broader world of Fortean coincidences , such experiences are often presented as suggestive evidence of telepathy or synchronicity . Yet they also illustrate one of the most important lessons in the study of coincidences: memorable \"hits\" are easy to recall, while the far larger number of thoughts that are not followed by a call almost never become stories. The central question is therefore not whether these experiences happen—they plainly do—but how they should be interpreted. Statistical reasoning, psychology and social behaviour all point to hidden background rates, sometimes called hidden baselines. Unless those baselines are counted, an apparently extraordinary coincidence can seem far more improbable than it really is. At the same time, the persistence of such reports has encouraged experimental research into so called \"telephone telepathy\", making this one of the few everyday coincidence claims that has been formally investigated. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 The memorable hit pattern A thought followed by a phone call forms a perfect miniature narrative. It has a beginning (\"I suddenly thought of Jane\"), an unexpected turning point (\"then she called\"), and an implied mystery. Human memory is especially good at preserving events with this kind of compact structure. By contrast, the much larger set of forgotten events has no story. Most people think briefly about relatives, colleagues, former classmates and acquaintances many times each week. Almost all of those thoughts are followed by nothing at all. Because nothing happens, those moments disappear from memory. This imbalance creates a classic selection effect: The successful matches are remembered and retold. The unsuccessful thoughts are rarely recorded. Later recollection exaggerates how unusual the successful match appeared. Psychologists studying coincidences have long argued that selective attention and selective memory are among the strongest normal explanations for apparently extraordinary correspondences. The coincidence is genuine, but the remembered sample is not representative of everything that happened. [koestler parapsychology.psy.ed.ac.uk] koestler parapsychology.psy.ed.ac.uk After a brief discussion of hidden causes.Read more Psychology and Coincidencesby CA Watt · Cited by 26 — Abstract: The paper presents a selective review of research suggesting possible nor... Joint Attention & Telephone Telepathy Dr. Rupert Sheldrake Channel: Simulation Open on YouTube Counting all the unreturned thoughts The idea of a hidden baseline is simple but surprisingly powerful. Imagine someone who briefly thinks about twenty different people each day. Across a year that amounts to several thousand opportunities for a later phone call, text message or unexpected encounter. Most of these thoughts will lead nowhere. However, even if only a tiny fraction are later followed by contact, the successful cases accumulate over months and years. This is an example of what statisticians Persi Diaconis and Frederick Mosteller describe when discussing coincidences. Rather than asking, \"What were the odds of this exact friend calling after I thought of them?\", the more appropriate question becomes, \"Across all the people I think about, all the days in my life and all the communications I receive, how often should I expect at least one striking match?\" Once the number of opportunities is recognised, apparently astonishing events become much less mathematically surprising. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 The same reasoning underlies the so called law of truly large numbers: with enough opportunities, events that seem individually improbable become practically inevitable somewhere in the stream of everyday life. [imstat.org] imstat.org hand writing the improbability principle Hand writing: The Improbability Principle16 Feb 2015 — The improbability principle has it: highly improbable events are commonplace. In m... Friendship routines as hidden causes Not every coincidence is purely random. Hidden causes often increase the likelihood of contact without the people involved recognising them. Friends frequently share routines that make communication cluster around particular times: weekends and holidays; birthdays or anniversaries; shared news events; regular commuting patterns; seasonal habits; recurring work schedules. Suppose two friends usually speak every few weeks. One begins thinking about the other because they have not talked recently. Independently, the other person notices the same interval and decides to call. The coincidence feels uncanny because neither person consciously recognised the shared routine that influenced both thoughts. Diaconis and Mosteller identify such unnoticed common causes as one of the principal mechanisms behind many reported coincidences. The apparent mystery may arise not from paranormal communication but from ordinary causal connections that remain hidden from the participants themselves. [Department of Statistics] stat.berkeley.edu Department of StatisticsMethods for Studying CoincidencesJuly 24, 2007 — by P DlACONlS · 1989 · Cited by 428 — This article illustrates b... Published: July 24, 2007 Why phone calls feel more convincing than other coincidences Telephone calls possess several features that amplify the feeling of impossibility. First, they appear to provide an objective external event. Unlike vague feelings or dreams, a ringing phone arrives at a precise moment that seems independent of the observer. Secondly, communication is emotionally weighted. Calls from close friends and family carry more personal significance than those from strangers, making any apparent anticipation feel especially meaningful. Thirdly, people rarely remember the precise timing of their own thoughts. If someone remembers thinking about a friend \"earlier today\", the interval may actually have been several hours. Memory naturally compresses the sequence into a tighter and more dramatic narrative than contemporary records might show. These characteristics make phone call coincidences especially memorable even when ordinary probability and social behaviour offer sufficient explanations. Richard Wiseman's Failed Debunking of Animal Telepathy Channel: Rupert Sheldrake Open on YouTube Experimental tests of \"telephone telepathy\" Unlike many coincidence stories, thought before call claims have inspired controlled experiments. Biologist Rupert Sheldrake conducted studies in which participants attempted to identify which of several familiar people was calling before answering the telephone. His published studies reported hit rates above chance, and he has argued that these findings support a genuine telepathic effect. [sheldrake.org] sheldrake.org Experimental Tests For Telephone Telepathy Rupert Sheldrakeby R Sheldrake · 2003 · Cited by 76 — If telephone telepathy exists, then wh... However, these experiments have remained highly controversial. Critics have questioned aspects of the methodology, statistical treatment and experimental controls, while sceptical researchers have argued that the evidence has not demonstrated a reliable paranormal phenomenon. More recent preregistered replication attempts have reported performance close to chance rather than above it, illustrating that the issue remains disputed rather than settled. [The Guardian+2ResearchGate] theguardian.com The GuardianTelepathy work dismissed as fantasy Science5 Sept 2006 — Dr Sheldrake asked volunteers, who claimed they had had experience... Within mainstream psychology and statistics, the prevailing interpretation continues to favour ordinary mechanisms such as expectation, selective memory, hidden social patterns and background probability over telepathy. [koestler parapsychology.psy.ed.ac.uk] koestler parapsychology.psy.ed.ac.uk After a brief discussion of hidden causes.Read more Psychology and Coincidencesby CA Watt · Cited by 26 — Abstract: The paper presents a"},{"title":"Odd Hums","url":"/odd-hums/","description":"Odd hum reports show how sound, place, health, industry, and rumour can merge into a persistent mystery.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Odd Hums Why Do Some Places Hear a Mysterious Hum? Odd Hums Odd Hums Why Do Some Places Hear a Mysterious Hum? Odd hum reports show how sound, place, health, industry, and rumour can merge into a persistent mystery. Odd hum reports show how sound, place, health, industry, and rumour can merge into a persistent mystery. mechanism What hum witnesses report Environmental and bodily causes Why communities keep investigating forteana odd hums Introduction A mysterious local hum is Fortean not because it proves anything paranormal, but because it sits in the awkward space between real distress and uncertain evidence. People report a low, pulsing drone like a distant diesel engine, often strongest indoors at night, yet neighbours in the same street may hear nothing and standard noise checks may find no single source. That combination turns sound into a community mystery: residents compare notes, suspect factories or infrastructure, call councils, form support groups , and sometimes feel dismissed when investigators cannot confirm what they hear. The best evidence suggests there is no single “Hum”. Some cases are traceable to industrial machinery, ventilation, compressors, power equipment or other low frequency sources. Others may involve tinnitus like low frequency sound perception, unusual hearing sensitivity, room resonance, stress, sleep disruption, or a mixture of all of these. This is why hum reports are valuable Fortean case studies: they show how place, health, technology and rumour can merge into a persistent anomaly without requiring a supernatural explanation. What Hum Witnesses Report The classic hum report is strikingly consistent. Witnesses describe a deep, steady, intrusive sound: a rumble, throb, vibration, distant lorry, idling engine, or low electrical drone. It is often reported as worse at night, worse indoors, and difficult to locate by walking towards it. The World Hum Database describes the typical report as sounding like a car or truck engine idling outside or down the road, usually louder at night and indoors than outdoors. [The World Hum Map] thehum.info The World Hum MapThe World Hum Map and Database Project Dr Glen...We are tracking down the cause of worldwide anomolous low frequency... That pattern matters because it partly explains why witnesses become anxious. A high pitched alarm or obvious mechanical noise can usually be followed to a source. A low frequency hum is different. Long wavelengths can pass through walls, reflect, form standing waves in rooms, and seem to come from everywhere or nowhere. The Institute of Acoustics notes that low frequency noise can be generated by pumps, large fans, compressors, diesel engines, rotary machinery and power transmission systems, but that not everyone can hear it and, in many cases, no source is identified. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of AcousticsLow Frequency Noise AnnoyanceLow Frequency Noise (LFN) can be an environmental noise problem. Not everyone can hear... Hum witnesses also often report bodily and emotional effects: poor sleep, headaches, irritability, pressure sensations, dizziness, fatigue, and a feeling that the home has become unsafe or unpeaceful. These symptoms do not by themselves prove an external source; they do show why the issue becomes urgent. The World Health Organization’s environmental noise guidance links excessive noise with annoyance, sleep disturbance, tinnitus, hypertension and other health impacts, while its night noise guidance treats noise induced sleep disturbance as a health problem in its own right. [World Health Organization] who.int World Health OrganizationGuidance on environmental noiseExcessive noise can cause annoyance; in addition research shows it increases the... [POLIS Network] polisnetwork.eu • While noise induced sleep disturbance is viewed as a health problem POLIS NetworkNight noise guidelines for EuropeOctober 7, 2009 — by World Health Organization · 2009 · Cited by 659 — night noise exposure... Published: October 7, 2009 The Fortean element is the mismatch between testimony and verification. A small group may be adamant that something is happening, while officials, neighbours or acoustic instruments fail to capture a clear cause. That gap is where theories multiply: hidden industry, electrical grids, military systems, geological vibration, unusual atmospheric effects, tinnitus, or mass suggestion. The hum becomes less a single sound than a social problem organised around a sound. Why Famous Hum Cases Stay Unsettled Taos: the model modern mystery The Taos Hum became one of the best known modern cases in the early 1990s, when some residents of Taos, New Mexico, reported a persistent low frequency noise usually compared to a diesel engine or tractor. Contemporary reporting stressed a feature that recurs in later cases: the noise was deeply disruptive for hearers, yet most residents did not hear it. [Los Angeles Times] latimes.com Los Angeles Times In Taos, Researchers Can Hum It, but They Can't Name Los Angeles Times In Taos, Researchers Can Hum It, but They Can't Name The case drew scientific attention because it was not merely a folklore rumour. A 1995 account in Echoes , the newsletter of the Acoustical Society of America, described the Taos investigation as involving researchers from several institutions after the reports reached New Mexico’s congressional delegation. [Acoustical Society of America] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of America The mystery of the Taos hum Acoustical Society of America The mystery of the Taos hum Later summaries of United States hum studies note that the Taos work was inconclusive: investigators did not identify a known acoustic signal that could account for the reports. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The results of hum studies in the United States The results of hum studies in the United States That inconclusive result is often misunderstood. It does not mean the witnesses invented the experience . It means the investigation failed to connect the reports to a measurable, shared external source. For Forteana , Taos is important because it shows the structure of a durable anomaly: sincere reports, serious attention, plausible mundane hypotheses, and no decisive public closure. Kokomo: when a source is found but the story continues Kokomo , Indiana, is useful because it complicates the idea that all hums are either “real” or “imagined”. In 2003, after many residents complained of a hum and associated health effects, an acoustic investigation identified two industrial low frequency sources: a 36 Hz tone linked to a cooling tower at a DaimlerChrysler casting plant and a 10 Hz tone linked to an air compressor intake at Haynes International. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Hum The Hum The important twist is that reports did not simply vanish once those sources were addressed. A later discussion by the lead investigator treated Kokomo alongside Taos and noted the difficulty of separating acoustical findings from non acoustical effects attributed to the same event. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The results of hum studies in the United States The results of hum studies in the United States Kokomo therefore demonstrates a common risk in hum cases: investigators may find a real source, but that does not automatically explain every witness experience, every symptom, or every later report. Once a community has formed around a mystery, the social investigation can outlast the technical one. Who’s Behind the Hum No One Can Escape on This Scottish Island? Channel: BRIGHT SIDE Open on YouTube Windsor: the border town hum and the problem of jurisdiction The Windsor Hum, reported from Windsor, Ontario, from around 2011, became a major local anxiety because many suspected heavy industry across the Detroit River, especially the industrial area around Zug Island. The Canadian government funded a study by researchers from the University of Windsor and the University of Western Ontario after years of complaints about recurring vibration and noise. [Canada] canada.ca Open source on canada.ca. The 2014 summary found signals in the 30 Hz and 35 Hz bands associated with public reports, but it did not definitively pinpoint a source. Crucially, the report said the most probable bearing pointed south of Zug Island and that the bulk of observations did not support the simple claim that the source emanated from Zug Island. It listed possible sources such as quarry activity or industrial ventilation systems, while emphasising the complexity of acoustic propagation in local conditions. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Global Affairs Canada Windsor shows why hum mysteries can become political. A low frequency sound may cross water, borders and jurisdictions. Residents want a responsible party; officials need evidence strong enough to act; suspected facilities may be outside the complaining authority’s control. The anxiety is not only “What is that sound?” but “Who has the power to make it"},{"title":"Body Sources","url":"/body-sources/","description":"Some hum reports may begin inside the listener through tinnitus-like perception, sensitivity, stress, or sleep disruption.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Body Sources Can the Hum Come From the Listener? Body Sources Body Sources Can the Hum Come From the Listener? Some hum reports may begin inside the listener through tinnitus like perception, sensitivity, stress, or sleep disruption. Some hum reports may begin inside the listener through tinnitus like perception, sensitivity, stress, or sleep disruption. mechanism Low frequency tinnitus and unusual sensitivity Stress, sleep loss, and symptom loops Why internal causes do not mean fake reports forteana odd hums body sources Introduction One of the most important explanations for unexplained hum reports is that, in some cases, the sound originates within the listener rather than the environment. This does not mean the experience is imaginary. Tinnitus, unusual sensitivity to low frequency sound, altered auditory processing, stress, and sleep disruption can all produce a genuine perception of humming, droning, or vibration without a shared external source. These possibilities help explain a long standing feature of hum cases: one person may be profoundly disturbed while family members, neighbours, and acoustic instruments detect nothing unusual. Research suggests that body based mechanisms probably account for some reports, while other cases involve genuine environmental low frequency noise. The challenge is distinguishing between the two rather than assuming that every report has the same cause. [PMC+2BMJ Best Practice] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Tinnitus: Characteristics, Causes, Mechanisms, and Treatmentsby BI Han · 2009 · Cited by 515 — Tinnitus is defined as a phantom audito... Can a Hum Come From the Listener? The best established internal explanation is subjective tinnitus: the perception of sound without an external acoustic source. Although tinnitus is popularly associated with ringing, clinicians recognise many other descriptions, including buzzing, hissing, roaring and humming. Some patients report sounds with an unusually low pitch that resemble an idling engine or distant machinery, making them easy to mistake for environmental noise. [BMJ Best Practice] bestpractice.bmj.com BMJ Best PracticeTinnitus Symptoms, diagnosis and treatmentSounds may be soft or loud and may be described as ringing, humming, buzzing... This distinction matters in Fortean discussions because a low frequency internal sound can initially behave exactly like an external mystery. A person may search the house, inspect electrical appliances, or go outside expecting to find a mechanical source. Only after repeated failures—or after noticing the sound follows them between locations—does an internal explanation become plausible. Recent research focusing specifically on people who report \"the Hum\" has strengthened this possibility. Investigators tested individuals who regularly perceived unexplained low frequency sounds and found little evidence that unusually sensitive low frequency hearing or audible sounds produced by the ear itself explained most cases. Instead, the findings pointed towards low frequency subjective tinnitus as the most likely mechanism for many participants, while still acknowledging that genuine external hums also exist. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.June 25, 2026 — For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has b... Published: June 25, 2026 ➡️¿Por qué tengo RUIDOS 🔊en los OÍDOS 👂 por la noche? tipos de TINNITUS Y ACÚFENOS Channel: Dr. Ignacio Alcalá Médico del Oído Open on YouTube Low frequency tinnitus and unusual sensitivity Low frequency tinnitus appears to be less common than the high pitched form familiar to most clinicians, but it can be especially confusing because its character resembles ordinary environmental sounds. Instead of a whistle or ring, sufferers may describe: A distant diesel engine. An electrical transformer. Heavy ventilation equipment. A continuous drone. A deep vibration or rumble. Because humans are relatively poor at locating low frequency sounds, whether internal or external, sufferers may genuinely feel that the sound comes from \"everywhere\" rather than from one ear or one direction. This perceptual ambiguity makes self diagnosis difficult. [BMJ Best Practice+2PMC] bestpractice.bmj.com BMJ Best PracticeTinnitus Symptoms, diagnosis and treatmentSounds may be soft or loud and may be described as ringing, humming, buzzing... Research into complaints about low frequency noise has also found that some complainants are exposed to measurable environmental sound while others appear to experience low frequency tinnitus instead. The two groups may describe remarkably similar symptoms despite having different underlying causes. [Aalborg Universitets forskningsportal] vbn.aau.dk Aalborg Universitets forskningsportalA detailed study of low frequency noise complaintsby CS Pedersen — The results showed that some of t... Pulsatile Tinnitus: 7 Different Anatomic Causes of Hearing Pulsations in the Ear Channel: Fauquier ENT Open on YouTube Stress, Sleep Loss, and Symptom Loops One reason hum reports can become persistent is that hearing and attention form a feedback loop. Once an intrusive sound becomes emotionally important, the brain allocates more attention to detecting it. Increased monitoring makes the sound seem more noticeable, which raises distress, which further increases monitoring. This cycle is well recognised in tinnitus research. Psychological stress does not simply arise because tinnitus is unpleasant; stress can also amplify the perceived loudness and intrusiveness of tinnitus through interactions between auditory processing, attention, and emotional centres of the brain. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The association between stress, emotional states, and tinnitusby JD Patil · 2023 · Cited by 92 — Patients suffering from tinnitus perc... Sleep disruption can intensify the same process. Many hum reports describe symptoms becoming worst late at night, when external background noise falls away and bedrooms become unusually quiet. In that environment, internal auditory sensations receive less masking from ordinary environmental sound and therefore become more noticeable. Poor sleep then increases fatigue, anxiety and sensory vigilance the following day, creating another reinforcing cycle. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The association between stress, emotional states, and tinnitusby JD Patil · 2023 · Cited by 92 — Patients suffering from tinnitus perc... This feedback model helps explain why some long running hum cases become increasingly distressing even when no environmental change can be identified. Why Internal Causes Do Not Mean Fake Reports A common misunderstanding is that if a hum has an internal origin then the witness must be mistaken or inventing the experience. Modern hearing science does not support that conclusion. Subjective tinnitus is a genuine sensory perception generated within the auditory system. The sound is real to the person experiencing it despite lacking an external acoustic source. Brain imaging and neurophysiological research indicate that tinnitus reflects altered neural activity across auditory pathways and interconnected brain networks rather than conscious imagination or deliberate fabrication. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Tinnitus: Characteristics, Causes, Mechanisms, and Treatmentsby BI Han · 2009 · Cited by 515 — Tinnitus is defined as a phantom audito... This distinction is important when evaluating unexplained hum reports: The experience is genuine. The listener is perceiving a sound. The source may differ. The sound may arise from internal auditory processing rather than the outside world. Environmental investigations may still be worthwhile. Internal explanations cannot automatically dismiss reports because some communities have experienced measurable low frequency industrial noise. Different people may experience different mechanisms. One household member may hear external low frequency noise while another develops heightened awareness or tinnitus that continues after the original trigger has disappeared. [Aalborg Universitets forskningsportal+2ResearchGate] vbn.aau.dk Aalborg Universitets forskningsportalA detailed study of low frequency noise complaintsby CS Pedersen — The results showed that some of t... For investigators of Fortean reports, this means sincerity and external reality are separate questions. Witnesses can honestly report what they hear even if the ultimate source lies within the auditory system. Understanding Pulsatile Tinnitus: What You Need to Know About Diagnosis and Treatment Channel: Weill Cornell Medicine Open on YouTube Why Body Based Explanations Fit Some Hum Cases Better Than Others Certain recurring features make an internal explanation more plausible, although none is individually decisive. A body based origin becomes more likely when the hum: persists across many different locations, remains"},{"title":"Support Groups","url":"/support-groups/","description":"Hum mysteries often grow through neighbour comparisons, support groups, official responses, and arguments over being dismissed.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Support Groups How a Local Hum Becomes a Community Mystery Support Groups Support Groups How a Local Hum Becomes a Community Mystery Hum mysteries often grow through neighbour comparisons, support groups, official responses, and arguments over being dismissed. Hum mysteries often grow through neighbour comparisons, support groups, official responses, and arguments over being dismissed. population context Why isolated complaints become shared cases How support groups shape evidence and anxiety The line between practical help and dismissal forteana odd hums support groups Introduction When an unexplained hum persists in a neighbourhood, the mystery is rarely confined to sound alone. A handful of people begin comparing experiences, neighbours discover that some hear the noise while others do not, online discussion groups appear, and local authorities receive growing numbers of complaints. At that point the phenomenon becomes a social event as much as an acoustic one. Within Fortean studies, this transformation is significant because it shows how uncertain sensory experiences develop into shared community narratives without requiring either deliberate deception or a paranormal explanation. Support groups can provide practical assistance, emotional validation and organised evidence gathering, but they can also influence how reports spread, how people interpret ambiguous experiences, and how disputes with officials become part of the story. [The World Hum Map] thehum.info The World Hum MapThe World Hum Map and Database Project Dr Glen...This website documents and maps the self reported data from people a... Why isolated complaints become shared cases Most reported hums begin with individuals who assume they are alone. Because low frequency sounds are difficult to localise and may not be audible to everyone, many witnesses initially suspect a fault in their own home before asking neighbours whether they hear the same thing. Once several residents recognise similar experiences, a distinct pattern often follows: Local social media groups and community forums become places to compare descriptions. Residents identify common times, locations and weather conditions. Complaints to councils or environmental health departments become coordinated rather than individual. Local newspapers report the story, attracting additional witnesses who may never previously have spoken about it. The World Hum Map and Database has documented this process for decades by collecting self reported cases from around the world. Rather than attempting to prove a single explanation, it functions as a repository where people can compare experiences, discuss possible causes and contribute observations that may otherwise remain isolated. [The World Hum Map] thehum.info The World Hum MapThe World Hum Map and Database Project Dr Glen...This website documents and maps the self reported data from people a... This pattern helps explain why famous hum cases often expand rapidly after the first media coverage. Publicity does not necessarily create the phenomenon, but it gives existing witnesses a language and community through which to describe it. The Controversial Sound Only 2% Of People Hear Channel: Benn Jordan Open on YouTube How support groups shape evidence and anxiety Support groups perform two different roles at the same time. On the practical side, they often improve investigations. Residents may compare recordings, identify recurring locations, log dates and times, or encourage members to submit complaints through official channels instead of relying on rumours. Organised reporting can help investigators determine whether reports cluster geographically or correspond to industrial activity, weather changes or particular times of day. Support groups also provide emotional reassurance. Many people reporting a hum describe frustration at being unable to persuade family members or neighbours that anything unusual is happening. Simply discovering that others report similar experiences can reduce feelings of isolation, even if the cause remains uncertain. Researchers studying environmental low frequency noise have long noted that unresolved complaints can become highly stressful, particularly when sufferers believe they are not being taken seriously. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Low frequency noise and annoyanceby HG Leventhall · 2004 · Cited by 469 — Low frequency noise, the frequency range from about 10 Hz... At the same time, these communities may unintentionally reinforce anxiety. When people repeatedly discuss symptoms, compare theories and monitor for the sound every evening, attention becomes focused on detecting the hum. Modern hearing research suggests that increased attention can make faint sounds—whether external or internally generated—more noticeable. This possibility does not invalidate witnesses' experiences, but it helps explain why support communities can sometimes strengthen awareness of an already intrusive perception. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.June 25, 2026 — For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has b... Published: June 25, 2026 When official investigations meet organised residents Community organisation frequently changes how authorities respond. Environmental health officers are more likely to investigate when complaints arrive from multiple households than from a single resident. Coordinated reporting may lead to: Temporary acoustic monitoring. Inspections of nearby industrial facilities. Examination of ventilation systems, pumps or compressors. Requests for specialist low frequency noise assessments. However, many investigations end without a definitive answer. Low frequency sound travels over long distances, changes with atmospheric conditions and may exist near the threshold of hearing. Professional guidance from the Institute of Acoustics notes that identifying a source can therefore be exceptionally difficult, especially when conventional noise measurements do not correspond neatly with residents' experiences. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of AcousticsLow Frequency Noise (LFN) AnnoyanceLFN can travel long distances, so sources of low frequency noise are often chall... This uncertainty often creates tension. Residents may interpret inconclusive findings as institutional dismissal , while investigators may conclude only that available evidence is insufficient to identify a single source. The disagreement concerns standards of evidence rather than necessarily implying bad faith on either side. The Mysterious Case of the Windsor Hum (HBO) Channel: VICE News Open on YouTube The line between practical help and dismissal One of the defining social features of hum reports is the conflict between validation and explanation. People affected by persistent low frequency sounds often object to being told that the problem is \"just tinnitus\" before environmental causes have been examined. Equally, clinicians point out that tinnitus and other auditory conditions are genuine medical experiences rather than imagined ones. Modern research increasingly suggests that reported hums may arise from several different mechanisms rather than one universal cause. Some cases involve measurable environmental sound, others appear consistent with low frequency tinnitus or related auditory perception, while still others remain unresolved. [Popular Mechanics+2PMC] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.June 25, 2026 — For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has b... Published: June 25, 2026 Support groups therefore face a delicate balance: Constructive communities encourage systematic observations, medical consultation where appropriate and cooperation with environmental investigators. Less constructive communities may become committed to a single explanation, treating contradictory evidence as proof of concealment or incompetence. Neither outcome is inevitable. Much depends on whether the group's culture encourages evidence gathering or primarily reinforces shared certainty. Community identity after the mystery Some hum cases eventually acquire a local identity that persists even after the original disturbance fades. Residents continue discussing the event, journalists revisit the story, documentaries appear, and newcomers learn about the area's \"hum mystery\" alongside other pieces of local folklore . The social memory can outlast the sound itself. In places where investigations identify probable industrial sources, debate may continue over whether the explanation was complete. In locations where no convincing source is found, uncertainty itself becomes part of local identity. Recent cases, including investigations in places such as Omagh, have shown how organised"},{"title":"Kokomo","url":"/kokomo/","description":"Kokomo shows how finding real industrial tones can still leave witness symptoms, later reports, and public doubt unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Kokomo When a Hum Source Is Found but Doubt Remains Kokomo Kokomo When a Hum Source Is Found but Doubt Remains Kokomo shows how finding real industrial tones can still leave witness symptoms, later reports, and public doubt unresolved. Kokomo shows how finding real industrial tones can still leave witness symptoms, later reports, and public doubt unresolved. event time window The industrial tones identified in Kokomo Why complaints did not neatly end What Kokomo teaches about mixed causes forteana odd hums kokomo Introduction The Kokomo Hum is one of the few famous hum cases in which investigators identified specific industrial sound sources rather than leaving the phenomenon entirely unexplained. In 2003, an extensive acoustic investigation in Kokomo, Indiana, linked residents' complaints to two distinct low frequency tones originating from local industrial facilities. Yet the case did not end with that technical success. Even after the identified equipment was modified, some residents continued to report hearing a hum or experiencing related symptoms. That outcome has made Kokomo an important case within Fortean discussions: not because it demonstrates a paranormal mystery, but because it shows that solving part of a problem does not necessarily resolve the entire social and personal experience surrounding it. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The results of hum studies in the United States hum study was commissioned in Kokomo, Indiana, where hundreds of residents reported hearing a hum and blamed other, non acoustical effect... The industrial tones identified in Kokomo Unlike many reported hums, Kokomo received a detailed engineering investigation commissioned after hundreds of residents complained about a persistent low frequency noise and associated discomfort. Acoustic consultant James P. Cowan and colleagues monitored sound and vibration throughout the city, compared recordings from multiple neighbourhoods, and traced two recurring tonal signals to specific industrial equipment. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The results of hum studies in the United States hum study was commissioned in Kokomo, Indiana, where hundreds of residents reported hearing a hum and blamed other, non acoustical effect... The investigation identified two principal sources: A 36 Hz tone generated by a cooling tower on the roof of the DaimlerChrysler casting plant. A 10 Hz tone produced by air compressor intakes at the Haynes International facility. Rather than relying on witness testimony alone, investigators used directional microphone techniques, spectral analysis and measurements taken progressively closer to the suspected equipment. The 36 Hz tone became stronger as investigators approached the cooling tower, while the 10 Hz tone could similarly be traced to compressor intakes. The measured frequencies matched those recorded in residential areas where complaints had been made. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... The investigation also examined other suspected industrial sites, including Martin Marietta Aggregates and the Gerber Plumbing Fixtures plant. After on site inspections and measurements, these facilities were ruled out because investigators could not detect tones matching those heard within affected neighbourhoods. This systematic elimination strengthened confidence that the DaimlerChrysler and Haynes facilities were genuine contributors to at least part of the reported noise problem. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... Another significant finding was what investigators did not discover. Ground vibration measurements remained well below recognised perception thresholds, suggesting that widespread building vibration was unlikely to explain residents' experiences. The evidence therefore pointed primarily towards airborne low frequency sound rather than seismic vibration. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... The HUM Sample (Kokomo Hum Taos Hum Bristol Hum) Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube Why complaints did not neatly end The Kokomo investigation is frequently misunderstood as proving that the mystery was completely solved. In reality, the engineering study itself anticipated a more complicated outcome. Its recommendations were cautious. Investigators proposed reducing the identified tones, repeating measurements afterwards, and then determining whether residents still experienced the hum. They explicitly recognised that identifying measurable sound sources was only one part of the overall problem. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... Subsequent accounts indicate that reports did not disappear entirely after industrial modifications. This persistence became an important feature of later discussions about the Hum. It did not necessarily mean that the original engineering conclusions were wrong. Instead, it suggested several possibilities: the industrial tones explained only some reports; additional low frequency sources may have existed; some listeners may have remained unusually sensitive to environmental sounds; prolonged anxiety, sleep disruption or heightened attention may have continued after the loudest sources were reduced. The investigators themselves distinguished between acoustic issues , which instruments could measure, and non acoustic issues , referring to experiences reported by people even when corresponding airborne sound could not be detected. They did not claim to have identified the cause of every complaint, and they recommended further research rather than declaring the case closed. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... This distinction is often lost in popular retellings . Finding real industrial noise did not automatically demonstrate that every reported symptom shared the same origin. What Kokomo teaches about mixed causes Kokomo has become an unusually valuable comparison point because it challenges two overly simple interpretations of hum reports. The first simplistic view is that every hum must have a mysterious or unknown origin. Kokomo clearly demonstrates that persistent low frequency industrial tones can travel through communities, be difficult to localise and create genuine disturbance. The engineering investigation identified measurable sources using conventional acoustic methods rather than extraordinary explanations. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... The opposite simplistic view is that identifying one source completely resolves every complaint. Kokomo does not support that conclusion either. Even the investigators acknowledged that measurable sounds and residents' wider experiences might not correspond perfectly. Some complaints continued after the identified equipment was addressed, leaving open the possibility that multiple mechanisms were operating simultaneously. [Scribd] scribd.com Haynes International facility. Daimler Chrysler Casting Plant. We have identified sources of 10 Hz... From a historical perspective, Kokomo therefore occupies an unusual position among famous hum cases. It represents one of the strongest demonstrations that genuine environmental low frequency noise can underlie community reports, while simultaneously illustrating that technical remediation may not eliminate public uncertainty or personal distress. The Kokomo Hum: An Acoustical Investigation Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube The Mystery of the Hum of Kokomo Channel: The World’s Most Mysterious Events Open on YouTube Why Kokomo remains important in Fortean discussions Within the broader study of unexplained hums, Kokomo is less a solved mystery than a reminder that environmental anomalies can have layered explanations. The case shows that engineering investigations can successfully identify real acoustic sources, yet still leave unanswered questions about why only certain people report hearing them, why symptoms vary between individuals, and why confidence does not necessarily return once a technical intervention has been made. [icben.ethz.ch+2Scribd] icben.ethz.ch The results of hum studies in the United States hum study was commissioned in Kokomo, Indiana, where hundreds of residents reported hearing a hum and blamed other, non acoustical effect... For Fortean studies, this makes Kokomo distinctive. Rather than supporting either complete scepticism or extraordinary speculation, it demonstrates how measurable physical phenomena, individual perception, health concerns and community expectations can become intertwined. The enduring significance of the case lies precisely in that incomplete closure: finding a source was an"},{"title":"Night Indoors","url":"/night-indoors/","description":"Low-frequency hums often feel worse indoors at night because walls, rooms, and sleep disruption can magnify the experience.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Night Indoors Why Hums Feel Worse Inside at Night Night Indoors Night Indoors Why Hums Feel Worse Inside at Night Low frequency hums often feel worse indoors at night because walls, rooms, and sleep disruption can magnify the experience. Low frequency hums often feel worse indoors at night because walls, rooms, and sleep disruption can magnify the experience. mechanism How low frequencies move through buildings Why rooms can create misleading hotspots How night time quiet changes perception forteana odd hums night indoors Introduction Many reports of unexplained hums share one striking feature: the sound seems most intrusive inside the home after dark. This pattern does not necessarily point to a mysterious source. Low frequency sound behaves differently from higher pitched noise, and ordinary buildings can change how it is experienced. At the same time, the quieter acoustic environment of night makes faint sounds easier to notice, while sleep disruption can increase awareness and distress. These interacting mechanisms help explain why a hum that is barely noticeable outdoors can seem overwhelming in a bedroom, even when investigators struggle to identify a single obvious source. [Institute of Acoustics+2LWW Journals] ioa.org.uk Institute of AcousticsLow Frequency Noise (LFN) AnnoyanceLFN can travel long distances, so sources of low frequency noise are often chall... Within the broader history of unexplained hum reports, this indoor night time pattern is one of the most consistent observations. Understanding how buildings, room acoustics and human perception interact provides a more useful explanation than assuming that every case reflects either a hidden industrial source or an entirely psychological experience . How low frequencies move through buildings Low frequency sound, generally below about 200 Hz, has wavelengths measured in metres rather than centimetres. Because of these long wavelengths, it behaves differently from speech or birdsong. Instead of being easily blocked by walls, it can pass through building structures, travel around obstacles and excite vibrations in floors, ceilings and window frames. [LWW Journals] journals.lww.com low frequency noise and annoyance.6.aspx LWW JournalsLow Frequency Noise and Annoyanceby HG Leventhall · 2004 · Cited by 472 — Low frequency noise, considered as the frequency ra... This means that a distant source such as large ventilation equipment, pumps, compressors, industrial machinery or heavy transport may be more noticeable indoors than expected. Buildings do not simply keep sound out; under some circumstances they can transmit or redistribute low frequency energy in ways that make it appear stronger in particular rooms. The Institute of Acoustics notes that these characteristics are one reason low frequency noise complaints are often difficult to investigate and trace to a single source. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of AcousticsLow Frequency Noise (LFN) AnnoyanceLFN can travel long distances, so sources of low frequency noise are often chall... Another complication is that low frequency sound is difficult for people to localise. Human hearing relies partly on differences between the ears to determine where sound originates, but those cues become much weaker at very low frequencies. As a result, listeners often describe the hum as coming from \"everywhere\", from inside the walls, or from no obvious direction at all. Recent research into persistent hum perception highlights this difficulty in localisation as one of the defining characteristics of reported cases. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.June 25, 2026 — For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has b... Published: June 25, 2026 Silencing unwanted vibrations: unlocking the best strategies Channel: Soundproofist Open on YouTube Why rooms can create misleading hotspots A room is not an acoustically neutral space. Reflections from walls, ceilings and floors can reinforce particular low frequencies while reducing them elsewhere. These standing waves, or room modes, create pressure maxima and minima, meaning a sound may seem loud beside one wall yet almost disappear only a metre away. [blog.soton.ac.uk+2deicon.com] blog.soton.ac.uk Room modes Sound WavesRoom modes. Standing wave phenomena can also occur in a room at specific frequencies called the resonance frequen... This helps explain several common witness descriptions: A hum is strongest in one bedroom but absent in another. Walking around the room causes the sound to fade and return. Moving the bed changes how noticeable the noise becomes. The sound appears to come from the floor, ceiling or corners rather than from outdoors. These effects can easily mislead listeners into believing the source is inside the house when the actual sound originates elsewhere. Conversely, an external sound may become less noticeable outside because there are fewer reflections reinforcing that particular frequency. Investigators of domestic low frequency complaints often encounter this uneven distribution. Measurements taken at one position may underestimate what a resident experiences at another location only a short distance away, making disagreements between occupants and inspectors understandable rather than evidence that either side is mistaken. [apexacoustics.co.uk+2Aalborg Universitets forskningsportal] apexacoustics.co.uk its tonality and impulsiveness. Annoyance and human perception to noise are not dealt...Read more... Why night time quiet changes perception The physical environment changes significantly after dark. Traffic falls, neighbourhood activity decreases and household appliances are switched off. As the general background noise level drops, faint low frequency sounds that were previously masked become easier to hear. [acoustics.asn.au] acoustics.asn.au Determinants of annoyance from humming sound as...by I van Kamp · 2017 · Cited by 10 — This is in line with the notion that low frequenc... This does not necessarily mean the hum itself has become louder. Instead, the contrast between the hum and its surroundings has increased. A steady drone that is effectively hidden during a busy afternoon may become the dominant sound in an otherwise quiet bedroom. Night also changes the listener. In quiet surroundings people naturally become more aware of subtle sensory input, particularly when trying to fall asleep. Once attention focuses on a repetitive sound, it becomes difficult to ignore. Repeated nights of disturbed sleep can further increase sensitivity and frustration, creating a cycle in which anticipation of the hum makes it easier to notice. Research on low frequency noise consistently identifies sleep disturbance and annoyance as among its most important consequences. [PubMed+2PMC] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Effects of low frequency noise on sleepby KP Waye · 2004 · Cited by 94 — Sleep disturbance, especially with regard to time to fall... Can You Feel It? Why Gigawatt Hyperscale Data Centers Are a Threat to Our Homes and Health Channel: Data Center Research Open on YouTube Why not everyone in the same house hears it One of the most puzzling aspects of unexplained hum reports is that different members of the same household often disagree about whether any sound exists. Several mechanisms may contribute: Different hearing sensitivity. People's ability to detect very low frequencies varies considerably, especially at frequencies near the limits of hearing. Some individuals are substantially more sensitive than average. [LWW Journals] journals.lww.com low frequency noise and annoyance.6.aspx LWW JournalsLow Frequency Noise and Annoyanceby HG Leventhall · 2004 · Cited by 472 — Low frequency noise, considered as the frequency ra... Different listening positions. Because room modes create acoustic hotspots, two people sleeping in different rooms—or even different parts of the same room—may experience different sound levels. [blog.soton.ac.uk] blog.soton.ac.uk Room modes Sound WavesRoom modes. Standing wave phenomena can also occur in a room at specific frequencies called the resonance frequen... Internal auditory perception. Some persistent hum experiences may arise from low frequency tinnitus rather than an external sound source. Recent studies suggest that both genuine environmental sounds and internally generated perception can occur, and distinguishing between them is not always straightforward. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.June 25, 2026 — For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has b... Published: June 25, 2026 These possibilities are not mutually exclusive. An external low frequency sound may exist while an individual's hearing characteristics or room acoustics"},{"title":"Taos Hum","url":"/taos-hum/","description":"The Taos Hum became the model modern hum mystery because sincere reports met serious study without a clear shared source.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Taos Hum Why Did the Taos Hum Stay Unsolved? Taos Hum Taos Hum Why Did the Taos Hum Stay Unsolved? The Taos Hum became the model modern hum mystery because sincere reports met serious study without a clear shared source. The Taos Hum became the model modern hum mystery because sincere reports met serious study without a clear shared source. event time window What residents reported in Taos What investigators looked for Why inconclusive evidence mattered forteana odd hums taos hum Introduction The Taos Hum became the defining modern example of an unexplained low frequency sound because it combined three unusual features: a consistent group of sincere witnesses, an unusually serious scientific investigation, and a complete failure to identify a single external cause. Beginning in the early 1990s, a small proportion of residents around Taos, New Mexico, reported hearing a persistent low, pulsing sound often compared to a distant diesel engine or idling lorry. Although the reports attracted the attention of engineers, acousticians, government laboratories and members of the US Congress, investigators could neither verify one shared acoustic source nor dismiss the experiences as imaginary. That unresolved gap between testimony and measurement is why the Taos Hum remains one of the best known cases in Fortean discussions of unexplained environmental phenomena. Acoustical Society of America+2icben.ethz.ch [acousticalsociety.org] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... What residents reported in Taos The people who heard the Hum did not describe it as a dramatic or mysterious noise. Instead, most accounts focused on its persistence. Witnesses commonly compared it to an idling engine, distant industrial machinery or a low electrical drone that seemed impossible to locate. Many said it was more noticeable indoors than outside, particularly at night when background sounds were minimal. [Los Angeles Times] latimes.com la xpm 1993 07 18 me 14295 story Grams hears it: a constant, irritating hum that deprives her of sleep and depletes her energy. Steven Walters hears it: a low... Just as striking was who did not hear it. Only a small fraction of local residents reported the phenomenon. During the investigation, surveys suggested that around two percent of respondents identified themselves as hearers, although the exact sound they described varied in pitch and character. This selective perception immediately complicated the investigation because investigators could not assume that everyone in the same location was experiencing the same physical stimulus. Acoustical Society of America+2icben.ethz.ch [acousticalsociety.org] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... For those affected, however, the experience was real and often distressing. Many described disrupted sleep, difficulty concentrating and increasing anxiety as they searched unsuccessfully for the source. Some repeatedly checked appliances, drove through surrounding areas or left town temporarily to see whether the sound followed them. These practical efforts convinced many witnesses that they were responding to something genuine rather than imagining it. [Los Angeles Times+2Thrillist] latimes.com la xpm 1993 07 18 me 14295 story Grams hears it: a constant, irritating hum that deprives her of sleep and depletes her energy. Steven Walters hears it: a low... Unsolved Mysteries: The Mystery of the Taos Hum: Unexplained Low Frequency Phenomenon Channel: One Of Us Open on YouTube What investigators looked for Public attention eventually prompted New Mexico's congressional delegation to request a formal investigation in 1993. The study brought together specialists from the University of New Mexico, Los Alamos National Laboratory, Sandia National Laboratories and Phillips Air Force Laboratory, combining expertise in acoustics, engineering and environmental measurement. [Acoustical Society of America+2legendsofamerica.com] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... Rather than beginning with extraordinary explanations, investigators first examined ordinary environmental possibilities. They searched for: industrial machinery and compressors; electrical infrastructure; ventilation systems; geological or atmospheric sources; low frequency and infrasonic sound; electromagnetic phenomena; patterns linking reports to particular locations or times. Researchers also interviewed hearers extensively, looking for common characteristics that might distinguish them from other residents. The survey examined age, hearing history, descriptions of the sound and where it was perceived. Acoustical Society of America+2icben.ethz.ch [acousticalsociety.org] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... Despite extensive field measurements, investigators failed to identify any single sound that matched the reports. Microphones and other instruments did not consistently detect an environmental signal corresponding to what witnesses described. Equally important, different hearers often described somewhat different frequencies and sensations, making it difficult to define exactly what the investigation should be measuring. [Acoustical Society of America] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... Why the evidence remained inconclusive The Taos investigation is often misunderstood as having \"failed\" because scientists could not explain the Hum. In reality, the study successfully ruled out many simple explanations but could not demonstrate one shared external source. Several factors contributed to this impasse. The reports were internally consistent but externally difficult to verify. Witnesses often agreed about the Hum's persistence and annoyance, yet differed in its precise pitch, loudness and location. That suggested either multiple contributing causes or individual differences in perception rather than one easily measurable noise. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The author of this paper was the lead The results of hum studies in the United Statesby JP Cowan · Cited by 4 — The two largest funded hum studies in the United States were in... No instrument consistently detected the reported sound. Environmental monitoring failed to reveal a persistent low frequency signal that matched the witnesses' experiences. Without a reproducible physical measurement, investigators could not confidently identify a source or test proposed mechanisms. [Acoustical Society of America] acousticalsociety.org Acoustical Society of AmericaThe mystery of the Taos hum16 Apr 2017 — In 1993, they got the attention of the New Mexico congressional del... Human hearing itself became part of the investigation. Researchers considered whether unusually sensitive hearing, low frequency tinnitus, otoacoustic emissions (sounds generated within the inner ear) or interactions between subtle environmental sounds and individual hearing could explain why only certain people were affected. None of these possibilities fully accounted for every report, but neither could they be ruled out. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia The Hum The Hum Ordinary sources remained plausible without being demonstrable. Low frequency environmental noise can travel long distances, interact with buildings and produce standing wave effects that make localisation extremely difficult. Even if an ordinary source existed, proving that it matched every report proved impossible. [icben.ethz.ch] icben.ethz.ch The author of this paper was the lead The results of hum studies in the United Statesby JP Cowan · Cited by 4 — The two largest funded hum studies in the United States were in... Mystery of The Taos Hum Channel: Arihant Online Academy Open on YouTube The Taos Hum Mystery Why Only Some People Hear This Unexplained Sound in New Mexico Channel: Arihant Online Academy Open on YouTube Why the lack of an answer mattered The importance of the Taos Hum lies less in the mystery itself than in how investigators handled it. The case demonstrated that unusual reports can receive serious scientific attention without leading either to confirmation or outright dismissal . For Fortean researchers, Taos became a model because it resisted the two simplest narratives. Investigators did not uncover evidence for secret military technology, exotic geophysical phenomena or paranormal forces. At the same time, they also did not conclude that witnesses had fabricated their experiences. Instead, the official outcome was essentially one"},{"title":"Windsor","url":"/windsor/","description":"The Windsor Hum turned a local noise complaint into a cross-border dispute about evidence, authority, and responsibility.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Odd Hums","level":3,"text":"Windsor Who Could Stop the Windsor Hum? Windsor Windsor Who Could Stop the Windsor Hum? The Windsor Hum turned a local noise complaint into a cross border dispute about evidence, authority, and responsibility. The Windsor Hum turned a local noise complaint into a cross border dispute about evidence, authority, and responsibility. event time window Why residents suspected industry across the river What the Canadian study did and did not prove How borders complicate low frequency noise complaints forteana odd hums windsor Introduction The Windsor Hum became far more than a mysterious low frequency noise. It exposed a practical problem that is unusual even among famous Hum cases: many residents in Windsor, Ontario, believed the source lay across the Detroit River in the United States, leaving Canadian authorities responsible for responding to complaints without having clear authority over the suspected industrial facilities. The result was a years long dispute in which scientific evidence, municipal responsibility, diplomatic channels and public frustration became tightly intertwined. Rather than asking only what caused the Hum, officials increasingly faced a different question: who, if anyone, had the power to make it stop? [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 Why residents suspected industry across the river From 2011 onwards, many Windsor and LaSalle residents described a deep rumble or vibration resembling an idling diesel engine or blast furnace. The reports were intermittent but often strong enough to disturb sleep and shake buildings. Early seismic and acoustic investigations commissioned in Canada consistently suggested that the strongest signals arrived from the direction of the heavily industrialised American shoreline, particularly the vicinity of Zug Island in Michigan. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 The suspicion was understandable for several reasons: The reported direction pointed west across the Detroit River rather than towards Canadian industry. Zug Island contained large steel making operations capable of producing substantial low frequency sound and vibration. Residents frequently reported that the Hum's intensity varied with apparent industrial activity rather than with local weather or neighbourhood conditions. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 This geographical setting made the Windsor case distinctive. Most environmental noise complaints are handled by the jurisdiction where the suspected source operates. Here, the people experiencing the disturbance and the facilities under suspicion appeared to fall under different national legal systems. Scientific study addresses the mystery of the Windsor hum Channel: CBC News: The National Open on YouTube What the Canadian study did and did not prove The Canadian government funded an extensive investigation involving researchers from the University of Windsor and Western University after recommendations from the International Joint Commission, the long standing Canada–United States body that addresses shared boundary water issues. The project aimed not merely to confirm that residents were hearing something unusual, but to identify a specific source that could support regulatory action. [Canada] canada.ca Results of Study on 'Windsor Hum' ReleasedThe results of a study to identify the cause of the so called Windsor Hum. The Hum refers... The investigation reached two important but sometimes misunderstood conclusions. First, researchers concluded that the Windsor Hum was a genuine physical phenomenon. Monitoring equipment recorded low frequency acoustic signals consistent with residents' descriptions, showing that the reports could not simply be dismissed as imagination or mass suggestion. One University of Windsor investigation judged that the Hum's characteristics strongly suggested blast furnace operations on Zug Island as the likely source. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 However, another component of the same broader study was more cautious. Using infrasound arrays to determine the direction of arriving sound waves, researchers were unable to obtain enough simultaneous observations to pinpoint one definitive origin. Their measurements did not conclusively support Zug Island itself as the exact source, instead indicating that the most probable direction lay generally to the south west and could include other industrial processes or quarry related activity. The Hum occurred only intermittently during field campaigns, making definitive localisation especially difficult. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 This distinction mattered enormously. The studies substantially narrowed the search area but stopped short of producing the kind of unequivocal proof that would compel regulatory enforcement against a specific American facility. How borders complicated low frequency noise complaints The Windsor Hum illustrates how environmental governance becomes difficult when pollution—or suspected pollution—crosses an international boundary. Canadian agencies could: investigate complaints, deploy monitoring equipment, commission scientific studies, and communicate findings to American counterparts. They could not, however, inspect or regulate industrial equipment located on private property in Michigan without American cooperation. Even obtaining permission to deploy specialised source identification equipment on suspected sites proved challenging, and researchers specifically recommended that future measurements be allowed on Zug Island during periods when the Hum was active. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 For residents, this created an accountability gap. Local officials received complaints but lacked direct authority over the suspected source. American local governments, meanwhile, had no equivalent volume of complaints from affected residents because most reports originated across the river in Canada. The burden of proving responsibility therefore became unusually high before any cross border intervention could realistically occur. The situation also demonstrated a broader limitation of environmental regulation. Low frequency sound travels differently from many conventional pollutants, can fluctuate over time, and may become difficult to distinguish from complex industrial backgrounds. Those characteristics make enforcement difficult even within a single jurisdiction; across an international border they become considerably more complicated. Still no explanation for the mysterious Windsor hum haunting Ontario city, southeastern Michigan Channel: Click On Detroit Local 4 WDIV Open on YouTube Why the dispute persisted despite strong suspicion Public discussion often simplified the issue into a straightforward claim that \"the Hum came from Zug Island.\" The official scientific record was more nuanced. Researchers accumulated several strands of evidence pointing towards industrial activity in the area surrounding Zug Island, including directional measurements, frequency characteristics and correlations with reported events. Yet they also acknowledged that their monitoring campaigns failed to capture enough simultaneous data while the Hum was active to identify one individual machine or process beyond reasonable doubt . [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the 'Windsor Hum Study' Results23 May 2014 — The conclusion of the research is that the Windsor Hum does exist and has both qu... Published: May 2014 That uncertainty had practical consequences: Regulators lacked definitive attribution. Industrial operators faced suspicion without conclusive identification of a single offending process. Residents experienced continuing disturbance while investigations remained technically inconclusive. As a result, the Windsor Hum became less a mystery about whether something existed than a dispute over what standard of evidence should be required before governments act across an international boundary. A later development that reshaped the debate Although the formal"},{"title":"Weather","url":"/weather/","description":"Storms, waterspouts, whirlwinds, and heavy rain can make rare events look supernatural.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Weather When Weather Makes the World Look Fortean Weather Weather When Weather Makes the World Look Fortean Storms, waterspouts, whirlwinds, and heavy rain can make rare events look supernatural. Storms, waterspouts, whirlwinds, and heavy rain can make rare events look supernatural. mechanism Waterspouts and whirlwinds Rainfall illusions and animals Why rare weather becomes folklore forteana weather Introduction Strange falls sit near the centre of Forteana because they feel like a direct violation of ordinary weather: fish on a road after a storm, frogs scattered across a field, red rain staining walls, spiders seeming to drift down from the sky. The most useful explanation is not that the sky is secretly full of animals or impossible matter, but that storms, waterspouts , whirlwinds, floodwater, dust plumes and animal behaviour can briefly join up in ways that look supernatural. The same event can be real, misdescribed, locally meaningful and meteorologically plausible at the same time. Weather does not explain every old “fall” report with equal confidence. Many historical accounts are second hand, exaggerated or missing details such as wind direction, nearby water, timing and species identification. But the mechanism first approach changes the question. Instead of asking whether frogs can literally “rain”, it asks what storm process could lift, move, expose, wash out or concentrate animals and debris so that witnesses reasonably believe something has fallen from above. The Library of Congress gives this as the central caution: numerous reports exist, but many cases may involve storms flushing animals from burrows, knocking birds from trees, washing fish from water margins, or driving frogs into view rather than lifting them high into the sky. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Instead, storms fill in worm burrows, knock birds from trees and roofs, wash fish onto the shores of rivers...Read more... WX Fun 1on1: Does it really rain frogs and fish? Channel: WVTM 13 News Open on YouTube Waterspouts and whirlwinds: the classic Fortean mechanism The best known natural explanation for animal falls is the vortex: a rotating column of air capable of lifting light material and dropping it elsewhere. In ordinary language this is usually described as a waterspout, but meteorologists distinguish between fair weather waterspouts and tornadic waterspouts. The US National Weather Service describes tornadic waterspouts as tornadoes that form over water or move from land to water, associated with severe thunderstorms, high winds, rough seas, hail and dangerous lightning. [National Weather Service] weather.gov National Weather ServiceAbout WaterspoutsWaterspouts are similar to tornadoes over water. simply tornadoes that form over water, often ac... NOAA’s Ocean Today gives the same distinction: tornadic waterspouts may begin as land tornadoes or form in severe thunderstorms over water, whereas fair weather waterspouts are generally a different, usually less violent, category. [Ocean Today] oceantoday.noaa.gov Ocean Today Waterspouts Ocean Today Ocean Today Waterspouts Ocean Today That distinction matters because “a waterspout did it” is often used too loosely. A weak, fair weather waterspout is not a magic vacuum that selectively harvests fish, frogs and nothing else. A stronger tornadic vortex, storm updraught or small tornado can move loose objects, spray, mud, leaves, insects and small animals, but the details of distance, height and survival still matter. A credible weather explanation should fit the local geography: a pond, marsh, river, flooded field or shoreline close enough for the animals to be lifted or displaced. The Library of Congress notes that many scientists regard tornadic waterspouts as a possible explanation for some frog and fish falls, while also stressing that the phenomenon remains difficult to verify because scientists generally have not observed the full chain from animal pickup to later fall. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Instead, storms fill in worm burrows, knock birds from trees and roofs, wash fish onto the shores of rivers...Read more... That is a useful Fortean lesson. A mechanism can be physically plausible without proving that every famous story happened exactly as retold. A vortex explanation becomes stronger when several clues line up: A severe storm was present. Animal fall reports are more credible when they coincide with thunderstorms, squalls, tornado like winds or violent local rain. The animals are small and light. Tiny fish, tadpoles, frogs, insects and small invertebrates are more plausible cargo than large animals. The fall is localised. A narrow patch of animals fits a small vortex or rain cell better than a broad regional event. A nearby source exists. Ponds, drainage channels, floodwater and shallow lakes make the event less mysterious than an alleged long distance fall from the sea. Other debris appears too. Leaves, mud, weed, silt or small stones can support a weather transport explanation, even if witnesses focus on the animals. Charles Fort himself was fascinated by such cases because they seemed to expose the limits of neat explanation. His Book of the Damned collected strange falls, including fish and frogs, as examples of data that conventional science had supposedly “damned” or pushed aside. Project Gutenberg’s summary of the book places mysterious falls of fish and frogs among the anomalous material Fort used to challenge accepted categories. [Project Gutenberg] gutenberg.org Open source on gutenberg.org. Modern meteorology does not remove the Fortean interest; it relocates it. The puzzle becomes less “what impossible thing happened?” and more “how did a rare but natural event become a story of impossible rain?” Rainfall illusions and animals: when the fall may not be a fall Many “rains” of animals may begin at ground level. Heavy rain can fill worm burrows, flood ant nests, flush frogs from damp hiding places, strand fish from overflowing watercourses, or reveal animals already present but unnoticed. The Library of Congress explicitly warns that suburban and urban witnesses may underestimate how many small organisms live around them, making a sudden storm driven appearance look like a fall from the sky. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Instead, storms fill in worm burrows, knock birds from trees and roofs, wash fish onto the shores of rivers...Read more... This is especially important for frogs and worms. Amphibians often move during wet weather because damp conditions reduce the risk of drying out. After a heavy shower, a road, lawn or school playing field can suddenly be dotted with frogs that were hidden in vegetation, drains or nearby wet ground minutes earlier. Worms may surface after rain because their burrows are saturated or because moist surfaces allow safer movement. To a witness who notices the animals only after the rain begins, the sequence can feel obvious: rain fell, animals appeared, therefore animals fell with the rain. Birds add another layer. Fish found after a storm are often assumed to have been lifted from water by wind, but birds can drop, vomit or abandon fish when startled. A modern example is the fish fall reported in Texarkana, Texas, in December 2021. Early reports framed it as “raining fish” after storms moved through the area, but later independent investigation by Sharon A. Hill and Paul Cropper argued that the fish probably came from birds, likely cormorants, which are known to expel stomach contents under stress. [WSLS] wsls.com Texas city warned of 'animal rain' after fish were seen Texas city warned of 'animal rain' after fish were seen [Sharon A. Hill] sharonahill.com Sharon A. Hill Texarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill Sharon A. Hill Texarkana Fish Rain Mystery Solved Sharon A. Hill Popular Science’s account of the investigation noted that some fish appeared partly digested and that local reports placed large flocks of cormorants near the airport at the relevant time. [Popular Science] popsci.com Popular Science We may know why fish rained from the sky over Texas Popular Science We may know why fish rained from the sky over Texas That case is valuable because it shows how a weather event can be real without the first weather explanation being correct. A storm may still be part of the story: it startles birds, concentrates witnesses indoors and outdoors at particular moments, and gives the event a dramatic frame. But the immediate transport mechanism may be biological rather than meteorological. The same caution applies to the famous fish events at Yoro in Honduras. Reports describe fish appearing after heavy seasonal storms, and the local tradition has become culturally important. The usual explanations include strong winds or waterspouts, but another possibility is that heavy rain floods underground streams, drainage channels or hidden freshwater habitats, leaving fish stranded after water recedes. The important point is that the"},{"title":"Debris Clues","url":"/debris-clues/","description":"Leaves, mud, weed and silt can make an animal-fall report more plausible by pointing to a nearby source and transport path.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Debris Clues The Debris That Makes Animal Falls Believable Debris Clues Debris Clues The Debris That Makes Animal Falls Believable Leaves, mud, weed and silt can make an animal fall report more plausible by pointing to a nearby source and transport path. Leaves, mud, weed and silt can make an animal fall report more plausible by pointing to a nearby source and transport path. dataset evidence Why debris matters in strange fall reports Matching animals to nearby sources When missing debris weakens a story forteana weather debris clues Introduction Reports of fish, frogs or other small animals apparently falling from the sky are often judged on the animals alone. In practice, one of the strongest clues lies in everything that falls with them. Leaves, aquatic weeds, mud, silt, twigs and other storm carried debris can reveal where the animals came from and how they were transported. Rather than treating an animal fall as an isolated mystery, investigators increasingly assess whether it fits a wider pattern of storm debris. When debris matches a nearby pond, marsh, river or flooded field, the event becomes more consistent with a weather driven transport process than with an unexplained anomaly. Conversely, an absence of expected debris does not automatically disprove a report, but it can weaken the case for a vortex or flood related explanation. Why debris matters in strange fall reports Storms capable of moving small aquatic animals rarely transport those animals in perfect isolation. A tornado, waterspout or intense convective updraught interacts with the surrounding environment, lifting spray, floating vegetation, sediment and loose organic matter alongside living organisms. Meteorologists therefore regard associated debris as an important part of the evidence chain rather than an incidental detail. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of Congress Can it rain frogs, fish, and other objects? Library of CongressMany scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for frog and fish rainfalls. According to Complete Wea... From an investigative perspective, debris provides several useful clues: It identifies the likely source environment. Pondweed, duckweed, reeds or algae point towards shallow freshwater rather than open sea. It supports a transport mechanism. Mud adhering to fish or frogs suggests recent disturbance of the bed or shoreline rather than animals appearing independently. It helps reconstruct the route. Wind thrown leaves, broken twigs and wet sediment may indicate the direction and intensity of the storm cell. It reduces reliance on witness interpretation. Debris can often be photographed, sampled or compared with local habitats even after the animals have been removed. This approach mirrors how severe weather investigators reconstruct tornado tracks from damaged vegetation and scattered material. In alleged animal falls, the surrounding debris effectively becomes part of the forensic record. Fish, Frogs And Ice Blocks Falling From The Sky Our World Channel: Our World Open on YouTube Matching animals to nearby sources A convincing weather explanation depends on whether the debris and the animals belong to the same local environment. For example, a report of small freshwater fish mixed with filamentous algae, pondweed and fine silt is easier to reconcile with a nearby pond or drainage ditch than with a distant lake. Likewise, frogs accompanied by marsh vegetation are more consistent with animals lifted from flooded wetlands than with an unexplained atmospheric origin. The key question is not simply \"Could a storm move these animals?\" but \"Does everything found at the scene resemble material from one identifiable source?\" Investigators therefore compare: the species present; attached vegetation or aquatic plants; sediment colour and texture; nearby water bodies and flood conditions; the storm track immediately before the reported fall. The well known observation that reported falls often contain animals of similar size also fits this reasoning. Material sharing similar weight, buoyancy and aerodynamic behaviour is more likely to remain together within turbulent airflow before being deposited over a relatively narrow area. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of Congress Can it rain frogs, fish, and other objects? Library of CongressMany scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for frog and fish rainfalls. According to Complete Wea... Debris patterns that strengthen a report No single piece of debris proves an animal fall occurred exactly as described, but certain combinations increase plausibility. Fresh aquatic vegetation. Plants that normally grow submerged or floating indicate recent disturbance of shallow water. If the vegetation is still wet and intertwined with fish or tadpoles, it supports a common origin. Fine mud and silt. Bottom sediments can be stirred into suspension by strong winds or vortex action. Fresh silt coating animals suggests they were removed together from the same habitat rather than arriving separately. Mixed organic material. Leaves, reed fragments, seeds and small sticks are frequently transported during severe storms. When these materials match vegetation surrounding a nearby pond or marsh, they help define a realistic source. Highly localised deposition. A patch containing animals, mud and vegetation confined to a few streets or fields is more consistent with local atmospheric transport than a broad regional distribution. Importantly, none of these indicators is unique to animal rain. Floodwaters, overflowing drainage channels and strong surface winds can produce similar mixtures. The debris therefore supports a weather explanation generally rather than proving one specific mechanism. Fish Raining from the Sky Channel: inFact with Brian Dunning Open on YouTube When missing debris weakens a story Some reported animal falls describe clean, undamaged animals scattered across dry ground with no accompanying mud, leaves or vegetation despite claims of violent weather. Such accounts deserve more cautious interpretation. Missing debris raises several questions: Was the event remembered or reported long after it occurred? Were witnesses focused only on the animals and ignored surrounding material? Could the animals already have been present on the ground before the storm? Might floodwater have receded before observers arrived? Historical reports often lack enough detail to answer these questions. Many nineteenth century newspaper accounts mention only the surprising animals while omitting ordinary environmental observations that modern investigators would consider essential. The absence of debris therefore does not invalidate older reports, but it limits how confidently they can be explained. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of Congress Can it rain frogs, fish, and other objects? Library of CongressMany scientists believe tornadic waterspouts may be responsible for frog and fish rainfalls. According to Complete Wea... Debris as a practical investigative tool Within Fortean research, debris has become less a curiosity than a practical means of testing competing explanations. Instead of asking whether a witness genuinely saw fish \"rain\" from the sky, investigators can examine whether the entire deposit behaves like storm transported material. Useful questions include: 1. Was there a recent thunderstorm, tornado, waterspout or intense local squall? 2. Did animals arrive with mud, aquatic plants or other storm debris? 3. Is there a plausible nearby source matching both the animals and the debris? 4. Does the debris distribution align with known storm movement? 5. Are there photographs showing the deposit before it was disturbed? These questions shift the investigation from extraordinary claims towards observable evidence. Fish fall from the sky during rainstorms in eastern Texas Rush Hour Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Why the wider debris field matters Treating animals as only one component of a storm debris field produces more reliable assessments than examining the animals alone. A fish surrounded by matching pondweed, wet silt and storm scattered vegetation tells a coherent environmental story; an isolated fish with no supporting context is much harder to interpret. For Fortean studies, this perspective preserves the mystery without abandoning critical analysis. The unusual feature is not merely that animals appeared after a storm, but whether every piece of accompanying debris fits a believable chain of transport from a real landscape to the place where witnesses found it. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to The Debris That Makes Animal Falls Believable. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains storm transport mechanisms behind debris falls. See on Amazon Book The Book of Unusual Knowledge By Publications International Ltd."},{"title":"Bird Drops","url":"/bird-drops/","description":"Some fish falls may come from startled birds dropping or regurgitating fish rather than from a waterspout.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Bird Drops When Birds Explain Falling Fish Bird Drops Bird Drops When Birds Explain Falling Fish Some fish falls may come from startled birds dropping or regurgitating fish rather than from a waterspout. Some fish falls may come from startled birds dropping or regurgitating fish rather than from a waterspout. mechanism How birds carry and expel fish Why storms can trigger sudden drops Signs of digestion, flocking and local sources forteana weather bird drops Introduction Not every report of fish appearing on roads, lawns or rooftops after a storm requires a waterspout or tornado. In some cases, a simpler explanation is that fish eating birds were startled into dropping or regurgitating their catch. Storms can flush birds from perches, disrupt feeding flights or force sudden evasive manoeuvres, causing fish to fall from relatively low altitude. The result may look like a miniature \"rain of fish\", especially if witnesses discover the fish only after the storm has passed. This mechanism does not explain every reported fish fall, but it provides a well supported alternative for localised incidents, particularly where only a handful of fish are involved or where the fish show signs of having been carried by birds rather than transported by violent weather . [The Library of Congress] loc.gov According to William Hayden Smith of Washington University, this makes sense The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?When it rains frogs or fishes, witnesses report only fish or only frogs... How birds carry and expel fish Many birds routinely transport fish through the air. Cormorants, pelicans, gulls, herons, ospreys and eagles all carry prey from feeding areas to roosts, nests or safer places to eat. Some swallow fish immediately, while others hold them in the bill or talons before consuming them. Fish may be dropped accidentally if another bird attacks, if the carrier is startled, or if it needs to lighten its load during difficult flight. Fish may also be regurgitated as part of a stress response or because an oversized meal cannot be swallowed safely. [Scottish Government+2Wikipedia] gov.scot Scottish Government Bird stomach contents analysis Scottish GovernmentBird stomach contents analysis final report: Goosander...September 21, 2022 — 21 Sept 2022 — This study analysed th... Published: September 21, 2022 This behaviour is well documented in ornithology. Fish eating birds regularly regurgitate partially digested food to feed their young, demonstrating that expelling fish is a normal biological process rather than an extraordinary event. Stress, disturbance or sudden escape manoeuvres can trigger similar responses outside the nesting context. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Open source on wikipedia.org. Does It Really Rain Fish in Honduras? (Lluvia de Peces) Channel: Smartest Year Ever Open on YouTube Why storms can trigger sudden drops Storms create several conditions that increase the likelihood of birds dropping prey without requiring the fish themselves to have been lifted by the weather. The most important mechanisms include: Sudden flushing from roosts. Thunder, lightning, hail or strong gusts can startle birds into rapid take off before they have finished handling prey. Loss of flight control. Turbulence makes carrying heavy or slippery fish more difficult. Predator and scavenger interactions. Storms may concentrate birds into smaller areas, increasing harassment by gulls or other birds attempting to steal food. Stress induced regurgitation. Birds sometimes eject stomach contents to reduce weight or as a physiological response to sudden disturbance. Unlike a waterspout, this process needs no powerful atmospheric lifting. The fish have already been removed from the water by the bird, and the storm merely interrupts the final stage of transport. [NBC 5 Dallas Fort Worth] nbcdfw.com NBC 5 Dallas Fort WorthScientists Figured Out Why Fish Fell From the Sky in...22 Jun 2022 — Two independent researchers say they may hav... Bird drops fish on driver's windshield Channel: ABC24 Memphis Open on YouTube Signs that point towards a bird origin Several clues can distinguish bird drops from a genuine weather transport event. A bird explanation becomes more convincing when observers find: only a few fish rather than hundreds; fish beneath known flight paths, nesting colonies or roosts; partially digested fish or damaged scales; puncture marks from talons or compression marks from a bill; species commonly taken by local fish eating birds; [assets.publishing.service.gov.uk] assets.publishing.service.gov.uk of Predation by Fish Eating Birds on Inland...• To gather evidence on the current impact of fish eating bird predation on inland fish st... no accompanying mud, aquatic vegetation or other storm carried debris. Conversely, reports involving large numbers of fresh, undamaged fish scattered over a broader area during severe storms may fit other mechanisms more closely, although each case still requires independent investigation. The Library of Congress notes that many apparent animal rains have more than one plausible explanation, and nearby wildlife behaviour should always be considered alongside meteorological causes. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov According to William Hayden Smith of Washington University, this makes sense The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?When it rains frogs or fishes, witnesses report only fish or only frogs... A modern example: the Texarkana fish fall A well known illustration came from Texarkana, Texas, in December 2021, when numerous people reported fish falling during stormy weather. Initial media coverage largely favoured the familiar waterspout explanation because \"animal rain\" has long been associated with tornadoes and powerful updraughts. Subsequent examination by independent researchers proposed a different interpretation. Airport personnel reported that many recovered fish appeared chewed or damaged rather than freshly lifted from water, and fish eating birds had been active in the area. Researchers argued that at least some of the fish were more consistent with being dropped or regurgitated by birds than transported through the atmosphere by a vortex. The case illustrates how a dramatic weather event can encourage a meteorological explanation even when biological evidence deserves equal attention. [NBC 5 Dallas Fort Worth+2Dallas News] nbcdfw.com NBC 5 Dallas Fort WorthScientists Figured Out Why Fish Fell From the Sky in...22 Jun 2022 — Two independent researchers say they may hav... Why this explanation matters in Fortean investigations Within Forteana , bird drops demonstrate why investigators benefit from separating the appearance of an event from its mechanism. Discovering fish after a thunderstorm naturally encourages people to connect the fish directly with the storm. Yet storms also disturb animal behaviour, creating opportunities for perfectly ordinary biological processes to produce extraordinary looking scenes. This does not eliminate the possibility that some reported fish falls involve waterspouts or other atmospheric transport. Instead, it widens the range of evidence that should be examined. Investigators can ask whether local fish eating birds were present, whether the fish bear feeding damage, whether nearby nesting or roosting sites exist, and whether the distribution matches individual bird movements rather than a descending column of storm debris. Bird related fish drops therefore occupy an important place among weather associated anomalies: they show that unusual post storm discoveries can arise from interactions between wildlife and weather, even when the atmosphere itself never carried the fish. The Library of Congress+2NBC 5 Dallas Fort Worth [loc.gov] loc.gov According to William Hayden Smith of Washington University, this makes sense The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?When it rains frogs or fishes, witnesses report only fish or only frogs... Bird Drops Fish in Backyard While Flying Low 1412496 Channel: RM Videos Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When Birds Explain Falling Fish. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains storms that may trigger fish drops. See on Amazon Book The Book of Unusual Knowledge By Publications International Ltd. Includes unusual natural events. See on Amazon Book Unexplained! By Jerome Clark Covers anomalous animal fall reports. See on Amazon Book Mysteries of the Unexplained By Reader's Digest Association Includes famous fish fall stories. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Weather For Dummies The Book of Unusual Knowledge Unexplained! As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia"},{"title":"Frog Rains","url":"/frog-rains/","description":"Many frog rains may begin when wet weather drives hidden amphibians onto roads, lawns and playing fields at once.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Frog Rains Did the Frogs Fall or Just Appear? Frog Rains Frog Rains Did the Frogs Fall or Just Appear? Many frog rains may begin when wet weather drives hidden amphibians onto roads, lawns and playing fields at once. Many frog rains may begin when wet weather drives hidden amphibians onto roads, lawns and playing fields at once. mechanism Why frogs move in wet weather How storms make hidden animals visible Clues that separate falls from migrations forteana weather frog rains Introduction Reports of “frog rain” are among the best known stories in Forteana , but not every apparent shower of amphibians requires frogs to have fallen from the sky. In many cases, heavy rain simply reveals animals that were already present but hidden. Wet weather triggers breeding migrations, encourages frogs and toads to leave shelter, and floods burrows or vegetation where they had been concealed. To someone stepping outside after a storm, a field or road that seemed empty an hour earlier can suddenly be alive with hundreds of amphibians, creating the powerful impression that they arrived with the rain rather than emerging from the ground. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Of course, it doesn't “rain” frogs or fish in the sense that it rains w... This ground level explanation does not rule out the possibility that some genuine animal falls occur during severe storms. Instead, it highlights an important distinction: before asking whether frogs fell from the clouds, investigators should ask whether the weather simply made hidden animals visible. Why frogs move in wet weather Rain transforms the landscape from a frog's perspective. Amphibians lose water easily through their skin, so cool, damp conditions allow them to travel safely across ground that would otherwise be dangerously dry. Many species therefore emerge during or immediately after rainfall to feed, disperse or migrate to breeding ponds. [The Orianne Society] oriannesociety.org The Orianne Society How to Find Amphibians During Their Spring Breeding The Orianne SocietyHow to Find Amphibians During Their Spring Breeding...March 9, 2020 — 9 Mar 2020 — Amphibians can move whenever roads... Published: March 9, 2020 For some species the response is especially dramatic. Large numbers may leave woodland, hedgerows or underground refuges on the same rainy evening, particularly when temperature, humidity and seasonal timing coincide. Conservation groups routinely warn motorists about these \"big nights\" because thousands of frogs, toads and salamanders may cross roads simultaneously. [GovDelivery] content.govdelivery.com Amphibian Migrations Forecast for TonightFor the lower and mid Hudson Valley, the predicted warm, rainy evening is looking p... The result is a classic perceptual trap. Observers who were unaware that amphibians had been hidden nearby may conclude that the storm deposited them from above. It's Raining FROGS and Poisonous Blobs The Proof Is Out There (Season 3) History Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube How storms make hidden animals suddenly visible The ground level illusion becomes especially convincing because several ordinary processes happen at once. Flooded hiding places: Rain fills cracks, burrows and leaf litter, forcing frogs onto the surface. Breeding migrations: Entire local populations may move towards ponds within a few hours when rainfall provides suitable conditions. Improved visibility: Wet pavements, short grass and open playing fields make animals much easier to notice than dense vegetation or dry woodland. Human timing: People often venture outdoors immediately after heavy rain, precisely when amphibian activity peaks. Together these factors can create the impression of instantaneous appearance. A lawn that appeared empty before a thunderstorm may contain dozens of frogs afterwards, not because they descended from the sky but because they emerged simultaneously from surrounding habitat. [northernwoodlands.org+2The Orianne Society] northernwoodlands.org Why Did the Frog Cross the Road? by Julia Janson7 Apr 2026 — The timing and presence of rainfall have a powerful effect on frogs. Researchers at University of North Carol... In some dry regions, certain frogs and toads remain dormant underground for long periods before emerging rapidly after substantial rainfall. To anyone unfamiliar with their life cycle, their sudden abundance can seem almost supernatural despite being a normal ecological response. [Reddit] reddit.com ELI5: How do frogs just suddenly appear in areas that...They can stay underground for so long because they coat themselves in mucu... Clues that separate true falls from mass migrations Because frog rain occupies the boundary between meteorology and animal behaviour, investigators look for evidence that distinguishes aerial transport from ground emergence. A genuine atmospheric fall becomes more plausible when witnesses actually observe animals descending, when severe rotating storms or waterspouts were present, when the deposition is confined to a narrow area, and when nearby water bodies could plausibly have supplied the animals. Even then, the complete sequence of animals being lifted and later deposited has rarely been directly documented. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Of course, it doesn't “rain” frogs or fish in the sense that it rains w... By contrast, a ground level migration is more likely when: frogs are concentrated around ponds, drainage ditches or woodland edges; many animals are alive, active and moving in consistent directions; there is little evidence of violent wind; the event coincides with known breeding migrations following warm rain. Rather than lying where they landed, migrating frogs typically continue hopping across roads, gardens or fields, reflecting purposeful movement rather than random impact. Why Frogs Sometimes Fall From the Sky Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Why the illusion is so persuasive Human perception naturally links unusual events with the most obvious recent cause. After an intense downpour, the appearance of hundreds of frogs seems connected to the rain itself, especially when no one noticed them beforehand. This tendency is strengthened by memory. Witnesses often remember that \"there were suddenly frogs everywhere after the storm,\" a description that can gradually become \"frogs came down with the rain\" as the story is retold. Historical reports are frequently second or third hand, making it difficult to determine whether anyone actually saw frogs falling or merely discovered them afterwards. The Library of Congress notes that many reported animal rains may instead involve storms exposing or displacing animals already present on the ground. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Of course, it doesn't “rain” frogs or fish in the sense that it rains w... I Love a Rainy Night: The Rhythm of Forest Amphibians Channel: Cary Institute of Ecosystem Studies Open on YouTube What this means for Fortean reports The ground level illusion does not explain every frog rain account, nor does it prove that every report is mistaken. Severe storms are capable of transporting lightweight animals under some circumstances, and meteorologists consider waterspouts a plausible explanation for at least some well documented cases. However, the simpler behavioural explanation often fits reports in which frogs are merely discovered after rainfall rather than observed falling through the air. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov The Library of CongressCan it rain frogs, fish, and other objects?Of course, it doesn't “rain” frogs or fish in the sense that it rains w... For Fortean investigations, this distinction is valuable. It shifts attention from the dramatic claim that frogs literally rained from clouds to a more careful question: did the storm transport amphibians through the atmosphere, or did it simply reveal a hidden migration already unfolding at ground level? In many reports, that overlooked difference provides the most economical explanation without diminishing the genuine strangeness experienced by witnesses. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Did the Frogs Fall or Just Appear?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains storms linked to apparent frog rain. See on Amazon Book The Book of Unusual Knowledge By Publications International Ltd. Collects memorable natural curiosities. See on Amazon Book Unexplained! By Jerome Clark Provides Fortean context. See on Amazon Book Mysteries of the Unexplained By Reader's Digest Association Includes legendary animal fall accounts. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Weather For Dummies The Book of Unusual Knowledge Unexplained! As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay"},{"title":"Dust Rain","url":"/dust-rain/","description":"Red or dirty rain can look supernatural when desert dust mixes with falling rain and leaves coloured residue behind.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Dust Rain Why Red Rain Looks Like Blood Dust Rain Dust Rain Why Red Rain Looks Like Blood Red or dirty rain can look supernatural when desert dust mixes with falling rain and leaves coloured residue behind. Red or dirty rain can look supernatural when desert dust mixes with falling rain and leaves coloured residue behind. mechanism How desert dust reaches rainy skies Why residue can look alarming How dust rain differs from stranger claims forteana weather dust rain Introduction Red or muddy rain has long been one of the most unsettling weather events in folklore . When raindrops leave crimson, rust coloured or brown stains on buildings, clothing and vehicles, it is easy to understand why earlier generations interpreted the phenomenon as an omen or believed that blood had literally fallen from the sky. In reality, the vast majority of so called \"blood rain\" in Europe is a well understood consequence of Saharan dust becoming mixed with rainfall. The event is visually dramatic, but its mechanism is entirely natural: desert dust is lifted high into the atmosphere, transported over long distances, and finally washed back to Earth by rain. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceColored Rains Often Begin with DustRed rains only occur if dust particles contain enough iron oxide. Dusty rains occurs throu... Within Fortean traditions, coloured rain remains fascinating because it demonstrates how an ordinary atmospheric process can produce an extraordinary appearance. The mystery lies less in the physics than in the powerful impression created when familiar rain suddenly leaves what looks like dried blood. How desert dust reaches rainy skies The Sahara is the world's largest source of mineral dust. Strong winds generated by weather systems and turbulent heating over the desert lift enormous quantities of fine particles thousands of metres into the atmosphere. Once aloft, these particles can travel hundreds or even thousands of kilometres across the Mediterranean, Europe and the Atlantic before settling out. [AOML+2USGS] aoml.noaa.gov what is the saharan air layer The Saharan Air Layer: What is it? Why does NOAA track it?24 Jun 2020 — A mass of very dry, dusty air that forms over the Sahara Dese... The dust itself does not produce coloured rain. Instead, it remains suspended until a weather front or rain cloud passes through the dusty air. As raindrops fall, they collect the airborne particles and carry them to the ground. The result is often called dust rain , mud rain , or blood rain , depending on how much dust is present and how reddish the deposited residue appears. [Met Office] metoffice.gov.uk Met OfficeWhat is 'blood rain' and will we see it this week?4 Mar 2026 — Typically, these particles are sand or dust rich in iron oxides... Southern Europe experiences these events most frequently because it lies directly downwind of major Saharan dust transport routes. Spain, Portugal, Italy, Greece and the Mediterranean islands regularly receive dusty air masses, while stronger outbreaks occasionally extend into France, the United Kingdom and even Scandinavia. [NASA Science+2NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA ScienceColored Rains Often Begin with DustRed rains only occur if dust particles contain enough iron oxide. Dusty rains occurs throu... 'Blood Rain': Saharan Dust Cloud Set to Turn UK Rain Red WION Fineprint Channel: WION Open on YouTube Why the residue can look alarming The striking colour comes largely from iron rich minerals within the desert dust. Iron oxides, the same compounds responsible for the reddish appearance of many desert rocks and soils, tint the deposited mud orange, red or reddish brown when concentrated enough. The rainwater itself is rarely bright red while falling; instead, the colour becomes obvious after evaporation leaves a film of fine sediment on surfaces. [Met Office] metoffice.gov.uk Met OfficeWhat is 'blood rain' and will we see it this week?4 Mar 2026 — Typically, these particles are sand or dust rich in iron oxides... Several factors amplify the illusion: Thin splashes dry into streaks resembling diluted blood. White walls, vehicles and pavements make the reddish deposits appear more vivid. Heavy showers can concentrate dust into muddy droplets rather than washing it away. News reports often use the dramatic phrase \"blood rain\", reinforcing supernatural associations despite the natural explanation. [Met Office] metoffice.gov.uk Met OfficeWhat is 'blood rain' and will we see it this week?4 Mar 2026 — Typically, these particles are sand or dust rich in iron oxides... For people encountering the phenomenon unexpectedly, especially before the development of modern meteorology, the appearance could easily be interpreted as a miraculous or ominous sign. Why \"blood rain\" entered folklore Historical records from Europe describe blood coloured rain over many centuries. Ancient Roman writers, medieval chroniclers and later observers sometimes treated these events as divine warnings, political portents or supernatural manifestations. Only during the nineteenth century did systematic scientific investigation increasingly link many such reports with airborne desert dust. [journals.ametsoc.org] journals.ametsoc.org Seasonality in European Red Dust/“Blood” Rain Events inby JR White · 2012 · Cited by 25 — Sahara derived dust rains, termed “red rains” o... The persistence of the traditional name illustrates an important Fortean pattern. A genuine natural event acquires symbolic meaning because its visual effect is so emotionally powerful. The coloured residue survives long after the rain itself has stopped, encouraging witnesses to preserve samples, tell stories and connect the event with other unusual happenings. Modern satellite observations have strengthened the natural explanation by allowing scientists to track Saharan dust plumes moving across Europe before dust rain occurs. What once appeared mysterious can now often be forecast days in advance. [NASA Science+2NOAA NESDIS] science.nasa.gov dust outbreak reaches europe NASA ScienceDust Outbreak Reaches EuropeMar 11, 2026 — Winter winds lofted clouds of dust from the Sahara Desert, carrying it north towar... SELTENES NATURSPEKTAKEL: Sahara Staub bringt Blutregen nach Deutschland! Wetter zum Wochenende! Channel: BILD Open on YouTube How dust rain differs from stranger claims Not every report of coloured rain has the same explanation. Fortean literature often groups many different events under the label \"blood rain\", but they deserve separate treatment. Ordinary Saharan dust rain is characterised by several recognisable features: Weather forecasts identify an incoming Saharan dust plume. [spanienpress.com] spanienpress.com s, leading to what is known as “Lluvia de Barro” – a rain that leaves reddish brown... The residue consists mainly of fine mineral particles. Large regions may experience similar deposits at the same time. Vehicles, windows and outdoor furniture often show a uniform reddish coating after the rain. [NASA Science+2University of Reading] science.nasa.gov dust outbreak reaches europe NASA ScienceDust Outbreak Reaches EuropeMar 11, 2026 — Winter winds lofted clouds of dust from the Sahara Desert, carrying it north towar... By contrast, some famous reports of coloured rain have involved proposed biological particles, local pollution or poorly documented historical accounts. These require separate investigation and should not automatically be attributed either to Saharan dust or to unexplained phenomena. The presence of a dramatic colour alone is not enough to establish a single cause. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Blood rain Blood rain This distinction is important because the overwhelming majority of modern European \"blood rain\" episodes coincide with tracked Saharan dust outbreaks, making mineral dust the simplest and best supported explanation. A natural event that still feels uncanny Dust rain demonstrates why weather occupies such a prominent place in Forteana . Nothing supernatural is required: wind lifts desert dust, atmospheric circulation transports it over great distances, and rainfall deposits it where people least expect it. Yet the result can transform an ordinary shower into a landscape streaked with red mud, echoing stories that once inspired fear, prophecy and speculation. The episode remains memorable because perception and symbolism amplify an otherwise routine atmospheric process. The mechanism is straightforward, but the experience of seeing apparently blood coloured rain descend from the sky continues to make one of meteorology's most striking natural illusions. LIVE: Watch the ‘fiery sunset’ over the Sussex coast as Saharan dust hits UK Channel: The Sun Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Red Rain Looks Like Blood. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains dust transport and coloured rain. See on Amazon Book"},{"title":"Worm Falls","url":"/worm-falls/","description":"Worms can suddenly cover pavements after rain, creating a vivid but misleading impression that they came down with the storm.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Worm Falls Why Worms Seem to Rain Down Worm Falls Worm Falls Why Worms Seem to Rain Down Worms can suddenly cover pavements after rain, creating a vivid but misleading impression that they came down with the storm. Worms can suddenly cover pavements after rain, creating a vivid but misleading impression that they came down with the storm. population context Why worms surface during wet weather How pavements create false fall scenes What witnesses should look for next forteana weather worm falls Introduction Few scenes create a stronger impression of a \"strange fall\" than a pavement suddenly covered with writhing earthworms after heavy rain. To someone arriving after the storm, the worms can appear to have dropped from the sky along with the rain itself. Within Fortean collections, such scenes sometimes become retold as \"worm rain\". In nearly all documented everyday cases, however, the evidence points to a far less mysterious sequence: the worms were already in the ground, emerged during wet conditions, and only became highly visible once they reached hard surfaces. The mystery lies less in animals falling from the sky than in how weather changes where people notice them. Studies of earthworm behaviour and soil ecology consistently support this interpretation, although researchers still debate the precise reasons why worms leave the soil during rainfall. [NIH Environmental Health+2earthwormsoc.org.uk] niehs.nih.gov NIH Environmental HealthWhy do Earthworms Crawl on the Sidewalk?As they need to stay consistently wet, earthworms wait until the top laye... Why worms surface during wet weather Earthworms breathe through their moist skin rather than with lungs, so they require damp conditions to survive. Rain provides an unusual opportunity: the soil surface remains wet enough for worms to travel across it without drying out quickly. Moving over the surface is often much faster than tunnelling through dense soil, allowing worms to disperse, seek mates or colonise new patches of habitat. This \"migration\" explanation currently has the strongest support among earthworm specialists. [earthwormsoc.org.uk+2NIH Environmental Health] earthwormsoc.org.uk ace when it is wet, to find food, new habitats or a mate.Read more... Older explanations suggested that worms simply emerge to avoid drowning in flooded burrows. While this can happen in severely waterlogged soils with poor oxygen levels, it is no longer regarded as the universal explanation. Many earthworm species tolerate saturated soils surprisingly well because oxygen can still diffuse through water, provided enough remains available. Researchers therefore view oxygen shortage as one possible factor rather than the whole story. [News+2The Times] news.wisc.edu Curiosities: After rains, why do worms crawl out onto the...Jun 29, 2007 — “The worms can't get enough oxygen when the soil is flood... Another long discussed idea concerns vibration. Raindrops striking the ground create vibrations that may resemble those produced by burrowing predators such as moles. Earthworms are known to respond to ground vibrations, and this sensitivity is exploited in traditional \"worm charming\", where vibrations encourage worms to emerge. Whether rainfall consistently triggers this response remains debated, but it may contribute alongside migration and local soil conditions rather than replacing them. [Scientific American+2Science Focus] scientificamerican.com why earthworms surface after rain Rain can set up vibrations on top of the soil like mole vibrations,\" said Professor Josef...Read more... Why Worms Come Out After a Storm Channel: The Weather Channel Open on YouTube How pavements create false fall scenes The illusion of worms having \"rained down\" depends heavily on where they end up. Grass, leaf litter and loose soil hide emerging worms remarkably well. Concrete, tarmac and paving stones do the opposite: every worm becomes conspicuous against a flat, exposed surface. Hundreds of worms may therefore seem to appear suddenly, even though they have simply crawled out from the surrounding ground over several hours. Hard surfaces also trap worms in unfamiliar terrain. Unlike soil, pavements provide no burrows into which they can retreat once conditions begin drying. Artificial lighting, kerbs, roads and buildings may further disrupt their ability to return underground. As the rain stops, stranded worms often remain scattered across the pavement, creating the impression that they have just arrived from above rather than crawled there earlier. [News+2NIH Environmental Health] news.wisc.edu Curiosities: After rains, why do worms crawl out onto the...Jun 29, 2007 — “The worms can't get enough oxygen when the soil is flood... This timing matters. Most witnesses encounter the aftermath rather than watching the worms emerge. Human memory naturally compresses events, encouraging a narrative in which the worms and the rain arrived together from the sky. Why the illusion fits Fortean storytelling Stories of mysterious falls often gain strength because observers encounter an unusual pattern without witnessing its beginning. Worm covered streets fit this pattern perfectly. Unlike reports of fish or frogs associated with tornadoes or waterspouts , earthworms require no aerial transport to produce a striking spectacle. A local population emerging simultaneously after favourable rainfall can create a scene every bit as surprising as a genuine animal fall, especially when viewed without knowledge of earthworm ecology. This makes worm \"rains\" an instructive example within Forteana . They demonstrate how an authentic natural event can produce eyewitness descriptions that sound extraordinary while requiring no impossible mechanism. The phenomenon sits at the boundary between meteorology, animal behaviour and human perception rather than serving as evidence that worms literally descended with the rain. [earthwormsoc.org.uk+2NIH Environmental Health] earthwormsoc.org.uk ace when it is wet, to find food, new habitats or a mate.Read more... Why Do Earthworms Come Out After It Rains? Channel: SciShow Open on YouTube Were U Wondering: Why do worms come out on the sidewalks after it rains? Channel: UW Madison Open on YouTube What witnesses should look for next When evaluating a report that worms appeared after rain, several observations help distinguish surface emergence from a genuine atmospheric fall: Check whether the worms are concentrated on pavements, roads and other exposed hard surfaces while remaining less obvious in adjacent grass or flowerbeds. Look for nearby lawns, woodland, fields or gardens that could supply large numbers of earthworms. Ask whether anyone actually observed worms descending through the air, or whether they were simply noticed after rainfall ended. Examine whether the worms are alive and actively crawling, suggesting recent emergence from surrounding soil rather than impact after long distance transport. Consider whether the event followed ordinary rain or an exceptional storm capable of producing powerful local vortices. Most post rain worm sightings occur without evidence of tornadoes or waterspouts. [NIH Environmental Health+2earthwormsoc.org.uk] niehs.nih.gov NIH Environmental HealthWhy do Earthworms Crawl on the Sidewalk?As they need to stay consistently wet, earthworms wait until the top laye... These clues cannot explain every historical anecdote, particularly older reports lacking detail, but they account for the overwhelming majority of modern observations of \"worm falls\". Rather than revealing animals raining from the sky, they show how wet weather briefly brings a hidden underground population into plain sight, where ordinary pavements transform a familiar ecological behaviour into what appears to be a remarkable atmospheric mystery. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Worms Seem to Rain Down. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains rainfall conditions behind worm appearances. See on Amazon Book The Book of Unusual Knowledge By Publications International Ltd. Features memorable natural oddities. See on Amazon Book Unexplained! By Jerome Clark Provides Fortean context. See on Amazon Book Mysteries of the Unexplained By Reader's Digest Association Includes reports of unusual animal falls. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Weather For Dummies The Book of Unusual Knowledge Unexplained! As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Example eBay listing Art Print Fish Man Human Rain Umbrella Weird Strange Funny Ironic Out Of Water Search eBay.co.uk : fish rain art print Browse similar on eBay.co.uk Example eBay listing Man Monochrome Rain Rainy Cloudy Walk Walking"},{"title":"Yoro Fish","url":"/yoro-fish/","description":"The Yoro fish rain is harder to explain as a simple sea-to-town fall, making local flooding and hidden waterways central to the puzzle.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Weather","level":3,"text":"Yoro Fish What Really Feeds the Yoro Fish Rain? Yoro Fish Yoro Fish What Really Feeds the Yoro Fish Rain? The Yoro fish rain is harder to explain as a simple sea to town fall, making local flooding and hidden waterways central to the puzzle. The Yoro fish rain is harder to explain as a simple sea to town fall, making local flooding and hidden waterways central to the puzzle. event time window Why distance from the sea matters Floodwater, drains and hidden habitats Tradition, repetition and natural explanations forteana weather yoro fish Introduction The annual \"rain of fish\" reported around Yoro in Honduras is one of the world's best known Fortean traditions, yet it is also one of the least compatible with the classic idea of fish being carried inland from the sea. Yoro lies well inland, and the repeated appearance of small freshwater fish after intense seasonal storms has led many researchers to look beyond waterspouts towards local hydrology. Rather than asking how fish could survive a long aerial journey, the more focused question is whether heavy rain, underground waterways, flood channels and hidden freshwater habitats could explain why fish repeatedly appear on the ground after storms. The evidence does not settle the mystery completely, but it shifts attention from spectacular long distance transport to the interaction between extreme rainfall and the local landscape. [IFLScience+2Live Science] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 Why distance from the sea matters Many reports of animal falls can plausibly involve waterspouts lifting fish from nearby lakes, rivers or coastal waters. Yoro is different because the event is reported in essentially the same region year after year despite its inland location. This geographical consistency has made many commentators question whether a marine waterspout could repeatedly deliver fish to the same area over decades. [IFLScience+2The Weather Channel] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 That does not mean a weather explanation is impossible. Instead, it narrows the range of realistic mechanisms. Any explanation based on atmospheric transport must account for: the repeated occurrence during the rainy season rather than random years; the apparent concentration in the same locality; the fact that the fish are reported after prolonged thunderstorms and flooding rather than during ordinary rain; reports that many of the fish are alive when collected. [IFLScience+2Wikipedia] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 Because those characteristics are difficult to reconcile with repeated long distance transport from the coast, many investigators have instead explored whether the fish originate much closer to where they are found. The Town where Fish Rain from the Sky Channel: PPPeter Open on YouTube Floodwater, drains and hidden habitats The leading local alternative is not that fish fall from the sky at all, but that they emerge from hidden freshwater systems when exceptional rainfall overwhelms the landscape. Heavy tropical storms can rapidly flood low ground, connect isolated pools, overflow drainage channels and recharge underground cavities. If fish occupy normally inaccessible streams, sinkholes, caves or temporary channels, sudden floodwater may carry them onto fields and roads before the water quickly recedes. To people arriving after the storm, the result resembles a shower of fish even if the animals were transported at ground level. [IFLScience] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 The underground waterway hypothesis has several features that fit the reported pattern: Freshwater origin. The fish are generally described as freshwater species rather than marine fish. Seasonal timing. Events coincide with the period of the strongest tropical rains, when underground drainage systems are most likely to overflow. Local repetition. A stable network of hidden waterways could explain why the phenomenon is associated with the same region rather than appearing randomly across Honduras. Limited transport distance. Fish moved only a short distance through floodwater are more likely to survive than fish carried high into severe storms. [IFLScience+2Wikipedia] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 The hypothesis remains difficult to prove directly because the proposed underground routes have not been comprehensively mapped or observed transporting fish during a storm. Nevertheless, many researchers regard it as better matched to Yoro's geography than the traditional waterspout explanation. [IFLScience] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 Tradition, repetition and natural explanations Yoro's importance in Forteana comes not simply from unusual weather but from repetition. Residents describe the phenomenon as occurring during the mid year rainy season, often after hours of darkness, thunder and exceptionally heavy rainfall. Once the storm passes, people search roads and fields for fish that can be gathered by hand. The consistency of this narrative over many decades distinguishes Yoro from isolated reports of fish falls elsewhere. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia t has been occurring for over a...Read more... Local tradition also includes a religious explanation. According to popular accounts, the nineteenth century missionary José Manuel Subirana prayed for food for the poor, after which the fish began appearing as a recurring blessing. Whether understood as miracle, folklore or historical memory, this story has become inseparable from the identity of the event and helps explain why the annual occurrence is celebrated rather than treated merely as an odd meteorological incident. [IFLScience] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 From a naturalistic perspective, however, the recurring schedule itself strengthens interest in local environmental mechanisms. Unlike an unpredictable tornado crossing open water, seasonal flooding follows established rainfall patterns. If hidden freshwater habitats are periodically connected to the surface by intense storms, the event would be expected to recur under broadly similar conditions each year. ¡El misterio continúa! Lluvia de peces en lloro Channel: HCH En Vivo Open on YouTube En este País LLueven Peces Lluvia de Peces en Honduras 😲 Channel: PlanetAventura Open on YouTube What the Yoro case adds to the study of strange falls The Yoro fish rain illustrates an important distinction within the study of unusual weather reports. Many famous animal fall stories ask whether storms can transport animals through the air. Yoro instead raises the possibility that witnesses may correctly associate fish with exceptional storms while misidentifying the direction from which they arrived. The case therefore encourages a broader view of \"strange falls\". Extreme rainfall can create temporary links between underground channels, floodplains, drainage systems and the surface, producing dramatic concentrations of animals without requiring a long aerial journey. Although no single explanation has been conclusively demonstrated, the hidden freshwater route hypothesis fits the locality, seasonal timing and repeated nature of Yoro's reports more closely than a simple sea to town waterspout model, making it one of the most distinctive natural explanations within Fortean studies. [IFLScience+2Live Science] iflscience.com IFLScience\"Lluvia De Peces\": The Mystery Of Why It Rains Fish...16 May 2023 — According to residents, the fish rain, called \"Lluvia De P... Published: May 2023 Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Really Feeds the Yoro Fish Rain?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Weather For Dummies By John D. Cox Explains storms and waterspouts relevant to fish falls. See on Amazon Book The Book of Unusual Knowledge By Publications International Ltd. Contains unusual weather and natural stories. See on Amazon Book The Weather Machine By Andrew Blum Provides broader understanding of weather systems. See on Amazon Book Unexplained! By Jerome Clark Discusses celebrated"},{"title":"Witnesses","url":"/witnesses/","description":"Witness accounts are vital to Forteana, but memory, expectation, fear, and retelling can change a case.","breadcrumb":"Forteana","level":2,"text":"Witnesses How Much Can Strange Witnesses Tell US? Witnesses Witnesses How Much Can Strange Witnesses Tell US? Witness accounts are vital to Forteana, but memory, expectation, fear, and retelling can change a case. Witness accounts are vital to Forteana, but memory, expectation, fear, and retelling can change a case. population context What witnesses actually saw Memory, rumour, and retelling When testimony becomes stronger forteana witnesses Introduction Witness testimony is the doorway into most of Forteana . A strange light, a creature at the roadside, a voice in a locked room or an object moving in a way that “should not” happen usually enters the record because someone says, in good faith or otherwise, that they saw, heard or felt it. The problem is that witness testimony is both indispensable and fragile. It can preserve real details that no instrument captured, but it can also be reshaped by fear, expectation, leading questions, local rumour, media coverage and the simple pressure of retelling an event that had no familiar name at the time. That tension is why witness accounts matter so much in strange event reports. They should not be dismissed merely because they are human reports; many unusual natural and aerial phenomena have first been described by witnesses. But a testimony is not the event itself. It is a reconstruction of the event, filtered through perception, memory and culture. The strongest Fortean reading asks not only “did this happen?” but “what exactly was reported, when, by whom, under what conditions, and how did the story change?” Research on eyewitness memory, stress and misinformation shows why confident testimony can still be wrong, while official UAP and ball lightning investigations show why carefully collected testimony can still be useful when handled with limits. [hgss.copernicus.org+3PMC+3PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 262 — Abducted by a UFO: Prevalence information... What witnesses actually saw In Fortean cases, the first witness account is often a description made under poor conditions. The event may be brief, distant, nocturnal, frightening, emotionally loaded or entirely unexpected. A witness may be trying to describe a light in the sky without knowing aircraft, satellites or astronomical objects; a sound in a bedroom while half awake; an animal glimpsed at the edge of a road; or an electrical phenomenon during a storm. Even when the person is sincere, “I saw a disc”, “I saw a figure” or “I heard a voice” is rarely a clean recording of sensory data. It is already an interpretation. This is why investigators usually try to separate observation from inference. “A bright light moved from west to east for ten seconds” is different from “a craft flew silently overhead”. “A dark upright shape crossed the path” is different from “a creature was watching me”. In many strong reports, the useful material is not the dramatic label but the small details: duration, direction, distance, weather , lighting, number of witnesses, whether witnesses spoke to one another before giving accounts, and whether any independent record exists. UAP work gives a clear example. The French official group GEIPAN , created within CNES in 1977, collects and analyses reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena and publishes sighting files and conclusions while protecting witness anonymity. Its public mission is not to declare every unexplained sighting extraordinary, but to give a structured response to people’s “lived weirdness” and to improve knowledge about events that remain unexplained after investigation. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPANSighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while m... The older US Project Blue Book material shows the same problem in a different institutional setting. The Air Force distinguished between reports that could be identified, reports with too little information, and reports that remained unidentified despite apparently containing enough data to test ordinary explanations. That distinction matters for Forteana: a weak report is not made stronger by remaining mysterious, but a detailed report is not automatically explained away simply because most reports are mundane. [ESD Business Systems] esd.whs.mil ESD Business Systems Project Blue Book ESD Business Systems Project Blue Book For a reader assessing a strange event witness, the first question is therefore not “do I believe them?” but “what level of observation is actually being claimed?” A credible person can still misidentify Venus, a satellite train, an owl, a reflection, a dreamlike state or a rumour enhanced encounter. Equally, an unusual report may deserve attention if it contains early, independent, detailed observations that remain consistent under questioning and fit no obvious known source. Why sincerity is not the same as accuracy Fortean debates often get stuck on the character of the witness. Was the witness honest? Respectable? Educated? A pilot, police officer, farmer, child, scientist or soldier? These details can matter, but they do not remove the ordinary limits of perception and memory. A witness can be truthful and still mistaken. Research on eyewitness memory is blunt about this. Memory is not a stored video that can be replayed unchanged. It is reconstructed, and later information can alter what people believe they remember. Reviews of memory science note that false memories can feel vivid and sincere, especially when people repeatedly imagine, discuss or are asked about an event. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Neuroscience of Memory: Implications for the Courtroomby JW Lacy · 2013 · Cited by 262 — Abducted by a UFO: Prevalence information... Stress adds another complication. Strange reports often involve fear, alarm or awe: a figure at the foot of the bed, a looming object, a sudden flash, a threatening sound, a crowd panic. A survey of eyewitness memory experts found broad agreement that very high levels of stress impair eyewitness accuracy, even though ordinary readers often assume that frightening events are “burned into” memory more reliably. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by C Marr · 2020 · Cited by 59 — High proportions of experts from both research fields agreed that very high levels of stress impair t... This does not mean every frightened witness is unreliable. It means fear changes what the report can prove. Under stress, people may focus on one salient feature, compress or stretch time, misjudge distance, lose peripheral details, or later fill gaps with the most available explanation. A ten second sighting can feel much longer; a distant object can seem closer; a confusing shadow can become a figure once the witness has a name for it. The difference between sincerity and accuracy is especially important in reports of alien abduction, night visitors and bedroom entities. Sleep paralysis can produce vivid experiences of immobility, presence, pressure, fear and sensed intruders. Studies of people reporting alien abduction experiences have examined links with sleep paralysis and memory processes, not as an insult to witnesses but as a way to explain why an experience can feel utterly real while arising from a known human state. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. For Forteana, this is not a debunking shortcut. It is a discipline. The fair question is not “are witnesses lying?” but “which parts of the experience are direct perception, which parts are interpretation, and which parts may have formed later?” Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Memory, rumour and retelling A strange event report rarely remains fixed after the first telling. The witness talks to family, police, journalists, investigators or other witnesses. They read reports of similar cases. They learn the vocabulary of UFOs, hauntings, cryptids , curses, monsters or miracles. Over time, the account may become smoother, more dramatic and more culturally recognisable. The uncertainty of the original moment can be replaced by a story that feels clearer than the experience itself. Memory conformity is one route by which this happens. Experiments show that when people witness an event and then discuss it, one person’s account can influence another person’s memory of what happened. Recent research on online misinformation has also shown that post event information can distort witness memory, which is directly relevant to modern Fortean cases that spread through social media before investigators can collect independent statements. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PMCOnline misinformation can distort witnesses' memories PMCOnline misinformation can distort witnesses' memories This is why timing matters. A testimony given immediately, before news coverage and"},{"title":"Sincerity","url":"/sincerity/","description":"Sincerity can make a report worth hearing, but it does not turn memory into a replayable recording.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"Sincerity Can a Truthful Witness Still Be Mistaken? Sincerity Sincerity Can a Truthful Witness Still Be Mistaken? Sincerity can make a report worth hearing, but it does not turn memory into a replayable recording. Sincerity can make a report worth hearing, but it does not turn memory into a replayable recording. mechanism Sincerity and accuracy are different questions Stress, fear, and distorted recall Respectable witnesses and ordinary misidentification forteana witnesses sincerity Introduction A witness can be completely honest and still be mistaken. This is one of the most important ideas in evaluating strange event reports. In Fortean investigations, debates often become polarised between those who assume that a sincere witness must be describing an extraordinary event and those who dismiss unusual accounts simply because memory is imperfect. Research on eyewitness memory supports neither position. Human memory is remarkably useful but it is not a video recording. It is a reconstructive process that can be shaped by attention, stress, later conversations and repeated retelling. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov An Examination of the Causes and Solutions to Eyewitness Errorby RA Wise · 2014 · Cited by 68 — Researchers have been studying eyewitn... This distinction matters because many reports of unexplained lights, mysterious creatures or other anomalous experiences rely almost entirely on personal testimony. The credibility of the witness deserves respect, but the reliability of a specific recollection must be assessed separately. A truthful person may accurately report what they believe they experienced while still misremembering important details or misinterpreting what they saw. Sincerity and accuracy are different questions The first question in evaluating a strange event report is not whether the witness is lying. It is whether the witness's memory accurately represents the original event. Psychological research consistently shows that memory is reconstructed whenever it is recalled rather than simply replayed. Each act of remembering can strengthen some details, weaken others and sometimes incorporate new information gathered after the event. This is not deception. It is a normal feature of human cognition. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov An Examination of the Causes and Solutions to Eyewitness Errorby RA Wise · 2014 · Cited by 68 — Researchers have been studying eyewitn... This explains why apparently trustworthy witnesses may sincerely disagree about the same incident. Two people standing together can notice different features because their attention was directed differently. One may remember movement, another colour, another sound. As time passes, these partial memories may become more coherent stories, even if some reconstructed details were never actually perceived. In Fortean cases this distinction is especially important because witnesses are often trying to describe events unlike anything they have previously encountered. When the brain lacks an obvious category, it naturally fills gaps using familiar concepts. A witness may therefore describe a \"craft\", \"creature\" or \"ghost\" when the original perception was simply an unfamiliar shape, movement or sound. How stress and fear reshape recall Unusual experiences frequently occur under conditions that are already unfavourable for accurate perception. Darkness, surprise, distance, poor weather and emotional shock all reduce the quality of the original observation before memory even begins to reconstruct it. Stress narrows attention Stress does not simply erase memory. Instead, it often concentrates attention on what seems most immediately important while reducing memory for surrounding details. Reviews of eyewitness research show that high emotional arousal can improve memory for certain central features while impairing memory for peripheral information. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Emotional stress and eyewitness memory: a critical reviewby SA Christianson · 1992 · Cited by 2030 — The eyewitness literature ofte... In a Fortean encounter this might mean that a witness vividly remembers an intensely bright light but cannot accurately estimate its size, distance or direction. Someone frightened by an unexpected figure may remember their emotional reaction far more clearly than the object's precise appearance. This helps explain why reports may contain striking certainty about one feature alongside uncertainty about others without implying dishonesty. The misinformation effect Elizabeth Loftus Nobel Prize Summit 2023 Channel: Nobel Prize Open on YouTube Surprise encourages rapid interpretation Humans rapidly interpret incomplete information because quick decisions are usually more useful than prolonged uncertainty. A brief glimpse through trees, a moving shadow or an unfamiliar sound often forces the brain to choose the closest available explanation before sufficient evidence exists. Once an interpretation has formed, later recollections frequently preserve that interpretation rather than the raw sensory information. Consequently, witnesses may honestly report, \"I saw a large animal standing upright,\" when their original perception consisted of only a few seconds of movement under poor viewing conditions. Memory changes after the event Memory remains vulnerable after the experience itself has ended. Studies of the misinformation effect demonstrate that exposure to misleading questions, conversations or later descriptions can alter what witnesses subsequently remember. Importantly, people often become confident in these altered memories because the reconstructed version feels just as real as the original recollection. [ResearchGate+2PMC] researchgate.net 254899688 Misinformation Effects and the Suggestibility of Eyewitness Memory ResearchGate(PDF) Misinformation Effects and the Suggestibility of...3 Jun 2015 — In the misinformation paradigm, exposure to misleading... For strange event reports, contamination can occur through: Discussion with other witnesses before independent statements are recorded. Media coverage suggesting particular explanations. Reading internet discussions that introduce details absent from the original account. Repeated interviews that unintentionally encourage elaboration. None of these mechanisms require deliberate fabrication. Respectable witnesses can still misidentify ordinary events Appeals to authority are common in Fortean discussions. Reports often emphasise that witnesses were police officers, pilots, scientists, military personnel or experienced outdoors enthusiasts. Such backgrounds may increase confidence that the witness tried to observe carefully, but they do not eliminate the limits of human perception. Expertise usually improves performance only within familiar conditions. A pilot is trained to identify aircraft, not necessarily unusual atmospheric optics viewed under unexpected circumstances. A police officer may be highly observant during investigations yet still experience the same attentional narrowing and reconstructive memory processes found across the wider population. Research on eyewitness testimony repeatedly shows that confidence and accuracy are related only imperfectly. A confident witness may be correct, but confidence alone cannot verify that a memory accurately reflects the original perception. Confidence itself can increase after repeated retelling or positive feedback from others. [Springer] link.springer.com Eyewitness Memory Springer Nature Link13 Dec 2022 —... accuracy relationship and the influence of fake news on memory. The con... For Fortean investigators, the practical implication is straightforward: the witness's reputation should not substitute for examining the conditions under which the observation occurred. Can we trust eyewitness memory after all? It’s complicated. John Wixted TEDxUCSanDiego Channel: TEDx Talks · Views: 19.0K · Uploaded: November 2025 · Length: 18 minutes Open on YouTube Why character evidence cannot settle extraordinary claims One persistent misunderstanding in Fortean debates is the assumption that questioning a report necessarily means accusing the witness of lying. These are different claims. A witness can simultaneously be: Honest. Intelligent. Careful. Emotionally sincere. Mistaken about some aspect of what was perceived. Likewise, discovering a conventional explanation for an event does not imply bad faith on the witness's part. A sincere observer can misidentify a rare atmospheric display, an astronomical object, an unusual animal or an unfamiliar aircraft without behaving irrationally. Separating honesty from accuracy also protects genuinely unusual reports from weak reasoning. If every sincere testimony were accepted automatically, there would be no reliable way to distinguish genuinely unexplained events from ordinary misidentifications. Why eyewitnesses fail Thomas Albright TEDxSanDiego Channel: TEDx Talks · Views: 104.2K · Uploaded: November 2016 · Length: 15 minutes Open"},{"title":"London Monster","url":"/london-monster/","description":"The 1790 London Monster panic shows how fear, publicity, and accusation can make testimony harder to separate from social contagion.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"London Monster The Panic That Made Witnesses Dangerous London Monster London Monster The Panic That Made Witnesses Dangerous The 1790 London Monster panic shows how fear, publicity, and accusation can make testimony harder to separate from social contagion. The 1790 London Monster panic shows how fear, publicity, and accusation can make testimony harder to separate from social contagion. event time window Attacks, rewards, and public fear False accusations and vigilante pressure Lessons for later Fortean waves forteana witnesses london monster Introduction The London Monster panic of 1788–1790 is one of the clearest historical examples of why witness testimony must be handled with caution during periods of intense public fear. Although women undoubtedly reported genuine assaults by an unknown attacker, the investigation quickly became entangled with sensational newspaper coverage, public rewards, rumours, copycat incidents, and mounting pressure to identify a culprit. As a result, reliable eyewitness evidence became increasingly difficult to distinguish from mistaken identification, social expectation, and collective anxiety. Rather than showing that witnesses are inherently unreliable, the episode demonstrates how a climate of panic can alter the conditions under which testimony is produced, remembered and evaluated. [ePrints Soton+2Springer] eprints.soton.ac.uk ePrints SotonCrime, Deviance, and the Social Discovery of Moral Panic in...May 4, 2017 — by C Hamerton · 2016 · Cited by 1 — The thesis... Published: May 4, 2017 For students of Forteana , the London Monster serves as a warning case rather than a paranormal mystery. It illustrates how extraordinary narratives can gain momentum before investigators have securely established what actually happened, making later witness reports progressively harder to interpret. Attacks, rewards, and public fear The attacks began in 1788 and intensified dramatically during the first half of 1790. Victims commonly described a man who approached them with obscene remarks before cutting or stabbing their clothing or bodies with concealed blades. More than fifty attacks were reported, although historians continue to debate whether they were all committed by one individual, by several offenders, or whether some reports reflected imitation or misunderstanding. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster A turning point came after a cluster of attacks in January 1790 attracted exceptional public attention. Newspapers carried increasingly dramatic accounts, while philanthropist John Julius Angerstein organised a reward of 100 guineas for information leading to the attacker's conviction. The reward, enormous by contemporary standards, encouraged a flood of tips and accusations. Historians studying the case argue that the combination of intense media coverage and financial incentive transformed a criminal investigation into one of Britain's earliest recognisable moral panics. [Wikipedia+2ePrints Soton] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster The public reaction spread well beyond fear of assault. Women altered their clothing by adding protective linings or improvised armour beneath dresses. Citizens began detaining strangers on suspicion alone. Bow Street magistrates received numerous reports that proved impossible to verify. Newspapers reinforced expectations by repeatedly describing the mysterious \"Monster\", encouraging readers to interpret unrelated encounters through the same narrative. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster From the perspective of witness testimony, this is the crucial moment. Once a widely recognised story existed, new witnesses were no longer reporting into an informational vacuum. They were reporting within a powerful cultural script that already suggested what they were expected to have seen. Rhynwick Williams & The London Monster The Dark Histories Podcast Channel: Dark Histories Open on YouTube False accusations and vigilante pressure One of the strongest lessons from the London Monster affair is how rapidly eyewitness confidence can be influenced by social pressure. Contemporary accounts record that more than thirty innocent men were brought before Bow Street after being identified or detained by members of the public hoping to claim the reward. None proved to be the attacker. Innocent employers, relatives and complete strangers were seized simply because they vaguely matched circulating descriptions. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster This pattern illustrates several well known problems in eyewitness evidence. First, descriptions of the attacker varied considerably. Witnesses differed over his appearance, build, methods and even the weapons supposedly used. Some reports described concealed knives attached to knees, others blades hidden in bouquets, while still others suggested claw like devices fixed to the hands. Some variation is expected in genuine observations made under stressful conditions, but the diversity also raises the possibility that multiple offenders—or evolving rumours—became merged into a single legendary figure. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster Second, public expectation encouraged confirmation rather than careful discrimination. Once newspapers repeatedly referred to \"the Monster\", people encountering suspicious behaviour were more likely to interpret ambiguous experiences as connected to the famous attacker rather than as isolated incidents. Third, the reward created incentives that complicated witness behaviour. While most victims appear to have acted in good faith, the broader environment rewarded certainty more than caution. Investigators therefore had to separate sincere testimony from mistaken identification without the benefit of modern forensic methods. The disputed conviction of Renwick Williams The arrest of Renwick Williams did not end questions about the reliability of witness testimony. Williams was identified after one victim, Anne Porter, believed she recognised him in public. Other witnesses, however, either failed to identify him or expressed uncertainty. Despite these inconsistencies, Williams was convicted after two trials, although historians have continued to question whether the evidence justified the verdict. No forensic evidence linked him directly to the attacks, and some incidents appear incompatible with his known whereabouts. Several modern historians therefore argue that his guilt cannot be regarded as conclusively established, even if he may have committed some assaults. [Wikipedia+2History Today] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster For a witness study perspective, the significance lies less in Williams himself than in the conditions surrounding the identification. By the time of his arrest: witnesses had been exposed to months of intensive publicity; fear of the attacker had become widespread; authorities faced enormous pressure to solve the case; many citizens already believed they knew what the attacker looked like. These are precisely the circumstances under which mistaken identifications become more likely. The London Monster Channel: Public Pictures AI Open on YouTube Lessons for later Fortean waves The London Monster case has become valuable well beyond criminal history because it demonstrates mechanisms that later appear in reports of unusual or mysterious events. Fear changes the information environment As publicity grows, witnesses are increasingly influenced by previous reports. Later testimony may therefore reflect both original perception and knowledge acquired afterwards. This does not require deliberate deception; memory is reconstructive and naturally incorporates later information. [ePrints Soton] eprints.soton.ac.uk ePrints SotonCrime, Deviance, and the Social Discovery of Moral Panic in...May 4, 2017 — by C Hamerton · 2016 · Cited by 1 — The thesis... Published: May 4, 2017 Genuine events can generate unreliable secondary reports The existence of false accusations does not prove that no assaults occurred. Modern historians broadly accept that numerous women were genuinely attacked while also recognising that rumours, copycats and mistaken identifications probably expanded the apparent scope of the phenomenon. This distinction is essential in Fortean investigations, where scepticism about some reports should not automatically invalidate every report. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia London Monster London Monster Independent early testimony usually carries the greatest value Reports recorded before widespread publicity are generally less contaminated by collective expectation than those collected after a narrative has become culturally familiar. Investigators of unusual claims therefore often give particular weight to the earliest independent accounts rather than to the largest body of later testimony. Confidence is not the same as accuracy Many participants in the London Monster panic were undoubtedly sincere. Victims honestly described frightening"},{"title":"Observation","url":"/observation/","description":"The most useful testimony often begins by stripping dramatic labels away from what was actually seen, heard, or felt.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"Observation What Did the Witness Actually Report? Observation Observation What Did the Witness Actually Report? The most useful testimony often begins by stripping dramatic labels away from what was actually seen, heard, or felt. The most useful testimony often begins by stripping dramatic labels away from what was actually seen, heard, or felt. mechanism Observation versus inference Details that survive poor viewing conditions How labels can outrun evidence forteana witnesses observation Introduction The most useful witness testimony in Fortean investigations often begins by removing the explanation and preserving the experience . A report that says, “I saw a glowing sphere hovering above the trees for twenty seconds” contains an observation. A report that says, “I saw an alien craft” contains an interpretation. The difference matters because observations can be tested against weather , astronomy, aircraft movements, geography and other witness accounts, whereas interpretations reflect the witness's attempt to explain what those observations meant. This distinction does not imply that witnesses are dishonest or unreliable. On the contrary, sincere witnesses almost always combine direct perception with immediate interpretation because the human brain naturally tries to make unfamiliar events meaningful. The challenge for investigators is therefore not to reject testimony but to separate what was actually perceived from the conclusions attached to it. That process preserves the evidence that remains useful even if later explanations change. What Did the Witness Actually Report? A practical way to assess any strange event account is to divide every statement into two categories: ObservationInterpretation\"There was a white light above the hill.\"\"It was a UFO.\"\"The object changed direction suddenly.\"\"It was intelligently controlled.\"\"I heard three loud knocks from inside the wall.\"\"Something paranormal was trying to communicate.\"\"A dark figure crossed the road.\"\"It was a cryptid.\" Only the first column consists of sensory observations. The second consists of inferences. This distinction is standard practice in investigative interviewing because memory is reconstructive rather than photographic. Witnesses do not merely replay stored recordings; they reconstruct events from perception, memory and later knowledge. As psychologists studying eyewitness testimony have repeatedly shown, later questioning, discussion with others and repeated recollection can gradually blend observation with interpretation. [OUP Academic] academic.oup.com Academic Eyewitness Memory OUP AcademicEyewitness Memory Oxford Academic22 Mar 2023 — Indeed, the very act of testing memory changes (i.e., contaminates) memory... In Fortean cases this matters especially because unusual experiences often lack familiar reference points. Faced with something unexpected, witnesses naturally search for the closest available explanation, whether that comes from folklore , popular culture, aviation, religion or previous beliefs. Observation Versus Inference Separating observation from inference is not about stripping away everything interesting. It is about identifying which parts of the account remain stable regardless of explanation. For example, suppose two investigators examine the same report. The witness says: \"A silent triangular craft flew over my house.\" One investigator immediately asks: How large did it appear? What colour were the lights? Which direction was it travelling? Approximately how long was it visible? Did it pass behind clouds? Could you hear traffic or wind at the same time? These questions recover observations. By contrast, questions such as \"Did it seem extraterrestrial?\" or \"Was it cloaked?\" encourage further interpretation rather than recovering perception. Investigative interview methods such as the Cognitive Interview similarly encourage witnesses to report everything they remember without filtering details through assumptions about importance. Open ended recall generally produces richer observational material than leading questions, while avoiding unnecessary suggestion. [Cambridge University Press & Assessment] resolve.cambridge.org Based on this theoretical framework, we developed an interview protocol containing four general memory enhancement...Read more... Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Details That Survive Poor Viewing Conditions Many strange event reports occur under conditions that naturally reduce perceptual accuracy: darkness fog or rain long viewing distance brief exposure emotional surprise partial obstruction divided attention These conditions do not automatically invalidate testimony. Instead, they determine which observations deserve greater confidence. Certain features often remain relatively robust even under poor conditions: approximate direction duration sequence of events relative movement number of sounds whether multiple objects were present whether the event was continuous or intermittent Other details become much less reliable: exact size precise distance facial features fine surface detail colour under poor lighting complex shape viewed briefly A witness who honestly says, \"I couldn't tell exactly what shape it had\" is often preserving the integrity of the report rather than weakening it. Careful uncertainty is generally more valuable than confident embellishment. How Labels Can Outrun the Evidence Human beings rapidly categorise unfamiliar experiences. A witness who initially describes: \"A bright orange light moving silently\" may later refer to it simply as: \"the UFO\" The label becomes shorthand, but it also changes how the event is remembered and discussed. This process can become stronger over time through: conversations with family or friends media reports documentaries internet discussions repeated interviews exposure to similar stories Research on eyewitness memory demonstrates that later information can alter recollection, a phenomenon known as the misinformation effect. Repeated exposure to explanations can become incorporated into remembered experience even when the original observation was more limited. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Misinformation effect Misinformation effect For Fortean researchers, this means that the earliest recorded account is often the most valuable. It is usually closest to the original perception and least influenced by subsequent interpretation. Scott Fraser: The problem with eyewitness testimony Channel: TED · Views: 246.2K · Uploaded: September 2012 · Length: 20 minutes Open on YouTube Practical Techniques for Preserving Observation Investigators who specialise in unusual reports often use simple techniques that reduce interpretive contamination. Instead of asking: \"Was it hovering unnaturally?\" they ask: \"Describe its movement.\" Instead of: \"Did the creature threaten you?\" they ask: \"What did it do next?\" Instead of: \"Did you think it was paranormal?\" they ask: \"What made you think something unusual had happened?\" Useful prompts include: \"What happened first?\" \"What could you actually see?\" \"What could you hear?\" \"What made you notice it?\" \"What happened immediately afterwards?\" \"Which parts are you certain about?\" \"Which parts are your best guess?\" Notice that the final question explicitly separates memory from interpretation. Many witnesses readily distinguish between what they know and what they inferred once invited to do so. Why Honest Witnesses Still Add Interpretations Interpretation is not a sign of deception. It is a normal feature of perception. Psychologists describe perception as an active process in which the brain combines incoming sensory information with existing knowledge to construct a coherent understanding of events. This allows people to react quickly in everyday life but also means that unfamiliar events are interpreted through familiar mental models. Someone unfamiliar with astronomical objects may interpret Venus as an approaching aircraft. Someone familiar with UFO literature may interpret the same observation as an unidentified craft. Someone raised with local folklore may identify an indistinct figure as a known legendary being. The underlying observation may be identical while the interpretation differs dramatically. For this reason, disagreement over explanations does not necessarily imply disagreement about what was actually observed. Psychology Eyewitness Testimony Channel: Cognito · Views: 2.6K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube A Strong Strange Event Report Keeps the Two Separate The most valuable witness testimony often contains explicit distinctions such as: \"I saw a bright object move silently across the sky. I don't know what it was.\" or: \"Something large crossed the road. I assumed it was an animal at first, but I couldn't identify the species.\" These statements preserve both elements: the observation"},{"title":"UAP Files","url":"/uap-files/","description":"GEIPAN and Project Blue Book show why testimony becomes more useful when reports are sorted, checked, and limited by evidence quality.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"UAP Files Can Official Files Make Testimony Stronger? UAP Files UAP Files Can Official Files Make Testimony Stronger? GEIPAN and Project Blue Book show why testimony becomes more useful when reports are sorted, checked, and limited by evidence quality. GEIPAN and Project Blue Book show why testimony becomes more useful when reports are sorted, checked, and limited by evidence quality. dataset evidence Report categories and evidence quality GEIPAN and witness anonymity Project Blue Book and unexplained leftovers forteana witnesses uap files Introduction Official archives of unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP) do not make witness testimony automatically reliable, nor do they prove extraordinary explanations. Their value lies elsewhere: they provide a consistent system for collecting, comparing and evaluating reports. Instead of treating every unusual sighting as equally convincing, programmes such as France's GEIPAN and the United States Air Force's Project Blue Book attempted to separate detailed observations from weak or incomplete claims, document investigative methods, and preserve records for later review. For readers interested in Forteana , this structured approach is significant because it demonstrates that testimony becomes more useful when it is organised, checked against external evidence, and classified according to evidential quality rather than dramatic content. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPANSighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while m... Why official files improve the value of testimony Eyewitness accounts are inherently variable. Two people can witness the same event yet describe different shapes, colours or movements. Official UAP programmes recognised this problem early and attempted to reduce it through standardised reporting. Rather than asking simply, \"What did you see?\", investigators typically recorded a much wider set of information, including: Exact time and location. Weather and visibility. Direction and duration of the observation . Number and independence of witnesses. Whether photographs, radar records or other physical evidence existed. Possible conventional explanations such as aircraft, astronomical objects or atmospheric phenomena. The important shift is methodological rather than sensational. Structured testimony allows later investigators to compare reports using the same criteria instead of relying on memory or folklore . Even when a case remains unexplained, readers can see why it remained so and what evidence was available at the time. [DocsTeach] docsteach.org UFO Sighting Questionnaire from Project Blue Book Status...Project Bluebook was the codename for the most well known of the U.S... UFO Interview, 1966 Channel: US National Archives · Views: 688.8K · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 4 minutes 31 seconds Open on YouTube Report categories and evidence quality One of the strongest lessons from official UAP archives is that \"unidentified\" is not a single category. Good investigation distinguishes between reports that cannot be solved because information is missing and reports that remain unexplained despite relatively complete evidence. This distinction matters because poor quality reports rarely become stronger simply by remaining mysterious. Conversely, a carefully documented case may still resist identification without implying an extraordinary cause. Several recurring principles appear across official investigations: Completeness matters. Reports containing precise observational details are more valuable than vague recollections. Independent confirmation increases confidence. Multiple witnesses who report separately generally provide more robust evidence than a single narrative repeated over time. External records are important. Radar data, weather information or aviation records may support or contradict witness observations. Ordinary explanations are tested first. Investigators routinely compare reports against astronomical, meteorological and aviation data before leaving a case unresolved. These practices transform testimony from anecdote into evidence that can at least be evaluated consistently, even if no final explanation emerges. [DocsTeach] docsteach.org UFO Sighting Questionnaire from Project Blue Book Status...Project Bluebook was the codename for the most well known of the U.S... How GEIPAN balances openness with witness protection France's GEIPAN , operating within the national space agency CNES since 1977, offers one of the clearest examples of structured handling of civilian reports. Its purpose is not to prove extraterrestrial visitation but to collect, analyse and archive reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena while seeking conventional explanations wherever possible. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPAN7 Jul 2025 — GEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives inf... GEIPAN and witness anonymity A distinctive feature of GEIPAN is its commitment to publishing investigation files while protecting the identities of witnesses. This serves two goals simultaneously. First, anonymity reduces unnecessary exposure for people reporting unusual experiences, making it more likely that ordinary citizens will submit reports without fear of ridicule. Second, public release of investigation summaries allows outside researchers to examine methods and conclusions without compromising personal privacy. Rather than asking readers to trust hidden investigations, GEIPAN makes much of its reasoning visible while removing identifying information. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPANSighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while m... The organisation also works with police, the French gendarmerie, aviation authorities and scientific specialists when appropriate. Investigators may revisit locations, reconstruct viewing conditions and consult experts in fields such as meteorology, optics or image analysis before reaching a conclusion. This illustrates how testimony functions as the starting point of an investigation rather than its endpoint. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPANSighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while m... What the categories achieve GEIPAN classifies reports according to the outcome of investigation rather than by how unusual they appear. Well known categories include: Identified cases , where a conventional explanation is established. Probably identified cases , where evidence strongly favours an explanation. Insufficient information cases , where reliable conclusions cannot be reached. Unexplained cases , where available evidence does not permit identification despite investigation. The practical effect is that an unexplained report does not automatically carry more evidential weight than an identified one. Instead, readers can distinguish between genuine investigative uncertainty and simple lack of information. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPANSighting reports and investigation conclusions are published on the GEIPAN website (www.geipan.fr) while m... Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What Project Blue Book still teaches Project Blue Book investigated more than 12,000 reported UFO sightings between 1952 and 1969, making it one of the largest official collections of witness testimony ever assembled. The records remain publicly available through the US National Archives, allowing historians and researchers to examine individual case files rather than relying solely on later summaries. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... Blue Book ultimately concluded that most reports were attributable to conventional causes, while 701 cases remained officially unidentified after investigation. The Air Force also stated that it found no evidence that the unidentified reports represented extraterrestrial vehicles or technologies beyond established scientific knowledge. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... For students of Forteana, the enduring lesson is not the final conclusion but the preservation of the investigative record. Because the files include witness statements, questionnaires, correspondence and supporting documentation, later researchers can reassess cases as new information becomes available or compare them with similar reports. Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube The importance of unexplained leftovers A common misunderstanding is that every unexplained Blue"},{"title":"Rumor Waves","url":"/rumor-waves/","description":"Once a strange story spreads, later witnesses may describe not only what happened but what the rumor taught them to notice.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"Rumor Waves When a Sighting Becomes a Panic Rumor Waves Rumor Waves When a Sighting Becomes a Panic Once a strange story spreads, later witnesses may describe not only what happened but what the rumor taught them to notice. Once a strange story spreads, later witnesses may describe not only what happened but what the rumor taught them to notice. mechanism Memory conformity after shared discussion Social media and post event contamination Separating a wave from independent reports forteana witnesses rumor waves Introduction Rumour waves are one of the most important mechanisms shaping witness testimony in Fortean reports. A strange event may begin with one ambiguous observation , but as the story spreads through conversation, newspapers , television or social media, later witnesses often describe not only what they experienced but also what the circulating narrative encouraged them to notice. This does not mean that later witnesses are dishonest. Rather, decades of psychological research show that memory is reconstructive: recollections can be updated by information encountered after an event, especially when it comes from trusted people or appears to confirm an emerging public story. [PMC+2DigitalCommons] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect PMC NIHby DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 35 — The phenomenon of changes to report... For researchers studying Forteana , this distinction matters. A wave of similar reports may indicate a genuinely widespread event, but it may also reflect the spread of expectations, vocabulary and shared interpretations. Understanding how rumours reshape testimony helps explain why investigators place special weight on early independent accounts while treating later reports with greater caution. When a Sighting Becomes a Panic Many classic Fortean episodes follow a recognisable pattern. An unusual incident is reported. Local discussion expands the story. Journalists amplify dramatic details. People begin paying attention to phenomena they would normally ignore. Fresh reports appear, often using remarkably similar language. The important point is that the rumour does not necessarily invent experiences from nothing. Instead, it changes the way ambiguous experiences are interpreted. Someone who previously would have described \"a bright light behind the trees\" may later describe \"the glowing craft everyone has been talking about.\" The sensory experience may remain largely unchanged while the interpretation becomes increasingly standardised. Historians of folklore have long noted that legends often spread in this way. Modern cognitive psychology explains part of the mechanism: people naturally incorporate later information into their recollection of earlier events, particularly when the original perception was brief, uncertain or emotionally significant. [PMC+2BPS PsychHub] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect PMC NIHby DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 35 — The phenomenon of changes to report... In Fortean history this process has been observed across many categories of reports, including UFO flaps, phantom animal sightings, haunted location stories and episodes that later become labelled as local \"panics\". The shared narrative can become almost as important as the original event itself. Memory Conformity After Shared Discussion One of the strongest findings in eyewitness research is memory conformity: witnesses who discuss an event with one another can unintentionally influence each other's recollections. Experimental studies have repeatedly demonstrated that people often adopt details supplied by another witness, particularly when they believe the other person had a better view or is more reliable. After discussion, participants may confidently remember objects, actions or sequences that they never personally observed. [DigitalCommons+2PMC] digitalcommons.unl.edu Memory Conformity Between Eyewitnessesby F Gabbert · 2012 · Cited by 39 — post event misinformation is encountered socially... For strange event reports, this has several consequences: Separate witnesses can become less independent after they compare stories. Small uncertainties become filled with details borrowed from others. Confidence often increases even as accuracy declines. Later investigators may mistake agreement between witnesses for independent confirmation when the agreement actually arose through conversation. This explains why experienced investigators prefer to interview witnesses separately and as soon as possible after an event. Independence is often more informative than agreement. Another subtle effect is the adoption of vocabulary. One witness may initially describe \"a dark animal\", while another suggests \"it looked like a wolf\". After repeated discussion, both may sincerely remember seeing a wolf like creature, even though that description emerged only after conversation. How reliable is your memory? Elizabeth Loftus Channel: TED · Views: 2.7M · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube How Media Coverage Changes What People Remember Press coverage has long acted as an amplifier for rumour waves. Once newspapers begin highlighting a particular explanation or dramatic interpretation, later witnesses often report details that fit the emerging narrative. This does not require deliberate fabrication. Instead, media reports provide a framework that shapes later recall. Research on the misinformation effect has consistently shown that post event information—including leading questions, news reports and conversations—can alter remembered details of an original experience. People may incorporate these later suggestions into what feels like a genuine memory. [PMC+2BPS PsychHub] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov A Behavioral Account of the Misinformation Effect PMC NIHby DM Challies · 2011 · Cited by 35 — The phenomenon of changes to report... Historically, this process helps explain why: UFO waves often develop increasingly similar descriptions after the first widely publicised reports. Creature sightings become more detailed as local folklore accumulates. Haunted locations acquire additional recurring features over time, such as identical apparitions or sounds reported by successive visitors. The similarity of later testimony is therefore not automatically evidence that everyone observed the same phenomenon independently. Social Media and Post Event Contamination Digital communication has accelerated processes that once unfolded over weeks or months. Witnesses now encounter photographs, speculative reconstructions, viral posts and comment threads within minutes of an event. These materials provide enormous amounts of post event information before many people have recorded their own independent recollections. Recent research has found that misinformation delivered in social media style formats can reduce eyewitness accuracy, while carefully designed warnings only partly reduce the effect. Online misinformation can also reshape memory through repeated exposure, trusted social connections and the blending of genuine and false details. [Taylor & Francis Online+2PMC] tandfonline.com Taylor & Francis OnlineWitnesses' susceptibility to misleading post event...by SJ Sharman · 2024 · Cited by 3 — This study examined the... For Fortean events, common contamination pathways include: reading eyewitness discussions before writing one's own account; viewing edited photographs or speculative diagrams; repeated exposure to dramatic headlines; participating in online groups that reward increasingly elaborate interpretations. Modern rumour waves therefore spread much faster than historical ones, while leaving extensive digital traces that investigators can sometimes reconstruct. How Memory can be manipulated, with Elizabeth Loftus, PhD Speaking of Psychology Channel: American Psychological Association · Views: 62.4K · Uploaded: October 2019 · Length: 17 minutes Open on YouTube Separating a Rumour Wave from Independent Reports Because rumour contamination is common, investigators try to distinguish genuinely independent testimony from socially connected testimony. Useful indicators of relative independence include: Early documentation. Statements recorded before extensive publicity generally carry greater evidential value. Witness isolation. Reports made before witnesses communicated with one another are less vulnerable to memory conformity. Unique observational details. Independent witnesses often differ in wording while agreeing on core observations. Perfect verbal similarity can itself be suspicious. Chronology. Mapping exactly when each report appeared helps determine whether later accounts could have been influenced by earlier publicity. Original notes. Diaries, emergency calls, photographs with timestamps and contemporaneous sketches preserve observations before later reinterpretation. Conversely, investigators become more cautious when numerous reports emerge only after intensive"},{"title":"Sleep Visitors","url":"/sleep-visitors/","description":"Night-visitor reports can feel utterly real while sharing features with known sleep paralysis experiences.","breadcrumb":"Forteana / Witnesses","level":3,"text":"Sleep Visitors When the Intruder Is Felt in Bed Sleep Visitors Sleep Visitors When the Intruder Is Felt in Bed Night visitor reports can feel utterly real while sharing features with known sleep paralysis experiences. Night visitor reports can feel utterly real while sharing features with known sleep paralysis experiences. population context Presence, pressure, and immobility Alien abduction reports and sleep states Respectful explanation without ridicule forteana witnesses sleep visitors Introduction Among the most unsettling forms of witness testimony in Fortean literature are reports of waking in bed unable to move while sensing that an unseen figure has entered the room. Witnesses often insist they were awake, describe the experience as more vivid than a dream, and remember it for years afterwards. These accounts have contributed to stories of ghosts , demons, witches, shadow people, and, in the late twentieth century, extraterrestrial visitors. Modern sleep research has shown that many of these experiences closely resemble episodes of sleep paralysis : a temporary overlap between rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and wakefulness in which muscle paralysis persists while consciousness returns. During this state, intensely realistic hallucinations may accompany a powerful feeling that another being is present. This does not mean every bedroom encounter can be categorically explained by sleep paralysis, but it does provide one of the strongest evidence based mechanisms for understanding why so many sincere witnesses independently report remarkably similar experiences. [PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... Presence, pressure, and immobility The classic sleep paralysis report follows a recognisable pattern. A person awakens or believes they have awakened, discovers they cannot move or call for help, and becomes aware of a threatening presence nearby. The figure may remain in a corner of the room, stand at the foot of the bed, or approach the sleeper. Many witnesses report intense fear before any visual image appears. Researchers have identified several recurring features: Temporary paralysis despite feeling mentally awake. A sensed presence , often interpreted as another conscious being. Visual or auditory hallucinations , including shadowy figures, footsteps, whispering, doors opening, or breathing. Chest pressure or difficulty breathing , sometimes experienced as someone sitting or kneeling on the chest. A feeling of imminent danger , even when no physical threat is visible. Episodes lasting seconds to a few minutes , after which movement suddenly returns. [PMC+2Harvard Health] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... Although witnesses often regard these perceptions as external events, neuroscience suggests they arise from the unusual combination of wake like awareness and REM dreaming processes. During REM sleep, the brain naturally suppresses muscle movement to prevent dreams being acted out. If consciousness returns before this paralysis ends, dream imagery can become blended with awareness of the bedroom, creating an experience that feels indistinguishable from reality. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov by V Bhalerao · 2024 · Cited by 10 — Sleep paralysis (SP) is a phenomenon wherein individuals awaken from deep sleep but are unable to... One striking feature is the overwhelming certainty reported by experiencers. Many later say they had no doubt someone was physically present, even after recognising that no intruder could be found. This conviction helps explain why bedroom visitor testimony is often emotionally powerful despite lacking external evidence. Why the \"presence\" feels so real The sensation of another being nearby is not merely a visual hallucination. Researchers describe it as a distinct psychological phenomenon known as a \"sensed presence\". Rather than simply seeing an imagined figure, people experience another mind sharing the room. Studies by James Allan Cheyne and colleagues found that this \"intruder\" pattern forms one of the principal clusters of sleep paralysis experiences. The accompanying fear appears to organise the hallucination around a threatening observer or pursuer, producing remarkably consistent narratives across individuals who have never encountered one another. [PubMed+2PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov more... Some researchers propose that the brain's threat monitoring systems become unusually active during this mixed sleep wake state. Unable to move and searching for an explanation, the mind constructs an external agent responsible for the danger. This interpretation remains a scientific hypothesis rather than an established fact, but it fits many recurring reports. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Paralysis, “The Ghostly Bedroom Intruder” and Out of...by B Jalal · 2017 · Cited by 49 — A disturbance of these interactions wo... Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Why cultures describe different visitors The underlying experience appears surprisingly consistent across cultures, while the identity of the visitor varies dramatically. Historical accounts have described: [pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov more... medieval demons or devils; witches entering bedrooms; the \"Old Hag\" in Newfoundland folklore ; ghosts or ancestral spirits; shadow people; extraterrestrial visitors. The core sensations—paralysis, fear, chest pressure, and a nearby presence—remain broadly similar even as cultural interpretation changes. Researchers therefore distinguish between the physiological experience and the explanation assigned to it. The body may generate a comparable state, while religion, folklore, media, or personal belief influences whether the visitor is understood as supernatural, extraterrestrial, or psychological. [PMC+2PMC] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The Assessment of the Phenomenology of Sleep Paralysisby C Paradis · 2009 · Cited by 61 — In the United States, some English speakers... This helps explain why reports from different eras often resemble one another despite describing entirely different beings. Alien abduction reports and sleep states Sleep paralysis occupies an important place in discussions of alleged alien abductions because many reported abduction narratives begin in the bedroom during the night. Witnesses commonly describe: awakening unable to move; sensing figures around the bed; bright lights; floating sensations; being taken through walls or windows; missing time or fragmented memories. These themes overlap substantially with recognised features of sleep paralysis and REM related hallucinations, particularly the combination of paralysis, perceived intruders, vestibular sensations such as floating, and dream like transitions. [PMC+2PubMed] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Sleep Paralysis and Lucid Dreaming—Between Waking and...by S Ableidinger · 2023 · Cited by 21 — Further, alien abductions and UFO enc... Research by Richard McNally and Susan Clancy examined individuals reporting alien abduction experiences associated with apparent sleep paralysis. Their work suggested that at least some abduction narratives are best understood as interpretations of vivid hypnopompic hallucinations occurring during awakening rather than evidence of literal extraterrestrial encounters. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov intruders in the bedroom). In this article, we summarize two studies. In the first study, we assessed 10 individuals who reported abducti... This conclusion does not imply that every person reporting an abduction experienced sleep paralysis. Abduction testimony varies considerably, and many reports include daytime experiences or narratives extending beyond recognised sleep phenomena. Nevertheless, sleep paralysis provides a well supported explanation for a significant subset of bedroom based encounter reports and remains one of the most influential naturalistic models in the field. [PubMed+2PMC] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Alien abduction experiences: Some clues from...by KJ Holden · 2002 · Cited by 79 — Four areas of neuroscience are considered with... 163 Paranormal Beliefs and Sleep Paralysis with Dr. Chris French Channel: Roots of Reality Open on YouTube Why witnesses remain convinced One reason these reports persist in Fortean literature is that they possess qualities usually associated with genuine perception rather than dreaming. Witnesses often emphasise that: [sleepfoundation.org] sleepfoundation.org sleep demon Sleep Paralysis Demon: Causes, Types, and Prevention25 Jul 2025 — Sleep paralysis demons refer to the vivid, often terrifying hallucinati... they could clearly see familiar furniture; the room appeared exactly as"}]};
